in-for-beginners-6-1","title":"Lesson XXXVII (Part 1): possum and the Uses of the Infinitive","steps":["Learn the principal parts and indicative of possum (§ 495)","Learn the accusative subject of the infinitive, the complementary infinitive, and the infinitive as a noun","Learn the vocabulary; translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_XXXVII"},{"label":"Special vocabulary for Lesson XXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec217vocab"}],"pages":"pp. 93–96","questions":["Translate: Diū sine aquā vīvere nōn potestis.","Write in Latin: To see is to believe.","In what case is the subject of an infinitive?"],"answers":["You cannot live long without water","Vidēre est crēdere","The accusative"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-6-2","title":"Lesson XXXVII (Part 2): The Faithless Tarpeia","steps":["Read the Tarpeia story aloud in Latin, a sentence at a time","Translate it, explaining why cōnsilium cēpērunt is perfect while the earlier verbs are imperfect","Narrate the first half of the story in English"],"links":[{"label":"Read § 218","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec218"}],"pages":"p. 96","questions":["Where had the Romans fled from the Sabines?","What did Tarpeia carry to the Roman troops every day?","What did Tarpeia long for?"],"answers":["To the Capitol","Water","The Sabines'' gold ornaments"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-6-3","title":"Lesson XXXVIII (Part 1): The Relative Pronoun quī, quae, quod","steps":["Read § 219 on simple, compound, and complex sentences","Decline quī, quae, quod and compare it with is, ea, id","Learn the rule for agreement of the relative and explain the two examples in § 223"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_XXXVIII"}],"pages":"pp. 97–99","questions":["Give the dative plural of quī.","Translate: Rōmānī interfēcērunt fēminam quae capta est.","With what must a relative agree in gender and number?"],"answers":["Quibus","The Romans killed the woman who was captured","Its antecedent"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-6-4","title":"Lesson XXXVIII (Part 2): The Interrogative Pronoun — Tarpeia (Concluded)","steps":["Learn quis, quid (pronoun) and quī, quae, quod (adjective)","Translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin","Read the end of the Tarpeia story"],"links":[{"label":"Read §§ 225–229","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec225"}],"pages":"pp. 99–101","questions":["Translate: Quī vir eōs dūcit?","Write in Latin: What victory will you announce?","How did the Sabines kill Tarpeia?"],"answers":["What man is leading them?","Quam victōriam nūntiābis?","They threw their shields on her"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-6-5","title":"Lesson XXXIX: The Third Declension — Consonant Stems (Class I)","steps":["Read about bases and stems (§§ 230–232)","Decline prīnceps, mīles, lapis, rēx, iūdex, virtūs; then dux, eques, pedes, pēs","Learn the vocabulary; translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_XXXIX"},{"label":"Special vocabulary for Lesson XXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec234vocab"}],"pages":"pp. 101–104","questions":["Give the genitive singular of mīles.","How do you find the base of a third-declension noun?","Translate: Pedēs mīlitum lapidibus vulnerābantur."],"answers":["Mīlitis","Drop -is from the genitive singular","The soldiers'' feet were wounded by stones"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-6-6","title":"Lesson XL: Consonant Stems (Class II) — cōnsul, legiō, ōrdō, pater","steps":["Decline cōnsul, legiō, ōrdō, and pater","Note how -ōn-, -in-, and -tr- stems change in the nominative","Learn the vocabulary; translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XL","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_XL"},{"label":"Special vocabulary for Lesson XL","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec237vocab"}],"pages":"pp. 104–105","questions":["Give the genitive singular of legiō.","Give the accusative plural of pater.","Write in Latin: Who has seen my father today?"],"answers":["Legiōnis","Patrēs","Quis patrem meum hodiē vīdit?"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-6-7","title":"Lesson XLI: Neuter Consonant Stems — flūmen, tempus, opus, caput","steps":["Decline flūmen, tempus, opus, and caput","Apply the neuter rules of § 74 (nominative and accusative alike, plural in -a)","Learn the vocabulary; translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_XLI"},{"label":"Special vocabulary for Lesson XLI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec239vocab"}],"pages":"pp. 105–107","questions":["Give the nominative plural of tempus.","Translate: Rōma est in rīpīs flūminis magnī.","Write in Latin: To save the fatherland was a great task."],"answers":["Tempora","Rome is on the banks of a great river","Patriam servāre erat magnum opus"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-6-8","title":"Lesson XLII: Review Lesson — Terror Cimbricus","steps":["Review the paradigms of prīnceps, cōnsul, and flūmen (§§ 233, 236, 238)","Translate the story of the Cimbri and Teutons and Marius","Decline every third-declension noun you find in the story"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_XLII"}],"pages":"pp. 107–108","questions":["Who was called back from Africa to save Rome?","What was Marius called after the victory?","Translate: Servāte patriam!"],"answers":["Marius","Father of his country (pater patriae)","Save the fatherland!"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-6-9","title":"Lesson XLIII: The Third Declension — I-Stems","steps":["Learn which nouns are i-stems (§ 241) and how they differ from consonant stems","Decline caedēs, hostis, urbs, cliēns, then īnsigne, animal, calcar","Learn the vocabulary; translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_XLIII"},{"label":"Special vocabulary for Lesson XLIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec245vocab"}],"pages":"pp. 108–111","questions":["Give the genitive plural of urbs.","Give the nominative plural of animal.","Translate: Alia animālia terram, alia mare amant."],"answers":["Urbium","Animālia","Some animals love the land, others the sea"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-6-10","title":"Lesson XLIV: Irregular Nouns and Gender in the Third Declension — The First Bridge over the Rhine","steps":["Decline vīs and iter","Learn the gender rules in § 247 and give the gender of the nouns in § 248","Learn the vocabulary; translate the Rhine bridge story and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_XLIV"},{"label":"Special vocabulary for Lesson XLIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec249vocab"}],"pages":"pp. 111–113","questions":["What gender is mōns?","Give the genitive singular of iter.","Why did the Germans flee when they saw the bridge?"],"answers":["Masculine","Itineris","They were struck with terror, for no bridge had been seen on the Rhine before"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-6-11","title":"Review V: Lessons XXXVII–XLIV","steps":["Give the English of the words in § 517; translate the list in § 518 with genitives, genders, and principal parts","Drill the words you missed","Answer the review questions on possum, the infinitive, the relative, and the third declension"],"links":[{"label":"Read Review V","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#review_V"}],"pages":"pp. 273–275","questions":["Give the English of flūmen.","Give the Latin for \"never.\"","What gender is a third-declension noun ending in -or?"],"answers":["River","Numquam","Masculine"]}]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-u7","title":"Unit 6: Fourth and Fifth Declensions; Pronouns (Lessons XLV–LII)","lessons":[{"id":"latin-for-beginners-7-1","title":"Lesson XLV: Adjectives of the Third Declension — The Romans Invade the Enemy''s Country","steps":["Learn the three classes: ācer, ācris, ācre; omnis, omne; pār","Decline vir ācer, ager omnis, and proelium pār; note the ablative in -ī","Learn the vocabulary; translate the invasion story and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_XLV"},{"label":"Special vocabulary for Lesson XLV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec258vocab"}],"pages":"pp. 113–116","questions":["Give the ablative singular of omnis.","Translate: Fortūna fortibus semper favet.","Who was among the captives?"],"answers":["Omnī","Fortune always favors the brave","The king''s mother and sister"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-7-2","title":"Lesson XLVI: The Fourth (U-) Declension","steps":["Decline adventus and cornū; note the feminines domus and manus","Learn the vocabulary; translate Exercise I","Put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_XLVI"},{"label":"Special vocabulary for Lesson XLVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec261vocab"}],"pages":"pp. 116–117","questions":["Give the genitive singular of adventus.","Translate: Ante adventum Caesaris","Write in Latin: I trained my horse with my own hands."],"answers":["Adventūs","Before Caesar''s arrival","Equum meum manibus meīs exercuī"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-7-3","title":"Lesson XLVII: Expressions of Place; the Locative; domus — Daedalus and Icarus","steps":["Learn the rules for place to which, from which, and where, and the exceptions for towns, domus, and rūs","Learn the locative (Rōmae, Corinthī, domī) and the declension of domus (§ 468)","Learn the vocabulary; translate Exercise I and read the start of Daedalus and Icarus"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_XLVII"},{"label":"Special vocabulary for Lesson XLVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec270vocab"}],"pages":"pp. 117–121","questions":["Write in Latin: Galba lives at Rome.","Write in Latin: Galba hastens home.","Why could Daedalus not escape from Crete at first?"],"answers":["Galba Rōmae habitat","Galba domum properat","The king would not let him go and the sea blocked his flight"],"notes":"Exercise II (English to Latin) can be done the next day if time runs short."},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-7-4","title":"Lesson XLVIII: The Fifth (Ē-) Declension; the Ablative of Time","steps":["Decline diēs and rēs","Learn the rule for the ablative of time (prīmā lūce, merīdiē, hieme)","Learn the vocabulary; translate Galba the Farmer, put Exercise II into Latin, and continue Daedalus"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_XLVIII"},{"label":"Special vocabulary for Lesson XLVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec276vocab"}],"pages":"pp. 121–123","questions":["Give the genitive singular of rēs.","Translate: Hieme agricola eōs in lūdum mittit.","What did Daedalus make out of feathers?"],"answers":["Reī","In winter the farmer sends them to school","Real wings"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-7-5","title":"Lesson XLIX: Personal and Reflexive Pronouns — Daedalus and Icarus (Concluded)","steps":["Learn the eight classes of pronouns, then decline ego, tū, nōs, vōs, and suī","Practice reflexives (videō mē, sē videt) and forms like mēcum, nōbīscum","Learn the vocabulary; translate the exercises and finish the Daedalus story"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_XLIX"},{"label":"Special vocabulary for Lesson XLIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec283vocab"}],"pages":"pp. 123–126","questions":["Give the dative of ego.","Write in Latin: The boy sees himself.","Why did Icarus fall into the sea?"],"answers":["Mihi","Puer sē videt","He flew too near the sun and his wings were burned"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-7-6","title":"Lesson L: ipse and īdem — How Horatius Held the Bridge","steps":["Learn ipse (-self, very) and how it differs from the reflexive suī","Decline īdem, eadem, idem and note how m becomes n before d","Learn the vocabulary; translate the exercises and the start of the Horatius story"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson L","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_L"},{"label":"Special vocabulary for Lesson L","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec288vocab"}],"pages":"pp. 126–128","questions":["Translate: Caesar ipse scūtum ēripuit.","Write in Latin: in the same city","Who came with an army to help Tarquin the Proud?"],"answers":["Caesar himself snatched a shield","In eādem urbe","Porsena, king of the Etruscans"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-7-7","title":"Lesson LI: The Demonstratives hic, iste, ille — Horatius (Continued)","steps":["Learn hic, haec, hoc and the forms of iste and ille","Study the model sentences (§ 293): this near me, that near you, that over there","Learn the vocabulary; translate the German chieftain''s speech, Exercise II, and the next part of Horatius"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LI"},{"label":"Special vocabulary for Lesson LI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec294vocab"}],"pages":"pp. 128–130","questions":["Give the genitive singular of hic.","Translate: Iste equus est validus, sed ille est īnfīrmus.","How many comrades stood with Horatius at the bridge?"],"answers":["Huius","That horse of yours is strong, but that one yonder is weak","Two"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-7-8","title":"Lesson LII: The Indefinite Pronouns — Horatius (Concluded)","steps":["Learn quis, aliquis, quīdam, quisquam, and quisque and when each is used","Learn the vocabulary; translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin","Read how Horatius held the bridge alone and swam to safety"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LII"},{"label":"Special vocabulary for Lesson LII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec298vocab"}],"pages":"pp. 130–133","questions":["Translate: sī quis","Write in Latin: Each one was praising his own work.","What did the Roman people set up in honor of Horatius?"],"answers":["If any one","Quisque suum opus laudābat","A statue in a public place"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-7-9","title":"Review VI: Lessons XLV–LII","steps":["Give the English of the words in § 521 and translate the list in § 522","Drill the words you missed","Answer the review questions on the fourth and fifth declensions, place, time, and pronouns"],"links":[{"label":"Read Review VI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#review_VI"}],"pages":"pp. 275–278","questions":["Give the English of exercitus.","Give the Latin for \"hope.\"","How do you say \"at home\" in Latin?"],"answers":["Army","Spēs","Domī"]}]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-u8","title":"Unit 7: Comparison — The Labors of Hercules (Lessons LIII–LVI)","lessons":[{"id":"latin-for-beginners-8-1","title":"Lesson LIII: Regular Comparison of Adjectives","steps":["Learn the endings -ior, -ius and -issimus, and decline clārior","Compare longus, fortis, recēns, and the -er adjectives ācer, pulcher, miser","Learn the vocabulary; translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LIII"},{"label":"Special vocabulary for Lesson LIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec306vocab"}],"pages":"pp. 133–135","questions":["Compare clārus (all three degrees).","Give the superlative of pulcher.","Write in Latin: Of all birds the eagle is the swiftest."],"answers":["Clārus, clārior, clārissimus","Pulcherrimus","Omnium avium aquila est vēlōcissima"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-8-2","title":"Reading Matter: How to Translate; The Labors of Hercules","steps":["Read the six suggestions for translating and the section on the parts of a sentence","Read the English summary of the life of Hercules","Practice the method on one sentence from an earlier story: find subject, verb, and object first"],"links":[{"label":"Read the introductory suggestions","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#readings"}],"pages":"pp. 194–196","questions":["What gives you the \"backbone\" of a Latin sentence?","For how many years did Hercules serve Eurystheus?","Which goddess hated Hercules all his life?"],"answers":["The nominatives and accusatives with the verb (subject, verb, object)","Twelve","Juno"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-8-3","title":"Reading LIII: The Infant Hercules and the Serpents","steps":["Translate the selection, using the six-step method","Find the comparatives and superlatives in it","Tell the story back in English"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LIII"}],"pages":"pp. 197–198","questions":["Who sent the serpents?","On what were Hercules and his brother sleeping?","What did the frightened brother do?"],"answers":["Juno","A huge shield","He called loudly for his mother"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-8-4","title":"Lesson LIV: Irregular Comparison (-lis Adjectives); Ablative with Comparatives","steps":["Learn the six -lis adjectives with superlatives in -limus (facillimus, simillimus …)","Learn the ablative with comparatives without quam (Nihil est clārius sōle)","Learn the vocabulary; translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LIV"},{"label":"Special vocabulary for Lesson LIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec310vocab"}],"pages":"pp. 135–137","questions":["Give the superlative of facilis.","Translate: Nihil pulchrius urbe Rōmā vīdī.","Write in Latin (without quam): The eagle is not slower than the horse."],"answers":["Facillimus","I have seen nothing more beautiful than the city of Rome","Aquila nōn est tardior equō"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-8-5","title":"Reading LIV: Hercules Conquers the Minyae; the Delphic Oracle","steps":["Translate the two short sections","List every superlative you find","Narrate why Hercules went to Delphi"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LIV"}],"pages":"pp. 198–199","questions":["What did the Minyae demand from Thebes every year?","What did King Creon give Hercules after the victory?","Who gave advice at the oracle of Delphi?"],"answers":["Tribute","His daughter in marriage","A woman called the Pythia"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-8-6","title":"Lesson LV: Irregular Comparison (Continued); plūs","steps":["Learn bonus–melior–optimus, magnus–maior–maximus, malus–peior–pessimus, multus–plūs–plūrimus, parvus–minor–minimus","Learn the double superlatives (extrēmus, īnfimus/īmus, postrēmus, suprēmus/summus) and decline plūs","Learn the vocabulary; translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LV"},{"label":"Special vocabulary for Lesson LV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec314vocab"}],"pages":"pp. 137–138","questions":["Compare malus.","Give the comparative and superlative of parvus.","Write in Latin: Slavery is worse than death."],"answers":["Malus, peior, pessimus","Minor, minimus","Servitūs est peior quam mors (morte)"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-8-7","title":"Reading LV: Hercules Serves Eurystheus; the Nemean Lion","steps":["Translate the selection","Find the connecting relative (Quae ubi audīvit) and explain it","Tell how Hercules killed the lion"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LV"}],"pages":"pp. 199–200","questions":["To which king did Hercules give himself as a slave?","Why could arrows not stop the lion?","What did Hercules wear afterward?"],"answers":["Eurystheus, king of Tiryns","No weapon could wound it","The lion''s skin"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-8-8","title":"Lesson LVI: Irregular Comparison (Concluded); Ablative of Measure of Difference","steps":["Learn citerior, interior, prior, propior, ulterior and their superlatives","Learn the ablative of the measure of difference (multō, paulō, nihilō)","Learn the vocabulary; translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LVI"},{"label":"Special vocabulary for Lesson LVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec318vocab"}],"pages":"pp. 138–140","questions":["Translate: Haec via est multō lātior quam illa.","Give the superlative of prior.","Write in Latin: Your horse is a little swifter than mine."],"answers":["This road is much wider than that one","Prīmus","Tuus equus est paulō celerior quam meus"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-8-9","title":"Reading LVI: Slaying the Lernaean Hydra","steps":["Translate the selection","Notice the second connecting relative (Quod ubi vīdit)","Draw or describe the Hydra from the Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LVI"}],"pages":"p. 200","questions":["How many heads did the Hydra have?","How did Hercules stop new heads from growing?","What did he do with the last head?"],"answers":["Nine","He burned the necks with fire","He put it under a huge rock, because it was immortal"]}]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-u9","title":"Unit 8: Adverbs, Numerals, Deponents — Hercules Concluded (Lessons LVII–LX)","lessons":[{"id":"latin-for-beginners-9-1","title":"Lesson LVII: Formation and Comparison of Adverbs","steps":["Learn how adverbs are made with -ē, -iter, and -ter, and compared","Memorize the irregular adverbs (bene, melius, optimē; diū; magnopere; parum; prope; saepe)","Learn the vocabulary; form the adverbs in § 324, translate Exercise I, and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LVII"},{"label":"Special vocabulary for Lesson LVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec326vocab"}],"pages":"pp. 140–142","questions":["Form the adverb from fortis.","Compare the adverb bene.","Translate: Rōmānī saepius quam hostēs vīcērunt."],"answers":["Fortiter","Bene, melius, optimē","The Romans won more often than the enemy"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-9-2","title":"Reading LVII: The Arcadian Stag and the Erymanthian Boar","steps":["Translate the selection","Find the adverbs and say which adjective each comes from","Narrate both labors"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LVII"}],"pages":"pp. 200–201","questions":["What was special about the stag?","How long did Hercules chase the stag?","Where did the boar hide?"],"answers":["It had golden horns and feet swifter than the wind","A whole year","In a deep ditch"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-9-3","title":"Lesson LVIII: Numerals; the Partitive Genitive","steps":["Learn the cardinals 1–20, centum, ducentī, mīlle, and decline duo, trēs, mīlle (§ 479)","Learn the partitive genitive and why cardinals take ex or dē","Learn the vocabulary; translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LVIII"},{"label":"Special vocabulary for Lesson LVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec332vocab"}],"pages":"pp. 142–144","questions":["What is the Latin for 12?","Translate: decem mīlia mīlitum","Write in Latin: one of my brothers"],"answers":["Duodecim","Ten thousand soldiers","Ūnus ex frātribus meīs"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-9-4","title":"Reading LVIII: The Augean Stables and the Stymphalian Birds","steps":["Translate the selection","Find the numerals in it","Tell how Hercules cleaned the stables in one day"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LVIII"}],"pages":"pp. 201–202","questions":["How many cattle did Augeas have?","How long had the stable gone uncleaned?","What did the Stymphalian birds have?"],"answers":["Three thousand","Thirty years","Iron beaks"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-9-5","title":"Lesson LIX: Ordinals and Distributives; the Accusative of Extent — Caesar in Gaul","steps":["Learn the ordinals 1st–20th (§ 478) and singulī, bīnī, ternī","Learn the accusative of extent (how long? how far?) and sort the examples in § 336","Learn the vocabulary; translate Caesar in Gaul and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LIX"},{"label":"Special vocabulary for Lesson LIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec337vocab"}],"pages":"pp. 144–146","questions":["Translate: Vir tōtum diem cucurrit.","How many years did Caesar wage war in Gaul?","Write in Latin: the third legion"],"answers":["The man ran for a whole day","Seven","Legiō tertia"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-9-6","title":"Reading LIX: The Cretan Bull and the Horses of Diomedes","steps":["Translate the two sections","Find an accusative of extent and an ablative of time","Narrate how Diomedes was punished"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LIX"}],"pages":"pp. 202–203","questions":["How many days did Hercules sail to reach Crete?","How did Hercules seize the bull?","What did the horses eat?"],"answers":["Three days","By the horns","Men"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-9-7","title":"Lesson LX: Deponent Verbs; Prepositions with the Accusative","steps":["Learn the principal parts and synopses of hortor, vereor, sequor, patior, partior (§ 493)","Learn the prepositions with the accusative in § 340 and review those with the ablative","Learn the vocabulary; translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LX"},{"label":"Special vocabulary for Lesson LX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec341vocab"}],"pages":"pp. 146–149","questions":["Give the principal parts of sequor.","Translate: Imperātor eōs hortātus est.","What does deponent mean?"],"answers":["Sequor, sequī, secūtus sum","The general encouraged them","Passive in form but active in meaning (it has laid aside its active forms)"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-9-8","title":"Reading LX: The Belt of Hippolyte; Cerberus","steps":["Translate the selection","Find the deponent verbs","Narrate the last labor and the end of Hercules"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LX"}],"pages":"pp. 203–204","questions":["Who wanted Hippolyte''s belt?","How many heads did Cerberus have?","What did Jupiter finally give Hercules?"],"answers":["The daughter of Eurystheus","Three","Immortality"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-9-9","title":"Review VII: Lessons LIII–LX","steps":["Give the English of the words in § 524 and translate the list in § 525","Drill the words you missed","Answer the review questions on comparison, adverbs, numerals, and deponents"],"links":[{"label":"Read Review VII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#review_VII"}],"pages":"pp. 278–281","questions":["Give the English of celeritās.","Give the Latin for \"best.\"","Compare the adjective magnus."],"answers":["Speed, swiftness","Optimus","Magnus, maior, maximus"]}]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-u10","title":"Unit 9: The Subjunctive — The Story of Publius (Lessons LXI–LXV)","lessons":[{"id":"latin-for-beginners-10-1","title":"Lesson LXI: The Subjunctive Mood — the Present Subjunctive","steps":["Read the introduction to Part III and § 342–343 on the four subjunctive tenses","Inflect the present subjunctive, active and passive, of all four conjugations and of sum (sim, sīs, sit)","Compare the indicative and subjunctive ideas in § 346 and do the exercise in § 347"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LXI"}],"pages":"pp. 149–151","questions":["What is the mood sign of the present subjunctive in the first conjugation?","Translate: Statim proficīscāmur.","Give the present subjunctive, 3rd singular, of regō."],"answers":["-ē-","Let us set out at once","Regat"],"notes":"Part III begins here; the special vocabularies end, and review of the first 600 words takes their place."},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-10-2","title":"Reading LXI: Publius Is Born near Pompeii","steps":["Read the note on Roman names, then translate the selection","Find Vesuvius and Pompeii on a map","Describe the villa in English from the Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LXI"}],"pages":"pp. 204–205","questions":["What was the name of Publius''s father?","Which mountain stood behind the villa?","How far away was Pompeii?"],"answers":["Marcus","Vesuvius","Eight miles"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-10-3","title":"Lesson LXII: The Subjunctive of Purpose","steps":["Learn the rule for purpose clauses and the words that introduce them: quī, ut, quō, nē","Study the four examples in § 351 and remember that Latin prose never uses the infinitive for purpose","Translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LXII"}],"pages":"pp. 151–153","questions":["Translate: Fugiunt nē vulnerentur.","When is quō used to introduce a purpose clause?","Write in Latin: The Helvetii send ambassadors to seek peace."],"answers":["They flee so that they may not be wounded","When the clause contains a comparative","Helvētiī lēgātōs mittunt quī (ut) pācem petant"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-10-4","title":"Reading LXII: His Life on the Farm","steps":["Translate the selection","Find the purpose clauses (ut … gerant, nē tardī sint, quō … spectet)","Narrate a day of Publius and Lydia"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LXII"}],"pages":"pp. 205–206","questions":["Who was the vīlicus of the farm?","Who was Publius''s playmate?","Why could Publius''s father not come home?"],"answers":["Davus, the slave overseer","Lydia, Davus''s daughter","He was waging wars for the state in faraway lands"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-10-5","title":"Lesson LXIII: The Imperfect Subjunctive; Sequence of Tenses","steps":["Form the imperfect subjunctive from the present infinitive, active and passive, and of sum (essem)","Learn the table of primary and secondary tenses (§ 357)","Translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LXIII"}],"pages":"pp. 153–156","questions":["Give the imperfect subjunctive, 3rd singular active, of audiō.","Which tenses of the indicative are secondary?","Write in Latin: He sent messengers to announce the victory."],"answers":["Audīret","Imperfect, perfect, pluperfect","Nūntiōs mīsit quī victōriam nūntiārent"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-10-6","title":"Reading LXIII: Marcus Is Shipwrecked; Julia''s Letter","steps":["Read note 9 on how a Roman letter begins, then translate the letter","Find the purpose clauses and say why their verbs are imperfect","Write a three-line letter home using Sī valēs, bene est; ego valeō"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LXIII"}],"pages":"pp. 206–207","questions":["Where had Marcus been fighting?","For how many days was the ship driven by the storm?","From which city did Marcus write?"],"answers":["Asia","Three days and three nights","Athens"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-10-7","title":"Lesson LXIV (Part 1): The Perfect and Pluperfect Subjunctive","steps":["Inflect the perfect (-erim) and pluperfect (-issem) subjunctive active of the model verbs","Form the passives with sim and essem (amātus sim, amātus essem)","Inflect fuerim and fuissem, then the same tenses of cūrō, iubeō, sūmō"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LXIV"}],"pages":"pp. 156–157","questions":["How does the perfect subjunctive active differ from the future perfect indicative?","Give the pluperfect subjunctive, 3rd singular active, of regō.","Give the perfect subjunctive passive, 1st singular, of audiō."],"answers":["Only in the 1st singular: -erim instead of -erō","Rēxisset","Audītus sim"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-10-8","title":"Lesson LXIV (Part 2): Substantive Clauses of Purpose","steps":["Learn the rule: verbs of commanding, urging, asking, persuading, advising take ut/nē with the subjunctive","Learn the verb list in § 367 and the difference between iubeō (infinitive) and imperō (ut clause)","Translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read §§ 365–368","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec365"}],"pages":"pp. 157–159","questions":["Translate: Caesar Helvētiīs imperāvit nē per prōvinciam iter facerent.","Write in Latin with imperō: I order him to come.","Which verb of ordering takes the infinitive?"],"answers":["Caesar ordered the Helvetii not to march through the province","Imperō eī ut veniat","Iubeō"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-10-9","title":"Reading LXIV: Lentulus Reaches Home; a Trip to Pompeii","steps":["Translate the selection","Find the clauses after suādet and hortātur","Describe what Publius saw in the streets of Pompeii"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LXIV"}],"pages":"pp. 207–208","questions":["At which harbor did Lentulus''s ship land?","Why did Lentulus go to Pompeii?","By which gate did they enter the city?"],"answers":["Misenum","To buy fruit and food for a dinner with friends","The Stabian Gate"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-10-10","title":"Lesson LXV: The Subjunctive of possum; Verbs of Fearing","steps":["Learn the subjunctive of possum (§ 495) and its accents","Learn why ut after a verb of fearing means \"that not\" and nē means \"that\" or \"lest\"","Translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LXV"}],"pages":"pp. 160–161","questions":["Translate: Vereor ut hostium impetum sustinēre possim.","Write in Latin: We fear lest they are coming.","Give the present subjunctive, 3rd plural, of possum."],"answers":["I fear that I cannot withstand the enemy''s attack","Timēmus nē veniant","Possint"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-10-11","title":"Reading LXV: A Day at Pompeii","steps":["Translate the selection","Find the verb of fearing and the clauses after imperāvit","Read note 5 and explain how Romans counted the hours"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LXV"}],"pages":"pp. 208–209","questions":["Where did they plan to buy cakes?","What did Publius want to stay and see?","What later destroyed the buildings of Pompeii?"],"answers":["At the baker''s shop by the lion-head fountain","The gladiator shows in the amphitheater","The fire and earthquake of Vesuvius"]}]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-u11","title":"Unit 10: Participles, Irregular Verbs, and Result (Lessons LXVI–LXVIII)","lessons":[{"id":"latin-for-beginners-11-1","title":"Lesson LXVI (Part 1): The Participles — Forms","steps":["Learn the four participles of each conjugation: present (-ns), future active (-ūrus), perfect passive, gerundive (-ndus)","Decline amāns and note -ī (adjective) versus -e (participle) in the ablative","Give the four participles of a deponent verb (hortāns, hortātūrus, hortātus, hortandus)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LXVI"}],"pages":"pp. 161–163","questions":["Give the present active participle of capiō.","Give the future active participle of regō.","Which verbs have a perfect participle with active meaning?"],"answers":["Capiēns","Rēctūrus","Deponent verbs"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-11-2","title":"Lesson LXVI (Part 2): Translating Participles","steps":["Study the five model sentences in § 377 and say which conjunction (when, since, though) fits each","Translate Exercise I without translating the participles word for word","Put Exercise II into Latin, using participles for the clauses"],"links":[{"label":"Read §§ 377–378","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec377"}],"pages":"pp. 163–164","questions":["Translate: Oppidum captum vīdimus.","Translate: Sōl oriēns multōs interfectōs vīdit.","Write in Latin with a participle: frightened by the length of the way"],"answers":["We saw the town which had been captured","The rising sun saw many slain","Longitūdine viae perterritī"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-11-3","title":"Reading LXVI: Lentulus Engages a Tutor for His Son","steps":["Translate the selection","Find the relative clause of purpose (quī … docēret)","Read notes 9–10 and explain the bulla and the toga praetexta"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LXVI"}],"pages":"pp. 209–211","questions":["Who had taught Publius before the tutor came?","What language did the tutor teach besides music?","Where did the boys sit for lessons?"],"answers":["His mother Julia","Greek","In the peristyle of Marcus''s house"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-11-4","title":"Lesson LXVII (Part 1): The Irregular Verbs volō, nōlō, mālō","steps":["Learn the principal parts and conjugation of volō, nōlō, mālō (§ 497)","Note the irregular present indicative, subjunctive, infinitive, and imperfect subjunctive","Do Exercise I, 1–4, and Exercise II, 1–3"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LXVII"}],"pages":"pp. 164–165","questions":["Translate: māvīs","Give the present infinitive of mālō.","Translate: Nōlīte!"],"answers":["You prefer","Mālle","Do not! (Be unwilling!) — plural"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-11-5","title":"Lesson LXVII (Part 2): The Ablative Absolute","steps":["Study the four model sentences in § 380 and the rule in § 381","Read Notes 1–3 (tē duce; when not to use the construction; changing the voice)","Translate Exercise I, 5–13, and put Exercise II, 4–8, into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read §§ 380–382","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec380"}],"pages":"pp. 165–166","questions":["Translate: Hīs rēbus cognitīs, mīlitēs fūgērunt.","Translate: tē duce","Write in Latin: the Gauls having been conquered by Caesar (ablative absolute)"],"answers":["When these things were learned, the soldiers fled","With you as leader","Gallīs ā Caesare victīs"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-11-6","title":"Reading LXVII: Publius Goes to Rome to Finish His Education","steps":["Translate the selection, tracing the route on a map of Italy","Find each ablative absolute","List the towns Publius passed through on the Appian Way"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LXVII"}],"pages":"pp. 211–212","questions":["How old was Publius when he went to Rome?","Which famous orator welcomed them at Formiae?","How did travelers cross the great marshes?"],"answers":["Fifteen","Cicero","By boat"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-11-7","title":"Lesson LXVIII: The Irregular Verb fīō; the Subjunctive of Result","steps":["Learn the principal parts and conjugation of fīō (§ 500), the passive of faciō","Learn result clauses with ut and ut nōn, and compare them with purpose clauses (§ 387)","Translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LXVIII"}],"pages":"pp. 167–169","questions":["Translate: Terror erat tantus ut omnēs fugerent.","How does a negative result clause differ from a negative purpose clause?","Translate: fīet"],"answers":["The terror was so great that all fled","Result uses ut nōn; purpose uses nē","It will happen (it will be made)"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-11-8","title":"Reading LXVIII: Publius Puts On the Toga Virilis","steps":["Translate the selection and Marcus''s letter","Find the result clauses (ut … praebēret, ut … audīret)","Explain the difference between the toga praetexta and the toga virilis"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LXVIII"}],"pages":"pp. 212–213","questions":["At what age did Publius put on the toga virilis?","What did Marcus dedicate to the household gods?","Where was Publius led after he put on the toga?"],"answers":["Sixteen","The golden bulla","To the forum"]}]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-u12","title":"Unit 11: Characteristic, cum, Gerunds, Indirect Statement (Lessons LXIX–LXXII)","lessons":[{"id":"latin-for-beginners-12-1","title":"Lesson LXIX: The Subjunctive of Characteristic; the Predicate Accusative","steps":["Study the four examples in § 389 and the difference between is quī dūcit and is quī dūcat","Learn the predicate accusative with verbs of making, choosing, and calling (creō, appellō, nōminō, vocō, faciō)","Translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LXIX"}],"pages":"pp. 169–171","questions":["Translate: Nihil videō quod timeam.","Write in Latin: The Romans made Caesar consul.","Write in Latin: These are not the men to betray their friends."],"answers":["I see nothing to fear","Rōmānī Caesarem cōnsulem fēcērunt","Hī nōn sunt iī quī amīcōs suōs trādant"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-12-2","title":"Reading LXIX: Publius Joins Caesar''s Army in Gaul","steps":["Translate the selection","Find the clause of characteristic (quī … praedīcerent) and the purpose clause with nē","Narrate how Publius got through the Gallic lines"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LXIX"}],"pages":"pp. 213–215","questions":["Which mountains did Publius cross?","How did he get past the Gauls?","What rank did Caesar give him?"],"answers":["The Alps","He put on Gallic clothing","Military tribune"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-12-3","title":"Review VIII: Lessons LXI–LXIX","steps":["Review the vocabularies of the first seventeen lessons (§§ 502, 503, 506, 507)","Answer the review questions in § 528 on the subjunctive, participles, and irregular verbs","Inflect the subjunctive of one verb of each conjugation, active and passive"],"links":[{"label":"Read Review VIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#review_VIII"}],"pages":"pp. 281–282","questions":["Name the four tenses of the subjunctive.","Which tenses are primary?","Give the participles of vereor."],"answers":["Present, imperfect, perfect, pluperfect","Present, future, future perfect (and perfect definite)","Verēns, veritūrus, veritus, verendus"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-12-4","title":"Lesson LXX: Constructions with cum; the Ablative of Specification","steps":["Learn cum temporal, causal, and concessive, and when cum takes the indicative","Learn the ablative of specification and the idioms certiōrem facere, iter dare, obsidēs dare","Translate Exercise I, explaining each mood after cum, and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LXX"}],"pages":"pp. 171–173","questions":["Translate: Homō erat corpore īnfīrmus sed validus animō.","What does cum mean with a concessive subjunctive?","Write in Latin: We shall inform Caesar."],"answers":["The man was weak in body but strong in courage","Although","Caesarem certiōrem faciēmus"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-12-5","title":"Reading LXX: The Rival Centurions","steps":["Translate the story of Pullo and Vorenus","Find the cum clauses and say whether each is temporal, causal, or concessive","Narrate how the quarrel ended"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LXX"}],"pages":"pp. 215–216","questions":["What were the two centurions'' names?","What stopped Pullo from drawing his sword?","Which of them was judged braver?"],"answers":["Titus Pullo and Lucius Vorenus","A weapon stuck in his belt pushed the scabbard aside","Neither; no one could judge between them"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-12-6","title":"Lesson LXXI (Part 1): Vocabulary Review; the Gerund and Gerundive","steps":["Review the word lists in §§ 510–511","Learn the gerund (amandī, amandō, amandum) and how it supplies the cases the infinitive lacks","Compare gerund and gerundive in § 405 and learn the purpose forms ad + accusative and genitive + causā"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LXXI"}],"pages":"pp. 173–175","questions":["Translate: Superandō Gallōs Caesar magnam glōriam reportāvit.","Write in Latin with the gerundive: the hope of making peace","Write in Latin with causā: They came to see the city."],"answers":["By overcoming the Gauls Caesar won great glory","Spēs faciendae pācis","Urbis videndae causā vēnērunt"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-12-7","title":"Lesson LXXI (Part 2): The Predicate Genitive","steps":["Learn the predicate genitive (equus est Galbae) and the idioms in § 410","Translate Exercise I, saying which forms are gerunds and which gerundives","Put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read §§ 408–411","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec408"}],"pages":"pp. 175–177","questions":["Translate: Iubēre est rēgīnae.","What does novīs rēbus studēre mean?","Write in Latin: These ornaments belong to Cornelia."],"answers":["To command is the queen''s part (belongs to the queen)","To be eager for a revolution","Haec ōrnāmenta sunt Cornēliae"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-12-8","title":"Reading LXXI: The Enemy Besieging the Camp Are Repulsed","steps":["Translate the selection","Find the gerund and the result clause in the same sentence","Narrate Publius''s part in the battle"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LXXI"}],"pages":"pp. 216–217","questions":["How long had the battle lasted before Caesar ordered the sally?","Why could the soldiers not throw their spears?","How far did Publius chase the enemy?"],"answers":["Six hours","The enemy were too close; there was no room","To the Rhine, five miles away"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-12-9","title":"Lesson LXXII (Part 1): The Irregular Verb eō and Its Compounds","steps":["Learn the principal parts and conjugation of eō (§ 499)","Learn the compounds adeō, exeō, ineō, redeō, trānseō","Do Exercise I, 1–4"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LXXII"}],"pages":"pp. 177–178","questions":["Give the principal parts of eō.","Translate: ībunt","What does redeō mean?"],"answers":["Eō, īre, iī (īvī), itum","They will go","Return"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-12-10","title":"Lesson LXXII (Part 2): Indirect Statements","steps":["Compare English and Latin indirect statements (§§ 414–415)","Learn the rules for the accusative with infinitive and the tense of the infinitive","Learn the verbs of saying, knowing, thinking, perceiving; translate Exercise I, 5–14, and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read §§ 414–422","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec414"}],"pages":"pp. 178–181","questions":["Translate: Dīxit Gallōs fuisse fortīs.","Write in Latin: He says that the boy will be slow.","A future indicative in a direct statement becomes what in the indirect?"],"answers":["He said that the Gauls had been brave","Dīcit puerum tardum futūrum esse","A future infinitive"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-12-11","title":"Reading LXXII: Publius Goes to Germany; Its Forests and Strange Animals","steps":["Translate the selection","Find each indirect statement and turn one back into a direct statement","Describe the strange animals of the German forest"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LXXII"}],"pages":"pp. 217–218","questions":["Which lieutenant led the legions against the Germans?","What was said about the size of the great forest?","Why could the elk not get up if it fell?"],"answers":["Labienus","No one could cross it or knew its beginning or end","Its legs had no joints"]}]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-u13","title":"Unit 12: Final Constructions and Review (Lessons LXXIII–LXXIX)","lessons":[{"id":"latin-for-beginners-13-1","title":"Lesson LXXIII: Vocabulary Review; ferō; the Dative with Compounds","steps":["Review the word lists in §§ 513–514","Learn the principal parts and conjugation of ferō (§ 498) and its compounds adferō, cōnferō, dēferō, īnferō, referō","Learn the dative with compound verbs and the idioms; translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LXXIII"}],"pages":"pp. 181–183","questions":["Give the principal parts of ferō.","Translate: Germānī Gallīs bellum īnferunt.","What does pedem referre mean?"],"answers":["Ferō, ferre, tulī, lātus","The Germans make war upon the Gauls","To retreat"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-13-2","title":"Reading LXXIII: The Storming of a City","steps":["Translate the selection","List the Roman siege machines and say what each did","Draw a simple sketch of an agger with a tower and vineae"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LXXIII"}],"pages":"pp. 218–220","questions":["How high was the wall of the town?","What were the vīneae for?","What did the battering rams do?"],"answers":["Twenty-five feet","To shelter soldiers as they came up to the wall","Broke down the wall and gates"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-13-3","title":"Lesson LXXIV: Vocabulary Review; Indirect Questions","steps":["Review the word lists in §§ 517–518","Compare direct and indirect questions (§ 431) and the use of num for whether","Learn the idioms; translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LXXIV"}],"pages":"pp. 183–185","questions":["Translate: Rogāvit ubi esset Rōma.","Write in Latin: He asks who is conquering the Gauls.","What does dē tertiā vigiliā mean?"],"answers":["He asked where Rome was","Rogat quis Gallōs vincat","About the third watch"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-13-4","title":"Reading LXXIV: The City Is Taken; the Captives Are Questioned","steps":["Translate the selection","Find the indirect questions","Narrate how the town fell"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LXXIV"}],"pages":"pp. 220–222","questions":["Who gave the plan of attack in the council?","Whom had Caesar put in charge of the siege work?","At what time was the wall broken?"],"answers":["One of the centurions","Publius","About the third watch"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-13-5","title":"Lesson LXXV: Vocabulary Review; the Dative of Purpose","steps":["Review the word lists in §§ 521–522","Learn the dative of purpose, often with a second dative (praesidiō castrīs)","Learn the idioms; translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LXXV"}],"pages":"pp. 185–186","questions":["Translate: Duās legiōnēs praesidiō castrīs relīquit.","Write in Latin: to choose a place for a camp","Translate: Hoc erat magnō impedīmentō Gallīs."],"answers":["He left two legions as a guard for the camp","Locum castrīs dēligere","This was a great hindrance to the Gauls"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-13-6","title":"Reading LXXV: Civil War between Caesar and Pompey; the Battle of Pharsalia","steps":["Translate the selection","Read the notes on the Rubicon and on Labienus","Find the dative of purpose (praesidiō castrīs) and narrate the battle"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LXXV"}],"pages":"pp. 222–224","questions":["Which river did Caesar cross to begin the civil war?","How large was Pompey''s army compared with Caesar''s?","Which line did Caesar send in to win the battle?"],"answers":["The Rubicon","Twice as large","His third line, kept fresh until then"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-13-7","title":"Lesson LXXVI: Vocabulary Review; Genitive and Ablative of Description","steps":["Review the word lists in §§ 524–525","Learn when description uses the genitive (measure) and when the ablative (physical traits)","Learn the idioms; translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LXXVI"}],"pages":"pp. 186–189","questions":["Translate: fossa duodecim pedum","Write in Latin: a man with big feet and a small head","Translate: Helvētiīs in animō est."],"answers":["A ditch of twelve feet","Homō magnīs pedibus et parvō capite","The Helvetii intend"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-13-8","title":"Reading LXXVI: The Triumph of Caesar","steps":["Translate the end of the story of Publius","Read the notes on the Sacred Way, the toga picta, and the lictors","Describe Caesar''s triumphal procession in order"],"links":[{"label":"Read selection LXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#reading_LXXVI"}],"pages":"pp. 224–225","questions":["What office was Caesar given?","How many horses drew his chariot?","Where did Publius go after the triumph?"],"answers":["Dictator","Four white horses","Home to the villa to greet his father and mother"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-13-9","title":"Lesson LXXVII: Review of Agreement and of the Genitive, Dative, and Accusative","steps":["Name the four kinds of agreement (§ 448)","List the uses of the genitive, dative, and accusative with an example of each","Translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LXXVII"}],"pages":"pp. 189–191","questions":["Name three uses of the accusative.","Translate: Tōtīus Galliae Helvētiī plūrimum valuērunt.","Write in Latin: We marched for three hours."],"answers":["Any three: direct object, predicate accusative, subject of the infinitive, object of a preposition, extent of time or space, place to which","The Helvetii were the most powerful of all Gaul","Trēs hōrās iter fēcimus"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-13-10","title":"Lesson LXXVIII: Review of the Ablative","steps":["Sort the ablative uses into with/by, from/by, and in/at (§ 453)","Give a Latin example of each use from earlier lessons","Translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LXXVIII"}],"pages":"pp. 191–192","questions":["Which kind of ablative is ā Caesare in Bellum ā Caesare gestum est?","Write in Latin: This road is ten miles shorter than that.","Write in Latin: At midnight the general set out."],"answers":["Personal agent","Haec via est decem mīlibus passuum brevior quam illa","Mediā nocte imperātor profectus est"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-13-11","title":"Lesson LXXIX: Review of the Gerund and Gerundive, the Infinitive, and the Subjunctive","steps":["Review the uses of the gerund, gerundive, and infinitive (§§ 455–457)","List the five uses of the subjunctive and give an example of each","Translate Exercise I and put Exercise II into Latin"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#lesson_LXXIX"}],"pages":"pp. 192–194","questions":["Name the five uses of the subjunctive in § 458.","Translate: Nēmō relinquēbātur quī arma ferre posset.","Write in Latin with ad: to choose a place for a camp"],"answers":["Purpose, result, characteristic, cum clauses, indirect questions","No one was left who could bear arms","Ad locum castrīs dēligendum"]},{"id":"latin-for-beginners-13-12","title":"Appendixes: Tables of Forms and Rules of Syntax","steps":["Use Appendix I to recite one full declension and one full conjugation without the book","Read through the Rules of Syntax in Appendix II (§ 501) and mark any you need to review","Translate one favorite passage from the reading selections again, naming every construction"],"links":[{"label":"Open Appendix I","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec460"},{"label":"Open Appendix II: Rules of Syntax","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/18251/pg18251-images.html#sec501"}],"pages":"pp. 226–264","questions":["What does the genitive singular end in for the fourth declension?","Give the terminations of the fifth-declension genitive singular.","How many declensions are there?"],"answers":["-ūs","-ēī (-eī)","Five"],"notes":"A wrap-up sitting; repeat it as needed for review."}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"“Latin for Beginners” by Benjamin L. D''Ooge (1909), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/18251. Lesson plan by Freerange."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (listing_id, version) do update set content = excluded.content;
insert into public.listings (id, seller_name, seller_kind, seller_verified, title, subjects, grade_min, grade_max, philosophies, format, price, description, includes, lessons, lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement, family_style, lesson_steps, cover, color, esa_eligible, created_at, package_id, package_version, license, attribution, outline) values ('l-lib-mcguffey-fifth-reader', 'William Holmes McGuffey', 'open', false, 'McGuffey''s Fifth Eclectic Reader', array['reading', 'writing']::text[], 5, 7, array['charlotte_mason', 'classical', 'traditional', 'faith_based']::text[], 'online', 0, 'The classic Fifth Reader: 117 selections from Irving, Longfellow, Whittier, Dickens, Shakespeare, Audubon, and many more, from ''The Village Blacksmith'' to ''William Tell'' and the passenger pigeon. Each day your child learns a few words from the lesson, reads it aloud with expression, and writes a short narration or memorizes a poem; a short course in elocution opens the year. The full text is free online.', array['123 lessons in 13 units: 6 elocution lessons plus all 117 Fifth Reader selections', 'Link to the free full text for every lesson', 'Vocabulary from each lesson''s own definitions, plus a read-aloud and narration activity each day', 'Short written narration prompts for prose and memory work for poems', 'Comprehension questions with answers', 'Author notes and parent notes on dated content']::text[], 123, 4, 30, 'low', false, '{}'::text[], '🖋️', '#9B6BD6', false, '2026-11-11T12:00:00.000Z', 'pk-lib-mcguffey-fifth-reader', 1, '{"kind":"public_domain"}'::jsonb, '“McGuffey''s Fifth Eclectic Reader, Revised Edition” by William Holmes McGuffey (1879), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/15040. Lesson plan by Freerange.', '[{"title":"Unit 1: Introduction to Elocution","lessons":["Elocution: Preliminary Remarks and Articulation","Elocution: Inflections","Elocution: Accent and Emphasis","Elocution: Modulation and Poetic Pauses","Elocution: Exercises I–III","Elocution: Exercises IV–V"]},{"title":"Unit 2: Lessons I–X","lessons":["Lesson I: The Good Reader","Lesson II: The Bluebell","Lesson III: The Gentle Hand","Lesson IV: The Grandfather","Lesson V: A Boy on a Farm","Lesson VI: The Singing Lesson","Lesson VII: Do Not Meddle","Lesson VIII: Work","Lesson IX: The Maniac","Lesson X: Robin Redbreast"]},{"title":"Unit 3: Lessons XI–XX","lessons":["Lesson XI: The Fish I Didn''t Catch","Lesson XII: It Snows","Lesson XIII: Respect for the Sabbath Rewarded","Lesson XIV: The Sands o'' Dee","Lesson XV: Select Paragraphs","Lesson XVI: The Corn Song","Lesson XVII: The Venomous Worm","Lesson XVIII: The Festal Board","Lesson XIX: How to Tell Bad News","Lesson XX: The Battle of Blenheim"]},{"title":"Unit 4: Lessons XXI–XXX","lessons":["Lesson XXI: \"I Pity Them\"","Lesson XXII: An Elegy on Madam Blaize","Lesson XXIII: King Charles II and William Penn","Lesson XXIV: What I Live For","Lesson XXV: The Righteous Never Forsaken","Lesson XXVI: Abou Ben Adhem","Lesson XXVII: Lucy Forester","Lesson XXVIII: The Reaper and the Flowers","Lesson XXIX: The Town Pump","Lesson XXX: Good Night"]},{"title":"Unit 5: Lessons XXXI–XL","lessons":["Lesson XXXI: An Old-Fashioned Girl","Lesson XXXII: My Mother''s Hands","Lesson XXXIII: The Discontented Pendulum","Lesson XXXIV: The Death of the Flowers","Lesson XXXV: The Thunderstorm","Lesson XXXVI: April Day","Lesson XXXVII: The Tea Rose","Lesson XXXVIII: The Cataract of Lodore","Lesson XXXIX: The Bobolink","Lesson XL: Robert of Lincoln"]},{"title":"Unit 6: Lessons XLI–L","lessons":["Lesson XLI: Rebellion in Massachusetts State Prison","Lesson XLII: Faithless Nelly Gray","Lesson XLIII: The Generous Russian Peasant","Lesson XLIV: Forty Years Ago","Lesson XLV: Mrs. Caudle''s Lecture","Lesson XLVI: The Village Blacksmith","Lesson XLVII: The Relief of Lucknow","Lesson XLVIII: The Snowstorm","Lesson XLIX: Behind Time","Lesson L: The Old Sampler"]},{"title":"Unit 7: Lessons LI–LX","lessons":["Lesson LI: The Goodness of God","Lesson LII: My Mother","Lesson LIII: The Hour of Prayer","Lesson LIV: The Will","Lesson LV: The Nose and the Eyes","Lesson LVI: An Iceberg","Lesson LVII: About Quail","Lesson LVIII: The Blue and the Gray","Lesson LIX: The Machinist''s Return","Lesson LX: Make Way for Liberty"]},{"title":"Unit 8: Lessons LXI–LXX","lessons":["Lesson LXI: The English Skylark","Lesson LXII: How Sleep the Brave","Lesson LXIII: The Rainbow","Lesson LXIV: Supposed Speech of John Adams","Lesson LXV: The Rising","Lesson LXVI: Control Your Temper","Lesson LXVII: William Tell (Part 1)","Lesson LXVIII: William Tell (Concluded)","Lesson LXIX: The Crazy Engineer","Lesson LXX: The Heritage"]},{"title":"Unit 9: Lessons LXXI–LXXX","lessons":["Lesson LXXI: No Excellence Without Labor","Lesson LXXII: The Old House Clock","Lesson LXXIII: The Examination","Lesson LXXIV: The Isle of Long Ago","Lesson LXXV: The Boston Massacre","Lesson LXXVI: Death of the Beautiful","Lesson LXXVII: Snow Falling","Lesson LXXVIII: Squeers''s Method","Lesson LXXIX: The Gift of Empty Hands","Lesson LXXX: Capturing the Wild Horse"]},{"title":"Unit 10: Lessons LXXXI–XC","lessons":["Lesson LXXXI: Sowing and Reaping","Lesson LXXXII: Taking Comfort","Lesson LXXXIII: Calling the Roll","Lesson LXXXIV: Turtle Soup","Lesson LXXXV: The Best Kind of Revenge","Lesson LXXXVI: The Soldier of the Rhine","Lesson LXXXVII: The Winged Worshipers","Lesson LXXXVIII: The Peevish Wife","Lesson LXXXIX: The Rainy Day","Lesson XC: Break, Break, Break"]},{"title":"Unit 11: Lessons XCI–C","lessons":["Lesson XCI: Transportation and Planting of Seeds","Lesson XCII: Spring Again","Lesson XCIII: Religion the Only Basis of Society","Lesson XCIV: Rock Me to Sleep","Lesson XCV: Man and the Inferior Animals","Lesson XCVI: The Blind Men and the Elephant","Lesson XCVII: A Home Scene","Lesson XCVIII: The Light of Other Days","Lesson XCIX: A Chase in the English Channel","Lesson C: Burial of Sir John Moore"]},{"title":"Unit 12: Lessons CI–CX","lessons":["Lesson CI: Little Victories","Lesson CII: The Character of a Happy Life","Lesson CIII: The Art of Discouragement","Lesson CIV: The Mariner''s Dream","Lesson CV: The Passenger Pigeon","Lesson CVI: The Country Life","Lesson CVII: The Virginians","Lesson CVIII: Minot''s Ledge","Lesson CIX: Hamlet","Lesson CX: Dissertation on Roast Pig"]},{"title":"Unit 13: Lessons CXI–CXVII","lessons":["Lesson CXI: A Pen Picture","Lesson CXII: The Great Voices","Lesson CXIII: A Picture of Human Life","Lesson CXIV: A Summer Longing","Lesson CXV: Fate","Lesson CXVI: The Bible the Best of Classics","Lesson CXVII: My Mother''s Bible"]}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set seller_name = excluded.seller_name, seller_kind = excluded.seller_kind, seller_verified = excluded.seller_verified, title = excluded.title, subjects = excluded.subjects, grade_min = excluded.grade_min, grade_max = excluded.grade_max, philosophies = excluded.philosophies, format = excluded.format, price = excluded.price, description = excluded.description, includes = excluded.includes, lessons = excluded.lessons, lessons_per_week = excluded.lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson = excluded.minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement = excluded.parent_involvement, family_style = excluded.family_style, lesson_steps = excluded.lesson_steps, cover = excluded.cover, color = excluded.color, esa_eligible = excluded.esa_eligible, package_id = excluded.package_id, package_version = excluded.package_version, license = excluded.license, attribution = excluded.attribution, outline = excluded.outline;
insert into public.package_releases (listing_id, version, content) values ('l-lib-mcguffey-fifth-reader', 1, '{"units":[{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-u1","title":"Unit 1: Introduction to Elocution","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-1-1","title":"Elocution: Preliminary Remarks and Articulation","steps":["Read the Preliminary Remarks together and put Rule 1 in your own words","Say the vocals, subvocals, and aspirates in the tables aloud; feel the difference between cognate pairs like b/p and v/f","Read the ''Faults to Be Remedied'' examples, saying the incorrect form and then the correct one","Practice five of the Exercises in Articulation sentences until each is crisp and clear"],"links":[{"label":"Preliminary Remarks","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00025"},{"label":"Articulation","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00030"},{"label":"Exercises in Articulation","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00080"}],"questions":["What is the great object of reading aloud, according to the book?","What is the difference between a subvocal and an aspirate?","Name one common fault in articulation the book warns about."],"answers":["To give the hearer the writer''s ideas and feelings fully and clearly","A subvocal uses the voice with the breath partly blocked; an aspirate is breath only","Dropping unaccented vowels (sep''rate for separate), dropping final sounds (an'' for and), or running words together"],"notes":"The Fifth Reader opens with a short course in elocution. Treat these lessons as voice practice, not memorization; come back to them as warm-ups all year."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-1-2","title":"Elocution: Inflections","steps":["Read the definitions of rising and falling inflection and practice ''Did you walk or ride?'' several ways","Work through the rules for the falling inflection (complete sense, strong emphasis, commencing and concluding series)","Practice the rising inflection on yes-or-no questions and on direct address","Try the circumflex and monotone examples and talk about when each would be used"],"links":[{"label":"Inflections","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00083"}],"questions":["Which inflection usually goes on a question that can be answered yes or no?","Which inflection is usual when the sense of a sentence is complete?","What is a monotone, and when does the book say to use it?"],"answers":["The rising inflection","The falling inflection","Reading on one unchanging note; for solemn or awe-filled passages"],"notes":"Long section; it is fine to split it over two days. The book marks inflections with accent marks that may look like plain apostrophes in the online text."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-1-3","title":"Elocution: Accent and Emphasis","steps":["Read about accent and say the word pairs whose meaning changes with the accent (like CON-duct and con-DUCT)","Read about emphasis; say one sentence several times, stressing a different word each time, and talk about how the meaning shifts","Practice the absolute and relative emphasis examples and the emphatic pause"],"links":[{"label":"Accent","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00216"},{"label":"Emphasis","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00227"}],"questions":["What is the difference between accent and emphasis?","What is ''relative emphasis''?"],"answers":["Accent is stress on a syllable within a word; emphasis is stress on a word or phrase within a sentence","Emphasis that brings out a contrast between two words or ideas"]},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-1-4","title":"Elocution: Modulation and Poetic Pauses","steps":["Read about pitch and compass; find your own comfortable keynote and read a paragraph on it","Practice the voice exercise from Remark 5: the same lines at low, middle, and high pitch, without getting louder","Read about quantity and quality, then try the examples in a soft, a full, and a harsh tone","Read about the final and caesural pauses and read the Marmion lines both ways"],"links":[{"label":"Modulation","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00270"},{"label":"Poetic Pauses","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00302"}],"questions":["What is the ''compass'' of the voice?","What two faults of pitch does the book warn about?","What is a caesural pause?"],"answers":["The range of the voice above and below its keynote","Monotony (staying on one note) and changing pitch or force without regard to the sense, like singsong","A pause in the middle of a line of poetry"]},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-1-5","title":"Elocution: Exercises I–III","steps":["Read ''Death of Franklin'' in a solemn tone, watching the marked inflections","Read ''Bonaparte'' aloud, giving the falling inflection to each sharp contrast","Read Hamlet''s lines on Yorick slowly and thoughtfully","Choose one of the three to read to the family with full expression"],"links":[{"label":"Exercises","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00312"},{"label":"Bonaparte","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00316"},{"label":"Hamlet on Yorick","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00319"}],"questions":["Why does the Franklin passage say nations should mourn him?","Which writer''s character is ''Hamlet on Seeing the Skull of Yorick'' taken from?"],"answers":["He was a benefactor of humanity, not merely a great man in rank","Shakespeare''s Hamlet"],"notes":"Short practice pieces with inflection marks. The Bonaparte passage is a harsh political portrait of Napoleon."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-1-6","title":"Elocution: Exercises IV–V","steps":["Read the battle lines from Scott''s Marmion with energy, letting the pace quicken","Read ''Lord Ullin''s Daughter'' aloud, marking your own inflections as the book suggests","Narrate the story of the chieftain, the lady, and the boatman"],"links":[{"label":"Description of a Battle","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00321"},{"label":"Lord Ullin''s Daughter","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00323"}],"questions":["Why are the chieftain and Lord Ullin''s daughter fleeing?","What happens to them on the lake?","How does Lord Ullin feel at the end?"],"answers":["Her father, Lord Ullin, and his men are chasing them","The boat is swamped by the storm and they drown","Full of grief and regret; he would forgive them if they came back"],"notes":"Poems by Sir Walter Scott and Thomas Campbell. A sad ending: the lovers drown."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-u2","title":"Unit 2: Lessons I–X","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-2-1","title":"Lesson I: The Good Reader","steps":["New words: petition (a formal request), articulate (to utter sounds clearly), monotony (lack of variety), concise (brief and full of meaning), dictate (to speak so another can write it down)","Read the story aloud, showing the difference between the two pages'' bad reading and Ernestine''s good reading","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell what went wrong with each page''s reading and why Ernestine succeeded"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson I","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00343"}],"questions":["What was wrong with the first page''s reading?","What was the widow''s petition about?","What reward did the King give Ernestine?"],"answers":["He ran his words together in a fast, dull monotone","Her delicate son had been drafted into the army, though he wanted to be a portrait painter","She carried the order for the son''s discharge; later the King paid for her education and made her father chief gardener"],"notes":"Frederick the Great ruled Prussia from 1740 to 1786. This story sets the theme of the whole book: read to make your listener understand and feel."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-2-2","title":"Lesson II: The Bluebell","steps":["New words: rift (a narrow opening), chalice (a cup), bonny (beautiful)","Read the poem aloud gently, letting each three-line stanza flow","Memorize: the last stanza, ''The patient child whose watchful eye...''","Talk about the lesson the poet draws from the flower"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson II","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00359"}],"questions":["What color was the flower at first?","What did the flower watch every day and night?","What lesson does the poem give children?"],"answers":["White as snow","The narrow strip of sky and one bright star","A child who keeps looking toward pure and high things will grow like them"]},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-2-3","title":"Lesson III: The Gentle Hand","steps":["New words: vicinity (neighborhood), potent (powerful), peremptory (commanding), ally (one who joins with another), crotchet (a whim)","Read the story aloud, using a rough voice for the farmer and a soft one for the girl","Written narration: in a paragraph, describe three times the girl''s gentle hand changed things"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson III","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00371"}],"questions":["How did the girl quiet the big dog Tiger?","Why wouldn''t the pony Dick move, and what finally made him go?","What did the farmer say was the secret of his daughter''s power?"],"answers":["She laid her hand on his neck and spoke a low word","He was stubborn; whipping and jerking failed, but the girl stroked him and spoke kindly","She''s good; everybody and everything loves her"],"notes":"By T. S. Arthur, a popular 19th-century writer of moral tales."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-2-4","title":"Lesson IV: The Grandfather","steps":["New words: hale (healthy), manteltree (shelf over a fireplace), plodded (went slowly)","Read the poem aloud quietly, as a peaceful summer afternoon","Copywork: \"Fast asleep were they both on that summer day.\"","Narrate: why did the grandfather really cry?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson IV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00404"}],"questions":["What did the little girl think made her grandfather cry?","What was the real reason for his tear?","What was the grandmother doing?"],"answers":["His pipe smoke","He remembered her dead mother sitting in the same place","Clearing away dinner and then spinning at the wheel"],"notes":"Poem by Charles G. Eastman. Gently mentions a mother''s death."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-2-5","title":"Lesson V: A Boy on a Farm","steps":["New words: factotum (one who does all kinds of work), indispensable (absolutely necessary), economize (to save), dispatch (haste), chores (light household work)","Read the selection aloud, enjoying its humor","Written narration: in a paragraph, list the farm boy''s jobs and tell which you would like least","List your own chores and see how they compare"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson V","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00411"}],"questions":["Why does the boy turn cartwheels along the road, according to the author?","Why does the boy take so long getting water from the spring?","What does the author think chores do for a boy?"],"answers":["He''s ''inventing a new way to travel'' to save his legs","He stops to poke frogs and play with the water","They give him a real education and make him useful as a man"],"notes":"By Charles Dudley Warner, from ''Being a Boy.'' Humor and gentle exaggeration; a few period jobs (penstock, grindstone, stoning raisins) may need explaining."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-2-6","title":"Lesson VI: The Singing Lesson","steps":["New words: sauntering (strolling), snicker (a half-hidden laugh), crest (a tuft on a bird''s head), moral (the lesson a story teaches)","Read the poem aloud with expression, giving the dove a coaxing voice","Memorize: the first stanza, ''A nightingale made a mistake...''","Discuss the moral the poem asks you to find"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson VI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00423"}],"questions":["Why did the nightingale hide?","Who cheered her up?","When she sang again, where did she fix her eyes?"],"answers":["She sang a few notes out of tune and was ashamed","A dove","On the sky, not on the people below"],"notes":"Poem by Jean Ingelow. Nightingales live in Europe, not America."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-2-7","title":"Lesson VII: Do Not Meddle","steps":["New words: aversion (dislike), consternation (dismay), principle (a right rule of conduct), integrity (honesty), legacy (a gift left by will)","Read the story aloud, pausing after each boy to guess what trap comes next","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell what happened to each of the seven boys","Discuss the old gentleman''s saying about peeping into drawers"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson VII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00433"}],"questions":["What trap caught Henry Wilkins?","What happened when Rufus Wilson touched the drawer knob?","Why did Harry Gordon get the job?"],"answers":["Fake cherries filled with cayenne pepper","A hidden bell rang loudly","He sat still for twenty minutes and touched nothing"],"notes":"A pound was about $4.86 at the time. The ''electrical machine'' trap is a 19th-century gadget; don''t try it at home."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-2-8","title":"Lesson VIII: Work","steps":["New words: mesh (network), rife (abounding), stagnant (inactive), torpor (laziness), lolls (lounges)","Read the poem aloud briskly and boldly, like a call to action","Memorize: the first stanza, ''Work, work, my boy, be not afraid...''"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson VIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00459"}],"questions":["What does the poet say there is in the shuttle''s song and the anvil''s stroke?","Who does the poet doubt enjoys his bread as much as a worker?","When is a man most blessed, according to the last stanza?"],"answers":["Glory and triumph","The idle man who has plenty without working","When a busy day ends in a well-earned, contented rest"],"notes":"Poem by Eliza Cook."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-2-9","title":"Lesson IX: The Maniac","steps":["New words: exterior (outward look), revenues (income from taxes), deficit (shortfall), defaulter (one who can''t account for money in his care), injunction (a command)","Read the story aloud in a low, serious voice","Narrate: how did a small arithmetic mistake ruin Conrad Lange?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson IX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00468"}],"questions":["What phrase did the old man keep repeating?","What caused the shortage in his accounts?","What lesson does the last paragraph draw?"],"answers":["\"Once one is two\"","A multiplication mistake: he figured once one is two","Our minds are easily overthrown if we rely only on this world; we should cast our cares on God"],"notes":"A sad story about mental illness, told in old-fashioned terms (''maniac''). A ducat was a gold coin worth about $2.00."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-2-10","title":"Lesson X: Robin Redbreast","steps":["Read the poem aloud, letting the refrain ''Robin, Robin Redbreast'' sing","Compare the English robin (described in the note) with the American robin you know","Copywork: \"Robin sings so sweetly / In the falling of the year.\""],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson X","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00479"}],"questions":["What season is the poem about?","What does the poet say the trees are like, and what will they turn into?","What does the last stanza ask us to give Robin?"],"answers":["Late autumn, as summer ends","Indian princes in bright colors who will soon turn to ghosts","A crumb of bread to cheer him"],"notes":"Poem by William Allingham. The European robin is a different, smaller bird than the American robin."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-u3","title":"Unit 3: Lessons XI–XX","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-3-1","title":"Lesson XI: The Fish I Didn''t Catch","steps":["New words: genial (cheerful), haunts (favorite places), propriety (proper behavior), anticipate (to take before the right time), achievement (a deed done)","Read the story aloud, with the uncle''s calm voice and the boy''s excitement","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell the story and explain the uncle''s proverb in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00485"}],"questions":["How did the boy lose his first fish?","What advice did his uncle give him?","How does the author say a child''s sorrow differs from a grown-up''s?"],"answers":["He shouted to his uncle before landing it, and it slipped off the hook into the stream","Never brag of your fish before you catch him","A child''s grief is complete and unreasoning; adults hold theirs back with reason"],"notes":"By John Greenleaf Whittier, the Quaker poet, remembering his boyhood."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-3-2","title":"Lesson XII: It Snows","steps":["New words: trow (believe), imbecile (here, a feeble person), intervened (lay between), stifled (choked back)","Read the poem aloud, giving each speaker (schoolboy, invalid, traveler, belle, widow) a different voice","Narrate: how does each person feel about the snow, and why?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00497"}],"questions":["Who shouts ''Hurrah!'' at the snow?","Why is the traveler glad?","Why is the snow bitter for the widow?"],"answers":["The schoolboy","He thinks of his warm home and family waiting","She is poor, with no food for her children and her last wood burning"],"notes":"Poem by Sarah Josepha Hale, who also wrote ''Mary Had a Little Lamb.'' ''Imbecile'' here means someone weak and sickly, not an insult."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-3-3","title":"Lesson XIII: Respect for the Sabbath Rewarded","steps":["New words: genteel (fashionable), violate (to break), dialect (local speech), identity (being who one claims), extremity (greatest need)","Read the story aloud, building suspense in the stranger''s questions","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell how William Reed''s choice led to his fortune"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00506"}],"questions":["Why did the barber lose his fashionable customers?","Why did he ask the stranger for a half-penny?","Who was the stranger, and what news did he bring?"],"answers":["He stopped working on Sundays","To buy a candle, since it was too dark to shave safely","A lawyer searching for him; his uncle had died and left him a fortune"],"notes":"Set in Bath, England. A moral tale about keeping Sunday; ''half a crown'' was a British coin."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-3-4","title":"Lesson XIV: The Sands o'' Dee","steps":["New words: dank (damp), tress (a lock of hair)","Read the poem in a low, musical voice, varying the three repeated lines in each stanza as the book suggests","Memorize: the first stanza"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00515"}],"questions":["What was Mary sent to do?","What happened to her?","What do the boatmen still hear?"],"answers":["Call the cattle home across the sands","The tide and mist came in and she drowned","Her voice calling the cattle home"],"notes":"Poem by Charles Kingsley. The book calls the Dee a Scottish river; the famous Dee sands Kingsley wrote about are actually on the border of England and Wales. A sad poem about a drowning."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-3-5","title":"Lesson XV: Select Paragraphs","steps":["New words: ordained (established), dominion (rule), haven (harbor)","Read the six Bible passages aloud slowly and reverently","Memorize: Psalm 23 (paragraph 6), if your child doesn''t know it already","Find which psalm each paragraph comes from"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00523"}],"questions":["In paragraph 2, where does the psalmist say God has set man?","In paragraph 5, what do sailors do when they are at their wit''s end?","In Psalm 23, what is the Lord compared to?"],"answers":["A little lower than the angels, crowned with glory and honor","Cry to the Lord, who calms the storm","A shepherd"],"notes":"Passages from the Psalms (King James Version): 105, 8, 91, 95/96, 107, and 23."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-3-6","title":"Lesson XVI: The Corn Song","steps":["New words: hoard (a store laid up), lavish (generous), meads (meadows), vapid (dull), samp (boiled crushed corn)","Read the poem aloud with a hearty, harvest-festival spirit","Trace the corn through the seasons: April, May, June, autumn","Memorize: the first stanza"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00531"}],"questions":["Which crops does the poem say other lands may boast of?","What drove the crows from the sprouting grain?","What does the farmer thank God for at the end?"],"answers":["Apples, oranges, and grapes","The farmers frightened them away","The golden corn"],"notes":"Poem by John Greenleaf Whittier, from his ''Songs of Labor.''"},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-3-7","title":"Lesson XVII: The Venomous Worm","steps":["New words: reptiles (crawling animals), infested (troubled), delirium (a wandering mind), paroxysm (a fit), still (a vessel for making liquor)","Read the lesson aloud, keeping the riddle a secret until the last line","Narrate: what is the ''worm'' really, and why did the author describe it as a snake?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00547"}],"questions":["What is the ''worm of the still''?","Which animals avoid this worm?","What does the bitten person do as soon as he recovers?"],"answers":["The coiled pipe used to distill liquor; it stands for strong drink","All animals; only people are ''bitten''","Goes back to be bitten again"],"notes":"By John Russell. A temperance piece written as a riddle. A good chance to talk about addiction in your own family''s terms."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-3-8","title":"Lesson XVIII: The Festal Board","steps":["New words: festal (joyous), enhance (increase), sundered (separated), felon (a criminal), lure (to tempt)","Read the poem aloud: lightly for the party, then slowly and sadly for what came after","Narrate: what happened to the guests after the feast?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00557"}],"questions":["What became of the beautiful young woman?","Where is the strong young man now?","What does the poet blame for all these sorrows?"],"answers":["She died of a broken heart","In prison, on a felon''s bed","Too many feasts and drinking"],"notes":"Another temperance poem; heavy themes of ruin and death. Golconda was an Indian city famous for diamonds."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-3-9","title":"Lesson XIX: How to Tell Bad News","steps":["Read the dialogue aloud with two readers, one as Mr. H. and one as the Steward","Keep the Steward''s voice matter-of-fact to bring out the joke","Retell the chain of bad news backward, from the bank failure to the magpie"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00574"}],"questions":["What was the first piece of ''bad news'' the steward gave?","How did the father''s house burn down?","Why is the steward''s way of telling the news funny?"],"answers":["The magpie was dead","From the torches at his mother''s funeral","He starts with the smallest thing and works backward to the biggest disasters"],"notes":"A comic dialogue. There are no definitions for this lesson."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-3-10","title":"Lesson XX: The Battle of Blenheim","steps":["Read the poem aloud; give Kaspar a tired voice and the children curious ones","Find Bavaria on a map","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell what Kaspar knows about the battle and what he doesn''t know","Discuss the poet''s point in the repeated ''famous victory''"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00606"}],"questions":["What did Peterkin find by the stream?","Who won the battle?","What can''t Kaspar explain?"],"answers":["A skull from the battle","The English (with Prince Eugene) beat the French","What the war was for and what good came of it"],"notes":"Poem by Robert Southey about the 1704 battle in the War of the Spanish Succession. Its point is anti-war irony; it mentions dead bodies and suffering civilians."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-u4","title":"Unit 4: Lessons XXI–XXX","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-4-1","title":"Lesson XXI: \"I Pity Them\"","steps":["New words: emigrate (to move to a new place to live), predicament (plight), predominate (to prevail), destitute (needy), destination (journey''s end)","Read the story aloud","Narrate: how did the westerner show that pity must be more than words?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00620"}],"questions":["What happened to the family''s horse?","What did the man from the West hold up?","What became of the emigrant family?"],"answers":["It fell through a rotten bridge and drowned","A ten-dollar bill, ''the amount of my pity''","They reached Illinois and he became a thriving farmer"]},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-4-2","title":"Lesson XXII: An Elegy on Madam Blaize","steps":["New words: accord (agreement), pledge (property left as security for a loan), hangers-on (followers), mortal (deadly)","Read the poem aloud, pausing at each dash before the twist","Explain the joke in two of the stanzas"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00628"}],"questions":["Who did Madam Blaize lend money to?","When did she never sleep in church?","Why is the last stanza funny?"],"answers":["To the poor, but only if they left a pledge","Except when she shut her eyes","It states the obvious: had she lived longer, she wouldn''t have died today"],"notes":"Poem by Oliver Goldsmith. A mock elegy: each stanza begins with praise and ends by undercutting it."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-4-3","title":"Lesson XXIII: King Charles II and William Penn","steps":["New words: regiment (a body of soldiers), aggressors (those who strike first), venison (deer meat), excesses (misdeeds)","Read the dialogue with two readers, Penn speaking plainly in ''thee'' and ''thou''","Written narration: in a paragraph, explain Penn''s argument against the ''right of discovery''"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00638"}],"questions":["What did Penn depend on instead of soldiers?","How did Penn plan to get land from the Indians?","What imaginary situation did Penn use to change the King''s mind?"],"answers":["The Indians'' own sense of justice","By buying it from them","Indians discovering Britain and claiming it for themselves"],"notes":"By Mason L. Weems (Parson Weems). The King''s words call Native Americans ''savages'' and ''cannibals''; Penn argues against him. Talk about that dated language and the real history of Penn''s treaties."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-4-4","title":"Lesson XXIV: What I Live For","steps":["New words: emulate (to try to equal), wake (the track left behind a ship), bard (poet), martyr (one who suffers for a principle), sage (wise man)","Read the poem aloud with warmth and conviction","Memorize: the first stanza","Talk about which line best matches what you live for"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00669"}],"questions":["Whose story does the poet live to learn?","What kind of season does the poet hope for in stanza 3?","Which line ends both the first and last stanzas?"],"answers":["Those who suffered for us: poets, patriots, martyrs, and sages","A time when people live by reason, not gold, and every wrong is righted","And the good that I can do"],"notes":"Poem by George Linnaeus Banks (not named in the book)."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-4-5","title":"Lesson XXV: The Righteous Never Forsaken","steps":["New words: fagots (bundles of sticks), dissipate (to scatter), sustenance (food), fortitude (brave endurance), proffered (offered)","Read the story aloud, softly at first, then with joy at the surprise","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell the story from the traveler''s point of view"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00675"}],"questions":["What was the only food the widow had left?","Why did she share it with the stranger?","Who was the stranger?"],"answers":["One smoked herring","She hoped others would treat her own lost son the same way","Her long-lost eldest son, home rich from the Indies"],"notes":"Quotes William Cowper''s hymn ''God Moves in a Mysterious Way.''"},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-4-6","title":"Lesson XXVI: Abou Ben Adhem","steps":["Read the poem aloud with quiet dignity","Memorize: the whole poem (it''s only 18 lines)","Narrate: why did Ben Adhem''s name lead all the rest?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00688"}],"questions":["What was the angel writing?","What did Abou ask the angel to write for him?","What did the angel show him the next night?"],"answers":["The names of those who love the Lord","Write me as one that loves his fellow men","The list of names blessed by God''s love, with his name first"],"notes":"Poem by Leigh Hunt, a classic for memorizing."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-4-7","title":"Lesson XXVII: Lucy Forester","steps":["New words: brae (a hillside), brake (a thicket), reverberating (echoing), plaid (a Scottish shawl), scour (to search swiftly)","Read the story aloud in two sittings if needed, building suspense","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell how Lucy was lost and found"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00695"}],"questions":["Where was Lucy supposed to spend the day?","Why did Aunt Isabel believe Lucy was still alive?","Who found Lucy?"],"answers":["At Ladyside, with the Mayne children","There were no signs of violence; the blood seemed put there to deceive, and a murderer would have taken the clothes","Jacob Mayne"],"notes":"By John Wilson (''Christopher North''), set in Scotland. The kidnapping is blamed on a ''gypsy'' woman; this is an old stereotype about the Romani people and is worth a short talk."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-4-8","title":"Lesson XXVIII: The Reaper and the Flowers","steps":["New words: sheaves (bundles of grain), token (keepsake), transplanted (moved and replanted)","Read the poem aloud slowly and tenderly","Memorize: the first stanza","Discuss what the Reaper and the flowers stand for"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00718"}],"questions":["Who is the Reaper?","What do the flowers stand for?","How does the mother find comfort?"],"answers":["Death","Little children who die","She knows she will find them again in heaven"],"notes":"Poem by Henry Wadsworth Longfellow, about the death of children. Read it with care if your family has had a loss."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-4-9","title":"Lesson XXIX: The Town Pump","steps":["New words: perpetuity (forever), municipality (a town), unadulterated (pure), rubicund (red-faced), replenish (to fill again)","Read the speech aloud as the Town Pump, in a booming, self-important voice","Narrate: who comes to drink at the pump, and what does it say to each?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00728"}],"questions":["Which town officers does the pump claim to be like?","What does the pump call its water?","Which partner will help the pump ''reform the age''?"],"answers":["Treasurer, overseer of the poor, fire chief, physician, constable, and town clerk","The unadulterated ale of father Adam","The cow (milk and water)"],"notes":"By Nathaniel Hawthorne, from ''Twice-Told Tales.'' A humorous temperance speech; gout was a painful disease linked with rich food and wine."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-4-10","title":"Lesson XXX: Good Night","steps":["Read the poem aloud in a hushed voice, slowing on each ''Good night''","Copywork: one stanza of your choice","At bedtime, say your own good nights to things in your yard"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00748"}],"questions":["Which birds have gone quiet?","What creatures are still awake at night?","Who ''will sleep'' at the end?"],"answers":["The jay and the robin (and the crow dreams)","The bat, the fox, and the owl","Love and innocence"],"notes":"Poem by Samuel Goodrich (''Peter Parley'')."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-u5","title":"Unit 5: Lessons XXXI–XL","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-5-1","title":"Lesson XXXI: An Old-Fashioned Girl","steps":["New words: imposing (impressive), meteor (shooting star), blasé (bored with everything), invalid (a sickly person)","Read the passage aloud, giving Madam Shaw a slow old voice and Polly a fresh young one","Written narration: in a paragraph, describe Polly''s visit to the Shaws","Discuss Madam Shaw''s idea of what childhood should be"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00755"}],"questions":["Why was Polly glad when dinner was over?","How did Polly win over Madam Shaw?","How did Madam earn her first money?"],"answers":["Tom stared at her, Madam peered at her, and Maud fussed; it was uncomfortable","She sang old songs for her","By sewing the best darn in her father''s silk stockings, for a five-dollar prize"],"notes":"From ''An Old-Fashioned Girl'' by Louisa May Alcott, author of ''Little Women.'' A good lead-in to the whole novel."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-5-2","title":"Lesson XXXII: My Mother''s Hands","steps":["Read the poem aloud with feeling","Copywork: \"Such beautiful, beautiful hands!\" and the two lines after it","Narrate: why does the writer find her mother''s plain hands beautiful?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00781"}],"questions":["Are the mother''s hands white and small?","What did the hands do while the child played?","What does the writer hope for in the last stanza?"],"answers":["No; they are aged and wrinkled","Kept working for the children","To hold her mother''s hands again in heaven"]},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-5-3","title":"Lesson XXXIII: The Discontented Pendulum","steps":["New words: instituted (began), calculating (reckoning), harangue (a long speech), illustrate (to make clear), execute (to carry out)","Read the fable aloud, giving voices to the dial plate and the pendulum","Check the minute hand''s arithmetic: how many seconds are in a day?","Narrate: how did the dial plate change the pendulum''s mind?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00786"}],"questions":["Why did the pendulum stop?","How many times does it tick in a day?","What did the farmer think when he saw the clock?"],"answers":["It was tired of thinking about how many ticks it had ahead","86,400","That his watch had gained half an hour in the night"],"notes":"By Jane Taylor (who wrote ''Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star''). The math: 60 × 60 × 24 = 86,400."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-5-4","title":"Lesson XXXIV: The Death of the Flowers","steps":["New words: wailing (mourning), sear (withered), glade (an open space in a forest), unmeet (unfitting)","Read the poem aloud slowly, in a soft autumn voice","Memorize: the first four lines","Name the flowers in the poem in the order they die"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00799"}],"questions":["What month is it in the poem?","Which flowers last into autumn?","Who does the poet remember in the last stanza?"],"answers":["November","The golden-rod, the aster, and the yellow sunflower","A beautiful young woman who died young (his sister)"],"notes":"Poem by William Cullen Bryant, written in memory of his sister."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-5-5","title":"Lesson XXXV: The Thunderstorm","steps":["New words: languor (dullness), predecessor (the one before), clamorously (noisily), defile (a narrow pass), rack (flying broken clouds)","Read the passage aloud, quietly at first, then with growing force as the storm breaks","Written narration: in a paragraph, describe a storm you remember, using two of Irving''s words"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00807"}],"questions":["What river is Dolph sailing on?","What signs showed the storm was coming?","Why did the thunder seem so loud?"],"answers":["The Hudson, through the Highlands","White clouds piling up, faint thunder, a dark ripple on the water, and birds flying to shelter","It echoed from mountain to mountain"],"notes":"By Washington Irving, from ''Bracebridge Hall.'' Find the Hudson Highlands on a map."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-5-6","title":"Lesson XXXVI: April Day","steps":["New words: garnered (stored up), studs (buds), cleaving (splitting open), amber (golden yellow)","Read the poem aloud as gently as a spring rain","Copywork: \"I could have half believed I heard / The leaves and blossoms growing.\"","Go outside after a rain and notice what has grown"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00818"}],"questions":["What sound did the poet listen to all day?","What did she half believe she heard?","What happened at the end of the rain?"],"answers":["The steady rain","The leaves and blossoms growing","Golden light broke through, then one last shower fell"],"notes":"Poem by Caroline Southey (Caroline Bowles)."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-5-7","title":"Lesson XXXVII: The Tea Rose","steps":["New words: ottoman (a backless padded seat), asylum (a place of refuge), bijou (a jewel), sentimental (overly full of feeling), yearns (longs)","Read the conversation aloud with two readers as Kate and Florence","Written narration: in a paragraph, give Florence''s reasons for giving the rose to the Stephens family"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00833"}],"questions":["Who did Kate want Florence to give the rose to?","Who did Florence give it to instead?","What does Florence say about the beautiful things God makes?"],"answers":["Mrs. Marshall, a wealthy friend with a greenhouse","Little Mary Stephens and her mother, a poor seamstress family","They are his gifts to everyone alike, rich or poor"],"notes":"By Harriet Beecher Stowe (not named in the book). Kate''s remarks about the poor show old class attitudes that the story itself argues against."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-5-8","title":"Lesson XXXVIII: The Cataract of Lodore","steps":["New words: tarn (a small mountain lake), fell (a stony hill), gills (brooks), brawling (roaring), riving (splitting)","Read the poem aloud, speeding up as the water rushes; it is great practice for crisp ''-ing'' endings","Pick your favorite run of rhyming words and say it as fast and clearly as you can","Copywork: \"Helter-skelter, / Hurry-skurry.\""],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00858"}],"questions":["Who asked the poet how the water comes down at Lodore?","Where does the stream rest before the falls?","Why does the poet call himself ''Laureate''?"],"answers":["His little boy (and then his daughters)","In its own little lake","He was the official poet laureate of England"],"notes":"Poem by Robert Southey, about a waterfall near his home in England''s Lake District."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-5-9","title":"Lesson XXXIX: The Bobolink","steps":["New words: varlet (a rascal), voluptuary (one who lives for pleasure), gourmand (a glutton), corpulent (fat), eschew (to avoid)","Read the essay aloud, cheerful in the first half and mock-serious in the second","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell the bobolink''s ''career'' from spring meadow to dinner table","Look up a picture of a bobolink in breeding plumage"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00874"}],"questions":["When is the bobolink happiest and most in song?","What two other names does he get as he flies south?","What moral does Irving draw for ''little birds and little boys''?"],"answers":["From mid-May to mid-June, when the clover blooms","Reedbird and ricebird","Keep to refined, worthy pursuits and avoid greedy self-indulgence"],"notes":"By Washington Irving. Shooting bobolinks for food was common then; today they are protected songbirds. Pairs well with the next poem."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-5-10","title":"Lesson XL: Robert of Lincoln","steps":["Read the poem aloud, making the ''Bobolink, bobolink, spink, spank, spink'' chorus bubble","Compare Bryant''s bobolink with Irving''s from the last lesson","Memorize: the first stanza"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XL","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00889"}],"questions":["How is Robert of Lincoln dressed?","How is his wife different from him?","Why is Robert ''sober with work'' later in the summer?"],"answers":["In a black ''wedding coat'' with white shoulders and crest","She is quiet and plain brown, and sits patiently on the nest","He has six hungry nestlings to feed"],"notes":"Poem by William Cullen Bryant."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-u6","title":"Unit 6: Lessons XLI–L","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-6-1","title":"Lesson XLI: Rebellion in Massachusetts State Prison","steps":["New words: brigands (robbers), demeanor (behavior), parley (a talk with an enemy), expostulated (reasoned earnestly), intrepid (fearless)","Read the account aloud, letting the tension build toward the three minutes","Written narration: in a paragraph, compare the courage of the unarmed officer and of Major Wainright"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00899"}],"questions":["Why did the prisoners gather in the dining hall?","What did the mild officer carry into the hall?","How did Major Wainright end the rebellion without firing a shot?"],"answers":["To stop three fellow prisoners from being whipped","Only his walking stick (a rattan)","He had the marines load and aim, then gave the prisoners three minutes to leave"],"notes":"By J. T. Buckingham. Describes prison flogging and a tense armed standoff; the prisoners are described harshly."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-6-2","title":"Lesson XLII: Faithless Nelly Gray","steps":["New words: devoirs (respects), scoff (a laughingstock), uniform (both ''consistent'' and ''military dress''), blithe (merry)","Read the poem aloud and hunt for the puns: count them as you go","Explain three of the puns in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00923"}],"questions":["How did Ben Battle lose his legs?","What is the double meaning of ''laid down his arms''?","What is the pun in ''stand upon another footing''?"],"answers":["A cannonball took them off","He put down his weapons, and he lost his limbs","His situation has changed, and he literally stands on different (wooden) feet"],"notes":"Comic poem by Thomas Hood, full of puns. It ends with Ben hanging himself, told as a joke. Preview it, and you may want to skip it or discuss it with care."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-6-3","title":"Lesson XLIII: The Generous Russian Peasant","steps":["New words: extol (to praise highly), annals (records), granary (a storehouse for grain), succor (help), superfluities (more than is needed)","Read the story aloud","Find the Volga River and Simbirsk on a map","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell how Flor Silin used his full granaries"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00943"}],"questions":["What was the ''Famine Year''?","What did Flor Silin do with his grain?","What did he tell his neighbors to do with the grain they tried to repay?"],"answers":["A year of drought that ruined the harvests on the Lower Volga","Gave it to his starving neighbors and to anyone who asked","Share it with poor neighbors who still had nothing"],"notes":"By Nikolai Karamzin, a Russian writer and historian. Old books use ''corn'' for any grain."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-6-4","title":"Lesson XLIV: Forty Years Ago","steps":["Read the poem aloud slowly, as an old man talking to his friend Tom","List what has changed at the old school and what is the same","Copywork: the last four lines of any stanza"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00959"}],"questions":["Where is the old schoolmaster now?","What did the speaker cut into the elm tree?","How many of the old class are still alive?"],"answers":["Buried on the hill where they used to slide","Tom''s name and his sweetheart''s","Only the speaker and Tom"],"notes":"A nostalgic song-poem. Ask a grandparent what has changed since they were in school."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-6-5","title":"Lesson XLV: Mrs. Caudle''s Lecture","steps":["New words: aggravating (irritating), vindictive (revengeful), paltry (petty)","Read the monologue aloud in Mrs. Caudle''s voice, answering things Mr. Caudle never gets to say","Narrate: what is the whole quarrel about, and how does it grow?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00967"}],"questions":["What started Mrs. Caudle''s complaint?","What does she threaten to stop doing?","How does the lecture end?"],"answers":["Mr. Caudle grumbled that a button was off his shirt","Sewing on his buttons","She accuses him of snoring"],"notes":"By Douglas Jerrold, from ''Mrs. Caudle''s Curtain Lectures'' in Punch. A comic monologue that pokes fun at a nagging wife; an old-style joke."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-6-6","title":"Lesson XLVI: The Village Blacksmith","steps":["Read the poem aloud with a steady, hammering rhythm","Memorize: the first two stanzas","Narrate: what lesson does the poet take from the blacksmith?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00980"}],"questions":["Where does the smithy stand?","Why can the blacksmith ''look the whole world in the face''?","What makes him think of his wife in church?"],"answers":["Under a spreading chestnut tree","He owes no one anything","His daughter''s voice singing in the choir"],"notes":"Poem by Henry Wadsworth Longfellow, one of the best-loved American poems."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-6-7","title":"Lesson XLVII: The Relief of Lucknow","steps":["New words: siege (an army surrounding a place to force its surrender), slogan (a Highland war cry), pibroch (bagpipe music), sappers (soldiers who dig toward a fort), simultaneous (at the same moment)","Read the letter aloud, giving Jessie Brown''s Scottish words their sound","Find Lucknow, India, on a map","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell how Jessie heard help coming before anyone else"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id00989"}],"questions":["What did Jessie Brown hear?","Why did the others not believe her at first?","What tune did the pipers play at the banquet?"],"answers":["The Highlanders'' bagpipes and war cry","They could hear only the guns; their Lowland ears didn''t catch the pipes","Auld Lang Syne"],"notes":"From a letter to the London Times about the 1857 siege of Lucknow during the Indian Rebellion. It tells only the British side; historians doubt the Jessie Brown story, and Indians remember the uprising as a fight against British rule."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-6-8","title":"Lesson XLVIII: The Snowstorm","steps":["New words: mazy (winding), hoar (grayish white), boon (a gift), askance (sideways), dire (dreadful)","Read the blank verse aloud, pausing by sense, not at line ends","Narrate: how does the snow affect the ox, the robin, the hare, and the sheep?","Copywork: \"The cherished fields / Put on their winter robe of purest white.\""],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01004"}],"questions":["Which bird visits the house?","What does the robin do once it feels at home?","What does the poet urge shepherds to do?"],"answers":["The redbreast (robin)","Pecks at the crumbs on the table","Feed and shelter their flocks, and watch for drifting snow"],"notes":"From ''The Seasons'' by James Thomson, a Scottish poet of the 1700s."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-6-9","title":"Lesson XLIX: Behind Time","steps":["New words: collision (a crash), corps (a body of troops), remittances (money sent from a distance), ignominious (shameful), weal (well-being)","Read the essay aloud, pausing after each example","Written narration: in a paragraph, retell one of the four examples and add one of your own about being on time"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01015"}],"questions":["Why did the two trains crash?","Whose lateness, according to the essay, lost the battle of Waterloo?","Why was the condemned man hanged?"],"answers":["An engineer was behind time","Marshal Grouchy''s","The rider with his reprieve arrived five minutes too late"],"notes":"Includes a train wreck and a hanging. The Waterloo claim simplifies history."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-6-10","title":"Lesson L: The Old Sampler","steps":["New words: warder (a guard), antique (old), saffron (deep yellow), chapeau (a hat), supine (listless)","Read the poem aloud tenderly","Look at pictures of old samplers; if you sew, stitch your name and age","Narrate: what can we guess about Elizabeth''s life from the chest?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson L","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01026"}],"questions":["What did Elizabeth stitch on her sampler?","Which war does the rusty sword come from?","What sorrow did Elizabeth suffer later?"],"answers":["\"Elizabeth, Aged Nine,\" inside a wreath of berry and vine","The American Revolution (from Lexington to Yorktown)","The man who wore the plumed hat was killed by a sword thrust"],"notes":"Poem by Margaret E. Sangster. A sampler is a piece of practice needlework."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-u7","title":"Unit 7: Lessons LI–LX","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-7-1","title":"Lesson LI: The Goodness of God","steps":["New words: habitation (dwelling), refuge (shelter), cony (a small rock-dwelling animal)","Read the passages aloud reverently","Make a list of all the creatures the psalm names","Copywork: \"O Lord, how manifold are thy works!\""],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01040"}],"questions":["What does the psalm say God uses as his chariot?","Where does the stork make her home?","What does man do when the sun rises?"],"answers":["The clouds","In the fir trees","Goes out to his work until evening"],"notes":"Mostly from Psalm 104, with lines from Psalms 107 and 105."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-7-2","title":"Lesson LII: My Mother","steps":["Read the poem aloud quietly","Copywork: \"Whispering, ''God will keep my child.''\"","Narrate: how does the speaker feel about the trouble he caused his mother?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01056"}],"questions":["What did the mother whisper after praying?","What is the mother compared to now?","What does the speaker promise in the last stanza?"],"answers":["\"God will keep my child.\"","A faded autumn leaf","To share her troubles, watch beside her, and pray with her"]},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-7-3","title":"Lesson LIII: The Hour of Prayer","steps":["Read the poem aloud, pausing on the refrain ''Lift the heart, and bend the knee''","List everyone the poem calls to prayer","Memorize: the first stanza"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01060"}],"questions":["What time of day is the poem set?","Name three people far from home whom the poet calls to pray.","What one thing do the joyful and the sorrowful all see?"],"answers":["Sunset, as evening falls","The traveler, the captive, and the sailor (also the mourner)","Heaven''s first star"],"notes":"Poem by Felicia Hemans."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-7-4","title":"Lesson LIV: The Will","steps":["New words: reprobate (a person gone bad), deceased (dead), convened (gathered), chaise (a light two-wheeled carriage), profligate (a shameless spendthrift)","Read the play aloud with four readers (or one reader doing voices), letting the Squire pause slowly with his spectacles","Narrate: how did the two greedy cousins change as the will was read?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01065"}],"questions":["Why did Swipes and Currie expect to inherit?","What two words in the will changed everything?","Why were the cousins released from being trustees at the end?"],"answers":["They heard the old lady had cut off her nephew Frank","\"In trust\"","The will was three years old, so Frank was already twenty-one"],"notes":"A short comic play. Legal phrases like ''will and testament'' pair words of the same meaning, as the book''s note explains."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-7-5","title":"Lesson LV: The Nose and the Eyes","steps":["New words: argued (reasoned), discerning (telling things apart), behalf (on the side of), decreed (ordered by a judge)","Read the poem aloud as a pompous courtroom speech","Narrate: what were the lawyer''s arguments for the Nose?","Discuss what is silly about the judge''s decision"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01109"}],"questions":["What were the Nose and the Eyes arguing about?","Who were the lawyer and the judge?","What did the judge decide?"],"answers":["Which of them the spectacles belong to","Tongue was the lawyer and Ear was the judge","Whenever the Nose wears the spectacles, the Eyes must be shut"],"notes":"Comic poem by William Cowper, poking fun at lawyers and courts."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-7-6","title":"Lesson LVI: An Iceberg","steps":["New words: serenity (calm), exquisite (very fine), monochromist (one who paints in one color), polychroic (many-colored), obliquely (slantwise)","Read the passage aloud, building to the great roll of the iceberg","Written narration: in a paragraph, describe the ''polar bear'' piece of ice and how the waves attacked it","Float an ice cube in a glass and see how much is under water"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01120"}],"questions":["What did the small piece of floating ice look like?","What happened to the big iceberg while the travelers watched?","How much of an iceberg is above water, according to the note?"],"answers":["A huge polar bear","It cracked and rolled over in the water","About one eighth"],"notes":"By Louis Legrand Noble, from ''After Icebergs with a Painter'' (a voyage with the painter Frederic Church). Scientists today usually say about one tenth of an iceberg shows above water."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-7-7","title":"Lesson LVII: About Quail","steps":["New words: aquatic (living in or near water), cupidity (greed), tussock (a tuft of grass), cradler (a reaper with a grain cradle), devious (winding)","Read the essay aloud warmly","Written narration: in a paragraph, describe how a mother quail protects her chicks","Listen online to a bobwhite quail''s call"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01131"}],"questions":["How is the quail different from the birds that migrate?","What does the mother quail do when someone startles her family?","What does the male quail call?"],"answers":["He stays home all year, near the farm where he was born","Pretends to be hurt and leads the intruder away, then flies back to her chicks","\"Bob White!\""],"notes":"By William Post Hawes, a sportsman writer. Boreas was the Greek north wind."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-7-8","title":"Lesson LVIII: The Blue and the Gray","steps":["Read the poem aloud slowly, varying the last lines of each stanza","Learn what ''the Blue'' and ''the Gray'' mean","Memorize: the last stanza","Narrate: what generous deed does the poem honor?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01144"}],"questions":["Who are ''the Blue'' and ''the Gray''?","What did the mourners bring to the graves?","What does the poem hope for in the last stanza?"],"answers":["Union and Confederate soldiers of the Civil War","Flowers, for friend and foe alike","That anger is banished forever and both sides are mourned with love"],"notes":"By Francis Miles Finch (1867), honoring women in Columbus, Mississippi, who decorated both Union and Confederate graves. A good place to talk about the Civil War and Memorial Day."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-7-9","title":"Lesson LIX: The Machinist''s Return","steps":["New words: maneuvers (movements), portmanteau (a traveling bag), confidant (one you tell secrets to), mortgaged (pledged for a debt), pantomime (acting without words)","Read the story aloud, enjoying the nervous traveler","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell the machinist''s story in his own voice"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01153"}],"questions":["Why was the stranger so excited?","Who believed in his invention when no one else did?","How did his wife support the family while he was away?"],"answers":["He was coming home rich after three years in Europe, earlier than expected","His wife","By sewing shirts for thirteen cents apiece"],"notes":"From a newspaper letter. £50,000 was a huge fortune then."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-7-10","title":"Lesson LX: Make Way for Liberty","steps":["New words: phalanx (soldiers in close ranks), rampart (a defensive wall), impregnable (unbreakable), annihilates (destroys), breach (a gap)","Read the poem aloud with rising force, shouting the refrain","Narrate: how did Arnold Winkelried open the Austrian line?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01171"}],"questions":["What does the poem compare the Austrian army to?","What did Winkelried do?","Where and when did this battle happen?"],"answers":["A living wall and a human wood","Gathered the enemy spears into his own body, opening a gap for his comrades","At Sempach, Switzerland, in 1386"],"notes":"Poem by James Montgomery about a Swiss hero. Includes a soldier''s death in battle."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-u8","title":"Unit 8: Lessons LXI–LXX","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-8-1","title":"Lesson LXI: The English Skylark","steps":["New words: ecstasy (great joy), axiom (a self-evident truth), gyrations (circling motions), undulating (rising and falling like waves), hamlet (a small village)","Read the passage aloud lightly, letting the sentences soar","Copywork: \"It mounts up like a human aspiration.\"","Listen to a recording of a skylark"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01186"}],"questions":["What does the skylark look like on the ground?","How is its flight different from an eagle''s?","How far away can its song be heard?"],"answers":["Plain, shy, and silent","It rises straight up, not in circles","Nearly a mile"],"notes":"By Elihu Burritt, ''the learned blacksmith,'' who taught himself many languages."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-8-2","title":"Lesson LXII: How Sleep the Brave","steps":["Read the short poem aloud slowly and reverently","Memorize: the whole poem (12 lines)","Talk about who the ''brave'' are and how they are honored"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01195"}],"questions":["Who are ''the brave'' in the poem?","Who comes as a gray pilgrim to bless their graves?","Who will dwell there like a weeping hermit?"],"answers":["Soldiers who died for their country","Honor","Freedom"],"notes":"Ode by William Collins (1746), a fine poem for Memorial Day or Veterans Day."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-8-3","title":"Lesson LXIII: The Rainbow","steps":["Read the poem aloud thoughtfully","Copywork: the last stanza","Narrate: what does the rainbow teach about joy and grief?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01199"}],"questions":["Where was the storm an hour ago?","According to the poem, when will grief become joy?"],"answers":["Right where the poet was (now the rainbow is there)","When the ''holiest ray,'' the light of heaven, falls on it"],"notes":"Poem by John Keble, from a book of religious verse."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-8-4","title":"Lesson LXIV: Supposed Speech of John Adams","steps":["New words: reconciliation (making peace again), colleague (a fellow officer), proscribed (outlawed), predestined (decided beforehand), clemency (mercy)","Read the speech aloud as an orator, building to ''Independence forever''","Written narration: in a paragraph, give three of Adams''s reasons for declaring independence now","Memorize: the opening sentence, ''Sink or swim, live or die...''"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01204"}],"questions":["Who actually wrote this speech?","Why does Adams say Hancock and Samuel Adams are already outlaws?","How does he predict future Americans will celebrate the day?"],"answers":["Daniel Webster, imagining what Adams might have said","The British had excluded them from any pardon","With thanksgiving, festivity, bonfires, and illuminations"],"notes":"Daniel Webster wrote this in 1826 as part of a eulogy for Adams and Jefferson; it isn''t Adams''s real speech. Read it near the Fourth of July."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-8-5","title":"Lesson LXV: The Rising","steps":["New words: boreal (northern), yeoman (a farmer who owns land), manor (an estate), theme (a subject), guise (appearance)","Read the poem aloud with growing energy, ringing out the ''WAR! WAR! WAR!''","Narrate: what happened in the church at Berkley Manor?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01220"}],"questions":["What news came ''out of the North''?","What did the women do to help?","What did the pastor reveal under his gown?"],"answers":["The fighting at Lexington and Concord","Poured lead into molds to make bullets","A soldier''s uniform; he called for volunteers and a hundred men enlisted"],"notes":"From ''The Wagoner of the Alleghanies'' by Thomas Buchanan Read, about Pastor Peter Muhlenberg, who became a Revolutionary general."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-8-6","title":"Lesson LXVI: Control Your Temper","steps":["New words: culture (training), disciplined (trained), deference (respect), reprimanded (scolded), superannuated (weakened by age)","Read the lesson aloud","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell the two stories about Roger Sherman and what they show","Talk about one thing that tries your temper and how you could answer it calmly"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01237"}],"questions":["What did Roger Sherman do when a student flashed sunlight in his eyes?","Why did Sherman''s mother box his ear?","Who was Roger Sherman?"],"answers":["He quietly closed the window blind","Because he tapped his own child''s ear during family prayers","A shoemaker who became a judge, congressman, and signer of the Declaration of Independence"],"notes":"By Dr. John Todd. Mentions caring for ''deranged people'' (an old term for mental illness)."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-8-7","title":"Lesson LXVII: William Tell (Part 1)","steps":["New words: comeliness (gracefulness), avalanche (a mass of snow sliding down a mountain), fledgling (a young bird), caitiff (a mean villain), thralldom (slavery)","Read the scene aloud as a play, with readers for Tell, Gesler, Sarnem, and Albert","Narrate: how did Gesler find out that Albert was Tell''s son, and what cruel test did he set?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01249"}],"questions":["Why did Albert pretend not to know his father?","What does Tell say vengeance cannot take away?","What does Gesler order Tell to do?"],"answers":["To keep from sharing his father''s death","His honor, good name, and virtue","Shoot an apple off his son''s head from a hundred paces"],"notes":"From the play ''William Tell'' by James Sheridan Knowles. Tell is a Swiss legend set in 1307. The next lesson finishes the story."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-8-8","title":"Lesson LXVIII: William Tell (Concluded)","steps":["New words: accords (grants), issue (outcome), stanch (sound and true), shaft (the stem of an arrow), quiver (a case for arrows)","Read the scene aloud as a play, slowly building to the shot","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell how Tell prepared for the shot and why he hid the second arrow"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01398"}],"questions":["Why did Tell want the sun at his back?","Why did he break and throw away the arrows the soldier gave him?","What was the hidden arrow for?"],"answers":["So it would shine on the target, not in his eyes","They were bent, jagged, or too heavy for such an aim","To kill Gesler if he had killed his son"],"notes":"The book''s note tells the rest of the legend: Tell escaped and later shot Gesler. Ending a tyrant''s life is presented as heroic; worth a short talk."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-8-9","title":"Lesson LXIX: The Crazy Engineer","steps":["New words: competent (qualified), juncture (a critical moment), remonstrate (to protest), apprehend (to fear), tender (the fuel car behind a locomotive)","Read the story aloud, letting the speed and danger grow","Find Danzig (Gdańsk) and the Vistula River on a map","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell how the train was saved"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01469"}],"questions":["Why did the conductor need a new engineer?","Who was Martin Kroller really?","How was Kroller stopped?"],"answers":["His engineer had been badly hurt","A madman escaped from the hospital at Stettin, obsessed with an engine to reach the moon","A German student knocked the pistol from his hand and struck him with a stick of wood"],"notes":"An exciting story set in old Prussia (now Poland). Uses dated words like ''crazy'' and ''maniac'' for mental illness, and includes a pistol and a near-crash."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-8-10","title":"Lesson LXX: The Heritage","steps":["New words: heritage (what is inherited), sated (overfull), hinds (farm workers), adjudged (decided), benign (wholesome)","Read the poem aloud, contrasting the rich man''s son and the poor man''s son","Memorize: the fourth stanza, ''What doth the poor man''s son inherit?''","Narrate: what does the poet say both sons inherit in the end?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01504"}],"questions":["Name two cares the rich man''s son inherits.","What does the poor man''s son inherit?","What does the poet advise the rich man''s son to do?"],"answers":["The bank may break, the factory may burn, his shares may fail; he has soft hands that can''t earn a living","Strong muscles, a hardy spirit, contentment in work, patience, and courage","Do large charity, the best crop from his lands"],"notes":"Poem by James Russell Lowell. ''To hold in fee'' means to own as an inheritance."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-u9","title":"Unit 9: Lessons LXXI–LXXX","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-9-1","title":"Lesson LXXI: No Excellence Without Labor","steps":["New words: architects (builders), mediocrity (ordinary ability), absolve (to release), fiat (a decree), prowess (bravery)","Read the essay aloud like a speech","Written narration: in a paragraph, explain Wirt''s comparison of the genius and the plodder, and say whether you agree"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01517"}],"questions":["Who does Wirt say must do most of a person''s education?","What often happens to the lazy genius and the hardworking ordinary student?","What bird and mountain does he use for true genius?"],"answers":["The person himself","The genius sinks into poverty while the plodder rises to success","The condor soaring from Chimborazo"],"notes":"By William Wirt, U.S. Attorney General. Find Chimborazo in Ecuador on a map. Quotes Shakespeare''s ''Henry IV, Part 1.''"},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-9-2","title":"Lesson LXXII: The Old House Clock","steps":["Read the poem aloud, ticking out the rhythm of the refrain","Compare the three messages the clock gives: at night, at dawn, and at the end of life","Copywork: the ''Tick! tick!'' lines from the first stanza"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01530"}],"questions":["What does the clock warn at ten o''clock?","What does it say at five in the morning?","What ''morning'' does the last stanza mean?"],"answers":["Go to bed, or you''ll never rise early","Get up, for no one has health or wealth without rising early","The heavenly morning after death"]},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-9-3","title":"Lesson LXXIII: The Examination","steps":["New words: tempered (hardened), compact (dense), porous (full of tiny holes), evaporated (passed off as vapor), apparatus (equipment for experiments)","Read the scene aloud, giving Bunker a shrewd country voice","Try Locke''s demonstration: put a heavy object in a cup of water and watch the water rise","Narrate: how did Locke teach the boy why smoke rises?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01534"}],"questions":["Why does breath disappear faster from a good knife, according to Bunker?","What does Bunker say is half the battle for a teacher?","Why does smoke go up the chimney?"],"answers":["Its steel is more compact, with smaller pores","Being able to teach others to think","Heavier cold air settles and pushes the warm air (with the smoke) upward"],"notes":"From ''Locke Amsden, the Schoolmaster'' by Daniel Pierce Thompson. Bunker''s theories about the knife and frozen paths are clever but not modern science; the lesson on teaching is the point."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-9-4","title":"Lesson LXXIV: The Isle of Long Ago","steps":["New words: realm (region), rhythm (a flowing beat), surge (a great swell of water), mirage (an illusion of things floating in the air), aye (always)","Read the poem aloud dreamily, feeling its rocking rhythm","Narrate: what ''treasures'' are buried on the Isle of Long Ago?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01575"}],"questions":["What is time compared to in the poem?","What is the Isle of Long Ago?","Name two things kept on the isle."],"answers":["A river flowing into the ocean of years","Our memories of the past and of loved ones gone","Locks of hair, unsung songs, a child''s prayer, broken rings, a harp without strings"],"notes":"Poem by Benjamin Franklin Taylor, about memory and loss."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-9-5","title":"Lesson LXXV: The Boston Massacre","steps":["New words: indiscriminately (without distinction), ensued (followed), ensign (a junior officer), brandishing (waving), warrant (an order to arrest)","Read the account aloud","Find King Street (now State Street) on a map of Boston","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell how the trouble began and how it ended"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01587"}],"questions":["What date did the Boston Massacre happen?","Who warned Captain Preston to take his men back?","How did the townspeople respond the next day?"],"answers":["March 5, 1770","Henry Knox","They stood calm and demanded justice by law; Preston and his soldiers were arrested"],"notes":"From George Bancroft''s ''History of the United States,'' which tells the story strongly from the colonists'' side. John Adams later defended the soldiers in court and most were acquitted. Crispus Attucks, killed here, was a man of African and Native American descent."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-9-6","title":"Lesson LXXVI: Death of the Beautiful","steps":["Read the short poem aloud gently","Copywork: the last stanza","Talk about the comfort the poet offers"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01611"}],"questions":["What does the poet say happens too soon to earth''s fairest flowers?","Why does she say we should dry our tears?"],"answers":["They decay","The lovely ones who die came to raise our hearts to heaven and go to call us there"],"notes":"Poem by Eliza Lee Follen, about the death of the young. Pair it with the next short poem for one sitting if you like."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-9-7","title":"Lesson LXXVII: Snow Falling","steps":["New words: wold (open country), spectral (ghostly), banished (driven away)","Read the two stanzas aloud softly","Memorize: the whole poem (8 lines)","Watch snow (or picture it) and write one sentence comparing it to something"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01616"}],"questions":["What does the snow look like on the trees?","What does the poet imagine the snowflakes are?"],"answers":["Ghostly blossoms","Flowers banished from a garden in heaven"],"notes":"Poem by John James Piatt."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-9-8","title":"Lesson LXXVIII: Squeers''s Method","steps":["New words: fustian (a coarse cotton cloth), grotesque (absurd), corporeal (bodily), temporary (for the time being), disconcerted (flustered)","Read the scene aloud, giving Squeers a blustering voice","Written narration: in a paragraph, describe Dotheboys Hall and explain what is wrong with Squeers''s ''practical mode of teaching''"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01621"}],"questions":["What did Mrs. Squeers give the boys before breakfast?","Where was the first boy of the spelling class?","What is Squeers really using his ''system'' for?"],"answers":["Brimstone and treacle, from a huge wooden spoon","Cleaning the back parlor window","To make the boys do his chores"],"notes":"From ''Nicholas Nickleby'' by Charles Dickens, which exposed cruel boarding schools in Yorkshire. Describes mistreated and disabled children."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-9-9","title":"Lesson LXXIX: The Gift of Empty Hands","steps":["New words: doomed (condemned), charmed (enchanted), weird (magical), quick (alive), mute (silent)","Read the poem aloud like a fairy tale","Narrate: why did the king value the empty, scarred hands more?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01653"}],"questions":["What did the two princes promise the king?","How did one prince get his treasures so easily?","What did the king say about true treasure?"],"answers":["To bring him fair gifts if he spared their lives","They fell or flew to him without any struggle","It is not lightly won; the scars of honest effort are worth more"],"notes":"Poem by Sarah Morgan Bryan Piatt."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-9-10","title":"Lesson LXXX: Capturing the Wild Horse","steps":["New words: diversified (varied), ruminating (chewing the cud), lariat (a rope with a noose), covert (a hiding place), mercurial (lively, changeable)","Read the account aloud, quick and lively in the chase","Draw a diagram of the ''ringing the wild horse'' plan","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell how the plan was supposed to work and how Tonish spoiled it"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01664"}],"questions":["What is ''ringing the wild horse''?","Who spoiled the plan?","What horses did the hunters catch?"],"answers":["Surrounding a herd with a big ring of riders and running them until they tire","Tonish, the excitable little Frenchman","A black mare and a cream-colored colt"],"notes":"From Washington Irving''s ''A Tour on the Prairies'' (1835), in what is now Oklahoma. The guides are called ''half-breeds,'' a term now considered offensive for people of mixed Native and European descent."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-u10","title":"Unit 10: Lessons LXXXI–XC","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-10-1","title":"Lesson LXXXI: Sowing and Reaping","steps":["Read the poem aloud earnestly","Memorize: the first stanza","Talk about what kinds of ''seed'' a person can sow besides grain"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01682"}],"questions":["When does the poem say to wait for the harvest?","Should the sower care who reaps the harvest?","What does the last stanza promise?"],"answers":["Till autumn","No; sow today even if someone else reaps it","You will reap in joy what you sowed in tears"],"notes":"Poem by Adelaide Anne Procter. Echoes Psalm 126:5."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-10-2","title":"Lesson LXXXII: Taking Comfort","steps":["New words: transient (brief), picturesque (pretty as a picture), panorama (a wide view), reverie (a daydream), malediction (a curse)","Read the sketch aloud: peaceful in the first half, then sad","Written narration: in a paragraph, contrast the autumn scene with the man lying in the dirt"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01689"}],"questions":["Where was the writer walking?","What did the little boy ask the man on the ground?","What did the man answer?"],"answers":["Along the Merrimack River near Lowell, Massachusetts","What ails you? Why do you lie there?","\"I''m taking comfort\""],"notes":"By John Greenleaf Whittier. A temperance sketch about a drunken man; a chance to talk about addiction."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-10-3","title":"Lesson LXXXIII: Calling the Roll","steps":["Read the poem aloud slowly; let a second reader call out the names and answer ''Here!''","Narrate: what happened to each soldier whose name was called?","Talk about why the poet ends with a number"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01704"}],"questions":["What happened when Cyrus Drew''s name was called?","How did Herbert Cline answer?","How many men answered ''here'' at the end?"],"answers":["No one answered; he had fallen","Two soldiers carried him in, wounded, to answer his name","Twenty out of a hundred"],"notes":"Civil War poem by N. G. Shepherd. Vivid images of battle and death."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-10-4","title":"Lesson LXXXIV: Turtle Soup","steps":["New words: aft (toward the back of a ship), unctuous (rich and fatty), binnacle (the box holding a ship''s compass), galley (a ship''s kitchen), tureen (a large soup bowl)","Read the story aloud, enjoying the narrator''s hungry daydream","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell what happened to the turtle soup and why the narrator laughed"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01710"}],"questions":["Why was the turtle saved until near the end of the voyage?","Why did the ship lurch?","What did the passengers eat for dinner instead?"],"answers":["It wouldn''t spoil, so the other animals were eaten first","The narrator was daydreaming about the soup and let the wheel slip","Cold ham"],"notes":"By Charles F. Briggs, from ''Working a Passage.'' Ships'' time: four bells here means 2 o''clock."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-10-5","title":"Lesson LXXXV: The Best Kind of Revenge","steps":["New words: scurrilous (insulting and coarse), libeler (one who prints lies about another), audacity (boldness), delinquent (an offender), stint (to cut short)","Read the story aloud, building to the surprise when Mr. Grant signs","Written narration: in a paragraph, explain what ''the best kind of revenge'' is"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01722"}],"questions":["What had the warehouseman done to the Grant Brothers?","Why did he need their signature?","What did William Grant give him besides his signature?"],"answers":["Published a pamphlet mocking them","He was bankrupt and needed all his creditors to sign his certificate to do business again","A ten-pound note for his family"],"notes":"The Grant brothers of Manchester were the models for the kind Cheeryble brothers in Dickens''s ''Nicholas Nickleby.''"},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-10-6","title":"Lesson LXXXVI: The Soldier of the Rhine","steps":["New words: legion (a division of an army), dearth (lack), ebbed (flowed away), coquetry (flirting), yore (old times)","Read the poem aloud with feeling, slowing on each line about Bingen","Find Bingen on the Rhine River in Germany on a map","Narrate: what messages did the soldier send home?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01730"}],"questions":["Where was the soldier dying?","What did he keep when his father died?","What did he dream of the night before?"],"answers":["In Algiers, far from home","His father''s sword","Walking with the girl he loved on the vine-clad hills of Bingen"],"notes":"Poem (''Bingen on the Rhine'') by Caroline Norton. A soldier''s death in battle."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-10-7","title":"Lesson LXXXVII: The Winged Worshipers","steps":["New words: penance (suffering for sin), lays (songs), choir (a group of singers), consecrated (set apart for God), trackless (without a path)","Read the poem aloud lightly","Copywork: one stanza of your choice","Narrate: what does the poet wish the birds could teach him?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01741"}],"questions":["Who are the ''winged worshipers''?","Why don''t the birds need to pray, according to the poet?","What does the poet wish he could do?"],"answers":["Two swallows that flew into church during the service","They have no sins to be forgiven","Fly up with them to soar through the light and praise God"],"notes":"Poem by Charles Sprague."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-10-8","title":"Lesson LXXXVIII: The Peevish Wife","steps":["New words: irony (saying the opposite of what you mean), derision (mocking laughter), incompatible (unable to exist together)","Read the dialogue with two readers, keeping Mr. Bolingbroke patient and Mrs. Bolingbroke cross","Narrate: how does Mrs. Bolingbroke twist everything her husband says?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01754"}],"questions":["What news was in the paper?","Why was Mrs. Bolingbroke upset that her husband told her the first marriage?","Why does she finally decide to visit Mrs. Granby?"],"answers":["Two friends had married: Mrs. Nettleby and Mr. Granby","It spoiled the pleasure of being surprised","Out of curiosity, to see if she is really Emma Cooke"],"notes":"By Maria Edgeworth. A comic sketch of a quarrelsome mood; a good chance to talk about being peevish."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-10-9","title":"Lesson LXXXIX: The Rainy Day","steps":["Read the poem aloud, gloomy in the first two stanzas and hopeful in the third","Memorize: the whole poem (15 lines)","Narrate: how does the poet compare the weather to his life?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01793"}],"questions":["What clings to the moldering wall?","What does the poet tell his sad heart in the last stanza?"],"answers":["The vine","Stop complaining; the sun still shines behind the clouds, and into each life some rain must fall"],"notes":"Poem by Henry Wadsworth Longfellow. Pairs well with the next poem for one sitting."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-10-10","title":"Lesson XC: Break, Break, Break","steps":["Read the poem aloud slowly, letting the ''Break, break, break'' sound like waves","Memorize: the whole poem (16 lines)","Talk about why the happy people in stanza 2 make the poet sadder"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XC","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01797"}],"questions":["Who is playing and singing by the sea?","What does the poet long for in the third stanza?"],"answers":["A fisherman''s boy and his sister, and a sailor lad","The touch of a vanished hand and the sound of a voice that is still"],"notes":"Poem by Alfred Tennyson, written after his dear friend Arthur Hallam died young."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-u11","title":"Unit 11: Lessons XCI–C","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-11-1","title":"Lesson XCI: Transportation and Planting of Seeds","steps":["New words: membrane (a thin tissue), propagation (reproduction), deciduous (losing leaves in fall), germinating (sprouting), disseminating (scattering)","Read the essay aloud, a section at a time","Written narration: in a paragraph, explain three ways seeds travel, according to Thoreau","Nature walk: look for pine seeds, acorn caps, or squirrel diggings"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XCI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01803"}],"questions":["How do pine seeds travel?","What do squirrels do with nuts that helps forests grow?","What bird does Thoreau say could replant all the cleared lands?"],"answers":["On a thin wing the wind carries","They bury them or drop them in open land, where some sprout","The jay"],"notes":"By Henry David Thoreau, from ''The Succession of Forest Trees.'' The American chestnut trees he gathered from were later wiped out by blight."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-11-2","title":"Lesson XCII: Spring Again","steps":["Read the poem aloud, wintry at first, then growing warm and glad","Memorize: the seventh stanza, ''Frost-locked, storm-beaten, and lonely...''","Narrate: what signs of coming spring does the poet notice on her island?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XCII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01821"}],"questions":["Where is the poet standing?","What still lies in the hollows?","What tidings does the bleak rock hear?"],"answers":["On a high point of her rocky island, looking at sea and land","Snow","\"There shall be spring again!\""],"notes":"Poem by Celia Thaxter, who grew up in a lighthouse on the Isles of Shoals off New Hampshire."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-11-3","title":"Lesson XCIII: Religion the Only Basis of Society","steps":["New words: community (society), diffusion (spreading), perpetrator (one who commits a crime), extinction (ending), atheism (disbelief in God)","Read the essay aloud as a sermon","Written narration: in a paragraph, sum up Channing''s argument and say what you think of it"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XCIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01835"}],"questions":["Why does Channing say religion is not just a private matter?","What does he say would happen to society if all belief in God were erased?","What does he compare trusting human laws alone to?"],"answers":["It supports the virtues and principles society rests on","Selfishness would rule and society would fall into ruin","Hoping torches could light the world if the sun went out"],"notes":"By William Ellery Channing, a Boston minister. A persuasive essay; older students can discuss it in light of your family''s faith."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-11-4","title":"Lesson XCIV: Rock Me to Sleep","steps":["Read the poem aloud softly, with the refrain like a lullaby","Copywork: the first two lines","Narrate: why does the speaker wish to be a child again?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XCIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01843"}],"questions":["Whom is the speaker calling to?","What is she weary of?","What does she remember about her mother''s hair?"],"answers":["Her mother, who has died","Toil and tears, and sowing for others to reap","It was brown, touched with gold, and fell over her forehead"],"notes":"By Elizabeth Akers Allen. The book notes that five different people falsely claimed to have written it."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-11-5","title":"Lesson XCV: Man and the Inferior Animals","steps":["New words: distinction (a difference), implements (tools), medicinal (healing), endowed (gifted), rectified (corrected)","Read the essay aloud","Written narration: in a paragraph, explain the three differences between people and animals given in the essay","Discuss whether anything you know about animals (tool use, learning) challenges the essay"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XCV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01851"}],"questions":["What is the first difference between man and animals?","Why does the author say animals don''t make mistakes?","How does she show that animals make no improvements?"],"answers":["Man needs tools; animals use what nature gave them","They follow instinct, which is always perfect","A swallow''s nest today is no better than one built in Noah''s ark"],"notes":"By Jane Taylor. Uses ''savage'' for early peoples. Modern science has found animals that use tools and learn, a good topic for discussion."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-11-6","title":"Lesson XCVI: The Blind Men and the Elephant","steps":["Read the poem aloud with a different voice for each blind man","Act it out: blindfold family members and let them describe an object by touch","Memorize: the first and last stanzas","Narrate: what is the poem''s lesson?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XCVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01862"}],"questions":["What did the man who felt the elephant''s side think it was like?","What did the man holding the trunk say?","Why were all six both right and wrong?"],"answers":["A wall","A snake","Each knew one part truly, but none understood the whole animal"],"notes":"Poem by John Godfrey Saxe, retelling an old fable from India."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-11-7","title":"Lesson XCVII: A Home Scene","steps":["New words: inexorable (unchangeable), wonted (usual), admonition (gentle warning), ponderous (very heavy), vague (unclear)","Read the passage aloud slowly, as a remembered evening","Written narration: in a paragraph, describe your own home on an ordinary evening, the way Mitchell does"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XCVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01872"}],"questions":["What does the boy find in the family Bible?","Who was Charlie?","What does the mother tell the boy as twilight deepens?"],"answers":["The family record of births, marriages, and deaths","His little brother, who died at four","That she cannot be with him long, and he must learn to face the world on his own"],"notes":"From ''Dream Life'' by Donald Grant Mitchell (''Ik Marvel''). Mentions a child''s death."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-11-8","title":"Lesson XCVIII: The Light of Other Days","steps":["Read the song-poem aloud gently (try listening to it sung)","Memorize: the first stanza","Narrate: what is the ''light of other days''?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XCVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01887"}],"questions":["When does memory bring back the light of other days?","What does the poet compare his lost friends to?","How does he feel, like someone in a deserted banquet hall?"],"answers":["At night, before he falls asleep","Leaves falling in winter","Alone, with all the lights and guests gone"],"notes":"Poem (''Oft in the Stilly Night'') by Thomas Moore, from his Irish songs."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-11-9","title":"Lesson XCIX: A Chase in the English Channel","steps":["New words: frigate (a warship), equipage (equipment), artifice (a clever trick), broadside (all of one side''s guns firing at once), manifest (plain to see)","Read the passage aloud with energy, shouting Griffith''s and the Pilot''s orders","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell how the Pilot saved the American ship"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XCIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01891"}],"questions":["Why were Griffith and the Pilot worried during the battle?","What danger did Merry report?","How did the danger turn into their salvation?"],"answers":["The English ship was their equal and the big ninety-gun ship was catching up","Breakers: they were running onto shoals","The Pilot steered them through the narrow channels, where the enemy couldn''t follow"],"notes":"From ''The Pilot'' by James Fenimore Cooper. The mysterious Pilot is based on John Paul Jones of the Revolutionary navy. Sea battle with men killed."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-11-10","title":"Lesson C: Burial of Sir John Moore","steps":["New words: martial (military), upbraid (to scold), reck (to care), random (without aim)","Read the poem aloud solemnly, at a slow march","Memorize: the first two stanzas","Find Corunna (A Coruña), Spain, on a map"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson C","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01920"}],"questions":["When and how was Sir John Moore buried?","What did they wrap him in?","Why did the soldiers have to hurry?"],"answers":["Quickly, at midnight, by lantern light, without drums or a coffin","His soldier''s cloak","The enemy was near and they had to leave by sea"],"notes":"Poem by Charles Wolfe about a British general killed at Corunna in 1809 fighting Napoleon''s army."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-u12","title":"Unit 12: Lessons CI–CX","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-12-1","title":"Lesson CI: Little Victories","steps":["New words: afflicted (deeply troubled), reckoned (counted on), composer (one who writes music), orchestra (a group of musicians), dejected (discouraged)","Read the story aloud, with two readers for Hugh and his mother","Written narration: in a paragraph, explain what Hugh''s mother means by ''little victories''"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01932"}],"questions":["Why was Hugh so upset?","Which two famous men did his mother tell him about?","What good things did his mother say might come from his lameness?"],"answers":["He had lost his leg and could never be a soldier or sailor or go round the world","Huber, the blind naturalist, and Beethoven, the deaf composer","He would read more, be a better scholar, be more loved, and love God more"],"notes":"By Harriet Martineau, from ''The Crofton Boys.'' Hugh has had a leg amputated; the story describes ''phantom limb'' feelings honestly and kindly."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-12-2","title":"Lesson CII: The Character of a Happy Life","steps":["Read the poem aloud slowly, one stanza at a time","Make a list of what the happy man has and what he is free from","Memorize: the last stanza, ''This man is freed from servile bands...''"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01980"}],"questions":["What is the happy man''s armor?","How does he spend a ''harmless day''?","What does the last line say he has, though he has nothing?"],"answers":["His honest thought","With a religious book or a friend","All"],"notes":"Poem by Sir Henry Wotton (around 1614). Some words are old: ''servile bands'' means the chains of a servant."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-12-3","title":"Lesson CIII: The Art of Discouragement","steps":["New words: designated (named), yam (a starchy root), impaled (stuck on a stake), dilate (to talk at length), projector (one who plans a project)","Read the essay aloud, enjoying the imaginary ''cold-water pourer'' talking to the inventor of the wheel","Written narration: in a paragraph, give the reasons Helps says people discourage others","Talk about a time you were discouraged, or encouraged, about a new idea"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id01988"}],"questions":["What does Helps''s friend call people who discourage new ideas?","Name two arguments the ''friend'' gave against the wheel.","Why is it ''safe'' to predict failure?"],"answers":["Cold-water pourers","People got along fine without it; it may be irreligious; it can''t go uphill; you''ll neglect your yams; it will ruin the burden carriers","Hardly anything succeeds at first exactly as planned"],"notes":"By Arthur Helps. Includes a mention of a man being executed for inventing flint and steel, in a joking story."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-12-4","title":"Lesson CIV: The Mariner''s Dream","steps":["New words: hammock (a hanging bed), transport (great joy), impearled (decked with pearls), ''larums (alarms), dirge (funeral music)","Read the poem aloud: soft and happy for the dream, loud and fast for the storm","Narrate: what did the sailor boy dream, and what woke him?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id02003"}],"questions":["What did the sailor boy dream about?","What woke him?","Where does the poem say he will lie?"],"answers":["Coming home to his parents and the girl he loved","Lightning, thunder, and a storm wrecking the ship","Deep in the sea, with coral growing around his bones"],"notes":"Poem by William Dimond. Ends with the boy drowning; old ideas about amber being made in the sea are poetic, not scientific."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-12-5","title":"Lesson CV: The Passenger Pigeon","steps":["New words: aerial (in the air), anon (soon), mast (nuts and acorns on the forest floor), rendezvous (a meeting place), perambulate (to walk through)","Read the account aloud","Written narration: in a paragraph, describe the flocks Audubon saw, then add what happened to the passenger pigeon later","Look up a painting of passenger pigeons by Audubon"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id02022"}],"questions":["How many flocks did Audubon count in twenty-one minutes?","What happened to the trees at the roosting place?","How far away could the noise of the roost be heard?"],"answers":["163","Many large trees and branches broke under the weight of the birds","Three miles"],"notes":"By John James Audubon. Passenger pigeons were hunted to extinction; the last one, Martha, died at the Cincinnati Zoo on September 1, 1914. Includes mass killing of birds; a powerful lesson in conservation."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-12-6","title":"Lesson CVI: The Country Life","steps":["Read the poem aloud thoughtfully","Narrate: where does the poet live now, and where would he rather be?","Copywork: \"Well days, sound nights,—oh, can there be / A life more rational and free?\"","Look up the Indian pipe plant"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id02043"}],"questions":["What does the first stanza say makes up ''the sum of living''?","Where was the poet''s cradle?","Who does the last stanza call happy?"],"answers":["Not what we want, but what we must do","Beside the sea","The man content to till his own field"],"notes":"Poem by Richard Henry Stoddard."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-12-7","title":"Lesson CVII: The Virginians","steps":["New words: patrimonial (inherited), disaffected (discontented), obsequious (too eager to please), urbanity (polished manners), epitaph (words on a tombstone)","Read the passage aloud","Talk together about how the narrator describes slavery, and what is false about it","Narrate: how were the twins George and Harry alike and different?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id02052"}],"questions":["Who were George and Harry Esmond?","How were the twins different in temper?","What did George do for his young servant that angered his mother?"],"answers":["Twin grandsons of Mr. Esmond of Castlewood, Virginia","George was quiet, studious, and gentle; Harry loved sport and fighting","He fanned the flies off the sleeping boy instead of punishing him"],"notes":"From ''The Virginians'' by W. M. Thackeray. IMPORTANT: this passage pictures slavery as mild and enslaved people as ''lazy and not unhappy,'' and uses racist terms. That is false and offensive. Preview it; you may skip it, or read it with an older child as an example of how slavery was excused and why that view was wrong."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-12-8","title":"Lesson CVIII: Minot''s Ledge","steps":["Read the poem aloud with rising tension, then quiet at the end","Find Minot''s Ledge near Boston on a map","Narrate: how did the lighthouse keeper save his son?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id02076"}],"questions":["What are the clouds compared to?","Who was the keeper praying for most?","How did he pull his son from the sea?"],"answers":["Ghostly hounds","His son Charlie, at sea","He threw a rope with grappling hooks and pulled him onto the rocks"],"notes":"Poem by Fitz-James O''Brien, who died in the Civil War. The Minot''s Ledge lighthouse was famous for being destroyed by a storm in 1851 and rebuilt."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-12-9","title":"Lesson CIX: Hamlet","steps":["New words: truant (one who skips duty), attent (attentive), cap-a-pie (head to foot), truncheon (a short staff), beaver (the face guard of a helmet)","Read the scene aloud as a play with three or four readers; use the book''s notes for old phrases","Narrate: what did Horatio and the guards see, and what will Hamlet do?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id02086"}],"questions":["Why does Hamlet joke that Horatio came for his mother''s wedding?","What did the guards see on their watch?","What does Hamlet decide to do?"],"answers":["Because his mother married again very soon after his father''s funeral","A ghost that looked like Hamlet''s dead father, in armor","Keep watch that night and speak to the ghost"],"notes":"From Shakespeare''s ''Hamlet,'' Act I, Scene 2. A good first taste of Shakespeare; the play itself is dark and better for older students."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-12-10","title":"Lesson CX: Dissertation on Roast Pig","steps":["New words: younkers (youngsters), antediluvian (very ancient), premonitory (giving warning), obnoxious (offensive), culinary (to do with cooking)","Read the essay aloud, enjoying Lamb''s mock-serious tone","Written narration: in a paragraph, retell how roast pig was ''discovered'' and why the jury said ''Not Guilty''"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id02158"}],"questions":["How did Bo-bo discover roast pig?","Why did the jury find Ho-ti and Bo-bo not guilty?","What ''discovery'' finally ended the house fires?"],"answers":["He burned down the cottage with the piglets inside, burned his fingers on one, and licked them","They tasted the pig themselves","That meat could be cooked without burning down a whole house"],"notes":"By Charles Lamb, from ''Essays of Elia.'' A tall tale, not real Chinese history. A good example of humor and exaggeration."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-u13","title":"Unit 13: Lessons CXI–CXVII","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-13-1","title":"Lesson CXI: A Pen Picture","steps":["New words: glamour (a magic charm on the eyes), loch (a lake or arm of the sea), peat (turf burned for fuel), curlew (a shore bird), plover (a shore bird)","Read the description aloud slowly, noticing every color word","Find the Isle of Lewis in the Outer Hebrides on a map","Written narration: in a paragraph, write your own ''pen picture'' of a sunset you have seen"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id02188"}],"questions":["What did Lavender imagine Sheila and her father to be?","Why did Sheila say she could walk to Borvabost alone?","Name three colors Black uses for the evening sky."],"answers":["A princess and the King of Thule","It wouldn''t get darker than the twilight all night in the north","Gold, violet, crimson, rose, pale yellow"],"notes":"From ''A Princess of Thule'' by William Black, set in the Scottish Hebrides, where summer nights are short and light."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-13-2","title":"Lesson CXII: The Great Voices","steps":["Read the poem aloud, letting the sea, torrents, and forests ''call'' to each other","Memorize: the last stanza","Talk about what nature ''says'' to the human heart in the poem"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id02210"}],"questions":["What does the voice from the sea say?","What do the torrents add?","How should the human heart sing, according to the last stanza?"],"answers":["\"O spirit! be glad and be free\"","\"O spirit! be free and be strong\"","With joy like the fountains, calm as the mountains, and deep as the woods and the sea"],"notes":"Poem by Charles T. Brooks."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-13-3","title":"Lesson CXIII: A Picture of Human Life","steps":["New words: caravansary (an inn for travelers in the East), meanders (windings), deviations (wanderings off course), palliation (excusing), labyrinth (a maze)","Read the allegory aloud","Written narration: in a paragraph, explain how each part of Obidah''s journey stands for a part of life"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id02217"}],"questions":["Why did Obidah leave the main road?","What happened when he lost his way?","What hope does the hermit give at the end?"],"answers":["The shady path through the grove seemed pleasant and looked as if it went the same way","A storm came, night fell, and wild beasts howled around him","It is never too late to turn back; a wanderer who asks God for help can still return"],"notes":"By Samuel Johnson, from ''The Rambler.'' An allegory: a story where each part stands for something else."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-13-4","title":"Lesson CXIV: A Summer Longing","steps":["Read the poem aloud with longing, stressing the refrain ''green fields wait for me''","Memorize: the first stanza","List the summer sights and sounds the poet dreams of"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id02236"}],"questions":["What refrain ends each stanza?","Name two summer sounds the poet longs to hear."],"answers":["(The) green fields wait for me (or thee)","Birds twittering, lowing herds, the distant sea, breezes in the reeds, insects chirping"],"notes":"Poem by George Arnold."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-13-5","title":"Lesson CXV: Fate","steps":["Read the short poem aloud, letting the surprise land in the last stanza","Memorize: the whole poem (12 lines)","Narrate: what is the twist?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id02243"}],"questions":["Why did the first speaker refuse to go to sea?","What happened to the ship and the hunters?","What happened to the ''safe'' town?"],"answers":["It looked stormy and dangerous","They came home safely","An earthquake swallowed it"],"notes":"Poem by Bret Harte. A good starting point for talking about trust, risk, and providence."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-13-6","title":"Lesson CXVI: The Bible the Best of Classics","steps":["New words: classic (a work of lasting excellence), authenticity (genuineness), sanction (authority), vindicated (defended), seraph (a high angel)","Read the speech aloud, noticing how many sentences begin ''If we...''","Written narration: in a paragraph, list the reasons Grimké gives for calling the Bible the best classic"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id02248"}],"questions":["Who does the author say recorded the Bible''s truths?","What blessings does he say the Bible brings on earth?"],"answers":["The patriarch and the prophet, the evangelist and the martyr","Humility, resignation, purity, order, peace, faith, hope, and charity"],"notes":"By Thomas S. Grimké of South Carolina. Notice the repeated ''If we...'' pattern, a speaker''s device called anaphora."},{"id":"mcguffey-fifth-reader-13-7","title":"Lesson CXVII: My Mother''s Bible","steps":["Read the poem aloud tenderly","Look at your own family Bible or family records, if you have them","Memorize: the last stanza","Narrate: what memories does the Bible bring back to the speaker?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15040/pg15040-images.html#id02258"}],"questions":["Who gave the speaker the Bible?","What memories does it bring back?","What does the last line say the Bible taught him?"],"answers":["His mother, as she was dying","The family gathered at the hearth after evening prayer, his father reading aloud","How to die (as well as how to live)"],"notes":"Poem by George Pope Morris. The final lesson of the Fifth Reader."}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"“McGuffey''s Fifth Eclectic Reader, Revised Edition” by William Holmes McGuffey (1879), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/15040. Lesson plan by Freerange."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (listing_id, version) do update set content = excluded.content;
insert into public.listings (id, seller_name, seller_kind, seller_verified, title, subjects, grade_min, grade_max, philosophies, format, price, description, includes, lessons, lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement, family_style, lesson_steps, cover, color, esa_eligible, created_at, package_id, package_version, license, attribution, outline) values ('l-lib-mcguffey-first-reader', 'William Holmes McGuffey', 'open', false, 'McGuffey''s First Eclectic Reader', array['phonics', 'reading']::text[], 0, 1, array['charlotte_mason', 'classical', 'traditional', 'faith_based']::text[], 'online', 0, 'The classic First Reader takes a beginning reader from “The dog ran” to real little stories about pets, picnics, and brave dogs. Each lesson starts with its new words; you practice them, read the story together, copy a line, and talk about it. The full text is free online.', array['61 lessons in 8 units covering all 63 First Reader lessons', 'Link to the free full text for every lesson', 'New words and a sound focus for each lesson', 'Copywork line and a hands-on or narration activity', 'Simple comprehension questions with answers', 'Review and script (cursive) slate lessons marked in the notes']::text[], 61, 4, 20, 'high', false, '{}'::text[], '📖', '#4C7FD9', false, '2026-11-11T12:00:00.000Z', 'pk-lib-mcguffey-first-reader', 1, '{"kind":"public_domain"}'::jsonb, '“McGuffey''s First Eclectic Reader, Revised Edition” by William Holmes McGuffey (1879), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/14640. Lesson plan by Freerange.', '[{"title":"Unit 1: Lessons I–IX","lessons":["Lessons I–II: The Dog and the Cat","Lesson III: The Man and the Pen","Lesson IV: The Fat Hen","Lesson V: Rab Has the Hat","Lesson VI: Ann Catches Rab","Lesson VII: The Black Hen","Lesson VIII: The Big Black Hat","Lesson IX: Review"]},{"title":"Unit 2: Lessons X–XVI","lessons":["Lesson X: Nell and the Eggs","Lesson XI: Jip and the Cart","Lesson XII: Kitty''s Pet Bird","Lesson XIII: Tom and His Dog","Lesson XIV: The Duck on the Pond","Lesson XV: Run, Skip, and Jump","Lesson XVI: Review: By the Pond"]},{"title":"Unit 3: Lessons XVII–XXVII","lessons":["Lessons XVII–XVIII: Slate Work","Lesson XIX: The Old Cow","Lesson XX: Riding on Prince","Lesson XXI: Fanny''s Ball","Lesson XXII: At the Pond with Tom","Lesson XXIII: Here Comes the Band","Lesson XXIV: In the Woods","Lesson XXV: Kate''s Doll","Lesson XXVI: By the Brook","Lesson XXVII: Review: What Shall We Do?"]},{"title":"Unit 4: Lessons XXVIII–XXXIII","lessons":["Lesson XXVIII: The Little Chick","Lesson XXIX: The Kite","Lesson XXX: A Boat for the Dolls","Lesson XXXI: Brave Old Ponto","Lesson XXXII: A June Day","Lesson XXXIII: Review: The Lost Ball"]},{"title":"Unit 5: Lessons XXXIV–XL","lessons":["Lesson XXXIV: The Big Flag","Lesson XXXV: The White Kitten","Lesson XXXVI: Frank''s Boat","Lesson XXXVII: The Little Boat","Lesson XXXVIII: Going to Market","Lesson XXXIX: Dear Old Grandma","Lesson XL: The Bee in the Flower"]},{"title":"Unit 6: Lessons XLI–XLVI","lessons":["Lesson XLI: Frank and James White","Lesson XLII: The Bird''s Nest","Lesson XLIII: The Lightning Train","Lesson XLIV: Playing School","Lesson XLV: Polly the Parrot","Lesson XLVI: Review: A Nice Time in the Woods"]},{"title":"Unit 7: Lessons XLVII–LV","lessons":["Lesson XLVII: Home from the Beach","Lesson XLVIII: Frank Goes Fishing","Lesson XLIX: The Rain","Lesson L: Winter Fun","Lesson LI: Fido''s Tricks","Lesson LII: The Rat in the Shed","Lesson LIII: At the Seaside","Lesson LIV: Willie''s Rabbits","Lesson LV: The Bird''s Nest in the Bush"]},{"title":"Unit 8: Lessons LVI–LXIII","lessons":["Lesson LVI: How the Nest Was Made","Lesson LVII: The Little Brown Birds","Lesson LVIII: The Lord Is Near","Lesson LIX: Mary Meets Frank","Lesson LX: The Willow Whistle","Lesson LXI: The Hen and Her Chickens","Lesson LXII: Chippy Tried","Lesson LXIII: The Last Lesson"]}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set seller_name = excluded.seller_name, seller_kind = excluded.seller_kind, seller_verified = excluded.seller_verified, title = excluded.title, subjects = excluded.subjects, grade_min = excluded.grade_min, grade_max = excluded.grade_max, philosophies = excluded.philosophies, format = excluded.format, price = excluded.price, description = excluded.description, includes = excluded.includes, lessons = excluded.lessons, lessons_per_week = excluded.lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson = excluded.minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement = excluded.parent_involvement, family_style = excluded.family_style, lesson_steps = excluded.lesson_steps, cover = excluded.cover, color = excluded.color, esa_eligible = excluded.esa_eligible, package_id = excluded.package_id, package_version = excluded.package_version, license = excluded.license, attribution = excluded.attribution, outline = excluded.outline;
insert into public.package_releases (listing_id, version, content) values ('l-lib-mcguffey-first-reader', 1, '{"units":[{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-u1","title":"Unit 1: Lessons I–IX","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-1-1","title":"Lessons I–II: The Dog and the Cat","steps":["New words: dog, the, ran, cat, mat, is, on; sound focus: short a, short o, short i; th","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Slate work: copy “The cat is on the mat.”","Sound out d-o-g and c-a-t, then change one letter to make log, hat, sat"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson I","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00041"},{"label":"Read Lesson II","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00047"}],"questions":["What did the dog do?","Where is the cat?"],"answers":["It ran","On the mat"],"notes":"Two very short opening lessons combined into one sitting."},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-1-2","title":"Lesson III: The Man and the Pen","steps":["New words: it, his, pen, hand, in, has, man; sound focus: short e; p, h","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Slate work: copy “The man has a pen.”","Hold a pen in your hand and act out the last two lines"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson III","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00054"}],"questions":["What does the man have?","Where is the pen?"],"answers":["A pen","In his hand"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-1-3","title":"Lesson IV: The Fat Hen","steps":["New words: hen, fat, rat, box, big, run, from, can; sound focus: short u; f, b, x","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Slate work: copy “The fat hen is on the box.”","Narrate: who is on the box and who ran away?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson IV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00063"}],"questions":["Where is the fat hen?","What ran from the box?"],"answers":["On the box","The big rat"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-1-4","title":"Lesson V: Rab Has the Hat","steps":["New words: Rab, Ann, hat, catch, see; sound focus: ch","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Slate work: copy “See Rab! See Ann!”","Look at the picture: why is Ann running?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson V","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00071"}],"questions":["What does Rab have?","Who is trying to catch Rab?"],"answers":["The hat","Ann"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-1-5","title":"Lesson VI: Ann Catches Rab","steps":["New words: she, pat, too, now, let, me; sound focus: sh, oo, ow","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Slate work: copy “Now Ann can pat Rab.”","Tell how the story from Lesson V ends"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson VI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00078"}],"questions":["Did Ann catch Rab?","What does Ann do to Rab now?"],"answers":["Yes","Pats him"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-1-6","title":"Lesson VII: The Black Hen","steps":["New words: Ned, eggs, black, left, fed, nest, them, get, will; sound focus: ck; w","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Slate work: copy “She is a black hen.”","Count the eggs in the picture"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson VII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00086"}],"questions":["What color is the hen?","Who fed the hen?"],"answers":["Black","Ned"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-1-7","title":"Lesson VIII: The Big Black Hat","steps":["New words: head, he, Nat, come, with; sound focus: ea as short e (head)","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Slate work: copy “Ned has it on his head.”","Play dress-up with a big hat and read the lesson in a ''big man'' voice"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson VIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00096"}],"questions":["Who has the black hat on his head?","Who is called to come and see?"],"answers":["Ned","Nat"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-1-8","title":"Lesson IX: Review","steps":["Read the old words at the top quickly, like a game","Slate work: copy “The man has a hat.”","Narrate: what is in the box, and who has the black hat?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson IX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00101"}],"questions":["Where is the big rat?","What should Nat get?"],"answers":["In the box","The eggs"],"notes":"Review lesson: the words at the top are old words to practice. Let your child read as independently as possible. Ends with slate work (script) to copy."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-u2","title":"Unit 2: Lessons X–XVI","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-2-1","title":"Lesson X: Nell and the Eggs","steps":["New words: Nell, some, pan, him, yes, do, you, have; sound focus: y as a consonant; v","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Slate work: copy “She has a pan with some eggs in it.”","Read it as a conversation, with your child as Nell"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson X","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00111"}],"questions":["What does Nell have?","Who will run to catch Rab?"],"answers":["A pan with some eggs in it","The speaker (I)"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-2-2","title":"Lesson XI: Jip and the Cart","steps":["New words: O, whip, Ben, up, still, sit, if, stand, Jip; sound focus: wh; j","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Slate work: copy “Get up, Jip!”","Look at the picture: what kind of animal is Jip?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00120"}],"questions":["What does Ann want to do?","What must she do first?"],"answers":["Get in the cart","Sit still"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-2-3","title":"Lesson XII: Kitty''s Pet Bird","steps":["New words: Kitty, nice, sweet, sing, just, hang, cage, then, song, pet, put, not; sound focus: ng; long i (nice)","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Slate work: copy “It can sing a sweet song.”","Narrate: why does Kitty hang the cage up?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00130"}],"questions":["What can Kitty''s pet do?","Why will Kitty hang the cage up?"],"answers":["Sing a sweet song","So the cat can not catch the bird"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-2-4","title":"Lesson XIII: Tom and His Dog","steps":["New words: Tom, top, Kitty''s, at, back, look, good, doll, think, spot; sound focus: short oo (look, good)","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Slate work: copy “Tom has a big top, too.”","Spin a top if you have one, or talk about the picture"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00138"}],"questions":["Where is the black spot on the dog?","Where is Tom''s top?"],"answers":["On his back","On the box with Kitty''s doll"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-2-5","title":"Lesson XIV: The Duck on the Pond","steps":["New words: sun, we, how, pond, stop, for, go, swim, her, us, hot, duck; sound focus: er (her)","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Slate work: copy “Now the duck will swim in the pond.”","Tell what the man fed, and where each animal went"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00145"}],"questions":["What will the duck do now?","Why don''t they stop at the pond?"],"answers":["Swim in the pond","It is hot"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-2-6","title":"Lesson XV: Run, Skip, and Jump","steps":["New words: John, rock, set, jump, fun, must, may, under, skip, bank, but, touch; sound focus: two-syllable word: un-der","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Slate work: copy “We must not touch the nest.”","Go outside and run, skip, and jump like the children"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00154"}],"questions":["Where is the duck''s nest?","May they touch the nest?"],"answers":["On the bank, under the rock","No, only look at it"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-2-7","title":"Lesson XVI: Review: By the Pond","steps":["Read the review in turns, one sentence each","Slate work: copy “The sun has set, and the pond is still.”","Find every name in the lesson and count them"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00161"}],"questions":["Where is Kitty''s doll?","Where does the duck have her nest?"],"answers":["On the rock","Under the rock"],"notes":"Review lesson: the words at the top are old words to practice. Let your child read as independently as possible."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-u3","title":"Unit 3: Lessons XVII–XXVII","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-3-1","title":"Lessons XVII–XVIII: Slate Work","steps":["New words: are, ink, moss, this, tub, upset, nut, did, shut, shall, lost, fox, men, met, step, into, hunt, mud; sound focus: two-syllable words: up-set, in-to","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Slate work: copy “Ben upset the tub.”","Choose three short sentences from the two slate pages and copy them neatly"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00167"},{"label":"Read Lesson XVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00171"}],"questions":["Who upset the tub?","What did Ben lose?"],"answers":["Ben","His hat"],"notes":"Lessons XVII and XVIII are script (cursive) slate pages only, so they are combined. Read the script together before copying."},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-3-2","title":"Lesson XIX: The Old Cow","steps":["New words: Kate, old, no, grass, dear, likes, be, drink, milk, cow, out, gives; sound focus: long a (Kate)","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Slate work: copy “She gives us sweet milk.”","Narrate: why won''t the cow come out of the pond?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00177"}],"questions":["Where is the old cow?","What does the cow give us?"],"answers":["In the pond","Sweet milk"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-3-3","title":"Lesson XX: Riding on Prince","steps":["New words: mamma, large, as, papa, arms, ride, far, barn, both, Prince, trot, your; sound focus: ar (far, barn, arms)","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Slate work: copy “We are both on Prince.”","Pretend to trot to the barn as you reread it"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00185"}],"questions":["What is Prince?","How far will Prince trot?"],"answers":["A horse","As far as the barn"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-3-4","title":"Lesson XXI: Fanny''s Ball","steps":["New words: of, that, toss, fall, well, Fanny, ball, wall, was, pretty, done, what; sound focus: all (ball, wall, fall)","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Slate work: copy “That was well done.”","Toss a ball back and forth, reading one line per catch"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00192"}],"questions":["What does Fanny have?","Where should Fanny toss it next?"],"answers":["A pretty ball","To the top of the wall"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-3-5","title":"Lesson XXII: At the Pond with Tom","steps":["New words: had, went, call, might, flag, near, swam, swing; sound focus: short a (had, flag, swam)","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Slate work: copy “We fed her.”","Narrate what the girl and Tom did at the pond"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00202"}],"questions":["What did Tom take to the pond?","Where do they want to go next?"],"answers":["His flag","To the swing"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-3-6","title":"Lesson XXIII: Here Comes the Band","steps":["New words: here, band, hear, horse, play, they, pass, where, front, fine, hope, comes; sound focus: hear and here sound alike","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Slate work: copy “Here comes the band!”","March around the room like the band"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00210"}],"questions":["Who is in front of the band?","What do the boys hope?"],"answers":["A large man with a big hat","That the band will stop and play for them"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-3-7","title":"Lesson XXIV: In the Woods","steps":["New words: Bess, happy, make, cart, tent, woods, little, very, bed, Robert, gone, draw; sound focus: two-syllable words: hap-py, lit-tle","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Slate work: copy “Bess and Robert are very happy.”","Build a blanket tent and read the lesson inside it"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00221"}],"questions":["What does Robert have?","What will Jip pull?"],"answers":["A big tent and a flag","The cart with Bess and her doll"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-3-8","title":"Lesson XXV: Kate''s Doll","steps":["New words: James, Mary, made, sang, my, lay, sport, spade, lap, dig, doll''s, sand, said; sound focus: said is a sight word","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Slate work: copy “Kate made the doll''s bed.”","Read the talking parts with two voices"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00228"}],"questions":["What did James want to do?","Why couldn''t Kate play?"],"answers":["Dig in the sand with his spade","She had to make her doll''s bed"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-3-9","title":"Lesson XXVI: By the Brook","steps":["New words: its, shade, brook, picks, all, by, help, stones, glad, soft; sound focus: long a (shade)","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Slate work: copy “They are all in the shade.”","Pick up some small stones and talk about what you find"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00238"}],"questions":["Where are the children playing?","What does Mary pick up?"],"answers":["In the shade by the brook","Little stones"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-3-10","title":"Lesson XXVII: Review: What Shall We Do?","steps":["Read it as a play between Fanny and John","Slate work: copy “Shall we go to the big woods?”","Plan your own picnic: what would you take?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00246"}],"questions":["What does John like to do?","Who will they ask to hang up a swing?"],"answers":["Play on the grass","Mamma"],"notes":"Review lesson: the words at the top are old words to practice. Let your child read as independently as possible."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-u4","title":"Unit 4: Lessons XXVIII–XXXIII","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-4-1","title":"Lesson XXVIII: The Little Chick","steps":["New words: peep, while, take, sleep, tuck, safe, oh, wet, feet, chick, can''t, feels, wing; sound focus: ee (peep, sleep, feet)","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Slate work: copy “Peep, peep!”","Act it out with a toy chick hiding under a ''wing''"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00252"}],"questions":["Where did the girl take the chick?","Where does the chick go to sleep?"],"answers":["Back to the hen","Under the hen''s wing"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-4-2","title":"Lesson XXIX: The Kite","steps":["New words: wind, time, there, fence, kite, high, eyes, bright, flies, why, day, shines; sound focus: long i spelled i-e, igh, y","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Slate work: copy “The sun shines bright.”","Copy the slate work, then fly a kite or draw one"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00260"}],"questions":["Why does the kite fly high?","Where was the dog?"],"answers":["There is a good wind","Under the cart"],"notes":"Ends with slate work to copy."},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-4-3","title":"Lesson XXX: A Boat for the Dolls","steps":["New words: wish, float, tie, know, rope, boat, try, shore, give, pole, don''t, push, drag, won''t, oar, funny; sound focus: oa (float, boat, oar)","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Slate work: copy “Nell let the pole fall on the tub.”","Float a small tub or bowl in water to test Kate''s plan"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00271"}],"questions":["What did they use for a boat?","What happened at the end of the slate work?"],"answers":["A little tub","Nell let the pole fall and upset the tub"],"notes":"Ends with slate work that continues the story."},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-4-4","title":"Lesson XXXI: Brave Old Ponto","steps":["New words: bound, Rose, called, got, drown, found, brave, came, Ponto, jumped, mouth, around, brought, water; sound focus: ou (bound, found, mouth)","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Slate work: copy “Good, old Ponto!”","Answer the question at the end: what do you think of Ponto?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00285"}],"questions":["Who is Ponto?","Which doll did Ponto bring out first?"],"answers":["Kate''s dog","Bess"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-4-5","title":"Lesson XXXII: A June Day","steps":["New words: June, Lucy''s, air, kind, trees, singing, blue, when, pure, says, sky, picnic; sound focus: says is a sight word","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Slate work: copy “The sky is as blue as it can be.”","Describe the weather today the way the lesson describes June"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00295"}],"questions":["Where are Lucy and her mamma?","When will they have a picnic?"],"answers":["In the woods","When papa can get a tent"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-4-6","title":"Lesson XXXIII: Review: The Lost Ball","steps":["Read it with expression, especially the questions","Slate work: copy “Ponto has found it.”","Hide a ball and let your child find it like Ponto"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00304"}],"questions":["Who found the lost ball?","Whose feet will he lay it at?"],"answers":["Ponto","Little Lucy''s"],"notes":"Review lesson: the words at the top are old words to practice. Let your child read as independently as possible."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-u5","title":"Unit 5: Lessons XXXIV–XL","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-5-1","title":"Lesson XXXIV: The Big Flag","steps":["New words: boy, our, spoil, hurrah, own, coil, noise, fourth, such, join, thank, about, hoist, pay, July, playing; sound focus: oi and oy","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Slate work: copy “Hurrah for the flag, boys!”","Read the conversation between James and papa with two voices"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00310"}],"questions":["What were the boys playing?","What did papa give with the flag?"],"answers":["Fourth of July","A coil of rope"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-5-2","title":"Lesson XXXV: The White Kitten","steps":["New words: finished, bonnet, lesson, saved, white, away, I''ve, am, work, scamper, ready, garden; sound focus: two-syllable words; I''ve","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Slate work: copy “Kitty, my pretty, white kitty.”","Memorize the first four lines"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00324"}],"questions":["What has the child finished?","What did the child save for the kitty?"],"answers":["Work and lesson","Some milk"],"notes":"This lesson is a script (cursive) poem. Read it together and use a stanza as copywork."},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-5-3","title":"Lesson XXXVI: Frank''s Boat","steps":["New words: care, always, line, Frank, row, been, keeps, home; sound focus: ow as long o (row)","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Slate work: copy “Frank has a pretty boat.”","Draw Frank''s boat with its black line"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00332"}],"questions":["What color is Frank''s boat?","Where does he keep it?"],"answers":["White, with a black line near the water","In the pond near his home"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-5-4","title":"Lesson XXXVII: The Little Boat","steps":["New words: much, one, yet, hungry, seen, grandma, corn, would; sound focus: one is a sight word","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Slate work: copy “Has grandma seen it?”","Narrate: what will John give Lucy, and what is in Lucy''s pan?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00339"}],"questions":["What will John give Lucy?","What is in Lucy''s pan?"],"answers":["The little boat, when it is finished","Corn for her hens"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-5-5","title":"Lesson XXXVIII: Going to Market","steps":["New words: market, bread, basket, bought, meat, tea, trying, tell, which; sound focus: ea (bread vs. meat, tea)","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Slate work: copy “She has bought some bread.”","Play market: ''buy'' bread, meat, and tea with play money"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00348"}],"questions":["What did mamma buy?","Where are the things?"],"answers":["Bread, meat, and tea","In the basket on her arm"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-5-6","title":"Lesson XXXIX: Dear Old Grandma","steps":["New words: reads, so, wears, please, could, hair, fast, love, easy, gray, chair, who, glasses; sound focus: air (hair, chair)","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Slate work: copy “She wears glasses when she reads.”","Tell one way your child can help a grandparent"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00355"}],"questions":["What does grandma wear when she reads?","What does Robert do when she asks for a drink?"],"answers":["Glasses","Runs as fast as he can to get it"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-5-7","title":"Lesson XL: The Bee in the Flower","steps":["New words: does, wonder, mother, other, bee, honey, listen, flower; sound focus: o as short u (mother, other, honey)","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Slate work: copy “Shall we let it out?”","Look at a real flower and find where a bee could go in"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XL","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00363"}],"questions":["What was shut up in the flower?","Why did it go into the flower?"],"answers":["A bee","For some honey"]}]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-u6","title":"Unit 6: Lessons XLI–XLVI","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-6-1","title":"Lesson XLI: Frank and James White","steps":["New words: best, hitched, their, should, or, riding, live, holds, hay, driving, tight, early; sound focus: -ing endings","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Slate work: copy “Some horses can trot very fast.”","Copy the slate work, then talk about being kind to animals"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00372"}],"questions":["Who is riding and who is driving?","What will the boys give the horses?"],"answers":["Frank is riding; James is driving","Some hay or corn"],"notes":"Ends with slate work to copy."},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-6-2","title":"Lesson XLII: The Bird''s Nest","steps":["New words: looking, thought, picking, heard, chirp, were, told, birds, search, dearly, young, girl, loved, children, besides; sound focus: ir, ur, ear (chirp, girl, heard, search)","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Slate work: copy “She left the birds.”","Narrate: what did the girl find, and why did she leave it?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00384"}],"questions":["What was the girl looking for?","What did she find besides flowers?"],"answers":["Flowers for her mother","A nest with young birds"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-6-3","title":"Lesson XLIII: The Lightning Train","steps":["New words: eight, ask, after, town, past, ah, ticket, right, half, two, train, ding, lightning; sound focus: eigh (eight)","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Slate work: copy “All right! Time is up!”","Play train in a basket or box and read it as a play"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00393"}],"questions":["What time will the train get back?","What does mamma want from town?"],"answers":["Half past eight","A big basket with two good little children in it"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-6-4","title":"Lesson XLIV: Playing School","steps":["New words: school, even, three, room, small; sound focus: oo (school, room)","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Slate work: copy “It is noon, and the school is out.”","Play school: your child is the teacher and reads to you"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00408"}],"questions":["Who is the teacher?","What is Fanny reading about?"],"answers":["Mary","A poor little boy who was lost in the woods"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-6-5","title":"Lesson XLV: Polly the Parrot","steps":["New words: apple, mew, tease, cracker, down, new, silly, asleep, wants, calls, knew, friends, upon, flew, Poll, Polly; sound focus: ew (mew, new, knew, flew)","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Slate work: copy “Polly wants a cracker.”","Say Polly''s lines in a parrot voice"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00420"}],"questions":["What kind of bird is Polly?","What did Polly do to tease the cat?"],"answers":["A parrot","Lit on the cat''s back while she was asleep"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-6-6","title":"Lesson XLVI: Review: A Nice Time in the Woods","steps":["Read it as a conversation between mother and the child","Slate work: copy “Rab was such a good dog.”","Narrate the whole day in the woods in order"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00429"}],"questions":["Who are the best flowers for?","What did Rab guard?"],"answers":["Grandma","The dolls and the basket"],"notes":"Review lesson: the words at the top are old words to practice. Let your child read as independently as possible."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-u7","title":"Unit 7: Lessons XLVII–LV","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-7-1","title":"Lesson XLVII: Home from the Beach","steps":["New words: beach, shells, these, seat, waves, going, ever, sea, watch, evening, lazy, side; sound focus: ea (beach, seat, sea)","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Slate work: copy “They have been to the beach.”","If you have shells, sort them while you talk about the story"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00440"}],"questions":["What did John find?","Who is driving?"],"answers":["Some pretty shells","Ben White"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-7-2","title":"Lesson XLVIII: Frank Goes Fishing","steps":["New words: log, quiet, proud, pulled, fish, stump, river, father; sound focus: ou (proud)","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Slate work: copy “How proud he feels!”","Narrate: where are they, and what did Frank catch?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00450"}],"questions":["Who took Frank fishing?","What is Frank''s seat?"],"answers":["His father","The stump of a big tree"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-7-3","title":"Lesson XLIX: The Rain","steps":["New words: rain, outside, often, pitter, say, window, sound, patter, drops, sometimes, only, music; sound focus: compound words (outside, sometimes)","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Slate work: copy “Pit, pitter, patter, pat.”","Listen to rain (or a recording) and tap out the pitter-patter rhythm"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00459"}],"questions":["Where do the drops pitter-patter?","What does the child think the sound is like?"],"answers":["On the window","Pretty music"],"notes":"This lesson is all script (cursive) slate work. Read it together first, then copy the little rhyme at the end."},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-7-4","title":"Lesson L: Winter Fun","steps":["New words: sled, throw, winter, hurt, ice, cover, Henry, next, skate, ground, merry, snow, sister, laughing, pair; sound focus: er (winter, cover, sister)","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Slate work: copy “What fun it is to skate on the ice!”","Name your favorite winter game"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson L","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00465"}],"questions":["Why does Henry pull his sister on the sled?","How do we know the boy who fell is not hurt?"],"answers":["She is too small to skate","He is laughing"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-7-5","title":"Lesson LI: Fido''s Tricks","steps":["New words: paw, polite, means, isn''t, speak, sir, shake, Fido, tricks, teach, dinner, Ellen, bowwow; sound focus: aw (paw)","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Slate work: copy “Give me your paw, Fido.”","Teach a pet or stuffed animal a ''trick'' while reading"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00475"}],"questions":["What is Fido wearing?","What does Fido''s ''Bowwow'' mean?"],"answers":["The boy''s hat","Thank you, I will"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-7-6","title":"Lesson LII: The Rat in the Shed","steps":["New words: puss, shed, pain, way, stole, saw, hid, eat, Hattie, suffer, sorry, something, caught, tried, Nero; sound focus: ai, ay (pain, way)","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Slate work: copy “Puss was hid on a big box.”","Read it as a conversation between Frank and Hattie"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00483"}],"questions":["Who caught the rat?","Why does Hattie feel sorry?"],"answers":["The old cat","She does not like to see even a rat suffer pain"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-7-7","title":"Lesson LIII: At the Seaside","steps":["New words: roll, build, grandpa, hard, foam, ships, houses, long, sail, break, wooden, blow; sound focus: oa, ai (foam, sail)","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Slate work: copy “They watch the waves.”","Draw a sand house with a wall around it"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00497"}],"questions":["What does grandpa like to watch?","Who are the shells for?"],"answers":["The big ships sail away","Their little sister"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-7-8","title":"Lesson LIV: Willie''s Rabbits","steps":["New words: asked, wanted, four, Willie''s, night, rabbits, lad, carried, cents, telling, fifty, master; sound focus: -ed endings (asked, wanted, carried)","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Slate work: copy “What soft, kind eyes they have.”","Copy the slate work about rabbits"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00507"}],"questions":["How many rabbits did father buy?","How much did they cost?"],"answers":["Four","Fifty cents"],"notes":"Ends with script slate work."},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-7-9","title":"Lesson LV: The Bird''s Nest in the Bush","steps":["New words: bush, cunning, place, show, find, broken, over, bring, again, fasten; sound focus: silent t (fasten)","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Slate work: copy “O Willie! a bird''s nest!”","Narrate: why shouldn''t they take the nest?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00521"}],"questions":["Where did Willie find the nest?","Why can''t they take it and bring it back?"],"answers":["In a bush","They could not fasten it in its place again"]}]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-u8","title":"Unit 8: Lessons LVI–LXIII","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-8-1","title":"Lesson LVI: How the Nest Was Made","steps":["New words: strong, round, dry, bill, worked, sends, claws, flit, God, spring; sound focus: aw (claws)","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Slate work: copy “God made the little birds to sing.”","Copy the four-line verse"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00530"}],"questions":["What is the nest made of?","Did they take the nest?"],"answers":["Horse-hairs and dry grass","No"],"notes":"Ends with a script verse to copy."},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-8-2","title":"Lesson LVII: The Little Brown Birds","steps":["New words: feathers, ago, fly, worm, crumb, feeding, ugly, off, feed, brown, guess, things; sound focus: silent b (crumb); gu (guess)","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Slate work: copy “Here comes the old bird.”","Watch birds outside for five minutes and tell what they do"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00541"}],"questions":["What did the brown bird carry away?","What did the mother bird bring?"],"answers":["A crumb","A worm"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-8-3","title":"Lesson LVIII: The Lord Is Near","steps":["New words: falling, counts, woes, nigh, begun, griefs, stars, tear, morning, Lord, each, joys; sound focus: igh (nigh)","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Slate work: copy “Think the Lord is near.”","Read the hymn together twice; explain nigh and woes"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00553"}],"questions":["When does the poem say to think the Lord is nigh?"],"answers":["When the morning has begun"],"notes":"This lesson is a script (cursive) hymn. Read it together and copy one stanza."},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-8-4","title":"Lesson LIX: Mary Meets Frank","steps":["New words: whistle, pocket, willow, note, filled, dead, sick, walk, every, blew, lane, lame, taking, cane, took; sound focus: silent t (whistle)","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Slate work: copy “Let us whistle it to sleep.”","Narrate: who is Frank, and who takes care of him?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00560"}],"questions":["Who takes care of Frank?","What did Frank take out of his pocket?"],"answers":["His grandma","A willow whistle"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-8-5","title":"Lesson LX: The Willow Whistle","steps":["New words: turned, face, cried, low, almost, soon, more, cry, once, because; sound focus: once is a sight word","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Slate work: copy “It whistles!”","Try to whistle, or blow a straw whistle, like Mary"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00570"}],"questions":["Why did Frank give Mary the whistle?","What happened to the doll''s eyes?"],"answers":["Because she was so good to his grandma","They shut, as if it were asleep"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-8-6","title":"Lesson LXI: The Hen and Her Chickens","steps":["New words: stood, himself, flapping, first, twelve, flapped, walked, flap, obey, better, Chippy, food, stone, before, chickens, kept; sound focus: -ed and -ing endings","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Slate work: copy “Just flap your wings.”","Act it out: jump from a ''stone'' (a pillow) to the other side"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00582"}],"questions":["How many chickens did the hen have?","Where did she want to take them?"],"answers":["Twelve","Across the brook"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-8-7","title":"Lesson LXII: Chippy Tried","steps":["New words: chirped, never, indeed, slowly, really, brood, began, didn''t, use, door, bite, piece; sound focus: ie (piece); didn''t","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Slate work: copy “Well, I tried.”","Talk about why Chippy got the bread"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00596"}],"questions":["Who got the piece of bread?","Why?"],"answers":["Chippy","He was the only one who really tried"]},{"id":"mcguffey-first-reader-8-8","title":"Lesson LXIII: The Last Lesson","steps":["New words: last, slates, write, waste, neat, taken, clean, learn, reader, parents, second; sound focus: silent w (write)","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Slate work: copy “We have finished the First Reader.”","Celebrate finishing the book; look over the Phonic Chart together"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00609"},{"label":"Phonic Chart","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14640/pg14640-images.html#id00619"}],"questions":["What book comes next?","How should children keep their books?"],"answers":["The Second Reader","Neat and clean"],"notes":"The Phonic Chart after this lesson lists every sound taught; it makes a good review sheet."}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"“McGuffey''s First Eclectic Reader, Revised Edition” by William Holmes McGuffey (1879), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/14640. Lesson plan by Freerange."}'::jsonb)
