pywork: “It eats just enough.”","Talk about how the kitten, bee, and squirrel know when to stop"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLVII"}],"questions":["What is a glutton?","What does the bee do with the honey it doesn''t eat?"],"answers":["One who eats too much because the food tastes good","Carries it to its hive"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-8-2","title":"Lesson LVIII: A Place for Everything","steps":["New words: thimble, borrow, comfort, offended, ashamed","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Copywork: “I have a place for everything.”","Read it as a play: one of you is Mary, one is Sarah"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLVIII"}],"questions":["What did Mary want to borrow?","What is Sarah''s secret?"],"answers":["A thimble","She has a place for everything and puts everything in its place"],"notes":"Written as a dialogue."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-8-3","title":"Lesson LIX: My Mother","steps":["New words: constant, lull, rejoice, fretful, meek","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Copywork: “None can love so well as thou.”","Make a card for mother with one line from the poem"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLIX"}],"questions":["What does the child give mother in return?"],"answers":["A fond heart"],"notes":"Script page: the whole lesson is printed in cursive handwriting (an image in the online text). Read it together, then use a few lines as copywork. It is a poem with old words (thou, thy, didst); explain them as you read."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-8-4","title":"Lesson LX: The Broken Window","steps":["New words: gift, engaged, skipping, mischief","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Copywork: “I will go and pay for it.”","Predict: what will happen when George goes back?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLX"}],"questions":["What was George''s New-year gift?","How did the window break?"],"answers":["A bright silver dollar","His snowball missed James Mason and hit it"],"notes":"Part one of a two-part story."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-8-5","title":"Lesson LXI: The Broken Window (Concluded)","steps":["New words: merchant, honestly, partner, without","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Copywork: “He called him an honest boy.”","Narrate the whole story and say why honesty paid off"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLXI"}],"questions":["What did George find in his cap?","What did George become in a few years?"],"answers":["Two silver dollars","The merchant''s partner"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-8-6","title":"Lesson LXII: Frank and the Hourglass","steps":["New words: hourglass, figure, impatient, advice, perfectly","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Copywork: “Study all the time.”","Time a task with a sand timer or clock"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLXII"}],"questions":["What shape is an hourglass?","How long did it take Frank to learn the hymn?"],"answers":["The figure 8","One hour and a half"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-8-7","title":"Lesson LXIII: March","steps":["New words: sleet, cruel, cheerly, taps","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Copywork: “Little flowers begin their growing.”","Look for the first spring shoots outside"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLXIII"}],"questions":["Who taps on the flowers to wake them?","Where is Winter going?"],"answers":["Spring","On his way; his time is past"],"notes":"Poem by Mary Mapes Dodge. Poem: read it aloud twice, the second time with expression."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-8-8","title":"Lesson LXIV: Jenny''s Call","steps":["New words: grazing, harness, rogue, snorted, caressed, service","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Copywork: “She gave love for love.”","Narrate: why would Fanny come to Jenny but not to the men?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLXIV"}],"questions":["What was the horse''s name?","Why did the horse come to Jenny?"],"answers":["Fanny","Jenny was always kind and gentle; the men had been rough"]}]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-u9","title":"Unit 9: Lessons LXV–LXXI","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-9-1","title":"Lesson LXV: Poor Davy","steps":["New words: recess, violet, ferns, maple, dainty, arrange","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Copywork: “Now the sunshine and the bird''s songs make him glad.”","Pick or draw a little bouquet like Davy''s"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLXV"}],"questions":["Why was Davy sad?","How did his teacher help?"],"answers":["Children said cruel words about his ragged clothes","She helped him become a little flower merchant"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-9-2","title":"Lesson LXVI: Alice''s Supper","steps":["New words: reapers, cradles, miller, flour, dough, kneading","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Copywork: “And it is for Alice''s supper!”","Trace bread from wheat to plate: wheat, flour, dough, bread"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLXVI"}],"questions":["What do the reapers cut?","What is Alice''s supper?"],"answers":["Wheat","Bread and butter"],"notes":"Poem: read it aloud twice, the second time with expression."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-9-3","title":"Lesson LXVII: A Snowstorm","steps":["New words: flakes, plumes, picket, captains, fairies","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Copywork: “The whole white world sparkled.”","Draw the snowy yard with the fence ''soldiers'' and gatepost ''captains''"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLXVII"}],"questions":["What did the snow turn the picket fence into?","What did the old corn basket become?"],"answers":["A row of soldiers","A little snow house with a round roof"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-9-4","title":"Lesson LXVIII: Bessie","steps":["New words: apron, closets, workbasket, dandelions, heaped, toad","Read the lesson aloud together, pointing to each word; then let your child read it alone","Copywork: “Waiting, waiting, waiting.”","Predict: who is ringing the bell for Bessie?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLXVIII"}],"questions":["What did Aunt Annie give Bessie?","What jumped into Bessie''s garden bed?"],"answers":["A large workbasket","A big brown toad"],"notes":"Part one of a two-part story."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-9-5","title":"Lesson LXIX: Bessie (Concluded)","steps":["New words: visit, soaked, ruined, obedient","You read a line, then your child reads it back; finish by reading the whole lesson together","Copywork: “You did quite right.”","Narrate: why was coming home at once the right choice?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLXIX"}],"questions":["Who wanted Bessie?","What happened to the basket?"],"answers":["Her mother, who was leaving for a long visit","It was left out in the rain"]},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-9-6","title":"Lesson LXX: Cheerfulness","steps":["New words: lightsome, cherished, lofty, maiden, introduce","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only on words they can''t sound out","Copywork: “Her name is Cheerfulness.”","Guess the riddle before reading the last line"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLXX"}],"questions":["What is the little maiden''s name?"],"answers":["Cheerfulness"],"notes":"Script page: the whole lesson is printed in cursive handwriting (an image in the online text). Read it together, then use a few lines as copywork. It is a riddle poem."},{"id":"mcguffey-second-reader-9-7","title":"Lesson LXXI: Lullaby","steps":["New words: western, breathe, dying, babe, sails","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you (or to a sibling or stuffed animal)","Copywork: “Sweet and low, sweet and low”","Read it softly as a lullaby; celebrate finishing the Second Reader"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14668/pg14668-images.html#lessonLXXI"}],"questions":["Who will come to the baby soon?"],"answers":["Father"],"notes":"Poem by Tennyson. Poem: read it aloud twice, the second time with expression."}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"“McGuffey''s Second Eclectic Reader, Revised Edition” by William Holmes McGuffey (1879), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/14668. Lesson plan by Freerange."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (listing_id, version) do update set content = excluded.content;
insert into public.listings (id, seller_name, seller_kind, seller_verified, title, subjects, grade_min, grade_max, philosophies, format, price, description, includes, lessons, lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement, family_style, lesson_steps, cover, color, esa_eligible, created_at, package_id, package_version, license, attribution, outline) values ('l-lib-mcguffey-sixth-reader', 'William Holmes McGuffey', 'open', false, 'McGuffey''s Sixth Eclectic Reader', array['reading', 'writing']::text[], 7, 9, array['charlotte_mason', 'classical', 'traditional', 'faith_based']::text[], 'online', 0, 'The last and richest McGuffey Reader: 138 selections from Shakespeare, Milton, Webster, Irving, Longfellow, Bryant, the Bible, and many more, opening with a short course in elocution. Each day your student learns a few hard words, reads a speech, story, or poem aloud with expression, then narrates it in speech or writing, or memorizes a stanza. A classic of American schooling, and the full text is free online.', array['145 lessons in 15 units: 7 elocution lessons plus all 138 Sixth Reader selections', 'Link to the free full text for every lesson', 'Key vocabulary, a read-aloud step, and a narration, written narration, or memory work each day', 'Comprehension questions with answers', 'Parent notes on authors, historical context, and dated or sensitive content']::text[], 145, 4, 40, 'low', false, '{}'::text[], '📜', '#2F6B5E', false, '2026-11-11T12:00:00.000Z', 'pk-lib-mcguffey-sixth-reader', 1, '{"kind":"public_domain"}'::jsonb, '“McGuffey''s Sixth Eclectic Reader, Revised Edition” by William Holmes McGuffey (1879), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/16751. Lesson plan by Freerange.', '[{"title":"Unit 1: Introduction to Elocution","lessons":["Elocution: Articulation","Elocution: Inflection","Elocution: Series, Parenthesis, Circumflex, and Monotone","Elocution: Accent and Emphasis","Elocution: Reading Verse","Elocution: The Voice","Elocution: Gesture, and Elocution and Reading"]},{"title":"Unit 2: Lessons I–X","lessons":["Lesson I: Anecdote of the Duke of Newcastle","Lesson II: The Needle","Lesson III: Dawn","Lesson IV: Description of a Storm","Lesson V: After the Thunderstorm","Lesson VI: House Cleaning","Lesson VII: Schemes of Life Often Illusory","Lesson VIII: The Brave Old Oak","Lesson IX: The Artist Surprised","Lesson X: Pictures of Memory"]},{"title":"Unit 3: Lessons XI–XX","lessons":["Lesson XI: The Morning Oratorio","Lesson XII: Short Selections in Poetry","Lesson XIII: Death of Little Nell","Lesson XIV: Vanity of Life","Lesson XV: A Political Pause","Lesson XVI: My Experience in Elocution","Lesson XVII: Elegy in a Country Churchyard","Lesson XVIII: Tact and Talent","Lesson XIX: Speech before the Virginia Convention","Lesson XX: The American Flag"]},{"title":"Unit 4: Lessons XXI–XXX","lessons":["Lesson XXI: Ironical Eulogy on Debt","Lesson XXII: The Three Warnings","Lesson XXIII: The Memory of Our Fathers","Lesson XXIV: Short Selections in Prose","Lesson XXV: The Jolly Old Pedagogue","Lesson XXVI: The Teacher and Sick Scholar","Lesson XXVII: The Snow Shower","Lesson XXVIII: Character of Napoleon Bonaparte","Lesson XXIX: Napoleon at Rest","Lesson XXX: War"]},{"title":"Unit 5: Lessons XXXI–XL","lessons":["Lesson XXXI: Speech of Walpole in Reproof of Mr. Pitt","Lesson XXXII: Pitt''s Reply to Sir Robert Walpole","Lesson XXXIII: Character of Mr. Pitt","Lesson XXXIV: The Soldier''s Rest","Lesson XXXV: Henry V. to His Troops","Lesson XXXVI: Speech of Paul on Mars Hill","Lesson XXXVII: God Is Everywhere","Lesson XXXVIII: Lafayette and Robert Raikes","Lesson XXXIX: Fall of Cardinal Wolsey","Lesson XL: The Philosopher"]},{"title":"Unit 6: Lessons XLI–L","lessons":["Lesson XLI: Marmion and Douglas","Lesson XLII: The Present","Lesson XLIII: The Baptism","Lesson XLIV: Sparrows","Lesson XLV: Observance of the Sabbath","Lesson XLVI: God''s Goodness to Such as Fear Him","Lesson XLVII: Character of Columbus","Lesson XLVIII: \"He Giveth His Beloved Sleep\"","Lesson XLIX: Description of a Siege","Lesson L: Marco Bozzaris"]},{"title":"Unit 7: Lessons LI–LX","lessons":["Lesson LI: Song of the Greek Bard","Lesson LII: North American Indians","Lesson LIII: Lochiel''s Warning","Lesson LIV: On Happiness of Temper","Lesson LV: The Fortune Teller","Lesson LVI: Rienzi''s Address to the Romans","Lesson LVII: Character of the Puritan Fathers of New England","Lesson LVIII: Landing of the Pilgrim Fathers","Lesson LIX: Necessity of Education","Lesson LX: Riding on a Snowplow"]},{"title":"Unit 8: Lessons LXI–LXX","lessons":["Lesson LXI: The Quarrel of Brutus and Cassius","Lesson LXII: The Quack","Lesson LXIII: Rip Van Winkle","Lesson LXIV: Bill and Joe","Lesson LXV: Sorrow for the Dead","Lesson LXVI: The Eagle","Lesson LXVII: Political Toleration","Lesson LXVIII: What Constitutes a State?","Lesson LXIX: The Brave at Home","Lesson LXX: South Carolina"]},{"title":"Unit 9: Lessons LXXI–LXXX","lessons":["Lesson LXXI: Massachusetts and South Carolina","Lesson LXXII: The Church Scene from Evangeline","Lesson LXXIII: Song of the Shirt","Lesson LXXIV: Diamond Cut Diamond","Lesson LXXV: Thanatopsis","Lesson LXXVI: Indian Jugglers","Lesson LXXVII: Antony over Caesar''s Dead Body","Lesson LXXVIII: The English Character","Lesson LXXIX: The Song of the Potter","Lesson LXXX: A Hot Day in New York"]},{"title":"Unit 10: Lessons LXXXI–XC","lessons":["Lesson LXXXI: Discontent.—An Allegory","Lesson LXXXII: Jupiter and Ten","Lesson LXXXIII: Scene from \"The Poor Gentleman\"","Lesson LXXXIV: My Mother''s Picture","Lesson LXXXV: Death of Samson","Lesson LXXXVI: An Evening Adventure","Lesson LXXXVII: The Barefoot Boy","Lesson LXXXVIII: The Glove and the Lions","Lesson LXXXIX: The Folly of Intoxication","Lesson XC: Starved Rock"]},{"title":"Unit 11: Lessons XCI–C","lessons":["Lesson XCI: Prince Henry and Falstaff","Lesson XCII: Studies","Lesson XCIII: Surrender of Granada","Lesson XCIV: Hamlet''s Soliloquy","Lesson XCV: Ginevra","Lesson XCVI: Inventions and Discoveries","Lesson XCVII: Enoch Arden at the Window","Lesson XCVIII: Lochinvar","Lesson XCIX: Speech on the Trial of a Murderer","Lesson C: The Closing Year"]},{"title":"Unit 12: Lessons CI–CX","lessons":["Lesson CI: A New City in Colorado","Lesson CII: Importance of the Union","Lesson CIII: The Influences of the Sun","Lesson CIV: Colloquial Powers of Franklin","Lesson CV: The Dream of Clarence","Lesson CVI: Homeward Bound","Lesson CVII: Impeachment of Warren Hastings","Lesson CVIII: Destruction of the Carnatic","Lesson CIX: The Raven","Lesson CX: A View of the Colosseum"]},{"title":"Unit 13: Lessons CXI–CXX","lessons":["Lesson CXI: The Bridge","Lesson CXII: Objects and Limits of Science","Lesson CXIII: The Downfall of Poland","Lesson CXIV: Labor","Lesson CXV: The Last Days of Herculaneum","Lesson CXVI: How Men Reason","Lesson CXVII: Thunderstorm on the Alps","Lesson CXVIII: Origin of Property","Lesson CXIX: Battle of Waterloo","Lesson CXX: \"With Brains, Sir\""]},{"title":"Unit 14: Lessons CXXI–CXXX","lessons":["Lesson CXXI: The New England Pastor","Lesson CXXII: Death of Absalom","Lesson CXXIII: Abraham Davenport","Lesson CXXIV: The Falls of the Yosemite","Lesson CXXV: A Psalm of Life","Lesson CXXVI: Franklin''s Entry into Philadelphia","Lesson CXXVII: Lines to a Waterfowl","Lesson CXXVIII: Goldsmith and Addison","Lesson CXXIX: Immortality of the Soul","Lesson CXXX: Character of Washington"]},{"title":"Unit 15: Lessons CXXXI–CXXXVIII","lessons":["Lesson CXXXI: Eulogy on Washington","Lesson CXXXII: The Solitary Reaper","Lesson CXXXIII: Value of the Present","Lesson CXXXIV: Happiness","Lesson CXXXV: Marion","Lesson CXXXVI: A Common Thought","Lesson CXXXVII: A Definite Aim in Reading","Lesson CXXXVIII: Ode to Mt. Blanc"]}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set seller_name = excluded.seller_name, seller_kind = excluded.seller_kind, seller_verified = excluded.seller_verified, title = excluded.title, subjects = excluded.subjects, grade_min = excluded.grade_min, grade_max = excluded.grade_max, philosophies = excluded.philosophies, format = excluded.format, price = excluded.price, description = excluded.description, includes = excluded.includes, lessons = excluded.lessons, lessons_per_week = excluded.lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson = excluded.minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement = excluded.parent_involvement, family_style = excluded.family_style, lesson_steps = excluded.lesson_steps, cover = excluded.cover, color = excluded.color, esa_eligible = excluded.esa_eligible, package_id = excluded.package_id, package_version = excluded.package_version, license = excluded.license, attribution = excluded.attribution, outline = excluded.outline;
insert into public.package_releases (listing_id, version, content) values ('l-lib-mcguffey-sixth-reader', 1, '{"units":[{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-u1","title":"Unit 1: Introduction to Elocution","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-1-1","title":"Elocution: Articulation","steps":["New words: articulation (clear speaking of sounds), vocals (pure vowel tones), subvocals (sounds mixing voice and breath, like b, d, v), aspirates (breath-only sounds, like p, t, f)","Read the explanation aloud, then say each sound in the Table of Vocals sharply, ''like the crack of a rifle''","Practice the ''Faults to Be Remedied'' lists: say each word correctly, then hear how the careless version sounds"],"links":[{"label":"Articulation","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00056"},{"label":"Faults to Be Remedied","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00097"}],"questions":["Into what three groups does the book divide the elementary sounds?","Name two common faults of articulation.","What is a diphthong?"],"answers":["Vocals, subvocals, and aspirates","Dropping or mis-sounding an unaccented vowel, dropping final consonants, skipping syllables, and running words together (any two)","Two vowel sounds joined in one, as in oil or now"],"notes":"The book''s introduction teaches elocution (reading aloud well). Spread these lessons over the first two weeks, and return to the drills all year."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-1-2","title":"Elocution: Inflection","steps":["New words: inflection (a slide of the voice up or down), interrogative (asking a question), antithesis (a contrast of opposites), emphasis (special force on a word)","Read the explanation and say each example aloud, sliding your voice as marked","Learn Rules I–VIII: make up one sentence of your own for any three of them"],"links":[{"label":"Inflection","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00126"}],"questions":["Which way does the voice slide at the end of a complete statement?","What inflection does a question answered by yes or no usually take?","What inflection do negative sentences usually take?"],"answers":["Downward (falling inflection)","Rising","Rising"],"notes":"The Gutenberg text shows inflection marks with plain apostrophes, so the rising and falling marks look alike. Use the rules and your ear."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-1-3","title":"Elocution: Series, Parenthesis, Circumflex, and Monotone","steps":["New words: series (a list of words or phrases), parenthesis (words set apart within a sentence), circumflex (a voice slide that goes both up and down), monotone (one unchanging tone)","Read the rules and examples aloud, from ''Of Series'' through ''Monotone''","Practice: read a list sentence of your own, then a sarcastic sentence using the circumflex"],"links":[{"label":"Of Series","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00299"}],"questions":["How should a parenthesis be read?","What is the circumflex used to express?","When is the monotone used?"],"answers":["More quickly and in a lower key than the rest of the sentence","Irony, sarcasm, supposition, or contrast","Mainly for grave, solemn, or awe-filled passages"]},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-1-4","title":"Elocution: Accent and Emphasis","steps":["New words: accent (stress on a syllable), absolute emphasis (stress not depending on a contrast), relative emphasis (stress that marks a contrast), emphatic pause (a pause that makes a word stand out)","Read the explanations and say each example aloud","Read one sentence three times, stressing a different word each time, and talk about how the meaning changes"],"links":[{"label":"Accent and Emphasis","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00360"}],"questions":["What is the difference between accent and emphasis?","Besides stress, name two other ways to give a word emphasis.","Is ''We can do nothing against the truth, but for the truth'' an example of absolute or relative emphasis?"],"answers":["Accent falls on a syllable within a word; emphasis falls on a word or phrase in a sentence","Lowering the voice (even to a whisper) or pausing before the word","Relative (it contrasts ''against'' and ''for'')"]},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-1-5","title":"Elocution: Reading Verse","steps":["New words: caesura (a pause near the middle of a line of poetry), poetic pause (a slight pause at the end of a line), simile (a comparison using like or as), sing-song (a chanting, rocking delivery)","Read the instructions and examples aloud","Choose a short poem you know and read it twice: once in a sing-song, once correctly with natural pauses"],"links":[{"label":"Instructions for Reading Verse","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00446"}],"questions":["What is the hardest thing about reading poetry aloud, according to the book?","What is the caesura?","How should a simile be read?"],"answers":["Keeping its measured flow without falling into a chant","A pause at or near the middle of a line","In a lower key and a little faster, like a parenthesis"]},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-1-6","title":"Elocution: The Voice","steps":["New words: pitch (how high or low the voice is), compass (the voice''s range), quantity (how long a sound is held), movement (speed of reading)","Read the explanations and examples for pitch, quantity, and movement aloud","Read one example passage slowly, one moderately, and one quickly, as the book suggests for each mood"],"links":[{"label":"The Voice","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00498"}],"questions":["What is the natural pitch of the voice?","What is quantity in reading?","What fault does the book say most pupils have with movement?"],"answers":["Its keynote, the pitch used in ordinary conversation","The length of time spent sounding a syllable or word","Reading too fast"]},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-1-7","title":"Elocution: Gesture, and Elocution and Reading","steps":["New words: gesture (movement of the body that adds meaning), attitude (posture), propriety (fitness, rightness), modulation (varying the voice)","Read the section on gesture and try its advice: stand tall, weight on one foot, open hand, free arm","Read ''Elocution and Reading'' and the verses ''On Modulation'' aloud","Copywork: “But none emphatic can that speaker call, / Who lays an equal emphasis on all.”"],"links":[{"label":"Gesture","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00577"},{"label":"Elocution and Reading","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00593"}],"questions":["What is the usual fault of young speakers in gesture?","Where does the book say training in good speaking must begin?","What happens if you put equal emphasis on every word?"],"answers":["Too much action","In childhood, in the nursery","No word stands out; it is the same as no emphasis at all"],"notes":"This ends the introduction. Keep coming back to these sections as your student reads the selections aloud."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-u2","title":"Unit 2: Lessons I–X","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-2-1","title":"Lesson I: Anecdote of the Duke of Newcastle","steps":["New words: levees (morning receptions held by a great man), borough (a town that sends a member to Parliament), excise (a tax on goods; an exciseman collects it), post haste (as fast as possible)","Read the anecdote aloud, giving the Duke and the Cornish voter different voices","Narrate the story in order: the promise, the long wait, and the midnight mix-up"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson I","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00611"}],"questions":["What favor did the Cornish voter ask of the Duke?","Why did the Duke think the voter was a messenger?","What is the joke in the last line?"],"answers":["That his son-in-law get the post of supervisor of excise when the old supervisor died","He was waiting up for news that the King of Spain had died, and had ordered his porter to send any messenger straight up","The Duke could not put the man on the throne of Spain, so he gave him the post he actually wanted: exciseman"],"notes":"From Blackwood''s Magazine. The Duke of Newcastle was a chief minister under George II; the King of Spain was Ferdinand VI."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-2-2","title":"Lesson II: The Needle","steps":["New words: cotillon and quadrille (old dances), whist (a card game), talisman (a charm with magic power), rout (a large evening party)","Read the poem aloud, letting the repeated refrain ring each time","Copywork: “The bright little needle, the swift-flying needle”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson II","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00634"}],"questions":["What does the poet prefer to dancing, painting, and music?","What line is repeated at the end of every stanza?"],"answers":["A young woman who loves her home and is skilled with the needle","“The needle directed by beauty and art.”"],"notes":"Poem by Samuel Woodworth. Its view of what makes a young woman admirable is very much of its time; a good chance to talk about how ideas of ''accomplishments'' have changed."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-2-3","title":"Lesson III: Dawn","steps":["New words: spectral (ghostly), zenith (the point straight overhead), concave (the curved bowl of the sky), Magians (ancient Persian sun and fire worshipers)","Read the short life of Edward Everett, then read the description aloud slowly, as if painting the sky with your voice","Written narration: in a paragraph, describe how the night sky changed into morning on Everett''s train ride","Look out at dawn this week and find Jupiter or the Pleiades if they are visible"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson III","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00638"}],"questions":["Why was Everett up at two o''clock in the morning?","Which stars does he say went to rest first?","What amazed Everett more than the ancient sun worshipers?"],"answers":["To catch the early train from Providence to Boston","The smaller stars, like little children","That people in his own day can watch the sunrise and still say there is no God"],"notes":"By Edward Everett, from an address on the uses of astronomy. Point out the figures of speech in the third and fourth paragraphs."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-2-4","title":"Lesson IV: Description of a Storm","steps":["New words: desolation (ruin), eddies (whirling currents), sluices (channels for water), firmament (the sky), cataract (a great waterfall)","Read the storm aloud, building speed and force through the long lists of sounds","Narrate: tell the storm in three stages, from the wind, to the silence, to the falling mountain"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson IV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00651"}],"questions":["What broke the sudden dead silence?","What made the mountain peak fall?"],"answers":["Only the splash of the stirred-up lake against the rocks","A lake burst on its summit and the waterfall became a falling ocean"],"notes":"By Benjamin Disraeli, novelist and later British prime minister. This is an excerpt from a novel, so the characters are not introduced."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-2-5","title":"Lesson V: After the Thunderstorm","steps":["New words: tumultuous (wild and disorderly), azure (clear blue), kine (cattle), articulate (able to speak in words)","Read the poem aloud twice, the second time slowly enough to hear each picture","Memorize the first four lines, “As from the face of heaven the shattered clouds…”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson V","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00657"}],"questions":["What sounds fill the fields after the storm?","Whom does the poet say ought to lead the hymn of thanks?"],"answers":["Birdsong, cattle lowing, and sheep bleating","Man, who can speak words"],"notes":"Poem by James Thomson, from ''The Seasons.''"},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-2-6","title":"Lesson VI: House Cleaning","steps":["New words: prognostics (warning signs), scullion (a kitchen servant), testers (canopies over beds), wainscots (wooden panels on walls), pristine (original)","Read the essay aloud with a dry, mock-serious voice to bring out the humor","Written narration: in a paragraph, retell the whitewashing ''storm'' from the point of view of the servant or the house","Make a list of the old-fashioned household words and what replaced them"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson VI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00663"}],"questions":["What sign tells the husband it is time to run?","How was the gentleman with the spoiled clothes ''doubly non-suited''?","What did the philosopher build, and did it work?"],"answers":["A wheelbarrow of lime or buckets of whitewash in the yard","He lost both his suit of clothes and his lawsuit","A small room in the garden for the women to clean; no, it failed"],"notes":"By Francis Hopkinson, a signer of the Declaration of Independence. It is satire; its jokes about ''female rage'' belong to the 1700s, so talk about the humor and the way the target has aged."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-2-7","title":"Lesson VII: Schemes of Life Often Illusory","steps":["New words: caliph (a Muslim ruler), viceroy (a governor ruling for a king), docility (willingness to be taught), connubial (of marriage), insatiable (never satisfied)","Read Omar''s speech aloud as a wise old man talking to a young one","Written narration: list Omar''s four plans and what actually happened to each"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson VII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00683"}],"questions":["What did Caleb ask Omar to teach him?","Why did Omar never travel?","What lesson does Omar give Caleb?"],"answers":["The secret of how he gained his wisdom, power, and fortune","Business always kept him at court, and his presence was always needed","That it is of little use to form plans of life; time slips away"],"notes":"By Samuel Johnson. An allegory in an Arabian Nights setting (Bagdad, the caliph''s court)."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-2-8","title":"Lesson VIII: The Brave Old Oak","steps":["New words: renown (fame), rebec (an old stringed instrument), swains (country lads), hale (healthy)","Read the song aloud with a strong, steady beat","Memorize the first stanza"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson VIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00702"}],"questions":["What does the oak do on a wild midnight?","Who once gathered dew at the oak''s feet?"],"answers":["Shows his might as the storm shouts through his branches","Young maidens, on May Day"],"notes":"Poem by Henry Fothergill Chorley. The ''ruthless king'' is gold: the fear is that the oak will be cut down for ship timber."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-2-9","title":"Lesson IX: The Artist Surprised","steps":["New words: propitiation (winning someone''s favor), indefatigable (tireless), attenuation (thinness), thalers (old German silver coins)","Read the story aloud; let the auction bids grow louder as they climb","Narrate the story from Samuel''s hidden nights of painting to the final check"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson IX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00708"}],"questions":["What did Samuel hope to get for his picture?","How much did the painting finally sell for?","What happened to Samuel after he became rich?"],"answers":["Enough to buy food for about a week","One hundred thousand thalers","He lived richly but soon died of indigestion"],"notes":"The author is not named. Albert Durer was a real German painter and engraver; Samuel''s story is a tale, not history."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-2-10","title":"Lesson X: Pictures of Memory","steps":["New words: gnarled (twisted and knotty), coquetting (flirting), cowslip (a yellow wildflower), immortal (never dying)","Read the poem aloud gently and slowly","Copywork: “Among the beautiful pictures / That hang on Memory''s wall”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson X","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00738"}],"questions":["Why is the old forest the best picture on Memory''s wall?","What bed did the speaker make for her brother?"],"answers":["Because her little brother is buried there","A bed of yellow leaves"],"notes":"Poem by Alice Cary. It is about the death of a child, so read it first yourself if your child is sensitive."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-u3","title":"Unit 3: Lessons XI–XX","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-3-1","title":"Lesson XI: The Morning Oratorio","steps":["New words: oratorio (a long musical work for voices), welkin (the sky), matin (morning), vermilion (bright red), subterranean (underground)","Read the essay aloud; pause after each bird is named and say what its song is like","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell the order in which the birds join the morning chorus","Go outside at dawn one morning this week and list the birds you hear first"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00743"}],"questions":["Which bird sings first and stops last?","What are the robins doing when they grow quiet?","How is the chorus different after sunrise?"],"answers":["The hairbird (chipping sparrow)","Hopping on the ground hunting worms and insects for breakfast","It is more brilliant and lively but less continuous, and the bobolinks are loudest"],"notes":"By Wilson Flagg, a New England nature writer. The ''hairbird'' is the chipping sparrow."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-3-2","title":"Lesson XII: Short Selections in Poetry","steps":["New words: emblem (a symbol), filches (steals), descries (sees, makes out), adorn (decorate, honor)","Read all six short poems aloud, one by one; say in a sentence what each is about","Memorize “My mind to me a kingdom is” (the stanza by William Byrd) or Shakespeare''s “Good name in man or woman”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00759"}],"questions":["In ''A Good Name,'' why is stealing a good name worse than stealing a purse?","In ''Old Age and Death,'' what lets new light into ''the soul''s dark cottage''?","Who are the three poets in Dryden''s lines on Milton?"],"answers":["It does not make the thief richer but leaves the victim truly poor","The chinks (cracks) that time has made","Homer, Dante, and Milton"],"notes":"Six short pieces: The Cloud (John Wilson), My Mind (William Byrd), A Good Name (Shakespeare, Othello), Sunrise (James Thomson), Old Age and Death (Edmund Waller), and Milton (John Dryden)."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-3-3","title":"Lesson XIII: Death of Little Nell","steps":["New words: languid (weak, limp), waning (fading), remorseless (without pity), decrepit (worn out with age), palsied (shaking, paralyzed)","Read the passage aloud quietly and slowly, with long pauses at the repeated words “She was dead”","Narrate: tell what Nell asked for, how she died, and how the little boy helped her grandfather"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00776"}],"questions":["What did Nell ask to have placed near her when she died?","What did she say often in her sleep?","How did the little boy help the old man?"],"answers":["Something that had loved the light and had the sky above it always","“God bless you!”","His talk of Nell comforted him; he got him to rest and walk, and led him away when she was buried"],"notes":"By Charles Dickens, from ''The Old Curiosity Shop'' (1840). A famously sad scene about a child''s death; preview it if your child is sensitive."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-3-4","title":"Lesson XIV: Vanity of Life","steps":["New words: hireling (a hired worker), transgression (a sin, wrongdoing), iniquity (wickedness), countenance (face)","Read the poem aloud as a solemn lament","Open a Bible to Job 14 and compare a few lines with this translation"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00792"}],"questions":["What hope does a cut-down tree have that a man does not?","What questions does the speaker ask about death?"],"answers":["It can sprout new shoots from its old root","Whether a man who dies shall live again, and where he is when he is taken away"],"notes":"Herder''s verse translation of Job 14, from his ''Hebrew Poetry.''"},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-3-5","title":"Lesson XV: A Political Pause","steps":["New words: carnage (slaughter), implacable (unable to be calmed), expiring (dying), devastation (great destruction)","Read the speech aloud, making the sarcasm in “they are only pausing!” clear","Narrate: what did Fox think of calling the war a ''pause''?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00805"}],"questions":["What word was Fox mocking?","What did the other side say the ''pause'' was meant to test?"],"answers":["“Pause” (a political pause in fighting)","Whether Bonaparte would behave better than before"],"notes":"By Charles James Fox, from a speech against continuing the war with Napoleon''s France. The book points out how a negative calls for a rising inflection here."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-3-6","title":"Lesson XVI: My Experience in Elocution","steps":["New words: preceptor (teacher), obstreperous (noisy and unruly), aptitude (natural skill), depredations (raids; here, reading greedily)","Read the essay aloud; enjoy the lisping ''thunderbolths'' line","Written narration: in a paragraph, describe Master Moody''s way of teaching speaking and why Neal disliked it"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00811"}],"questions":["What nickname did Neal give the shy schoolmate?","What did Neal decide noises like stamping and thumping are not?","Who was the audience for Neal''s first recitation?"],"answers":["“O roarer” (from ''Aurora'')","Oratory; untranslatable sounds are not language","His mother and sister"],"notes":"By John Neal, an American writer from Portland, Maine."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-3-7","title":"Lesson XVII: Elegy in a Country Churchyard","steps":["New words: curfew (evening bell), lea (meadow), glebe (soil), heraldry (family coats of arms), penury (poverty), sequestered (secluded)","Read the whole elegy aloud slowly, one stanza at a time","Memorize the first stanza, “The curfew tolls the knell of parting day…”","Copywork: “The paths of glory lead but to the grave.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00836"}],"questions":["Who is buried in the country churchyard?","What kept these villagers from becoming great?","What does the flower ''born to blush unseen'' stand for?"],"answers":["The poor, humble forefathers of the village","Poverty and lack of learning","Gifted people whose talents were never known to the world"],"notes":"By Thomas Gray (1751), one of the best-known poems in English. Hampden, Milton, and Cromwell are explained in the notes."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-3-8","title":"Lesson XVIII: Tact and Talent","steps":["New words: tact (the knack of saying and doing the right thing at the right time), momentum (force of motion), preferment (promotion), dexterously (skillfully)","Read the essay aloud, stressing each pair of opposites (talent is power, tact is skill…)","Written narration: in a paragraph, explain the difference between tact and talent, with an example from your own life"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00875"}],"questions":["Name two of the places where the author sets tact against talent.","According to the essay, which usually wins in practical life?"],"answers":["The theater, the law courts (the bar), the church, the royal court, and the senate","Tact, “ten to one”"],"notes":"The author is not named in the book. A good model of antithesis (balanced opposites)."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-3-9","title":"Lesson XIX: Speech before the Virginia Convention","steps":["New words: siren (a sea nymph who lured sailors to ruin), comports (fits, agrees), subjugation (conquest), remonstrated (protested), supinely (lying idle on one''s back)","Read the life of Patrick Henry, then deliver the speech aloud, building to the last line","Memorize the final paragraph, from “Gentlemen may cry peace, peace…” to “give me liberty, or give me death.”","Written narration: in a paragraph, list Henry''s reasons why the colonies must fight now"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00883"}],"questions":["What is the one lamp that guides Henry''s feet?","What did Henry say the British fleets and armies were really meant for?","Why did he say waiting would not make the colonies stronger?"],"answers":["The lamp of experience","To force the colonies into submission","Waiting would only let Britain disarm them and station a guard in every house"],"notes":"By Patrick Henry, March 1775. Pair with a history of the opening of the Revolution. The book points to emphasis and the power of a pause or whisper in delivery."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-3-10","title":"Lesson XX: The American Flag","steps":["New words: standard (a flag), baldric (a belt worn over the shoulder; here, the Milky Way), harbingers (signs of what is coming), welkin (the sky)","Read the poem aloud with energy and a strong rhythm","Memorize the first stanza, “When Freedom, from her mountain height…”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00895"}],"questions":["Where did Freedom get the stars and stripes for the flag?","To whom does Freedom give the flag to guard?"],"answers":["The stars from the night sky and the stripes from the morning light and the Milky Way","The eagle"],"notes":"Poem by Joseph Rodman Drake. The battle imagery is vivid and glorifies war, as poems of its time often did."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-u4","title":"Unit 4: Lessons XXI–XXX","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-4-1","title":"Lesson XXI: Ironical Eulogy on Debt","steps":["New words: ironical (saying the opposite of what is meant), eulogy (a speech of praise), duns (people demanding payment), regale (feast), extortioners (people who squeeze money out of others)","Read the essay aloud in a mock-serious voice, as if you truly believed it","Talk it over: what is the author really saying about debt and saving?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00903"}],"questions":["Why is Mr. Payall unknown in his neighborhood?","What happens to the schoolboy who saves his plum cake?","Does the writer really think debt is good? How can you tell?"],"answers":["He pays cash, so his name is in no one''s account books","The others eat theirs, then rob and thrash him","No; it is irony, praising debt in absurd terms to show its folly"],"notes":"The author is not named in the book. Explain irony before reading so the joke lands."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-4-2","title":"Lesson XXII: The Three Warnings","steps":["New words: jocund (merry), delinquent (one who fails a duty), reprieve (a delay of punishment), fourscore (eighty), captious (quick to find fault)","Read the poem aloud, with a different voice for Death and for Dodson","Narrate the story of the bargain and how Death kept his word"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00911"}],"questions":["When did Death first come for Dodson?","What were the three warnings?","How old was Dodson when Death returned?"],"answers":["On his wedding day","He became lame, blind, and deaf","Eighty (fourscore)"],"notes":"Poem by Hester Lynch Thrale, friend of Samuel Johnson. A humorous fable about old age and death."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-4-3","title":"Lesson XXIII: The Memory of Our Fathers","steps":["New words: veneration (deep respect), sedulously (carefully, busily), innovation (change), obloquy (blame, disgrace), bigotry (intolerance)","Read the address aloud as a preacher would","Written narration: in a paragraph, give Beecher''s answer to people who mock the Puritan founders"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00922"}],"questions":["Why does Beecher think it dangerous to ridicule the founders?","What fault does he admit the founders had?"],"answers":["Once their memory is despised, no one will value or keep up the institutions they built","They sometimes persecuted others"],"notes":"By Lyman Beecher, a New England minister. This is a one-sided defense of the Puritans; his claim that other nations began as ''barbarians'' and his praise of Puritan ''bigots'' are worth discussing."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-4-4","title":"Lesson XXIV: Short Selections in Prose","steps":["New words: antithesis (a contrast of opposites), adamantine (unbreakably hard), iris (the rainbow), ethereal (light and heavenly), evanescent (quickly fading)","Read the six short pieces aloud, one at a time; give each a one-line summary","Copywork: “Dryden is read with frequent astonishment, and Pope with perpetual delight.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00929"}],"questions":["In Johnson''s comparison, whose writing is like a natural field and whose like a velvet lawn?","Whom is Las Casas begging not to fight?","According to Davy, what should we say instead of ''change'' in nature?"],"answers":["Dryden''s is the natural field; Pope''s the velvet lawn","Pizarro and his chieftains (the Spanish conquerors of Peru)","Progress"],"notes":"Six pieces: Dryden and Pope (Samuel Johnson), Las Casas Dissuading from Battle (R. B. Sheridan), Action and Repose (John Ruskin), Time and Change (Sir Humphry Davy), The Poet (W. E. Channing), and Mountains (William Howitt)."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-4-5","title":"Lesson XXV: The Jolly Old Pedagogue","steps":["New words: pedagogue (schoolteacher), sallow (pale yellowish), Rule of Three (an old method for solving proportions), litigate (go to court), cronies (old friends)","Read the poem aloud with a warm, cheerful tone","Memorize the first stanza"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00949"}],"questions":["How did the old pedagogue feel about whipping?","How did he die?"],"answers":["He thought it barbarous and seldom used the rod","Peacefully, sitting at his door on a midsummer evening"],"notes":"Poem by George Arnold. The teacher enjoys ''a pipe and a friendly glass,'' an ordinary pleasure in the 1860s."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-4-6","title":"Lesson XXVI: The Teacher and Sick Scholar","steps":["New words: forms (long school benches), conning (studying, memorizing), impunity (freedom from punishment), lattice (a window of crisscrossed strips), chafing (rubbing to warm)","Read the chapter aloud; change pace from the noisy schoolroom to the quiet sickroom","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell how the schoolmaster''s sorrow changed his school that day"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00959"}],"questions":["Whose copy did the master hold up as a model in writing time?","Why did the master give the boys a half holiday?","What did the sick boy ask the master to tie to the window?"],"answers":["The sick scholar''s","He could not keep his mind on lessons and wanted the village quiet for the sick child","His handkerchief, so the boys at play might see it and think of him"],"notes":"From Charles Dickens''s ''The Old Curiosity Shop.'' The little scholar dies at the end; preview it for a sensitive child."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-4-7","title":"Lesson XXVII: The Snow Shower","steps":["New words: prone (headlong), spangles (small glittering ornaments), burlier (bigger, heavier), myriads (huge numbers)","Read Bryant''s life, then read the poem aloud, slowing on each “Flake after flake” refrain","Copywork: “Flake after flake / They sink in the dark and silent lake.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00978"}],"questions":["What do the paired snowflakes remind the poet of?","Why does the companion have a tear in her eye?","How does the poem end?"],"answers":["Friends, or husband and wife, going through life hand in hand","She is thinking of dear friends who have died","The clouds part, a sunbeam falls, and the snow is gone, at rest in the lake"],"notes":"Poem by William Cullen Bryant."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-4-8","title":"Lesson XXVIII: Character of Napoleon Bonaparte","steps":["New words: prodigy (a wonder), despotic (like a tyrant), subaltern (a junior officer), immutable (unchangeable), vicissitudes (ups and downs of fortune)","Read the portrait aloud, giving weight to each balanced contrast","Written narration: in a paragraph, list three contradictions Phillips sees in Napoleon"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00988"}],"questions":["What does Phillips say Napoleon worshiped instead of God?","Name one way Napoleon acted against learning while pretending to favor it."],"answers":["Ambition","He jailed the press, banned books, persecuted authors, had the printer Palm shot, and banished Madame de Staël"],"notes":"By Charles Phillips, an Irish orator. A one-sided, rhetorical portrait; the notes explain the many names."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-4-9","title":"Lesson XXIX: Napoleon at Rest","steps":["New words: falchion (a curved sword), miters (bishops'' hats), dirge (a funeral song), surge (rolling waves)","Read the poem aloud solemnly","Find St. Helena on a map and trace Napoleon''s campaigns named in the first stanza (Egypt, the Alps, Moscow)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id00999"}],"questions":["Where is Napoleon buried in this poem?","What are the only sounds that mourn him?"],"answers":["On the seagirt rock of St. Helena","The sea bird''s cry, the surf, the clouds, and the wind"],"notes":"Poem by John Pierpont. Napoleon''s body was moved from St. Helena to Paris in 1840."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-4-10","title":"Lesson XXX: War","steps":["New words: lurid (glaring, horrible), bereavement (loss of a loved one), wormwood and gall (bitter things), Golgotha (a place of skulls and death)","Read the address aloud; let the repeated answer ring out each time","Written narration: in a paragraph, describe one of Sumner''s two scenes, the battlefield or the two ships"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01007"}],"questions":["What answer does Sumner give, twice, to ''wherefore this contest''?","What does he find more revolting than the wounded bodies?"],"answers":["That this is the established method of deciding justice between nations","The lawless passions that sweep through the fight"],"notes":"By Charles Sumner, from ''The True Grandeur of Nations'' (1845). Graphic battlefield description; the notes explain Austerlitz, St. Bartholomew, and other references."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-u5","title":"Unit 5: Lessons XXXI–XL","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-5-1","title":"Lesson XXXI: Speech of Walpole in Reproof of Mr. Pitt","steps":["New words: vehemence (great force of feeling), petulancy (peevishness), invective (abusive speech), epithets (labels, descriptive names), deference (respect)","Read the speech aloud in a cool, superior tone","Narrate: what does Walpole accuse young Pitt of?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01018"}],"questions":["What does Walpole say young men should learn to do instead of declaiming?","What does he say is the difference between accusing and proving?"],"answers":["To reason, and to prefer sound argument and facts to grand words","Reproaches without evidence only hurt the person who makes them"],"notes":"By Sir Robert Walpole, prime minister of Great Britain. Read this and the next lesson as a pair."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-5-2","title":"Lesson XXXII: Pitt''s Reply to Sir Robert Walpole","steps":["New words: palliate (excuse), abhorrence (disgust), dissimulation (pretending), calumniator (a slanderer), supercilious (haughty)","Read the reply aloud with mounting indignation","Written narration: in a paragraph, say who won the exchange between Walpole and Pitt, and why"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01024"}],"questions":["What ''atrocious crime'' does Pitt admit to?","When, says Pitt, does old age deserve contempt?"],"answers":["Being a young man","When its years have passed without improvement and a man still blunders, or grows more wicked"],"notes":"By William Pitt (later Earl of Chatham), the ''Great Commoner.''"},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-5-3","title":"Lesson XXXIII: Character of Mr. Pitt","steps":["New words: chicanery (trickery), venal (open to bribes), posterity (future generations), subvert (overthrow)","Read the portrait aloud with dignity","Narrate: how did Pitt''s speaking differ from Demosthenes''s and Cicero''s, according to Grattan?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01030"}],"questions":["What were Pitt''s object and his ambition?","How did Pitt unite a corrupt age?"],"answers":["His object was England; his ambition was fame","Without corrupting anyone, by the force of his character"],"notes":"By Henry Grattan, an Irish statesman."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-5-4","title":"Lesson XXXIV: The Soldier''s Rest","steps":["New words: pibroch (bagpipe battle music), fallow (plowed but unplanted land), bittern (a marsh bird with a booming call), reveille (a wake-up call)","Read the song aloud softly, like a lullaby","Memorize the first stanza, “Soldier, rest! thy warfare o''er…”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01039"}],"questions":["Who is being sung to sleep in the third stanza?","What gentle sounds may still be heard?"],"answers":["A huntsman","The lark''s song at daybreak and the bittern''s booming"],"notes":"Song by Sir Walter Scott, from ''The Lady of the Lake.''"},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-5-5","title":"Lesson XXXV: Henry V. to His Troops","steps":["New words: breach (a gap broken in a wall), sinews (muscles and tendons), yeomen (common farmers serving as soldiers), mettle (courage, spirit)","Read Shakespeare''s life, then declaim the speech aloud with fire","Memorize the opening, “Once more unto the breach, dear friends, once more…”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01047"}],"questions":["What animal should soldiers imitate in war?","What does Henry say his yeomen look like before the charge?","What is the battle cry?"],"answers":["The tiger","Greyhounds straining at the leash","“God for Harry, England, and St. George!”"],"notes":"From Shakespeare''s ''King Henry V,'' Act III, Scene 1, at the siege of Harfleur in France."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-5-6","title":"Lesson XXXVI: Speech of Paul on Mars Hill","steps":["New words: superstitious (here, very religious), offspring (children), Godhead (God''s nature), Areopagite (a member of the court of the Areopagus)","Read the speech aloud as Paul would speak it to the Athenians","Compare with Acts 17:22–34 in your own Bible"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01056"}],"questions":["What altar inscription did Paul use to begin?","How did the Athenians respond?"],"answers":["“To the Unknown God”","Some mocked, some wanted to hear more later, and a few believed, including Dionysius and Damaris"],"notes":"From the Bible, Acts 17."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-5-7","title":"Lesson XXXVII: God Is Everywhere","steps":["New words: vault (an arched roof; here, the sky), orbs (heavenly bodies), immensity (vastness), finny nations (the fish of the sea)","Read the poem aloud as a dialogue: the first stanza asks, the others answer","Copywork: “The sun, the moon, the stars, his majesty declare.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01062"}],"questions":["What question does the first speaker ask?","Name the three places where the answer finds God."],"answers":["Where is God, and who is He?","The sky, the mountains, and the sea"],"notes":"Poem by Joseph Hutton."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-5-8","title":"Lesson XXXVIII: Lafayette and Robert Raikes","steps":["New words: pageant (a grand public show), jubilee (a great anniversary celebration), amaranthine (unfading), philanthropist (one who works for the good of others), lambent (softly glowing)","Read the address aloud; contrast the two imagined welcomes in your voice","Written narration: in a paragraph, explain why Grimké thought Raikes the greater benefactor. Do you agree?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01067"}],"questions":["Who was Robert Raikes?","When did Lafayette revisit America?","How would Raikes''s welcome have differed from Lafayette''s?"],"answers":["An English printer, the founder of Sunday schools","In 1824–1825","No cannons, parades, or balls; he would be welcomed by preachers, Sunday-school teachers, and children in churches"],"notes":"By Thomas S. Grimké, from a Sunday-school address."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-5-9","title":"Lesson XXXIX: Fall of Cardinal Wolsey","steps":["New words: wanton (playful, careless), Lucifer (the fallen angel), fortitude (strength to endure), inventory (a list of possessions)","Read the scene aloud with a partner, one taking Wolsey and the other Cromwell","Memorize from “Cromwell, I charge thee, fling away ambition” to “Corruption wins not more than honesty.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01084"}],"questions":["What does Wolsey compare a man''s rise and fall to?","What news hurts Wolsey most?","What does Wolsey wish he had done differently?"],"answers":["A plant that puts out leaves and blossoms, then is killed by frost","That Anne (Boleyn) has been seen in public as the king''s queen","Served God with half the zeal he had served his king"],"notes":"From Shakespeare''s ''Henry VIII,'' Act III, Scene 2. Cardinal Wolsey was Henry VIII''s minister, disgraced in 1529."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-5-10","title":"Lesson XL: The Philosopher","steps":["New words: embowered (sheltered by trees), sloven (untidy), anachronistical (out of its proper time), folios (large old books), residuum (what is left over)","Read the sketch aloud, enjoying the gentle humor","Narrate: describe Parson Chub, then tell what Ned found in the library at midnight"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XL","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01092"}],"questions":["Why did Parson Chub have to leave Ireland?","Why did Chub''s plan for arranging the library fail?","What were Frank and the parson really doing in the library?"],"answers":["He took part in the rebellion of 1798; his house was burned and his property seized","Several of his subject sections had no books at all","Sleeping over their books, with a pitcher of whisky punch"],"notes":"By John P. Kennedy, from ''Swallow Barn,'' sketches of plantation life in Virginia. The notes explain the many classical names."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-u6","title":"Unit 6: Lessons XLI–L","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-6-1","title":"Lesson XLI: Marmion and Douglas","steps":["New words: behest (command), peer (an equal; a noble), portcullis (a heavy grated castle gate), rowels (spur points), gauntlet (an armored glove)","Read the notes first, then read the poem aloud with growing excitement at the chase","Memorize from “And dar''st thou then / To beard the lion in his den” to “Let the portcullis fall.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01111"}],"questions":["Why would Douglas not shake Marmion''s hand?","How narrowly did Marmion escape?","What did Douglas admire about Marmion after all?"],"answers":["Though he honored Marmion as the king''s messenger, he despised his character","The falling gate grazed the plume of his helmet","He was bold in speech and a fine rider, a tried warrior"],"notes":"By Sir Walter Scott, from ''Marmion.'' Marmion is England''s envoy at Tantallon Castle."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-6-2","title":"Lesson XLII: The Present","steps":["New words: reverence (deep respect), monarch (a ruler), girt (surrounded), laurels (crowns of honor)","Read the poem aloud with conviction","Copywork: “Bids us serve the great To-day.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01122"}],"questions":["Who is the dead king, and who is the reigning queen, in this poem?","What will the Present do with the Past''s promises?"],"answers":["The Past is the dead king; the Present is the queen","Fulfill them"],"notes":"Poem by Adelaide Anne Procter."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-6-3","title":"Lesson XLIII: The Baptism","steps":["New words: kirk (church, in Scotland), chasm (a deep gorge), pellucid (crystal clear), sentinel (a lookout), Covenanters (Scottish Presbyterians who resisted the king''s control of their church)","Read the story aloud; slow down for the gorge, speed up for the soldiers and the flood","Written narration: in a paragraph, describe the ''church'' the Covenanters worshiped in"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01128"}],"questions":["Why did the congregation meet in a gorge and not in their kirk?","What signal warned them of danger?","What happened to the soldiers?"],"answers":["It was a time of persecution; their worship was forbidden","A stone fell into the pool and a plaid was hung out on a shepherd''s staff","A flash flood from a burst waterspout swept them away"],"notes":"By John Wilson (''Christopher North''). Set during the persecution of the Scottish Covenanters in the 1600s; the soldiers'' deaths are told as God''s judgment."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-6-4","title":"Lesson XLIV: Sparrows","steps":["New words: sires (fathers, ancestors), chitter (twitter), contrives (plans, arranges)","Read the poem aloud lightly, then again more thoughtfully for the last two stanzas","Copywork: “He planned for the birds, with the larger things.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01145"}],"questions":["What runs under the sparrows'' feet without their knowing?","What does the poet compare to sparrows on God''s telegraph strings?"],"answers":["The news of the world, carried by the telegraph","The thoughts, works, and prayers of people, the little things in our lives"],"notes":"Poem by Adeline D. T. Whitney. Explain the telegraph if needed."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-6-5","title":"Lesson XLV: Observance of the Sabbath","steps":["New words: retribution (punishment for wrongdoing), auxiliaries (helpers), profligacy (reckless wickedness), profanation (treating something holy with disrespect), squalid (filthy and wretched)","Read the sermon aloud in a clear, earnest voice","Written narration: in a paragraph, list the good effects Spring says keeping the Sabbath has on a nation"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01151"}],"questions":["What does Spring call the Sabbath in his picture of an arch?","What did Lord Chief Justice Hale notice about the criminals he sentenced?"],"answers":["The keystone of the arch that holds up the temple of virtue","Most said their wrongdoing began with neglecting the Sabbath"],"notes":"By Gardiner Spring, a New York minister. A strongly argued sermon; his claims about Sabbath-breakers and prosperity are opinion, worth discussing."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-6-6","title":"Lesson XLVI: God''s Goodness to Such as Fear Him","steps":["New words: fret (worry), iniquity (wickedness), inherit (receive as one''s own), bay tree (a spreading evergreen)","Read the psalm aloud slowly and reverently","Memorize “Trust in the Lord, and do good; so shalt thou dwell in the land, and verily thou shalt be fed.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01161"}],"questions":["Who shall inherit the earth, according to the psalm?","What has the old speaker never seen?"],"answers":["The meek (those who wait upon the Lord)","The righteous forsaken, or his children begging bread"],"notes":"From Psalm 37."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-6-7","title":"Lesson XLVII: Character of Columbus","steps":["New words: commensurate (equal in size), magnanimity (generosity of spirit), irascible (quick-tempered), seditions (revolts), opulent (wealthy)","Read Irving''s portrait aloud","Written narration: in a paragraph, describe Columbus''s character as Irving saw it, and one thing Columbus never knew about his discovery"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01170"}],"questions":["Whom does Irving blame for Columbus''s failure to build orderly colonies?","What did Columbus believe he had found, to the end of his life?"],"answers":["The unruly rabble he had to command","A new route to the rich lands of the East (Asia)"],"notes":"By Washington Irving, from his ''Life and Voyages of Columbus.'' Irving''s admiring portrait leaves out Columbus''s harsh treatment of the native peoples; older students can compare it with a modern account."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-6-8","title":"Lesson XLVIII: \"He Giveth His Beloved Sleep\"","steps":["New words: undisproved (not shown false), overweep (weep over), delved (dug), mummers (costumed actors)","Read Psalm 127:2, then read the poem aloud quietly","Copywork: the refrain “He giveth his beloved, sleep.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01181"}],"questions":["What gifts might we wish to give the people we love?","What does God give instead?"],"answers":["A hero''s courage, a poet''s harp, a patriot''s voice, a king''s crown","Sleep, peaceful rest"],"notes":"Poem by Elizabeth Barrett Browning, built on Psalm 127:2. ''Sleep'' here also means the rest of death."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-6-9","title":"Lesson XLIX: Description of a Siege","steps":["New words: ensign (a flag), craven (coward), barbacan (a fortified outwork guarding a gate), palisades (fences of stakes), postern (a small back gate)","Read the notes on castles first, then read the scene aloud with two voices, Rebecca and Ivanhoe","Draw a castle with keep, walls, moat, barbacan, and postern gate"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01192"}],"questions":["Why can Ivanhoe not see the battle himself?","Who falls in the fight on the breach?","Whom does Ivanhoe guess the Black Knight to be?"],"answers":["He is wounded and lying on a couch","Front de Boeuf","King Richard the Lion-Hearted"],"notes":"By Sir Walter Scott, from ''Ivanhoe.'' Rebecca''s horror at the bloodshed is a good discussion point."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-6-10","title":"Lesson L: Marco Bozzaris","steps":["New words: suppliance (humble begging), signet (a seal ring), Suliote (from the Suli Mountains of Greece), pestilence (a deadly plague), knell (a funeral bell)","Read the poem aloud, with a rousing voice for Bozzaris''s battle cry","Memorize Bozzaris''s cheer, from “Strike—till the last armed foe expires” to “God—and your native land!”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson L","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01206"}],"questions":["What was the Turk dreaming about?","Who won the battle, and what happened to Bozzaris?","How does Death sound to the hero, unlike to others?"],"answers":["Conquering Greece and sitting on his monarch''s throne","The Greeks won, but Bozzaris was killed","Like a prophet''s word, carrying the thanks of millions yet to be"],"notes":"Poem by Fitz-Greene Halleck about the Greek war of independence against the Ottoman Turks (1823)."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-u7","title":"Unit 7: Lessons LI–LX","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-7-1","title":"Lesson LI: Song of the Greek Bard","steps":["New words: bard (a poet-singer), lay (a song), degenerate (decline), phalanx (a close battle formation), Bacchanal (a reveler)","Read the notes, then read the song aloud; find Marathon, Salamis, and Thermopylae on a map of Greece","Memorize the stanza beginning “The mountains look on Marathon”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01217"}],"questions":["What does the bard mean by ''all, except their sun, is set''?","Why does he keep calling to ''dash down'' the wine?","What does the bard ask the earth to give back?"],"answers":["Greece''s glory is gone; only its sunny climate remains","Drinking and dancing are a shameful escape from fighting for freedom","Three of the three hundred Spartans who died at Thermopylae"],"notes":"By Lord Byron, from ''Don Juan'' (''The Isles of Greece''). Byron died helping the Greeks fight for independence; the book''s sketch of his life is frank about his faults."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-7-2","title":"Lesson LII: North American Indians","steps":["New words: embellishes (decorates), sedgy (grassy, marshy), progenitors (ancestors), chronicles (written histories), exterminators (destroyers)","Read the address aloud with feeling","Talk it over: what does Sprague get right about what happened to Native peoples, and what does he get wrong in his view of them?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01236"}],"questions":["What two kinds of seeds did the pilgrim ship bring, according to Sprague?","What does Sprague ask of those who write about the Indians'' fate?"],"answers":["Seeds of life (for the settlers) and of death (for the native peoples)","To be faithful to their virtues and pay due tribute to their unhappy fate"],"notes":"By Charles Sprague, Fourth of July address, Boston, 1825. Parent note: the speech mourns Native Americans but calls them ''ignorant'' and ''the poor child of nature'' and wrongly assumes they would vanish. Native nations are very much alive today; a good chance to discuss this."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-7-3","title":"Lesson LIII: Lochiel''s Warning","steps":["New words: seer (a prophet), array (battle order), dotard (a foolish old man), claymore (a Scottish broadsword), fagots (bundles of sticks for burning)","Read the notes, then read the poem aloud as a dialogue between the Seer and Lochiel","Memorize the line “And coming events cast their shadows before,” and use it in a sentence of your own"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01243"}],"questions":["What battle does the Seer foresee?","Who wins it?","What does Lochiel vow at the end?"],"answers":["Culloden (1746)","The English under the Duke of Cumberland; the Highlanders are routed","Never to flee or be taken; to fight or die with his feet to the foe"],"notes":"Poem by Thomas Campbell, about the Jacobite rising for Charles Stuart (''Bonnie Prince Charlie'')."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-7-4","title":"Lesson LIV: On Happiness of Temper","steps":["New words: disposed (inclined in mood), constitution (natural makeup), poignant (sharp, keen), construed (interpreted), sharper (a swindler), halter (a rope noose)","Read the essay aloud; enjoy the joke at the end","Written narration: in a paragraph, retell one of Goldsmith''s examples of a happy temper and say whether you think he is right"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01253"}],"questions":["What did Cardinal de Retz do while in prison?","What did Dick''s father leave him?","How did Dick answer?"],"answers":["Laughed at his troubles and wrote the life of his jailer","A shilling to buy a halter (a rope to hang himself)","“May heaven give you life and health to enjoy it yourself!”"],"notes":"By Oliver Goldsmith. The opening example, a chained and maimed slave who seems merry, treats slavery lightly; worth discussing."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-7-5","title":"Lesson LV: The Fortune Teller","steps":["New words: russet (reddish brown), wag (a joker), alms (charity), intelligencers (informers), stewardship (managing money for another)","Read the story aloud, with a cheerful, sly voice for the beggar","Narrate: how did the beggar become a fortune teller, and how does he gather his ''fortunes''?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01263"}],"questions":["Why did the beggar give up telling the truth about his misfortunes?","How does he learn things to tell people?","What did the dog do with Harley''s shilling?"],"answers":["People did not believe him or give him money without visible proof such as a wooden leg","He picks up names and gossip from servants and neighbors, and people tell him themselves","Snapped it up and gave it straight to his master"],"notes":"By Henry Mackenzie, from ''The Man of Feeling.''"},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-7-6","title":"Lesson LVI: Rienzi''s Address to the Romans","steps":["New words: thraldom (slavery, bondage), feudal (ruled by lords over their lands), rapine (plunder), limners (painters), distained (stained, dishonored)","Read the notes, then deliver the speech aloud with rising passion","Practice the speech for inflection and emphasis, as the book suggests"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01273"}],"questions":["What are the Romans slaves to, according to Rienzi?","What personal wrong spurred Rienzi to call for vengeance?","What does Rienzi swear at the end?"],"answers":["Petty tyrants and feudal lords","His gentle young brother was killed","That Rome shall be free again"],"notes":"From Mary Russell Mitford''s play ''Rienzi.'' Cola di Rienzi led a revolt against Rome''s nobles in 1347."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-7-7","title":"Lesson LVII: Character of the Puritan Fathers of New England","steps":["New words: allegorized (shown as a symbol or story), unalienable (unable to be taken away), theocratical (ruled by religious law), ecclesiastical (of the church), Pentateuch (the first five books of the Bible)","Read the essay aloud","Written narration: in a paragraph, explain how Greenwood says the Revolution grew out of the Puritans'' homes"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01283"}],"questions":["How was the Puritans'' view of liberty different from the ancients''?","Where was every settler''s ''school of independence''?","How did the Puritans get their name?"],"answers":["They did not make it a goddess; they held it as a birthright from the Creator","At his own hearth (fireside)","From their strict purity of manners and speech; it was first given in mockery"],"notes":"By F. W. P. Greenwood. A warm defense of the Puritans that passes over their intolerance of dissenters (compare Lesson XXIII)."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-7-8","title":"Lesson LVIII: Landing of the Pilgrim Fathers","steps":["New words: bark (a ship), exiles (people forced from home), hoary (gray with age), shrine (a holy place)","Read the poem aloud, steady and strong","Memorize the whole poem, a few stanzas a day this week"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01298"}],"questions":["How did the Pilgrims NOT come, according to the poem?","What did they seek in the new land?"],"answers":["Not as conquerors with drums and trumpets, nor as frightened fugitives","A faith''s pure shrine: freedom to worship God"],"notes":"Poem by Felicia Hemans. The Pilgrims landed at Plymouth in December 1620."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-7-9","title":"Lesson LIX: Necessity of Education","steps":["New words: inexorable (unstoppable), perpetuity (lasting forever), suffrage (the right to vote), foundering (sinking), imminence (closeness of danger)","Read the address aloud, giving force to the opening cry","Written narration: in a paragraph, explain why Beecher thought the West needed schools and churches quickly"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01312"}],"questions":["What does Beecher say will happen if we do not educate?","Why is the fear of patriots ''the star of hope''?"],"answers":["We will perish by our own prosperity","Fear stirs people to act, like wind that keeps a ship from foundering"],"notes":"By Lyman Beecher, a plea for schools and churches in the growing West of the 1830s."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-7-10","title":"Lesson LX: Riding on a Snowplow","steps":["New words: Cyclops (a one-eyed giant), windlass (a winch), momentum (force of motion), landlubber (a person unused to the sea)","Read the sketch aloud with lively expression","Narrate the trip from Buffalo to Erie as if you had been the passenger"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01324"}],"questions":["How many engines pushed the snowplow?","Why did the plow back up before each big drift?","What did the author resolve at the end?"],"answers":["Three","To get a running start and momentum for a hard blow","Never again to ''ride a hammer'' when three engines held the handle"],"notes":"By Benjamin F. Taylor. A humorous travel sketch of an early railroad snowplow."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-u8","title":"Unit 8: Lessons LXI–LXX","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-8-1","title":"Lesson LXI: The Quarrel of Brutus and Cassius","steps":["New words: itching palm (greed for bribes), choler (anger), testy (irritable), drachmas (Greek coins), indirection (dishonest means)","Read the notes on the Ides of March, then read the scene aloud with a partner","Narrate: what started the quarrel, and how was it made up?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01336"}],"questions":["What does Brutus accuse Cassius of?","Why does Brutus say he could not raise money himself?","How does the quarrel end?"],"answers":["Having an ''itching palm,'' selling offices for gold, and refusing him money","He would not take it from peasants by dishonest means","They are reconciled and clasp hands; Brutus forgives Cassius''s hot temper"],"notes":"From Shakespeare''s ''Julius Caesar,'' Act IV, Scene 3. This is the scene John Neal mocked the lisping boys for performing in Lesson XVI."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-8-2","title":"Lesson LXII: The Quack","steps":["New words: quack (a fake doctor), phlebotomize (bleed a patient), farrier (a horse doctor), pilchard (a small fish), Pandora (in Greek myth, the woman who opened the box of evils)","Read the scene aloud with three voices: Hostess, Lampedo, and Balthazar","Narrate: why were the hostess and the quack keeping Balthazar sick?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01345"}],"questions":["Why did the hostess want Balthazar kept another fortnight?","What did Balthazar make Lampedo do as punishment?","How did the hostess ''ease her conscience'' about her bad wine?"],"answers":["To keep earning money from his stay at her inn","Swallow his own pills","By always giving short measure"],"notes":"By John Tobin, from the comedy ''The Honeymoon.'' Old medicine like bleeding is part of the joke."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-8-3","title":"Lesson LXIII: Rip Van Winkle","steps":["New words: grizzled (gray-streaked), akimbo (hands on hips), austere (stern), tory (a supporter of the king), precipitation (great haste)","Read the note first to learn how Rip fell asleep, then read the scene aloud","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell the story of Rip''s return in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01355"}],"questions":["Why did the crowd shout ''A tory! a spy!''?","How did Rip finally learn who he was?","How long had Rip been asleep?"],"answers":["He called himself a loyal subject of the king, after the Revolution","Through his grown daughter, Judith Gardenier, and an old woman who recognized him","Twenty years"],"notes":"By Washington Irving, from ''The Sketch Book.'' Read the whole story aloud if you can; the book''s note calls Rip a drunkard, and the jab about his wife''s death is old-fashioned humor."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-8-4","title":"Lesson LXIV: Bill and Joe","steps":["New words: comrade (friend), ermined (trimmed with fur, as a judge''s robe), chaffing (teasing), hic jacet (Latin: here lies)","Read the poem aloud warmly, as one old friend to another","Memorize the first stanza"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01371"}],"questions":["What honors has Bill won?","What does Holmes say fame is like?","What do Bill and Joe care about more than tombstones?"],"answers":["Titles like Hon. and LL.D., a judge''s robe, worldwide fame","A flickering flame, or a gust of wind that lifts a pinch of dust","Being remembered in the hearts of those who love them"],"notes":"Poem by Oliver Wendell Holmes, written for his college classmates."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-8-5","title":"Lesson LXV: Sorrow for the Dead","steps":["New words: attributes (qualities), compunctious (regretful), assiduities (careful attentions), unrequited (not repaid), chaplet (a wreath)","Read the essay aloud slowly","Written narration: in a paragraph, give Irving''s lesson at the end and how you could act on it this week"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01384"}],"questions":["Why does Irving call this the one sorrow we do not want healed?","What lesson does he draw for how we treat the living?"],"answers":["Because remembering the dead is a form of love we cherish","Be more faithful and loving to the living while we can"],"notes":"By Washington Irving, from ''The Sketch Book.''"},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-8-6","title":"Lesson LXVI: The Eagle","steps":["New words: beetling (overhanging), imperial (of an empire), legions (Roman armies), clarions (trumpets), deluge (flood)","Read the poem aloud with a sweeping rhythm","Copywork: “Bird of the broad and sweeping wing! / Thy home is high in heaven”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01390"}],"questions":["What did the Roman legions carry the eagle on?","What was the ''one awful hour''?"],"answers":["Their standards (the eagle was the Roman battle standard)","The fall of Rome to the northern barbarians"],"notes":"Poem by James Gates Percival."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-8-7","title":"Lesson LXVII: Political Toleration","steps":["New words: toleration (allowing views you disagree with), minority (the smaller group), countenance (approve, allow), theoretic (based on theory, not fact)","Read Jefferson''s words aloud as an inaugural address","Memorize “We are all Republicans; we are all Federalists.” and talk about what it means","Written narration: in a paragraph, explain Jefferson''s ''sacred principle'' about the majority and the minority"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01399"}],"questions":["What must the will of the majority be, to be rightful?","Why does Jefferson let those who would dissolve the Union ''stand undisturbed''?","Why does he think this the strongest government on earth?"],"answers":["Reasonable, and it must protect the equal rights of the minority","Error of opinion is safe when reason is left free to combat it","Every citizen would rally to the law and treat attacks on public order as his own concern"],"notes":"From Thomas Jefferson''s First Inaugural Address (1801), after the bitter election of 1800."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-8-8","title":"Lesson LXVIII: What Constitutes a State?","steps":["New words: constitutes (makes up), battlement (a castle wall), endued (gifted), sovereign (supreme), empress (a female ruler)","Read the poem aloud as a question and answer","Memorize the second stanza, beginning “No:—men, high-minded men”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01410"}],"questions":["Name two things the poet says do NOT make a state.","What does make a state?"],"answers":["Walls, battlements, proud cities, harbors and navies, or glittering courts","High-minded people who know their duties and their rights, ruled by law"],"notes":"Poem by Sir William Jones."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-8-9","title":"Lesson LXIX: The Brave at Home","steps":["New words: sash (a band worn across the body), dissembles (hides), girds (fastens on), asunder (apart)","Read the poem aloud tenderly","Copywork: the last four lines of any stanza"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01414"}],"questions":["Which three women does the poem honor?","What does the poet say their hidden tears are equal to?"],"answers":["The maid, the wife, and the mother who send their men to war","The blood shed by soldiers on the field of battle"],"notes":"Poem by Thomas Buchanan Read, from ''The Wagoner of the Alleghanies.''"},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-8-10","title":"Lesson LXX: South Carolina","steps":["New words: zealous (eager and devoted), adversity (hard times), filial (as a child to a parent), whigs (American patriots in the Revolution), invincible (unconquerable)","Read the speech aloud with pride, as a senator defending his state","Narrate: what does Hayne say South Carolina did in the Revolution?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01420"}],"questions":["What does Hayne claim about South Carolina''s devotion to the Union?","Which two Revolutionary leaders does he name?"],"answers":["That no state has been more devoted or made greater sacrifices","Sumter and Marion"],"notes":"By Robert Y. Hayne, from his 1830 Senate debate with Daniel Webster. Hayne was a leading defender of ''states'' rights'' and nullification, ideas later tied to the defense of slavery and secession. Read with the next lesson, Webster''s reply."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-u9","title":"Unit 9: Lessons LXXI–LXXX","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-9-1","title":"Lesson LXXI: Massachusetts and South Carolina","steps":["New words: eulogium (praise), circumscribed (fenced in, limited), gangrened (rotted, poisoned), tithe (a tenth; a tiny bit), encomium (formal praise)","Read Webster''s life, then deliver the reply aloud with dignity","Memorize from “There is Boston, and Concord, and Lexington, and Bunker Hill” to “there they will remain forever.”","Written narration: in a paragraph, compare how Hayne and Webster each spoke about their states and the Union"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01428"}],"questions":["Why does Webster claim South Carolina''s heroes as his own?","What does Webster say Massachusetts needs instead of praise?","Where does he say liberty would fall, if it must fall?"],"answers":["They are Americans all; their fame belongs to the whole country, not one state","Nothing; just look at her history: Boston, Concord, Lexington, Bunker Hill","Beside its own cradle, on the very spot where it was born"],"notes":"By Daniel Webster, from his 1830 reply to Hayne, one of the most famous speeches in Senate history."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-9-2","title":"Lesson LXXII: The Church Scene from Evangeline","steps":["New words: sonorous (deep and loud), dissonant (harsh-sounding), forfeited (taken away as a penalty), imprecations (curses), tocsin (an alarm bell)","Read the note on the Acadians, then read the scene aloud in its long, rolling lines","Narrate: tell what the commander announced and how Father Felician calmed the people"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01441"}],"questions":["What did the king''s order take from the Acadians?","Who shouted against the English and was struck down?","What prayer did Father Felician lead?"],"answers":["Their lands, homes, and cattle; and they were to be sent away from the province","Basil the blacksmith","“O Father, forgive them!”"],"notes":"From Longfellow''s ''Evangeline'' (1847), about the expulsion of the French Acadians from Nova Scotia in 1755. Find Nova Scotia on a map."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-9-3","title":"Lesson LXXIII: Song of the Shirt","steps":["New words: dolorous (sorrowful), gusset (a piece sewn in to strengthen a seam), respite (a short rest), briny (salty)","Read the poem aloud, letting “Work! work! work!” sound tired and heavy","Copywork: “O God! that bread should be so dear, / And flesh and blood so cheap!”","Talk about what Hood wanted his readers to do"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01453"}],"questions":["What is the woman really sewing besides a shirt?","What are her wages?","What does she long for, just for one hour?"],"answers":["Her own shroud; the work is killing her","A bed of straw, a crust of bread, and rags","To breathe fresh air among flowers, with sky above and grass beneath"],"notes":"Poem by Thomas Hood (1843), written to protest the treatment of London''s seamstresses. The line about the ''barbarous Turk'' reflects old prejudice."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-9-4","title":"Lesson LXXIV: Diamond Cut Diamond","steps":["New words: cadi (a Muslim judge), Banian (a Hindu merchant), ruse (a trick), piaster (a Turkish coin), pasha (a Turkish governor)","Read the story aloud; pause before each clever answer","Narrate the two cases and how Omar won each"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01466"}],"questions":["What payment did Omar demand from Ali the jeweler?","How did Omar answer his father''s claim on the casket?","How did the cadi settle the second case?"],"answers":["Twenty pounds of winged ants'' wings, half male and half female","He offered a coin stamped with the sultan''s face; refusing it would insult the ruler","The Banian was to pay Mansour a hundred thousand piasters, the worth of the necklace"],"notes":"Adapted from ''Abdallah'' by Édouard Laboulaye. The description of the young Banian is a dated stereotype."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-9-5","title":"Lesson LXXV: Thanatopsis","steps":["New words: thanatopsis (a view of death), communion (close sharing), sepulcher (a tomb), patriarchs (founding fathers), quarry slave (a slave working in a stone quarry)","Read the poem aloud slowly; mark where the ''still voice'' of Nature begins","Memorize the closing lines, from “So live, that when thy summons comes” to the end"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01509"}],"questions":["What does Nature do for us in our dark thoughts?","Why does Bryant say we will not lie down alone?","How should we approach our grave, according to the last lines?"],"answers":["She steals away their sharpness with healing sympathy","All the great and good of past ages, and everyone who lives, lie in the same great tomb","Sustained by trust, like someone lying down to pleasant dreams"],"notes":"Poem by William Cullen Bryant, begun when he was about seventeen."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-9-6","title":"Lesson LXXVI: Indian Jugglers","steps":["New words: ingenuity (cleverness), dexterity (skill with the hands), lambent (flickering), pliancy (flexibility), fastidious (hard to please)","Read the essay aloud","Written narration: in a paragraph, explain why the juggler made Hazlitt think less of his own work. Is he fair to himself?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01520"}],"questions":["How many balls could the juggler keep up?","Which feat did Hazlitt think the police should stop?","What does Hazlitt say his own essays lack that the juggler has?"],"answers":["Four","Sword swallowing","Exact, flawless perfection that no one can find fault with"],"notes":"By William Hazlitt, from ''Table Talk.'' ''Indian'' here means from India."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-9-7","title":"Lesson LXXVII: Antony over Caesar''s Dead Body","steps":["New words: interred (buried), coffers (treasure chests), mutiny (revolt), testament (a will), vesture (clothing)","Read the notes, then deliver the speech aloud, watching how ''honorable'' changes in meaning each time","Memorize the opening, from “Friends, Romans, countrymen” to “So let it be with Caesar.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01532"}],"questions":["What three things does Antony give as proof Caesar was not ambitious?","How does Antony make the crowd want to hear the will?","What did the crowd cry out at the end?"],"answers":["He filled the treasury with ransoms, wept for the poor, and refused the crown three times","He keeps saying he must not read it, which makes them demand it","For revenge on the conspirators: “Burn! Fire! Kill! Slay!”"],"notes":"From Shakespeare''s ''Julius Caesar,'' Act III, Scene 2. A master class in irony and persuasion."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-9-8","title":"Lesson LXXVIII: The English Character","steps":["New words: aristocracy (the noble class), tenantry (tenant farmers), Nimrod (a mighty hunter), arrogance (pride that looks down on others), John Bull (a nickname for the typical Englishman)","Read the essay aloud","Written narration: in a paragraph, list the good qualities and the faults Prescott found in the English"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01553"}],"questions":["Where, says Prescott, is the Englishman seen at his best?","What fault makes the Englishman unpopular abroad?","What does Prescott mean by ''The body travels, not the mind''?"],"answers":["In his country home, at his fireside and farm","His air of national superiority, his arrogance","English travelers go everywhere but come home with their ideas and prejudices unchanged"],"notes":"By William H. Prescott, the historian, from a letter written while visiting England. Some remarks (on women, on ''Hottentots,'' on the ''superficial Frenchman'') are broad stereotypes of his era."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-9-9","title":"Lesson LXXIX: The Song of the Potter","steps":["New words: marl (a clay-rich soil), wax and wane (grow and shrink), kindred (related by birth), potsherds (broken pieces of pottery)","Read the poem aloud with a turning, even rhythm like a potter''s wheel","Memorize the stanza beginning “Turn, turn, my wheel! The human race”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01562"}],"questions":["What lesson does the potter draw from the clay?","What does the furnace flame do to the vessels?"],"answers":["All people, whatever their rank or race, are made of the same clay and are kin","Tests them and stamps them with honor or shame"],"notes":"Poem by Henry Wadsworth Longfellow, from ''Kéramos.''"},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-9-10","title":"Lesson LXXX: A Hot Day in New York","steps":["New words: labyrinth (a maze), languor (weakness, listlessness), truculence (fierceness), somnambulist (sleepwalker), apothecary (druggist)","Read the scene aloud; let your voice sound heavy with the heat","Narrate: describe the man with sunstroke in the drugstore and how he was treated"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01572"}],"questions":["What did the thermometer at the drugstore door read?","How was the sunstruck man being cooled?","Why does Basil say he would rather have an accident in New York?"],"answers":["Ninety-seven degrees","His wife rubbed his head with a handkerchief full of pounded ice","The biggest place is both the cruelest and the kindest; there a good Samaritan is sure to be near"],"notes":"By William Dean Howells, from the novel ''Their Wedding Journey.'' ''Stages'' were horse-drawn omnibuses."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-u10","title":"Unit 10: Lessons LXXXI–XC","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-10-1","title":"Lesson LXXXI: Discontent.—An Allegory","steps":["New words: allegory (a story with a hidden meaning), ruminating (thinking over), chimerical (imaginary), fardel (a bundle), commodious (convenient)","Read the allegory aloud; picture the mountain of troubles","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell what happened when people swapped their troubles, and Addison''s lesson"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01587"}],"questions":["Who led each mortal to the heap of troubles?","What did no one throw on the heap?","Who made the heap shrink, and how big did it become?"],"answers":["Fancy, carrying a magnifying glass","A single vice or folly","Patience; it shrank to less than a third of its size"],"notes":"By Joseph Addison, from ''The Spectator.'' One man throws down ''his wife'' as a burden, an old joke."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-10-2","title":"Lesson LXXXII: Jupiter and Ten","steps":["New words: portly (stout), sagacious (wise; used here as a joke), patron (a supporter), Lempriere (an old dictionary of mythology)","Read the poem aloud and save the surprise for the last line","Write the title as Mrs. Chub read it and as it really was; explain the mix-up"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01608"}],"questions":["What did Mrs. Chub say her painting was called?","What was its real title?"],"answers":["Jupiter and Ten","Jupiter and Io (she misread ''Io'' as the number 10)"],"notes":"Humorous poem by James T. Fields, poking fun at people who show off a learning they do not have."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-10-3","title":"Lesson LXXXIII: Scene from \"The Poor Gentleman\"","steps":["New words: baronet (a British title below a lord), valet (a man''s personal servant), flummery (empty flattery), syllogism (a three-step logical argument), indigence (poverty)","Read the scene aloud with three readers: Sir Robert, Humphrey, and Frederic","Narrate: what kind of man is Sir Robert under his gruffness?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01623"}],"questions":["Why does Humphrey answer Sir Robert''s question with only ''Hem''?","Why did Sir Robert quarrel with his brother Job?","What does Frederic rush off to do at the end?"],"answers":["If he contradicts him he lies, and if he agrees Sir Robert picks a fight","Job was so meek he never argued back","To visit and help Lieutenant Worthington, the poor disabled officer"],"notes":"From George Colman''s comedy ''The Poor Gentleman.''"},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-10-4","title":"Lesson LXXXIV: My Mother''s Picture","steps":["New words: maternal (motherly), beguiled (fooled), dupe (one who is easily fooled), contemplation (quiet thought)","Read Cowper''s life, then read the poem aloud gently","Copywork: “Oh that those lips had language! Life has passed / With me but roughly since I heard them last.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01732"}],"questions":["How old was Cowper when his mother died?","What did the maids promise the little boy?","What does Cowper boast of instead of noble birth?"],"answers":["Six years old","That his mother would soon come back","Being the son of parents who have gone to heaven"],"notes":"Poem by William Cowper, written on receiving his mother''s portrait. The book''s life sketch mentions his mental illness and suicide attempt; preview it for younger readers."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-10-5","title":"Lesson LXXXV: Death of Samson","steps":["New words: livery (a servant''s uniform), timbrels (tambourines), cataphracts (soldiers in full armor), thrall (a captive slave), immixed (mixed in among)","Read Judges 16:23–30, then read Milton''s account aloud","Narrate the scene as the Hebrew messenger who saw it"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01739"}],"questions":["Who tells the story of Samson''s death in this passage?","What did Samson ask his guide to let him do?","Who escaped the destruction?"],"answers":["A Hebrew who happened to be in Gaza, telling Samson''s father, Manoah","Lean on the two great pillars that held up the roof, saying he was tired","Only the common people standing outside"],"notes":"From John Milton''s ''Samson Agonistes.'' Milton wrote it after he had gone blind."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-10-6","title":"Lesson LXXXVI: An Evening Adventure","steps":["New words: gig (a light two-wheeled carriage), withes (flexible twigs used as ties), yeomanry (small farmers), altercation (a heated argument), sophistry (clever but false reasoning)","Read the story aloud; keep the surprise for the end","Narrate: what did the young lawyers assume about the old man, and how were they proved wrong?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01748"}],"questions":["Why did the travelers think the old man was a plain farmer?","What were the young men arguing about?","Who turned out to be the old gentleman?"],"answers":["His gig was mended with hickory bark and his clothes were plain and careless","The merits of the Christian religion (pulpit versus courtroom eloquence)","John Marshall, Chief Justice of the United States"],"notes":"The author is not named in the book. A good lesson on not judging by appearances."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-10-7","title":"Lesson LXXXVII: The Barefoot Boy","steps":["New words: pantaloons (trousers), jaunty (lively, carefree), eschewing (avoiding), Hesperides (in Greek myth, keepers of the golden apples), moil (drudgery)","Read the poem aloud cheerfully","Memorize the opening lines, from “Blessings on thee, little man” to “I was once a barefoot boy!”","List five things the barefoot boy learned from nature, not from school"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01758"}],"questions":["What does the boy have that the rich man cannot buy?","Where does the boy get his knowledge?","What does the poet fear will happen to the boy''s feet when he grows up?"],"answers":["Outward sunshine and inward joy","From nature: bees, birds, flowers, berries, and animals","They will be shut in shoes and set to work in the mills of toil"],"notes":"Poem by John Greenleaf Whittier, Quaker poet and abolitionist."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-10-8","title":"Lesson LXXXVIII: The Glove and the Lions","steps":["New words: valor (courage), ramped (reared up), smother (a thick cloud of dust), quoth (said)","Read the ballad aloud with a galloping rhythm","Narrate the story and give the king''s verdict in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01767"}],"questions":["Why did the lady drop her glove among the lions?","What did the Count do with the glove?","What did King Francis say about the lady''s test?"],"answers":["To prove the Count''s love and win glory for herself","Fetched it and threw it in her face","That vanity, not love, sets love a task like that"],"notes":"Poem by Leigh Hunt. The king is Francis I of France."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-10-9","title":"Lesson LXXXIX: The Folly of Intoxication","steps":["New words: bestial (like a beast), fustian (pompous nonsense), inordinate (excessive), importune (beg), Hydra (a many-headed monster of myth)","Read the note on Iago and Cassio, then read the scene aloud with a partner","Copywork: “Oh that men should put an enemy in their mouths to steal away their brains!”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01774"}],"questions":["What does Cassio grieve over losing?","What advice does Iago give him?","Is Iago really Cassio''s friend?"],"answers":["His reputation","To beg Desdemona, the general''s wife, to win his place back","No; he is the villain who got Cassio drunk to ruin him"],"notes":"From Shakespeare''s ''Othello,'' Act II, Scene 3. Chosen for its warning against drunkenness, a frequent theme in the McGuffey Readers."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-10-10","title":"Lesson XC: Starved Rock","steps":["New words: plummet (a weight on a line for sounding depth), palisade (a fence of stakes), timorous (fearful), cantonments (camps), aegis (protection)","Read the description aloud","Find Starved Rock on the Illinois River on a map; sketch the cliff and the valley from Parkman''s words"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XC","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01796"}],"questions":["Why did La Salle choose the rock for his fort?","Whom did the Illinois tribes see La Salle as protecting them from?","How did Starved Rock get its name, according to the legend in the notes?"],"answers":["It was steep on three sides and could be climbed only by one narrow path","The Iroquois","A band of Illinois Indians besieged there by the Potawatomi starved (or died of thirst)"],"notes":"By Francis Parkman, the historian of French America. Some wording reflects 19th-century views of Native peoples."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-u11","title":"Unit 11: Lessons XCI–C","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-11-1","title":"Lesson XCI: Prince Henry and Falstaff","steps":["New words: sack (a sweet wine), buckram (stiff cloth), doublet (a close-fitting jacket), strappado (an instrument of torture), extempore (made up on the spot)","Read the scene aloud with a group, one reader for Falstaff and one for the Prince","Keep a tally as Falstaff tells his story: how many ''men in buckram'' does he claim?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XCI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01807"}],"questions":["How many men in buckram does Falstaff''s story grow to?","What really happened at the robbery?","What excuse does Falstaff give at the end?"],"answers":["From two, to four, to seven, nine, and eleven","The Prince and Poins, just two, robbed the four robbers, and Falstaff ran away roaring","He knew it was the Prince and would not harm the true heir; he was ''a coward on instinct''"],"notes":"From Shakespeare''s ''Henry IV, Part 1,'' Act II, Scene 4. Falstaff drinks and swears an oath that insults Jews, both of their time; worth a word."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-11-2","title":"Lesson XCII: Studies","steps":["New words: affectation (showing off), contemn (scorn), confute (prove wrong), conference (discussion), subtle (sharp, keen)","Read the essay aloud slowly; it is packed with ideas","Copywork: “Some books are to be tasted, others to be swallowed, and some few to be chewed and digested.”","Written narration: in a paragraph, explain one of Bacon''s sayings with an example from your own reading"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XCII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01871"}],"questions":["What are the three uses of studies?","What, says Bacon, should we read for?","What does reading make a man? conference? writing?"],"answers":["Delight, ornament, and ability","To weigh and consider, not to contradict, believe blindly, or find talk","Reading makes a full man; conference a ready man; writing an exact man"],"notes":"By Sir Francis Bacon, from his ''Essays.''"},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-11-3","title":"Lesson XCIII: Surrender of Granada","steps":["New words: barb (a Barbary horse), ecclesiastic (a churchman), Te Deum (a hymn of praise), mien (bearing, appearance), stoical (bearing pain without complaint)","Read the notes, then read the chapter aloud; make the final scene slow","Find Granada on a map of Spain; talk about why 1492 was a turning point"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XCIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01878"}],"questions":["Who was Boabdil?","To whom did Ferdinand give the keys of Granada?","What is the place where Boabdil wept still called?"],"answers":["The last Moorish king of Granada","Queen Isabel (Isabella)","The Last Sigh of the Moor"],"notes":"From Bulwer-Lytton''s novel ''Leila, or the Siege of Granada.'' Granada fell to Spain in 1492."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-11-4","title":"Lesson XCIV: Hamlet''s Soliloquy","steps":["New words: soliloquy (a speech to oneself), consummation (an ending), contumely (insulting treatment), quietus (release, death), bourn (boundary)","Read the soliloquy aloud slowly and thoughtfully","Memorize the opening lines, to “And by opposing end them?”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XCIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01900"}],"questions":["What question is Hamlet weighing?","What keeps people bearing their troubles rather than escaping them?"],"answers":["Whether it is better to live and endure troubles or to die","Dread of what comes after death, ''the undiscovered country''"],"notes":"From Shakespeare''s ''Hamlet,'' Act III, Scene 1. Hamlet is thinking about ending his life; be ready to talk about it with care."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-11-5","title":"Lesson XCV: Ginevra","steps":["New words: heirloom (an object handed down in a family), coronet (a small crown), decorum (proper behavior), nuptial (of a wedding), ambush (a hidden trap)","Read the poem aloud as a traveler''s tale, slowing down for the discovery","Narrate the story of Ginevra from the wedding feast to the opening of the chest"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XCV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01902"}],"questions":["What happened to Ginevra at her wedding feast?","How was the mystery solved fifty years later?","What was the ''ambush'' in the chest?"],"answers":["She vanished during a game of hide-and-seek and was never found","The old chest burst open while being moved, revealing her skeleton, jewels, and ring","A spring lock that shut her in"],"notes":"Poem by Samuel Rogers, from ''Italy.'' A sad, eerie tale based on a legend."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-11-6","title":"Lesson XCVI: Inventions and Discoveries","steps":["New words: circumnavigated (sailed around), diffused (spread widely), ascendency (dominance), maximum (the greatest amount), impious (lacking reverence)","Read the essay aloud","Written narration: in a paragraph, list the inventions Calhoun names and what each did for the world. What would he add today?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XCVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01918"}],"questions":["What new power does Calhoun say these inventions gave rise to?","What does he credit the mariner''s compass with?","Did he think inventions would end up helping or harming mankind?"],"answers":["Public opinion, with the press as its voice","Making it possible to sail around and explore the globe and spread commerce","Helping; to doubt it would be impious"],"notes":"By John C. Calhoun, from ''A Disquisition on Government.'' Parent note: the book''s sketch says plainly that Calhoun defended slavery as ''neither morally nor politically wrong.'' His view that gunpowder settled the contest between ''civilization and barbarism'' is also worth discussing."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-11-7","title":"Lesson XCVII: Enoch Arden at the Window","steps":["New words: harassed (troubled), allured (drew in), shingle (beach pebbles), burnished (polished), swoon (faint)","Read the note first, then read the poem aloud quietly","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell what Enoch saw through the window and what he decided"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XCVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01931"}],"questions":["Why had Enoch been gone so long?","Whom did Enoch see by Philip''s fireside?","What did Enoch resolve as he walked home?"],"answers":["He was shipwrecked on a lonely island and thought dead","His wife Annie, now married to Philip, their baby, and his own grown children","Never to tell her or let her know he was alive"],"notes":"From Alfred Tennyson''s ''Enoch Arden'' (1864). A story of self-sacrifice; he dies soon after of a broken heart."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-11-8","title":"Lesson XCVIII: Lochinvar","steps":["New words: dauntless (fearless), laggard (a slowpoke), craven (cowardly), galliard (a lively dance), croup (a horse''s rump)","Read the ballad aloud with galloping speed","Memorize the first stanza, “Oh, young Lochinvar is come out of the west”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XCVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01941"}],"questions":["Why did Lochinvar arrive too late?","What did he ask to do at the wedding?","How did he carry off the bride?"],"answers":["The bride had already agreed to wed another man","Dance one dance and drink one cup of wine with the bride","He swung her onto his horse at the hall door and galloped away"],"notes":"By Sir Walter Scott, a song from ''Marmion.''"},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-11-9","title":"Lesson XCIX: Speech on the Trial of a Murderer","steps":["New words: opprobrium (disgrace, scorn), precedent (an earlier example), depravity (wickedness), bludgeon (a club), poniard (a dagger)","Read the note on the Salem case, then read the speech aloud","Copywork: “The secret which the murderer possesses soon comes to possess him.”","Written narration: in a paragraph, explain why Webster says such a secret can never be safe"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XCIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01953"}],"questions":["What made this murder different from most?","Why, says Webster, will such a crime be discovered?","What does the guilty conscience do to the murderer?"],"answers":["It was cool and calculated, done only for pay, not anger or revenge","Thousands of eyes and minds are searching, and Providence orders that ''murder will out''","Torments him until the secret demands to be confessed"],"notes":"By Daniel Webster, from the 1830 Salem trial for the murder of Captain Joseph White. The murder is described in detail, and the speech mentions suicide; preview it."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-11-10","title":"Lesson C: The Closing Year","steps":["New words: knell (the toll of a funeral bell), cadences (rhythms), specter (a ghost), serried (in close ranks), blazonry (bright display)","Read the poem aloud solemnly, as at midnight on New Year''s Eve","Copywork: three lines that you find most striking"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson C","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01962"}],"questions":["At what hour is the poem set?","What does the poet compare to the condor that rests at night?"],"answers":["Midnight at the end of the year","Time, which, unlike the condor, never rests"],"notes":"Poem by George D. Prentice, editor of the ''Louisville Journal.'' A good reading for New Year''s Eve."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-u12","title":"Unit 12: Lessons CI–CX","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-12-1","title":"Lesson CI: A New City in Colorado","steps":["New words: plateau (high, flat land), gulch (a narrow ravine), apertures (openings), candelabrum (a branched candlestick), impromptu (made up on the spot)","Read the sketch aloud; let the hammers ''clink'' in your voice","Narrate: describe Perry''s Hotel and the ''bedrooms'' in it"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01970"}],"questions":["How old was Garland City when the author arrived?","Why were houses not going up even faster?","How had the landlord moved his hotel?"],"answers":["Twelve days old","The sawmills could not cut lumber fast enough","He took it down over his guests'' heads at Wagon Creek and was putting it up again at Garland"],"notes":"By Helen Hunt Jackson, from ''Bits of Travel at Home.'' She later wrote ''A Century of Dishonor'' about wrongs done to Native Americans."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-12-2","title":"Lesson CII: Importance of the Union","steps":["New words: benign (kindly), copious (plentiful), precipice (a cliff edge), belligerent (warlike), peroration (the closing part of a speech)","Read the speech aloud, building to the final words","Memorize “Liberty and Union, now and forever, one and inseparable!” and the sentence leading up to it"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01992"}],"questions":["What does Webster say the Union grew out of?","What does he pray never to see?","What two mottoes does he reject?"],"answers":["Hard times: disordered finances, ruined commerce, and credit","The Union broken, the states at war, the land drenched in fraternal blood","“What is all this worth?” and “Liberty first, and Union afterwards”"],"notes":"By Daniel Webster, the close of his 1830 reply to Hayne. Thirty years later the Civil War he feared came."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-12-3","title":"Lesson CIII: The Influences of the Sun","steps":["New words: terrestrial (of the earth), organic (living), inorganic (not living), projectile (something thrown or fired), Proteus (a sea god who could change shape)","Read the essay aloud","Written narration: trace one chain of energy from the sun to something you used today (food, a car, electricity)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id01999"}],"questions":["What two kinds of earthly power does Tyndall leave out of the sun''s work?","How does the sun lift rivers to the mountains?","What is the point of the comparison with the clock?"],"answers":["Volcanoes and tides","By evaporating water that falls as rain and snow on the heights","As a clock''s power comes from the hand that wound it, all earthly power comes from the sun"],"notes":"By John Tyndall, physicist. Accurate for its day; today we would add nuclear and geothermal energy to his exceptions."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-12-4","title":"Lesson CIV: Colloquial Powers of Franklin","steps":["New words: colloquial (of conversation), vociferous (loud and forceful), adventitious (added from outside), coruscations (flashes of light), felicity (happy skill)","Read the essay aloud","Narrate: compare Franklin''s way of talking with Dr. Johnson''s, as Wirt describes them"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02007"}],"questions":["Why were Wirt and Franklin shut indoors for weeks?","How did Johnson''s conversation differ from Franklin''s?","How did Franklin make what he read his own?"],"answers":["Deep, constant snows","Johnson''s was a stormy contest for victory; Franklin''s was plain, easy, and unaffected","He read and thought deeply and worked what he learned into something more valuable"],"notes":"By William Wirt, U.S. Attorney General and biographer of Patrick Henry."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-12-5","title":"Lesson CV: The Dream of Clarence","steps":["New words: inestimable (too valuable to measure), perjury (lying under oath), Furies (spirits of vengeance), requites (repays), environed (surrounded)","Read the notes on the Wars of the Roses, then read the scene aloud with a partner","Narrate Clarence''s dream in three parts: the fall overboard, the sea floor, and the land of the dead"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02018"}],"questions":["Who knocked Clarence overboard in his dream?","What did he see on the bottom of the sea?","Which two spirits accused him after death?"],"answers":["His brother Gloster (Gloucester)","Wrecks, drowned men, and gold, pearls, and jewels scattered, some in dead men''s skulls","Warwick, his father-in-law, and Prince Edward, whom he stabbed at Tewkesbury"],"notes":"From Shakespeare''s ''Richard III,'' Act I, Scene 4. A dark, vivid scene; Clarence is murdered shortly afterward."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-12-6","title":"Lesson CVI: Homeward Bound","steps":["New words: taffrail (the rail at a ship''s stern), halyards (ropes for raising sails), studding sail (an extra sail set beside the regular ones), knots (sea miles per hour), forecastle (the forward part of a ship)","Read the passage aloud with the energy of a ship running before a gale","Find Cape Horn and the Falkland Islands on a map; trace the ship''s way home to Boston"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02027"}],"questions":["Why were the sailors so excited?","What did the mate keep saying to the ship?","How fast was the ship going at four bells?"],"answers":["The captain turned the ship north, straight for Boston, with Cape Horn behind them","“Hurrah, old bucket! The Boston girls have got hold of the towrope!” (and ''As long as she cracks, she holds!'')","Eleven knots"],"notes":"By Richard Henry Dana Jr., from ''Two Years Before the Mast'' (1840). Lots of sailing terms; enjoy them rather than define every one."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-12-7","title":"Lesson CVII: Impeachment of Warren Hastings","steps":["New words: impeachment (a formal charge against a public official), grenadiers (elite soldiers), proconsul (a governor of a province), arraign (accuse formally), misdemeanors (wrongdoings)","Read the scene aloud; let Burke''s final lines ring out","Written narration: in a paragraph, describe the trial scene as if you were in the gallery"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02042"}],"questions":["Where was Hastings tried?","Who opened the case against him, and how long did his speech take?","In whose names did Burke impeach Hastings?"],"answers":["In Westminster Hall (the great hall of William Rufus)","Edmund Burke, over four sittings of the court","The House of Commons, the English nation, the people of India, and human nature itself"],"notes":"By Thomas Babington Macaulay. Hastings, governor-general of British India, was acquitted in 1795 after a seven-year trial."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-12-8","title":"Lesson CVIII: Destruction of the Carnatic","steps":["New words: convention (an agreement), incorrigible (unable to be reformed), animosities (hatreds), exigency (an emergency), decorum (good taste, propriety)","Read the notes first, then read Burke''s speech aloud","Find Madras (Chennai) and the Carnatic coast on a map of India"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02055"}],"questions":["Why did Hyder Ali decide to lay waste the Carnatic?","What did British armies find when they marched through it later?","Why does Burke stop describing the famine?"],"answers":["The East India Company''s men broke every treaty and could not be trusted","Not one man, woman, child, or animal; only silence","The details were too horrible to describe with decency"],"notes":"By Edmund Burke, 1785, blaming the East India Company''s greed for this catastrophe. Strong descriptions of war and famine."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-12-9","title":"Lesson CIX: The Raven","steps":["New words: quaint (old-fashioned and odd), surcease (an end), obeisance (a bow), Pallas (Athena, goddess of wisdom), nepenthe (a drug that eases grief)","Read the poem aloud, enjoying its rhyme and repetition; take turns by stanza","Memorize the first stanza, “Once upon a midnight dreary…”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02070"}],"questions":["Whom is the speaker mourning?","Where does the raven perch?","What is the only word the raven says?"],"answers":["Lenore, a lost maiden","On a bust of Pallas above his chamber door","“Nevermore”"],"notes":"Poem by Edgar Allan Poe (1845). Moody and dark, but a favorite to read aloud."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-12-10","title":"Lesson CX: A View of the Colosseum","steps":["New words: prostrated (laid flat), gladiatorial (of gladiators, who fought for show), amphitheater (an oval arena with rising seats), lambent (softly glowing), coronet (a small crown)","Read the description aloud","Look at a picture of the Colosseum and describe it in your own words, as Dewey did by moonlight"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02095"}],"questions":["What happened in the Colosseum''s arena long ago?","What sounds did Dewey hear inside the ruin at night?","What did the moonlit Colosseum look like from outside?"],"answers":["Gladiator fights and the martyrdom of Christians","Only bats, a distant carriage, and a convent bell","A coronet of the heavens, a crown on the brow of night"],"notes":"By Orville Dewey, a Unitarian minister, from his travels."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-u13","title":"Unit 13: Lessons CXI–CXX","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-13-1","title":"Lesson CXI: The Bridge","steps":["New words: goblet (a drinking cup), eddying (swirling), brine (salt water), encumbered (weighed down)","Read the poem aloud slowly and quietly","Copywork: “And only the sorrow of others / Throws its shadow over me.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02107"}],"questions":["What does the moon''s reflection look like to the poet?","How have the poet''s feelings changed since his younger days on the bridge?","Whom does he think of when he crosses the bridge now?"],"answers":["A golden goblet falling and sinking into the sea","His own heavy burden is gone; only the sorrows of others shadow him now","The thousands of care-burdened people who have crossed it since, young and old"],"notes":"Poem by Henry Wadsworth Longfellow, about the bridge between Boston and Cambridge."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-13-2","title":"Lesson CXII: Objects and Limits of Science","steps":["New words: opportune (well-timed), nebulae (cloudy patches of stars or gas), strata (layers of rock), Leviathan and Behemoth (great beasts described in Job), solvents (substances that dissolve)","Read the address aloud, giving each ''Let them…'' its own weight","Written narration: in a paragraph, explain what Winthrop says science may freely do and where he says it must stop. Do you agree?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02123"}],"questions":["Name three things Winthrop invites scientists to do.","Where does he say science should not go?"],"answers":["Find new planets, track comets, forecast storms, relieve pain with ether, study rock layers and fossils (any three)","It should not try to search out God or test the mysteries of faith"],"notes":"By Robert C. Winthrop, Speaker of the U.S. House. A 19th-century view of science and faith worth discussing."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-13-3","title":"Lesson CXIII: The Downfall of Poland","steps":["New words: pandours and hussars (kinds of fierce soldiers), van (the front of an army), presaging (foretelling), Sarmatia (an old name for Poland''s region), tocsin (an alarm bell)","Read the poem aloud with rising feeling","Memorize the last two lines, “Hope, for a season, bade the world farewell, / And Freedom shrieked as Kosciusko fell!”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02132"}],"questions":["Who was Warsaw''s last champion?","What oath did his warriors swear?","Which three powers attacked Poland, according to the notes?"],"answers":["Kosciusko","To live for their country and die with her; ''Revenge or death''","Russia, Prussia, and Austria"],"notes":"From Thomas Campbell''s ''The Pleasures of Hope.'' Kosciusko had fought for America in the Revolution."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-13-4","title":"Lesson CXIV: Labor","steps":["New words: productive industry (work that makes useful goods), vocation (a calling, a job), buttress (a support), diversity (variety), disparity (inequality)","Read the essay aloud","Written narration: in a paragraph, explain why Greeley says every child should learn a useful trade. What practical skill would you choose?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02139"}],"questions":["Why does Greeley want even future lawyers and poets to learn a trade?","Why are regions with only one kind of work usually poor?","What did Webster mean by ''There is plenty of room higher up''?"],"answers":["So they always have something to fall back on and need never compromise their honesty","One occupation cannot use the different abilities of men, women, old, young, strong, and weak","There is always work for people of real skill and ability"],"notes":"By Horace Greeley, editor of the ''New York Tribune.'' His figures and comparisons of states are from the 1800s."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-13-5","title":"Lesson CXV: The Last Days of Herculaneum","steps":["New words: solace (comfort), fettered (chained), ominous (threatening), frenzy (wild excitement), corse (a corpse)","Read the notes first, then read the poem aloud with feeling","Find Vesuvius, Pompeii, and Herculaneum on a map of Italy"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02146"}],"questions":["Why was the soldier in a dungeon?","What had happened to the boy?","How did the father and son die?"],"answers":["For some daring deed that broke the law","He was killed by lightning while the earth shook","The ground moved the boy''s body into his arms, and the prison fell into the opening earth"],"notes":"From Edwin Atherstone''s ''The Last Days of Herculaneum.'' The eruption of Vesuvius buried the city in A.D. 79. A sad and dramatic poem."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-13-6","title":"Lesson CXVI: How Men Reason","steps":["New words: saccharine (sugary sweet), placid (calm), stringent (sharp, harsh), windfalls (fruit blown down by the wind)","Read the essay aloud in a chatty, humorous voice","Narrate: explain Holmes''s comparison of people to pears"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02163"}],"questions":["Why does Holmes say he would not trust praise from a critic over fifty?","How are people like pears?"],"answers":["Older authors tend to stop finding fault and praise everything","Some ripen early, some late; a hard, sour one may turn out to be a fine winter pear, so don''t judge too quickly"],"notes":"By Oliver Wendell Holmes, from ''The Autocrat of the Breakfast-Table.''"},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-13-7","title":"Lesson CXVII: Thunderstorm on the Alps","steps":["New words: placid (calm), concentered (gathered together), phosphoric (glowing), cleaves (cuts through), thwarted (blocked)","Read the notes on Lake Geneva, then read the stanzas aloud: quietly at first, then with the storm","Memorize the stanza beginning “The sky is changed!”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02176"}],"questions":["How does the poet feel about the calm lake?","What mountains answer the Alps in the storm?","What does Byron compare the two heights split by the Rhone to?"],"answers":["It warns him to leave earth''s troubles for something purer, like a sister''s gentle rebuke","The Jura","Two lovers who parted in anger and can never meet again"],"notes":"From Lord Byron''s ''Childe Harold''s Pilgrimage,'' Canto III."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-13-8","title":"Lesson CXVIII: Origin of Property","steps":["New words: dominion (rule, control), primeval (of the earliest times), usufructuary (having the use, but not the ownership, of a thing), sequacious (inclined to follow; tame), concomitants (things that go along with something)","Read the essay aloud in sections; stop to summarize each step","Written narration: in a paragraph, trace Blackstone''s steps from shared land to private property and government"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02184"}],"questions":["What was the first kind of ''ownership'' Blackstone describes?","Why did farming lead to owning land?","What Bible story does he use about sharing land?"],"answers":["A right to use a thing only as long as one was using it, like a seat in a theater","No one would till the soil if another could seize the harvest","Abraham and Lot separating to the left and right"],"notes":"By Sir William Blackstone, from his ''Commentaries on the Laws of England.'' A challenging reading; take two sittings if needed."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-13-9","title":"Lesson CXIX: Battle of Waterloo","steps":["New words: revelry (merrymaking), chivalry (knights; brave gentlemen), knell (a death bell), impetuous (rushing), verdure (green growth)","Read the notes, then read the stanzas aloud; change from the dance to the cannon","Memorize the first stanza, “There was a sound of revelry by night”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02200"}],"questions":["Where were the officers when the cannon began?","What did the dancers first think the sound was?","What does the last stanza say happened in a single day?"],"answers":["At a ball in Brussels","The wind, or a carriage rattling over the street","They went from life and dancing to battle and death, buried together, friend and foe"],"notes":"From Lord Byron''s ''Childe Harold''s Pilgrimage.'' Waterloo, June 18, 1815, ended Napoleon''s rule."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-13-10","title":"Lesson CXX: \"With Brains, Sir\"","steps":["New words: dilettante (a dabbler in an art), commodity (a useful thing), chiaroscuro (light and shade in a painting), aesthetics (the study of beauty)","Read the essay aloud","Copywork: “Suppose you look again.”","Try it: look at a picture or object for five minutes and write down what you see on the second look"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02209"}],"questions":["What did Opie say he mixed his colors with?","What did Etty answer when a student asked what something meant?","Why does the author think these short answers were best?"],"answers":["“With brains, sir.”","“Suppose you look,” and then “Suppose you look again.”","They made students think and see for themselves instead of being told"],"notes":"By Dr. John Brown, author of ''Rab and His Friends.'' A favorite essay for Charlotte Mason–minded families."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-u14","title":"Unit 14: Lessons CXXI–CXXX","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-14-1","title":"Lesson CXXI: The New England Pastor","steps":["New words: verdant (green), hallowed (holy), swains (country youths), engrossed (completely occupied), steep (a steep slope)","Read the poem aloud gently","Narrate: describe the pastor''s house, his looks, and his one care"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02220"}],"questions":["How long had the pastor served?","What did his face look like, according to the poet?","What was his only care?"],"answers":["Forty years (''twice twenty years'')","Grave and wise, glowing with an ''October light,'' like evening","To bless and save his people and point them to heaven"],"notes":"From a poem by Timothy Dwight, president of Yale College and grandson of Jonathan Edwards."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-14-2","title":"Lesson CXXII: Death of Absalom","steps":["New words: succor (help), shekels (Hebrew silver coins), tidings (news), compassed about (surrounded)","Read the chapter aloud; give David''s cry at the end its full sorrow","Read 2 Samuel 15 in your Bible to learn why Absalom rebelled"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02225"}],"questions":["What order did David give his captains about Absalom?","How was Absalom caught?","Which runner told David the truth about Absalom?"],"answers":["Deal gently with the young man for David''s sake","His head caught in the branches of an oak as his mule ran on","Cushi (Ahimaaz did not say)"],"notes":"From the Bible, 2 Samuel 18."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-14-3","title":"Lesson CXXIII: Abraham Davenport","steps":["New words: sagas (old Norse tales), inexorable (unbending), adjourn (end a meeting), alewives (a kind of herring), self-poised (calm and balanced)","Read the note on the Dark Day, then read the poem aloud","Memorize from “Let God do his work, we will see to ours” to “Bring in the candles.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02241"}],"questions":["What happened on May 19, 1780?","What did some lawmakers want to do?","What business did the legislature go on with?"],"answers":["A strange darkness covered New England at midday","Adjourn, thinking it was Judgment Day","An act to regulate the shad and alewive fisheries"],"notes":"Poem by John Greenleaf Whittier. The Dark Day was probably caused by smoke from huge forest fires."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-14-4","title":"Lesson CXXIV: The Falls of the Yosemite","steps":["New words: perpendicular (straight up and down), rampart (a protective wall), declivity (a downward slope), cataract (a great waterfall), serpentine (snaking)","Read the description aloud","Look up a photo of Yosemite Falls and compare it to King''s description"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02249"}],"questions":["How high is the Yosemite fall, according to King?","What shape does the upper fall take as it widens?","What does the wind sometimes do to the fall?"],"answers":["Nearly 2,500 feet, in three leaps","A comet","Gets behind it and swings or twists it like a pendulum of lace"],"notes":"By Thomas Starr King. Yosemite Falls is in fact among the tallest in North America, though not the highest in the world as he believed."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-14-5","title":"Lesson CXXV: A Psalm of Life","steps":["New words: numbers (lines of verse), bivouac (a temporary camp), sublime (noble, grand), forlorn (lonely, hopeless)","Read the poem aloud with energy","Memorize the whole poem over the week, a few stanzas each day"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02264"}],"questions":["What does the poet say life is NOT?","What do the lives of great men remind us?","What are the last instructions of the poem?"],"answers":["An empty dream","That we too can make our lives noble and leave footprints on the sands of time","Be up and doing; achieve and pursue; learn to labor and to wait"],"notes":"Poem by Henry Wadsworth Longfellow, one of the most memorized poems in American schools."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-14-6","title":"Lesson CXXVI: Franklin''s Entry into Philadelphia","steps":["New words: wharf (a landing place for boats), fatigued (very tired), draught (a drink), accosting (going up to and speaking to), reputable (respectable)","Read Franklin''s account aloud","Written narration: in a paragraph, tell Franklin''s first day in Philadelphia as if you were the young Miss Read watching from her door"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02274"}],"questions":["How much money did Franklin have when he arrived?","What did he do with the three rolls?","Where did he first fall asleep in Philadelphia?"],"answers":["A Dutch dollar and about a shilling in copper","Ate one, carried two under his arms, then gave the other two to a woman and child from the boat","In the Quaker meetinghouse"],"notes":"From Benjamin Franklin''s ''Autobiography.'' He was seventeen."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-14-7","title":"Lesson CXXVII: Lines to a Waterfowl","steps":["New words: fowler (a bird hunter), plashy (marshy, splashy), marge (edge), illimitable (boundless), zone (a region of the earth)","Read the poem aloud, then reread the last stanza slowly","Copywork the last stanza"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02287"}],"questions":["What guides the bird along its pathless way?","What lesson does the poet take for himself?"],"answers":["A Power (God) who cares for it","The same One who guides the bird will lead his steps aright on his own lonely way"],"notes":"Poem by William Cullen Bryant, written when he was a young man uncertain of his future."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-14-8","title":"Lesson CXXVIII: Goldsmith and Addison","steps":["New words: wayward (willful), truant (wandering from duty), minstrel (a traveling singer), eccentricities (odd habits), misanthrope (a hater of people)","Read both portraits aloud; read Addison''s hymn verses slowly","Narrate: why, according to Thackeray, do readers love Goldsmith?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02296"}],"questions":["Which of Goldsmith''s works were based on his boyhood home of Lissoy?","What does Thackeray say our love for Goldsmith is half made of?","Whose life does he call one of the most enviable?"],"answers":["''The Deserted Village'' (Auburn) and ''The Vicar of Wakefield''","Pity","Joseph Addison''s"],"notes":"By William Makepeace Thackeray, from ''The English Humourists of the Eighteenth Century.'' The lesson has two parts, ''1. Goldsmith'' and ''II. Addison.''"},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-14-9","title":"Lesson CXXIX: Immortality of the Soul","steps":["New words: immortality (living forever), conjectures (guesses), bane (something that destroys), antidote (a cure)","Read the soliloquy aloud slowly","Talk it over: what reasons does Cato give for believing the soul lives on?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02312"}],"questions":["What book is Cato reading?","What does Cato call ''doubly armed''?","What will outlast the stars and the sun?"],"answers":["Plato''s book on the immortality of the soul","His sword (death) and his book (the hope of life after death)","The soul"],"notes":"From Joseph Addison''s tragedy ''Cato.'' The speech comes just before Cato takes his own life; read ahead and be ready to talk about it."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-14-10","title":"Lesson CXXX: Character of Washington","steps":["New words: benignant (kindly), apposite (fitting), affable (friendly), sagacity (wisdom), subterfuge (trickery)","Read the portrait aloud","Written narration: in a paragraph, choose three of Washington''s traits from Sparks and give an example of each from what you know of his life"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02318"}],"questions":["What does Sparks say was perhaps Washington''s most remarkable trait?","What could Washington never forgive easily?","What was his strongest passion?"],"answers":["Self-control","Dishonesty and trickery (subterfuge)","Love of his country"],"notes":"By Jared Sparks, Washington''s early biographer. An admiring portrait that leaves out that Washington held enslaved people; older students may want to discuss this."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-u15","title":"Unit 15: Lessons CXXXI–CXXXVIII","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-15-1","title":"Lesson CXXXI: Eulogy on Washington","steps":["New words: eulogy (a speech of praise for the dead), compeer (an equal, companion), despondency (low spirits), effulgence (radiance)","Read the notes on the battles, then deliver the eulogy aloud","Memorize “First in war, first in peace, and first in the hearts of his countrymen.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02330"}],"questions":["Who gave this eulogy, and when?","Whom did Washington bring to surrender at the end of the war?","How did Washington face his death?"],"answers":["General Henry (''Light-Horse Harry'') Lee, at Congress''s request in 1799","Cornwallis, at Yorktown","With calm serenity; not a sigh or groan despite great pain"],"notes":"By Henry Lee, father of Robert E. Lee."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-15-2","title":"Lesson CXXXII: The Solitary Reaper","steps":["New words: solitary (alone), vale (valley), plaintive (sad, mournful), Hebrides (islands off western Scotland), lay (a song)","Read the poem aloud softly","Memorize the last four lines, from “I listened motionless and still”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02343"}],"questions":["What is the Highland girl doing as she sings?","Why can the poet not understand her song?","What did he carry away with him?"],"answers":["Reaping and binding grain alone in the field","She sings in Gaelic, a language he does not know","The music in his heart, long after he could no longer hear it"],"notes":"Poem by William Wordsworth, written after a walking tour in Scotland."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-15-3","title":"Lesson CXXXIII: Value of the Present","steps":["New words: aliment (food, nourishment), illusory (deceptive), dupe (one who is fooled), dizened (dressed up showily), bivouacs (camps out)","Read the essay aloud slowly; Emerson packs a thought into every sentence","Copywork: “Write it on your heart that every day is the best day in the year.”","Written narration: in a paragraph, explain Emerson''s idea that the gods ''come in low disguises,'' using his examples"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02351"}],"questions":["What illusion, says Emerson, hides the value of the present hour?","Name two examples he gives of greatness hidden in humble form.","What did Bonaparte say about a general''s troops?"],"answers":["That the present hour is not the important, decisive one; we always think we are doing something less than our best task","Odin in a fisher''s hut, Apollo with shepherds, Jesus born in a barn with fishermen for friends (any two)","A general always has troops enough if he knows how to use the ones he has"],"notes":"By Ralph Waldo Emerson, from ''Works and Days.'' One of the hardest readings in the book; read it in two sittings if needed."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-15-4","title":"Lesson CXXXIV: Happiness","steps":["New words: competence (enough to live on), temperance (self-control), prunella (a cloth used for shoes), huzzas (cheers)","Read the verses aloud, pausing at each couplet","Memorize “Honor and shame from no condition rise; / Act well your part, there all the honor lies.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02361"}],"questions":["In what three words does Pope say happiness lies?","Does God change his laws for good people, according to Pope?","What does Pope call ''the noblest work of God''?"],"answers":["Health, peace, and competence","No; gravitation does not stop for a good man passing under a falling rock","An honest man"],"notes":"From Alexander Pope''s ''Essay on Man.'' The book''s sketch of Pope''s character is frank."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-15-5","title":"Lesson CXXXV: Marion","steps":["New words: impregnable (unconquerable), partisan (a guerrilla fighter), circumspection (caution), fords (shallow river crossings), privation (hardship, want)","Read the description aloud","Written narration: in a paragraph, describe how Marion kept his plans secret and how his men guessed them"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02373"}],"questions":["What served as Marion''s moat and walls?","Why did Marion prefer fords to bridges?","How did his men know a long march was coming?"],"answers":["The swamp was his moat and the deep ravines his walls","Bridges helped the enemy and warned them he was coming","They watched his cook preparing extra portable food; and he usually set out at sunset"],"notes":"By William Gilmore Simms, from ''The Life of Francis Marion,'' the ''Swamp Fox'' of the Revolution. See also Lesson LXX."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-15-6","title":"Lesson CXXXVI: A Common Thought","steps":["New words: solemn (serious, grave), musky (heavy-scented), zenith (the highest point of the sky)","Read the poem aloud slowly","Talk it over: why might the book''s editors call this poem almost a prophecy?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02388"}],"questions":["What ''solemn day'' is the poet thinking of?","At what time of day does he imagine his death?"],"answers":["The day of his own death","At dawn, as the day breaks"],"notes":"Poem by Henry Timrod, the South Carolina poet, who died young in poverty after the Civil War, much as the poem imagines. A brief poem about death."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-15-7","title":"Lesson CXXXVII: A Definite Aim in Reading","steps":["New words: definite (clear, exact), affinity (natural attraction), omnivorous (taking in everything), indiscriminate (without choosing), nucleus (a center)","Read the essay aloud","Make a plan: choose one subject and list three or four different kinds of books (history, story, diary, poem) you could read about it","Written narration: in a paragraph, give Porter''s main rules for reading and say which one you will try"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02395"}],"questions":["What question does Porter say we should often ask ourselves?","Why do self-educated readers often do better than others?","What is the ''iron in the fire'' rule?"],"answers":["“Why am I reading this, and why now?”","They know what they are reading for and choose books with a purpose","Always have one solid book in hand that you carry forward every day until finished"],"notes":"By Noah Porter, president of Yale, from ''Books and Reading.'' A natural fit for a family that reads living books."},{"id":"mcguffey-sixth-reader-15-8","title":"Lesson CXXXVIII: Ode to Mt. Blanc","steps":["New words: sovran (sovereign, supreme), ebon (black), invulnerable (unable to be harmed), hierarch (a chief priest), incense (sweet smoke offered in worship)","Read the hymn aloud, building to the great answer “God!”","Find Mont Blanc, the highest peak in the Alps, on a map","Memorize the last line, “Earth, with her thousand voices, praises God.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson CXXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16751/pg16751-images.html#id02411"}],"questions":["What questions does the poet ask the mountain, the torrents, and the icefalls?","What answer does he want all of nature to give?","What does he ask the mountain to tell the sky, stars, and sun?"],"answers":["Who made them, gave them their strength, light, and beauty","God!","That Earth, with her thousand voices, praises God"],"notes":"By Samuel Taylor Coleridge (''Hymn Before Sunrise, in the Vale of Chamouni''). The last selection in the book; a fitting close to the year."}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"“McGuffey''s Sixth Eclectic Reader, Revised Edition” by William Holmes McGuffey (1879), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/16751. Lesson plan by Freerange."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (listing_id, version) do update set content = excluded.content;
insert into public.listings (id, seller_name, seller_kind, seller_verified, title, subjects, grade_min, grade_max, philosophies, format, price, description, includes, lessons, lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement, family_style, lesson_steps, cover, color, esa_eligible, created_at, package_id, package_version, license, attribution, outline) values ('l-lib-mcguffey-third-reader', 'William Holmes McGuffey', 'open', false, 'McGuffey''s Third Eclectic Reader', array['reading']::text[], 2, 3, array['charlotte_mason', 'classical', 'traditional', 'faith_based']::text[], 'online', 0, 'The classic Third Reader: 79 stories, fables, poems, and nature lessons, from the boy who cried wolf and the clever way to weigh an elephant to beavers, bats, hummingbirds, and Wordsworth''s “We Are Seven.” Each day your child learns a few new words, reads the lesson aloud, and narrates it back or learns a few lines of a poem by heart. The full text is free online.', array['80 lessons in 10 units: the introduction plus all 79 Third Reader lessons', 'Link to the free full text for every lesson', 'Key new words, a reading activity, and narration, copywork, or memory work each day', 'Comprehension questions with answers', 'Poem authors, dated content, and the two untranscribed script lessons flagged in the notes']::text[], 80, 4, 20, 'medium', false, '{}'::text[], '🦉', '#E07A5F', false, '2026-11-11T12:00:00.000Z', 'pk-lib-mcguffey-third-reader', 1, '{"kind":"public_domain"}'::jsonb, '“McGuffey''s Third Eclectic Reader, Revised Edition” by William Holmes McGuffey (1879), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/14766. Lesson plan by Freerange.', '[{"title":"Unit 1: Introduction and Lessons I–VIII","lessons":["Getting Started: Sounds, Emphasis, and Punctuation","Lesson I: The Shepherd Boy","Lesson II: Johnny''s First Snowstorm","Lesson III: Let It Rain","Lesson IV: Castle-Building","Lesson V: Castle-Building (Concluded)","Lesson VI: Lend a Hand","Lesson VII: The Truant","Lesson VIII: The White Kitten"]},{"title":"Unit 2: Lessons IX–XVI","lessons":["Lesson IX: The Beaver","Lesson X: The Young Teacher","Lesson XI: The Blacksmith","Lesson XII: A Walk in the Garden","Lesson XIII: The Wolf","Lesson XIV: The Little Bird''s Song","Lesson XV: Harry and Annie","Lesson XVI: Bird Friends"]},{"title":"Unit 3: Lessons XVII–XXIV","lessons":["Lesson XVII: What the Minutes Say","Lesson XVIII: The Widow and the Merchant","Lesson XIX: The Birds Set Free","Lesson XX: A Moment Too Late","Lesson XXI: Humming Birds","Lesson XXII: The Wind and the Sun","Lesson XXIII: Sunset","Lesson XXIV: Beautiful Hands"]},{"title":"Unit 4: Lessons XXV–XXXII","lessons":["Lesson XXV: Things to Remember","Lesson XXVI: Three Little Mice","Lesson XXVII: The New Year","Lesson XXVIII: The Clock and the Sundial","Lesson XXIX: Remember","Lesson XXX: Courage and Cowardice","Lesson XXXI: Weighing an Elephant","Lesson XXXII: The Soldier"]},{"title":"Unit 5: Lessons XXXIII–XL","lessons":["Lesson XXXIII: The Echo","Lesson XXXIV: George''s Feast","Lesson XXXV: The Lord''s Prayer","Lesson XXXVI: Finding the Owner","Lesson XXXVII: Bats","Lesson XXXVIII: A Summer Day","Lesson XXXIX: I Will Think of It","Lesson XL: Charlie and Rob"]},{"title":"Unit 6: Lessons XLI–XLVIII","lessons":["Lesson XLI: Ray and His Kite","Lesson XLII: Beware of the First Drink","Lesson XLIII: Speak Gently","Lesson XLIV: The Seven Sticks","Lesson XLV: The Mountain Sister","Lesson XLVI: Harry and the Guidepost","Lesson XLVII: The Money Amy Didn''t Earn","Lesson XLVIII: Who Made the Stars?"]},{"title":"Unit 7: Lessons XLIX–LVI","lessons":["Lesson XLIX: Deeds of Kindness","Lesson L: The Alarm Clock","Lesson LI: Spring","Lesson LII: True Courage","Lesson LIII: The Old Clock","Lesson LIV: The Waves","Lesson LV: Don''t Kill the Birds","Lesson LVI: When to Say No"]},{"title":"Unit 8: Lessons LVII–LXIV","lessons":["Lesson LVII: Which Loved Best?","Lesson LVIII: John Carpenter","Lesson LIX: Persevere","Lesson LX: The Contented Boy","Lesson LXI: Little Gustava","Lesson LXII: The Insolent Boy","Lesson LXIII: We Are Seven","Lesson LXIV: Mary''s Dime"]},{"title":"Unit 9: Lessons LXV–LXXII","lessons":["Lesson LXV: Mary Dow","Lesson LXVI: The Little Loaf","Lesson LXVII: Susie and Rover","Lesson LXVIII: The Violet","Lesson LXIX: No Crown for Me","Lesson LXX: Young Soldiers","Lesson LXXI: How Willie Got Out of the Shaft","Lesson LXXII: The Pert Chicken"]},{"title":"Unit 10: Lessons LXXIII–LXXIX","lessons":["Lesson LXXIII: Indian Corn","Lesson LXXIV: The Snowbird''s Song","Lesson LXXV: Mountains","Lesson LXXVI: A Child''s Hymn","Lesson LXXVII: Holding the Fort","Lesson LXXVIII: The Little People","Lesson LXXIX: Good Night"]}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set seller_name = excluded.seller_name, seller_kind = excluded.seller_kind, seller_verified = excluded.seller_verified, title = excluded.title, subjects = excluded.subjects, grade_min = excluded.grade_min, grade_max = excluded.grade_max, philosophies = excluded.philosophies, format = excluded.format, price = excluded.price, description = excluded.description, includes = excluded.includes, lessons = excluded.lessons, lessons_per_week = excluded.lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson = excluded.minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement = excluded.parent_involvement, family_style = excluded.family_style, lesson_steps = excluded.lesson_steps, cover = excluded.cover, color = excluded.color, esa_eligible = excluded.esa_eligible, package_id = excluded.package_id, package_version = excluded.package_version, license = excluded.license, attribution = excluded.attribution, outline = excluded.outline;
insert into public.package_releases (listing_id, version, content) values ('l-lib-mcguffey-third-reader', 1, '{"units":[{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-u1","title":"Unit 1: Introduction and Lessons I–VIII","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-1-1","title":"Getting Started: Sounds, Emphasis, and Punctuation","steps":["Say the long and short vowel sounds from the tables together, then a few subvocals and aspirates","Read the emphasis questions aloud, stressing the word that answers each one (“Did you ride to town? No, I walked.”)","Find a hyphen, comma, dash, quotation marks, and an apostrophe on the Punctuation page and say what each one does"],"links":[{"label":"Articulation","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00024"},{"label":"Emphasis","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00042"},{"label":"Punctuation","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00047"}],"questions":["What is emphasis?","What does an apostrophe show?"],"answers":["Saying some words with more force than others because the meaning depends on them","That letters are left out (o''er, ''t is), or who owns something (Helen''s book)"],"notes":"The book''s introductory pages. Return to the sound tables for a minute or two of drill before lessons all year."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-1-2","title":"Lesson I: The Shepherd Boy","steps":["New words: pasture, brooklet, crook, glossy, horse-chestnuts, jackstones","Read the poem aloud to your child, then take turns reading a stanza each","Narrate: what were Roy''s ''sheep,'' ''cows,'' and ''pasture'' really made of?","Copywork: “And the sparkle was all in his eyes.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson I","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00061"}],"questions":["Where was Roy really playing?","What were Roy''s sheep?","What did big brother Walter do while Roy slept?"],"answers":["Indoors, at a table in a house in town, on a rainy day","Snowy-white pebbles from the seashore","Cut the cows (horse-chestnuts) into baskets and turned the sheep (pebbles) into jackstones"],"notes":"Poem by Emily S. Oakey. Jackstones is an old name for jacks. Let your child set up a pretend farm from things in the house afterward."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-1-3","title":"Lesson II: Johnny''s First Snowstorm","steps":["New words: snowstorm, orange groves, joyfully, feathery, several","Let your child read the lesson aloud; you read Johnny''s mother''s lines","Narrate: what did Johnny think the snow was before his mother explained?","If it''s snowing, catch a flake on dark cloth and look with a magnifying glass"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson II","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00079"}],"questions":["Why had Johnny never seen snow?","What did the snowflake look like under the glass?","Which did Johnny decide he liked better, snowballs or oranges?"],"answers":["He had lived in a warm country","A pretty little star","Snowballs"]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-1-4","title":"Lesson III: Let It Rain","steps":["New words: quench, thirst, shocking, wreaths, ground (as in a mill)","Read it as a play: you are Father, your child is Rose","Narrate: list all the good things the rain does that Father helped Rose see","Copywork: “I am truly very glad to see the rain falling.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson III","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00087"}],"questions":["Why was Rose unhappy at the start?","How does rain help make bread?"],"answers":["It was raining and she had to stay indoors","It makes the wheat grow, and water turns the mill that grinds the wheat into flour"],"notes":"A dialogue: point out how the speaker''s name comes before each speech."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-1-5","title":"Lesson IV: Castle-Building","steps":["New words: dismay, mimic, fiercely, passion, foundation","Read the lesson aloud together, a paragraph each","Narrate: what happened to Herbert''s castle, and what did he do next?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson IV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00092"}],"questions":["What knocked down Herbert''s castle?","Who protected the cat?","What did Papa do to help?"],"answers":["The cat rubbed against it","His little sister Hetty","Sat on the floor and began building a new castle with him"],"notes":"Part 1 of 2; the story finishes in the next lesson."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-1-6","title":"Lesson V: Castle-Building (Concluded)","steps":["New words: retorted, heedlessly, guard, forehead","Let your child read the lesson aloud; help with the conversation parts","Narrate: what does ''crying over spilled milk'' mean, and how did Papa say to handle trouble?","Copywork: “If your castles get knocked down, build them up again.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson V","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00097"}],"questions":["What had made Joe cry once?","What should Herbert do if the cat knocks the castle down again?"],"answers":["His kite string broke and he lost his kite","Build it up again"]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-1-7","title":"Lesson VI: Lend a Hand","steps":["Look at the Lesson VI heading in the online text; the script page itself is not included","If you have a print or scanned copy, read the script poem together and copy two lines","Otherwise, talk about one way your child can ''lend a hand'' at home today and do it"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson VI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00104"}],"questions":["What does it mean to ''lend a hand''?"],"answers":["To help someone"],"notes":"Script lesson: the Gutenberg edition has only the title (the cursive page is not transcribed). Use a print copy or treat this as a short helping-day lesson."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-1-8","title":"Lesson VII: The Truant","steps":["New words: truant, falsely, haste, ignorant, conduct","Read it once aloud for your child, then have them read it to you","Narrate: tell what happened to James on the day he went to the boats"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson VII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00108"}],"questions":["What does it mean to ''play truant''?","Where did James get his money?","Who saved the boys?"],"answers":["To stay away from school without permission","From his aunt","Some men who rowed out to them in a boat"]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-1-9","title":"Lesson VIII: The White Kitten","steps":["New words: frolic, counsel, needful, beggar-like","Read the poem aloud with expression; let your child read it back","Memorize: the last two lines, “It is needful that they / Should this counsel obey, / And be careful in choosing their places to play.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson VIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00113"}],"questions":["How did the kitten look when she came home at night?","What lesson does the poem give children?"],"answers":["Dirty, with gray streaks and a black nose","Be careful where you choose to play (and keep yourself good)"],"notes":"Poem: read it twice, the second time with feeling."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-u2","title":"Unit 2: Lessons IX–XVI","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-2-1","title":"Lesson IX: The Beaver","steps":["New words: chiefly, material, commence, gnawing, trappers, inhuman","Your child reads the lesson aloud; stop after each paragraph to picture it","Narrate: how does a beaver build its dam and hut?","Look at a picture of a beaver lodge online or in a book"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson IX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00122"}],"questions":["Why do beavers build near water?","What do beavers eat in winter?","Why didn''t the gentleman shoot the young beavers?"],"answers":["They swim more easily than they walk","The bark they peel and store","Their play reminded him of children he knew, and it seemed cruel"],"notes":"Fur trapping was ordinary in 1879; talk about how we protect wild animals today."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-2-2","title":"Lesson X: The Young Teacher","steps":["New words: amuse, inquire, venture, confused, parcels, copybook","Read the lesson together, taking turns by paragraph","Narrate: how did Charles use his free hour, and what did his father find?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson X","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00130"}],"questions":["Why couldn''t Joe go to school?","What did Charles''s father think he was doing at first?","What did Charles''s father give the boys?"],"answers":["His parents were too poor","Going out on the river in the fisherman''s boat","Books, writing paper, pens, and ink"],"notes":"Joe''s mother ''kept her wheel going'': she spun thread on a spinning wheel to earn money."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-2-3","title":"Lesson XI: The Blacksmith","steps":["New words: forge, aweary, shrink, intense","Read the poem aloud, tapping a ''hammer'' on the table for each “Clink, clink”","Memorize: the first stanza","Copywork: “We''ll work each form / While the iron is warm”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00138"}],"questions":["Why is a blacksmith''s face black?","What does the blacksmith make with many links?"],"answers":["From the soot and smoke of the forge fire","A chain"],"notes":"Poem. ''Strike while the iron is hot'' comes from blacksmith work: talk about what it means."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-2-4","title":"Lesson XII: A Walk in the Garden","steps":["New words: gravel, meddle, borders, admiring, rare","Let your child read the lesson aloud slowly; help only with hard words","Narrate: compare Frank with the boy at the gate"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00145"}],"questions":["What did Frank''s mother ask him to promise?","Why wouldn''t the gardener let the other boy in?","What did the gardener give Frank and his mother?"],"answers":["Not to meddle with anything in the garden","He had meddled with the flowers and picked rare fruit the day before","Some pretty flowers"]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-2-5","title":"Lesson XIII: The Wolf","steps":["New words: forest, village, earnest, dismay, deceived","Read the lesson aloud together","Narrate: tell the story of the boy who cried wolf in your own words","Copywork: “The truth itself is not believed, / From one who often has deceived.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00152"}],"questions":["Why did John cry ''Wolf!'' the first time?","What happened when the wolf really came?"],"answers":["To have some fun","No one came to help, and the wolf killed many sheep, including John''s lamb"],"notes":"This is the old fable of the boy who cried wolf."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-2-6","title":"Lesson XIV: The Little Bird''s Song","steps":["New words: melody, modest, unnoticed, Maker","Read the poem aloud; let your child read Gracie''s words and you read Father''s","Memorize: the last stanza, “So live, my child, all through your life …”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00158"}],"questions":["Why couldn''t the people see the bird?","What lesson did Father want Gracie to learn?"],"answers":["It was small and plain brown, so it didn''t show","To do good without trying to be seen or praised"],"notes":"Poem."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-2-7","title":"Lesson XV: Harry and Annie","steps":["New words: exploring, pretend, thaw, good-naturedly, disobedient","Read the lesson aloud, a paragraph each","Narrate: what did Harry say to excuse himself, and was he right?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00165"}],"questions":["What did mother ask the children not to do?","Who saved Annie?","What did Harry do when he got home?"],"answers":["Not to cross the pond, because the ice was thawing","A man working near the shore","Told his mother how disobedient he had been"],"notes":"A good time to go over ice safety rules for your family."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-2-8","title":"Lesson XVI: Bird Friends","steps":["New words: grove, rod (a measure), faith, crumbs, bedpost","Read it aloud to your child, then let them read their favorite part back","Narrate: how did the old man win the birds'' trust?","Put out crumbs or seed and watch who comes"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00169"}],"questions":["Why did the birds follow the old man''s rake?","How did the birds wake him in the morning?","What made the birds trust him?"],"answers":["To catch the worms in the fresh dirt","They sat on his bedpost and chirped","His kind eyes and voice"],"notes":"Adapted from Elihu Burritt. A rod is about 16½ feet."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-u3","title":"Unit 3: Lessons XVII–XXIV","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-3-1","title":"Lesson XVII: What the Minutes Say","steps":["New words: furnished, unseen, track","Read the short poem aloud twice","Memorize: the whole poem (eight lines)","Set a one-minute timer and see how much you can tidy in it"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00176"}],"questions":["How many ''wings'' does each minute have?","What happens to someone who loses minutes?"],"answers":["Sixty (seconds)","They lose whole years"],"notes":"Poem."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-3-2","title":"Lesson XVIII: The Widow and the Merchant","steps":["New words: merchant, widow, assistance, hesitation, convinced, check","Let your child read the lesson aloud; you read the banker''s lines","Narrate: tell what happened at the bank, step by step"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00181"}],"questions":["How much did the widow ask for?","Why did the widow go back instead of keeping the money?","How much did she get in the end?"],"answers":["Five dollars","She was honest and thought it was a mistake","Five hundred dollars"],"notes":"Dollar amounts are from 1879; five dollars was then several days'' wages."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-3-3","title":"Lesson XIX: The Birds Set Free","steps":["New words: prisoners, apiece, bargain, resolved","Read the lesson together","Narrate: why did the man let the birds go?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00185"}],"questions":["How much did each bird cost?","How much did the man pay for them all?","Where had the man been a prisoner?"],"answers":["Fifty cents","Five dollars","In a French prison, as a prisoner of war"]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-3-4","title":"Lesson XX: A Moment Too Late","steps":["New words: downy, disturbed, petals, sped","Read the poem aloud; your child reads stanzas 5 and 6","Copywork: “Better be an hour early, and stand and wait”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00189"}],"questions":["What happened to the bird''s nest?","What did the bee miss?"],"answers":["The wind blew it away","The honey had already dropped from the flower"],"notes":"Poem. ''Staid'' is an old spelling of ''stayed.''"},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-3-5","title":"Lesson XXI: Humming Birds","steps":["New words: crest, brilliant, adorn, fibers, instinct, approach","Your child reads the lesson aloud","Narrate: describe the hummingbird''s nest and eggs","Watch a short slow-motion video of hummingbird wings"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00198"}],"questions":["Why are they called humming birds?","How big were the eggs?","How does the mother bird protect her nest?"],"answers":["Their fast-moving wings make a humming sound","About as large as a small sugarplum","She darts at you and pecks at your eyes"]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-3-6","title":"Lesson XXII: The Wind and the Sun","steps":["New words: dispute, blast, mantle, sultry, forcibly, fable","Read the fable aloud, then act it out with a blanket as the cloak","Narrate: tell the fable in three sentences"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00202"}],"questions":["What contest did the Wind and the Sun agree on?","Who won?","What is the lesson?"],"answers":["To see who could make a traveler take off his cloak","The Sun","Gentleness often succeeds where force fails"],"notes":"An Aesop fable."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-3-7","title":"Lesson XXIII: Sunset","steps":["Look at the Lesson XXIII heading in the online text; the script page is not transcribed","If you have a print copy, read the script lines and copy one","Otherwise, watch tonight''s sunset together and name its colors"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00206"}],"questions":["What colors do you see in a sunset?"],"notes":"Script lesson: the Gutenberg edition has only the title. Use a print copy, or make it a sunset-watching day."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-3-8","title":"Lesson XXIV: Beautiful Hands","steps":["New words: coarse, opinion, deeds, cheerfully, ashamed","Read it as a conversation: you are Miss Roberts, your child is Daisy","Narrate: list the things Mary''s hands do","Copywork: “The good alone are really beautiful.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00209"}],"questions":["Why did Daisy think Mary''s hands were ugly?","Why did Miss Roberts call them beautiful?"],"answers":["They were red and hard","They are always busy doing kind, helpful work, and do it gladly"]}]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-u4","title":"Unit 4: Lessons XXV–XXXII","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-4-1","title":"Lesson XXV: Things to Remember","steps":["New words: protection, peevish, pout, manners, secret","Read the lesson aloud, one numbered rule each","Narrate: choose three rules and say them in your own words","Pick one rule to practice all week"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00215"}],"questions":["How should you ask for food at the table?","What should you think about at night before sleep?"],"answers":["Ask someone to pass it instead of reaching","Whether you did anything wrong that day, ask forgiveness, and forgive others"],"notes":"A list of manners and devotional rules; trim or discuss to suit your family."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-4-2","title":"Lesson XXVI: Three Little Mice","steps":["New words: cunning, lattice, protruded, perplexed, comical","Read the poem aloud for your child; then let them read a stanza","Narrate: what happened when the narrator caught a mouse''s tail?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00219"}],"questions":["What color were the mice?","How did the mice ask for food?","What did the other mice do to ''help''?"],"answers":["White, with red eyes","They stood up in a row, folding their paws and rattling the bars","Began to gnaw off the tail"],"notes":"Poem by Julia C. R. Dorr. Long lines: read slowly."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-4-3","title":"Lesson XXVII: The New Year","steps":["New words: gayly, distress, gratitude, doubtless, wretched","Let your child read the lesson aloud","Narrate: what did Edward plan to do with his money, and what did he do instead?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00226"}],"questions":["What did Edward''s father give him?","Why couldn''t the man answer Edward?","What reward did Edward get?"],"answers":["Two silver dollars","He spoke only German, not English","A whole bundle of books"]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-4-4","title":"Lesson XXVIII: The Clock and the Sundial","steps":["New words: steeple, stock (a post), boasting, folly, modesty, moral","Read the fable aloud, giving the clock a proud voice","Narrate: why was the clock wrong to boast?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00231"}],"questions":["Why did the clock call the sundial stupid?","What did the sun show when it came out?"],"answers":["The sundial can only tell time when the sun shines","That the clock was half an hour behind"],"notes":"Make a simple sundial with a stick in the yard on a sunny day."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-4-5","title":"Lesson XXIX: Remember","steps":["New words: ever-wakeful, falsehood, forgiveness, delight","Read the poem aloud together, slowly","Memorize: the first stanza"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00234"}],"questions":["Who does the poem say sees all we do?","What should you do if you have done wrong?"],"answers":["God","Be sorry and pray to be forgiven"],"notes":"A devotional poem."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-4-6","title":"Lesson XXX: Courage and Cowardice","steps":["New words: quarrel, mischief, reproach, deserved, schoolmates","Read the lesson aloud, taking turns","Narrate: who really showed courage, and how?","Copywork: “Never be afraid to do good, but always fear to do evil.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00246"}],"questions":["Why wouldn''t Henry go see the fight?","What happened to Robert while bathing?"],"answers":["He did not want to meddle or get into trouble","He went out of his depth and Henry saved him from drowning"],"notes":"Today we teach children to call an adult or throw something that floats rather than swim out to rescue someone."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-4-7","title":"Lesson XXXI: Weighing an Elephant","steps":["New words: gratitude, deliverance, vowed, enormous, expense","Read the lesson aloud; stop at paragraph 8 and let your child guess the answer first","Narrate: explain the sailor''s trick step by step","Try it: float a bowl in water, mark the waterline with a toy in it, then match the mark with coins"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00250"}],"questions":["What did the king promise to give the poor?","Where did Teddy put the elephant?","What did Teddy win?"],"answers":["The elephant''s weight in silver","In a boat","An orange"],"notes":"Adapted from A. L. O. E."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-4-8","title":"Lesson XXXII: The Soldier","steps":["New words: arrayed, contest, warrior, enlist, mustered","Read the poem aloud with a marching beat","Narrate: what kind of soldier does the speaker want to be?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00257"}],"questions":["Does the soldier in the poem want to shed blood?","What does he fight for?"],"answers":["No","Truth (and love)"],"notes":"Poem by J. G. Adams."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-u5","title":"Unit 5: Lessons XXXIII–XL","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-5-1","title":"Lesson XXXIII: The Echo","steps":["New words: rambling, mocking, thicket, offended, peevish","Read the lesson together; you read Mother''s part","Narrate: what is an echo, and what did Robert''s mother teach with it?","Copywork: “A soft answer turneth away wrath.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00264"}],"questions":["Who did Robert think was mocking him?","How did his mother explain an echo?"],"answers":["A boy hiding in the woods","Like a ball bouncing back off a wall, your voice comes back from a hill"]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-5-2","title":"Lesson XXXIV: George''s Feast","steps":["New words: blazing, collect, scarlet, refresh, longing","Your child reads the lesson aloud","Narrate: tell how George decided what to do with the strawberries"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00270"}],"questions":["Why did George go to the woods?","Who were the strawberries for in the end?"],"answers":["To gather sticks for the fire","His sick mother"]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-5-3","title":"Lesson XXXV: The Lord''s Prayer","steps":["New words: hallow, bounty, transgressions, compassion, temptation","Read the verse version aloud, then compare it with Matthew 6:9–13","Memorize: the first stanza (or the prayer in your family''s version)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00277"}],"questions":["What does ''our portion of bread'' ask for?","What does the poem ask God to keep us from?"],"answers":["Food for each day","Temptation, weakness, and sin"],"notes":"A verse paraphrase. The script page that follows (An Evening Prayer) is not transcribed in the Gutenberg text; use a print copy if you have one."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-5-4","title":"Lesson XXXVI: Finding the Owner","steps":["New words: evident, satisfaction, critical, conscious, anxiety","Read the lesson aloud, taking turns","Narrate: why didn''t Fred enjoy the knife at first?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00283"}],"questions":["Whose knife was it?","How did Fred feel while he kept it?","What did the doctor do?"],"answers":["Dr. Perry''s","Like a thief; worried all week","Let Fred keep it"]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-5-5","title":"Lesson XXXVII: Bats","steps":["New words: crevices, quadruped, prowling, devour, policy","Your child reads the lesson aloud","Narrate: how did the bat trick the owl and then the cat?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00289"}],"questions":["What are a bat''s wings made of?","What did the bat tell the owl?","What did the bat tell the cat?"],"answers":["Thin skin stretched over bones","That he was not a bird but like a mouse","That he was a bird, not a mouse"],"notes":"Adapted from S. G. Goodrich. Bats are mammals; the fable''s harsh ending is about being two-faced, not about real bats, which are helpful insect-eaters."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-5-6","title":"Lesson XXXVIII: A Summer Day","steps":["New words: tints, sheaves, lance, lowing","Read the poem aloud, one stanza each","Copywork: “This is the way the morning dawns”","Draw one stanza''s picture"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00295"}],"questions":["What are the cows doing at evening?","What do fireflies do in the poem?"],"answers":["Lowing (mooing) in the lane","Wink on hill and plain"],"notes":"Poem by George Cooper."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-5-7","title":"Lesson XXXIX: I Will Think of It","steps":["New words: chandelier, pendulum, mainspring, whalebone, lectures, motto","Your child reads the lesson aloud; pause after each inventor","Narrate: name the four thinkers and what set each one thinking"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00308"}],"questions":["What did Newton see fall?","What did James Watt watch move?","What was James Ferguson''s motto?"],"answers":["An apple","The lid of a teakettle, pushed by steam","“I will think of it”"],"notes":"The Newton apple story is a well-loved legend. Find Pisa and Scotland on a map."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-5-8","title":"Lesson XL: Charlie and Rob","steps":["New words: hinder, kindling, conquer, splendid, accounts, chores","Read it as a dialogue: you are Charlie, your child is Rob","Narrate: how are Charlie''s and Rob''s ideas about work different?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XL","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00313"}],"questions":["How does Rob make splitting wood fun?","How is Rob ''working toward'' being a bank clerk?","Which boy do you think did well, and why?"],"answers":["He pretends the tough stick is a hard lesson to conquer","By keeping his father''s account books","Rob, who worked steadily and honestly; the story hints that Charlie ended up a tramp"],"notes":"The ending asks a question; let your child answer it. ''Tramps'' meant homeless wanderers."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-u6","title":"Unit 6: Lessons XLI–XLVIII","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-6-1","title":"Lesson XLI: Ray and His Kite","steps":["New words: companions, daydreams, lantern, comet, hobgoblin, approached","Read the lesson aloud together, a paragraph each","Narrate: what did the villagers guess the light was?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00318"}],"questions":["What did Ray tie to his kite?","Where was Ray while the men looked at the light?","What did the men decide to do?"],"answers":["A tin lantern","Sitting in a fence corner behind a tree","Keep the secret and enjoy the joke"],"notes":"Never fly a kite with a flame or near power lines; this one is just a story."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-6-2","title":"Lesson XLII: Beware of the First Drink","steps":["New words: ramble, decent, debts, resist, sot","Read the lesson aloud with your child","Narrate: tell how Tom Smith''s life went wrong, step by step"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00324"}],"questions":["What kind of boy was Tom Smith at school?","What did Tom himself say to beware of?","Where was Tom sent at the end?"],"answers":["Decent and well-behaved","The first drink","To state prison for ten years"],"notes":"A temperance lesson, common in 1870s readers. Preview it and discuss in a way that fits your family."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-6-3","title":"Lesson XLIII: Speak Gently","steps":["New words: mar, accents, aged, careworn, erring, eternity","Read the poem aloud softly, as the title says","Memorize: the first stanza"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00330"}],"questions":["Name two kinds of people the poem says to speak gently to.","Why is it better to rule by love than by fear?"],"answers":["Any two: little children, the aged, the poor, those who do wrong","Kind words win love and do lasting good; harsh words spoil it"],"notes":"Poem by George Washington Langford."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-6-4","title":"Lesson XLIV: The Seven Sticks","steps":["New words: quarreling, property, strained, vain, union, prosper","Read the lesson aloud","Try it: tie several pencils or sticks together and try to bend them, then one alone","Copywork: “Home, city, country, all are prosperous found, / When by the powerful link of union bound.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00339"}],"questions":["Why did bad men hope the brothers would quarrel?","How did the father break the bundle?","What did the sticks stand for?"],"answers":["So they could cheat them out of their property","He untied it and broke the sticks one at a time","The sons: together they are strong, apart they are weak"],"notes":"An Aesop fable."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-6-5","title":"Lesson XLV: The Mountain Sister","steps":["New words: autumn, tassels, prickly, burs, donkey","Your child reads the lesson aloud","Narrate: describe a nut-gathering day with Jeannette","If you can, find chestnuts or another nut in its shell and look inside"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00345"}],"questions":["What does the frost do for the nut gatherers?","What carries the bags of nuts?","How many nuts can be in one bur?"],"answers":["It cracks open the prickly burs","A donkey","One to four"]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-6-6","title":"Lesson XLVI: Harry and the Guidepost","steps":["New words: heath, owlet, benighted, groped, dismal, betide","Read the poem aloud in a spooky voice, then a cheerful one for the ending","Narrate: what did Harry think he saw, and what was it really?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00351"}],"questions":["What was the ''ghost''?","What did Harry decide to do the next time something frightened him?"],"answers":["A white guidepost (signpost)","March straight up to it"],"notes":"Poem. A guidepost is a road sign with arms pointing the way."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-6-7","title":"Lesson XLVII: The Money Amy Didn''t Earn","steps":["New words: tasks, season, quart, immediately, figuring","Read the lesson aloud together","Check Amy''s math: 5 quarts at 13 cents, and 12 quarts at 13 cents","Copywork: “One doer is worth a hundred dreamers.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00365"}],"questions":["How much would Amy be paid for each quart?","How much for five quarts?","Why didn''t Amy earn any money?"],"answers":["Thirteen cents","Sixty-five cents","She spent the morning figuring, and boys picked the berries first"],"notes":"Prices are from 1879."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-6-8","title":"Lesson XLVIII: Who Made the Stars?","steps":["New words: formed, brilliant, prattling, fragrant, homage, clime","Read the poem aloud; your child asks the first stanza and you answer","Memorize: the first two stanzas"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00372"}],"questions":["What question does the child ask?","Name two things the poem says God made."],"answers":["Who made the stars and the moon","Any two: the sun, stars, moon, leaves, flowers, feet, eyes, tongue"],"notes":"A devotional poem written as a question and answer."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-u7","title":"Unit 7: Lessons XLIX–LVI","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-7-1","title":"Lesson XLIX: Deeds of Kindness","steps":["New words: overtook, widow, cottage, trudged, gutter, occasions","Your child reads the lesson aloud","Narrate: tell the two little stories of kindness","Plan one small kind deed for today"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson XLIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00382"}],"questions":["Why was the woman taking apples to market?","Did the boys take apples as pay?","Why did the girl pick up the orange peel?"],"answers":["To pay her rent","No","So no one would slip on it and fall"]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-7-2","title":"Lesson L: The Alarm Clock","steps":["New words: roused, faithfully, ceased, conscience, trifling","Read the lesson aloud","Narrate: how is a conscience like the alarm clock?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson L","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00386"}],"questions":["Why did the lady stop hearing the clock?","What is the clock compared to?"],"answers":["She got in the habit of not obeying it","Our conscience"]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-7-3","title":"Lesson LI: Spring","steps":["New words: alder, powdery, columbine, clover","Read the poem aloud, one stanza each","Memorize: the first stanza","Go outside and look for any of the plants named in the poem"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00389"}],"questions":["Name three flowers in the poem.","What sound do the frogs make?"],"answers":["Any three: buttercups, columbine, dandelions, daisies, clover, violet","A clear, sweet music (they ''ripple'')"],"notes":"Poem by Celia Thaxter."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-7-4","title":"Lesson LII: True Courage","steps":["New words: scholars, credit, folly, heartily, fearless","Read it as a play: assign Henry, James, and George, with you as narrator","Narrate: why does the lesson call George a coward even though he threw the snowball?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00399"}],"questions":["Who talked George into throwing the snowball?","What would real courage have looked like?"],"answers":["Henry","Saying no and not caring if he was laughed at"],"notes":"Mentions whipping as a school punishment, normal in 1879. Good springboard for talking about peer pressure."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-7-5","title":"Lesson LIII: The Old Clock","steps":["New words: steady, course, ere, wrinkled","Read the poem aloud slowly, ticking with your tongue at each “tick, tick, tick”","Copywork: “Tick, tick, tick! Time passes away.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00404"}],"questions":["What does Nell not understand about the clock?","Who is telling the poem?"],"answers":["That it marks the silent passing of time","Someone older who heard the same clock as a child"],"notes":"Poem. A short one; pair it with practice reading an analog clock."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-7-6","title":"Lesson LIV: The Waves","steps":["New words: earnest, glorious, timid, fretful, moaning","Let your child read the lesson aloud; you read the sea''s lines","Narrate: what job did each little wave do on the shore?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00412"}],"questions":["What did the pebbles say they wanted?","Why did the big wave think he had done nothing?","What did the child on the shore notice?"],"answers":["Washing","He only broke on a rock and splashed into a pool","The sand was clean and the water in the pool was clear"]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-7-7","title":"Lesson LV: Don''t Kill the Birds","steps":["New words: joyous, cheerless, warble, innocent, tuneful","Read the poem aloud together","Memorize: the first stanza","Make a list of the birds you see near your home this week"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00417"}],"questions":["What does the poem say about the life of a bird?","What would the earth be like without birds?"],"answers":["We should not take a life we can never give","A cheerless place"],"notes":"Poem."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-7-8","title":"Lesson LVI: When to Say No","steps":["New words: idleness, loiter, interrupt, tempted, conceal","Read the lesson aloud, one numbered paragraph each","Narrate: list the times the lesson says to say “No”","Practice saying a polite but firm “No” to a pretend dare"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00427"}],"questions":["Why is ''No'' sometimes hard to say?","What should we say when tempted to hide a wrong with a lie?"],"answers":["Because we fear what others will think, though it is a little word","No; lying is wicked and cowardly"]}]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-u8","title":"Unit 8: Lessons LVII–LXIV","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-8-1","title":"Lesson LVII: Which Loved Best?","steps":["New words: pouted, rejoiced, softly","Read the poem aloud with expression","Narrate: what did John, Nell, and Fan each do after saying “I love you”?","Memorize: the last stanza"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00432"}],"questions":["Which child really loved mother best?","How did Fan show her love?"],"answers":["Fan","She rocked the baby, swept, dusted, and helped cheerfully all day"],"notes":"Poem by Joy Allison."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-8-2","title":"Lesson LVIII: John Carpenter","steps":["New words: satisfaction, bridle, turner, shavings, ingenious, compliment","Your child reads the lesson aloud","Narrate: tell how John built his horse, step by step","Make a simple toy from things around the house"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00440"}],"questions":["How much did Tom''s horse cost?","Where did John get the wheels?","What did John become when he grew up?"],"answers":["A dollar","From a turner, a man who shapes wood on a lathe","A master workman in a machine shop"]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-8-3","title":"Lesson LIX: Persevere","steps":["New words: persevere, patient","Read the two stanzas aloud","Memorize: the whole poem","Copywork: “The man who travels a mile each day, / May get round the world at last.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00447"}],"questions":["What happens to a fisher who pulls in his net too soon?","What does ''persevere'' mean?"],"answers":["He has no fish to sell","To keep on trying and not give up"],"notes":"Poem. Very short; pair it with review of an earlier lesson."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-8-4","title":"Lesson LX: The Contented Boy","steps":["New words: contented, tending, errands, ninepins, obliging, Testament","Read it as a dialogue: you are Mr. Lenox, your child is Peter","Narrate: what did Mr. Lenox offer, and how did Peter answer?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00451"}],"questions":["How did Peter help Mr. Lenox?","What did Peter do if he got hungry in the field?","What gift did Mr. Lenox promise?"],"answers":["He caught his runaway horse","Ate a raw turnip, or went without","The books he would need for school"],"notes":"By Dr. John Aikin (spelled Aiken in this text). An eight-year-old working in the fields was ordinary then; a dime was a lot of money."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-8-5","title":"Lesson LXI: Little Gustava","steps":["New words: quaint, eaves, marigolds, terrier, bashful, biddy","Read the poem aloud; your child joins in on each “little Gustava” line","Narrate: list all the animals that came to Gustava''s breakfast, in order"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00460"}],"questions":["What was in Gustava''s bowl?","Who drank up her milk?","Which birds stayed outside?"],"answers":["Bread and milk","Rags, the little Scotch terrier","The sparrow and the crow"],"notes":"Poem by Celia Thaxter. ''Biddy'' is an old pet name for a hen."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-8-6","title":"Lesson LXII: The Insolent Boy","steps":["New words: insolent, abuse, dandy, skulked, tavern, injury","Read the lesson aloud, taking turns","Narrate: tell what James did to the stranger and how he found out who it was"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00475"}],"questions":["Who was the stranger?","Why had he walked into the village?","What did James lose because of his behavior?"],"answers":["James''s own uncle","To surprise his brother (James''s father)","The gold watch and books his uncle had brought him"]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-8-7","title":"Lesson LXIII: We Are Seven","steps":["New words: rustic, clad, porringer, kerchief, released","Read the poem aloud: you read the traveler, your child reads the little girl","Narrate: why does the girl keep saying “we are seven”?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00484"}],"questions":["How old was the girl?","Where were two of her brothers and sisters?","What did the girl do near the graves?"],"answers":["Eight","In the churchyard; they had died","Knitted, hemmed, sang, and ate her supper there"],"notes":"Poem by William Wordsworth. It is about the death of two children; preview it and talk gently about loss."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-8-8","title":"Lesson LXIV: Mary''s Dime","steps":["New words: sugar apples, faded, earnestly, sorrowfully, resist","Your child reads the lesson aloud as if they were Mary","Narrate: what did Mary give up, and how did she feel about it?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00507"}],"questions":["Who gave Mary the dime?","What was Mary going to buy?","What did the beggar girl want?"],"answers":["Her grandpa","A candy sugar apple","A gingerbread roll; she had not eaten all day"]}]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-u9","title":"Unit 9: Lessons LXV–LXXII","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-9-1","title":"Lesson LXV: Mary Dow","steps":["New words: innocence, delicate, forsake, rejoice","Read the poem aloud together","Narrate: tell Mary Dow''s story in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00512"}],"questions":["What was Mary Dow selling?","What happened to her father?","How many matches did the speaker buy?"],"answers":["Matches, a penny a bunch","He was lost at sea when his ship sank","All of them"],"notes":"Poem. Children selling matches in the street was common then."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-9-2","title":"Lesson LXVI: The Little Loaf","steps":["New words: famine, eagerly, quarreled, modestly, peaceable","Read the lesson aloud","Narrate: how was Gretchen different from the other children?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00527"}],"questions":["Why did the children quarrel?","What did Gretchen find in her small loaf?","Was the silver a mistake?"],"answers":["Each wanted the biggest loaf","Shining silver coins","No, the baker put it there to reward her"]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-9-3","title":"Lesson LXVII: Susie and Rover","steps":["New words: Newfoundland, tripped, mood, plentiful, underbrush, capered","Read the lesson together, taking turns by paragraph","Narrate: what did Susie do wrong, and how did Rover show his loyalty?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00531"}],"questions":["What did Rover carry?","Why didn''t Susie enjoy the afternoon?","What did Rover do to the snake?"],"answers":["A tin pail for the berries","She felt bad about not sharing her lunch with Rover","Seized it by the neck and killed it"],"notes":"Adapted from Mrs. M. O. Johnson."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-9-4","title":"Lesson LXVIII: The Violet","steps":["New words: bower, graced, humility","Read the poem aloud softly","Memorize: the first stanza","Find or draw a violet"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00538"}],"questions":["Where did the violet grow?","What does the speaker want to learn from the violet?"],"answers":["In a green and shady bed, hidden from view","To grow in sweet humility"],"notes":"Poem by Jane Taylor (who also wrote “Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star”)."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-9-5","title":"Lesson LXIX: No Crown for Me","steps":["New words: tiresome, stress, tauntingly, confessing, dignified","Read the lesson aloud, taking turns","Narrate: why did Susan throw the crown on the ground?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00544"}],"questions":["What was Susan''s task?","How did she cheat?","Did her grandmother know?"],"answers":["Knitting twenty turns on a stocking","She moved the mark her grandmother had put in the stocking","Yes, all along"]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-9-6","title":"Lesson LXX: Young Soldiers","steps":["New words: plumes, kettledrum, muskets, ramrods, gander, mullein, fray","Read the poem aloud with a marching beat","Narrate: what did the schoolboys ''fight,'' and what were their weapons made of?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00550"}],"questions":["What were the boys'' muskets made of?","Which goose stood up to them?","What called them home at the end of the day?"],"answers":["Cedar wood","One sturdy gander","The supper bell"],"notes":"Poem about boys playing soldier. Mullein is a tall roadside weed."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-9-7","title":"Lesson LXXI: How Willie Got Out of the Shaft","steps":["New words: shaft, neighborhood, bounding, balance, sod, scampered","Your child reads the lesson aloud","Narrate: how did Willie''s birthday present save him?","Talk about staying away from holes, wells, and edges"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00565"}],"questions":["What was Willie''s birthday present?","Why couldn''t anyone hear Willie call?","How did the letter get home?"],"answers":["A little writing book with a pencil","He was too far from the house","Willie tied it to Caper''s neck and lifted him out"],"notes":"A shaft is a deep hole dug for a mine or well."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-9-8","title":"Lesson LXXII: The Pert Chicken","steps":["New words: pert, forward, goslings, paddling, chilled","Read the poem aloud; give the chicken a bossy voice and Mrs. Dorking a wise one","Memorize: the last stanza, “To be very wise, and show it …”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00572"}],"questions":["Why did the chicken have so few friends?","What did he tell Mrs. Goose?","How did Mrs. Dorking put him in his place?"],"answers":["He thought he knew everything and bossed everyone","That getting wet would kill her goslings","She reminded him he was in an eggshell a month ago"],"notes":"Poem by Marian Douglas. A Dorking is a breed of hen."}]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-u10","title":"Unit 10: Lessons LXXIII–LXXIX","lessons":[{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-10-1","title":"Lesson LXXIII: Indian Corn","steps":["New words: maize, cultivated, furrows, fodder, hominy, succotash","Read the lesson aloud in two parts, stopping after paragraph 9","Narrate: how does a farmer plant ''hills'' of corn, and how does the corn grow?","Look at an ear of corn: find the husk, silk, and kernels"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00580"}],"questions":["Why is it called Indian corn?","Which part of the corn plant has the male flowers?","Name three foods or products made from corn."],"answers":["American Indians were the first to grow it","The tassel","Any three: popcorn, hominy, succotash, cornstarch, syrup, oil, hasty pudding"],"notes":"Uses the 1879 word ''Indians'' and calls native stone bowls ''rude.'' Mention that Native peoples developed corn over thousands of years."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-10-2","title":"Lesson LXXIV: The Snowbird''s Song","steps":["New words: merrily, sleet, glee, frock, occasion, hobble","Read the poem aloud; your child reads the bird''s answer","Narrate: what did Emily want to give the bird, and why did he say no thanks?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00588"}],"questions":["What song did the bird sing?","What did Emily want to give the bird?","Why didn''t the bird need clothes?"],"answers":["Chick-a-de-dee","Stockings, shoes, a frock, and a hat","God had already clothed him warmly (with feathers)"],"notes":"Poem by F. C. Woodworth."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-10-3","title":"Lesson LXXV: Mountains","steps":["New words: ascend, retreat, rugged, torrents, chasm, precipice","Your child reads the lesson aloud","Find the Himalayas and the Andes on a map or globe","Narrate: describe a trip up the Himalayas or across an Andes pass"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00600"}],"questions":["Which mountains are the highest on earth?","What makes a bridge over a Himalayan river?","What animal carries travelers in the Andes?"],"answers":["The Himalayas","Three ropes","The mule"],"notes":"''Thibet'' is an old spelling of Tibet. Travel in these mountains is much easier today."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-10-4","title":"Lesson LXXVI: A Child''s Hymn","steps":["New words: glow, native, comforteth, waxeth","Read the hymn aloud slowly","Memorize: one stanza of your child''s choosing","Copywork: “God make my life a little light”"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00605"}],"questions":["Name the four things the child asks God to make his life like.","What does a ''little song'' do in the poem?"],"answers":["A light, a flower, a song, a hymn","Comforts the sad and helps others be strong"],"notes":"A devotional poem."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-10-5","title":"Lesson LXXVII: Holding the Fort","steps":["New words: hobbling, sprained, lecturer, furloughs, Commandments","Read it as a dialogue: you are Genie, your child is Rob","Narrate: how did Rob sprain his ankle?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00612"}],"questions":["What did Jack Lee do to Rob''s books?","Why did Rob stop chasing Jack?","Why did Genie call Rob a ''wounded soldier''?"],"answers":["Threw them in the mud","He remembered to return good for evil","He was hurt while doing the right thing"]},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-10-6","title":"Lesson LXXVIII: The Little People","steps":["New words: dreary, mirth, sterner, doleful","Read the poem aloud","Narrate: what would the world be like with no children, according to the poem?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00617"}],"questions":["Who are the ''little people''?","What would the song of life lose without them?"],"answers":["Children and babies","Its mirth (joy) and charm"],"notes":"Poem by John Greenleaf Whittier."},{"id":"mcguffey-third-reader-10-7","title":"Lesson LXXIX: Good Night","steps":["New words: hidden, watches, weary","Read the poem aloud at bedtime","Memorize: the first stanza","Celebrate finishing the Third Reader!"],"links":[{"label":"Read Lesson LXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/14766/pg14766-images.html#id00625"}],"questions":["Name three things the child says good night to.","What is lighting up the sky?"],"answers":["Any three: father, mother, friends, birds, flowers, stars","The moon"],"notes":"Poem by Eliza Lee Follen (Mrs. Follen). The last lesson of the book."}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"“McGuffey''s Third Eclectic Reader, Revised Edition” by William Holmes McGuffey (1879), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/14766. Lesson plan by Freerange."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (listing_id, version) do update set content = excluded.content;
insert into public.listings (id, seller_name, seller_kind, seller_verified, title, subjects, grade_min, grade_max, philosophies, format, price, description, includes, lessons, lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement, family_style, lesson_steps, cover, color, esa_eligible, created_at, package_id, package_version, license, attribution, outline) values ('l-lib-our-island-story', 'H. E. Marshall', 'open', false, 'Our Island Story', array['history']::text[], 2, 6, array['charlotte_mason', 'classical']::text[], 'online', 0, 'The beloved classic read-aloud history of Britain, told as a chain of stories from the legends of Albion to the early twentieth century. Siblings listen together, narrate the chapter back, find the place on a map, and add a date to the family timeline. The full text of this classic is free online.', array['114 lessons in 13 units, one chapter each', 'Link to the free full text for every chapter', 'Narration, map work, and timeline steps for each lesson', 'Questions with answers for every chapter', 'Parent notes where legend mixes with history or the book shows its imperial-era viewpoint']::text[], 114, 4, 20, 'high', true, '{}'::text[], '🏰', '#2F6B5E', false, '2026-11-11T12:00:00.000Z', 'pk-lib-our-island-story', 1, '{"kind":"public_domain"}'::jsonb, '“Our Island Story: A Child''s History of England (US edition: An Island Story: A History of England for Boys and Girls, 1920)” by H. E. Marshall (1905), in the public domain. Free full text: https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html. Lesson plan by Freerange.', '[{"title":"Unit 1: Ancient Britain and the Romans (Chapters 1–8)","lessons":["Chapter 1: The Stories of Albion and Brutus","Chapter 2: The Coming of the Romans","Chapter 3: The Romans Come Again","Chapter 4: How Caligula Conquered Britain, and How Caractacus Refused to Be Conquered","Chapter 5: The Story of a Warrior Queen","Chapter 6: The Last of the Romans","Chapter 7: The Story of St. Alban","Chapter 8: Vortigern and King Constans"]},{"title":"Unit 2: Saxons, Arthur, and Alfred (Chapters 9–17)","lessons":["Chapter 9: The Story of the Coming of Hengist and Horsa","Chapter 10: Hengist''s Treachery","Chapter 11: The Story of How the Giant''s Dance Was Brought to Britain","Chapter 12: The Coming of Arthur","Chapter 13: The Founding of the Round Table","Chapter 14: The Story of Gregory and the Pretty Children","Chapter 15: How King Alfred Learned to Read","Chapter 16: King Alfred in the Cowherd''s Cottage","Chapter 17: More About Alfred the Great"]},{"title":"Unit 3: Danes, Saxons, and the Norman Conquest (Chapters 18–26)","lessons":["Chapter 18: Ethelred the Unready","Chapter 19: How Edmund Ironside Fought for the Crown","Chapter 20: Canute and the Waves","Chapter 21: Edward the Confessor","Chapter 22: Harold","Chapter 23: The Battle of Stamford Bridge","Chapter 24: The Battle of Hastings","Chapter 25: William the Conqueror – The Story of Hereward the Wake","Chapter 26: William the Conqueror – Death of the King"]},{"title":"Unit 4: Normans and the First Plantagenets (Chapters 27–36)","lessons":["Chapter 27: The Story of William the Red","Chapter 28: Henry I. – The Story of the \"White Ship\"","Chapter 29: The Story of King Stephen","Chapter 30: Henry Plantagenet – The Story of Gilbert and Rohesia","Chapter 31: Henry Plantagenet – The Story of Thomas à Becket","Chapter 32: Henry Plantagenet – The Story of the Conquest of Ireland","Chapter 33: The Story of Richard Cœur de Lion","Chapter 34: Richard Cœur de Lion – The Story of How Blondel Found the King","Chapter 35: John Lackland – The Story of Prince Arthur","Chapter 36: John Lackland – The Story of the Great Charter"]},{"title":"Unit 5: Plantagenet Kings and the Hundred Years'' War (Chapters 37–48)","lessons":["Chapter 37: Henry III. of Winchester – The Story of Hubert de Burgh","Chapter 38: Henry III. of Winchester – The Story of Simon de Montfort","Chapter 39: Henry III. – The Story of the Poisoned Dagger","Chapter 40: Edward I. – The Little War of Chalons","Chapter 41: Edward I. – The Lawgiver – The Story of the First Prince of Wales","Chapter 42: Edward I. – The Hammer of the Scots","Chapter 43: Edward II. of Caernarvon – The Story of King Robert the Bruce and Bohun","Chapter 44: Edward II. of Caernarvon – The Story of the Battle of Bannockburn","Chapter 45: Edward III. of Windsor – The Story of the Battle of Sluys","Chapter 46: Edward III. of Windsor – The Story of the Battle of Crecy","Chapter 47: Edward III. of Windsor – The Story of the Siege of Calais","Chapter 48: Edward III. of Windsor – The Story of the Battle of Poitiers"]},{"title":"Unit 6: Lancaster, York, and the Wars of the Roses (Chapters 49–59)","lessons":["Chapter 49: Richard II. of Bordeaux – The Story of Wat Tyler''s Rebellion","Chapter 50: How King Richard II. Lost His Throne","Chapter 51: Henry IV. of Bolingbroke – The Story of the Battle of Shrewsbury","Chapter 52: Henry IV. of Bolingbroke – The Story of How Prince Hal Was Sent to Prison","Chapter 53: Henry V. of Monmouth – The Story of the Battle of Agincourt","Chapter 54: Henry VI. of Windsor – The Story of the Maid of Orleans","Chapter 55: Henry VI. of Windsor – The Red Rose and the White","Chapter 56: Edward IV. – The Story of Queen Margaret and the Robbers","Chapter 57: Edward IV. – The Story of the King-Maker","Chapter 58: Edward V. – The Story of the King Who Was Never Crowned","Chapter 59: Richard III. – The Story of the Two Little Princes in the Tower"]},{"title":"Unit 7: The Early Tudors (Chapters 60–68)","lessons":["Chapter 60: Henry VII. – The Story of a Make-Believe Prince","Chapter 61: Henry VII. – The Story of Another Make-Believe Prince","Chapter 62: Henry VIII. – The Story of the Field of the Cloth of Gold","Chapter 63: Henry VIII. – How the King Became the Defender of the Faith and How the Great Cardinal Died","Chapter 64: Henry VIII. – The Story of the King''s Six Wives","Chapter 65: Edward VI. – The Story of a Boy King","Chapter 66: The Story of Lady Jane Grey","Chapter 67: Mary I. – How the Princess Elizabeth Became a Prisoner","Chapter 68: Mary I. – How a Candle Was Lit in England Which Has Never Been Put Out"]},{"title":"Unit 8: Elizabeth I (Chapters 69–73)","lessons":["Chapter 69: Elizabeth – How the Imprisoned Princess Became a Queen","Chapter 70: Elizabeth – The Story of a Most Unhappy Queen","Chapter 71: Elizabeth – The Story of How England Was Saved from the Spaniards","Chapter 72: Elizabeth – The Story of Sir Walter Raleigh","Chapter 73: Elizabeth – The Story of the Queen''s Favorite"]},{"title":"Unit 9: The Stuarts and the Civil War (Chapters 74–82)","lessons":["Chapter 74: James VI. of Scotland, I. of England – The Story of Guy Fawkes","Chapter 75: James VI. of Scotland, I. of England – The Story of the \"Mayflower\"","Chapter 76: Charles I. – How a Woman Struck a Blow for Freedom","Chapter 77: Charles I. – The Story of How the King and the Parliament Quarreled and at Last Fought","Chapter 78: Charles I. – The Story of How the King Was Brought to His Death","Chapter 79: The Commonwealth – The Adventures of a Prince","Chapter 80: The Commonwealth – The Lord Protector","Chapter 81: Charles II. – How the King Came to His Own, and How Death Walked in the Streets of London","Chapter 82: Charles II. – The Story of How London Was Burned"]},{"title":"Unit 10: The Later Stuarts and the Union (Chapters 83–89)","lessons":["Chapter 83: James II. of England and VII. of Scotland – The Fiery Cross","Chapter 84: James II. of England and VII. of Scotland – The Story of King Monmouth","Chapter 85: James II. of England and VII. of Scotland – The Story of the Seven Bishops","Chapter 86: James II. of England and VII. of Scotland – William the Deliverer","Chapter 87: William III. and Mary II. – The Story of Brave Londonderry","Chapter 88: William III. and Mary II. – The Story of a Sad Day in a Highland Glen","Chapter 89: Anne – How the Union Jack Was Made"]},{"title":"Unit 11: Georgian Britain (Chapters 90–100)","lessons":["Chapter 90: George I. – The Story of the Earl of Mar''s Hunting Party","Chapter 91: George II. – The Story of Bonnie Prince Charlie","Chapter 92: George II. – The Story of Flora Macdonald","Chapter 93: George II. – The Story of the Black Hole of Calcutta","Chapter 94: George II. – The Story of How Canada Was Won","Chapter 95: George III. – The Story of How America Was Lost","Chapter 96: George III. – A Story of the Spinning-Wheel","Chapter 97: George III. – England Expects That Every Man Will Do His Duty","Chapter 98: George III. – The Battle of Waterloo","Chapter 99: George IV. – The First Gentleman in Europe","Chapter 100: William IV. – The Story of Two Peaceful Victories"]},{"title":"Unit 12: Queen Victoria''s Reign (Chapters 101–107)","lessons":["Chapter 101: Victoria – The Girl Queen","Chapter 102: Victoria – When Bread Was Dear","Chapter 103: Victoria – Peace","Chapter 104: Victoria – War","Chapter 105: Victoria – The Land of Snow","Chapter 106: Victoria – The Siege of Delhi","Chapter 107: Victoria – The Pipes at Lucknow"]},{"title":"Unit 13: Empire and the Twentieth Century (Chapters 108–114)","lessons":["Chapter 108: Victoria – Under the Southern Cross","Chapter 109: Victoria – From Cannibal to Christian","Chapter 110: Victoria – Boer and Briton","Chapter 111: Edward VII – The Peacemaker","Chapter 112: George V. – Armed Peace","Chapter 113: World War","Chapter 114: The Hope of the Future"]}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set seller_name = excluded.seller_name, seller_kind = excluded.seller_kind, seller_verified = excluded.seller_verified, title = excluded.title, subjects = excluded.subjects, grade_min = excluded.grade_min, grade_max = excluded.grade_max, philosophies = excluded.philosophies, format = excluded.format, price = excluded.price, description = excluded.description, includes = excluded.includes, lessons = excluded.lessons, lessons_per_week = excluded.lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson = excluded.minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement = excluded.parent_involvement, family_style = excluded.family_style, lesson_steps = excluded.lesson_steps, cover = excluded.cover, color = excluded.color, esa_eligible = excluded.esa_eligible, package_id = excluded.package_id, package_version = excluded.package_version, license = excluded.license, attribution = excluded.attribution, outline = excluded.outline;
insert into public.package_releases (listing_id, version, content) values ('l-lib-our-island-story', 1, '{"units":[{"id":"our-island-story-u1","title":"Unit 1: Ancient Britain and the Romans (Chapters 1–8)","lessons":[{"id":"our-island-story-1-1","title":"Chapter 1: The Stories of Albion and Brutus","steps":["Read chapter 1 aloud together","Narrate how the little mermaid won Albion his island and how Brutus came to rename it","Find Great Britain on a world map, then point to the white cliffs of Dover on its south coast","Add ''Albion and Brutus (legend)'' to the legends strip at the start of your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 1","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#1"}],"pages":"1–4","questions":["Who was Albion''s father, and what did he rule?","What was the island called before Albion took it over?","Where did Brutus sail from, and what name did he give the islands?"],"answers":["Neptune, king of the sea and the waves","Samothea","From Troy; he called them Britain (Britannia), after himself"],"notes":"This chapter is myth and legend, not history; the book itself says it may be only a fairy tale. The closing lines about ''Greater Britain'' reflect the book''s 1900s imperial outlook and could prompt a short talk."},{"id":"our-island-story-1-2","title":"Chapter 2: The Coming of the Romans","steps":["Read chapter 2 aloud together","Narrate how the Roman standard-bearer leaped into the sea and how the British war chariots fought","Find the Strait of Dover between France and Kent on a map, where Caesar crossed","Add ''Julius Caesar''s first landing in Britain, 55 BC'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 2","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#2"}],"pages":"5–9","questions":["Why did Julius Caesar want to conquer Britain?","What did the Romans carry instead of a flag?","What stuck out from the wheels of the British war chariots?"],"answers":["He heard it was rich in tin, lead and other metals, with pearls on its shores","A golden eagle on a pole","Swords"]},{"id":"our-island-story-1-3","title":"Chapter 3: The Romans Come Again","steps":["Read chapter 3 aloud together","Narrate how Cassivellaunus led the Britons and how Caesar finally left the island","Locate the Thames valley north of London on a map of England, where Cassivellaunus led the Britons","Add ''Caesar''s second invasion, 54 BC'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 3","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#3"}],"pages":"10–12","questions":["How many ships did Caesar bring the second time?","Why were British swords weaker than Roman ones?","What do the letters B.C. and A.D. mean?"],"answers":["About eight hundred","They were made of soft copper, which bent easily","B.C. means before Christ; A.D. (Anno Domini) means in the year of our Lord"]},{"id":"our-island-story-1-4","title":"Chapter 4: How Caligula Conquered Britain, and How Caractacus Refused to Be Conquered","steps":["Read chapter 4 aloud together","Narrate Caligula''s seashell ''conquest'' and what Caractacus said to the Emperor Claudius","Find the Welsh borders near Shropshire on a map, where Caractacus made his last stand","Add ''Claudius invades Britain, AD 43'' and ''Caractacus captured, AD 51'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 4","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#4"}],"pages":"13–16","questions":["What did Caligula order his soldiers to gather on the beach?","What was a Roman Triumph?","What did Claudius do after hearing Caractacus speak?"],"answers":["Seashells","A victory parade through Rome with the conqueror, his soldiers, and his captives","He set Caractacus and his family free"],"notes":"Caligula''s seashell episode comes from Roman writers and may be exaggerated. The site of Caractacus''s last battle is not known for certain."},{"id":"our-island-story-1-5","title":"Chapter 5: The Story of a Warrior Queen","steps":["Read chapter 5 aloud together","Narrate why Boadicea rose against the Romans and what she said to her people","Find Norfolk and Suffolk, then London and St Albans, on a map of England","Add ''Boadicea (Boudica) leads the revolt, AD 60–61'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 5","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#5"}],"pages":"17–21","questions":["Which part of Britain did Boadicea rule?","What did her husband do with his wealth before he died?","What was St Albans called in Roman times?"],"answers":["The land now called Norfolk and Suffolk","He gave half to the Emperor and half to his wife and daughters","Verulamium"],"notes":"This chapter describes the sacking of towns and ends with Boadicea poisoning herself and her daughters; parents may want to preview it for younger children."},{"id":"our-island-story-1-6","title":"Chapter 6: The Last of the Romans","steps":["Read chapter 6 aloud together","Narrate the good things Agricola did in Britain and why Hadrian built his wall","Trace Hadrian''s Wall from the Tyne to the Solway on a map of northern England","Add ''Hadrian''s Wall begun, AD 122'' and ''Romans leave Britain, c. 410'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 6","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#6"}],"pages":"22–25","questions":["Name two good things Agricola did for the Britons.","Who were the British priests, and which tree did they hold sacred?","About what year did the Romans leave Britain?"],"answers":["He lowered taxes, built schools and courts, and taught people to read and write","The Druids; the oak tree","About AD 410"],"notes":"The book''s picture of the ancient Britons as ''ignorant and wild'' reflects its era; archaeology shows a skilled Iron Age culture. Worth a brief talk."},{"id":"our-island-story-1-7","title":"Chapter 7: The Story of St. Alban","steps":["Read chapter 7 aloud together","Narrate how Alban hid Amphibalus and took his place before the governor","Find St Albans, north of London, on a map of England","Add ''Martyrdom of St Alban, c. 304'' to your timeline (the exact date is uncertain)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 7","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#7"}],"pages":"26–30","questions":["Why did Amphibalus come to Alban''s house?","How did Alban trick the soldiers?","What is the hill where Alban died called today?"],"answers":["He was a Christian fleeing the Emperor''s soldiers and needed a hiding place","He put on Amphibalus''s robe and hood so they took him for the priest","Holywell Hill"],"notes":"Alban''s execution by beheading is told plainly. The book itself says the miracles at his death need not be believed."},{"id":"our-island-story-1-8","title":"Chapter 8: Vortigern and King Constans","steps":["Read chapter 8 aloud together","Narrate how Vortigern made Constans king and then plotted to take the power himself","Find Hadrian''s Wall, then Brittany in north-west France, on a map","Add ''The Groans of the Britons appeal to Rome, c. 446'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 8","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#8"}],"pages":"31–35","questions":["What was the Britons'' sad letter to Rome called?","What was Constans before he became king?","Where did Aurelius Ambrosius and Uther Pendragon flee?"],"answers":["The Groans of the Britons","A monk","To Brittany in France"],"notes":"Constans, Vortigern''s plot, and Uther Pendragon come from Geoffrey of Monmouth''s legendary history; the ''Groans'' letter is from the early writer Gildas. The murder of Constans is told plainly."}]},{"id":"our-island-story-u2","title":"Unit 2: Saxons, Arthur, and Alfred (Chapters 9–17)","lessons":[{"id":"our-island-story-2-1","title":"Chapter 9: The Story of the Coming of Hengist and Horsa","steps":["Read chapter 9 aloud together","Narrate how Hengist won land with an ox-hide thong and how Rowena met Vortigern","Find Kent, then Caistor near Lincoln, on a map of England","Add ''Hengist and Horsa land in Kent, 449'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 9","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#9"}],"pages":"36–40","questions":["Why did Vortigern invite the Saxons to Britain?","How did Hengist make one leather thong surround so much land?","What did Hengist get in return for letting Vortigern marry Rowena?"],"answers":["To help fight the Picts and Scots","He cut a bullock''s hide round and round into one long thin strip","The whole of Kent"],"notes":"Hengist, Horsa and the date 449 come from early chronicles; the thong trick and Rowena are legend. Try the paper-spiral experiment the book suggests."},{"id":"our-island-story-2-2","title":"Chapter 10: Hengist''s Treachery","steps":["Read chapter 10 aloud together","Narrate what happened at the May feast on Salisbury Plain and how Vortigern met his end","Find Salisbury Plain in Wiltshire, then Wales, on a map","Add ''The treachery of the long knives (legend), 5th century'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 10","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#10"}],"pages":"41–44","questions":["Where did Hengist''s men hide their daggers?","How did Edol, Earl of Gloucester, escape?","How did Aurelius and Uther capture Vortigern''s castle?"],"answers":["In their stockings","He fought with a wooden stake he found on the ground","They piled wood around it and set it on fire"],"notes":"This chapter is legend from Geoffrey of Monmouth and includes a massacre and Vortigern burning in his castle; consider previewing for young listeners."},{"id":"our-island-story-2-3","title":"Chapter 11: The Story of How the Giant''s Dance Was Brought to Britain","steps":["Read chapter 11 aloud together","Narrate how Merlin brought the Giant''s Dance from Ireland and where the stones were set up","Find Stonehenge on Salisbury Plain in Wiltshire on a map of England","Put ''Merlin and the Giant''s Dance (legend)'' on your legends strip; Stonehenge itself was built c. 3000–2000 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 11","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#11"}],"pages":"45–50","questions":["Who cut off Hengist''s head?","Where did Merlin say the Giant''s Dance stood?","What did Aurelius rename the Giant''s Dance?"],"answers":["Edol, Earl of Gloucester","In Ireland","Stonehenge"],"notes":"The book admits this is probably a fairy tale. Stonehenge is real but was built thousands of years before Merlin''s supposed time; a picture of it makes a good discussion starter."},{"id":"our-island-story-2-4","title":"Chapter 12: The Coming of Arthur","steps":["Read chapter 12 aloud together","Narrate how Merlin brought Arthur to the nobles and what happened with the sword in the stone","Find Cornwall and Wales, the lands tied to the Arthur stories, on a map of Britain","Add ''King Arthur (legend), c. 500'' to your legends strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 12","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#12"}],"pages":"51–53","questions":["Whose son did Merlin say Arthur was?","What was written beneath the sword in the stone?","What were the names of Arthur''s sword and spear?"],"answers":["Uther Pendragon''s","Whoever could draw it out was the rightful king of Britain","Excalibur and Ron"],"notes":"Arthur is a figure of legend; a British war leader may lie behind the stories, but nothing is certain. The verse is from Tennyson''s ''Idylls of the King.''"},{"id":"our-island-story-2-5","title":"Chapter 13: The Founding of the Round Table","steps":["Read chapter 13 aloud together","Narrate the quarrel at the Easter feast and how a round table solved it","Find Winchester in Hampshire, where a medieval ''Round Table'' hangs, on a map of England","Add ''Knights of the Round Table (legend)'' to your legends strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 13","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#13"}],"pages":"54–57","questions":["What were the nobles fighting about at the Easter feast?","Why did a round table stop the quarreling?","What did Arthur teach about being brave?"],"answers":["Who would get the best seats near the top of the table","It has no top or bottom, so every knight was equal","That you can be brave yet kind, and strong yet gentle"],"notes":"Legend, not history. The Winchester Round Table was made around 1290, centuries after Arthur''s supposed time."},{"id":"our-island-story-2-6","title":"Chapter 14: The Story of Gregory and the Pretty Children","steps":["Read chapter 14 aloud together","Narrate what Gregory said about the children in the Roman market and how Augustine came to England","Find Rome in Italy, then Canterbury in Kent, on a map of Europe","Add ''Augustine arrives in Kent, 597'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 14","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#14"}],"pages":"58–63","questions":["What did Gregory say when he heard the children were Angles?","Which king first listened to Augustine?","What did the priest Coifi do to the old idol?"],"answers":["Not Angles but angels","Ethelbert, King of Kent","He rode at it with a spear and knocked it over"],"notes":"The children were being sold as slaves; a short talk about slavery may help. Coifi''s story actually happened in Northumbria, not Kent."},{"id":"our-island-story-2-7","title":"Chapter 15: How King Alfred Learned to Read","steps":["Read chapter 15 aloud together","Narrate how Alfred won his mother''s book of songs","Find Wessex in southern England, and Wantage, Alfred''s birthplace, on a map","Add ''Alfred born at Wantage, 849'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 15","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#15"}],"pages":"64–67","questions":["Where did the Danes come from?","What was Alfred''s mother''s name?","How did Alfred win the book?"],"answers":["Denmark, Norway and Sweden","Osburga","He was the first of the brothers to learn to read it"]},{"id":"our-island-story-2-8","title":"Chapter 16: King Alfred in the Cowherd''s Cottage","steps":["Read chapter 16 aloud together","Narrate how Alfred burned the cakes and what the cowherd''s wife said","Find the Somerset Levels (marshes) in south-west England on a map","Add ''Alfred hides in the Somerset marshes, 878'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 16","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#16"}],"pages":"68–72","questions":["Why did Alfred build ships?","What was the cowherd''s name?","What was Alfred thinking about while the cakes burned?"],"answers":["To stop the Danes at sea before they could land","Denewulf","His kingdom, and how to free his people from the Danes"],"notes":"The cake story is a much later legend, not recorded in Alfred''s lifetime. The poem quoted is Thomas Campbell''s ''Ye Mariners of England.''"},{"id":"our-island-story-2-9","title":"Chapter 17: More About Alfred the Great","steps":["Read chapter 17 aloud together","Narrate how Alfred spied on the Danes as a minstrel and what he did once peace came","Find Athelney and Wedmore in Somerset on a map of England","Add ''Alfred defeats the Danes at Edington; Peace of Wedmore, 878'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 17","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#17"}],"pages":"73–76","questions":["What does Athelney mean?","What was the Danes'' magic banner called?","How did Alfred keep track of the hours?"],"answers":["The Isle of Nobles","The Raven","With marked candles that each burned for one hour"],"notes":"The minstrel disguise is a later legend. Trial by jury grew up over centuries rather than being created by Alfred alone."}]},{"id":"our-island-story-u3","title":"Unit 3: Danes, Saxons, and the Norman Conquest (Chapters 18–26)","lessons":[{"id":"our-island-story-3-1","title":"Chapter 18: Ethelred the Unready","steps":["Read chapter 18 aloud together","Narrate how Ethelred tried to buy off the Danes and what Danegelt was","Find Mercia in the English Midlands, then Denmark across the North Sea, on a map","Add ''St Brice''s Day massacre of the Danes, 13 November 1002'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 18","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#18"}],"pages":"77–80","questions":["Who was the Lady of Mercia?","What did ''Unready'' mean for Ethelred?","What was Danegelt?"],"answers":["Ethelfleda, Alfred''s daughter and Edward the Elder''s sister","He was foolish and slow (it really meant ''badly advised'')","A tax the people paid so the king could pay the Danes to go away"],"notes":"The massacre of Danish families, including Gunhilda''s death, is described in some detail; consider previewing. The book gives Alfred''s death as 901, but it was 899."},{"id":"our-island-story-3-2","title":"Chapter 19: How Edmund Ironside Fought for the Crown","steps":["Read chapter 19 aloud together","Narrate Edmund Ironside''s single combat with Canute and how they agreed to share England","Find Normandy in northern France on a map, where Ethelred fled","Add ''Edmund Ironside and Canute divide England, 1016'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 19","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#19"}],"pages":"81–84","questions":["Why did Sweyn of Denmark attack England?","Why was Edmund called Ironside?","Which part of England did each king rule after the fight?"],"answers":["To avenge the murder of his sister Gunhilda","Because of his strength and courage","Edmund ruled the south and Canute the north"],"notes":"The single combat comes from later chroniclers and may not have happened. In 1016 Edmund actually kept Wessex while Canute took the rest."},{"id":"our-island-story-3-3","title":"Chapter 20: Canute and the Waves","steps":["Read chapter 20 aloud together","Narrate how Canute answered his flattering nobles at the seashore","Find Winchester in Hampshire, and Denmark and Norway across the North Sea, on a map","Add ''Canute becomes king of all England, 1016'' and ''Canute dies, 1035'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 20","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#20"}],"pages":"85–87","questions":["Besides England, which countries did Canute rule?","What lesson did Canute teach by sitting before the waves?","Into how many earldoms did Canute divide England?"],"answers":["Denmark, Norway and Sweden","That only God has power over the sea, not any king","Four"],"notes":"The waves story was first written down about a century after Canute and is probably legend; note that Canute was showing the limits of a king''s power, not trying to command the sea."},{"id":"our-island-story-3-4","title":"Chapter 21: Edward the Confessor","steps":["Read chapter 21 aloud together","Narrate the fight at Dover and why Earl Godwin refused to punish the townspeople","Find Dover in Kent and Normandy across the Channel on a map","Add ''Edward the Confessor dies; Westminster Abbey consecrated, 1066'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 21","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#21"}],"pages":"88–93","questions":["Why did Edward the Confessor prefer the Normans?","What did Godwin say when Edward ordered him to punish Dover?","How did Harold end up in Duke William''s power?"],"answers":["He had grown up in Normandy","That the king had heard only one side of the story","His ship was wrecked on the coast of Normandy"]},{"id":"our-island-story-3-5","title":"Chapter 22: Harold","steps":["Read chapter 22 aloud together","Narrate Harold''s crowning and how Duke William took the news","Find Westminster in London and Rouen in Normandy on a map","Add ''Harold crowned king, January 1066'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 22","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#22"}],"pages":"94–100","questions":["Who was the real heir to the throne, and why wasn''t he chosen?","What did Harold tell William''s messenger about the crown?","What did William promise his nobles to get them to come?"],"answers":["Edgar the Ætheling; he was a little boy raised in Hungary","That it was not his to give; the people had given it to him in trust","Land, castles and money taken from the English"]},{"id":"our-island-story-3-6","title":"Chapter 23: The Battle of Stamford Bridge","steps":["Read chapter 23 aloud together","Narrate what Harold offered Tostig before the battle and what Tostig answered","Find York and Stamford Bridge in Yorkshire on a map of England","Add ''Battle of Stamford Bridge, 25 September 1066'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 23","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#23"}],"pages":"101–107","questions":["Who invaded the north with Tostig?","How much English land did Harold say Harold Hardrada would get?","Why had Harold''s army gone home before this news came?"],"answers":["Harold Hardrada, King of Norway","Seven feet for a grave, or a little more since he was so tall","It was autumn and they went home to bring in the harvest"]},{"id":"our-island-story-3-7","title":"Chapter 24: The Battle of Hastings","steps":["Read chapter 24 aloud together","Narrate how William landed in England and how the battle on Senlac Hill was won and lost","Find Pevensey and Hastings on the Sussex coast, then the town of Battle, on a map","Add ''Battle of Hastings, 14 October 1066'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 24","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#24"}],"pages":"108–114","questions":["What did William say when he fell as he landed?","Why wouldn''t Harold let Gurth burn the land between London and the coast?","Who rode out first for the Normans, singing?"],"answers":["That he had already taken hold of his kingdom","He would never harm English homes or the people he was given to rule","Taillefer, a minstrel"],"notes":"The battle was fought on 14 October 1066; the book''s date of the 15th is a slip. The Bayeux Tapestry is a wonderful picture source to look at alongside."},{"id":"our-island-story-3-8","title":"Chapter 25: William the Conqueror – The Story of Hereward the Wake","steps":["Read chapter 25 aloud together","Narrate how Hereward held out at Ely and how he was finally betrayed","Find Ely and the Fens in Cambridgeshire on a map of England","Add ''William crowned, Christmas 1066'' and ''Hereward''s stand at Ely, 1070–71'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 25","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#25"}],"pages":"115–120","questions":["Who would not crown William, and who crowned Harold?","How did William try to reach the camp at Ely?","Who betrayed Hereward, and why?"],"answers":["Archbishop Stigand, who had also crowned Harold","He built a strong road across the marshes","The monks, who were tired of fighting and plain food"],"notes":"Includes the Harrying of the North, a burned ''witch,'' and mutilation of prisoners; preview for sensitive children. Stigand did not in fact crown Harold or William; the book follows older tradition."},{"id":"our-island-story-3-9","title":"Chapter 26: William the Conqueror – Death of the King","steps":["Read chapter 26 aloud together","Narrate what the curfew and the Domesday Book were, and why William made them","Find the New Forest in Hampshire, then Caen in Normandy where William is buried, on a map","Add ''Domesday Book, 1086'' and ''Death of William the Conqueror, 1087'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 26","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#26"}],"pages":"121–124","questions":["What does ''curfew'' come from, and what did it mean?","What was written in the Domesday Book?","What did William destroy villages to make?"],"answers":["French ''couvre feu,'' meaning ''cover fire'': put out lights and fires at eight","All the great houses and lands in the kingdom and who owned them","The New Forest, a hunting ground"]}]},{"id":"our-island-story-u4","title":"Unit 4: Normans and the First Plantagenets (Chapters 27–36)","lessons":[{"id":"our-island-story-4-1","title":"Chapter 27: The Story of William the Red","steps":["Read chapter 27 aloud together","Narrate how William Rufus won English support and how he died in the forest","Find the New Forest in Hampshire and Winchester, where Rufus is buried, on a map","Add ''William Rufus killed in the New Forest, 1100'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 27","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#27"}],"pages":"125–128","questions":["Why was he called William Rufus?","What did Rufus promise the English to win their help?","Who was suspected of shooting the king?"],"answers":["Because of his red hair","Lower taxes, more liberty, and protection from the Norman barons","Walter Tyrrell"],"notes":"Whether Rufus''s death was an accident or murder is still debated."},{"id":"our-island-story-4-2","title":"Chapter 28: Henry I. – The Story of the \"White Ship\"","steps":["Read chapter 28 aloud together","Narrate the sinking of the White Ship and who survived to tell the story","Find Barfleur on the coast of Normandy and trace the crossing to England on a map","Add ''Wreck of the White Ship, 1120'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 28","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#28"}],"pages":"129–136","questions":["Why was Henry I called Beauclerc?","Why did Prince William turn the small boat back?","Who was the only survivor, and what may have helped save him?"],"answers":["It means ''fine scholar''; he could read and write well","To save his sister Marie","Berthold, a butcher of Rouen; his warm sheepskin coat"],"notes":"The shipwreck and drownings are told vividly and could upset a sensitive child."},{"id":"our-island-story-4-3","title":"Chapter 29: The Story of King Stephen","steps":["Read chapter 29 aloud together","Narrate how Matilda escaped from Oxford over the snow and how Stephen and Henry made peace","Find Oxford and Wallingford on the River Thames, then Northallerton in Yorkshire, on a map","Add ''Matilda escapes Oxford, 1142'' and ''Treaty of Wallingford, 1153'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 29","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#29"}],"pages":"137–140","questions":["Where does the name Plantagenet come from?","Why was it called the Battle of the Standard?","How did Matilda and her knights slip past Stephen''s soldiers?"],"answers":["From planta genista, the sprig of broom Geoffrey wore in his helmet","The English gathered round a pole on a cart hung with four saints'' banners","They dressed in white and crossed the frozen Thames on the ice"],"notes":"The cruelty in the barons'' castles is mentioned briefly; this period is often called ''the Anarchy.''"},{"id":"our-island-story-4-4","title":"Chapter 30: Henry Plantagenet – The Story of Gilbert and Rohesia","steps":["Read chapter 30 aloud together","Narrate how Rohesia freed Gilbert and found him again in London","Find Jerusalem in the Holy Land, then London, on a map, and trace Rohesia''s long journey","Add ''Henry II crowned, 1154'' to your timeline (Gilbert and Rohesia are legend)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 30","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#30"}],"pages":"141–144","questions":["What did Henry II make the barons do with their castles?","What were the only two English words Rohesia knew?","What was the name of Gilbert and Rohesia''s son?"],"answers":["Pull them down","Gilbert and London","Thomas (Thomas à Becket)"],"notes":"The book says this tale may be only a fairy tale; Becket''s mother was in fact a Norman woman. Its words for Muslims (''Mohametans,'' ''Saracens'') are dated."},{"id":"our-island-story-4-5","title":"Chapter 31: Henry Plantagenet – The Story of Thomas à Becket","steps":["Read chapter 31 aloud together","Narrate how Henry and Becket went from close friends to bitter enemies","Find Canterbury in Kent on a map of England","Add ''Murder of Thomas Becket, 29 December 1170'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 31","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#31"}],"pages":"145–151","questions":["What happened with the beggar and Becket''s cloak?","How did Becket change when he became archbishop?","What did Henry and Becket disagree about over priests who did wrong?"],"answers":["The king pulled off Becket''s fine cloak and gave it to the beggar","He gave up fine clothes, rich food and servants for a plain, hard life","Henry wanted them tried in the king''s courts; Becket wanted bishops'' courts"],"notes":"Becket''s killing in the cathedral is described step by step; preview for younger children."},{"id":"our-island-story-4-6","title":"Chapter 32: Henry Plantagenet – The Story of the Conquest of Ireland","steps":["Read chapter 32 aloud together","Narrate how Henry showed sorrow for Becket''s death and how Ireland came under the English king","Find Ireland, Dublin, and the four nations of the United Kingdom on a map","Add ''Henry II goes to Ireland, 1171–72'' and ''Henry''s penance at Canterbury, 1174'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 32","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#32"}],"pages":"152–156","questions":["How did Henry show he was sorry at Becket''s grave?","Which Irish king asked Henry for help?","What news broke Henry''s heart at the end of his life?"],"answers":["He walked barefoot, prayed all night without food, and let monks beat his back","Dermot","That his favourite son John had rebelled against him too"],"notes":"The book presents the Norman takeover of Ireland as a simple ''joining''; it was an invasion with a long, painful legacy, worth a short talk."},{"id":"our-island-story-4-7","title":"Chapter 33: The Story of Richard Cœur de Lion","steps":["Read chapter 33 aloud together","Narrate why Richard went on crusade and how he was discovered in Austria","Find Palestine and Jerusalem, then Austria and Vienna, on a map; then Sherwood Forest near Nottingham","Add ''Richard I on the Third Crusade, 1190–92'' and ''Richard captured in Austria, 1192'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 33","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#33"}],"pages":"157–161","questions":["What does ''crusade'' mean, and where does the word come from?","What is a truce, and how long was Richard''s truce with Saladin?","What gave away the page in the market?"],"answers":["War of the cross, from the Latin crux","A promise not to fight for a set time; three years","He carried the king''s rich embroidered gloves"],"notes":"The book tells the Crusades only from the Christian side, and Robin Hood is legend. The page is beaten to make him talk."},{"id":"our-island-story-4-8","title":"Chapter 34: Richard Cœur de Lion – The Story of How Blondel Found the King","steps":["Read chapter 34 aloud together","Narrate how Blondel found Richard and how the English got their king back","Find Germany and Trifels Castle, then Châlus in France where Richard died, on a map","Add ''Richard ransomed and freed, 1194'' and ''Richard dies at Châlus, 1199'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 34","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#34"}],"pages":"162–167","questions":["How did Blondel know he had found the king?","What did the English have to pay to free Richard?","What did Richard do with the archer who shot him?"],"answers":["Richard sang back the next part of their song from a window","A large ransom","He forgave him, set him free, and gave him a hundred shillings"],"notes":"Blondel''s search is a well-loved legend, not history. Richard hanging the castle''s soldiers is mentioned briefly."},{"id":"our-island-story-4-9","title":"Chapter 35: John Lackland – The Story of Prince Arthur","steps":["Read chapter 35 aloud together","Narrate how Arthur begged Hubert to spare his eyes","Find Brittany, then Rouen on the River Seine in Normandy, on a map","Add ''Prince Arthur disappears at Rouen, 1203'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 35","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#35"}],"pages":"168–170","questions":["Why was John called Lackland?","Who was the rightful heir to the throne?","What did Hubert do after Arthur pleaded with him?"],"answers":["He was left no lands when his father died","Prince Arthur of Brittany, son of John''s elder brother Geoffrey","He sent the men with the hot irons away"],"notes":"Arthur''s death is a dark story (the blinding threat comes from Shakespeare''s ''King John''); how he really died is unknown. Preview for sensitive children."},{"id":"our-island-story-4-10","title":"Chapter 36: John Lackland – The Story of the Great Charter","steps":["Read chapter 36 aloud together","Narrate how the barons made John seal the Great Charter and what it promised","Find Runnymede on the Thames near Windsor, then the Wash on the east coast, on a map","Add ''Magna Carta sealed at Runnymede, 15 June 1215'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 36","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#36"}],"pages":"171–178","questions":["What was an Interdict?","Who helped the barons write the Great Charter?","Name one promise in Magna Carta."],"answers":["A ban on all church services in the land","Stephen Langton, Archbishop of Canterbury","No free man may be imprisoned or ruined without a lawful judgment (or: justice will not be sold, denied, or delayed)"],"notes":"The book spells it ''Langdon''; the usual spelling is Langton. Surviving copies are now in the British Library and some cathedrals."}]},{"id":"our-island-story-u5","title":"Unit 5: Plantagenet Kings and the Hundred Years'' War (Chapters 37–48)","lessons":[{"id":"our-island-story-5-1","title":"Chapter 37: Henry III. of Winchester – The Story of Hubert de Burgh","steps":["Read chapter 37 aloud together","Narrate how Hubert de Burgh beat the French fleet and how the smith refused to make his chains","Find Dover and Sandwich on the Kent coast, then the Tower of London, on a map","Add ''Hubert de Burgh defeats the French at sea, 1217'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 37","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#37"}],"pages":"179–183","questions":["How old was Henry III when he became king?","What trick did the English sailors use against the French?","What does ''taking sanctuary'' mean?"],"answers":["Nine","They threw quicklime into the wind so it blew into the Frenchmen''s eyes","Fleeing to a church and holding the altar cross so no one dares harm you"]},{"id":"our-island-story-5-2","title":"Chapter 38: Henry III. of Winchester – The Story of Simon de Montfort","steps":["Read chapter 38 aloud together","Narrate what Simon de Montfort did for the people and how his new council worked","Find Lewes in Sussex and Evesham in Worcestershire on a map of England","Add ''Battle of Lewes, 1264'' and ''Simon de Montfort''s Parliament, 1265'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 38","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#38"}],"pages":"184–188","questions":["What did Henry tell Simon he feared more than thunder?","What was special about the laws made at Oxford?","What does ''Parliament'' mean?"],"answers":["Simon himself","They were written in English","Talking-place"],"notes":"The torture of a Jewish man for money is mentioned; the book calls Jews ''despised'' without comment, which is worth discussing. Simon was killed at Evesham in 1265."},{"id":"our-island-story-5-3","title":"Chapter 39: Henry III. – The Story of the Poisoned Dagger","steps":["Read chapter 39 aloud together","Narrate how the Emir''s servant attacked Prince Edward and how Eleanor and the doctor helped him","Find Acre and Jaffa on a map of modern Israel","Add ''Prince Edward survives an assassin at Acre, 1272'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 39","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#39"}],"pages":"189–192","questions":["What did Princess Eleanor do as soon as she heard Edward was wounded?","Why did Edward call the servant who struck the dead slave a coward?","Why did Edward leave Palestine and sail for home?"],"answers":["She sucked the poisoned wound.","Because the slave was already dead, so hitting him was neither brave nor honorable.","He heard his father, Henry III, was ill."],"notes":"The story of Eleanor sucking out the poison is a later legend; chroniclers of the time do not mention it. The attack scene is briefly violent."},{"id":"our-island-story-5-4","title":"Chapter 40: Edward I. – The Little War of Chalons","steps":["Read chapter 40 aloud together","Narrate what a tournament was and how the Count of Chalons broke the rules","Find Chalon-sur-Saône in Burgundy on a map of France","Add ''The Little War of Chalons, 1273'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 40","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#40"}],"pages":"193–195","questions":["How many knights did the Count bring, and how many had been agreed?","Why would Edward not take the Count''s sword himself?","What was given to the winner of a tournament?"],"answers":["Two thousand, though one thousand had been agreed.","The Count had behaved unknightly, so Edward let a common soldier take it.","A crown of bay leaves."]},{"id":"our-island-story-5-5","title":"Chapter 41: Edward I. – The Lawgiver – The Story of the First Prince of Wales","steps":["Read chapter 41 aloud together","Narrate how Edward made money round and how Wales got a Prince who spoke no French or English","Find Caernarfon Castle and Mount Snowdon in North Wales on a map","Add ''Edward I conquers Wales; Llewellyn killed, 1282'' and ''Edward of Caernarfon born, 1284'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 41","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#41"}],"pages":"196–199","questions":["Why did the people feel Edward I was truly an English king?","What did people do with coins before Edward made his new law?","Who was the prince Edward showed to the Welsh barons?"],"answers":["He had an English name, was born and lived in England, spoke English, and loved his people.","They cut them into pieces to make change.","His baby son, born at Caernarvon Castle."],"notes":"The baby-prince story and Merlin''s prophecy are legends; the real title ''Prince of Wales'' was given to young Edward in 1301. Llewellyn''s head being paraded on a spear is mentioned briefly."},{"id":"our-island-story-5-6","title":"Chapter 42: Edward I. – The Hammer of the Scots","steps":["Read chapter 42 aloud together","Narrate how Edward came to choose John Balliol and what treasures he carried off from Scotland","Find Scone near Perth in Scotland and Westminster Abbey in London on a map","Add ''Edward I takes the Stone of Destiny to Westminster, 1296'' and ''Edward I dies, 1307'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 42","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#42"}],"pages":"200–203","questions":["Who was the Maid of Norway, and what happened to her?","What was the Stone of Destiny said to be?","Name two of Edward I''s nicknames."],"answers":["The little heir to Scotland''s throne; she died on her way from Norway.","The stone Jacob used as a pillow when he dreamed of the ladder to heaven.","Any two of Longshanks, Hammer of the Scots, the Lawgiver, Edward of Westminster."],"notes":"The book says the Stone is still at Westminster; it was returned to Scotland in 1996 and is now shown in Perth, travelling to London only for coronations."},{"id":"our-island-story-5-7","title":"Chapter 43: Edward II. of Caernarvon – The Story of King Robert the Bruce and Bohun","steps":["Read chapter 43 aloud together","Narrate how Bruce prepared the ground before battle and what happened when Bohun charged at him","Find Stirling Castle and the Bannock Burn in central Scotland on a map","Add ''Robert the Bruce slays Henry de Bohun, 23 June 1314'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 43","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#43"}],"pages":"204–208","questions":["How did Bruce make the firm ground dangerous for English horses?","What did Bruce mean when he told Randolph a rose had fallen from his crown?","What did Bruce say after defeating Bohun?"],"answers":["He had pits dug, filled with gorse branches, and covered with turf.","Randolph had missed his chance to win honor by letting English horsemen slip past.","That he had broken his good ax."],"notes":"Bohun''s death is described briefly but vividly (a split helmet). The chapter also tells of Piers Gaveston''s killing."},{"id":"our-island-story-5-8","title":"Chapter 44: Edward II. of Caernarvon – The Story of the Battle of Bannockburn","steps":["Read chapter 44 aloud together","Narrate how the camp followers helped win Bannockburn and how Edward II escaped","Find Stirling, the River Forth, Dunbar, and Berwick-upon-Tweed on a map","Add ''Battle of Bannockburn, 24 June 1314'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 44","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#44"}],"pages":"209–211","questions":["What did King Edward think the kneeling Scots were doing?","What was the ''new army'' that frightened the English?","How did Edward II finally get back to England?"],"answers":["Asking for mercy (but they were praying to God).","Servants and camp followers with cloths tied to poles for banners.","In a little fishing boat from Dunbar to Berwick."],"notes":"The chapter ends with Edward II being deposed and murdered in prison (1327); a parent may want to preview the last paragraph."},{"id":"our-island-story-5-9","title":"Chapter 45: Edward III. of Windsor – The Story of the Battle of Sluys","steps":["Read chapter 45 aloud together","Narrate why the Hundred Years'' War began and how the English won at Sluys","Find Sluis (Sluys) in the Netherlands, Ghent in Belgium, and Southampton in England on a map","Add ''English victory at sea at Sluys, 1340; Hundred Years'' War begins'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 45","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#45"}],"pages":"212–215","questions":["Why did Edward III say he should be King of France?","What was the Christopher?","Who finally told King Philip about the defeat, and how?"],"answers":["Because his mother Isabella was sister to the French king who died without a son.","An English ship the French had captured, which the English won back.","His court fool, with a joke that the English were too cowardly to jump into the sea."]},{"id":"our-island-story-5-10","title":"Chapter 46: Edward III. of Windsor – The Story of the Battle of Crecy","steps":["Read chapter 46 aloud together","Narrate how the English archers won at Crécy and why the King let his son ''win his spurs''","Find Crécy-en-Ponthieu in northern France on a map","Add ''Battle of Crécy, 26 August 1346'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 46","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#46"}],"pages":"216–222","questions":["Where did Edward III watch the battle from?","What does ''Ich dien'' mean, and whose motto was it first?","What did Edward say when he picked up the lady''s garter?"],"answers":["A windmill.","''I serve''; the blind King of Bohemia''s.","''Evil be to him who evil thinks'' (Honi soit qui mal y pense)."],"notes":"The Order of the Garter was founded around 1348; the dropped-garter tale and the Bohemian origin of the Prince of Wales feathers are traditions rather than proven history."},{"id":"our-island-story-5-11","title":"Chapter 47: Edward III. of Windsor – The Story of the Siege of Calais","steps":["Read chapter 47 aloud together","Narrate how the six burghers offered themselves and how Queen Philippa saved them","Find Calais on the French coast across the Strait of Dover","Add ''Siege of Calais ends; the six burghers spared, 1347'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 47","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#47"}],"pages":"223–229","questions":["What did Edward do for the poor and old people sent out of Calais?","Who was the first man to offer himself?","How did the six men have to be dressed?"],"answers":["Gave them a dinner and money and let them pass safely.","Eustace de St. Pierre.","In their shirts, bare-headed and barefoot, with ropes round their necks, carrying the keys."]},{"id":"our-island-story-5-12","title":"Chapter 48: Edward III. of Windsor – The Story of the Battle of Poitiers","steps":["Read chapter 48 aloud together","Narrate how the Black Prince won at Poitiers and how he treated King John afterward","Find Poitiers in France and Canterbury Cathedral in Kent on a map","Add ''Battle of Poitiers, 1356'' and ''Black Death reaches England, 1348'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 48","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#48"}],"pages":"230–233","questions":["Who was taken prisoner at Poitiers?","What did the Black Prince do at supper that surprised King John?","What still hangs over the Black Prince''s tomb?"],"answers":["King John of France and his son.","He waited on him at table instead of sitting down.","His black armor."],"notes":"The Black Death is mentioned briefly; it killed perhaps a third or more of England''s people."}]},{"id":"our-island-story-u6","title":"Unit 6: Lancaster, York, and the Wars of the Roses (Chapters 49–59)","lessons":[{"id":"our-island-story-6-1","title":"Chapter 49: Richard II. of Bordeaux – The Story of Wat Tyler''s Rebellion","steps":["Read chapter 49 aloud together","Narrate what started Wat Tyler''s rebellion and what Richard did at Mile End and Smithfield","Find Blackheath, Mile End, Smithfield, and the Tower on a map of London","Add ''Peasants'' Revolt led by Wat Tyler, 1381'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 49","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#49"}],"pages":"234–241","questions":["What was the poll-tax?","What did the people at Mile End ask the King for?","Who struck down Wat Tyler at Smithfield?"],"answers":["A tax on every person over fourteen (''poll'' means head).","To make them and their children free forever.","The Mayor of London."],"notes":"The book calls the peasants ''slaves''; historians say serfs or villeins, who were bound to the land. Includes riots, burnings, and executions."},{"id":"our-island-story-6-2","title":"Chapter 50: How King Richard II. Lost His Throne","steps":["Read chapter 50 aloud together","Narrate how Henry of Bolingbroke came back to England and took Richard''s crown","Find Flint Castle in North Wales and Pontefract in Yorkshire on a map","Add ''Richard II deposed; Henry IV becomes king, 1399'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 50","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#50"}],"pages":"242–244","questions":["What did Richard take that made Henry come back from exile?","Where did Richard and Henry meet?","Which of Richard''s companions left him for Henry?"],"answers":["The lands and money of Henry''s father, John of Gaunt.","Flint Castle in Wales.","His favorite dog."]},{"id":"our-island-story-6-3","title":"Chapter 51: Henry IV. of Bolingbroke – The Story of the Battle of Shrewsbury","steps":["Read chapter 51 aloud together","Narrate why the Percies turned against Henry IV and what happened at Shrewsbury","Find Shrewsbury in Shropshire near the Welsh border on a map","Add ''Battle of Shrewsbury; Hotspur killed, 1403'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 51","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#51"}],"pages":"245–248","questions":["Why was Harry Percy called Hotspur?","Why did several knights dress like the King at Shrewsbury?","How did young Prince James of Scotland become Henry''s prisoner?"],"answers":["Because he was so quick and brave in battle.","Rebels had sworn to kill the King, so the knights acted as decoys.","His ship to France was captured by the English."]},{"id":"our-island-story-6-4","title":"Chapter 52: Henry IV. of Bolingbroke – The Story of How Prince Hal Was Sent to Prison","steps":["Read chapter 52 aloud together","Narrate how Judge Gascoigne sent Prince Hal to prison and what the King said about it","Find Monmouth in Wales, Hal''s birthplace, and Westminster in London on a map","Add ''Henry IV dies; Prince Hal becomes Henry V, 1413'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 52","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#52"}],"pages":"249–251","questions":["What did Prince Hal do in the courtroom?","Why was the King pleased when he heard the story?","What did Hal take from his sleeping father''s bedside?"],"answers":["He struck the judge in the face.","He had a judge who dared do justice and a son who obeyed the law.","The crown."],"notes":"Both stories are traditional tales, made famous by Shakespeare (the verses are from Henry IV, Part 2), and are not proven history."},{"id":"our-island-story-6-5","title":"Chapter 53: Henry V. of Monmouth – The Story of the Battle of Agincourt","steps":["Read chapter 53 aloud together","Narrate the tennis-ball insult and how the tired English army won at Agincourt","Find Harfleur, Azincourt (Agincourt), and Calais in northern France on a map","Add ''Battle of Agincourt, 25 October 1415'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 53","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#53"}],"pages":"252–256","questions":["What present did the Dauphin send Henry?","Why did Henry not wish for more soldiers before the battle?","How did the battle get its name?"],"answers":["Tennis balls.","If they won, the glory was greater; if they died, England lost less.","From the nearby castle of Agincourt."]},{"id":"our-island-story-6-6","title":"Chapter 54: Henry VI. of Windsor – The Story of the Maid of Orleans","steps":["Read chapter 54 aloud together","Narrate how Joan of Arc led the French army and what happened to her","Find Domrémy, Orléans, Reims, and Rouen on a map of France","Add ''Joan of Arc relieves Orléans, 1429'' and ''Hundred Years'' War ends, 1453'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 54","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#54"}],"pages":"257–262","questions":["Why was Joan called the Maid of Orleans?","Where was the Dauphin crowned?","Why did the English say Joan was a witch?"],"answers":["She drove the English away from Orleans.","At Rheims (Reims).","They thought no one else could have done such wonderful things."],"notes":"Joan was burned at Rouen in 1431; consider previewing that sentence. The book dates the war''s end to 1451; most historians use 1453."},{"id":"our-island-story-6-7","title":"Chapter 55: Henry VI. of Windsor – The Red Rose and the White","steps":["Read chapter 55 aloud together","Narrate how the red and white roses became badges and how Richard of York claimed the throne","Find St Albans in Hertfordshire and the city of York on a map of England","Add ''First Battle of St Albans; Wars of the Roses begin, 1455'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 55","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#55"}],"pages":"263–268","questions":["Which rose did the Yorkists wear, and which did the Lancastrians wear?","What did the Duke of York do in the House of Lords?","Whom did the London crowd choose as king at the end?"],"answers":["York wore white; Lancaster wore red.","He sat on the throne and claimed it.","Edward, Duke of York (Edward IV)."],"notes":"The rose-plucking scene comes from Shakespeare, not from records. The deaths of the Duke and his twelve-year-old son at Wakefield (1460) are told in some detail."},{"id":"our-island-story-6-8","title":"Chapter 56: Edward IV. – The Story of Queen Margaret and the Robbers","steps":["Read chapter 56 aloud together","Narrate how Queen Margaret and her son escaped the robbers in the forest","Find Towton in Yorkshire and Hexham in Northumberland on a map","Add ''Battle of Towton, 1461'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 56","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#56"}],"pages":"269–271","questions":["What was the weather like at the battle of Towton?","How did Margaret get away from the first robbers?","What did the second robber do for the Queen and Prince?"],"answers":["A wild storm of wind and snow.","She ran off with her son while they quarreled over her jewels.","He hid them in his cave, fed them, and took them to a ship."],"notes":"The robber story (often set after Hexham, 1464) is a traditional tale, not well documented."},{"id":"our-island-story-6-9","title":"Chapter 57: Edward IV. – The Story of the King-Maker","steps":["Read chapter 57 aloud together","Narrate how Warwick the King-maker changed sides and what happened in the mist at Barnet","Find Barnet north of London and Tewkesbury in Gloucestershire on a map","Add ''Battles of Barnet and Tewkesbury, 1471'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 57","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#57"}],"pages":"272–276","questions":["Why was the Earl of Warwick called the King-maker?","Why did some Red Rose soldiers attack their own side at Barnet?","What badge did Edward''s men wear on their coats?"],"answers":["He was powerful enough to put Edward on the throne, and later Henry again.","In the mist they mistook the Earl of Oxford''s star badge for Edward''s sun.","A golden sun."],"notes":"Several killings are told plainly: Prince Edward stabbed, Henry VI''s mysterious death, and Clarence drowned in wine (a tradition)."},{"id":"our-island-story-6-10","title":"Chapter 58: Edward V. – The Story of the King Who Was Never Crowned","steps":["Read chapter 58 aloud together","Narrate how Richard of Gloucester got both young princes and took the crown","Find Ludlow Castle in Shropshire and Westminster Abbey in London on a map","Add ''Edward V reigns but is never crowned, 1483'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 58","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#58"}],"pages":"277–282","questions":["Where did Queen Elizabeth take her younger son for safety?","Who persuaded her to give up little Richard?","What did the people do when Dr. Shaw called Richard their true king?"],"answers":["Westminster Abbey, as sanctuary.","A bishop.","They stood silent and did not cheer."],"notes":"This picture of Richard III follows Tudor writers who were hostile to him; historians still debate his character. Lord Hastings'' sudden execution is described."},{"id":"our-island-story-6-11","title":"Chapter 59: Richard III. – The Story of the Two Little Princes in the Tower","steps":["Read chapter 59 aloud together","Narrate what happened to the princes in the Tower and how Richard III died at Bosworth","Find Bosworth Field near Market Bosworth in Leicestershire on a map","Add ''Princes in the Tower disappear, 1483'' and ''Battle of Bosworth, 1485'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 59","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#59"}],"pages":"283–285","questions":["Who was the one servant left with the princes?","Where was Richard''s crown found after the battle?","Which lord changed sides in the middle of the battle?"],"answers":["Black Bill.","Under a hawthorn tree.","Lord Stanley."],"notes":"The smothering of the princes is told gently but clearly; preview for young children. What happened to them is still unproven. Richard''s skeleton was found under a Leicester car park in 2012."}]},{"id":"our-island-story-u7","title":"Unit 7: The Early Tudors (Chapters 60–68)","lessons":[{"id":"our-island-story-7-1","title":"Chapter 60: Henry VII. – The Story of a Make-Believe Prince","steps":["Read chapter 60 aloud together","Narrate who Lambert Simnel pretended to be and how Henry VII proved him false","Find Dublin in Ireland and Stoke (East Stoke) in Nottinghamshire on a map","Add ''Lambert Simnel defeated at the Battle of Stoke, 1487'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 60","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#60"}],"pages":"286–289","questions":["How did Henry VII bring the White and Red Roses together?","How did Henry show Londoners that Simnel was a fake?","What jobs did Henry give Lambert Simnel?"],"answers":["He married Elizabeth of York, daughter of Edward IV.","He paraded the real Earl of Warwick through the streets.","Kitchen scullion, then keeper of the King''s falcons."]},{"id":"our-island-story-7-2","title":"Chapter 61: Henry VII. – The Story of Another Make-Believe Prince","steps":["Read chapter 61 aloud together","Narrate Perkin Warbeck''s adventures and how his rebellion ended","Find St Michael''s Mount in Cornwall, Exeter, and Taunton on a map","Add ''Perkin Warbeck surrenders, 1497'' and ''Columbus reaches America, 1492'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 61","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#61"}],"pages":"290–295","questions":["Whom did Perkin Warbeck claim to be?","What were the two great discoveries of this time?","Who brought printing to England?"],"answers":["Richard, Duke of York, the younger prince from the Tower.","Printing and America.","Caxton."]},{"id":"our-island-story-7-3","title":"Chapter 62: Henry VIII. – The Story of the Field of the Cloth of Gold","steps":["Read chapter 62 aloud together","Narrate the splendors of the Field of the Cloth of Gold and what happened in the wrestling match","Find Guînes near Calais in northern France on a map","Add ''Field of the Cloth of Gold, 1520'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 62","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#62"}],"pages":"296–302","questions":["What was Wolsey''s father''s trade?","What happened to King Francis''s grand tent?","Who won the wrestling match between the two kings?"],"answers":["He was a butcher.","A strong wind snapped the ropes and blew it down.","King Francis."]},{"id":"our-island-story-7-4","title":"Chapter 63: Henry VIII. – How the King Became the Defender of the Faith and How the Great Cardinal Died","steps":["Read chapter 63 aloud together","Narrate how Henry won the title Defender of the Faith and why he later quarreled with the Pope","Find Wittenberg in Germany and Leicester in England on a map","Add ''Henry VIII named Defender of the Faith, 1521'' and ''Wolsey dies at Leicester, 1530'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 63","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#63"}],"pages":"303–308","questions":["What does ''Fidei Defensor'' mean, and where can you still see it?","Who began the Reformation in Germany?","Why did Henry turn against Wolsey?"],"answers":["Defender of the Faith; as F.D. or Fid. Def. on British coins.","Martin Luther.","The Pope would not let Henry put away Katherine, and Anne Boleyn turned Henry against him."],"notes":"The book is written from a Protestant viewpoint; a short talk about how Catholics and Protestants each saw these events may help."},{"id":"our-island-story-7-5","title":"Chapter 64: Henry VIII. – The Story of the King''s Six Wives","steps":["Read chapter 64 aloud together","Narrate what happened to each of Henry VIII''s six wives","Find Kleve (Cleves) in Germany, home of Anne of Cleves, on a map","Add ''Henry VIII breaks with Rome, 1534'' and ''Henry VIII dies, 1547'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 64","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#64"}],"pages":"309–312","questions":["Why did Henry dislike Anne of Cleves when he met her?","Why were the church Bibles chained to desks?","What did the rebels of the Pilgrimage of Grace say was their leader?"],"answers":["She looked nothing like her portrait and could speak no English.","Books were costly, so they were chained in case someone stole them.","Poverty."],"notes":"The chapter lists several beheadings, including two of Henry''s wives. The rhyme ''divorced, beheaded, died; divorced, beheaded, survived'' can help children remember the wives."},{"id":"our-island-story-7-6","title":"Chapter 65: Edward VI. – The Story of a Boy King","steps":["Read chapter 65 aloud together","Narrate why the poor were angry about enclosures and what Ket did under the Oak of Reformation","Find Norwich in Norfolk and Bodmin in Cornwall on a map","Add ''Edward VI becomes king, 1547'' and ''Kett''s Rebellion, 1549'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 65","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#65"}],"pages":"313–317","questions":["How did enclosing the common lands hurt poor people?","What did Ket call the great tree in his camp?","Whom did Edward VI name to be queen after him?"],"answers":["They could no longer graze their cows and sheep there.","The Oak of Reformation.","His cousin, Lady Jane Grey."],"notes":"The tale of the Mayor of Bodmin being hanged on his own gallows is grim; parents may wish to preview it."},{"id":"our-island-story-7-7","title":"Chapter 66: The Story of Lady Jane Grey","steps":["Read chapter 66 aloud together","Narrate how Lady Jane Grey was made queen against her wishes and how Mary took the crown","Find the Tower of London on a map of London and Spain on a map of Europe","Add ''Lady Jane Grey reigns nine days; Mary I becomes queen, 1553'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 66","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#66"}],"pages":"318–321","questions":["How did Lady Jane feel when she was offered the crown?","What did Jane say when her father told her the reign was over?","Why did the English dislike Mary''s plan to marry Philip of Spain?"],"answers":["Sad and frightened; she fainted.","That those were the happiest words she had heard since being made queen.","They feared Spain and its cruel Inquisition."],"notes":"Jane''s beheading is told briefly. The nickname ''Bloody Mary'' reflects a Protestant view; a short discussion of both sides can help."},{"id":"our-island-story-7-8","title":"Chapter 67: Mary I. – How the Princess Elizabeth Became a Prisoner","steps":["Read chapter 67 aloud together","Narrate how Princess Elizabeth was taken to the Tower and the story of the little boy with flowers","Trace the River Thames to the Tower of London, then find Hatfield in Hertfordshire","Add ''Princess Elizabeth imprisoned in the Tower, 1554'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 67","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#67"}],"pages":"322–326","questions":["Why did Elizabeth refuse at first to land at the Traitors'' Gate?","What did the little boy bring Elizabeth?","What did Elizabeth say she would rather be when she heard a milkmaid singing?"],"answers":["She said she was no traitor.","Flowers.","A free milkmaid rather than a princess who was a prisoner."]},{"id":"our-island-story-7-9","title":"Chapter 68: Mary I. – How a Candle Was Lit in England Which Has Never Been Put Out","steps":["Read chapter 68 aloud together","Narrate what Latimer said to Ridley and what he meant by the candle","Find Oxford, where Latimer and Ridley died, and Calais on a map","Add ''Latimer and Ridley burned at Oxford, 1555'' and ''Calais lost; Mary I dies, 1558'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 68","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#68"}],"pages":"327–329","questions":["What did Latimer say they would light in England?","Which town did England lose at the end of Mary''s reign?","What did Mary say would be found on her heart?"],"answers":["A candle that would never be put out.","Calais.","The word ''Calais''."],"notes":"The chapter speaks of burnings for religion (about 280 people), without graphic detail. It praises the Protestant martyrs; you might note that Catholics were also put to death for their faith under other rulers."}]},{"id":"our-island-story-u8","title":"Unit 8: Elizabeth I (Chapters 69–73)","lessons":[{"id":"our-island-story-8-1","title":"Chapter 69: Elizabeth – How the Imprisoned Princess Became a Queen","steps":["Read chapter 69 aloud together","Narrate how Elizabeth heard she was queen and what she said entering the Tower","Find Hatfield House in Hertfordshire and the Tower of London on a map","Add ''Elizabeth I becomes queen, 17 November 1558'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 69","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#69"}],"pages":"330–332","questions":["Where was Elizabeth sitting when she heard she was queen?","How did Elizabeth enter the Tower this time compared with before?","What did the people love to call her?"],"answers":["Under an oak tree at Hatfield.","As a free queen, not a prisoner through the Traitors'' Gate.","Good Queen Bess."],"notes":"The book says no one was killed for religion under Elizabeth; in fact many Catholic priests and some others were executed during her reign."},{"id":"our-island-story-8-2","title":"Chapter 70: Elizabeth – The Story of a Most Unhappy Queen","steps":["Read chapter 70 aloud together","Narrate how the Little Douglas helped Mary escape and what happened to her in England","Find Loch Leven in Scotland and Fotheringhay in Northamptonshire on a map","Add ''Mary, Queen of Scots escapes Loch Leven, 1568'' and ''Mary executed, 1587'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 70","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#70"}],"pages":"333–337","questions":["How were Elizabeth and Mary, Queen of Scots related?","What did the Little Douglas do with the castle keys?","How many years was Mary kept a prisoner in England?"],"answers":["They were cousins.","He dropped them in the loch.","Nineteen years."],"notes":"Mary''s execution is described with some detail (the block, her little dog); consider previewing the end."},{"id":"our-island-story-8-3","title":"Chapter 71: Elizabeth – The Story of How England Was Saved from the Spaniards","steps":["Read chapter 71 aloud together","Narrate Drake''s game of bowls and how the Spanish Armada was beaten and scattered","Find Cádiz in Spain, Plymouth in Devon, and the Armada''s route round Scotland and Ireland","Add ''Defeat of the Spanish Armada, 1588'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 71","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#71"}],"pages":"338–341","questions":["What did Drake call his raid on the Spanish harbor?","What was Drake doing when the Armada was sighted?","Why could the English ships fight better than the Spanish ones?"],"answers":["Singeing the King of Spain''s beard.","Playing bowls.","They were smaller and easier to turn, and the wind favored them."],"notes":"Drake''s game of bowls is a much-loved legend that cannot be proven."},{"id":"our-island-story-8-4","title":"Chapter 72: Elizabeth – The Story of Sir Walter Raleigh","steps":["Read chapter 72 aloud together","Narrate the story of Raleigh''s cloak and the servant who threw water over him","Find Virginia and Roanoke Island on a map of the eastern United States","Add ''Raleigh sends colonists to Roanoke, Virginia, 1585'' and ''Sir Philip Sidney dies, 1586'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 72","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#72"}],"pages":"342–345","questions":["How did Raleigh first win the Queen''s notice?","Why did Raleigh name the new land Virginia?","What did Sir Philip Sidney do with his cup of water?"],"answers":["He laid his new cloak over a puddle for her.","To honor Elizabeth, the ''Virgin Queen''.","Gave it to a dying soldier who needed it more."],"notes":"The cloak and water-bucket stories are legends. The book overstates Raleigh''s role with tobacco and potatoes, which reached Europe through several people."},{"id":"our-island-story-8-5","title":"Chapter 73: Elizabeth – The Story of the Queen''s Favorite","steps":["Read chapter 73 aloud together","Narrate the quarrels of Elizabeth and Essex and the story of the lost ring","Find Ireland, where Essex was sent to govern, and the Tower of London on a map","Add ''Earl of Essex executed, 1601'' and ''Elizabeth I dies, 24 March 1603'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 73","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#73"}],"pages":"346–351","questions":["Why did the Queen box Essex''s ears?","What went wrong with the ring Essex sent?","Who became king after Elizabeth?"],"answers":["He rudely turned his back on her.","The boy gave it to the wrong lady, who kept it hidden.","Her cousin James, King of Scotland."],"notes":"The ring story is a legend that appeared long after Essex''s death."}]},{"id":"our-island-story-u9","title":"Unit 9: The Stuarts and the Civil War (Chapters 74–82)","lessons":[{"id":"our-island-story-9-1","title":"Chapter 74: James VI. of Scotland, I. of England – The Story of Guy Fawkes","steps":["Read chapter 74 aloud together","Narrate how the Gunpowder Plot was planned and how Guy Fawkes was caught","Find the Houses of Parliament at Westminster in London and Edinburgh in Scotland on a map","Add ''James VI of Scotland becomes James I of England, 1603'' and ''Gunpowder Plot, 5 November 1605'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 74","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#74"}],"pages":"352–357","questions":["How many barrels of gunpowder were hidden in the cellar?","How was the plot discovered?","How do people in Britain still remember the day?"],"answers":["Thirty-six.","A warning letter sent to a Catholic lord was shown to the King.","With bonfires and fireworks on 5 November."],"notes":"Guy Fawkes'' torture is mentioned briefly. The book notes Catholics were treated even more harshly afterward, a good point for discussion."},{"id":"our-island-story-9-2","title":"Chapter 75: James VI. of Scotland, I. of England – The Story of the \"Mayflower\"","steps":["Read chapter 75 aloud together","Narrate why the Pilgrims left England and how they lived through their first winter","Find Plymouth in Devon, England, and Plymouth in Massachusetts on a map","Add ''Mayflower Pilgrims land at Plymouth, December 1620'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 75","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#75"}],"pages":"358–362","questions":["Why did the Pilgrim Fathers leave England?","What did they name their new town, and why?","How many Pilgrims went home when the Mayflower sailed back?"],"answers":["To be free to worship God in the way they thought right.","Plymouth, after the English town they sailed from.","None."],"notes":"The book calls Native Americans ''wild Red Indians'', which is dated and unfair; the Wampanoag people, including Squanto and Massasoit, helped the colony survive. A short discussion is worthwhile."},{"id":"our-island-story-9-3","title":"Chapter 76: Charles I. – How a Woman Struck a Blow for Freedom","steps":["Read chapter 76 aloud together","Narrate what Jenny Geddes did in St Giles and how Scotland resisted the new Prayer Book","Find Edinburgh and St Giles'' Cathedral on a map of Scotland","Add ''Jenny Geddes riot at St Giles, 23 July 1637'' and ''National Covenant signed, 1638'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 76","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#76"}],"pages":"363–366","questions":["What did King James mean by ''The King can do no wrong''?","What did Jenny Geddes throw at the Dean?","What was the National Covenant?"],"answers":["That whatever the king did was right and people must obey without question.","Her stool.","A paper signed by thousands of Scots promising to fight for freedom of religion."],"notes":"Jenny Geddes may be partly legendary, though the 1637 riot was real. The book uses anti-Catholic cries like ''popery''; a short talk on religious freedom for all fits the chapter''s ending."},{"id":"our-island-story-9-4","title":"Chapter 77: Charles I. – The Story of How the King and the Parliament Quarreled and at Last Fought","steps":["Read chapter 77 aloud together","Narrate how Charles tried to seize the five members and how Cornet Joyce showed his ''commission''","Find Westminster in London and Naseby in Northamptonshire on a map of England","Add ''Civil War begins, 1642'' and ''Battle of Naseby, 1645'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 77","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#77"}],"pages":"367–371","questions":["What did King Charles say when he found the five members had gone?","Why were Cromwell''s soldiers called Ironsides?","What did Cornet Joyce point to when the King asked for his commission?"],"answers":["''I see the birds are flown.''","Cromwell drilled them into strong, disciplined, splendid soldiers","His mounted soldiers behind him"],"notes":"The book presents the Civil War largely from a Parliamentary/Protestant angle; you might ask children what each side was fighting for."},{"id":"our-island-story-9-5","title":"Chapter 78: Charles I. – The Story of How the King Was Brought to His Death","steps":["Read chapter 78 aloud together","Narrate the King''s trial in Westminster Hall and how he behaved before his judges","Find Carisbrooke Castle on the Isle of Wight and Whitehall in London on a map","Add ''Charles I executed, 30 January 1649'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 78","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#78"}],"pages":"372–375","questions":["Where did Charles flee when he escaped from the army?","What was Pride''s Purge?","How many times did Charles refuse to plead at his trial?"],"answers":["Carisbrooke Castle on the Isle of Wight","Colonel Pride''s soldiers turned away members of Parliament who disagreed with the army","Three times"],"notes":"The chapter ends with the King''s beheading described briefly; preview if you have sensitive listeners."},{"id":"our-island-story-9-6","title":"Chapter 79: The Commonwealth – The Adventures of a Prince","steps":["Tell back chapter 79 after reading it aloud together","Narrate Prince Charles''s escape after Worcester: the oak tree, the disguise, and the boat at Brighton","Find Worcester, Lyme Regis, and Brighton on a map of England","Add ''Battle of Worcester and the Royal Oak, 1651'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 79","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#79"}],"pages":"376–380","questions":["Where did Charles hide while Cromwell''s soldiers rode beneath him?","Why did the captain at Lyme Regis not sail?","What did Cromwell call the battle of Worcester?"],"answers":["In an oak tree","His wife locked him in a room","His ''crowning mercy''"],"notes":"Cromwell''s campaign in Ireland (1649–50) is mentioned with real cruelty, including massacres and people sent as slaves to the West Indies; worth a calm word if children ask."},{"id":"our-island-story-9-7","title":"Chapter 80: The Commonwealth – The Lord Protector","steps":["Read chapter 80 aloud together","Narrate how Cromwell broke up the Long Parliament and what he said about the mace","Find the North Sea between England and Holland (the Netherlands) on a map","Add ''Cromwell becomes Lord Protector, 1653'' and ''Cromwell dies, 1658'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 80","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#80"}],"pages":"381–384","questions":["What did Van Tromp hoist to his masthead?","What did Cromwell call the mace?","Why did Cromwell refuse the title of king?"],"answers":["A broom","''That bauble''","His soldiers hated the name of king and might turn against him"]},{"id":"our-island-story-9-8","title":"Chapter 81: Charles II. – How the King Came to His Own, and How Death Walked in the Streets of London","steps":["Read chapter 81 aloud together","Narrate how Charles II came back to England, then describe London during the plague","Find Dover and London on a map of England, and the River Thames","Add ''Restoration of Charles II, 1660'' and ''Great Plague of London, 1665'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 81","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#81"}],"pages":"385–389","questions":["On what special day did Charles II land at Dover?","What was painted on the doors of houses with plague?","How did the boatman care for his family during the plague?"],"answers":["His birthday, 29 May","A red cross and ''The Lord have mercy on us''","He left money and food on a stone and called to them from a distance"],"notes":"The plague scenes (death carts, mass graves) may upset younger children; a short talk about modern sanitation and medicine can help."},{"id":"our-island-story-9-9","title":"Chapter 82: Charles II. – The Story of How London Was Burned","steps":["Read chapter 82 aloud together","Narrate how the Great Fire started, spread, and was finally stopped","Find the Tower of London and the Monument on a map of London","Add ''Great Fire of London, 1666'' and ''Habeas Corpus Act, 1679'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 82","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#82"}],"pages":"390–393","questions":["Where did the Great Fire begin?","How did people finally stop the fire?","What does Habeas Corpus mean, and what did the law promise?"],"answers":["In a baker''s shop","By blowing up houses with gunpowder to make gaps","''Have his body''; prisoners must be brought to trial, not kept in prison forever"],"notes":"The book suggests the King and Catholics in a strongly Protestant way; the fire was an accident, as the chapter itself says."}]},{"id":"our-island-story-u10","title":"Unit 10: The Later Stuarts and the Union (Chapters 83–89)","lessons":[{"id":"our-island-story-10-1","title":"Chapter 83: James II. of England and VII. of Scotland – The Fiery Cross","steps":["Read chapter 83 aloud together","Narrate how a Highland chief used the Fiery Cross to gather his clan","Find Edinburgh and the Highlands of Scotland on a map","Add ''James II becomes king; Argyle''s rising fails, 1685'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 83","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#83"}],"pages":"394–397","questions":["How was the Fiery Cross passed from village to village?","Why did Argyle''s army scatter?","Why couldn''t Argyle fire his pistols when he was caught?"],"answers":["Swift runners carried it from one village to the next","His captains were jealous and would not obey him","The powder got wet when he sprang into the river"],"notes":"The book''s portrayal of James II and Catholics is strongly Protestant-biased; the torture and executions mentioned are real but not detailed."},{"id":"our-island-story-10-2","title":"Chapter 84: James II. of England and VII. of Scotland – The Story of King Monmouth","steps":["Read chapter 84 aloud together","Narrate Monmouth''s rising: his welcome, the battle at Sedgemoor, and his capture","Find Taunton and Sedgemoor in Somerset on a map of England","Add ''Battle of Sedgemoor and the Bloody Assize, 1685'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 84","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#84"}],"pages":"398–399","questions":["What did Monmouth''s followers call him?","Where was the last real battle on English ground fought?","What was the Bloody Assize?"],"answers":["King Monmouth","Sedgemoor","Judge Jeffreys''s cruel court journey punishing the rebels"],"notes":"Mentions torture and mass executions after Sedgemoor; the details are brief but you may want to preview."},{"id":"our-island-story-10-3","title":"Chapter 85: James II. of England and VII. of Scotland – The Story of the Seven Bishops","steps":["Read chapter 85 aloud together","Narrate why the seven bishops refused the King and how the people treated them","Find the Tower of London and Cornwall on a map of England","Add ''Trial of the Seven Bishops, 1688'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 85","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#85"}],"pages":"400–404","questions":["What paper did James order to be read in the churches?","Which bishop came from Cornwall?","How did people celebrate when the bishops were set free?"],"answers":["The Declaration of Indulgence","Trelawney","Bells, bonfires, and seven candles in their windows"]},{"id":"our-island-story-10-4","title":"Chapter 86: James II. of England and VII. of Scotland – William the Deliverer","steps":["Read chapter 86 aloud together","Narrate how William of Orange came to Britain and why it was called the Glorious Revolution","Find Torbay in Devon on a map of England, and Holland across the North Sea","Add ''William of Orange lands at Torbay, 5 November 1688'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 86","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#86"}],"pages":"405–407","questions":["What words were written on William''s banner?","What was the ''Protestant East Wind''?","Who became King and Queen together after James fled?"],"answers":["''I will maintain the liberties of England and the Protestant religion''","The wind that finally blew William''s fleet to Britain","William and Mary"],"notes":"The book calls James a ''Popish tyrant''; its anti-Catholic tone reflects its time and is worth a brief comment."},{"id":"our-island-story-10-5","title":"Chapter 87: William III. and Mary II. – The Story of Brave Londonderry","steps":["Read chapter 87 aloud together","Narrate the siege of Londonderry and how the ships broke the boom","Find Londonderry (Derry), the River Foyle, and the River Boyne in Ireland","Add ''Siege of Londonderry, 1689'' and ''Battle of the Boyne, 1690'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 87","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#87"}],"pages":"408–410","questions":["Who stirred up the people of Londonderry with a rousing sermon?","What had the enemy built across the river?","Which battle ended James''s hopes in Ireland?"],"answers":["A clergyman called Walker","A boom (a bar) to stop ships","The Battle of the Boyne"],"notes":"These events still carry strong feeling in Ireland today; the book tells them from the Protestant side."},{"id":"our-island-story-10-6","title":"Chapter 88: William III. and Mary II. – The Story of a Sad Day in a Highland Glen","steps":["Read chapter 88 aloud together","Narrate why the Macdonald chief was late with his oath and what the Campbells did at Glencoe","Find Glencoe and Killiecrankie in the Scottish Highlands on a map","Add ''Massacre of Glencoe, 1692'' and ''Death of William III, 1702'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 88","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#88"}],"pages":"411–414","questions":["Why was the Macdonald chief late to swear his oath?","How did the Campbells deceive the Macdonalds?","What illness did Queen Mary die of?"],"answers":["He went to the wrong town first, in deep winter","They stayed as guests for nearly two weeks before attacking","Smallpox"],"notes":"The Glencoe massacre is described with killing and families dying in the snow; preview for younger children."},{"id":"our-island-story-10-7","title":"Chapter 89: Anne – How the Union Jack Was Made","steps":["Read chapter 89 aloud together","Narrate how the English and Scottish flags were combined to make the Union Jack","Find Gibraltar on a map of Spain, and Blenheim in Bavaria, Germany","Add ''Act of Union: Great Britain formed, 1707'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 89","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#89"}],"pages":"415–418","questions":["Which fortress did Britain keep after the Peace of Utrecht?","What two crosses were joined first in the flag?","Why is the flag called the Union ''Jack''?"],"answers":["Gibraltar","St. George''s cross and St. Andrew''s cross","James VI signed his name ''Jacques'', which sounds like Jack"],"notes":"The ''Jacques'' explanation for the name ''Jack'' is a traditional story; historians aren''t sure where the name came from. Drawing the three crosses is a good hands-on activity."}]},{"id":"our-island-story-u11","title":"Unit 11: Georgian Britain (Chapters 90–100)","lessons":[{"id":"our-island-story-11-1","title":"Chapter 90: George I. – The Story of the Earl of Mar''s Hunting Party","steps":["Read chapter 90 aloud together","Narrate the Earl of Mar''s ''hunting party'' and what happened at Sheriffmuir","Find Hanover in Germany, and Sheriffmuir near Dunblane in Scotland","Add ''George I becomes king, 1714'' and ''The Fifteen Jacobite rising, 1715'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 90","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#90"}],"pages":"419–423","questions":["What language could George I not speak?","What fell from the Pretender''s standard, worrying the Highlanders?","Who won at Sheriffmuir?"],"answers":["English","The golden ball from the top of the staff","Nobody clearly; each side claimed victory"]},{"id":"our-island-story-11-2","title":"Chapter 91: George II. – The Story of Bonnie Prince Charlie","steps":["Read chapter 91 aloud together","Narrate how Bonnie Prince Charlie won the Highlanders and beat Johnnie Cope","Find Edinburgh, Holyrood, and Prestonpans on a map of Scotland","Add ''Bonnie Prince Charlie lands; Battle of Prestonpans, 1745'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 91","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#91"}],"pages":"424–428","questions":["Why was Walpole called a ''peace minister''?","What did Charlie mean by ''I have thrown away the scabbard''?","How long did the battle of Prestonpans last?"],"answers":["He tried to keep Britain out of wars so it could prosper","There was no turning back; he would win or die","About five minutes"]},{"id":"our-island-story-11-3","title":"Chapter 92: George II. – The Story of Flora Macdonald","steps":["Read chapter 92 aloud together","Narrate how Flora Macdonald helped the Prince escape disguised as her maid ''Betty''","Find Derby, Culloden near Inverness, and the Isle of Skye on a map","Add ''Battle of Culloden, 1746'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 92","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#92"}],"pages":"429–433","questions":["How far south did Charlie''s army march before turning back?","Where was the last Jacobite battle fought?","What disguise did Flora Macdonald bring for the Prince?"],"answers":["Derby","Culloden, near Inverness","A woman''s dress, as her maid Betty"],"notes":"Culloden and its aftermath were very bloody; the book mentions it briefly. The Skye escape is real, though some details are romanticized."},{"id":"our-island-story-11-4","title":"Chapter 93: George II. – The Story of the Black Hole of Calcutta","steps":["Read chapter 93 aloud together","Narrate who Robert Clive was and how he rose from clerk to general","Find Calcutta (Kolkata) and Plassey in Bengal on a map of India","Add ''Black Hole of Calcutta, 1756'' and ''Battle of Plassey, 1757'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 93","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#93"}],"pages":"434–436","questions":["What was Clive''s job before he became a soldier?","What company carried on Britain''s trade with India?","What battle did Clive win against the Indian prince?"],"answers":["A clerk for the East India Company","The East India Company","Plassey"],"notes":"This chapter tells events strictly from a British-imperial view; historians think the Black Hole death toll was exaggerated. Consider discussing how Indians might see the British takeover of their land."},{"id":"our-island-story-11-5","title":"Chapter 94: George II. – The Story of How Canada Was Won","steps":["Read chapter 94 aloud together","Narrate how Wolfe''s men climbed the cliff to the Heights of Abraham","Find Quebec and the St. Lawrence River on a map of Canada","Add ''Wolfe takes Quebec, 1759'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 94","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#94"}],"pages":"437–441","questions":["What poem did Wolfe recite in the boat?","How did the soldiers reach the top of the cliff?","What did Montcalm say when told he was dying?"],"answers":["Gray''s ''Elegy in a Country Churchyard''","By a narrow, steep path, climbing quietly in the dark","He was glad he would not see Quebec surrender"],"notes":"The book presents the British Empire''s growth as a glory; French Canadians and Native peoples saw it differently."},{"id":"our-island-story-11-6","title":"Chapter 95: George III. – The Story of How America Was Lost","steps":["Read chapter 95 aloud together","Narrate why the Americans refused the tax and what happened at the Boston Tea Party","Find Boston on a map of the United States","Add ''Boston Tea Party, 1773'' and ''Declaration of Independence, 4 July 1776'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 95","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#95"}],"pages":"442–447","questions":["Why did the Americans say the taxes were unjust?","How did the young men dress for the Tea Party?","Who led the American army?"],"answers":["They had no members in Parliament","As Native Americans, with painted faces and feathers","George Washington"],"notes":"The book uses the dated term ''Red Indians'' and says they had nearly died out; you may want to correct this and use ''Native Americans''. It tells the Revolution from the British side."},{"id":"our-island-story-11-7","title":"Chapter 96: George III. – A Story of the Spinning-Wheel","steps":["Read chapter 96 aloud together","Narrate Richard Arkwright''s story from penny barber to inventor of the spinning-frame","Find Lancashire and Derbyshire, the heart of the early cotton mills, on a map of England","Add ''Arkwright''s water-powered spinning frame, 1769'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 96","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#96"}],"pages":"448–452","questions":["What was Arkwright''s first trade?","Why did Arkwright''s wife smash his models?","What did the two old women think made the noise of his machine?"],"answers":["A barber","She thought they were making the family poor","Evil spirits"]},{"id":"our-island-story-11-8","title":"Chapter 97: George III. – England Expects That Every Man Will Do His Duty","steps":["Read chapter 97 aloud together","Narrate Nelson''s signal before Trafalgar and his last words aboard the Victory","Find Cape Trafalgar off the south coast of Spain on a map","Add ''Act of Union with Ireland, 1801'' and ''Battle of Trafalgar, 21 October 1805'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 97","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#97"}],"pages":"453–458","questions":["What signal did Nelson send to the whole fleet?","What was the name of Nelson''s ship?","Why did Nelson cover his face and medals when he was carried below?"],"answers":["''England expects that every man will do his duty''","The Victory","So his sailors would not see he was hurt and lose heart"],"notes":"The book calls Napoleon an ''ogre''; children may enjoy hearing that the French saw him very differently."},{"id":"our-island-story-11-9","title":"Chapter 98: George III. – The Battle of Waterloo","steps":["Read chapter 98 aloud together","Narrate the Battle of Waterloo and what Wellington meant by ''Night or Blücher''","Find Waterloo near Brussels in Belgium, the island of Elba, and St. Helena on a map","Add ''Battle of Waterloo, 18 June 1815'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 98","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#98"}],"pages":"459–462","questions":["Why was the Peninsular War given that name?","To which small island was Napoleon first sent?","Where was Napoleon sent after Waterloo?"],"answers":["It was fought in Spain and Portugal, which form a peninsula","Elba","St. Helena, in the Atlantic Ocean"]},{"id":"our-island-story-11-10","title":"Chapter 99: George IV. – The First Gentleman in Europe","steps":["Read chapter 99 aloud together","Narrate how George IV acted Irish, German, and Scottish on his visits, and what he refused","Find Dublin, Hanover, and Leith near Edinburgh on a map","Add ''Catholic Emancipation Act, 1829'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 99","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#99"}],"pages":"463–465","questions":["Why was George III called Farmer George?","Why was George IV called ''the first gentleman in Europe''?","What did the Catholic Emancipation Act allow?"],"answers":["He loved peaceful country life","He was handsome with fine manners","Catholics could sit in Parliament and hold public posts"]},{"id":"our-island-story-11-11","title":"Chapter 100: William IV. – The Story of Two Peaceful Victories","steps":["Read chapter 100 aloud together (see parent note first)","Narrate why the Reform Bill was needed and how slavery in the colonies was ended","Find the West Indies (Caribbean) and West Africa on a world map","Add ''Great Reform Act, 1832'' and ''Slavery abolished in British colonies, 1834'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 100","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#100"}],"pages":"466–470","questions":["What was unfair about which places sent members to Parliament?","Who worked for many years to end slavery?","How much money did Parliament pay the planters?"],"answers":["Empty places sent members while big factory towns sent none","Wilberforce (with Macaulay and others)","Twenty million pounds"],"notes":"This chapter contains a racial slur, written to describe how slave-owners thought; consider rewording it when reading aloud. Note the planters, not the enslaved people, were compensated."}]},{"id":"our-island-story-u12","title":"Unit 12: Queen Victoria''s Reign (Chapters 101–107)","lessons":[{"id":"our-island-story-12-1","title":"Chapter 101: Victoria – The Girl Queen","steps":["Read chapter 101 aloud together","Narrate how young Victoria learned she would be Queen, and how she was woken with the news","Find Kensington Palace and Windsor Castle on a map of London and its surroundings","Add ''Victoria becomes Queen, 1837'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 101","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#101"}],"pages":"471–473","questions":["What did Victoria promise when she learned she would be Queen?","How old was Victoria when she became Queen?","Why couldn''t Victoria be Queen of Hanover?"],"answers":["''I will be good''","Eighteen","Hanover''s law did not allow a woman to rule"]},{"id":"our-island-story-12-2","title":"Chapter 102: Victoria – When Bread Was Dear","steps":["Read chapter 102 aloud together","Narrate why bread was so dear and how the Irish potato famine came about","Find Ireland on a map of the British Isles","Add ''Irish potato famine begins, 1845'' and ''Corn Laws repealed, 1846'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 102","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#102"}],"pages":"474–476","questions":["What did the Corn Laws forbid?","What food did most poor Irish people live on?","Which country across the sea sent help to Ireland?"],"answers":["Bringing in foreign corn until British corn was very dear","Potatoes","The United States"],"notes":"The famine description includes starving children; about a million died and many more emigrated, which the book only touches on."},{"id":"our-island-story-12-3","title":"Chapter 103: Victoria – Peace","steps":["Read chapter 103 aloud together","Narrate Prince Albert''s idea for the Great Exhibition and how the Crystal Palace was built","Find Hyde Park on a map of London","Add ''Great Exhibition in the Crystal Palace, 1851'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 103","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#103"}],"pages":"477–479","questions":["What did Prince Albert hope the Exhibition would do?","Who thought of building with glass and iron?","What did people call the building?"],"answers":["Help world trade and make nations friendlier","Sir Joseph Paxton","The Crystal Palace"],"notes":"The book says the Crystal Palace still stands; it burned down in 1936."},{"id":"our-island-story-12-4","title":"Chapter 104: Victoria – War","steps":["Read chapter 104 aloud together","Narrate Florence Nightingale''s work in the hospitals and the Charge of the Light Brigade","Find the Crimea, the Black Sea, and Sebastopol (Sevastopol) on a map","Add ''Crimean War and the Charge of the Light Brigade, 1854'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 104","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#104"}],"pages":"480–486","questions":["What strange mistake was made with a shipload of boots?","Why was Florence Nightingale called ''the lady with the lamp''?","Why did the Light Brigade charge the Russian guns?"],"answers":["They were all for the left foot","She walked the wards at night with a lamp, caring for the men","An order was misunderstood"],"notes":"The book credits Nightingale with the red cross badge; the Red Cross was actually founded by Henri Dunant in 1863."},{"id":"our-island-story-12-5","title":"Chapter 105: Victoria – The Land of Snow","steps":["Read chapter 105 aloud together","Narrate Franklin''s voyage and how searchers finally learned what happened to his men","Find the Arctic Ocean and northern Canada on a globe or world map","Add ''Franklin''s Arctic expedition sets out, 1845'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 105","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#105"}],"pages":"487–490","questions":["What were Franklin''s two ships called?","How did searchers try to send messages using foxes?","Who kept urging the search for Franklin?"],"answers":["The Terror and the Erebus","They put copper collars with directions on white foxes","Lady Franklin, his wife"],"notes":"The book uses the older word ''Eskimos'' (Inuit). Both ships'' wrecks were finally found in 2014 and 2016, which children may enjoy looking up."},{"id":"our-island-story-12-6","title":"Chapter 106: Victoria – The Siege of Delhi","steps":["Read chapter 106 aloud together (parent may wish to preview)","Narrate why the sepoys refused the new cartridges and how the rising began at Meerut","Find Meerut, Delhi, and Cawnpore (Kanpur) on a map of India","Add ''Indian Rebellion (the ''Mutiny'') begins, 1857'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 106","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#106"}],"pages":"491–495","questions":["Why did the sepoys refuse to use the new cartridges?","Where did the rising first break out?","Whom did the sepoys want to make their Emperor again?"],"answers":["They believed the grease was cow and pig fat, against their religions","Meerut","The old Mughal Emperor at Delhi"],"notes":"The Cawnpore massacre of women and children is described in grim detail; preview it. The book tells only the British side; Indians remember 1857 as a war of independence, and British reprisals were also brutal."},{"id":"our-island-story-12-7","title":"Chapter 107: Victoria – The Pipes at Lucknow","steps":["Read chapter 107 aloud together","Narrate the siege of the Residency and how Jessie heard the pipes","Find Lucknow and the Gomti (Goomtee) River on a map of India","Add ''Relief of Lucknow, 1857'' and ''Crown takes over rule of India, 1858'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 107","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#107"}],"pages":"496–499","questions":["What did Sir Henry Lawrence tell his men before he died?","What sound did Jessie hear?","Who became the first Viceroy of India?"],"answers":["Never yield; die at your post rather than yield","The Highland bagpipes of the relieving army","Lord Canning"],"notes":"The story of Jessie''s dream is a popular legend of the time and may not be literally true. The poem''s ''Indian tiger'' language reflects a colonial viewpoint."}]},{"id":"our-island-story-u13","title":"Unit 13: Empire and the Twentieth Century (Chapters 108–114)","lessons":[{"id":"our-island-story-13-1","title":"Chapter 108: Victoria – Under the Southern Cross","steps":["Read chapter 108 aloud together","Narrate how the first convict colony at Sydney nearly starved and how Governor Phillip shared his food","Find Botany Bay and Sydney on a map of Australia","Add ''Cook lands at Botany Bay, 1770'' and ''First Fleet founds Sydney, 1788'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 108","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#108"}],"pages":"500–504","questions":["Why did Cook name the place Botany Bay?","Why were convicts sent to Australia instead of America?","What did Governor Phillip do with his own flour?"],"answers":["There were so many kinds of plants","America had been lost as a colony","He gave it up to share with everyone"],"notes":"The book calls Aboriginal Australians ''savages'' and ignores that the land was already their home; a short talk about Aboriginal peoples and the effects of colonization is worthwhile."},{"id":"our-island-story-13-2","title":"Chapter 109: Victoria – From Cannibal to Christian","steps":["Read chapter 109 aloud together (see parent note)","Narrate the meeting at Waitangi and what the chiefs said for and against the treaty","Find North Island, South Island, and Waitangi on a map of New Zealand","Add ''Treaty of Waitangi, 1840'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 109","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#109"}],"pages":"505–508","questions":["What missionary bought land in New Zealand for twelve axes?","What did the chief who opposed the treaty fear?","How did most chiefs sign the treaty?"],"answers":["Marsden","That they would become slaves and lose their land","With marks like their tattoo patterns"],"notes":"The chapter title and text describe the Maori in demeaning colonial terms. The Treaty of Waitangi is still debated today, as its English and Maori versions differ and land was later taken."},{"id":"our-island-story-13-3","title":"Chapter 110: Victoria – Boer and Briton","steps":["Read chapter 110 aloud together","Narrate the main causes of the Boer War and how peace was made at Vereeniging","Find Cape Town, Kimberley, Ladysmith, and Mafeking (Mahikeng) on a map of South Africa","Add ''Boer War, 1899–1902'' and ''Death of Queen Victoria, 1901'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 110","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#110"}],"pages":"509–516","questions":["Who were the Uitlanders and what right were they denied?","Which three besieged towns were relieved?","How long did Queen Victoria reign?"],"answers":["Mostly British newcomers in the Transvaal; the right to vote","Kimberley, Ladysmith, and Mafeking","Sixty-three years"],"notes":"The book tells the war from the British side and leaves out the black African population and the British concentration camps where many Boer and African civilians died."},{"id":"our-island-story-13-4","title":"Chapter 111: Edward VII – The Peacemaker","steps":["Read chapter 111 aloud together","Narrate why Edward VII was called the Peacemaker and what happened before his coronation","Find Paris, Rome, Vienna, and The Hague on a map of Europe","Add ''Coronation of Edward VII, 1902'' and ''Entente Cordiale with France, 1904'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 111","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#111"}],"pages":"517–523","questions":["Why was Edward''s coronation delayed?","What did the French call King Edward?","What was the Hague Court set up to do?"],"answers":["He fell dangerously ill and needed an operation","The Uncle of Europe","Settle quarrels between nations by arbitration instead of war"],"notes":"Written just after World War I, the chapter is strongly anti-German; you might point out how wartime feelings shaped the author''s view."},{"id":"our-island-story-13-5","title":"Chapter 112: George V. – Armed Peace","steps":["Read chapter 112 aloud together","Narrate the chain of events from the shooting at Sarajevo to Britain declaring war","Find Sarajevo in Bosnia, Serbia, and Belgium on a map of Europe","Add ''Archduke Franz Ferdinand shot at Sarajevo, June 1914'' and ''Britain enters the war, 4 August 1914'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 112","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#112"}],"pages":"524–531","questions":["Who was shot at Sarajevo?","Which neutral country did Germany march through?","What did the German Chancellor call the treaty protecting Belgium?"],"answers":["Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria and his wife","Belgium","''A scrap of paper''"],"notes":"The causes of WWI are presented one-sidedly, putting all blame on Germany and Austria; historians today give a more shared picture."},{"id":"our-island-story-13-6","title":"Chapter 113: World War","steps":["Read chapter 113 aloud together","Narrate how life in Britain changed during the war, including women''s work and rationing","Find Flanders in Belgium, northern France, and Jutland (Denmark) on a map of Europe","Add ''USA joins the war, April 1917'' and ''Armistice, 11 November 1918'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 113","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#113"}],"pages":"532–540","questions":["What kind of fighting happened along the long line in France and Belgium?","How did women help at home during the war?","What helped bring the United States into the war?"],"answers":["Trench warfare","They took over jobs in factories, farms, offices, and transport","Germany''s submarines sinking ships, including neutral ones"],"notes":"Mentions poison gas, air raids, and huge losses; the tone is strongly wartime-British. Older children may want to talk about how ordinary soldiers on both sides suffered."},{"id":"our-island-story-13-7","title":"Chapter 114: The Hope of the Future","steps":["Read the final chapter aloud together (skip the list of kings at the end)","Narrate what the League of Nations was meant to do and how the author compares nations to growing children","Find Geneva in Switzerland and Paris, where the peace conference met, on a map of Europe","Add ''Treaty of Versailles, 28 June 1919'' and ''League of Nations founded, 1920'' to your timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 114","url":"https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html#114"}],"pages":"541–548","questions":["Which country appeared again as an independent state after the war?","Where was the League of Nations to have its permanent office?","What did League members promise to do before going to war?"],"answers":["Poland","Geneva","Bring their quarrel to the League and give it time to settle it peacefully"],"notes":"The League failed to prevent World War II; you might close the book by mentioning the United Nations, which replaced it in 1945."}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"“Our Island Story: A Child''s History of England (US edition: An Island Story: A History of England for Boys and Girls, 1920)” by H. E. Marshall (1905), in the public domain. Free full text: https://digital.library.upenn.edu/women/marshall/england/england.html. Lesson plan by Freerange."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (listing_id, version) do update set content = excluded.content;
insert into public.listings (id, seller_name, seller_kind, seller_verified, title, subjects, grade_min, grade_max, philosophies, format, price, description, includes, lessons, lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement, family_style, lesson_steps, cover, color, esa_eligible, created_at, package_id, package_version, license, attribution, outline) values ('l-lib-parables-from-nature', 'Margaret Gatty', 'open', false, 'Parables from Nature', array['reading', 'science', 'bible']::text[], 2, 5, array['charlotte_mason', 'faith_based', 'nature_forest', 'classical']::text[], 'online', 0, 'Margaret Gatty''s beloved Victorian nature parables, a Charlotte Mason favorite: a caterpillar who learns faith from a lark, a robin who sings through a hard winter, a dragonfly grub wondering what lies above the pond, a spider exploring the ''empty space'' of a church. Read a parable (or half of a long one) aloud, have your child tell it back, then draw, observe, or try a small experiment that matches it. Each tale carries a Christian lesson alongside real natural history, and the old illustrated editions are free to read online.', array['50 lessons in 7 units covering all 37 parables', 'Long parables split into 2–4 sittings; two very short ones paired', 'A narration prompt and a nature-study drawing, observation, or copywork step every lesson', '2–3 questions per lesson, with answers', 'Parent notes on sad moments, Victorian views, and the real science behind the stories', 'Links to the page where each reading starts in free scanned editions']::text[], 50, 3, 20, 'high', true, '{}'::text[], '🦋', '#3D9970', false, '2026-11-11T12:00:00.000Z', 'pk-lib-parables-from-nature', 1, '{"kind":"public_domain"}'::jsonb, '“Parables from Nature (London: George Bell & Sons, 1882, 2 vols.; with the further parables from the G. P. Putnam''s Sons edition, New York, 1880)” by Margaret Gatty (Mrs. Alfred Gatty) (1855), in the public domain. Free full text: https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu01gattuoft. Lesson plan by Freerange.', '[{"title":"Unit 1: A Lesson of Faith to A Lesson of Hope","lessons":["Lesson 1: A Lesson of Faith","Lesson 2: The Law of Authority and Obedience","Lesson 3: The Unknown Land","Lesson 4: Knowledge Not the Limit of Belief","Lesson 5: Training and Restraining","Lesson 6: The Light of Truth","Lesson 7: Waiting","Lesson 8: A Lesson of Hope"]},{"title":"Unit 2: The Circle of Blessing, The Law of the Wood, Active and Passive","lessons":["Lesson 9: The Circle of Blessing, part 1","Lesson 10: The Circle of Blessing, part 2","Lesson 11: The Law of the Wood, part 1","Lesson 12: The Law of the Wood, part 2","Lesson 13: Active and Passive, part 1","Lesson 14: Active and Passive, part 2"]},{"title":"Unit 3: Daily Bread, Not Lost but Gone Before, Motes in the Sunbeam","lessons":["Lesson 15: Daily Bread, part 1","Lesson 16: Daily Bread, part 2","Lesson 17: Not Lost, but Gone Before, part 1","Lesson 18: Not Lost, but Gone Before, part 2","Lesson 19: Motes in the Sunbeam"]},{"title":"Unit 4: Red Snow to The Deliverer","lessons":["Lesson 20: Red Snow, part 1","Lesson 21: Red Snow, part 2","Lesson 22: Whereunto?","Lesson 23: Purring When You''re Pleased","Lesson 24: The Voices of the Earth","Lesson 25: The Master of the Harvest","Lesson 26: The Deliverer"]},{"title":"Unit 5: Inferior Animals to Night and Day","lessons":["Lesson 27: Inferior Animals, part 1","Lesson 28: Inferior Animals, part 2","Lesson 29: Inferior Animals, part 3","Lesson 30: The General Thaw","Lesson 31: The Light of Life","Lesson 32: Gifts","Lesson 33: Night and Day"]},{"title":"Unit 6: Kicking to Birds in the Nest","lessons":["Lesson 34: Kicking, part 1","Lesson 35: Kicking, part 2","Lesson 36: Kicking, part 3","Lesson 37: Imperfect Instruments, part 1","Lesson 38: Imperfect Instruments, part 2","Lesson 39: Cobwebs","Lesson 40: Birds in the Nest"]},{"title":"Unit 7: The Later Parables, Consequences to Unopened Parcels","lessons":["Lesson 41: Consequences, and The Cause and the Causer, part 1","Lesson 42: The Cause and the Causer, part 2","Lesson 43: Ghosts","Lesson 44: These Three","Lesson 45: The Universal Language and See-Saw","Lesson 46: A Vision","Lesson 47: Unopened Parcels, part 1","Lesson 48: Unopened Parcels, part 2","Lesson 49: Unopened Parcels, part 3","Lesson 50: Unopened Parcels, part 4"]}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set seller_name = excluded.seller_name, seller_kind = excluded.seller_kind, seller_verified = excluded.seller_verified, title = excluded.title, subjects = excluded.subjects, grade_min = excluded.grade_min, grade_max = excluded.grade_max, philosophies = excluded.philosophies, format = excluded.format, price = excluded.price, description = excluded.description, includes = excluded.includes, lessons = excluded.lessons, lessons_per_week = excluded.lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson = excluded.minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement = excluded.parent_involvement, family_style = excluded.family_style, lesson_steps = excluded.lesson_steps, cover = excluded.cover, color = excluded.color, esa_eligible = excluded.esa_eligible, package_id = excluded.package_id, package_version = excluded.package_version, license = excluded.license, attribution = excluded.attribution, outline = excluded.outline;
insert into public.package_releases (listing_id, version, content) values ('l-lib-parables-from-nature', 1, '{"units":[{"id":"parables-from-nature-u1","title":"Unit 1: A Lesson of Faith to A Lesson of Hope","lessons":[{"id":"parables-from-nature-1-1","title":"Lesson 1: A Lesson of Faith","steps":["Read “A Lesson of Faith” aloud together","Have your child tell the story back: the dying Butterfly, the worried Caterpillar, and the Lark''s news","Draw the life cycle of a butterfly in four boxes: egg, caterpillar, chrysalis, butterfly"],"links":[{"label":"Read “A Lesson of Faith”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu01gattuoft/page/n18/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 1, pp. 1–8","questions":["What did the dying Butterfly ask the Caterpillar to do?","What food did the Lark say the baby creatures would eat?","What did the Caterpillar find hatching from the Butterfly''s eggs?"],"answers":["Take care of her eggs and feed the babies dew and honey","Cabbage leaves","Little green caterpillars"],"notes":"Gatty wrote these parables to teach Christian faith through nature; each opens with a Bible verse. This one pictures the hope of life after death, so be ready for questions."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-1-2","title":"Lesson 2: The Law of Authority and Obedience","steps":["Read “The Law of Authority and Obedience” aloud together","Narrate the Traveller-bee''s adventure: the dining room, the speech in the hive, and the quarrelling swarm","Draw a honeybee and label the queen, the drone, and the worker"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Law of Authority and Obedience”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu01gattuoft/page/n26/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 1, pp. 9–20","questions":["What did the children say makes a bee grow into a queen instead of a worker?","Why did the runaway swarm get nothing done?","What did the old Relation want the Traveller-bee to see when they played at living together?"],"answers":["The food the nurse-bees give it and the shape of its cell","They could not agree on anything because no one led them","That even two creatures living together need someone to lead and someone to follow"],"notes":"This story argues for kings and against the idea that everyone should be equal, a common Victorian view. Older children can talk about whether they agree. The bee facts (queens raised by special food and cells) are accurate."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-1-3","title":"Lesson 3: The Unknown Land","steps":["Read “The Unknown Land” aloud together","Ask your child to tell how the Mother Warbler answered her children''s worries about leaving","On a world map, find Britain and Africa and trace the way small warblers fly south each autumn"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Unknown Land”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu01gattuoft/page/n38/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 1, pp. 21–33","questions":["What is the ''Unknown Land'' the Sedge Warblers sing about?","Who laughed at the young Warbler and told him there might be no such land?","What did the young birds do after their parents were shot?"],"answers":["The faraway country where they will fly for the winter","A Magpie","They flew off to the Unknown Land on their own, trusting the call"],"notes":"The parent birds are killed by a hunter near the end, which may upset sensitive listeners. The ''Unknown Land'' is both bird migration and a picture of heaven."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-1-4","title":"Lesson 4: Knowledge Not the Limit of Belief","steps":["Read “Knowledge Not the Limit of Belief” aloud together","Narrate the argument between the Zoophyte, the Seaweed, and the Bookworm","Draw something seen through a magnifying glass or microscope, next to how it looks to the bare eye"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Knowledge Not the Limit of Belief”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu01gattuoft/page/n51/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 1, pp. 34–46","questions":["What did the Bookworm try to explain that the Seaweed and Zoophyte could not understand?","How did the Naturalist find out the Seaweed was a plant?","Why could the Bookworm never visit his two friends again?"],"answers":["What seeing is","He dissolved its limy coat and looked at its fruit under a microscope","Their books were bound in Russia leather, and he hated the smell"],"notes":"A good chance to talk about how scientists change their minds when they look more closely. Coralline seaweeds really are red algae, as the Naturalist decides."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-1-5","title":"Lesson 5: Training and Restraining","steps":["Read “Training and Restraining” aloud together","Narrate what the Wind told each flower and what the garden looked like after the storm","Look at a garden or houseplant that is staked or trained, and draw it with its support"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Training and Restraining”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu01gattuoft/page/n64/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 1, pp. 47–56","questions":["What did the Wind say was wrong with the way the Gardener treated the flowers?","What happened to the Convolvulus when her pole fell?","What did the young lady say she finally understood?"],"answers":["He kept them tied up, clipped, and trained instead of letting them grow as they liked","Her flowers lay in the wet soil and were choked with mud","That people, like flowers, need training and restraint, or the garden turns into a wilderness"]},{"id":"parables-from-nature-1-6","title":"Lesson 6: The Light of Truth","steps":["Read “The Light of Truth” aloud together","Narrate the four travellers who saw the Will-o''-the-Wisp and what happened to each","Draw a lighthouse and a Will-o''-the-Wisp side by side, each warning travellers away from danger"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Light of Truth”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu01gattuoft/page/n74/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 1, pp. 57–66","questions":["What does the Will-o''-the-Wisp say its light is for?","Who took the light for his mother''s lantern?","What did the Naturalist compare the Will-o''-the-Wisp to?"],"answers":["To warn travellers away from the dangerous swamp","Little Colin, the farmer''s son","A lighthouse that warns sailors away from rocks"],"notes":"Two travellers (a statesman and a young woman) sink in the bog early in the story. Will-o''-the-wisps are faint lights over marshes, probably from burning marsh gas; scientists still are not completely sure."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-1-7","title":"Lesson 7: Waiting","steps":["Read “Waiting” aloud together","Ask your child to tell the Mole''s advice and the story of the young Ox''s horns","Copy the Mole''s saying “Everything will fit in at last” in your best handwriting"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Waiting”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu01gattuoft/page/n84/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 1, pp. 67–77","questions":["Why was the House Cricket unhappy before there were houses?","Why did the young Ox keep butting everything with his head?","What finally made the Crickets happy?"],"answers":["He loved warmth but had only cold, damp holes to live in","His horns were about to grow","The first fires on people''s hearthstones gave them warm homes"],"notes":"An Ape briefly suggests the crickets should kill themselves; the Mole talks them out of it. You may want to skip or soften that sentence for young listeners."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-1-8","title":"Lesson 8: A Lesson of Hope","steps":["Read “A Lesson of Hope” aloud together","Narrate the argument between the Owl and the Raven during the storm","Draw the trees in the moonlight after the storm, with the Owl in his hollow"],"links":[{"label":"Read “A Lesson of Hope”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu01gattuoft/page/n95/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 1, pp. 78–85","questions":["What did the Owl keep calling the moon?","What did the Raven say was ''the law of life''?","How did the trees feel when the moon came out after the storm?"],"answers":["The moon that shone in Paradise","Destruction","They were sorry they had doubted and were full of hope again"],"notes":"The storyteller has just lost both parents; the story is a picture of hope in grief. The language is poetic, so pause to explain words like ''transitory'' and ''revulsion''."}]},{"id":"parables-from-nature-u2","title":"Unit 2: The Circle of Blessing, The Law of the Wood, Active and Passive","lessons":[{"id":"parables-from-nature-2-1","title":"Lesson 9: The Circle of Blessing, part 1","steps":["Read “The Circle of Blessing” from the start up to the drought on the mountain (p. 92)","Narrate the quarrel between the Sea and the Vapours, and where the trade-winds carried the Vapours","Draw the water cycle: sea, rising vapour, clouds, rain, and rivers running back to the sea, with arrows"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Circle of Blessing”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu01gattuoft/page/n103/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 1, pp. 86–92","questions":["Why did the young man on the ship complain about the mists?","What did the Vapours leave behind in the Sea when they rose?","What happens to the Vapours when they reach cooler air high up?"],"answers":["The hot, steamy calm near the equator made everyone miserable","Their salts","They cling together into drops, grow heavy, and fall as rain"]},{"id":"parables-from-nature-2-2","title":"Lesson 10: The Circle of Blessing, part 2","steps":["Read “The Circle of Blessing” from the drought on the mountain to the end","Ask your child to follow the water''s journey: rain, the Sundews, the Waterfall, the lake, the corn, the child''s pinafore, and back to the sea","Add these stops to yesterday''s water-cycle drawing, and copy the line “Small and great, great and small, we all depend on each other”"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Circle of Blessing” from p. 92","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu01gattuoft/page/n109/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 1, pp. 92–104","questions":["How did the Sundews keep their sparkling drops when the other leaves dried?","What keeps the lake in the park fresh and pure?","What did the Vapours leave behind in the caves when the Earth asked for something in return?"],"answers":["Their sticky juices held the moisture longer","It gives out water through a stream at one end as it receives water at the other","Stalactites and crystals"],"notes":"A lovely science reading: evaporation, trade-winds, condensation, groundwater, and stalactites are all here. Gatty ends with a line from the Benedicite hymn."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-2-3","title":"Lesson 11: The Law of the Wood, part 1","steps":["Read “The Law of the Wood” up to “So things went on in the old way” (p. 111)","Narrate why the Birch and the Spruce-firs quarrelled","On a walk, find an evergreen and feel whether its inner branches are green or brown and dry"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Law of the Wood”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu01gattuoft/page/n122/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 1, pp. 105–111","questions":["What did the Spruce-firs shout when the Birch asked them to give way?","Why did the Wood-pigeons not want to nest in the fir grove?","How were the Spruce-firs hurting the Birch?"],"answers":["“Never!”","They wanted more breeze and leafy shade than the thick, close branches gave","Their branches scratched off pieces of his papery bark"]},{"id":"parables-from-nature-2-4","title":"Lesson 12: The Law of the Wood, part 2","steps":["Read “The Law of the Wood” from “So things went on in the old way” to the end","Narrate what became of the Spruce-fir grove and the one fir that survived","Copy the Birch''s words “Mutual accommodation is the law of the wood”"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Law of the Wood” from p. 111","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu01gattuoft/page/n128/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 1, pp. 111–119","questions":["Why did the inside branches of the firs turn brown and dry?","Which tree did the Birch point to as an example of making room for others?","What happened to the last Spruce-fir?"],"answers":["They grew so close that no fresh air could blow through","The Beech","It was made into a Christmas tree"]},{"id":"parables-from-nature-2-5","title":"Lesson 13: Active and Passive, part 1","steps":["Read “Active and Passive” up to the sailor saying “Anyhow, boys, we''ll watch” (p. 129)","Narrate what the Weathercock and the Dial each wished for, and what old Bowman noticed in the churchyard","Make a simple sundial with a stick in the ground and mark where its shadow falls each hour"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Active and Passive”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu01gattuoft/page/n137/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 1, pp. 120–129","questions":["Why did the Weathercock envy the Dial, and why did the Dial envy the Weathercock?","What did the motto on the Dial''s brass plate say?","Why was Bowman worried about the change of wind at three o''clock?"],"answers":["The Weathercock wanted the Dial''s rest; the Dial wanted the Weathercock''s busy, useful life","“Watch, for ye know not the hour”","It came round with the turn of the tide and would blow a gale straight onto the shore all night"]},{"id":"parables-from-nature-2-6","title":"Lesson 14: Active and Passive, part 2","steps":["Read “Active and Passive” from the night of the storm to the end","Narrate the rescue of the ship: who went out in the boat and how the village found out","Draw the shining Weathercock and the cleaned Dial, and copy the Captain''s lesson: “Everything is useful in its place at the appointed time”"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Active and Passive” from p. 129","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu01gattuoft/page/n146/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 1, pp. 129–144","questions":["What did the blue lights in the night mean?","Who was holding the helm of the ship all morning?","What reward did the Captain give the Dial and the Weathercock?"],"answers":["A ship was in distress and calling for help","The old Captain from the Hall","He had the Dial''s brass plate cleaned and the Weathercock gilded"],"notes":"A tense shipwreck story with a happy ending. The title comes from Milton''s line “They also serve who only stand and wait.”"}]},{"id":"parables-from-nature-u3","title":"Unit 3: Daily Bread, Not Lost but Gone Before, Motes in the Sunbeam","lessons":[{"id":"parables-from-nature-3-1","title":"Lesson 15: Daily Bread, part 1","steps":["Read “Daily Bread” up to the Tortoise disappearing into his winter bed (p. 158)","Narrate the Robin''s talks with the Tortoise, the Blackbird, and the Woodlark","Draw the Tortoise''s winter hole under the big stone, filled with leaves"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Daily Bread”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu01gattuoft/page/n162/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 1, pp. 145–158","questions":["Why was the Tortoise cross with the Robin for singing?","What had the Woodlark noticed about every past winter?","Why did the Tortoise think the ground was a good place to sleep through winter?"],"answers":["He was trying to go to sleep for the winter, and he thought there was nothing to sing about","Little bits of food always turned up somehow","It is much warmer under the ground than on the cold, damp surface"]},{"id":"parables-from-nature-3-2","title":"Lesson 16: Daily Bread, part 2","steps":["Read “Daily Bread” from the snowstorm to the end","Narrate how the Robin kept singing and how he finally found food","Put out crumbs or seeds for winter birds this week, and copy the verse at the head of the story"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Daily Bread” from p. 158","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu01gattuoft/page/n175/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 1, pp. 158–167","questions":["What happened to the holly berries the Robin had been eating?","Who heard the Robin''s song and put out bread for him?","What did the Tortoise think of the Robin''s story in the spring?"],"answers":["Other birds ate them, and the gardener cut the last branches to decorate the house","The sad father who owned the house","He did not believe it and thought the Robin was telling travellers'' tales"],"notes":"The father is grieving his wife, who died on this day; the Robin''s song comforts him. A good story to read near Christmas."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-3-3","title":"Lesson 17: Not Lost, but Gone Before, part 1","steps":["Read “Not Lost, but Gone Before” up to the Frog saying “Go to rest, little fellow, and awake in hope” (p. 179)","Narrate the Grub''s ride on the Frog''s back and what the Frog saw on the bulrush","Look at a picture of a dragonfly nymph and an adult dragonfly, and draw them side by side"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Not Lost, but Gone Before”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu01gattuoft/page/n185/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 1, pp. 168–179","questions":["What did the Dragon-fly Grub want to know?","What happened when the Grub''s face came out of the water?","What did the Frog see climbing up the bulrush stalk?"],"answers":["Where the Frog goes when he leaves the pond","He felt a terrible shock, fell back into the pond, and nearly died","A grub that split open and came out as a shining dragonfly"]},{"id":"parables-from-nature-3-4","title":"Lesson 18: Not Lost, but Gone Before, part 2","steps":["Read “Not Lost, but Gone Before” from the Grub''s promise to the end","Narrate the promise each brother made and why none of them came back","If you can, visit a pond on a sunny day and watch for dragonflies; draw one with its four wings"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Not Lost, but Gone Before” from p. 179","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu01gattuoft/page/n196/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 1, pp. 179–188","questions":["What did the Grub promise his friends before he went up?","Why could the Dragon-fly never go back to tell them?","Who was waiting to welcome each new Dragon-fly?"],"answers":["That he would come back and tell them if the great hopes were true","Touching the water gave him a deadly shock, so his wings carried him away","The ones who had gone before"],"notes":"This is a parable about death and heaven: the ones who have died cannot come back to tell us, but they are “not lost, but gone before.” A gentle story for a child who has lost someone."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-3-5","title":"Lesson 19: Motes in the Sunbeam","steps":["Read “Motes in the Sunbeam” aloud together","Narrate the game Kate and Undine played with the box","Shine a flashlight or let a sunbeam into a dim room and watch the dust sparkle; then copy “Sunshine is like love: it makes everything shine with its own beauty”"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Motes in the Sunbeam”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu01gattuoft/page/n206/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 1, pp. 189–195","questions":["What did the two girls try to catch in the box?","What did Undine first think the motes were?","Why does the dust look like stars, according to Mamma?"],"answers":["The bright specks dancing in the sunbeam","Stars","Because the sun shines on it"],"notes":"A short, quiet Christmas story. The hymn mentioned is “Christians, Awake, Salute the Happy Morn”; you might sing or listen to it together."}]},{"id":"parables-from-nature-u4","title":"Unit 4: Red Snow to The Deliverer","lessons":[{"id":"parables-from-nature-4-1","title":"Lesson 20: Red Snow, part 1","steps":["Read “Red Snow” up to “But he was interrupted at last” (p. 11)","Narrate why Siegfried felt so sorry for Mont Blanc","Find the Alps and Mont Blanc on a map of Europe, and draw the snowy mountain with its rosy patch"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Red Snow”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu02gattuoft/page/n12/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 2, pp. 1–11","questions":["What did Siegfried see on the snow of Mont Blanc that he called a rose?","Why did Siegfried cry so hard in church?","What did the farmer''s son say the pink snow was?"],"answers":["A round, rosy-coloured patch","The pastor said the mountain was lifeless and God-deserted, like a hard, wicked heart","Red snow that sometimes falls out of the sky"],"notes":"The pastor''s sermon about people ''appointed to destruction'' is harsh, and the story means it to be; the next part corrects it."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-4-2","title":"Lesson 21: Red Snow, part 2","steps":["Read “Red Snow” from the stranger''s arrival to the end","Narrate what the stranger showed Siegfried and his mother through the microscope","Draw the red snow plant as it looked under the microscope: tiny red balls, some splitting into four or eight"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Red Snow” from p. 11","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu02gattuoft/page/n22/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 2, pp. 11–23","questions":["What was the red colour on the snow really?","How does one red snow plant make new ones?","What did Siegfried grow up to become?"],"answers":["Millions of tiny plants growing in the snow","Its red inside divides into small balls that grow, burst out of the cell, and start new plants","A pastor who never gave up hope for anyone"],"notes":"Red or ''watermelon'' snow is real: it is caused by tiny algae and can still be seen on high mountains in summer. You could look up photographs together."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-4-3","title":"Lesson 22: Whereunto?","steps":["Read “Whereunto?” aloud together","Narrate what each creature (Star-fish, Limpet, Sea-mat, Coralline, and the Sea) said the tangle was for","Draw a big oarweed (kelp) plant with some of the creatures living on and around it"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Whereunto?”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu02gattuoft/page/n35/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 2, pp. 24–37","questions":["How did the gloomy visitor accidentally save the Star-fish?","What does the blue-eyed Limpet use the tangle for?","What did the great Sea roar that sea-weeds are for?"],"answers":["He flicked her with his stick into the shade of the tangle","As her home and her food","To keep the sea pure, sweet, and healthy by taking in bad vapours and giving out good ones"],"notes":"Each creature thinks the seaweed exists just for it, which is the joke. If you live near a rocky shore, visit at low tide to look for kelp, limpets, crabs, and starfish."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-4-4","title":"Lesson 23: Purring When You''re Pleased","steps":["Read “Purring When You''re Pleased” aloud together","Narrate how the two kittens were different and what happened when the children chose them","Draw Puss Missy and Puss Master, and copy the title as a reminder to say thank you"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Purring When You''re Pleased”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu02gattuoft/page/n49/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 2, pp. 38–46","questions":["Why did the mother cat scold Puss Missy?","Why did the boy choose Puss Missy?","Why was the pale little cousin so quiet and unsmiling?"],"answers":["For purring and showing thanks so freely, which she thought was beneath her dignity","Because she purred when she was pleased","Her parents had died, and her strict guardian had taught her to hide her feelings"],"notes":"A sweet story about thankfulness and about a grieving child who learns to show her feelings again."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-4-5","title":"Lesson 24: The Voices of the Earth","steps":["Read “The Voices of the Earth” aloud, pausing to explain hard words","Ask your child to tell what made the Wind so unhappy and how the Earth answered each complaint","Over a day, list the happy and thankful sounds you hear (laughter, birdsong, a thank-you) that the Wind would carry"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Voices of the Earth”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu02gattuoft/page/n58/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 2, pp. 47–55","questions":["Why did the Wind wish it could stop blowing?","What happy sounds did the Earth say outnumber the sad ones?","What did the Earth and the Wind agree to at the end?"],"answers":["It had to carry people''s curses, cries, and sins as well as their prayers","Healthy breathing, children''s laughter, happy love, and everyday thanks","The Wind would keep working as long as good outweighs evil in the world"],"notes":"This is the most abstract parable in the book: a poetic dialogue with no plot. For younger children, read it slowly in two halves or simply talk about the idea of the wind carrying our words."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-4-6","title":"Lesson 25: The Master of the Harvest","steps":["Read “The Master of the Harvest” aloud together","Narrate the year of the wheat: drought, rain, weak plants, storm, too much rain, and harvest","Draw a stalk of wheat at three stages (green blade, flowering ear, golden ripe ear), or plant a few wheat or grass seeds and watch them sprout"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Master of the Harvest”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu02gattuoft/page/n67/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 2, pp. 56–69","questions":["What did the Master of the Harvest do every time the weather went wrong?","What did his wife write in the back of her Bible?","How did the farmer change after reading her Bible?"],"answers":["He grumbled and complained","A Bible verse, the date, and a short prayer each time","He stopped complaining and grew patient and hopeful like her"],"notes":"The farmer''s wife is ill throughout and dies before the harvest is gathered. The story is about patience and about God''s patience with us."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-4-7","title":"Lesson 26: The Deliverer","steps":["Read “The Deliverer” aloud together","Ask your child where the different people expected the Deliverer to be born, and where He really came","Copy the names from Isaiah: “Wonderful, Counsellor, The Mighty God, The Everlasting Father, The Prince of Peace”"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Deliverer”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu02gattuoft/page/n81/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 2, pp. 70–79","questions":["Where did most people expect the Deliverer to be born?","What did the wise men and philosophers hope He would be?","Who first heard the news of His birth?"],"answers":["In a king''s palace","A great teacher of reason and wisdom","Shepherds watching their flocks at night"],"notes":"A Christmas meditation rather than a nature story. Good to read during Advent alongside Luke 2."}]},{"id":"parables-from-nature-u5","title":"Unit 5: Inferior Animals to Night and Day","lessons":[{"id":"parables-from-nature-5-1","title":"Lesson 27: Inferior Animals, part 1","steps":["Read “Inferior Animals” up to the heading “What the Rook Says” (p. 88)","Narrate what the storyteller wonders about as he hides by the hedge and watches the rooks","Watch a flock of crows, rooks, or other birds for ten minutes and write down everything they do"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Inferior Animals”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu02gattuoft/page/n91/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 2, pp. 80–88","questions":["What are the rooks doing in the field?","What job does the rook in the tall oak have?","What can a dog do that its master cannot explain?"],"answers":["Meeting in a great crowd, hopping, pecking, and cawing","He is a sentinel who keeps watch and warns the flock of danger","Find his way home by a short cut he has never taken, or bring back exactly the stone his master threw"]},{"id":"parables-from-nature-5-2","title":"Lesson 28: Inferior Animals, part 2","steps":["Read “Inferior Animals” from “What the Rook Says” to the question “By what possible means could birds ever degenerate into men?” (p. 97)","Narrate the Rook''s reasons for thinking men are not better than rooks","Make a two-column chart: things birds can do that people can''t, and things people can do that birds can''t"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Inferior Animals” from p. 88","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu02gattuoft/page/n99/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 2, pp. 88–97","questions":["What did the Rook say proves rooks are better than men?","What surprising idea did the Rook announce about where men came from?","What did the Rook imagine had happened to the young rooks that men shot and carried away?"],"answers":["Rooks fly, have warm feathers, and are content; men walk lop-sidedly, need clothes, and are always restless","That men were once rooks who lost their wings","That they were living with men as their honoured teachers"]},{"id":"parables-from-nature-5-3","title":"Lesson 29: Inferior Animals, part 3","steps":["Read “Inferior Animals” from p. 98 to the end","Narrate the Rook''s ''proofs'' from Mr. Raven-wing, Mr. Yellow-beak, and Mr. Grey-legs, and how the dream ended","Draw one of the Rook''s ''proofs'' (a chimney, a chimney-sweep, or a balloon) as the Rook saw it"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Inferior Animals” from p. 98","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu02gattuoft/page/n109/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 2, pp. 98–108","questions":["How did the Rook explain that men lost their wings?","What did Mr. Yellow-beak think the tall brick chimneys were for?","What was the storyteller doing when he woke from his dream?"],"answers":["By walking on the ground for ages until their unused wings shrank into arms","Trees that men would one day roost in","Lying by the hedge, with the field empty and the rooks gone"],"notes":"Written in 1861, two years after Darwin''s ''Origin of Species'', this is Gatty''s satire of evolution theories: the Rook builds a grand theory from guesses. One passage refers to dark-skinned people in a way that reflects Victorian attitudes; you may want to skip it or talk about it."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-5-4","title":"Lesson 30: The General Thaw","steps":["Read “The General Thaw” aloud together","Narrate the quarrel between the Ice, the Water, and the Snow, and how it ended","Put a piece of ice and a spoonful of snow (or frost from the freezer) in a cup and watch them all turn to water; draw before and after"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The General Thaw”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu02gattuoft/page/n120/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 2, pp. 109–122","questions":["Why did the Ice think he was so important?","How did the skaters get rid of the Snow that covered the Ice?","What did the Ice, Snow, and Water discover when the thaw came?"],"answers":["The skaters and sliders admired him, so he thought it was all in his honour","They had men sweep and shovel it into heaps at the sides","That they were all brothers, made of the same water"],"notes":"The last line says the ''general thaw'' that makes brothers of us all is death; you may want to talk about why it is better to make peace sooner."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-5-5","title":"Lesson 31: The Light of Life","steps":["Read “The Light of Life” aloud together","Narrate everything Hans did for his rose-tree, and what he forgot","Start an experiment: sprout two pots of seeds, one in a dark cupboard and one on a sunny sill, and compare them in a week"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Light of Life”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu02gattuoft/page/n134/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 2, pp. 123–134","questions":["What did the nursery gardener say makes roses beautiful?","Why did Hans''s rose-tree die?","What did the gardener say God''s grace is for a person?"],"answers":["Bones, hair, and soap-suds as manure","He boarded it in so carefully that it never got any light","What sunlight is for a plant: the main thing it needs to thrive"],"notes":"Hans is described with old words for a person with an intellectual disability. The story treats him kindly; you may want to explain that we use different words today."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-5-6","title":"Lesson 32: Gifts","steps":["Read “Gifts” aloud together","Narrate what each vegetable boasted about, and what the Rain-drops told them","Pull or buy a carrot, radish, onion, and beet; cut each open and draw what you see inside"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Gifts”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu02gattuoft/page/n146/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 2, pp. 135–147","questions":["Why were the Cress and the Mustard so unhappy?","What did the Rain-drops say about every plant?","What happened to the Carrot that tried to stop growing down?"],"answers":["They grew only little fibres instead of big roots like the others","Each one is good after its kind and has its own part to play","It grew into a thick, crooked lump and was only good for soup"],"notes":"Based on 1 Corinthians 12, “there are diversities of gifts.” A gentle story for a child who compares himself with a sibling."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-5-7","title":"Lesson 33: Night and Day","steps":["Read “Night and Day” aloud together","Narrate the quarrel between Night and Day and what they learned at the Poles","In a dark room, shine a flashlight on a globe or ball and spin it slowly to show how day and night really happen; find the North and South Poles"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Night and Day”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu02gattuoft/page/n159/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 2, pp. 148–159","questions":["In the old northern tales, what were the names of the horses of Night and Day?","What did Day wish would happen?","What did Night and Day find at the Poles, where each rules for six months?"],"answers":["Frosty-mane and Shiny-mane","That he could go on forever with no night at all","Ice and rock, with almost no life at all"],"notes":"The horses come from Norse myth. Gatty ends by explaining the real cause of day and night, the spinning earth."}]},{"id":"parables-from-nature-u6","title":"Unit 6: Kicking to Birds in the Nest","lessons":[{"id":"parables-from-nature-6-1","title":"Lesson 34: Kicking, part 1","steps":["Read “Kicking” up to Firefly saying he will go on kicking out of spite (p. 170)","Narrate how Firefly was caught and what the old mare Egeria told him","Draw a horse''s bridle and halter, and label the bit, reins, and headstall"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Kicking”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu02gattuoft/page/n171/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 2, pp. 160–170","questions":["How long had Firefly lived free in the fields before he was caught?","How did Egeria say colts are raised in the East, where her mother came from?","What did Firefly decide he would do?"],"answers":["Three years","They live among the tents and are fed and petted by the women from the start, so they are not afraid of people","Kick, kick, kick"]},{"id":"parables-from-nature-6-2","title":"Lesson 35: Kicking, part 2","steps":["Read “Kicking” from p. 170 to where Whitefoot and Silverstar have gone to good homes (p. 178)","Narrate the difference between Taffy''s advice and Egeria''s, and how the three colts took their training","Copy Egeria''s warning: “Those who indulge themselves in kicking at all, will sometimes kick when they would give worlds to forbear”"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Kicking” from p. 170","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu02gattuoft/page/n181/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 2, pp. 170–178","questions":["Who gave Whitefoot and Silverstar good advice?","What hard things did the colts have to learn to bear?","Why did Egeria warn Firefly about kicking ''with a reason''?"],"answers":["Taffy, the old Welsh pony","The iron bit in their mouths, the bridle, and going round in circles under the whip","Because kicking would become a habit he could not stop even when he wanted to"]},{"id":"parables-from-nature-6-3","title":"Lesson 36: Kicking, part 3","steps":["Read “Kicking” from p. 178 to the end","Narrate Firefly''s masters one by one, and what finally cured him","Talk together: when is it hardest to obey? Then draw Firefly standing still beside his young mistress"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Kicking” from p. 178","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu02gattuoft/page/n189/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 2, pp. 178–188","questions":["Name two of the masters Firefly had before the clergyman''s daughter.","What terrible thing happened on the Downs?","What did the young lady ask her father to do with Firefly?"],"answers":["A young squire, a country doctor, and a horse-dealer","Firefly kicked and threw his kind young mistress, and she lay stunned on the ground","Send him to Rarey to be cured"],"notes":"John Rarey was a famous American horse-tamer of the 1850s. The closing list of obediences (including ''wives under husbands'') reflects Victorian views; you may want to talk about it with older children."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-6-4","title":"Lesson 37: Imperfect Instruments, part 1","steps":["Read “Imperfect Instruments” up to Geronimo''s idea of a festival for his father''s birthday (p. 203)","Narrate the quarrel over Geronimo''s mother''s memorial tablet and how the village took it","Find Wales on a map of Britain, and draw the little church with its tower in the valley"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Imperfect Instruments”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu02gattuoft/page/n200/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 2, pp. 189–203","questions":["What did Geronimo want to move, and why was it so hard for his father?","Why would Roger the mason not do the job?","What did Geronimo plan in order to bring the village together again?"],"answers":["The marble tablet in memory of his mother, which his father loved to look at every Sunday","He thought it would break the old man''s heart to take down his own wife''s stone","A festival with music and a supper on his father''s birthday"],"notes":"This is a story about a stubborn young clergyman, written mostly for grown-ups. Younger children may enjoy it more if you tell the first half in your own words and read the organ-tuning part aloud."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-6-5","title":"Lesson 38: Imperfect Instruments, part 2","steps":["Read “Imperfect Instruments” from p. 203 to the end","Narrate what went wrong when Geronimo tuned the organ himself, and what the organ-builder explained","Fill glasses with different amounts of water, tap them, and try to tune two of them to sound sweet together"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Imperfect Instruments” from p. 203","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu02gattuoft/page/n214/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 2, pp. 203–215","questions":["Why did Geronimo try to tune the organ himself?","What happened when he played Haydn''s Mass after tuning it?","Why did the organ sound wrong, according to the organ-builder?"],"answers":["The organ-builder was away and he wanted it perfect for the festival","The chords clashed into horrible discords","He had tuned it too perfectly; an imperfect instrument needs some notes left a little flat or sharp"],"notes":"This is real music science: to make every key sound good, organs and pianos are tuned with some intervals slightly ''off'' (called temperament)."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-6-6","title":"Lesson 39: Cobwebs","steps":["Read “Cobwebs” aloud together","Narrate Twinette''s three trips down her ''family rope'' and what she met each time","Find a spider web outdoors (early morning dew makes them easy to see) and draw it carefully, thread by thread"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Cobwebs”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu02gattuoft/page/n227/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 2, pp. 216–226","questions":["What did Twinette''s mother say she should look for before spinning a web?","What three things surprised Twinette on her trips down?","How many eyes does Twinette have?"],"answers":["Empty space below, for flies to fly in","A dazzling sunbeam, a gust of wind, and the darkness of night","Eight"],"notes":"Gatty''s description of the spider''s silk (several spinnerets twisting fine strands together) is accurate. The ending pokes gentle fun at people who think they have figured everything out."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-6-7","title":"Lesson 40: Birds in the Nest","steps":["Read “Birds in the Nest” aloud together","Narrate each time the hen-mother thought she was ''perfectly happy'', and what came next","Draw the nest with eight eggs, then the eight young birds flying out into the wood"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Birds in the Nest”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu02gattuoft/page/n238/mode/1up"}],"pages":"vol. 2, pp. 227–234","questions":["How many eggs were in the nest?","Why did the hen-mother come to like night better than day?","What did she finally learn about being happy?"],"answers":["Eight","At night all her young ones came back to the nest","That rejoicing in her children''s joy, and loving the whole wood as a larger nest, made her happiest"],"notes":"A tender story for parents as much as children, about letting children grow up. This is the last parable in the 1882 Bell edition; the remaining eight are read from a different scan (see the next unit)."}]},{"id":"parables-from-nature-u7","title":"Unit 7: The Later Parables, Consequences to Unopened Parcels","lessons":[{"id":"parables-from-nature-7-1","title":"Lesson 41: Consequences, and The Cause and the Causer, part 1","steps":["Read the short parable “Consequences”, then “The Cause and the Causer” up to “But the professor was gone too!” (p. 171)","Narrate where the stone''s ripples went, then how the insects began their inquest on the dead moth","Drop a pebble into still water and watch the rings spread; draw them"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Consequences”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnat02unkngoog/page/n173/mode/1up"},{"label":"Read “The Cause and the Causer”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnat02unkngoog/page/n177/mode/1up"}],"pages":"Putnam ed. (Second Series), pp. 159–171","questions":["What did the stone shout as it sank to the bottom of the pond?","What did Dr. Earwig find stuck to the dead moth''s proboscis?","Where had the bumble-bee picked up the same kind of ''foreign body''?"],"answers":["“There an end!”","Several little sticky ''foreign bodies''","Inside an orchis (orchid) flower"],"notes":"“Consequences” is only two pages: a small lesson that our words and deeds spread like ripples. “The Cause and the Causer” is set up as a courtroom inquest among insects; enjoy the funny characters."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-7-2","title":"Lesson 42: The Cause and the Causer, part 2","steps":["Read “The Cause and the Causer” from Dr. Earwig climbing into the orchid to the end","Narrate what Dr. Earwig saw inside the orchid, and the three different opinions given at the inquest","Look closely at a flower (any kind) with a magnifying glass, find the pollen, and draw where it sits"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Cause and the Causer” from p. 172","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnat02unkngoog/page/n186/mode/1up"}],"pages":"Putnam ed. (Second Series), pp. 172–185","questions":["What were the ''foreign bodies'' really?","Why do orchids need insects to carry their flower-dust?","What did Sir Helix the Snail decide was behind this clever plan?"],"answers":["Little packets of flower-dust (pollen) with a sticky ball at the end","The flower cannot dust itself, because its pollen sits too far from the sticky spot that needs it","A great unseen Intelligence who designed it"],"notes":"The orchid facts come from Darwin''s 1862 book on orchid pollination, and they are accurate (even the pollen-packet bending down after it is picked up). Gatty uses them to argue for a Creator; Bumble major and Bumble minor speak for chance and for evolution."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-7-3","title":"Lesson 43: Ghosts","steps":["Read “Ghosts” aloud together","Narrate how the Draught talked the Fire into breaking out, and what became of the Fire","Talk about fire safety at home: find your smoke alarms, the fire guard or screen, and your family''s meeting place outside; draw a map of how you would get out"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Ghosts”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnat02unkngoog/page/n203/mode/1up"}],"pages":"Putnam ed. (Second Series), pp. 189–195","questions":["What was the brass guard in front of the grate for?","Who told the people at supper that the ball-room was on fire?","What finally put the Fire out?"],"answers":["To keep sparks and burning coals in, and the ladies'' dresses away from the fire","The Smoke","Barrels of water from the pond"],"notes":"The ''ghosts'' are Gatty''s playful name for the invisible gases in fire and air. One simile compares the flames to ''Indians after their prey'', a phrase of its time you may want to skip."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-7-4","title":"Lesson 44: These Three","steps":["Read “These Three” aloud together","Narrate the Moss''s journey: the broken flower-pot, the terrace walk, and the tombstone","Find moss growing on a wall, stone, or tree; look at it closely with a magnifying glass and draw it, then copy “And now abideth faith, hope, charity, these three”"],"links":[{"label":"Read “These Three”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnat02unkngoog/page/n213/mode/1up"}],"pages":"Putnam ed. (Second Series), pp. 199–209","questions":["What did the Moss always say as it crept onto something new?","What words did the granddaughter write under her drawing of the moss-covered flower-pot?","What did the Tombstone keep saying, and what did the Moss answer?"],"answers":["“By your leave”","“Life in death. CHARITY.”","“I belong to Death”; the Moss answered, “And I to Life”"],"notes":"The old rector and his wife die and are buried in this story, told very gently. The title and closing text come from 1 Corinthians 13: faith, hope, and charity (love)."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-7-5","title":"Lesson 45: The Universal Language and See-Saw","steps":["Read “The Universal Language” and the short parable “See-Saw” aloud together","Narrate who heard the Nightingale''s song and how it comforted each one; then tell what the Snail said to the grumbling Oak","Make a see-saw with a ruler balanced on a pencil and two coins; find where the middle has to be to balance"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Universal Language”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnat02unkngoog/page/n229/mode/1up"},{"label":"Read “See-Saw”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnat02unkngoog/page/n241/mode/1up"}],"pages":"Putnam ed. (Second Series), pp. 213–228","questions":["Which homesick plants in the kitchen-garden listened to the Nightingale?","What words did the Nightingale''s song always end with?","What did Sir Helix the Snail tell the Oak his job was?"],"answers":["Foreign plants such as the Portugal Onion, the Tomato, the French Beans and Marrows, and the Peach","“The same sun by day, the same moon by night, stars watching above”","Holding both ends of the see-saw up, steady in the middle, which the children could not do for themselves"],"notes":"The ''universal language'' is sympathy, which everyone understands without words. In this scan the last three words of “The Universal Language” are missing; the sentence ends “...and Love, are one.”"},{"id":"parables-from-nature-7-6","title":"Lesson 46: A Vision","steps":["Read “A Vision” aloud together","Narrate the walk to church on the spring morning, and why the storyteller wanted to leave the old church","Draw pink almond (or other) blossoms against a blue sky, the sight the storyteller loved"],"links":[{"label":"Read “A Vision”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnat02unkngoog/page/n247/mode/1up"}],"pages":"Putnam ed. (Second Series), pp. 231–240","questions":["What flowering trees did the storyteller want to see on the way to church?","Why was the storyteller annoyed when sitting in the old church?","What words from the service brought comfort?"],"answers":["Almond trees","It was the wrong church, with ugly old box pews and tablets instead of the beautifully restored one","“When the wicked man turneth away from the wickedness that he hath committed... he shall save his soul alive”"],"notes":"A reflective story for older children about going to church with the right heart; it quotes the Book of Common Prayer. Younger children may simply enjoy the spring walk."},{"id":"parables-from-nature-7-7","title":"Lesson 47: Unopened Parcels, part 1","steps":["Read “Unopened Parcels”, Part I","Narrate the excitement of the parcel from the old home, and what Honor found inside her walnut","Copy the motto at the top of the story: “Judge not the play before the play is done”"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Unopened Parcels”","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnat02unkngoog/page/n259/mode/1up"}],"pages":"Putnam ed. (Second Series), pp. 243–249","questions":["Who sent the parcels from the old home?","Why was Honor disappointed at first?","What was hidden inside the walnut shell?"],"answers":["Grandmamma","Her little parcel seemed to hold only a common walnut","A fine, soft kid glove"]},{"id":"parables-from-nature-7-8","title":"Lesson 48: Unopened Parcels, part 2","steps":["Read “Unopened Parcels”, Part II","Narrate the ''unopened parcels'' Honor''s father talked about: arithmetic, the Catechism, and the walnut itself","Crack open a walnut, find the tiny germ between the halves, and draw it; if you can, plant a nut or seed and watch for it to sprout"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Unopened Parcels” from p. 249","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnat02unkngoog/page/n265/mode/1up"}],"pages":"Putnam ed. (Second Series), pp. 249–258","questions":["How did arithmetic help sailors, according to Honor''s father?","What ''parcel'' did old John open when he wanted to come to Communion?","Why did Papa say a plain walnut is more wonderful than the glove?"],"answers":["They use calculation to find their way across the pathless ocean","The answer from the Catechism he had learned as a boy","Its tiny germ holds a whole walnut tree"]},{"id":"parables-from-nature-7-9","title":"Lesson 49: Unopened Parcels, part 3","steps":["Read “Unopened Parcels” from Part III to “And now comes the kernel of my story” (p. 264)","Narrate how Honor''s father was saved from drowning at school, and what he thought of the boy who saved him","Talk about water safety: why swimming beyond the safe limits is dangerous"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Unopened Parcels” from p. 258","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnat02unkngoog/page/n274/mode/1up"}],"pages":"Putnam ed. (Second Series), pp. 258–264","questions":["What book did Grandmamma send Honor two years later?","How did Honor''s father nearly drown?","What did his own father tell him about winning his hero''s friendship?"],"answers":["Longfellow''s poems","He swam out beyond the safe bounds, a current carried him away, and he got a cramp","That he would have to deserve it"]},{"id":"parables-from-nature-7-10","title":"Lesson 50: Unopened Parcels, part 4","steps":["Read “Unopened Parcels” from p. 264 to the end","Narrate the hero''s old secret and how his friend helped him","Copy Tennyson''s lines “That men may rise on stepping-stones / Of their dead selves to higher things”"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Unopened Parcels” from p. 264","url":"https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnat02unkngoog/page/n280/mode/1up"}],"pages":"Putnam ed. (Second Series), pp. 264–276","questions":["What had the hero done as a little child that troubled him for years?","Who turned out to be the hero?","What did his mother say when he finally told her?"],"answers":["He let everyone believe he had seen his dead father''s ghost","Honor''s Uncle Frederick","That she had suspected it for years, and to kiss her"],"notes":"A long story for older children about owning up to a wrong, and how even a fault repented of can make us better. The last of the parables."}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"“Parables from Nature (London: George Bell & Sons, 1882, 2 vols.; with the further parables from the G. P. Putnam''s Sons edition, New York, 1880)” by Margaret Gatty (Mrs. Alfred Gatty) (1855), in the public domain. Free full text: https://archive.org/details/parablesfromnatu01gattuoft. Lesson plan by Freerange."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (listing_id, version) do update set content = excluded.content;
insert into public.listings (id, seller_name, seller_kind, seller_verified, title, subjects, grade_min, grade_max, philosophies, format, price, description, includes, lessons, lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement, family_style, lesson_steps, cover, color, esa_eligible, created_at, package_id, package_version, license, attribution, outline) values ('l-lib-rays-intellectual-arithmetic', 'Joseph Ray', 'open', false, 'Ray''s New Intellectual Arithmetic', array['math']::text[], 2, 5, array['charlotte_mason', 'classical', 'traditional']::text[], 'online', 0, 'Joseph Ray''s classic 1877 book of mental arithmetic, the next step after Ray''s New Primary Arithmetic. Children reason through short spoken word problems with no paper, moving from the four operations through fractions, measures, ratio and percentage to interest. Each day you read the model solution together, work the book''s oral problems aloud, and finish with three practice problems. The full scanned book is free online.', array['85 lessons covering all 80 of the book''s lessons in 15 units', 'Link to the exact page of the free scanned book for every lesson', 'Oral problems with model solutions to say back in full sentences', '3 practice problems with answers per lesson']::text[], 85, 4, 20, 'high', false, '{}'::text[], '🧠', '#9B6BD6', false, '2026-11-11T12:00:00.000Z', 'pk-lib-rays-intellectual-arithmetic', 1, '{"kind":"public_domain"}'::jsonb, '“Ray''s New Intellectual Arithmetic (Eclectic Educational Series)” by Joseph Ray (1877), in the public domain. Free full text: https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog. Lesson plan by Freerange.', '[{"title":"Unit 1: Addition (Lessons I–V)","lessons":["Lesson I: Adding Small Numbers","Lesson II: Adding Numbers Up to 20","Lesson III: The Addition Table","Lesson IV: Adding to Larger Numbers","Lesson V (part 1): Adding Several Numbers","Lesson V (part 2): Story Problems and Counting By"]},{"title":"Unit 2: Subtraction (Lessons VI–XI)","lessons":["Lesson VI: Taking Away","Lesson VII: The Subtraction Table","Lesson VIII: Subtraction Problems","Lesson IX: Review of Adding and Subtracting","Lesson X: Adding and Subtracting in a Chain","Lesson XI: Harder Subtraction Problems"]},{"title":"Unit 3: Multiplication (Lessons XII–XV)","lessons":["Lesson XII: Equal Groups","Lesson XIII: The Multiplication Table","Lesson XIV: Multiplication Problems","Lesson XV: Mixed Problems with Multiplication"]},{"title":"Unit 4: Division (Lessons XVI–XIX)","lessons":["Lesson XVI: How Many Times?","Lesson XVII: The Division Table","Lesson XVIII: Sharing and Dividing","Lesson XIX: Times As Many"]},{"title":"Unit 5: What Fractions Are (Lessons XX–XXV)","lessons":["Lesson XX: Halves, Thirds, Fourths and More","Lesson XXI: Writing Fractions","Lesson XXII: Finding a Part of a Number","Lesson XXIII: What Part Is It?","Lesson XXIV: Finding the Whole from a Part","Lesson XXV: Sharing That Leaves Fractions"]},{"title":"Unit 6: Changing the Form of Fractions (Lessons XXVI–XXIX)","lessons":["Lesson XXVI: Mixed Numbers to Improper Fractions","Lesson XXVII: Lowest Terms","Lesson XXVIII: Higher Terms","Lesson XXIX: Common Denominators"]},{"title":"Unit 7: Adding, Subtracting and Multiplying Fractions (Lessons XXX–XXXVI)","lessons":["Lesson XXX: Adding Fractions","Lesson XXXI: Subtracting Fractions","Lesson XXXII: What Is Left?","Lesson XXXIII: Multiplying a Fraction","Lesson XXXIV: Fractions in Buying and Working","Lesson XXXV: Multiplying Mixed Numbers","Lesson XXXVI: Trading One Thing for Another"]},{"title":"Unit 8: Fractions of Fractions and Dividing Fractions (Lessons XXXVII–XLIV)","lessons":["Lesson XXXVII: A Fraction of a Fraction","Lesson XXXVIII: A Fraction of a Mixed Number","Lesson XXXIX: Finding the Whole from a Part, Again","Lesson XL: Comparing Parts","Lesson XLI: Dividing by a Fraction","Lesson XLII: Dividing Mixed Numbers","Lesson XLIII: How Often Is One Contained in Another?","Lesson XLIV: Handy Parts of a Dozen and a Dollar"]},{"title":"Unit 9: General Review (Lessons XLV–XLVI)","lessons":["Lesson XLV: Review of the Four Rules","Lesson XLVI (part 1): Review of Fractions","Lesson XLVI (part 2): Review of Fractions","Lesson XLVI (part 3): Review — Overtaking, Cisterns and Shares"]},{"title":"Unit 10: Money, Measures and Time (Lessons XLVII–LIII)","lessons":["Lesson XLVII: United States Money","Lesson XLVIII: Dry Measure","Lesson XLIX: Liquid Measure","Lesson L: Avoirdupois Weight","Lesson LI: Long Measure","Lesson LII: Time and the Calendar","Lesson LIII: Problems with Measures"]},{"title":"Unit 11: General Review: Harder Problems (Lessons LIV–LIX)","lessons":["Lesson LIV: Review Problems in Money","Lesson LV: Review Problems in Fractions","Lesson LVI: Sums and Differences","Lesson LVII: Dividing into Parts by Fractions","Lesson LVIII: A Number and Its Parts","Lesson LIX: Working Together"]},{"title":"Unit 12: Ratio and Proportion (Lessons LX–LXIII)","lessons":["Lesson LX: Ratio","Lesson LXI: Dividing in a Given Ratio","Lesson LXII: Men, Days and Loaves","Lesson LXIII: Partnership"]},{"title":"Unit 13: Percentage (Lessons LXIV–LXIX)","lessons":["Lesson LXIV: Per Cent as a Fraction","Lesson LXV: Finding a Per Cent of a Number","Lesson LXVI: Fractions as Per Cents","Lesson LXVII: What Per Cent?","Lesson LXVIII: Finding the Cost from the Selling Price","Lesson LXIX: Discounts Off the List Price"]},{"title":"Unit 14: Commission, Insurance and Interest (Lessons LXX–LXXIX)","lessons":["Lesson LXX: Commission","Lesson LXXI: Insurance","Lesson LXXII: Interest by Years","Lesson LXXIII: Interest for Months and Days","Lesson LXXIV: Finding the Principal","Lesson LXXV: Principal from the Amount","Lesson LXXVI: Finding the Time","Lesson LXXVII: Finding the Rate","Lesson LXXVIII: Present Worth and Discount","Lesson LXXIX: Interest Puzzles"]},{"title":"Unit 15: Miscellaneous Problems (Lesson LXXX)","lessons":["Lesson LXXX (part 1): Miscellaneous Problems","Lesson LXXX (part 2): Miscellaneous Problems","Lesson LXXX (part 3): Miscellaneous Problems"]}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set seller_name = excluded.seller_name, seller_kind = excluded.seller_kind, seller_verified = excluded.seller_verified, title = excluded.title, subjects = excluded.subjects, grade_min = excluded.grade_min, grade_max = excluded.grade_max, philosophies = excluded.philosophies, format = excluded.format, price = excluded.price, description = excluded.description, includes = excluded.includes, lessons = excluded.lessons, lessons_per_week = excluded.lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson = excluded.minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement = excluded.parent_involvement, family_style = excluded.family_style, lesson_steps = excluded.lesson_steps, cover = excluded.cover, color = excluded.color, esa_eligible = excluded.esa_eligible, package_id = excluded.package_id, package_version = excluded.package_version, license = excluded.license, attribution = excluded.attribution, outline = excluded.outline;
insert into public.package_releases (listing_id, version, content) values ('l-lib-rays-intellectual-arithmetic', 1, '{"units":[{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-u1","title":"Unit 1: Addition (Lessons I–V)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-1-1","title":"Lesson I: Adding Small Numbers","steps":["Act out the first problems with real apples, pennies or marbles","Answer each oral problem and tell why, e.g. \"because 4 and 2 are 6\"","Learn the words sum and amount and the signs + and ="],"links":[{"label":"Lesson I (p. 6)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n12/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 6–7","questions":["Sam has 4 marbles and finds 3 more. How many does he have now?","How many are 5 cakes and 2 cakes and 2 cakes?","What is the answer to an addition problem called?"],"answers":["7","9","The sum, or amount"],"notes":"This is mental arithmetic: problems are worked in the head and answered aloud in full sentences, with a slate only for checking."},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-1-2","title":"Lesson II: Adding Numbers Up to 20","steps":["Say each sum the way the model solution does: \"Two and four are six; six and one are seven\"","Count up by 2s from 4 to 16 and by 3s from 1 to 13","Work the story problems about eggs, tape and apples"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson II (p. 8)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n14/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 8–9","questions":["What are 7 and 5 and 2?","A girl spent 4 cents for pins and 6 cents for thread, and has 3 cents left. How many cents had she at first?","Name two numbers that make 12 when added."],"answers":["14","13","Any pair, such as 5 and 7"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-1-3","title":"Lesson III: The Addition Table","steps":["Read the addition table aloud, a column at a time","Cover the answers and quiz each other on the 7s, 8s and 9s","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson III (p. 10)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n16/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 10–11","questions":["What is 8 + 7?","What is 9 + 12?","What is 6 + 6?"],"answers":["15","21","12"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-1-4","title":"Lesson IV: Adding to Larger Numbers","steps":["Add 2 to 29, 49, 69 and 99 and notice how the ones spill over into the next ten","Run through the book''s drills briskly, answer only","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson IV (p. 11)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n17/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 11–13","questions":["What is 49 + 3?","What is 68 + 7?","What is 9 + 86?"],"answers":["52","75","95"],"notes":"Children who finished Ray''s New Primary Arithmetic may begin here, as the book itself suggests."},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-1-5","title":"Lesson V (part 1): Adding Several Numbers","steps":["Add long strings of numbers aloud, keeping a running total as in the model solution","Work problems 1–25","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson V (p. 13)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n19/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 13–15","questions":["What are 4 and 8 and 3 and 5?","What are 7 and 9 and 5 and 4?","What are 12 and 11 and 7?"],"answers":["20","25","30"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-1-6","title":"Lesson V (part 2): Story Problems and Counting By","steps":["Work the story problems 26–38 aloud","Count by 2s, 3s, 4s and up to 9s, as far as 100 (problems 39–53)","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson V, problems 26–53 (p. 15)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n21/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 15–16","questions":["A farmer buys 12 hens, then 9 more, then 6 more. How many hens did he buy?","Counting by 7s from 7, what number comes after 49?","Ben is 9 and Ann is 6. Their father is 20 years older than both their ages put together. How old is he?"],"answers":["27","56","35"]}]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-u2","title":"Unit 2: Subtraction (Lessons VI–XI)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-2-1","title":"Lesson VI: Taking Away","steps":["Act out taking away with pennies or apples","Answer the oral problems and tell why (\"because 2 from 5 leaves 3\")","Learn minuend, subtrahend, difference and the minus sign"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson VI (p. 17)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n23/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 17–18","questions":["Lucy has 11 cents and spends 4. How many are left?","Seven from 15 leaves how many?","In 14 − 6 = 8, which number is the subtrahend?"],"answers":["7","8","6"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-2-2","title":"Lesson VII: The Subtraction Table","steps":["Read the subtraction table aloud, a column at a time","Check a few lines backward with addition (9 − 4 = 5, so 5 + 4 = 9)","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson VII (p. 19)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n25/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 19–20","questions":["What is 13 − 5?","What is 16 − 7?","What is 20 − 8?"],"answers":["8","9","12"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-2-3","title":"Lesson VIII: Subtraction Problems","steps":["Read the model solution about the slate and say it back","Work problems 1–25, answering each in a full sentence","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson VIII (p. 20)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n26/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 20–22","questions":["A man has 30 miles to walk and has gone 17. How far is left?","A boy bought a book for 25 cents and sold it for 18 cents. How much did he lose?","The greater of two numbers is 15 and their difference is 6. What is the smaller?"],"answers":["13 miles","7 cents","9"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-2-4","title":"Lesson IX: Review of Adding and Subtracting","steps":["Read the two model solutions (the marbles given away, and the marble game)","Work review problems 1–15","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson IX (p. 22)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n28/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 22–23","questions":["Jim had 20 marbles. He gave 4 to Al and 7 to Ben. How many had he left?","Two boys each start a game with 15 marbles. At the end one has 21. How many has the other?","A grocer pays $8, $6 and $5 for goods and sells them all for $25. What does he gain?"],"answers":["9","9","$6"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-2-5","title":"Lesson X: Adding and Subtracting in a Chain","steps":["Work each chain left to right, saying the running answer: \"3 minus 2 is 1, plus 1 is 2\"","Do problems 1–27 aloud; go back to any you missed","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson X (p. 24)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n30/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 24","questions":["9 − 4 + 6 = ?","8 − 3 + 5 − 6 = ?","7 + 2 − 5 + 4 − 3 = ?"],"answers":["11","4","5"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-2-6","title":"Lesson XI: Harder Subtraction Problems","steps":["Read the model solution for problem 4 and copy its three steps","Work problems 1–22","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XI (p. 25)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n31/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 25–26","questions":["From the sum of 16 and 9, take the difference between 15 and 8.","What number must be added to 20 to make a sum 10 less than 50?","A man buys a cow for $30, pays $8 for her keep, and sells her for $45. What does he gain?"],"answers":["18","20","$7"]}]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-u3","title":"Unit 3: Multiplication (Lessons XII–XV)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-3-1","title":"Lesson XII: Equal Groups","steps":["Lay out groups of beans (2 groups of 3, 4 groups of 5) and count the totals","Work the oral price problems 1–22","Learn multiplicand, multiplier, product and the sign ×"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XII (p. 27)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n33/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 27–28","questions":["At 5 cents each, what do 4 pears cost?","If one peach is worth 6 apples, how many apples are 3 peaches worth?","In 4 × 7 = 28, what is 28 called?"],"answers":["20 cents","18","The product"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-3-2","title":"Lesson XIII: The Multiplication Table","steps":["Read the multiplication table aloud a few rows at a time","Drill the \"Exercises on the Table\" (4 times 7, 8 times 6, and so on)","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XIII (p. 28)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n34/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 28–31","questions":["What are 7 times 8?","What are 9 times 6?","What are 12 times 11?"],"answers":["56","54","132"],"notes":"Spread the table over several days if needed; it is the base for everything that follows."},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-3-3","title":"Lesson XIV: Multiplication Problems","steps":["Read the model solution: \"7 oranges will cost 7 times 2 cents\"","Work problems 1–25, including the travelers going opposite ways and the \"one man does it\" problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XIV (p. 31)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n37/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 31–32","questions":["At $6 a barrel, what do 7 barrels of flour cost?","Two boys walk away from each other, one 3 miles an hour and the other 4. How far apart are they after 5 hours?","If 3 men can do a job in 5 days, how long would 1 man take?"],"answers":["$42","35 miles","15 days"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-3-4","title":"Lesson XV: Mixed Problems with Multiplication","steps":["Work problems 1–19, saying each step of the solution","Work problems 20–38 and the chained products like 2 times 3 times 4","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XV (p. 33)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n39/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 33–35","questions":["Buy 3 pencils at 4 cents and 2 erasers at 5 cents. What is the cost?","Two men walk the same way, one 6 miles an hour and the other 4. How far apart are they after 8 hours?","How many are 2 times 3 times 5?"],"answers":["22 cents","16 miles","30"]}]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-u4","title":"Unit 4: Division (Lessons XVI–XIX)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-4-1","title":"Lesson XVI: How Many Times?","steps":["Share 12 beans into piles of 3 and count the piles","Answer the oral problems 1–26 and tell why","Learn divisor, dividend, quotient and the sign ÷"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XVI (p. 36)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n42/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 36–37","questions":["At 4 cents each, how many apples can you buy for 28 cents?","36 trees stand in 4 equal rows. How many are in each row?","In 20 ÷ 5 = 4, which number is the divisor?"],"answers":["7","9","5"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-4-2","title":"Lesson XVII: The Division Table","steps":["Read the division table aloud, a column at a time","Say each line backward as multiplication (56 ÷ 8 = 7 because 7 × 8 = 56)","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XVII (p. 38)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n44/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 38–39","questions":["What is 56 ÷ 8?","What is 72 ÷ 9?","What is 84 ÷ 12?"],"answers":["7","8","7"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-4-3","title":"Lesson XVIII: Sharing and Dividing","steps":["Read the model solutions for problems 7, 11 and 23","Work problems 1–25","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XVIII (p. 40)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n46/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 40–41","questions":["Five children share 40 cents equally. How much does each get?","If 1 man takes 48 days to do a job, how long would 6 men take?","If 4 men earn $48 in 3 days, how much does each man earn in 1 day?"],"answers":["8 cents","8 days","$4"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-4-4","title":"Lesson XIX: Times As Many","steps":["Read the model solutions for problems 4, 8 and 22","Work problems 1–35","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XIX (p. 42)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n48/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 42–44","questions":["Sixty is how many times 12?","How many oranges at 6 cents each must be given for 9 lemons at 4 cents each?","A man goes 9 miles while a boy goes 4. When the man has gone 54 miles, how far has the boy gone?"],"answers":["5","6","24 miles"]}]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-u5","title":"Unit 5: What Fractions Are (Lessons XX–XXV)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-5-1","title":"Lesson XX: Halves, Thirds, Fourths and More","steps":["Cut an apple or paper circles into halves, thirds and fourths","Answer how many halves, thirds and so on up to ninths are in 2 to 10 apples","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XX (p. 45)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n51/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 45–46","questions":["How many thirds in 4 apples?","How many fifths in 6 apples?","How many halves in 9 apples?"],"answers":["12","30","18"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-5-2","title":"Lesson XXI: Writing Fractions","steps":["Learn numerator, denominator, proper fraction, improper fraction and mixed number","Write on the slate the fractions the book gives in words, then read the printed ones aloud","Sort the book''s list into proper and improper fractions"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXI (p. 47)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n53/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 47–48","questions":["In 3/8, which number is the denominator?","Is 7/5 a proper or an improper fraction?","Write two and three-fourths in figures."],"answers":["8","Improper","2 3/4"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-5-3","title":"Lesson XXII: Finding a Part of a Number","steps":["Read the model solution: 1/3 of $6 is $2, so 2/3 is $4","Work problems 1–23","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXII (p. 48)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n54/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 48–50","questions":["What are 2/3 of 12?","If a yard of ribbon costs 10 cents, what do 3/5 of a yard cost?","If 4 pounds of rice cost 20 cents, what do 3 pounds cost?"],"answers":["8","6 cents","15 cents"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-5-4","title":"Lesson XXIII: What Part Is It?","steps":["Read the model solution: if $1 buys 1/3 of a yard, $2 buys 2/3","Work problems 1–24","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXIII (p. 50)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n56/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 50–51","questions":["3 is what part of 8?","A melon costs 7 cents. What part of it costs 4 cents?","2/3 of 9 is what part of 10?"],"answers":["3/8","4/7","6/10, or 3/5"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-5-5","title":"Lesson XXIV: Finding the Whole from a Part","steps":["Read the model solution about the lemon: 1/3 is 3 cents, so the whole is 9 cents","Work problems 1–25","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXIV (p. 51)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n57/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 51–53","questions":["If 1/4 of a pie costs 5 cents, what does the whole pie cost?","If 2/3 of a yard costs 8 cents, what does a yard cost?","12 is 3/4 of what number?"],"answers":["20 cents","12 cents","16"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-5-6","title":"Lesson XXV: Sharing That Leaves Fractions","steps":["Share 3 apples between 2 people with a knife and name each share","Work problems 1–29","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXV (p. 53)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n59/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 53–54","questions":["Share 7 apples equally between 2 boys. How much does each get?","What is 1/4 of 9?","How many times 3 is 14?"],"answers":["3 1/2 apples","2 1/4","4 2/3"]}]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-u6","title":"Unit 6: Changing the Form of Fractions (Lessons XXVI–XXIX)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-6-1","title":"Lesson XXVI: Mixed Numbers to Improper Fractions","steps":["Show 2 1/2 with paper circles and count the halves","Work problems 1–12","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXVI (p. 54)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n60/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 54–55","questions":["How many halves in 3 1/2?","How many fourths in 2 3/4?","How many sixths in 5 1/6?"],"answers":["7","11","31"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-6-2","title":"Lesson XXVII: Lowest Terms","steps":["Read the note: a fraction is in lowest terms when no number greater than 1 divides both terms","Reduce the book''s fractions, dividing both terms by the same number","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXVII (p. 55)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n61/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 55","questions":["Reduce 6/8 to lowest terms.","Reduce 10/15 to lowest terms.","Reduce 8/20 to lowest terms."],"answers":["3/4","2/3","2/5"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-6-3","title":"Lesson XXVIII: Higher Terms","steps":["Fold a paper strip in halves, then fourths, and see that 1/2 = 2/4","Work problems 1–23","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXVIII (p. 56)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n62/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 56","questions":["Change 1/2 to eighths.","Change 2/3 to twelfths.","Change 3/5 to twentieths."],"answers":["4/8","8/12","12/20"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-6-4","title":"Lesson XXIX: Common Denominators","steps":["Read the note on common and least common denominators","Work problems 1–25","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXIX (p. 57)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n63/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 57–58","questions":["Change 1/2 and 1/3 to sixths.","What is the least common denominator of 3/4 and 5/6?","Change 1/4 and 2/3 to twelfths."],"answers":["3/6 and 2/6","12","3/12 and 8/12"]}]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-u7","title":"Unit 7: Adding, Subtracting and Multiplying Fractions (Lessons XXX–XXXVI)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-7-1","title":"Lesson XXX: Adding Fractions","steps":["Read the model solution: change to a common denominator, then add","Work problems 1–17, including mixed numbers","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXX (p. 58)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n64/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 58–59","questions":["Add 1/2 and 1/3.","Add 2/3 and 3/4.","Add 2 1/2 and 3 1/4."],"answers":["5/6","1 5/12","5 3/4"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-7-2","title":"Lesson XXXI: Subtracting Fractions","steps":["Read the model solution for taking 2 3/4 from 5 1/4 (borrowing 1)","Work problems 1–15","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXXI (p. 59)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n65/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 59–60","questions":["Take 1/3 from 3/4.","Tom ate 1/2 of a pie and Sue ate 1/3. How much more did Tom eat?","Take 2 3/4 from 5 1/2."],"answers":["5/12","1/6 of a pie","2 3/4"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-7-3","title":"Lesson XXXII: What Is Left?","steps":["Read the model solution about the quart of pecans","Work problems 1–16","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXXII (p. 60)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n66/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 60–61","questions":["After taking 1/3 and 1/4 of an orange, what part is left?","A farmer plants 1/2 of a field in corn, 1/3 in oats and the rest in hay. What part is hay?","A man has 60 miles to go. He travels 1/3 of it the first day, 1/4 the second, and the rest the third. How far does he go the third day?"],"answers":["5/12","1/6","25 miles"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-7-4","title":"Lesson XXXIII: Multiplying a Fraction","steps":["Give each of 3 people half an apple and count the apples used","Work problems 1–18","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXXIII (p. 62)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n68/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 62","questions":["What are 4 times 2/3?","Give 3/4 of an apple to each of 5 children. How many apples does it take?","What are 7 times 3/5?"],"answers":["2 2/3","3 3/4","4 1/5"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-7-5","title":"Lesson XXXIV: Fractions in Buying and Working","steps":["Read the model solutions for problems 1, 9 and 18","Work problems 1–32","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXXIV (p. 63)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n69/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 63–64","questions":["If 3 pounds of tea cost $2, what do 2 pounds cost?","What are 3/4 of 7?","If 3 men build a fence in 8 days, how long would 4 men take?"],"answers":["$1 1/3","5 1/4","6 days"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-7-6","title":"Lesson XXXV: Multiplying Mixed Numbers","steps":["Read the two model solutions (change to an improper fraction, or multiply the whole and the fraction separately)","Work problems 1–48","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXXV (p. 65)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n71/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 65–66","questions":["How many are 3 times 2 1/2?","How many are 4 times 3 1/3?","A family uses 2 3/4 barrels of flour a month. How much do they use in 4 months?"],"answers":["7 1/2","13 1/3","11 barrels"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-7-7","title":"Lesson XXXVI: Trading One Thing for Another","steps":["Read the model solution about raisins paid for with flour","Work problems 1–23","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXXVI (p. 67)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n73/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 67–68","questions":["Buy 4 boxes of raisins at $2 1/2 a box and pay with flour at $5 a barrel. How many barrels?","Three times 4 2/3 are how many times 7?","How many dozen eggs at 10 cents a dozen will pay for 5 pounds of sugar at 8 cents a pound?"],"answers":["2","2","4 dozen"]}]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-u8","title":"Unit 8: Fractions of Fractions and Dividing Fractions (Lessons XXXVII–XLIV)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-8-1","title":"Lesson XXXVII: A Fraction of a Fraction","steps":["Cut half an apple into 2 pieces and name one piece","Work problems 1–28","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXXVII (p. 69)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n75/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 69–70","questions":["What is 1/2 of 1/3?","What are 2/3 of 3/4?","A man owns 3/4 of a boat and sells 1/3 of his share. What part of the boat does he sell?"],"answers":["1/6","6/12, or 1/2","1/4"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-8-2","title":"Lesson XXXVIII: A Fraction of a Mixed Number","steps":["Read the model solution about cloth worth 2 1/2 bushels of wheat","Work problems 1–15","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXXVIII (p. 71)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n77/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 71","questions":["What is 1/2 of 3 1/2?","If 2 yards cost $3 1/2, what do 3 yards cost?","What are 2/3 of 4 1/2?"],"answers":["1 3/4","$5 1/4","3"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-8-3","title":"Lesson XXXIX: Finding the Whole from a Part, Again","steps":["Read the model solution about the horse sold at a gain","Work problems 1–28","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXXIX (p. 72)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n78/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 72–73","questions":["A boy gave away 6 marbles, which were 2/5 of all he had. How many had he?","A man sold a horse for $60 and gained 1/5 of its cost. What did it cost?","3/4 of a pole is in the ground and 5 feet are above it. How long is the pole?"],"answers":["15","$50","20 feet"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-8-4","title":"Lesson XL: Comparing Parts","steps":["Read the model solutions for problems 1, 12 and 14","Work problems 1–32","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XL (p. 74)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n80/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 74–75","questions":["12 is 3/4 of how many times 4?","2/3 of 18 are how many times 4?","A boy keeps 1/4 of 24 apples and shares the rest equally among 3 friends. How many does each friend get?"],"answers":["4","3","6"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-8-5","title":"Lesson XLI: Dividing by a Fraction","steps":["Read the model solutions for problems 1, 5 and 19","Work problems 1–28","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XLI (p. 75)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n81/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 75–76","questions":["At $1/2 a yard, how much cloth can you buy for $3?","Divide 3/4 by 3/8.","Divide 2/3 by 3/4."],"answers":["6 yards","2","8/9"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-8-6","title":"Lesson XLII: Dividing Mixed Numbers","steps":["Read the model solution about 10 1/2 acres shared among 6 children","Work problems 1–17","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XLII (p. 77)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n83/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 77","questions":["Divide 7 1/2 acres equally among 5 children. How much does each get?","If 1 1/2 yards of ribbon cost 9 cents, what does 1 yard cost?","How many are 10 divided by 2 1/2?"],"answers":["1 1/2 acres","6 cents","4"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-8-7","title":"Lesson XLIII: How Often Is One Contained in Another?","steps":["Read the model solution: change both to improper fractions, then divide","Work problems 1–22","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XLIII (p. 78)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n84/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 78–79","questions":["At $3/4 a yard, how many yards can you buy for $6?","How often is 1 1/2 contained in 4 1/2?","6 2/3 is 2/3 of what number?"],"answers":["8 yards","3","10"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-8-8","title":"Lesson XLIV: Handy Parts of a Dozen and a Dollar","steps":["Learn the two tables: parts of 12, and parts of 100 (12 1/2 cents = 1/8 of a dollar, 33 1/3 = 1/3, and so on)","Work problems 1–31 with dozens and with prices like 25 or 37 1/2 cents","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XLIV (p. 79)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n85/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 79–81","questions":["What do 2/3 of a dozen shirts cost at $18 a dozen?","What do 24 pounds of soap cost at 12 1/2 cents a pound?","Multiply 36 by 33 1/3."],"answers":["$12","$3","1200"]}]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-u9","title":"Unit 9: General Review (Lessons XLV–XLVI)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-9-1","title":"Lesson XLV: Review of the Four Rules","steps":["Work problems 1–12 (adding and subtracting money)","Work problems 13–28 (\"if 2 cost so much, what will 7 cost\")","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XLV (p. 82)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n88/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 82–84","questions":["Tom has 15 cents, is given 6 more and then 9 more, and spends 12. How many has he left?","If 3 tons of hay cost $27, what do 5 tons cost?","If 4 horses eat 12 bushels of oats in a week, how many bushels will 7 horses eat?"],"answers":["18 cents","$45","21"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-9-2","title":"Lesson XLVI (part 1): Review of Fractions","steps":["Work review problems 1–18","Work review problems 19–36","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XLVI (p. 84)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n90/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 84–85","questions":["What are 5/8 of 40?","From 7 1/2 take 3 2/3.","If 3 kegs cost $1 1/2, what do 5 kegs cost?"],"answers":["25","3 5/6","$2 1/2"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-9-3","title":"Lesson XLVI (part 2): Review of Fractions","steps":["Work review problems 37–52","Work review problems 53–68","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XLVI, problems 37–68 (p. 85)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n91/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 85–87","questions":["A girl had 30 cents and spent 2/5 of them on 3 pencils. What did each pencil cost?","If 4 men can do a job in 9 days, in how many days can 6 men do it?","6 is what part of 3/4 of 32?"],"answers":["4 cents","6 days","1/4"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-9-4","title":"Lesson XLVI (part 3): Review — Overtaking, Cisterns and Shares","steps":["Work review problems 69–79","Work review problems 80–93 (overtaking, cisterns, working together)","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XLVI, problems 69–93 (p. 87)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n93/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 87–89","questions":["A is 20 miles ahead of B. B goes 9 miles a day and A goes 5. In how many days will B catch A?","A tank holding 30 gallons fills at 8 gallons an hour and drains at 3 gallons an hour. How long does it take to fill?","A can do a job in 3 days and B in 6 days. How long will they take together?"],"answers":["5 days","6 hours","2 days"]}]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-u10","title":"Unit 10: Money, Measures and Time (Lessons XLVII–LIII)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-10-1","title":"Lesson XLVII: United States Money","steps":["Learn the table: mills, cents, dimes, dollars, eagles","Handle real coins: 10 pennies for a dime, 10 dimes for a dollar","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XLVII (p. 90)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n96/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 90–91","questions":["How many mills in 5 cents?","How many cents in 4 dollars?","70 cents are how many dimes?"],"answers":["50","400","7"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-10-2","title":"Lesson XLVIII: Dry Measure","steps":["Learn the table: 2 pints = 1 quart, 8 quarts = 1 peck, 4 pecks = 1 bushel","Measure dry beans or rice with a pint and a quart cup","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XLVIII (p. 91)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n97/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 91–92","questions":["How many quarts in 3 pecks?","Change 2 pk. 3 qt. to quarts.","Change 19 pints to quarts."],"answers":["24","19 quarts","9 qt. 1 pt."]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-10-3","title":"Lesson XLIX: Liquid Measure","steps":["Learn the table: 4 gills = 1 pint, 2 pints = 1 quart, 4 quarts = 1 gallon","Pour water between a pint jar, a quart jar and a gallon jug","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XLIX (p. 93)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n99/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 93","questions":["How many pints in 3 gallons?","How many gills in 2 quarts?","Change 2 gal. 3 qt. to quarts."],"answers":["24","16","11 quarts"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-10-4","title":"Lesson L: Avoirdupois Weight","steps":["Learn the table: 16 drams = 1 ounce, 16 ounces = 1 pound, 100 pounds = 1 hundred-weight, 20 hundred-weights = 1 ton","Weigh a few things on a kitchen scale in pounds and ounces","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson L (p. 94)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n100/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 94–95","questions":["How many ounces in 3 pounds?","How many pounds in 3/4 of a hundred-weight?","8 ounces are what part of a pound?"],"answers":["48","75","1/2"],"notes":"The scan misprints the pound as \"10 ounces\" in places; it is 16 ounces."},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-10-5","title":"Lesson LI: Long Measure","steps":["Learn the table: inches, feet, yards, rods, furlongs, miles","Measure a room in feet and yards with a tape","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LI (p. 95)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n101/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 95–96","questions":["How many feet in 4 yards?","How many rods in a mile?","How many inches in 2/3 of a yard?"],"answers":["12","320","24"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-10-6","title":"Lesson LII: Time and the Calendar","steps":["Learn the time table and the rhyme \"Thirty days have September\"","Work the problems on parts of a minute, hour, day and week","Count days between dates on a real calendar (problems 15–21)"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LII (p. 96)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n102/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 96–98","questions":["How many minutes in 3/4 of an hour?","6 hours are what part of a day?","How many days in June, July and August together?"],"answers":["45","1/4","92"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-10-7","title":"Lesson LIII: Problems with Measures","steps":["Work problems 1–11 (pricing goods by measure)","Work problems 12–21 (wheels, trains and fences)","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LIII (p. 98)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n104/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 98–99","questions":["At 40 cents a bushel, what do 2 bu. 2 pk. of corn cost?","When milk is 4 cents a pint, what do 2 gallons cost?","A wheel goes 2 yd. 1 ft. in one turn. How far does it go in 6 turns?"],"answers":["$1","64 cents","14 yards"]}]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-u11","title":"Unit 11: General Review: Harder Problems (Lessons LIV–LIX)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-11-1","title":"Lesson LIV: Review Problems in Money","steps":["Work problems 1–9","Work problems 10–17","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LIV (p. 100)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n106/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 100–101","questions":["A man buys a horse for $95 and sells him for $130. What does he gain?","What do 4 yards cost at $1 and 25 cents a yard?","If a boat sails 45 miles in 9 hours, how far will it sail in 4 hours?"],"answers":["$35","$5","20 miles"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-11-2","title":"Lesson LV: Review Problems in Fractions","steps":["Read the model solution for problem 2 and say each step","Work problems 1–34","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LV (p. 101)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n107/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 101–103","questions":["If 1/3 of a yard costs $2, what will 3/4 of a yard cost?","If 2/3 of a ton of hay cost $12, what will 1/2 a ton cost?","John has 12 marbles, and 1/2 of John''s marbles equal 2/3 of James''s. How many has James?"],"answers":["$4 1/2","$9","9"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-11-3","title":"Lesson LVI: Sums and Differences","steps":["Read the model solution: twice the greater number is the sum plus the difference","Work problems 1–31","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LVI (p. 103)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n109/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 103–105","questions":["The sum of two numbers is 20 and their difference is 6. What are the numbers?","Divide 24 into two parts so that the second is 3 times the first.","If 4 is added to 3 times a number, the sum is 25. What is the number?"],"answers":["13 and 7","6 and 18","7"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-11-4","title":"Lesson LVII: Dividing into Parts by Fractions","steps":["Read the model solutions for problems 1, 6 and 21","Work problems 1–26, including the clock puzzles","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LVII (p. 106)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n112/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 106–108","questions":["Divide 20 into two parts so that the smaller is 2/3 of the larger.","The time past noon equals the time still to go until midnight. What time is it?","Divide 21 into two parts so that the smaller is 3/4 of the larger."],"answers":["12 and 8","6 o''clock","12 and 9"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-11-5","title":"Lesson LVIII: A Number and Its Parts","steps":["Read the model solution: a number plus its half is 3/2 of the number","Work problems 1–15","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LVIII (p. 108)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n114/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 108–109","questions":["A number plus its half is 18. What is the number?","Ann spent 2/5 of her money and had 30 cents left. How much had she at first?","A number doubled and increased by its half makes 25. What is the number?"],"answers":["12","50 cents","10"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-11-6","title":"Lesson LIX: Working Together","steps":["Read the model solutions for problems 10 and 14","Work problems 1–19","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LIX (p. 109)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n115/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 109–110","questions":["A can do a job in 4 days. What part of it can he do in 1 day?","A can paint a fence in 4 days and B in 12 days. How long will they take together?","A and B together mow a field in 3 days; A alone takes 4 days. How long would B take alone?"],"answers":["1/4","3 days","12 days"]}]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-u12","title":"Unit 12: Ratio and Proportion (Lessons LX–LXIII)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-12-1","title":"Lesson LX: Ratio","steps":["Learn the definition: ratio is the relation of one quantity to another of the same kind","Work problems 1–24, including dividing in a ratio","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LX (p. 111)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n117/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 111–112","questions":["What is the ratio of 35 to 7?","Divide 30 cents between two boys in the ratio of 3 to 2.","A class of 28 has 3 boys for every 4 girls. How many boys are there?"],"answers":["5","18 cents and 12 cents","12"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-12-2","title":"Lesson LXI: Dividing in a Given Ratio","steps":["Read the model solution for dividing 60 in the ratio 3, 4 and 5","Work problems 1–18","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXI (p. 113)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n119/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 113–114","questions":["Divide 36 into three parts in the ratio 2, 3 and 4.","Divide 20 into two parts so that one is 1 1/2 times the other.","Divide 30 plums so that B gets twice and C three times as many as A."],"answers":["8, 12 and 16","8 and 12","A 5, B 10, C 15"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-12-3","title":"Lesson LXII: Men, Days and Loaves","steps":["Read the model solutions for problems 1, 5, 8 and 17","Work problems 1–23","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXII (p. 114)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n120/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 114–116","questions":["If 6 men do a job in 10 days, how many men can do it in 4 days?","A merchant who fails pays 40 cents on the dollar. What does a creditor he owes $50 receive?","If the wages of 2 men for 3 days are $18, what are the wages of 5 men for 4 days?"],"answers":["15 men","$20","$60"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-12-4","title":"Lesson LXIII: Partnership","steps":["Read the model solutions about the pasture (oxen and sheep, horses for weeks)","Work problems 1–15","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXIII (p. 116)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n122/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 116–118","questions":["A and B rent a pasture for $30. A puts in 2 cows and B 4 cows. What should each pay?","C puts in $40 for 3 months and D $30 for 6 months. They gain $50. What is each one''s share?","A puts 3 horses in a pasture for 2 weeks and B puts in 2 horses for 6 weeks. The rent is $36. What should each pay?"],"answers":["A $10, B $20","C $20, D $30","A $12, B $24"]}]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-u13","title":"Unit 13: Percentage (Lessons LXIV–LXIX)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-13-1","title":"Lesson LXIV: Per Cent as a Fraction","steps":["Learn that per cent means hundredths: 1 per cent is 1/100","Work problems 1–20, giving each per cent as a fraction in lowest terms","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXIV (p. 119)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n125/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 119–120","questions":["What part is 20 per cent?","What part is 12 1/2 per cent?","What part is 75 per cent?"],"answers":["1/5","1/8","3/4"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-13-2","title":"Lesson LXV: Finding a Per Cent of a Number","steps":["Read the model solutions for problems 1, 8 and 13","Work problems 1–16","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXV (p. 120)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n126/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 120–121","questions":["What is 10 per cent of 70?","What is 25 per cent of $48?","Cloth costs 40 cents a yard. At what price must it be sold to gain 25 per cent?"],"answers":["7","$12","50 cents"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-13-3","title":"Lesson LXVI: Fractions as Per Cents","steps":["Read the model solution: 1/2 is 1/2 of 100, or 50 per cent","Work problems 1–22","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXVI (p. 121)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n127/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 121","questions":["How many per cent is 1/4?","How many per cent is 3/5?","How many per cent is 1/3?"],"answers":["25 per cent","60 per cent","33 1/3 per cent"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-13-4","title":"Lesson LXVII: What Per Cent?","steps":["Read the model solutions for problems 1 and 10","Work problems 1–19","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXVII (p. 122)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n128/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 122–123","questions":["3 is what per cent of 12?","Of 40 pupils, 30 are present. What per cent are present?","A hat bought for $5 is sold for $6. What per cent is gained?"],"answers":["25 per cent","75 per cent","20 per cent"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-13-5","title":"Lesson LXVIII: Finding the Cost from the Selling Price","steps":["Read the model solution: a 20 per cent gain makes the price 6/5 of the cost","Work problems 1–20","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXVIII (p. 123)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n129/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 123–124","questions":["A watch sold for $18 brought a gain of 20 per cent. What did it cost?","A horse sold for $27 brought a loss of 10 per cent. What did it cost?","Goods sold for $35 brought a gain of 25 per cent. What did they cost?"],"answers":["$15","$30","$28"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-13-6","title":"Lesson LXIX: Discounts Off the List Price","steps":["Learn what \"10 per cent off\" and \"20 and 5 off\" mean","Work problems 1–10","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXIX (p. 125)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n131/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 125","questions":["A $5 book is sold at 20 per cent off. What is paid?","A $200 bill of goods is sold at 1/4 off. What is paid?","A $100 bill is sold at \"20 and 5 off\". What is paid?"],"answers":["$4","$150","$76"]}]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-u14","title":"Unit 14: Commission, Insurance and Interest (Lessons LXX–LXXIX)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-14-1","title":"Lesson LXX: Commission","steps":["Learn what commission is: pay to an agent for buying or selling","Work problems 1–8","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXX (p. 126)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n132/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 126","questions":["An agent sells a house for $2000 at 3 per cent commission. What is his commission?","An agent sells $400 of goods at 5 per cent. How much does the owner receive?","A collector''s 10 per cent commission came to $30. How much did he collect?"],"answers":["$60","$380","$300"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-14-2","title":"Lesson LXXI: Insurance","steps":["Learn the words policy and premium","Work problems 1–6","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXI (p. 126)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n132/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 126–127","questions":["What is the premium on a $1000 policy at 2 per cent?","A $4000 house is insured for 3/4 of its value at 1 per cent. What is the premium?","What is the premium on $600 at 1/2 per cent?"],"answers":["$20","$30","$3"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-14-3","title":"Lesson LXXII: Interest by Years","steps":["Learn interest, principal and amount","Work problems 1–10: find the per cent for all the years, then take that part of the principal","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXII (p. 127)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n133/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 127–128","questions":["Find the interest of $10 for 2 years at 5 per cent.","Find the interest of $50 for 3 years at 6 per cent.","Find the interest of $40 for 4 years at 5 per cent."],"answers":["$1","$9","$8"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-14-4","title":"Lesson LXXIII: Interest for Months and Days","steps":["Read the model solutions; count a year as 12 months of 30 days","Work problems 1–21, including the amount (principal plus interest)","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXIII (p. 128)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n134/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 128–129","questions":["Find the interest of $60 for 6 months at 6 per cent.","Find the interest of $100 for 3 months at 8 per cent.","What is the amount of $50 for 2 years at 6 per cent?"],"answers":["$1.80","$2","$56"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-14-5","title":"Lesson LXXIV: Finding the Principal","steps":["Read the model solution: if 12/100 of the principal is $3, the principal is $25","Work problems 1–8","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXIV (p. 129)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n135/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 129","questions":["The interest for 2 years at 5 per cent is $4. What is the principal?","What principal at 6 per cent gives $12 interest a year?","What principal at 4 per cent for 5 years gives $10 interest?"],"answers":["$40","$200","$50"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-14-6","title":"Lesson LXXV: Principal from the Amount","steps":["Read the model solution: at 10 per cent the amount is 11/10 of the principal","Work problems 1–7","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXV (p. 130)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n136/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 130","questions":["What principal at 5 per cent for 2 years amounts to $66?","What principal at 10 per cent for 3 years amounts to $130?","What principal at 4 per cent for 5 years amounts to $60?"],"answers":["$60","$100","$50"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-14-7","title":"Lesson LXXVI: Finding the Time","steps":["Read the model solutions, including how long money takes to double","Work problems 1–10","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXVI (p. 130)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n136/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 130–131","questions":["In what time, at 5 per cent, will $40 give $6 interest?","In what time does money double at 10 per cent?","In what time, at 4 per cent, will $50 give $3 interest?"],"answers":["3 years","10 years","1 1/2 years"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-14-8","title":"Lesson LXXVII: Finding the Rate","steps":["Read the model solution: find one year''s interest, then what part of the principal it is","Work problems 1–10","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXVII (p. 131)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n137/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 131","questions":["At what per cent will $100 give $12 interest in 2 years?","At what per cent will $50 give $8 interest in 4 years?","At what per cent will money double itself in 20 years?"],"answers":["6 per cent","4 per cent","5 per cent"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-14-9","title":"Lesson LXXVIII: Present Worth and Discount","steps":["Learn present worth and discount: interest paid in advance","Work problems 1–12","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXVIII (p. 132)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n138/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 132","questions":["What is the present worth, at 5 per cent, of $60 due in 4 years?","What is the discount on that same note?","What is the present worth, at 10 per cent, of $90 due in 5 years?"],"answers":["$50","$10","$60"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-14-10","title":"Lesson LXXIX: Interest Puzzles","steps":["Work problems 1–8 (what part of the principal the interest is)","Work problems 9–14","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXIX (p. 133)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n139/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 133","questions":["At 5 per cent for 4 years, the interest is what part of the principal?","When the interest for 2 years is 1/10 of the principal, what is the rate?","In what time at 5 per cent will a principal give the same interest as in 2 years at 10 per cent?"],"answers":["1/5","5 per cent","4 years"]}]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-u15","title":"Unit 15: Miscellaneous Problems (Lesson LXXX)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-15-1","title":"Lesson LXXX (part 1): Miscellaneous Problems","steps":["Work problems 1–10","Work problems 11–20, using the model solutions for the beggars and the hare and hound","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXX (p. 134)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n140/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 134–136","questions":["If 3 pears are worth 6 apples, and 2 apples are worth 8 plums, how many plums should be given for 4 pears?","Five times a number is 12 more than 3 times the number. What is the number?","If a father gives each child 2 peaches he has 6 left; if he gives each 4 he has none left. How many children has he?"],"answers":["32","6","3"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-15-2","title":"Lesson LXXX (part 2): Miscellaneous Problems","steps":["Work problems 21–30","Work problems 31–40","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXX, problems 21–40 (p. 136)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n142/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 136–138","questions":["A chain cost 1/2 as much as a watch, and both cost $30. What did the watch cost?","Mary gives each playmate 4 cherries and has 10 left; to give each 6 she would need all of them. How many playmates?","Tom is 4 times as old as James, and the difference of their ages is 12 years. How old is each?"],"answers":["$20","5","Tom 16, James 4"]},{"id":"rays-intellectual-arithmetic-15-3","title":"Lesson LXXX (part 3): Miscellaneous Problems","steps":["Work problems 41–50, using the model solution about the idle laborer","Work problems 51–61","Solve the 3 practice problems below aloud, then check"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXX, problems 41–61 (p. 138)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog/page/n144/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 138–140","questions":["A worker earns $2 for each day he works and pays $1 for each idle day. After 10 days he has $14. How many days was he idle?","A is 3 times as old as B, and B is twice as old as C. A is 25 years older than C. How old is each?","Selling sugar at 8 cents a pound loses 20 cents; selling at 10 cents a pound gains 20 cents. How many pounds are there?"],"answers":["2","A 30, B 10, C 5","20 pounds"],"notes":"The book''s last pages are puzzle problems; it is fine to spread this lesson over extra days."}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"“Ray''s New Intellectual Arithmetic (Eclectic Educational Series)” by Joseph Ray (1877), in the public domain. Free full text: https://archive.org/details/raysnewintellec00raygoog. Lesson plan by Freerange."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (listing_id, version) do update set content = excluded.content;
insert into public.listings (id, seller_name, seller_kind, seller_verified, title, subjects, grade_min, grade_max, philosophies, format, price, description, includes, lessons, lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement, family_style, lesson_steps, cover, color, esa_eligible, created_at, package_id, package_version, license, attribution, outline) values ('l-lib-rays-primary-arithmetic', 'Joseph Ray', 'open', false, 'Ray''s New Primary Arithmetic', array['math']::text[], 1, 2, array['charlotte_mason', 'classical', 'traditional']::text[], 'online', 0, 'A classic 1877 primer that builds number sense one small step at a time: counting and writing numbers, then addition, subtraction, multiplication and division tables, then money and measures. Each day starts with real objects like beans, buttons or coins, moves to the short oral exercise in the book, and ends with three practice problems. The full scanned book is free online.', array['89 lessons in 13 units', 'Link to the exact page of the free scanned book for every lesson', 'Hands-on practice with real objects every day', '3 practice problems with answers per lesson']::text[], 89, 4, 20, 'high', false, '{}'::text[], '🧮', '#E3A33B', false, '2026-11-11T12:00:00.000Z', 'pk-lib-rays-primary-arithmetic', 1, '{"kind":"public_domain"}'::jsonb, '“Ray''s New Primary Arithmetic for Young Learners (Eclectic Educational Series)” by Joseph Ray (1877), in the public domain. Free full text: https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog. Lesson plan by Freerange.', '[{"title":"Unit 1: Numbers and Figures (Lessons I–VI)","lessons":["Lesson I: Counting to 10","Lesson II: Numbers 11 to 40","Lesson III: Numbers 41 to 70","Lesson IV: Numbers 71 to 100","Lesson V: Reading Numbers to 100","Lesson VI: Writing Numbers to 100"]},{"title":"Unit 2: Oral Exercises to 10 (Lessons VII–X)","lessons":["Lesson VII: Oral Exercises: Numbers to 5","Lesson VIII: Oral Exercises: Numbers to 8","Lesson IX: Oral Exercises: Numbers to 10","Lesson X: Counting Birds in a Picture"]},{"title":"Unit 3: Addition, Adding 1 to 5 (Lessons XI–XV)","lessons":["Lesson XI: Addition: Adding 1","Lesson XII: Addition: Adding 2","Lesson XIII: Addition: Adding 3","Lesson XIV: Addition: Adding 4","Lesson XV: Addition: Adding 5"]},{"title":"Unit 4: Addition, Adding 6 to 10 and Review (Lessons XVI–XXIII)","lessons":["Lesson XVI: Addition: Adding 6","Lesson XVII: Addition: Adding 7","Lesson XVIII: Addition: Adding 8","Lesson XIX: Addition: Adding 9","Lesson XX: Addition: Adding 10","Lesson XXI: Review of Addition","Lesson XXII: Review of Addition (continued)","Lesson XXIII: Addition Problems"]},{"title":"Unit 5: Subtraction, Taking 1 to 5 (Lessons XXIV–XXIX)","lessons":["Lesson XXIV: Subtraction: Owls and Bats","Lesson XXV: Subtraction: Taking 1","Lesson XXVI: Subtraction: Taking 2","Lesson XXVII: Subtraction: Taking 3","Lesson XXVIII: Subtraction: Taking 4","Lesson XXIX: Subtraction: Taking 5"]},{"title":"Unit 6: Subtraction, Taking 6 to 10 and Review (Lessons XXX–XXXVII)","lessons":["Lesson XXX: Subtraction: Taking 6","Lesson XXXI: Subtraction: Taking 7","Lesson XXXII: Subtraction: Taking 8","Lesson XXXIII: Subtraction: Taking 9","Lesson XXXIV: Subtraction: Taking 10","Lesson XXXV: Review of Subtraction","Lesson XXXVI: Addition and Subtraction Together","Lesson XXXVII: Counting Backward by 2s, 3s, 4s, 5s"]},{"title":"Unit 7: Multiplication, Times 1 to 5 (Lessons XXXVIII–XLIII)","lessons":["Lesson XXXVIII: Multiplication: Swallows in a Picture","Lesson XXXIX: Multiplication: Times 1","Lesson XL: Multiplication: Times 2","Lesson XLI: Multiplication: Times 3","Lesson XLII: Multiplication: Times 4","Lesson XLIII: Multiplication: Times 5"]},{"title":"Unit 8: Multiplication, Times 6 to 10 and Review (Lessons XLIV–LI)","lessons":["Lesson XLIV: Multiplication: Times 6","Lesson XLV: Multiplication: Times 7","Lesson XLVI: Multiplication: Times 8","Lesson XLVII: Multiplication: Times 9","Lesson XLVIII: Multiplication: Times 10","Lesson XLIX: Review of Multiplication","Lesson L: Review: Mixed Operations","Lesson LI: Promiscuous (Mixed) Questions"]},{"title":"Unit 9: Division, 2 In to 6 In (Lessons LII–LVII)","lessons":["Lesson LII: Division: Ducks and Geese","Lesson LIII: Division: 2 In","Lesson LIV: Division: 3 In","Lesson LV: Division: 4 In","Lesson LVI: Division: 5 In","Lesson LVII: Division: 6 In"]},{"title":"Unit 10: Division, 7 In to 10 In and Review (Lessons LVIII–LXIII)","lessons":["Lesson LVIII: Division: 7 In","Lesson LIX: Division: 8 In","Lesson LX: Division: 9 In","Lesson LXI: Division: 10 In","Lesson LXII: Review of Division","Lesson LXIII: Finding the Cost of One"]},{"title":"Unit 11: Signs and Larger Numbers (Lessons LXIV–LXXI)","lessons":["Lesson LXIV: Signs: + − × ÷ =","Lesson LXV: Adding to Numbers 16–20","Lesson LXVI: Adding to Larger Numbers","Lesson LXVII: Addition Word Problems","Lesson LXVIII: Subtraction Word Problems","Lesson LXIX: Using Several Signs","Lesson LXX: Money Word Problems","Lesson LXXI: Multiplying, Then Subtracting"]},{"title":"Unit 12: Review Problems (Lessons LXXII–LXXVIII)","lessons":["Lesson LXXII: How Many Times? (to 20)","Lesson LXXIII: How Many Times? (larger numbers)","Lesson LXXIV: Promiscuous (Mixed) Questions","Lesson LXXV: Review Problems","Lesson LXXVI: Trading Goods","Lesson LXXVII: Paying in Other Goods","Lesson LXXVIII: Counting by 2s, 3s, 4s to 100"]},{"title":"Unit 13: Tables of Money and Measures (Lessons LXXIX–LXXXIX)","lessons":["Lesson LXXIX: Tables: United States Money","Lesson LXXX: Tables: English Money","Lesson LXXXI: Tables: Troy Weight","Lesson LXXXII: Tables: Avoirdupois Weight","Lesson LXXXIII: Tables: Dry Measure","Lesson LXXXIV: Tables: Liquid Measure","Lesson LXXXV: Tables: Long Measure","Lesson LXXXVI: Tables: Square Measure","Lesson LXXXVII: Tables: Apothecaries'' Weight","Lesson LXXXVIII: Tables: Circles, Paper, Books, and Counting","Lesson LXXXIX: Review of Tables"]}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set seller_name = excluded.seller_name, seller_kind = excluded.seller_kind, seller_verified = excluded.seller_verified, title = excluded.title, subjects = excluded.subjects, grade_min = excluded.grade_min, grade_max = excluded.grade_max, philosophies = excluded.philosophies, format = excluded.format, price = excluded.price, description = excluded.description, includes = excluded.includes, lessons = excluded.lessons, lessons_per_week = excluded.lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson = excluded.minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement = excluded.parent_involvement, family_style = excluded.family_style, lesson_steps = excluded.lesson_steps, cover = excluded.cover, color = excluded.color, esa_eligible = excluded.esa_eligible, package_id = excluded.package_id, package_version = excluded.package_version, license = excluded.license, attribution = excluded.attribution, outline = excluded.outline;
insert into public.package_releases (listing_id, version, content) values ('l-lib-rays-primary-arithmetic', 1, '{"units":[{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-u1","title":"Unit 1: Numbers and Figures (Lessons I–VI)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-1-1","title":"Lesson I: Counting to 10","steps":["Count out 1 to 10 buttons, touching each one as you say its number","Point to each row of balls in the book and say the number; write the figures 1–9","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson I (p. 5)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n11/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 5","questions":["Count these: ● ● ● ● ●. How many?","Write the figure for seven.","Which is more, 6 or 9?"],"answers":["5","7","9"],"notes":"Movable objects beat pictures; keep a jar of beans handy all year."},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-1-2","title":"Lesson II: Numbers 11 to 40","steps":["Bundle straws into tens with a rubber band; show 23 as 2 bundles and 3 loose","Read the number words and figures from eleven to forty, one column at a time","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson II (p. 6)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n12/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 6","questions":["How many tens and ones in 34?","Write twenty-seven in figures.","What number comes right after 29?"],"answers":["3 tens and 4 ones","27","30"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-1-3","title":"Lesson III: Numbers 41 to 70","steps":["Make 56 with ten-bundles and loose straws","Read forty-one to seventy aloud, then review the earlier columns","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson III (p. 6)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n12/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 6","questions":["Write fifty-eight in figures.","What number is 6 tens and 1 one?","What number comes just before 50?"],"answers":["58","61","49"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-1-4","title":"Lesson IV: Numbers 71 to 100","steps":["Count by tens to 100 with your straw bundles","Read seventy-one to one hundred from the table","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson IV (p. 7)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n13/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 7","questions":["Write ninety-four in figures.","How many tens make 100?","What number comes after 89?"],"answers":["94","10","90"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-1-5","title":"Lesson V: Reading Numbers to 100","steps":["Write ten numbers on cards, shuffle, and read them aloud","Read the mixed numbers in the book''s table, left to right","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson V (p. 7)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n13/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 7","questions":["Read this number aloud: 63","Read this number aloud: 18","Which is greater, 71 or 17?"],"answers":["Sixty-three","Eighteen","71"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-1-6","title":"Lesson VI: Writing Numbers to 100","steps":["Parent says a number; child builds it with bundles, then writes it","Write the book''s number lists on a slate or paper","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson VI (p. 8)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n14/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 8","questions":["Write forty-five.","Write ninety.","Write sixty-two."],"answers":["45","90","62"]}]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-u2","title":"Unit 2: Oral Exercises to 10 (Lessons VII–X)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-2-1","title":"Lesson VII: Oral Exercises: Numbers to 5","steps":["Use 5 counters in small groups to act out each question","Answer the book''s oral questions about 1 through 5","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson VII (p. 9)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n15/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 9","questions":["How many are 3 and 2?","Two taken from 4 leaves how many?","How many twos in 4?"],"answers":["5","2","2"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-2-2","title":"Lesson VIII: Oral Exercises: Numbers to 8","steps":["Use 8 beans; split them into piles to answer each question","Work the oral exercises on 6, 7 and 8 aloud","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson VIII (p. 10)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n16/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 10","questions":["How many are 4 and 3?","Five from 8 leaves how many?","How many are two times 4?"],"answers":["7","3","8"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-2-3","title":"Lesson IX: Oral Exercises: Numbers to 10","steps":["Lay out 10 pebbles and make every pair that makes 10","Answer the oral exercises on 9 and 10","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson IX (p. 11)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n17/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 11","questions":["How many are 6 and 4?","Seven from 10 leaves how many?","How many fives in 10?"],"answers":["10","3","2"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-2-4","title":"Lesson X: Counting Birds in a Picture","steps":["Line up toy animals in two groups and count each group","Look at the bird picture, count the flocks, and answer the questions","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson X (p. 12)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n18/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 12","questions":["There are 3 birds on a fence and 4 more fly down. How many birds now?","How many are 5 and 6?","Two branches each hold 4 flowers. How many flowers in all?"],"answers":["7","11","8"]}]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-u3","title":"Unit 3: Addition, Adding 1 to 5 (Lessons XI–XV)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-3-1","title":"Lesson XI: Addition: Adding 1","steps":["Add one bean at a time to a pile and say the new total","Say the table ''1 and 1 are 2…'' then answer the book''s problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XI (p. 13)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n19/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 13","questions":["How many are 1 and 8?","Tom has 6 marbles and finds 1 more. How many now?","How many are 10 and 1?"],"answers":["9","7","11"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-3-2","title":"Lesson XII: Addition: Adding 2","steps":["Add pairs of buttons to a pile and count on","Recite the 2s addition table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XII (p. 14)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n20/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 14","questions":["How many are 2 and 7?","Mary has 5 cents; her mother gives her 2 more. How many now?","How many are 8 and 2?"],"answers":["9","7 cents","10"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-3-3","title":"Lesson XIII: Addition: Adding 3","steps":["Make groups of 3 blocks and add them to other groups","Recite the 3s table and solve the book''s problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XIII (p. 15)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n21/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 15","questions":["How many are 3 and 6?","A hen has 3 chicks in the yard and 5 in the barn. How many chicks?","How many are 3 and 9?"],"answers":["9","8","12"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-3-4","title":"Lesson XIV: Addition: Adding 4","steps":["Use 4-cup muffin tins or egg cartons to show 4 and more","Recite the 4s table and solve the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XIV (p. 16)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n22/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 16","questions":["How many are 4 and 7?","Jane picked 4 apples and Ann picked 6. How many apples?","How many are 9 and 4?"],"answers":["11","10","13"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-3-5","title":"Lesson XV: Addition: Adding 5","steps":["Show 5 with one hand of fingers, then add more counters","Recite the 5s table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XV (p. 17)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n23/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 17","questions":["How many are 5 and 8?","A boy has 5 tops and buys 4 more. How many tops?","How many are 6 and 5?"],"answers":["13","9","11"]}]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-u4","title":"Unit 4: Addition, Adding 6 to 10 and Review (Lessons XVI–XXIII)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-4-1","title":"Lesson XVI: Addition: Adding 6","steps":["Lay out 6 spoons and add other groups","Recite the 6s table and solve the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XVI (p. 18)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n24/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 18","questions":["How many are 6 and 7?","Six ducks swim and 6 more join them. How many ducks?","How many are 6 and 9?"],"answers":["13","12","15"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-4-2","title":"Lesson XVII: Addition: Adding 7","steps":["Count 7 days on a calendar and add more days","Recite the 7s table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XVII (p. 19)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n25/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 19","questions":["How many are 7 and 8?","Sam reads 7 pages one day and 5 the next. How many pages?","How many are 7 and 7?"],"answers":["15","12","14"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-4-3","title":"Lesson XVIII: Addition: Adding 8","steps":["Add groups of 8 crayons to smaller groups","Recite the 8s table and solve the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XVIII (p. 20)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n26/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 20","questions":["How many are 8 and 6?","A cat has 8 kittens and a dog has 3 puppies. How many babies?","How many are 8 and 9?"],"answers":["14","11","17"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-4-4","title":"Lesson XIX: Addition: Adding 9","steps":["Make 9 with beans, then add more; notice 9 and 4 is one less than 10 and 4","Recite the 9s table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XIX (p. 21)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n27/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 21","questions":["How many are 9 and 5?","Lucy had 9 cents and earned 9 more. How many cents?","How many are 9 and 8?"],"answers":["14","18","17"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-4-5","title":"Lesson XX: Addition: Adding 10","steps":["Use a ten-bundle of straws plus loose straws","Recite the 10s table and solve the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XX (p. 22)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n28/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 22","questions":["How many are 10 and 7?","A farmer has 10 cows and buys 6. How many cows?","How many are 4 and 10?"],"answers":["17","16","14"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-4-6","title":"Lesson XXI: Review of Addition","steps":["Roll two dice and add the dots aloud","Work the review questions, including adding three numbers","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXI (p. 23)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n29/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 23","questions":["How many are 5 and 7?","How many are 3 and 4 and 6?","How many are 2 and 8 and 9?"],"answers":["12","13","19"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-4-7","title":"Lesson XXII: Review of Addition (continued)","steps":["Add three small piles of beans together","Finish the second review set aloud","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXII (p. 24)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n30/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 24","questions":["How many are 6 and 6 and 3?","How many are 4 and 9?","How many are 7 and 2 and 8?"],"answers":["15","13","17"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-4-8","title":"Lesson XXIII: Addition Problems","steps":["Act out a shopping story with pennies","Read each word problem and answer with a full sentence","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXIII (p. 26)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n32/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 26","questions":["Ann paid 4 cents for a pencil, 3 for paper, and 5 for a ribbon. How much in all?","Three boys caught 2, 6, and 5 fish. How many fish?","A girl has 7 dolls and gets 8 more. How many dolls?"],"answers":["12 cents","13","15"]}]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-u5","title":"Unit 5: Subtraction, Taking 1 to 5 (Lessons XXIV–XXIX)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-5-1","title":"Lesson XXIV: Subtraction: Owls and Bats","steps":["Set out 7 toys; take some away and count what is left","Look at the owls-and-bats picture and answer the questions","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXIV (p. 27)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n33/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 27","questions":["Five owls sat on a branch; 3 flew away. How many were left?","Eight bats were flying; 6 went into a barn. How many still flying?","Six less 4 are how many?"],"answers":["2","2","2"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-5-2","title":"Lesson XXV: Subtraction: Taking 1","steps":["Take one bean at a time from a pile","Recite ''1 from 1 leaves 0…'' and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXV (p. 28)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n34/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 28","questions":["1 from 9 leaves how many?","Jim had 7 apples and ate 1. How many left?","1 from 11 leaves how many?"],"answers":["8","6","10"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-5-3","title":"Lesson XXVI: Subtraction: Taking 2","steps":["Take pairs of buttons away from a pile","Recite the 2s subtraction table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXVI (p. 29)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n35/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 29","questions":["2 from 9 leaves how many?","Kate had 8 cents and spent 2. How many left?","2 from 12 leaves how many?"],"answers":["7","6 cents","10"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-5-4","title":"Lesson XXVII: Subtraction: Taking 3","steps":["Remove 3 blocks from towers of different sizes","Recite the 3s subtraction table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXVII (p. 30)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n36/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 30","questions":["3 from 10 leaves how many?","A nest had 6 eggs; 3 hatched. How many have not hatched?","3 from 12 leaves how many?"],"answers":["7","3","9"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-5-5","title":"Lesson XXVIII: Subtraction: Taking 4","steps":["Take 4 spoons from a drawer pile","Recite the 4s subtraction table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXVIII (p. 31)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n37/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 31","questions":["4 from 11 leaves how many?","There were 9 pears; 4 were eaten. How many left?","4 from 13 leaves how many?"],"answers":["7","5","9"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-5-6","title":"Lesson XXIX: Subtraction: Taking 5","steps":["Hide one hand of 5 fingers to show taking 5","Recite the 5s subtraction table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXIX (p. 32)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n38/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 32","questions":["5 from 12 leaves how many?","Ned had 14 marbles and lost 5. How many now?","5 from 9 leaves how many?"],"answers":["7","9","4"]}]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-u6","title":"Unit 6: Subtraction, Taking 6 to 10 and Review (Lessons XXX–XXXVII)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-6-1","title":"Lesson XXX: Subtraction: Taking 6","steps":["Take 6 eggs from a carton of 12 and count what remains","Recite the 6s subtraction table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXX (p. 33)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n39/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 33","questions":["6 from 13 leaves how many?","A baker had 15 rolls and sold 6. How many left?","6 from 10 leaves how many?"],"answers":["7","9","4"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-6-2","title":"Lesson XXXI: Subtraction: Taking 7","steps":["Use 7 days of the week; cross off days","Recite the 7s subtraction table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXXI (p. 34)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n40/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 34","questions":["7 from 15 leaves how many?","Sue had 12 ribbons and gave away 7. How many left?","7 from 11 leaves how many?"],"answers":["8","5","4"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-6-3","title":"Lesson XXXII: Subtraction: Taking 8","steps":["Remove 8 crayons from a box of 16","Recite the 8s subtraction table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXXII (p. 35)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n41/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 35","questions":["8 from 14 leaves how many?","There were 17 sheep; 8 went into the barn. How many outside?","8 from 12 leaves how many?"],"answers":["6","9","4"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-6-4","title":"Lesson XXXIII: Subtraction: Taking 9","steps":["Take 9 from a pile by taking 10 and putting 1 back","Recite the 9s subtraction table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXXIII (p. 36)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n42/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 36","questions":["9 from 16 leaves how many?","Tom had 13 cents and spent 9. How many left?","9 from 18 leaves how many?"],"answers":["7","4 cents","9"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-6-5","title":"Lesson XXXIV: Subtraction: Taking 10","steps":["Remove a ten-bundle from bundles and loose straws","Recite the 10s subtraction table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXXIV (p. 37)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n43/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 37","questions":["10 from 17 leaves how many?","A tree had 19 apples; 10 fell. How many still on the tree?","10 from 20 leaves how many?"],"answers":["7","9","10"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-6-6","title":"Lesson XXXV: Review of Subtraction","steps":["Play ''how many left?'' by hiding beans under a cup","Work the review questions using the word ''less''","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXXV (p. 38)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n44/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 38","questions":["How many are 11 less 5?","How many are 14 less 8?","How many are 9 less 6?"],"answers":["6","6","3"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-6-7","title":"Lesson XXXVI: Addition and Subtraction Together","steps":["Add two piles of beans, then take some away","Solve each ''and … less'' question in two steps","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXXVI (p. 39)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n45/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 39","questions":["How many are 4 and 6, less 3?","How many are 8 and 5, less 7?","How many are 9 and 9, less 10?"],"answers":["7","6","8"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-6-8","title":"Lesson XXXVII: Counting Backward by 2s, 3s, 4s, 5s","steps":["Hop backward along a number line taped on the floor","Count down from 20 by 2s, 3s, 4s and 5s as the book directs","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXXVII (p. 40)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n46/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 40","questions":["Count down from 12 by 3s.","Count down from 20 by 5s.","Count down from 16 by 4s."],"answers":["12, 9, 6, 3, 0","20, 15, 10, 5, 0","16, 12, 8, 4, 0"]}]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-u7","title":"Unit 7: Multiplication, Times 1 to 5 (Lessons XXXVIII–XLIII)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-7-1","title":"Lesson XXXVIII: Multiplication: Swallows in a Picture","steps":["Set out 3 cups with 4 beans in each and count all","Count the swallows in the picture and answer the times questions","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXXVIII (p. 41)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n47/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 41","questions":["There are 2 nests with 5 eggs in each. How many eggs?","How many are 3 times 3?","Four birds each have 2 wings. How many wings?"],"answers":["10","9","8"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-7-2","title":"Lesson XXXIX: Multiplication: Times 1","steps":["Show 1 bean in each of 6 cups","Recite ''1 time 2 is 2…'' and work the cost problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XXXIX (p. 42)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n48/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 42","questions":["How many are 7 times 1?","Figs cost 1 cent each. What do 9 figs cost?","How many are 1 time 5?"],"answers":["7","9 cents","5"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-7-3","title":"Lesson XL: Multiplication: Times 2","steps":["Pair socks and count by 2s","Recite the 2s times table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XL (p. 43)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n49/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 43","questions":["How many are 2 times 6?","Each child has 2 shoes. How many shoes do 7 children have?","How many are 9 times 2?"],"answers":["12","14","18"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-7-4","title":"Lesson XLI: Multiplication: Times 3","steps":["Make 3-leaf clovers from paper and count leaves","Recite the 3s times table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XLI (p. 44)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n50/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 44","questions":["How many are 3 times 5?","Pencils are 3 cents each. What do 6 cost?","How many are 3 times 8?"],"answers":["15","18 cents","24"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-7-5","title":"Lesson XLII: Multiplication: Times 4","steps":["Count the legs on 4-legged toys or chairs","Recite the 4s times table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XLII (p. 45)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n51/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 45","questions":["How many are 4 times 6?","Each wagon has 4 wheels. How many wheels on 5 wagons?","How many are 4 times 9?"],"answers":["24","20","36"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-7-6","title":"Lesson XLIII: Multiplication: Times 5","steps":["Count nickels by 5s","Recite the 5s times table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XLIII (p. 46)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n52/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 46","questions":["How many are 5 times 7?","Tops cost 5 cents each. What do 4 tops cost?","How many are 5 times 10?"],"answers":["35","20 cents","50"]}]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-u8","title":"Unit 8: Multiplication, Times 6 to 10 and Review (Lessons XLIV–LI)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-8-1","title":"Lesson XLIV: Multiplication: Times 6","steps":["Count eggs in half-dozen cartons","Recite the 6s times table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XLIV (p. 47)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n53/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 47","questions":["How many are 6 times 4?","Each box holds 6 eggs. How many eggs in 7 boxes?","How many are 6 times 6?"],"answers":["24","42","36"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-8-2","title":"Lesson XLV: Multiplication: Times 7","steps":["Count days in several weeks on a calendar","Recite the 7s times table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XLV (p. 48)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n54/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 48","questions":["How many are 7 times 3?","How many days are in 5 weeks?","How many are 7 times 8?"],"answers":["21","35","56"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-8-3","title":"Lesson XLVI: Multiplication: Times 8","steps":["Count the legs on paper spiders (8 each)","Recite the 8s times table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XLVI (p. 49)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n55/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 49","questions":["How many are 8 times 5?","A spider has 8 legs. How many legs do 3 spiders have?","How many are 8 times 7?"],"answers":["40","24","56"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-8-4","title":"Lesson XLVII: Multiplication: Times 9","steps":["Use the finger trick or bean groups for 9s","Recite the 9s times table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XLVII (p. 50)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n56/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 50","questions":["How many are 9 times 4?","Melons cost 9 cents each. What do 6 cost?","How many are 9 times 9?"],"answers":["36","54 cents","81"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-8-5","title":"Lesson XLVIII: Multiplication: Times 10","steps":["Count dimes by 10s","Recite the 10s times table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XLVIII (p. 51)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n57/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 51","questions":["How many are 10 times 6?","Each bundle has 10 sticks. How many sticks in 8 bundles?","How many are 3 times 10?"],"answers":["60","80","30"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-8-6","title":"Lesson XLIX: Review of Multiplication","steps":["Build arrays with beans for any two numbers","Work the review, including three numbers multiplied together","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson XLIX (p. 52)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n58/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 52","questions":["How many are 4 times 7?","How many are 2 times 3 times 4?","How many are 6 times 9?"],"answers":["28","24","54"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-8-7","title":"Lesson L: Review: Mixed Operations","steps":["Add, take away, and then make groups with counters","Solve each question step by step, saying each result","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson L (p. 54)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n60/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 54","questions":["How many are 3 and 5, less 2, multiplied by 4?","How many are 6 and 4, less 5, multiplied by 3?","How many are 9 less 7, multiplied by 8?"],"answers":["24","15","16"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-8-8","title":"Lesson LI: Promiscuous (Mixed) Questions","steps":["Play store with pennies and price tags","Read each mixed word problem and explain your steps","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LI (p. 55)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n61/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 55","questions":["Ben had 15 cents and bought 2 apples at 4 cents each. How much did he have left?","A girl bought 3 ribbons at 5 cents each. What did she pay?","Two boys had 9 marbles each; one lost 3. How many marbles do they have together now?"],"answers":["7 cents","15 cents","15"]}]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-u9","title":"Unit 9: Division, 2 In to 6 In (Lessons LII–LVII)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-9-1","title":"Lesson LII: Division: Ducks and Geese","steps":["Share 12 crackers fairly among 3 plates","Look at the ducks-and-geese picture and answer the sharing questions","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LII (p. 56)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n62/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 56","questions":["Twelve ducks split into 3 equal flocks. How many in each flock?","How many times is 4 contained in 16?","Six geese have 12 feet. How many feet does each goose have?"],"answers":["4","4","2"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-9-2","title":"Lesson LIII: Division: 2 In","steps":["Split beans into groups of 2","Recite ''2 in 2, 1 time…'' and work the buying problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LIII (p. 57)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n63/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 57","questions":["How many times is 2 contained in 14?","Apples are 2 cents each. How many can you buy for 10 cents?","How many times is 2 contained in 18?"],"answers":["7","5","9"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-9-3","title":"Lesson LIV: Division: 3 In","steps":["Make groups of 3 buttons from a pile of 21","Recite the 3s division table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LIV (p. 58)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n64/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 58","questions":["How many times is 3 contained in 24?","Share 15 cherries among 3 children. How many each?","How many times is 3 contained in 9?"],"answers":["8","5","3"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-9-4","title":"Lesson LV: Division: 4 In","steps":["Put 4 beans in each cup until 28 are used","Recite the 4s division table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LV (p. 59)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n65/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 59","questions":["How many times is 4 contained in 32?","Lemons are 4 cents each. How many for 20 cents?","How many times is 4 contained in 36?"],"answers":["8","5","9"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-9-5","title":"Lesson LVI: Division: 5 In","steps":["Group pennies into nickels'' worth (5s)","Recite the 5s division table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LVI (p. 60)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n66/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 60","questions":["How many times is 5 contained in 35?","Kites are 5 cents each. How many for 30 cents?","How many times is 5 contained in 45?"],"answers":["7","6","9"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-9-6","title":"Lesson LVII: Division: 6 In","steps":["Pack 6 eggs per carton from a pile","Recite the 6s division table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LVII (p. 61)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n67/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 61","questions":["How many times is 6 contained in 42?","Share 24 nuts among 6 boys. How many each?","How many times is 6 contained in 54?"],"answers":["7","4","9"]}]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-u10","title":"Unit 10: Division, 7 In to 10 In and Review (Lessons LVIII–LXIII)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-10-1","title":"Lesson LVIII: Division: 7 In","steps":["Arrange 28 counters into rows of 7","Recite the 7s division table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LVIII (p. 62)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n68/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 62","questions":["How many times is 7 contained in 56?","A man walked 21 miles in 7 hours. How far in 1 hour?","How many times is 7 contained in 49?"],"answers":["8","3 miles","7"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-10-2","title":"Lesson LIX: Division: 8 In","steps":["Make 8-legged paper spiders from 40 ''legs''","Recite the 8s division table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LIX (p. 63)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n69/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 63","questions":["How many times is 8 contained in 48?","Eight boys share 64 marbles. How many each?","How many times is 8 contained in 72?"],"answers":["6","8","9"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-10-3","title":"Lesson LX: Division: 9 In","steps":["Make groups of 9 from 45 beans","Recite the 9s division table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LX (p. 64)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n70/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 64","questions":["How many times is 9 contained in 63?","Pears cost 9 cents each. How many 