gns and build number sentences with counters","Read about the signs, then solve the + problems with numbers past 10","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXIV (p. 69)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n75/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 69","questions":["11 + 6 = ?","What does the sign × mean?","12 + 7 = ?"],"answers":["17","Multiplied by","19"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-11-2","title":"Lesson LXV: Adding to Numbers 16–20","steps":["Use a ten-bundle plus loose straws to add","Work the rows of + problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXV (p. 70)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n76/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 70","questions":["17 + 5 = ?","8 + 18 = ?","19 + 9 = ?"],"answers":["22","26","28"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-11-3","title":"Lesson LXVI: Adding to Larger Numbers","steps":["Add to 2-digit numbers on a hundred chart","Work the rows, noticing the ones digits repeat","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXVI (p. 71)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n77/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 71","questions":["34 + 5 = ?","46 + 7 = ?","68 + 9 = ?"],"answers":["39","53","77"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-11-4","title":"Lesson LXVII: Addition Word Problems","steps":["Act out each story with coins","Read each problem and answer in a full sentence","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXVII (p. 72)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n78/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 72","questions":["I had 18 cents and was given 6 more. How many cents now?","A slate cost 15 cents and a book 12 cents. What did both cost?","Ann has 23 beads and buys 7. How many beads?"],"answers":["24 cents","27 cents","30"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-11-5","title":"Lesson LXVIII: Subtraction Word Problems","steps":["Act out losing and spending with real coins","Read each problem and answer in a full sentence","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXVIII (p. 73)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n79/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 73","questions":["Tom had 17 marbles and lost 5. How many now?","A girl had 20 cents and spent 6. How many left?","There were 19 plums; 7 were given away. How many left?"],"answers":["12","14 cents","12"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-11-6","title":"Lesson LXIX: Using Several Signs","steps":["Line up sign cards and number cards and work left to right","Solve each + and − string, saying each step","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXIX (p. 74)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n80/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 74","questions":["6 + 8 + 3 − 5 = ?","10 + 4 + 7 − 6 = ?","9 + 9 − 8 = ?"],"answers":["12","15","10"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-11-7","title":"Lesson LXX: Money Word Problems","steps":["Use real coins to spend and make change","Work each problem, saying what was spent and what is left","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXX (p. 75)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n81/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 75","questions":["Fred had 20 cents and spent 7. How many cents left?","Nell had 25 cents; she spent 5 for a top and 8 for a ball. How much left?","Mother gave me 10 cents and father enough more to make 18. How much did father give?"],"answers":["13 cents","12 cents","8 cents"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-11-8","title":"Lesson LXXI: Multiplying, Then Subtracting","steps":["Make an array, then take some counters away","Solve each × and − problem","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXI (p. 76)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n82/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 76","questions":["4 × 5 − 6 = ?","7 × 3 − 9 = ?","6 × 6 − 10 = ?"],"answers":["14","12","26"]}]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-u12","title":"Unit 12: Review Problems (Lessons LXXII–LXXVIII)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-12-1","title":"Lesson LXXII: How Many Times? (to 20)","steps":["Group counters to see how many 3s make 12","Answer ''Six are how many times 2?'' style questions","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXII (p. 77)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n83/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 77","questions":["Twelve are how many times 4?","Fifteen are how many times 5?","Eighteen are how many times 6?"],"answers":["3","3","3"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-12-2","title":"Lesson LXXIII: How Many Times? (larger numbers)","steps":["Use arrays of beans to check each answer","Answer the larger ''how many times'' questions","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXIII (p. 78)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n84/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 78","questions":["Thirty-two are how many times 8?","Forty-two are how many times 7?","Forty-five are how many times 9?"],"answers":["4","6","5"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-12-3","title":"Lesson LXXIV: Promiscuous (Mixed) Questions","steps":["Play store with several items and prices","Work each mixed problem, telling which operation you used","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXIV (p. 79)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n85/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 79","questions":["Kate bought 3 peaches at 2 cents and 1 orange at 5 cents. What did she pay?","Ed has 3 kites and Ted has 4 times as many. How many kites do both have?","A man had 30 dollars and spent 4 dollars a day for 5 days. How much was left?"],"answers":["11 cents","15","10 dollars"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-12-4","title":"Lesson LXXV: Review Problems","steps":["Trade marbles between two piles to act out the first problem","Read each problem and solve it in steps","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXV (p. 80)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n86/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 80","questions":["Ann and Bea each had 8 shells; Ann gave Bea 3. How many does each have now?","Jim owed me 15 cents and gave me 2 apples worth 4 cents each. How much does he still owe?","I bought 2 lemons at 3 cents and 2 pears at 4 cents. What did I spend?"],"answers":["Ann 5, Bea 11","7 cents","14 cents"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-12-5","title":"Lesson LXXVI: Trading Goods","steps":["Trade ''apples'' for ''oranges'' using price tags","Work each trading problem: find the cost first, then the number of things","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXVI (p. 81)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n87/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 81","questions":["Max bought 4 pears at 3 cents each and paid with apples at 2 cents each. How many apples?","Ribbon is 5 cents a yard. What do 4 yards cost, and how many 10-cent coins pay for it?","How many 4-cent tops pay for 2 books at 6 cents each?"],"answers":["6","20 cents; 2 coins","3"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-12-6","title":"Lesson LXXVII: Paying in Other Goods","steps":["Use play money to compare prices","Solve the ''how many … will pay for …'' problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXVII (p. 82)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n88/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 82","questions":["How many pencils at 2 cents each will pay for 3 erasers at 4 cents each?","How many kites at 5 cents each will pay for 2 balls at 10 cents each?","How many apples at 3 cents each will pay for 6 plums at 2 cents each?"],"answers":["6","4","4"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-12-7","title":"Lesson LXXVIII: Counting by 2s, 3s, 4s to 100","steps":["Skip-count aloud while bouncing a ball","Count up and back down by the steps the book gives","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXVIII (p. 83)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n89/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 83","questions":["Count by 4s from 4 to 24.","Count back by 3s from 30 to 15.","Count by 2s from 86 to 100."],"answers":["4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24","30, 27, 24, 21, 18, 15","86, 88, 90, 92, 94, 96, 98, 100"]}]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-u13","title":"Unit 13: Tables of Money and Measures (Lessons LXXIX–LXXXIX)","lessons":[{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-13-1","title":"Lesson LXXIX: Tables: United States Money","steps":["Sort real pennies and dimes; trade 10 pennies for a dime","Learn the money table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXIX (p. 84)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n90/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 84","questions":["How many cents in 4 dimes?","How many dimes in 70 cents?","How many dimes in 2 dollars?"],"answers":["40 cents","7 dimes","20 dimes"],"notes":"The book also lists the ''eagle'' (a $10 gold coin); just explain it as old money."},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-13-2","title":"Lesson LXXX: Tables: English Money","steps":["Use buttons as ''pence'' and bundle 12 into a ''shilling''","Read the English money table and work a few problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXX (p. 85)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n91/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 85","questions":["How many farthings in 2 pence?","How many pence in 1 shilling?","How many shillings in 2 pounds?"],"answers":["8","12","40"],"notes":"Historical units; treat this lesson as a fun look at old British money."},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-13-3","title":"Lesson LXXXI: Tables: Troy Weight","steps":["Weigh small things on a kitchen scale and talk about ounces","Read the Troy weight table and work a few problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXXI (p. 86)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n92/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 86","questions":["How many ounces in 2 pounds Troy?","How many pennyweights in 3 ounces?","How many grains in 1 pennyweight?"],"answers":["24","60","24"],"notes":"Troy weight was used for gold and silver; keep it light."},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-13-4","title":"Lesson LXXXII: Tables: Avoirdupois Weight","steps":["Weigh flour or apples on a scale in pounds and ounces","Learn the table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXXII (p. 87)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n93/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 87","questions":["How many ounces in 3 pounds?","How many pounds in 2 hundred-weight?","How many pounds in 1 ton?"],"answers":["48","200","2000"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-13-5","title":"Lesson LXXXIII: Tables: Dry Measure","steps":["Measure dry beans with pint and quart containers","Learn the dry measure table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXXIII (p. 88)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n94/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 88","questions":["How many pints in 4 quarts?","How many quarts in 2 pecks?","How many pecks in 3 bushels?"],"answers":["8","16","12"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-13-6","title":"Lesson LXXXIV: Tables: Liquid Measure","steps":["Pour water between cup, pint, quart and gallon containers","Learn the liquid measure table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXXIV (p. 89)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n95/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 89","questions":["How many pints in 3 quarts?","How many quarts in 2 gallons?","How many gills in 2 pints?"],"answers":["6","8","8"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-13-7","title":"Lesson LXXXV: Tables: Long Measure","steps":["Measure the table with a ruler and a yardstick","Learn the long measure table and work the problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXXV (p. 90)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n96/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 90","questions":["How many inches in 2 feet?","How many feet in 5 yards?","How many yards in 12 feet?"],"answers":["24","15","4"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-13-8","title":"Lesson LXXXVI: Tables: Square Measure","steps":["Cover a 3-by-3 square of floor tiles or paper squares","Learn the square measure table and work the area problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXXVI (p. 91)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n97/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 91","questions":["How many square feet in 2 square yards?","A rug is 4 feet long and 3 feet wide. How many square feet?","How many square yards in 27 square feet?"],"answers":["18","12","3"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-13-9","title":"Lesson LXXXVII: Tables: Apothecaries'' Weight","steps":["Look at a medicine cup and talk about small measures","Read the apothecaries'' table and work a few problems","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXXVII (p. 92)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n98/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 92","questions":["How many scruples in 2 drams?","How many drams in 1 ounce?","How many grains in 1 scruple?"],"answers":["6","8","20"],"notes":"An old druggist''s table; enjoy it as history."},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-13-10","title":"Lesson LXXXVIII: Tables: Circles, Paper, Books, and Counting","steps":["Count a dozen eggs, then talk about a ''score'' and a ''gross''","Read the circular measure, paper, and miscellaneous tables","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXXVIII (p. 93)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n99/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 93","questions":["How many things in 2 dozen?","How many things in a score?","How many sheets of paper in 2 quires?"],"answers":["24","20","48"]},{"id":"rays-primary-arithmetic-13-11","title":"Lesson LXXXIX: Review of Tables","steps":["Play a quick quiz game with money and measuring tools","Work the review of all the tables","Solve the 3 practice problems below, using objects if needed"],"links":[{"label":"Lesson LXXXIX (p. 94)","url":"https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog/page/n100/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 94","questions":["How many cents in 6 dimes?","How many inches in 3 feet?","How many quarts in 3 gallons?"],"answers":["60","36","12"]}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"“Ray''s New Primary Arithmetic for Young Learners (Eclectic Educational Series)” by Joseph Ray (1877), in the public domain. Free full text: https://archive.org/details/raysnewprimarya00raygoog. Lesson plan by Freerange."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (listing_id, version) do update set content = excluded.content;
insert into public.listings (id, seller_name, seller_kind, seller_verified, title, subjects, grade_min, grade_max, philosophies, format, price, description, includes, lessons, lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement, family_style, lesson_steps, cover, color, esa_eligible, created_at, package_id, package_version, license, attribution, outline) values ('l-lib-seven-little-sisters', 'Jane Andrews', 'open', false, 'The Seven Little Sisters', array['geography', 'reading']::text[], 1, 3, array['charlotte_mason', 'classical', 'traditional']::text[], 'online', 0, 'Jane Andrews''s classic 1861 storybook visits seven girls around the world, from an Arctic snow country to the desert, the Alps, a river boat in China, and a log cabin in the American woods. Read a chapter aloud, let your child tell it back, then find that sister''s home on a globe or draw her world. Some descriptions of peoples are badly dated, so parent notes suggest how to talk about them; the full text is free online.', array['18 lessons in 4 units covering every chapter', 'Link to the free full text for every lesson', 'A globe or map step for each little sister''s home', 'Narration and a drawing, copywork, or hands-on activity each day', 'Questions with answers for every lesson', 'Parent notes on dated or stereotyped portrayals and how life there looks today']::text[], 18, 3, 20, 'high', true, '{}'::text[], '🌍', '#3D9970', false, '2026-11-11T12:00:00.000Z', 'pk-lib-seven-little-sisters', 1, '{"kind":"public_domain"}'::jsonb, '“The Seven Little Sisters Who Live on the Round Ball That Floats in the Air” by Jane Andrews (1861), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/12631. Lesson plan by Freerange.', '[{"title":"Unit 1: The Round Ball, the Warm Country, and the Cold Country","lessons":["Lesson 1: The Ball Itself","Lesson 2: The Little Brown Baby","Lesson 3: Agoonack, the Esquimau Sister","Lesson 4: How Agoonack Lives Through the Long Summer"]},{"title":"Unit 2: Gemila of the Desert and Jeannette of the Mountains","lessons":["Lesson 5: Gemila, the Child of the Desert (part 1)","Lesson 6: Gemila, the Child of the Desert (part 2)","Lesson 7: Gemila, the Child of the Desert (part 3)","Lesson 8: The Little Mountain Maiden (part 1)","Lesson 9: The Little Mountain Maiden (part 2)"]},{"title":"Unit 3: Pen-se of China and Manenko of Africa","lessons":["Lesson 10: The Story of Pen-se (part 1)","Lesson 11: The Story of Pen-se (part 2)","Lesson 12: The Little Dark Girl (part 1)","Lesson 13: The Little Dark Girl (part 2)"]},{"title":"Unit 4: Louise, from the Rhine to the Western Forest","lessons":["Lesson 14: Louise, the Child of the Beautiful River Rhine (part 1)","Lesson 15: Louise, the Child of the Beautiful River Rhine (part 2)","Lesson 16: Louise, the Child of the Western Forest (part 1)","Lesson 17: Louise, the Child of the Western Forest (part 2)","Lesson 18: The Seven Little Sisters"]}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set seller_name = excluded.seller_name, seller_kind = excluded.seller_kind, seller_verified = excluded.seller_verified, title = excluded.title, subjects = excluded.subjects, grade_min = excluded.grade_min, grade_max = excluded.grade_max, philosophies = excluded.philosophies, format = excluded.format, price = excluded.price, description = excluded.description, includes = excluded.includes, lessons = excluded.lessons, lessons_per_week = excluded.lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson = excluded.minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement = excluded.parent_involvement, family_style = excluded.family_style, lesson_steps = excluded.lesson_steps, cover = excluded.cover, color = excluded.color, esa_eligible = excluded.esa_eligible, package_id = excluded.package_id, package_version = excluded.package_version, license = excluded.license, attribution = excluded.attribution, outline = excluded.outline;
insert into public.package_releases (listing_id, version, content) values ('l-lib-seven-little-sisters', 1, '{"units":[{"id":"seven-little-sisters-u1","title":"Unit 1: The Round Ball, the Warm Country, and the Cold Country","lessons":[{"id":"seven-little-sisters-1-1","title":"Lesson 1: The Ball Itself","steps":["Read “The Ball Itself” aloud together","Have your child tell back the different kinds of places the author shows on the ball: meadows, forests, mountains, ponds, desert, and ice","Spin a globe (or a ball) and find one green place, one sandy desert, and the icy top and bottom of the world"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Ball Itself”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12631/pg12631-images.html#id00038"}],"questions":["What is the big ball the author is really talking about?","Name the two very different places on the ball that she says you might not like.","Whose sledge does she promise we will come back to see?"],"answers":["The earth, the world we live on","The great hot plain of sand (the desert) and the cold land of ice and snow","Agoonack''s"],"notes":"Keep a globe or a world map handy for the whole book. Each sitting ends with finding where that little sister lives."},{"id":"seven-little-sisters-1-2","title":"Lesson 2: The Little Brown Baby","steps":["Read “The Little Brown Baby” aloud together","Narrate the baby''s night and morning: the swinging bed, the moon and stars, the bath in the brook","On the globe, run your finger along the warm band around the middle of the earth (near the equator) where she might live","Draw the little bed swinging in the tree with the moon peeping in"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Little Brown Baby”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12631/pg12631-images.html#id00049"}],"questions":["Why doesn''t the brown baby need warm clothes?","What is her bed made of, and where does it hang?","What does she drink from a cup made of a shell?"],"answers":["The sun shines so warmly where she lives","A swinging bed made from a twisting vine, hung in a tree","Sweet coconut milk"],"notes":"The book never says which warm country this is, and its picture of a baby who wears only beads is an 1860s storybook idea, not a real portrait. Families in tropical countries today live in towns and villages, wear clothes, and go to school. You might ask: “What did the author get right about warm places, and what might she have left out?”"},{"id":"seven-little-sisters-1-3","title":"Lesson 3: Agoonack, the Esquimau Sister","steps":["Read “Agoonack, the Esquimau Sister” aloud together","Have your child narrate, starting with the house that looks like an oven and ending with the feast for Nannook","Find Greenland and the Arctic at the top of the globe","Draw Agoonack on her bone sled pulled by two brown puppies under the northern lights"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Agoonack, the Esquimau Sister”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12631/pg12631-images.html#id00061"}],"questions":["What did Agoonack''s father make her sled from, and why not wood?","What keeps the little stone house warm inside?","What are the bright moving lights in the dark winter sky called?"],"answers":["Walrus and whale bones tied with sealskin, because no trees grow in that cold country","A lamp with moss wicks that burns walrus fat","The northern lights"],"notes":"“Esquimau” is an old spelling of “Eskimo,” a word many Arctic peoples today find outdated. Agoonack''s people would now be called Inuit (in Greenland, Kalaallit). Today Inuit families live in modern houses with schools, phones, and snowmobiles, and many still hunt and keep old traditions. The reindeer milk detail is the author''s mix-up; reindeer herding belongs to other northern peoples."},{"id":"seven-little-sisters-1-4","title":"Lesson 4: How Agoonack Lives Through the Long Summer","steps":["Read “How Agoonack Lives Through the Long Summer” aloud together","Narrate how the sun comes back a little more each day, and what the children do in the summer","With a lamp and a globe (or a flashlight and a ball), tilt the top toward the light and see how the top stays lit all the way around","Copywork: write one short sentence about the sun that never sets"],"links":[{"label":"Read “How Agoonack Lives Through the Long Summer”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12631/pg12631-images.html#id00091"}],"questions":["What strange thing does the summer sun do in Agoonack''s country?","How do the boys catch the white sea birds?","Why did no one throw a harpoon at the great white whale?"],"answers":["It shines all day and all night and does not set","They climb out on the rocks and catch them in long-handled nets","Her visit was thought to be a good sign of a mild winter"],"notes":"The midnight sun and the long polar night are real and a wonderful science moment. A lamp and a tilted ball show it better than any words."}]},{"id":"seven-little-sisters-u2","title":"Unit 2: Gemila of the Desert and Jeannette of the Mountains","lessons":[{"id":"seven-little-sisters-2-1","title":"Lesson 5: Gemila, the Child of the Desert (part 1)","steps":["Read the first part of “Gemila, the Child of the Desert,” from the start through the loading of the water and milk bags","Have your child narrate the evening at the tent and how the family packs up to move","Find the great deserts of North Africa and Arabia on the globe","Play tents: drape a blanket over two chairs as the author did when she was small"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Gemila” (part 1)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12631/pg12631-images.html#id00104"}],"questions":["What is Gemila''s house made of?","Why does the family have to move?","What do they carry their drinking water in?"],"answers":["A tent of goats''-hair cloth (or sheepskins) held up by poles","Their spring is drying up and the grass is gone","Big bags made of camel skin"],"notes":"Gemila''s family are Bedouin, desert herders of Arabia and North Africa. The author judges some of their customs by her own (“we could teach them better manners”), though she also praises their kindness to strangers. Many Bedouin today live in towns or settled villages, and some still keep herds and tents."},{"id":"seven-little-sisters-2-2","title":"Lesson 6: Gemila, the Child of the Desert (part 2)","steps":["Read the second part of “Gemila,” from Abdel Hassan mounting his black horse through the ostrich eggs","Narrate one day of the journey: starting under the stars, resting at noon, riding again at night","Go outside early or late in the day and look at how long your shadow is, the way Gemila watched the camels'' shadows","Draw an ostrich running with its wings spread like little sails"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Gemila” (part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12631/pg12631-images.html#id00121"}],"questions":["Why do the travelers rest in the tent in the middle of the day?","How do the ostrich''s small wings help it?","Why did Gemila clap when she saw the ostrich?"],"answers":["It is far too hot to travel then","It spreads them like little sails to help it run fast","She hoped the men would find its nest and the family would have big fresh eggs"],"notes":"The book repeats an old myth that ostriches hide their heads in the sand. They don''t! They lie flat and still to hide, which may have started the story. A fun chance to say that even books can be wrong."},{"id":"seven-little-sisters-2-3","title":"Lesson 7: Gemila, the Child of the Desert (part 3)","steps":["Read the last part of “Gemila,” from meeting Sheik Hassein''s caravan to the new home by the palm trees","Narrate the meeting with the caravan and the arrival at the spring","Draw the caravan of camels as a row of tiny dots far away, then large and close up"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Gemila” (part 3)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12631/pg12631-images.html#id00134"}],"questions":["What was Sheik Hassein''s long caravan of camels carrying?","How does Abdel Hassan greet the sheik?","How can a camel go four or five days without a drink?"],"answers":["Cloths, shawls, carpets, spices, and colored leather to sell","He presses the sheik''s hand to his lips and forehead and says “Peace be with you”","It stores enough water inside its body to last that long"],"notes":"The camel''s real secret is not a water bag but how well its body saves water; the hump stores fat. You can share that as a fun correction."},{"id":"seven-little-sisters-2-4","title":"Lesson 8: The Little Mountain Maiden (part 1)","steps":["Read the first part of “The Little Mountain Maiden,” from the carved chamois through Jeannette listening for her father''s cow bells","Have your child narrate Jeannette''s home and her summer days","Find Switzerland and the Alps on the map","Draw a chamois standing on a rock, or Jeannette picking strawberries"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Little Mountain Maiden” (part 1)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12631/pg12631-images.html#id00153"}],"questions":["What is a chamois?","How did Jeannette''s mother first come up the mountain?","Why do the cows wear little bells?"],"answers":["A small, nimble wild goat-antelope of the high mountains","Riding on a brown donkey led by Jeannette''s father","So her father can hear and find them if they wander too far"],"notes":"This chapter is mostly gentle and still close to life in the Swiss Alps, where farmers still take cows up to high summer pastures."},{"id":"seven-little-sisters-2-5","title":"Lesson 9: The Little Mountain Maiden (part 2)","steps":["Read the rest of “The Little Mountain Maiden,” from the travelers'' visit to the chestnut gathering","Narrate what happened to Joseph and how he spent his time while his leg healed","Copywork: copy a sentence about the chestnut days, or the names Jeannette, Joseph, and Switzerland"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Little Mountain Maiden” (part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12631/pg12631-images.html#id00173"}],"questions":["How was Joseph hurt?","What did he carve while he got better?","What opens the prickly chestnut burrs for the family?"],"answers":["He fell while guiding travelers over the mountain and broke his leg","A spoon and bowl for his sister, then knives, forks, plates, and the little chamois","The autumn frost"]}]},{"id":"seven-little-sisters-u3","title":"Unit 3: Pen-se of China and Manenko of Africa","lessons":[{"id":"seven-little-sisters-3-1","title":"Lesson 10: The Story of Pen-se (part 1)","steps":["Read the first part of “The Story of Pen-se,” from watching the sunset through Lin and his fishing birds","Narrate a morning on Pen-se''s boat, from letting out the ducks to going to work","With a globe and a flashlight, show that when the sun sets for us it is morning on the other side, then find China","Practice picking up dry beans or cereal with two chopsticks"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Story of Pen-se” (part 1)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12631/pg12631-images.html#id00185"}],"questions":["Where does Pen-se''s family live?","How does her father call the ducks home at night?","What birds catch fish for Lin and his uncle?"],"answers":["On a boat on a big river","He blows a shrill whistle","Cormorants"],"notes":"The long braid (queue) that Kang-hy wears was a hairstyle China''s rulers required of men in that era; it ended over a hundred years ago. The author calls these customs “funny” and “odd.” You might talk about how our own clothes and food could look funny to someone far away."},{"id":"seven-little-sisters-3-2","title":"Lesson 11: The Story of Pen-se (part 2)","steps":["Read the rest of “The Story of Pen-se,” from the busy city streets to the end","Narrate the city sights Pen-se remembers and the hills where she was born","Draw a tea plant or a silkworm on a mulberry leaf"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Story of Pen-se” (part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12631/pg12631-images.html#id00203"}],"questions":["Why can''t the lady in the great house run and play?","What grew on the hills where Pen-se was born?","What does the silkworm spin around itself?"],"answers":["Her feet were tightly bound when she was little to keep them small","Tea plants and mulberry trees for silkworms","A silken shell (cocoon)"],"notes":"Foot binding was real and painful; it was outlawed in China in the early 1900s and has long since ended. The menu of “fried mice” and puppies and the remark about “slanting” eyes are an outsider''s stereotypes, though the author does conclude “it is only custom.” China today is a modern country of big cities, high-speed trains, and schools, and families still live and work on its great rivers."},{"id":"seven-little-sisters-3-3","title":"Lesson 12: The Little Dark Girl (part 1)","steps":["Read the first part of “The Little Dark Girl,” from the start through the corn hill built by white ants","Have your child narrate the things Manenko can do to help her family","Find southern Africa and a great river such as the Zambezi on the map"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Little Dark Girl” (part 1)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12631/pg12631-images.html#id00214"}],"questions":["What is the roof of Manenko''s house made of?","Name two jobs Manenko can do.","Who built the hill where the family plants its corn?"],"answers":["River rushes tied with bark and covered with smooth grass","Paddle a canoe, hoe corn, find wild honey, gather fruit, or pound corn (any two)","Termites (white ants)"],"notes":"Please preview this chapter. Written in 1861, it describes African people''s looks in ways that are hurtful, calls Manenko “wild,” and says “we should not agree” with their idea of beauty. A good way through: read the parts about her family, work, and home, and say plainly that the author was wrong to describe people that way. Every culture has its own idea of beauty. Families across Africa today live in cities and villages, run businesses, and go to school."},{"id":"seven-little-sisters-3-4","title":"Lesson 13: The Little Dark Girl (part 2)","steps":["Read the rest of “The Little Dark Girl,” from the mother weaving baskets to the end","Narrate how Manenko followed the honey-bird","Draw a hippopotamus standing on the river bottom with her baby on her neck"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Little Dark Girl” (part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12631/pg12631-images.html#id00236"}],"questions":["What do the other women call Manenko''s mother, and why?","How does the honey-bird help Manenko?","Where does the hippopotamus spend the day?"],"answers":["Ma-Zungo, meaning Zungo''s mother, after her oldest son","Its call leads her to a tree where wild bees keep their honey","Standing under the river water, half asleep"],"notes":"The honey-bird (honeyguide) is real and still leads people to bees'' nests. Elephants were hunted for ivory then; now they are protected, and buying ivory is banned in most places. “Dark Continent” was an old outsiders'' name for Africa that isn''t used today."}]},{"id":"seven-little-sisters-u4","title":"Unit 4: Louise, from the Rhine to the Western Forest","lessons":[{"id":"seven-little-sisters-4-1","title":"Lesson 14: Louise, the Child of the Beautiful River Rhine (part 1)","steps":["Read the first part of “Louise, the Child of the Beautiful River Rhine,” through her knitting, garden, and lessons","Have your child narrate how Louise and her brother and sister helped the poor people in the valley","Trace the River Rhine on a map of Europe, from the Alps to the sea, and notice it starts near Jeannette''s mountains"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Louise of the Rhine” (part 1)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12631/pg12631-images.html#id00247"}],"questions":["What does Louise''s mother send the children to carry each day?","How did Louise help the old woman who was knitting?","Where does Christian go to school?"],"answers":["Hot dinners for the sick and poor people in the valley","She found her dropped needle and gave her the dinner","A music school on the hill"]},{"id":"seven-little-sisters-4-2","title":"Lesson 15: Louise, the Child of the Beautiful River Rhine (part 2)","steps":["Read the rest of “Louise of the Rhine,” from the grape harvest to Christmas Eve","Narrate the grape-picking day or the Christmas surprise","Draw the little Christmas tree with candles, or the peasant girls carrying grapes on their heads"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Louise of the Rhine” (part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12631/pg12631-images.html#id00260"}],"questions":["What grows on the steep hills by the Rhine?","What was the best Christmas present of all?","Why was the Christmas tree set up in Mother''s room?"],"answers":["Grapes, in vineyards","A new baby brother, Hans","The new baby was one of the presents and could not be carried to the hall"],"notes":"The 1860s family gives the children a little new wine at the harvest. You may want to mention that was a custom of the time and place."},{"id":"seven-little-sisters-4-3","title":"Lesson 16: Louise, the Child of the Western Forest (part 1)","steps":["Read the first part of “Louise, the Child of the Western Forest,” from the stormy night through arriving at the edge of the forest","Narrate why the family left their home and what the voyage was like","On the globe, trace their trip from the Rhine across the Atlantic Ocean to North America and inland"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Louise of the Western Forest” (part 1)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12631/pg12631-images.html#id00276"}],"questions":["Why did the family have to leave their house by the Rhine?","What did they bring besides clothes?","What did Louise knit on the ship?"],"answers":["Their father lost all his money","Two big wooden chests of linen sheets and tablecloths","A little stocking for the baby"],"notes":"Many German families did move to the American Midwest in the 1800s. The “western forest” they settled was land where Native American nations had long lived, which the book does not mention; older children may be ready to talk about that."},{"id":"seven-little-sisters-4-4","title":"Lesson 17: Louise, the Child of the Western Forest (part 2)","steps":["Read the rest of “Louise of the Western Forest,” from the night in the wagon through the hard first winter","Narrate how the family made the log house into a home","Build a tiny log cabin with sticks, pretzel rods, or toy logs, and stuff the cracks with moss or paper"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Louise of the Western Forest” (part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12631/pg12631-images.html#id00298"}],"questions":["Where did the children sleep the first night?","How did the linen chests help in the new house?","What gift did the family give each other that first Christmas?"],"answers":["In the back of the big wagon, on straw","They were used as the table and as beds","Love"]},{"id":"seven-little-sisters-4-5","title":"Lesson 18: The Seven Little Sisters","steps":["Read “The Seven Little Sisters” aloud together","Have your child name all seven sisters and tell one thing about each","Make a world map with seven stickers or drawings: the brown baby, Agoonack, Gemila, Jeannette, Pen-se, Manenko, and Louise"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Seven Little Sisters”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12631/pg12631-images.html#id00322"}],"questions":["Who are the seven little sisters, in order?","Why does Marnie say they can''t be sisters?","What does the author say makes them all sisters?"],"answers":["The brown baby, Agoonack, Gemila, Jeannette, Pen-se, Manenko, and Louise","They look different and live in different places, warm and cold","They are all children of the same loving heavenly Father who cares for every one of them"],"notes":"The book ends with a Christian message that all children are one family under God. The cotton paragraph mentions “a poor black man, and a very sad man” planting seeds: in 1861 that meant an enslaved person in the American South, a chance to talk gently about slavery if your child is ready."}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"“The Seven Little Sisters Who Live on the Round Ball That Floats in the Air” by Jane Andrews (1861), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/12631. Lesson plan by Freerange."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (listing_id, version) do update set content = excluded.content;
insert into public.listings (id, seller_name, seller_kind, seller_verified, title, subjects, grade_min, grade_max, philosophies, format, price, description, includes, lessons, lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement, family_style, lesson_steps, cover, color, esa_eligible, created_at, package_id, package_version, license, attribution, outline) values ('l-lib-story-book-of-science', 'Jean-Henri Fabre', 'open', false, 'The Story-Book of Science', array['science']::text[], 3, 6, array['charlotte_mason', 'classical']::text[], 'online', 0, 'The great French naturalist Jean-Henri Fabre tells science as a story: Uncle Paul answers his nieces'' and nephews'' questions about ants, spiders, silk, metals, thunder, volcanoes, the earth and sun, flowers, the sea, and bees. Each sitting is one short chapter read aloud, a narration, and a simple home demonstration or nature-journal entry. It''s a classic 1917 family read-aloud for grades 3–6, and the full text is free online.', array['77 lessons in 12 units covering all 80 chapters', 'Link to the free full text for every chapter', 'Safe home demonstrations and outdoor observations drawn from the book', 'Narration prompts and questions with answers', 'Parent notes where 1917 science or safety advice is out of date']::text[], 77, 3, 30, 'high', true, '{}'::text[], '🐜', '#3D9970', false, '2026-11-11T12:00:00.000Z', 'pk-lib-story-book-of-science', 1, '{"kind":"public_domain"}'::jsonb, '“The Story-Book of Science” by Jean-Henri Fabre (1917), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/56795. Lesson plan by Freerange.', '[{"title":"Unit 1: Ants and Their Cows (Chapters I–VII)","lessons":["Chapters I–II: The Six; The Fairy Tale and the True Story","Chapter III: The Building of the City","Chapter IV: The Cows","Chapter V: The Sheepfold","Chapter VI: The Wily Dervish","Chapter VII: A Numerous Family"]},{"title":"Unit 2: How Long Things Live (Chapters VIII–X)","lessons":["Chapter VIII: The Old Pear-Tree","Chapter IX: The Age of Trees","Chapter X: The Length of Animal Life"]},{"title":"Unit 3: How Things Are Made: Metals, Cloth, Paper, and Books (Chapters XI–XX)","lessons":["Chapters XI–XII: The Kettle; Metals","Chapter XIII: Metal Plating","Chapter XIV: Gold and Iron","Chapter XV: The Fleece","Chapter XVI: Flax and Hemp","Chapter XVII: Cotton","Chapter XVIII: Paper","Chapter XIX: The Book","Chapter XX: Printing"]},{"title":"Unit 4: Caterpillars, Silk, and Metamorphosis (Chapters XXI–XXIV)","lessons":["Chapter XXI: Butterflies","Chapter XXII: The Big Eaters","Chapter XXIII: Silk","Chapter XXIV: The Metamorphosis"]},{"title":"Unit 5: Spiders and Venomous Creatures (Chapters XXV–XXXIII)","lessons":["Chapter XXV: Spiders","Chapter XXVI: The Epeira''s Bridge","Chapter XXVII: The Spider''s Web","Chapter XXVIII: The Chase","Chapter XXIX: Venomous Insects","Chapter XXX: Venom","Chapter XXXI: The Viper and the Scorpion","Chapter XXXII: The Nettle","Chapter XXXIII: Processionary Caterpillars"]},{"title":"Unit 6: Storms, Electricity, Clouds, and Rain (Chapters XXXIV–XLIV)","lessons":["Chapter XXXIV: The Storm","Chapter XXXV: Electricity","Chapters XXXVI–XXXVII: The Experiment with the Cat; The Experiment with Paper","Chapter XXXVIII: Franklin and De Romas","Chapter XXXIX: Thunder and the Lightning-Rod","Chapter XL: Effects of the Thunderbolt","Chapter XLI: Clouds","Chapter XLII: The Velocity of Sound","Chapter XLIII: The Experiment with the Bottle of Cold Water","Chapter XLIV: Rain"]},{"title":"Unit 7: Volcanoes and the Power of Steam (Chapters XLV–L)","lessons":["Chapter XLV: Volcanoes","Chapter XLVI: Catania","Chapter XLVII: The Story of Pliny","Chapter XLVIII: The Boiling Pot","Chapter XLIX: The Locomotive","Chapter L: Emile''s Observation"]},{"title":"Unit 8: The Earth, the Air, and the Sun (Chapters LI–LVI)","lessons":["Chapter LI: A Journey to the End of the World","Chapter LII: The Earth","Chapter LIII: The Atmosphere","Chapter LIV: The Sun","Chapter LV: Day and Night","Chapter LVI: The Year and Its Seasons"]},{"title":"Unit 9: Plants: Poison, Flowers, Fruit, and Mushrooms (Chapters LVII–LXV)","lessons":["Chapter LVII: Belladonna Berries","Chapter LVIII: Poisonous Plants","Chapter LIX: The Blossom","Chapter LX: Fruit","Chapter LXI: Pollen","Chapter LXII: The Bumble-Bee","Chapter LXIII: Mushrooms","Chapter LXIV: In the Woods","Chapter LXV: The Orange-Agaric"]},{"title":"Unit 10: Earthquakes and the Heat Underground (Chapters LXVI–LXIX)","lessons":["Chapter LXVI: Earthquakes","Chapter LXVII: Shall We Kill Them Both?","Chapter LXVIII: The Thermometer","Chapter LXIX: The Subterranean Furnace"]},{"title":"Unit 11: Shells, Pearls, and the Sea (Chapters LXX–LXXV)","lessons":["Chapter LXX: Shells","Chapter LXXI: The Spiral Snail","Chapter LXXII: Mother-of-Pearl and Pearls","Chapter LXXIII: The Sea","Chapter LXXIV: Waves, Salt, Seaweeds","Chapter LXXV: Running Waters"]},{"title":"Unit 12: The Honeybees (Chapters LXXVI–LXXX)","lessons":["Chapter LXXVI: The Swarm","Chapter LXXVII: Wax","Chapter LXXVIII: The Cells","Chapter LXXIX: Honey","Chapter LXXX: The Queen Bee"]}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set seller_name = excluded.seller_name, seller_kind = excluded.seller_kind, seller_verified = excluded.seller_verified, title = excluded.title, subjects = excluded.subjects, grade_min = excluded.grade_min, grade_max = excluded.grade_max, philosophies = excluded.philosophies, format = excluded.format, price = excluded.price, description = excluded.description, includes = excluded.includes, lessons = excluded.lessons, lessons_per_week = excluded.lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson = excluded.minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement = excluded.parent_involvement, family_style = excluded.family_style, lesson_steps = excluded.lesson_steps, cover = excluded.cover, color = excluded.color, esa_eligible = excluded.esa_eligible, package_id = excluded.package_id, package_version = excluded.package_version, license = excluded.license, attribution = excluded.attribution, outline = excluded.outline;
insert into public.package_releases (listing_id, version, content) values ('l-lib-story-book-of-science', 1, '{"units":[{"id":"story-book-of-science-u1","title":"Unit 1: Ants and Their Cows (Chapters I–VII)","lessons":[{"id":"story-book-of-science-1-1","title":"Chapters I–II: The Six; The Fairy Tale and the True Story","steps":["Read Chapters I and II aloud: meet Uncle Paul, Jacques, Mother Ambroisine, Claire, Jules, and Emile","Narration: your child describes each of the three children in their own words","Talk it over: why does Jules want ''true stories,'' and why does Uncle Paul say true stories can be more marvelous than fairy tales?","Nature journal: start a journal with a page titled ''True Stories I Have Seen'' and add one small thing noticed outdoors today"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter I","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch01"},{"label":"Read Chapter II","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch02"}],"questions":["Who are the six?","Which of the children is oldest, and which is the youngest?","What kind of true story does Uncle Paul offer to tell first?"],"answers":["Uncle Paul, Jacques, Mother Ambroisine, Claire, Jules, and Emile","Claire is oldest; Emile is youngest","A true story about real ants"]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-1-2","title":"Chapter III: The Building of the City","steps":["Read Chapter III, \"The Building of the City\", aloud","Narration: your child explains the jobs of the ''miners'' and the ''carpenters''","Observation: find an anthill and watch for five minutes; do you see ants carrying grains of earth out or bits of straw in?","Nature journal: draw the mound and the hole at its top, and sketch the tunnels you imagine underneath"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter III","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch03"}],"questions":["Where does the little mound around an anthill come from?","How did the ants finally get the oat husk down the hole?"],"answers":["It is the earth the miner ants dig out and carry up, grain by grain","They tipped it so one end pointed down, then let it somersault into the hole while holding on"]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-1-3","title":"Chapter IV: The Cows","steps":["Read Chapter IV, \"The Cows\", aloud","Narration: your child tells what the ''ants'' cows'' really are and how the ants ''milk'' them","Observation: look under the leaves of roses, beans, or other garden plants for clusters of tiny aphids (plant-lice) and ants visiting them; use a magnifying glass if you have one","Nature journal: draw an aphid with its two little tubes, and an ant stroking it with its antennae"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter IV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch04"}],"questions":["What are the ants'' ''cows''?","How can you tell an ant going up the elder from one coming down?","How does an ant get a stingy plant-louse to give its drop?"],"answers":["Plant-lice (aphids)","Ants going up have thin, empty stomachs; ants coming down have full, swollen ones","It gently pats and tickles it with its antennae"],"notes":"Today''s science says the sweet drop (honeydew) comes out of the aphid''s rear end, not the two little tubes; the tubes give off a warning smell. Fabre''s picture of ants ''milking'' is still a good one."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-1-4","title":"Chapter V: The Sheepfold","steps":["Read Chapter V, \"The Sheepfold\", aloud","Narration: your child explains how the ants share the ''milk'' with the workers and the young ants back home","Observation: look at the base of potted plants or grass tufts in warm weather for little mud shelters built by ants","Nature journal: draw the life of an ant from egg to little white grub to adult"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter V","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch05"}],"questions":["Why do some ants build a little shelter of earth around a tuft of grass?","How does a milk-carrying ant feed the hungry workers?","What hatches out of an ant egg?"],"answers":["To keep their herd of plant-lice close by, shaded and ready to milk","It passes drops of food from its own stomach, mouth to mouth","A little white grub (larva) with no legs"]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-1-5","title":"Chapter VI: The Wily Dervish","steps":["Read Chapter VI, \"The Wily Dervish\", aloud","Narration: your child retells the story of the dervish, the king, and the chessboard","Demonstration: put 1 grain of rice on the first square of a chessboard (or a grid drawn on paper), 2 on the next, 4, 8, 16... How far can you get before you run out of rice?","Math: write the numbers for the first ten squares and add them up"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter VI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch06"}],"questions":["What did the dervish ask for as his reward?","Why couldn''t the king pay him?","How many grains go on the 10th square?"],"answers":["One grain of wheat on the first square, doubled on each square to the 64th","There wasn''t enough wheat in the whole world to fill all 64 squares","512"]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-1-6","title":"Chapter VII: A Numerous Family","steps":["Read Chapter VII, \"A Numerous Family\", aloud","Narration: your child explains why there are always plant-lice, even though so many creatures eat them","Observation: hunt on rosebushes or garden plants for ladybugs and their alligator-shaped larvae among the aphids","Nature journal: draw a ladybug, a ladybug larva, and a green lacewing (''golden-eyed fly'') and label them ''aphid eaters''"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter VII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch07"}],"questions":["Name three creatures that eat plant-lice.","How many eggs can one cod lay?","Why are weak little creatures like plant-lice so very many?"],"answers":["Any three of: young birds, the lacewing larva (''ants'' lion''), ladybugs","About nine million","So many are eaten that only huge numbers keep the kind alive"],"notes":"Fabre''s ''hemerobius'' is the green lacewing; its larva is often called an aphid lion. Gardeners still welcome ladybugs and lacewings today."}]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-u2","title":"Unit 2: How Long Things Live (Chapters VIII–X)","lessons":[{"id":"story-book-of-science-2-1","title":"Chapter VIII: The Old Pear-Tree","steps":["Read Chapter VIII, \"The Old Pear-Tree\", aloud","Narration: your child explains how you can tell a tree''s age","Observation: find a stump or a cut log (or a slice of firewood) and count its rings with a pin to guide you","Nature journal: draw the rings you counted and write the tree''s age beside it"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter VIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch08"}],"questions":["What are the rings in a tree trunk called, and how often does one form?","How old was the pear-tree?","How old was the big beech by the road?"],"answers":["Annual layers; one forms every year","Forty-five years old","One hundred seventy years old"]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-2-2","title":"Chapter IX: The Age of Trees","steps":["Read Chapter IX, \"The Age of Trees\", aloud","Narration: your child tells about the most amazing tree in the chapter","Map work: find Sicily and Mount Etna on a map, where the ''Chestnut of a Hundred Horses'' grows","Activity: measure around a big tree near you with a string, then compare it with the giant chestnut that took more than thirty people holding hands to circle"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter IX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch09"}],"questions":["Why is the chestnut on Mount Etna called ''the chestnut of a hundred horses''?","Which kind of tree in the chapter lived the longest?"],"answers":["A queen and her hundred horsemen sheltered under it from a storm","The yew (some in Scottish churchyards were thought to be thousands of years old)"],"notes":"Some of the trees Fabre names are still alive, including the Hundred Horse Chestnut. The oldest known trees today are bristlecone pines in California, over 4,800 years old."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-2-3","title":"Chapter X: The Length of Animal Life","steps":["Read Chapter X, \"The Length of Animal Life\", aloud","Narration: your child tells which animals surprised them by living a short or a long time","Activity: make a bar chart of the lifespans Uncle Paul gives (ox, horse, dog, cat, sheep, rabbit, rat, goose, sparrow)","Talk it over: what does Uncle Paul mean when he says ''he lives most who works most''?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter X","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch10"}],"questions":["How long does a horse usually live, by Uncle Paul''s account?","Which lives longer, a turkey or a goldfinch?","Does being big mean an animal lives a long time?"],"answers":["About thirty to thirty-five years","The goldfinch","No; the big ox is old at twenty or thirty, while little songbirds can live as long"],"notes":"Some of Fabre''s ages are generous: wild sparrows and goldfinches usually live only a few years, though caged ones can live much longer."}]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-u3","title":"Unit 3: How Things Are Made: Metals, Cloth, Paper, and Books (Chapters XI–XX)","lessons":[{"id":"story-book-of-science-3-1","title":"Chapters XI–XII: The Kettle; Metals","steps":["Read Chapters XI and XII, \"The Kettle\" and \"Metals\", aloud","Narration: your child explains how copper gets from a mountain to Mother Ambroisine''s kitchen","Demonstration: gather small objects (a copper penny, a steel spoon, aluminum foil, a brass key, a piece of jewelry); sort them by color and feel their weight","Nature journal: list the metals Uncle Paul names with their colors"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch11"},{"label":"Read Chapter XII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch12"}],"questions":["What is ore?","What two metals are melted together to make brass?","Which common metal is the heaviest, and which melts most easily?"],"answers":["Rock from the mine that has metal mixed in it","Copper and zinc","Gold is the heaviest; tin and lead melt most easily"],"notes":"Don''t try melting metal at home. Lead toy soldiers like Emile''s are no longer made because lead is poisonous."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-3-2","title":"Chapter XIII: Metal Plating","steps":["Read Chapter XIII, \"Metal Plating\", aloud","Narration: your child explains why the inside of copper pans was coated with tin","Demonstration: put a steel nail (not galvanized) in a damp paper towel inside a jar and check it each day for rust; set a second nail in a dry jar to compare","Nature journal: draw and color the three ''rusts'' Uncle Paul names: iron, copper, and lead"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch13"}],"questions":["What makes iron rust?","What color is copper rust?","What is tin-plate?"],"answers":["Damp air","Green","Thin sheets of iron coated with tin on both sides"],"notes":"Find something green with ''copper rust'' near you: an old penny, a copper roof, or the Statue of Liberty in pictures."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-3-3","title":"Chapter XIV: Gold and Iron","steps":["Read Chapter XIV, \"Gold and Iron\", aloud","Narration: your child tells why people used gold long before iron, yet iron proved far more useful","Discussion: make a timeline from the Stone Age to the age of copper and on to the age of iron, and draw a tool for each","Nature journal: look around the house and list five things made of iron or steel"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch14"}],"questions":["Why do old gold coins found in the ground still shine?","Why was iron the last metal people learned to use?","Why would an iron ax be worth more than a gold one to a person living in the wild?"],"answers":["Gold never rusts","It is found as a rusty, earthy ore, and it is very hard to get pure iron out of it","Iron is hard enough to chop trees, build, and hunt; gold is soft and would bend"],"notes":"Fabre''s description of early peoples as ''savages'' and ''wretched'' reflects 1917 attitudes; you may want to talk about how much skill Stone Age people really had."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-3-4","title":"Chapter XV: The Fleece","steps":["Read Chapter XV, \"The Fleece\", aloud","Narration: your child traces how wool goes from the sheep to a pair of stockings","Demonstration: pull a cotton ball or bit of wool roving into a thin strand and twist it between your fingers to spin your own little thread","Nature journal: list the gifts animals give us (wool, milk, eggs, leather, work) that Jacques names"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch15"}],"questions":["What happens to wool after it is sheared?","How is wool colored?","Which animal gives us what, according to Jacques? Name two."],"answers":["It is washed, spun into yarn, then knitted or woven","It is dyed in hot water with coloring matter","Any two: the sheep gives wool, milk, meat, and skin; the cow gives milk; the hen gives eggs; the ox gives strength; the dog gives help"]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-3-5","title":"Chapter XVI: Flax and Hemp","steps":["Read Chapter XVI, \"Flax and Hemp\", aloud","Narration: your child lists the steps from flax plant to linen cloth (harvest, retting, brake, heckle, spinning, weaving)","Demonstration: weave a little paper mat: cut slits in one sheet (the warp) and weave strips of another color over and under (the weft)","Nature journal: draw a flax plant with its little blue flowers"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch16"}],"questions":["What part of the flax and hemp plants gives us thread?","What is ''retting''?","What were Egyptian mummies wrapped in?"],"answers":["The long fibers in the bark (outside of the stem)","Letting the stems rot in a field or pond so the fibers come loose from the wood","Linen bands"]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-3-6","title":"Chapter XVII: Cotton","steps":["Read Chapter XVII, \"Cotton\", aloud","Narration: your child tells about all the people who share in the price of a piece of cotton cloth","Observation: in late spring, look for fluffy seeds from cottonwood, poplar, willow, thistle, or dandelion, and blow a dandelion clock","Nature journal: draw a cotton boll and a dandelion seed and write how each travels"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch17"}],"questions":["Where in the cotton plant is the cotton found?","Which bird lines its nest with willow and poplar fluff?","How can a piece of cotton cloth that passed through so many hands be so cheap?"],"answers":["In the bolls (seed pods), around the seeds","The goldfinch","Machines do the spinning and weaving in huge factories very quickly"],"notes":"Fabre''s phrase about who wears the cloth reflects the racial language of his time; consider skipping or discussing it."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-3-7","title":"Chapter XVIII: Paper","steps":["Read Chapter XVIII, \"Paper\", aloud","Narration: your child explains how dirty rags became white paper","Demonstration: tear scrap paper into small bits, soak it in warm water, blend or mash it into pulp, then press it thin on a screen or old towel and let it dry into homemade paper","Talk it over: what does Uncle Paul say about having ''humble beginnings''?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch18"}],"questions":["What was paper made from in Fabre''s time?","Name two steps that turn rags into paper.","What does Uncle Paul say is the only true greatness?"],"answers":["Old rags of cotton, linen, and hemp","Any two: washing, cutting and tearing, grinding into a watery pulp, whitening, spreading on sieves, pressing, drying","Greatness and nobility of the soul"],"notes":"Most paper today is made from wood pulp, though fine paper is still made from cotton."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-3-8","title":"Chapter XIX: The Book","steps":["Read Chapter XIX, \"The Book\", aloud","Narration: your child tells why Jules thought it was so hard to write, and what Uncle Paul said he really needed","Writing: like Jules, your child tells you what they know about how paper is made; then writes it down just as they said it","Copywork: one sentence from your child''s own narration"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch19"}],"questions":["What does Uncle Paul say grammar cannot teach?","How do we get ideas to write about?","Whose questions did Uncle Paul promise to put into his book?"],"answers":["How to write; it only teaches the rules","By study, reading, and talking with people who know more than we do","The children''s (Jules''s and Emile''s)"],"notes":"Fun fact: the book you are reading is the very book Uncle Paul promises to write here."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-3-9","title":"Chapter XX: Printing","steps":["Read Chapter XX, \"Printing\", aloud","Narration: your child explains how a page is printed with movable type","Demonstration: carve a letter into a potato half (backward!) or write a word backward on foam, ink it with paint or a stamp pad, and print it; look at the stamp in a mirror","Map work: find Germany on a map, where Gutenberg lived"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch20"}],"questions":["Why are the metal letters carved backward?","Who invented printing with movable type?","Why were books so rare before printing?"],"answers":["So the print comes out the right way around on the paper","Gutenberg","Each one had to be copied by hand, taking years"],"notes":"Grown-ups should do any cutting with a knife."}]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-u4","title":"Unit 4: Caterpillars, Silk, and Metamorphosis (Chapters XXI–XXIV)","lessons":[{"id":"story-book-of-science-4-1","title":"Chapter XXI: Butterflies","steps":["Read Chapter XXI, \"Butterflies\", aloud","Narration: your child explains what a larva is and what metamorphosis means","Observation: on a sunny day, watch butterflies on flowers; look for the coiled proboscis uncurling into a blossom","Nature journal: draw one butterfly you saw, with its antennae, and the caterpillar it came from if you can find out"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch21"}],"questions":["What does a butterfly drink with, and how?","Are the round ''eyes'' on butterfly wings real eyes?","What is a caterpillar called in the general language of insects?"],"answers":["With its proboscis, a thin tube it uncoils into the flower","No; they are only decorations, and the real eyes are on the head","A larva"],"notes":"Fabre''s ''Argus'' is a little blue butterfly; North American families can watch for spring azures or other blues."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-4-2","title":"Chapter XXII: The Big Eaters","steps":["Read Chapter XXII, \"The Big Eaters\", aloud","Narration: your child tells why larvae must eat so much","Observation: find a caterpillar on a garden plant and watch how fast it eats; check the leaf again tomorrow","Nature journal: make two columns, ''larvae that help us'' (clean-up crews) and ''larvae that harm crops'', and add examples from the chapter"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch22"}],"questions":["Does a butterfly keep growing after it comes out of the chrysalis?","Why does a larva have to ''eat for two''?","How do some larvae help keep the earth clean?"],"answers":["No; it is as big as it will ever be","It eats for itself and stores up material for the wings, legs, and other parts the adult will need","They eat dead animals, rotting matter, and dung, and return it to the soil"]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-4-3","title":"Chapter XXIII: Silk","steps":["Read Chapter XXIII, \"Silk\", aloud","Narration: your child tells how a silkworm spins its cocoon and how people get silk from it","Demonstration: if you have a silk scarf or tie, compare its feel and shine with cotton and wool; or watch a short video of silk being reeled from cocoons","Nature journal: draw a mulberry leaf and a silkworm cocoon"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch23"}],"questions":["What is the only food of the silkworm?","Where does the silk thread come out of the caterpillar?","Why is a hot cocoon put in boiling water at the factory?"],"answers":["Mulberry leaves","From a hole in its lower lip called the spinneret","To dissolve the gum holding the threads together so the silk can be unwound"],"notes":"Some children are sad to learn the moth inside is killed; this is a fair thing to talk about."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-4-4","title":"Chapter XXIV: The Metamorphosis","steps":["Read Chapter XXIV, \"The Metamorphosis\", aloud","Narration: your child names the four stages of an insect''s life","Activity: raise a caterpillar (or order a butterfly kit) and watch it pupate; or search shrubs and fences for a chrysalis","Nature journal: draw the four stages of an insect''s life in a circle"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch24"}],"questions":["What are the four stages of a metamorphosing insect''s life?","What is the difference between a chrysalis and a nymph, as Fabre uses the words?","How does the silkworm moth get out of its tough cocoon, according to Fabre?"],"answers":["Egg, larva, chrysalis or nymph, adult (perfect insect)","They mean the same stage; chrysalis is used for butterflies and moths, nymph for other insects like beetles","It moistens the silk and rubs through it with its hard, faceted eyes"],"notes":"Scientists today say the silkworm moth softens the silk with a special liquid and pushes its way out, rather than filing with its eyes. Also, today we call the beetle stage a ''pupa''; ''nymph'' now means the young of insects like grasshoppers."}]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-u5","title":"Unit 5: Spiders and Venomous Creatures (Chapters XXV–XXXIII)","lessons":[{"id":"story-book-of-science-5-1","title":"Chapter XXV: Spiders","steps":["Read Chapter XXV, \"Spiders\", aloud","Narration: your child tells about Mother Ambroisine''s proverb and what Uncle Paul thinks of it","Observation: find a spider and watch it let itself down on a thread; look for silk on bushes in the morning","Nature journal: draw a spider and count its legs and body parts"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch25"}],"questions":["What do spiders use their webs for?","Where does a spider''s silk come out of its body?","Why should we be glad to have spiders around?"],"answers":["To catch flying insects for food","From the spinnerets at the end of its abdomen","They eat flies, gnats, and mosquitoes"],"notes":"Spiders aren''t insects: they have eight legs and two body parts. A good point to add even though Fabre calls the spider an ''insect''."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-5-2","title":"Chapter XXVI: The Epeira''s Bridge","steps":["Read Chapter XXVI, \"The Epeira''s Bridge\", aloud","Narration: before you reveal the answer, let your child guess how the spider could build a bridge over a stream; then retell how it really does it","Demonstration: tie a long piece of sewing thread to the top of a chair by an open window or in front of a fan and watch the breeze carry the loose end","Nature journal: draw the spider on its branch and the floating thread crossing the water"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch26"}],"questions":["How does the epeira get its first thread across the stream?","What does Uncle Paul say about simple solutions?"],"answers":["It lets out a long thread and waits for the wind to blow the free end into the branches on the other side","Simplicity is a sign of excellence: to simplify is to know"],"notes":"The epeira is an orb-weaver, like the garden spiders found in late summer."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-5-3","title":"Chapter XXVII: The Spider''s Web","steps":["Read Chapter XXVII, \"The Spider''s Web\", aloud","Narration: your child explains the order in which the spider builds its web: cables, frame, radiating lines, spiral","Observation: early on a dewy or misty morning, look for orb webs; mist them lightly with a spray bottle to see the threads","Nature journal: draw an orb web step by step: frame, spokes, then the spiral"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch27"}],"questions":["What are the ''radiating lines''?","Where does the spider rest when it isn''t sitting in the middle of its web?"],"answers":["The threads that go out from the center to the edge like the spokes of a wheel","In a silk funnel-shaped den hidden among leaves nearby"]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-5-4","title":"Chapter XXVIII: The Chase","steps":["Read Chapter XXVIII, \"The Chase\", aloud","Narration: your child tells what happened to the damsel-fly, the bumble-bee, and the fly","Observation: visit a web on a sunny morning and watch quietly; how does the spider know something has landed?","Nature journal: draw the spider wrapping its prey in silk"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch28"}],"questions":["How did the epeira know prey was caught while it was in its den?","Why did it wrap the bumble-bee in silk before biting it?","What really kills the spider''s prey so quickly?"],"answers":["The threads leading to its den shook","The bee was strong and might sting, so the spider tied it up first to be safe","The venom from its fangs"],"notes":"Uncle Paul picks up the spider in his fingers; children should watch spiders, not handle them. A few spiders, such as black widows and recluses, have dangerous bites."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-5-5","title":"Chapter XXIX: Venomous Insects","steps":["Read Chapter XXIX, \"Venomous Insects\", aloud","Narration: your child explains why a bee sting hurts much more than a pinprick","Observation: from a safe distance, watch bees and wasps at flowers; tell bees (fuzzy) from wasps (smooth, narrow waist)","Nature journal: draw a bee and a wasp side by side"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch29"}],"questions":["Can any animal shoot poison at you from far away, as Jacques believed?","What makes a sting so painful: the tiny wound or something else?","Was the green ''poison'' in the crushed caterpillar really poison?"],"answers":["No; Uncle Paul says no animal can harm us with venom from a distance","The drop of venom the sting puts into the wound","No; it was green from the leaves it had eaten"],"notes":"A few animals today are known to spit or spray (spitting cobras, bombardier beetles), but none in Uncle Paul''s garden. Anyone allergic to stings needs a grown-up''s help right away."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-5-6","title":"Chapter XXX: Venom","steps":["Read Chapter XXX, \"Venom\", aloud","Narration: your child tells why venom has to get into the blood to cause harm","Safety talk: go over today''s real snakebite first aid together (see the parent note)","Map work: look up which venomous snakes live where you do, and draw one in your journal so you''d know it"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch30"}],"questions":["Why can venom on unbroken skin do no harm?","What does Uncle Paul say is half the battle in any danger?"],"answers":["It has to mix with the blood through a wound to act","Staying calm and keeping your head: a man master of himself is half-master of danger"],"notes":"IMPORTANT: the snakebite first aid in this 1917 chapter is out of date and can cause harm. Today''s advice: do NOT tie a tight band, cut, suck, or burn the bite. Keep the person calm and still, keep the bitten limb at about heart level, remove rings or tight clothing, and call emergency services right away. Also, never taste or touch venom."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-5-7","title":"Chapter XXXI: The Viper and the Scorpion","steps":["Read Chapter XXXI, \"The Viper and the Scorpion\", aloud","Narration: your child sums up Uncle Paul''s review: which weapon each venomous creature carries and where","Activity: make a chart with four columns (spider, bee/wasp, viper, scorpion) and draw where each one''s venom weapon is","Nature journal: draw a snake''s forked tongue and explain in a caption that it is harmless"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch31"}],"questions":["Is a snake''s darting tongue its sting?","Where are a viper''s venom fangs, and what do they do when not in use?","Which part of a scorpion is venomous: its pincers or its tail?"],"answers":["No; it is a harmless tongue","In the upper jaw; they fold back into a groove in the gum","The sting at the end of its tail"],"notes":"Snakes ''taste'' the air with their tongues to smell their surroundings. Teach children to leave all snakes alone rather than catch them like lame Louis."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-5-8","title":"Chapter XXXII: The Nettle","steps":["Read Chapter XXXII, \"The Nettle\", aloud","Narration: your child explains the difference between ''venomous'' and ''poisonous''","Observation (with care): find stinging nettle with a grown-up and look closely, without touching, at the tiny hairs on its stem and leaves; look for caterpillars feeding on it","Nature journal: draw a nettle leaf and a peacock butterfly (Fabre''s Vanessa Io)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch32"}],"questions":["How does a nettle sting you?","What is the difference between venomous and poisonous?","Why should you be careful with hairy caterpillars?"],"answers":["Its hollow bristles break in the skin and spill a stinging liquid into the tiny wound","Venomous things harm you when they get into the blood through a wound; poisonous things harm you when swallowed","Their hairs can stick in the skin and cause itching or painful swelling"],"notes":"Cool water helps a nettle sting. Fabre says venom can be swallowed safely; please don''t let children test that idea with anything."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-5-9","title":"Chapter XXXIII: Processionary Caterpillars","steps":["Read Chapter XXXIII, \"Processionary Caterpillars\", aloud","Narration: your child explains how the caterpillars find their way home","Demonstration: play ''processionary'' with the family: everyone walks in a line holding a string or yarn, and the leader lays the trail; then cut the string and see how the line ''repairs'' it","Nature journal: draw a line of caterpillars following a silk thread from their tent nest"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch33"}],"questions":["How do pine processionary caterpillars travel?","How do they find their way back to the nest?","What happens if you wipe a finger across their silk road?"],"answers":["In a single line, each one touching the one in front","They lay a silk thread along the road as they go and follow it home","They stop and search until one bold caterpillar crosses and lays new silk, and the rest follow"],"notes":"Processionary caterpillars live in Europe. In North America, look for the silk tents of tent caterpillars or webworms in spring and summer; don''t touch the hairy ones."}]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-u6","title":"Unit 6: Storms, Electricity, Clouds, and Rain (Chapters XXXIV–XLIV)","lessons":[{"id":"story-book-of-science-6-1","title":"Chapter XXXIV: The Storm","steps":["Read Chapter XXXIV, \"The Storm\", aloud","Narration: your child tells the story of Uncle Paul and Jules caught in the storm","Observation: the next time a storm is coming, watch (from indoors) for the signs Uncle Paul noticed: gusts of wind, darkening clouds, dusty smell","Nature journal: draw the storm clouds and the rainbow after the storm"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch34"}],"questions":["What signs told Uncle Paul a storm was coming?","Why did he lead Jules away from the big pine tree?","What did they see after the storm?"],"answers":["Louder crickets and frogs, pestering flies, sudden gusts of wind and dust, reddish mist in the south","A tall tree can be struck by lightning, so it is a dangerous shelter","A rainbow"],"notes":"Lightning safety today: ''When thunder roars, go indoors.'' A building or a hard-topped car is safest; a rock overhang like Uncle Paul''s is not truly safe."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-6-2","title":"Chapter XXXV: Electricity","steps":["Read Chapter XXXV, \"Electricity\", aloud","Narration: your child tells what Emile and Claire once imagined thunder to be, and what is wrong with the idea","Demonstration: rub a balloon or plastic comb on a wool sweater or your hair, then hold it near tiny bits of tissue paper and watch them jump","Nature journal: draw the experiment and label ''static electricity''"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch35"}],"questions":["Is the sky a solid blue vault?","How did Uncle Paul make the sealing-wax pick up paper?","What is the invisible thing that made the paper jump?"],"answers":["No; it is a thick layer of air that looks blue","He rubbed it quickly on his cloth sleeve","Electricity"]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-6-3","title":"Chapters XXXVI–XXXVII: The Experiment with the Cat; The Experiment with Paper","steps":["Read Chapters XXXVI and XXXVII aloud","Narration: your child explains how Uncle Paul made sparks from the cat and from the paper","Demonstration: in a dark room on a dry day, pull a wool or fleece sweater off over your head, or rub a balloon on a fleece blanket and touch it to a metal doorknob; watch and listen for tiny sparks","Nature journal: draw the sparks and write why they are the same thing as lightning, only much smaller"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch36"},{"label":"Read Chapter XXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch37"}],"questions":["Why did Uncle Paul light the stove in summer?","What do bits of paper and feathers do near the electrified strip of paper?","Were the sparks from the cat''s fur fire?"],"answers":["To warm and dry the cat''s fur so its electricity would show","They jump to the strip and away again, back and forth","No; they were electricity"],"notes":"Skip scorching paper over a stove; a balloon or fleece gives the same sparks safely. Static works best on cold, dry days."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-6-4","title":"Chapter XXXVIII: Franklin and De Romas","steps":["Read Chapter XXXVIII, \"Franklin and De Romas\", aloud","Narration: your child tells the story of de Romas''s kite and what it proved","Map work: find Philadelphia, where Franklin flew his kite in 1752, and Nérac and Bordeaux in southwestern France","Nature journal: draw Franklin''s kite with its silk string and key"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch38"}],"questions":["What did the kite experiments prove?","Why did de Romas use a silk cord and a glass handle?","What happened to de Romas when he tried the experiment again in Bordeaux?"],"answers":["That lightning is electricity","Silk and glass don''t let electricity pass easily, so they protected him","A frightened crowd threw stones at him and he had to flee"],"notes":"SAFETY: never fly a kite in a storm or near power lines. These experiments were extremely dangerous; a scientist named Richmann was killed by lightning repeating one in 1753."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-6-5","title":"Chapter XXXIX: Thunder and the Lightning-Rod","steps":["Read Chapter XXXIX, \"Thunder and the Lightning-Rod\", aloud","Narration: your child explains (as Claire did) why lightning likes to strike tall trees","Observation: look around your town for lightning rods on church steeples, barns, or tall buildings","Nature journal: draw a house with a lightning rod and show the path the lightning takes down into the ground"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch39"}],"questions":["What is lightning, according to Uncle Paul?","Why is it dangerous to stand under a tall, lonely tree in a storm?","Who invented the lightning-rod, and how does it work?"],"answers":["A giant spark that jumps between oppositely charged clouds, or between a cloud and the ground","Lightning tends to strike the highest points, and the tree is the tallest thing around","Benjamin Franklin; a pointed metal rod on top of a building carries the lightning safely down into the ground"],"notes":"Uncle Paul says there are no other precautions; today we know there are: go inside a building or hard-topped car, stay away from water and metal fences, and wait 30 minutes after the last thunder. Lightning does make some ozone and helps fix nitrogen for plants, a little like Fabre''s idea that it ''freshens'' the air."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-6-6","title":"Chapter XL: Effects of the Thunderbolt","steps":["Read Chapter XL, \"Effects of the Thunderbolt\", aloud","Narration: your child lists the things a thunderbolt can do","Activity: look at pictures of fulgurites, the glass tubes lightning makes when it melts sand","Safety talk: make a family plan for what to do in a thunderstorm at home, at the park, and at the pool"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XL","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch40"}],"questions":["What does lightning make when it strikes sand?","What kinds of objects does lightning prefer?","How does Uncle Paul say to help someone who has stopped breathing after a lightning strike?"],"answers":["Glass tubes (fulgurites)","Metal objects","The same way as a drowned person: try to start their breathing again"],"notes":"Today: call emergency services, and if the person isn''t breathing, start CPR. It is safe to touch someone struck by lightning; they carry no charge."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-6-7","title":"Chapter XLI: Clouds","steps":["Read Chapter XLI aloud: Uncle Paul tells the children what clouds really are","Narration: your child explains why a cloud that looks like golden cotton from far away is only gloomy fog up close","Go outside and look at the sky; decide together whether you see cirrus, cumulus, stratus, or nimbus clouds","Nature journal: draw today''s sky and label the cloud type and the weather that followed"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch41"}],"questions":["What are clouds made of?","Which clouds are the highest, and what do they look like?","What kind of cloud usually brings steady rain?"],"answers":["Damp vapor, the same as fog","Cirrus clouds, which look like curly wool or thin white threads","Nimbus clouds (dark, uniform gray)"]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-6-8","title":"Chapter XLII: The Velocity of Sound","steps":["Read Chapter XLII aloud: Uncle Paul explains why the thunder comes after the lightning","Narration: your child tells how the family worked out how far away the storm was","Demonstration: stand far across a field or park while someone claps two blocks together or bangs a pot; notice that you see it before you hear it","Practice the storm count: ''one-one-thousand, two-one-thousand...''; every 3 seconds is about 1 kilometer (5 seconds is about 1 mile)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch42"}],"questions":["Why do we see the lightning before we hear the thunder?","How far does sound travel in one second?","Claire counted 12 seconds. How far away was the storm?"],"answers":["Light travels incredibly fast and reaches us almost instantly; sound travels much more slowly","About 340 meters","12 × 340 = 4080 meters (about 4 kilometers)"],"notes":"Claire''s idea that the danger is over once you see the flash is not safe advice. If you can hear thunder, you are close enough to be struck: go indoors and wait 30 minutes after the last thunder."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-6-9","title":"Chapter XLIII: The Experiment with the Bottle of Cold Water","steps":["Read Chapter XLIII aloud: Uncle Paul shows where the water in the air comes from","Narration: your child explains what happens to the water in wet laundry and in the garden after watering","Demonstration: wipe a bottle or glass dry, fill it with ice water, and set it on a dry plate; watch drops form on the outside and collect in the plate","Set a saucer of water in a sunny window and one in a shady spot; check each day which dries first"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch43"}],"questions":["Where do the drops on the outside of the cold bottle come from?","What is evaporation?","What is condensation?"],"answers":["From invisible water vapor in the air, which cools against the bottle","Water turning into invisible vapor in the air","Vapor cooling and turning back into fog or drops of water"]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-6-10","title":"Chapter XLIV: Rain","steps":["Read Chapter XLIV aloud: Uncle Paul explains how rain forms, and the so-called rains of blood and sulphur","Narration: your child tells the true explanation of a ''rain of sulphur''","Demonstration: shake a lily, pine, or other flower over white paper to see its yellow pollen dust","Nature journal: draw the water cycle: sea and puddles, vapor rising, a cloud, and rain falling"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch44"}],"questions":["Why do clouds turn into rain when the vapor rises high?","What was the yellow ''sulphur rain'' really?","Why is it good that rain falls in small drops?"],"answers":["It gets colder higher up, so the vapor condenses into drops","Pollen from blossoming fir-tree forests carried by the wind","Huge columns of water would crush plants and harvests and damage roofs"]}]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-u7","title":"Unit 7: Volcanoes and the Power of Steam (Chapters XLV–L)","lessons":[{"id":"story-book-of-science-7-1","title":"Chapter XLV: Volcanoes","steps":["Read Chapter XLV aloud: Uncle Paul describes an eruption of Vesuvius","Narration: your child tells the eruption in order, from the column of smoke to the river of lava","Map work: find Vesuvius, Etna, and Hecla on a map of Europe, plus one volcano near your own country","Nature journal: draw a volcano cut in half and label the crater, chimney, and lava"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch45"}],"questions":["What is a volcano''s crater?","What is lava?","What warning signs come before an eruption?"],"answers":["The great funnel-shaped hollow at the top","Melted rock that flows out of the volcano","A column of smoke, the earth trembling, and loud rumbling underground"]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-7-2","title":"Chapter XLVI: Catania","steps":["Read Chapter XLVI aloud: Uncle Paul tells how a lava flow from Etna threatened the town of Catania","Narration: your child tells how the brave workmen turned the lava away from the town","Map work: find Etna and Catania on the island of Sicily","Discussion: Uncle Paul says knowledge is a means of helping others; talk about someone your child admires for courage"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch46"}],"questions":["Why did the people of Catania have time to escape the lava?","What happened when the lava reached the sea?","How did the workmen turn the lava stream away from Catania?"],"answers":["Lava is sticky and moves slowly, so people could be warned in time","Huge clouds of hissing steam rose and fell as salty rain, and the lava pushed the shoreline out","They broke through the hardened crust on the side of the lava stream with iron bars so the lava flowed out a new way"],"notes":"This eruption happened in 1669. People still try walls and channels to steer lava today."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-7-3","title":"Chapter XLVII: The Story of Pliny","steps":["Read Chapter XLVII aloud: Uncle Paul tells the eruption of Vesuvius in A.D. 79 as Pliny the Younger described it","Narration: your child tells what happened to Pliny the uncle and what Pliny the nephew did for his mother","Map work: find Vesuvius, Pompeii, and Herculaneum near Naples, Italy","Look at pictures of Pompeii''s uncovered streets together and draw one scene"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch47"}],"questions":["Which two towns were buried by Vesuvius?","What shape did the cloud over Vesuvius first take?","Why did the elder Pliny sail toward the volcano?"],"answers":["Herculaneum and Pompeii","Like a tree trunk rising straight up, then spreading out at the top","To help the people on the coast and to observe the strange cloud"],"notes":"The translation says the Plinys were at ''Messina''; historically the fleet and the nephew were at Misenum, near Naples."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-7-4","title":"Chapter XLVIII: The Boiling Pot","steps":["Read Chapter XLVIII aloud: Uncle Paul explains the power of steam","Narration: your child explains why the corked vial burst","Demonstration (adult only): boil water in an open pot with the lid loosely on and watch steam lift and rattle the lid; keep children at a safe distance from the steam","Nature journal: draw the rattling lid and write one sentence about what pushes it"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch48"}],"questions":["What happens to water when it is heated a long time in an open pot?","Why did the tightly corked vial explode?","What part of every steam engine produces its power?"],"answers":["It turns to vapor (steam) and boils away until the pot is dry","Steam had no way to escape, and its pressure grew until the glass broke","The boiler, a closed pot of boiling water"],"notes":"Uncle Paul forbids heating water in a closed container, and so should you: it can explode and cause burns or blinding. Never seal a heated container."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-7-5","title":"Chapter XLIX: The Locomotive","steps":["Read Chapter XLIX aloud: Uncle Paul explains, with a picture, how a steam locomotive works","Narration: your child repeats Jules''s chain: steam pushes the piston, the piston pushes the crank...","Demonstration: act out the piston: one child pushes a toy car''s wheel forward and back with a straw or pencil ''rod'' to make it turn","Nature journal: draw a locomotive and label boiler, furnace, smoke-stack, cylinder, and wheels"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch49"}],"questions":["What does the stoker do?","Why does the piston move back and forth?","Why can a locomotive pull such heavy loads on rails?"],"answers":["He shovels coal into the furnace to keep the fire hot","Steam enters first on one side, then the other, while the used steam escapes","Smooth iron rails have no ruts or stones, so little energy is wasted"],"notes":"Steam locomotives have mostly been replaced by diesel and electric trains; many heritage railways still run them for visitors."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-7-6","title":"Chapter L: Emile''s Observation","steps":["Read Chapter L aloud: Emile wonders why the trees seemed to run past the train","Narration: your child retells the story of the man who turned the fire around the lark","Observation: on your next car or bus ride, watch whether near things or far things seem to move faster","Discussion: what in the sky seems to move each day, and what is really moving?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter L","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch50"}],"questions":["Why did the trees seem to be moving when Emile rode the train?","What does the story of the lark on the spit teach about the sun and the Earth?","Is it the sun or the Earth that really moves to make day and night?"],"answers":["Because he was moving smoothly and did not feel it, so the things outside seemed to move the other way","That it makes as little sense for the huge sun and stars to circle the small Earth as to turn the whole fire around a little bird","The Earth turns"]}]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-u8","title":"Unit 8: The Earth, the Air, and the Sun (Chapters LI–LVI)","lessons":[{"id":"story-book-of-science-8-1","title":"Chapter LI: A Journey to the End of the World","steps":["Read Chapter LI aloud: Uncle Paul tells of the little boy who set out with his lunch basket to touch the edge of the sky","Narration: your child explains why the boy could never reach the ''end of the world''","Demonstration: let an ant, ladybug, or a toy walk around a ball or orange; it never meets an edge and comes back to where it started","Nature journal: draw the Earth as a ball floating in space with people standing on all sides, feet toward the ground"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch51"}],"questions":["What did the little boy believe the sky was?","What holds the Earth up?","Why doesn''t the Earth fall?"],"answers":["A blue cover or dome resting on the edge of the earth","Nothing; it floats in space without any support, like the moon and sun","Falling means moving toward the Earth, and the Earth can''t fall toward itself; there is no real ''up'' or ''down'' in space"]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-8-2","title":"Chapter LII: The Earth","steps":["Read Chapter LII aloud: Uncle Paul gives proofs that the Earth is round and tells how big it is","Narration: your child explains what a person on shore sees as a ship sails toward them","Demonstration: move a small toy boat over the top of a large ball toward your child''s eye at ball level; the mast appears first, then the hull","Nature journal: copy Uncle Paul''s sketch of the tower hidden by the curve of the Earth"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch52"}],"questions":["When a ship comes in from sea, which part do you see first?","How long would it take a strong walker to go around the Earth at ten leagues a day?","On a ball two meters across, how big would the highest mountain be?"],"answers":["The tops of the masts","About three years (if it were all land)","Like a tiny grain of sand, about a millimeter high"],"notes":"Fabre calls the highest mountain ''Gaurisankar''; today we know it as Mount Everest (8,849 m)."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-8-3","title":"Chapter LIII: The Atmosphere","steps":["Read Chapter LIII aloud: Uncle Paul describes the sea of air we live at the bottom of","Narration: your child tells why air is even more necessary than food or water","Demonstration: wave a sheet of cardboard to feel the air push back; look at faraway hills and notice how blue they look","Discussion: Uncle Paul asks the children always to help the hungry; talk about one way your family could do that"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch53"}],"questions":["Why do distant hills and the sky look blue?","Why does Jules have to stop holding his breath?","How thick is the air compared with the Earth?"],"answers":["A very thick layer of air has a faint blue color","Every living thing needs air all the time; without it he would suffocate","Very thin, like the fuzz on a peach compared with the peach"],"notes":"Do not repeat the breath-holding experiment for long. Uncle Paul mentions a cruel experiment with a bird; you may skip that sentence."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-8-4","title":"Chapter LIV: The Sun","steps":["Read Chapter LIV aloud: the family climbs a hill to watch the sunrise, and Uncle Paul tells how far away and how big the sun is","Narration: your child describes the sunrise the way the children saw it, from the dawn light to the full sun","Get up early one morning to watch a sunrise together; NEVER look straight at the sun","Demonstration: put one grain of rice beside a big pile of rice to picture the Earth beside the sun"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch54"}],"questions":["Why does the sun look so small?","How long does it take sunlight to reach the Earth?","What are the stars?"],"answers":["Because it is very, very far away","About eight minutes","Suns like ours, but much farther away"],"notes":"Fabre''s figures are close to modern ones: about 150 million km to the sun, and the sun''s volume is about 1.3 million Earths."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-8-5","title":"Chapter LV: Day and Night","steps":["Read Chapter LV aloud: Uncle Paul explains that the Earth turns, making day and night","Narration: your child explains why we don''t feel upside-down at night","Demonstration: in a dark room, set a lamp on a table as the sun; your child slowly spins while walking around it, noticing when their face is ''in day'' and ''in night''","Spin a top and notice it can spin in place and also travel in a circle, like the Earth"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch55"}],"questions":["How long does the Earth take to turn once on its axis?","How long does it take to go around the sun?","Why can''t you see the Earth turning from a balloon?"],"answers":["Twenty-four hours (one day)","One year","The air turns with the Earth and carries the balloon along"],"notes":"The book describes some peoples with outdated, unkind words; you may want to rephrase that passage as you read."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-8-6","title":"Chapter LVI: The Year and Its Seasons","steps":["Read Chapter LVI aloud: Uncle Paul explains the year, the months, leap years, and the seasons","Narration: your child describes what winter is like in the lands near the North Pole","Demonstration: use the knuckle calendar: make a fist and count months on the knuckles and the hollows between them to find which months have 31 days","Nature journal: write today''s date and season, and note sunrise and sunset times; compare again in three months"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch56"}],"questions":["Using the knuckle trick, how many days does September have?","Why does February get a 29th day every fourth year?","On which days are day and night about 12 hours each?"],"answers":["30","The Earth takes almost six hours more than 365 days to go around the sun; four of those extra quarter-days add up to one day","Around March 20 and September 22"],"notes":"The translator notes that this was written before anyone reached the North Pole (Peary in 1909; Amundsen reached the South Pole in 1911). The real cause of the seasons, which Fabre skips, is the tilt of Earth''s axis."}]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-u9","title":"Unit 9: Plants: Poison, Flowers, Fruit, and Mushrooms (Chapters LVII–LXV)","lessons":[{"id":"story-book-of-science-9-1","title":"Chapter LVII: Belladonna Berries","steps":["Read Chapter LVII aloud: the sad story of Joseph and Louis and the berries they mistook for cherries","Narration: your child tells what the boys did wrong and what they should have done","Safety rule: make a family rule together: never eat any wild berry, leaf, or mushroom unless a grown-up who knows plants says it is safe","Nature journal: write the rule at the top of a page and decorate it"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch57"}],"questions":["What did Joseph and Louis think the black berries were?","What was the plant really called?","What should you do if you find berries outdoors?"],"answers":["Cherries","Belladonna (deadly nightshade)","Never eat them; ask a grown-up who knows plants"],"notes":"This chapter ends with a child''s death and may upset sensitive children; preview it first. Belladonna is rare in North America, but many other wild berries are poisonous. Save your local Poison Control number (in the US, 1-800-222-1222)."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-9-2","title":"Chapter LVIII: Poisonous Plants","steps":["Read Chapter LVIII aloud: Uncle Paul teaches the villagers to know the poisonous plants","Narration: your child names three poisonous plants and how to recognize each","Look at photographs (not real plants) of foxglove, hemlock, aconite (monkshood), and arum; draw one in your journal and label its danger signs","Walk around your yard or a park with a grown-up and look for any plant from the chapter; look, but don''t touch or pick"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch58"}],"questions":["How can you tell poison hemlock from parsley?","Which beautiful garden plant can slow down and stop the heart?","What happened to the boy who bit the arum flower?"],"answers":["Hemlock has a bad, nauseating smell when crushed (but the safest rule is never to pick and eat wild plants)","Foxglove (digitalis)","His tongue burned as if he had bitten a hot coal"],"notes":"Never let children smell or crush poisonous plants to test them, as the men do in the story; hemlock can even harm through the skin of some people. Learn from pictures instead."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-9-3","title":"Chapter LIX: The Blossom","steps":["Read Chapter LIX aloud: under the elder tree, Uncle Paul shows the parts of a flower","Narration: your child explains the difference between the calyx and the corolla","Demonstration: gather a few garden flowers; count the petals and sepals and see if the number five (or three, in lilies and tulips) appears","Nature journal: draw one flower and label the sepals, calyx, petals, and corolla"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch59"}],"questions":["What is the calyx made of?","How can you tell that a snapdragon''s one-piece corolla is really five petals?","Do oak and wheat plants have blossoms?"],"answers":["Small green leaves called sepals","Its lips are split into two and three parts, showing five petals joined together","Yes; every plant blooms, but some flowers are tiny and have no colored corolla"]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-9-4","title":"Chapter LX: Fruit","steps":["Read Chapter LX aloud: Uncle Paul takes apart a flower to show the stamens and pistil","Narration: your child repeats the six new words: stamen, anther, pollen, pistil, stigma, ovary","Demonstration: with a grown-up, cut a large flower (lily or tulip) in half lengthwise and find the ovary with its tiny seeds; tap an anther over paper to see pollen","Cut an apple across its middle and find the star-shaped seed chambers, which were once the flower''s ovary"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch60"}],"questions":["Which part of a flower becomes the fruit?","What is the yellow dust in the anthers called?","What does a flower of wheat become?"],"answers":["The ovary","Pollen","A grain of wheat"]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-9-5","title":"Chapter LXI: Pollen","steps":["Read Chapter LXI aloud: Uncle Paul explains why a flower needs pollen to make fruit","Narration: your child tells how the Arab farmers make sure their date palms bear fruit","If you have squash, pumpkin, or zucchini plants, find a flower with a tiny fruit (swelling) under it and one without; these are the pistil and stamen flowers","Nature journal: draw both kinds of squash flower side by side"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch61"}],"questions":["What must land on the stigma before the ovary can grow into fruit?","Why will a lone date palm with pistils never bear dates?","How can you tell a pumpkin flower with a pistil from one with only stamens?"],"answers":["Pollen","No pollen reaches its flowers unless a tree with stamens is nearby","The pistil flower has a little swelling (the baby pumpkin) under the petals"],"notes":"Fabre calls pollination a mystery; today we know a pollen grain grows a tiny tube down to the ovary and fertilizes the seeds."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-9-6","title":"Chapter LXII: The Bumble-Bee","steps":["Read Chapter LXII aloud: the children''s pumpkin experiment, and how insects carry pollen","Narration: your child explains the snapdragon''s yellow ''signboard''","Demonstration: gently squeeze the sides of a snapdragon flower to make it ''open its mouth''","Observation: watch a flowering bush for ten minutes; count the bees and other insects that visit and look for pollen on their bodies (watch quietly; don''t touch)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch62"}],"questions":["What happened to the pumpkin flowers that got no pollen?","What sweet drop draws insects into flowers?","Why does the vallisneria flower break off and float to the surface?"],"answers":["They dried up and made no pumpkins","Nectar","So that it can release its pollen in the air, where it can reach the pistil flowers on the surface"]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-9-7","title":"Chapter LXIII: Mushrooms","steps":["Read Chapter LXIII aloud: Uncle Paul warns the villagers about mushrooms","Narration: your child tells which ''tests'' for poisonous mushrooms Uncle Paul shows are false","Safety rule: add to your journal rule page: ''We never pick or eat wild mushrooms''","Look at pictures of a few deadly mushrooms (such as the death cap) beside look-alike edible ones and see how similar they are"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch63"}],"questions":["Can you tell a poisonous mushroom by its color, smell, or the tree it grows under?","Is it true that worms won''t eat poisonous mushrooms?","Does a silver coin in the pot show whether mushrooms are poisonous?"],"answers":["No","No; worms eat poisonous mushrooms too","No; that is a false old saying"],"notes":"Important: Uncle Paul''s idea that boiling mushrooms in salted water removes their poison is WRONG and dangerous. The deadliest mushroom poisons are not destroyed by boiling. Never eat a wild mushroom unless an expert has identified it."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-9-8","title":"Chapter LXIV: In the Woods","steps":["Read Chapter LXIV aloud: in a beech wood, Uncle Paul explains what a mushroom really is","Narration: your child explains how the hidden mycelium is like a buried rosebush","Nature walk: look for mushrooms in a park or woods after rain; look underneath without picking to see gills, holes, or spikes (use a mirror, or wear gloves and wash hands after)","Nature journal: draw a mushroom with its cap, stalk, and the white threads of mycelium underground"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch64"}],"questions":["What is the mycelium?","Why do mushrooms sometimes grow in a circle?","What are mushroom seeds called?"],"answers":["The real mushroom plant: white, thread-like strands that grow underground","The mycelium spreads out evenly in all directions from the center","Spores"],"notes":"Today scientists call mushrooms the fruiting bodies of fungi, and fungi are not plants at all but a kingdom of their own."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-9-9","title":"Chapter LXV: The Orange-Agaric","steps":["Read Chapter LXV aloud: Uncle Paul shows the spore print and two look-alike orange mushrooms","Narration: your child tells the differences between the good and the deadly orange-agaric","Demonstration: buy a store mushroom with open gills, cut off the stem, and set the cap gill-side down on paper under a bowl overnight to make a spore print","Nature journal: glue in or draw the spore print and label it"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch65"}],"questions":["How can the edible orange-agaric be told from the false one?","How is the store mushroom (the hotbed mushroom) grown?","Which animal helps find truffles?"],"answers":["The edible one has yellow gills; the false one has white gills and white shreds on its cap","From mushroom ''spawn'' (mycelium) planted in beds of manure and soil","The pig"],"notes":"The ''false orange-agaric'' is the fly agaric (Amanita muscaria). Its edible look-alike, Caesar''s mushroom, is also an Amanita; the two are easy to confuse, which is the whole point of the chapter."}]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-u10","title":"Unit 10: Earthquakes and the Heat Underground (Chapters LXVI–LXIX)","lessons":[{"id":"story-book-of-science-10-1","title":"Chapter LXVI: Earthquakes","steps":["Read Chapter LXVI aloud: after a small earthquake in the village, Uncle Paul tells of great earthquakes in Lisbon and southern Italy","Narration: your child tells what the neighbors noticed during the night''s small earthquake","Map work: find Lisbon, Portugal, and Calabria in southern Italy","Safety talk: practice ''Drop, Cover, and Hold On'' under a sturdy table"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch66"}],"questions":["What did Mother Ambroisine notice during the earthquake?","What often comes just before an earthquake, according to Uncle Paul?","Which city was destroyed by the earthquake on All Saints'' Day?"],"answers":["Dishes rattling and falling, and her bed shaking twice","A deep rumbling noise underground","Lisbon, Portugal"],"notes":"The Lisbon earthquake was in 1755 (the translation says 1775). Uncle Paul''s harsh words about the Calabrian peasants are softened by the next chapter''s story."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-10-2","title":"Chapter LXVII: Shall We Kill Them Both?","steps":["Read Chapter LXVII aloud: Uncle Paul reads a French soldier''s letter about a frightening night in a charcoal-burner''s house","Narration: your child retells the story, saving the surprise ending for last","Discussion: why did the soldier misjudge the family? Talk about judging people too quickly","Copywork: copy Uncle Paul''s closing idea in your own words: every country has its good and its bad people"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch67"}],"questions":["What mistake did the young companion make at supper?","What did the charcoal-burner really mean by ''Shall we kill them both?''","What lesson does Uncle Paul want the children to learn?"],"answers":["He told the strangers all about who they were, where they were going, and his valuable bag","Whether to kill both capons (chickens) for the guests'' breakfast","That every country has good and bad people, and we shouldn''t judge a whole people by a few"],"notes":"This is a story, not a science lesson; it may give younger children a moment of fright before the happy ending."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-10-3","title":"Chapter LXVIII: The Thermometer","steps":["Read Chapter LXVIII aloud: Uncle Paul explains the thermometer and degrees","Narration: your child explains why the steps on a thermometer are called ''degrees''","Demonstration: use a kitchen or outdoor thermometer to measure ice water, tap water, and the air outside; hold the bulb in your hand and watch it rise","Nature journal: start a week-long temperature chart, reading the outdoor thermometer at the same time each day"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch68"}],"questions":["At what Celsius temperature does water freeze, and at what does it boil?","What is the normal temperature of the human body?","Why did the red liquid rise when Emile touched the bulb?"],"answers":["0 degrees and 100 degrees","About 37–38 degrees Celsius","The warmth of his hand heated it"],"notes":"Fabre uses the Celsius (centigrade) scale. In Fahrenheit, water freezes at 32° and boils at 212°."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-10-4","title":"Chapter LXIX: The Subterranean Furnace","steps":["Read Chapter LXIX aloud: Uncle Paul explains that the deeper you go into the earth, the hotter it gets","Narration: your child describes an eruption of the Great Geyser of Iceland","Demonstration: draw the Earth as a hard-boiled egg cut in half: a thin shell (crust) around the inside","Look at pictures or a video of Old Faithful in Yellowstone or the Great Geysir in Iceland"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch69"}],"questions":["How much warmer does it get for every 30 meters you go down?","What is a geyser?","What did Fabre believe caused earthquakes?"],"answers":["About one degree","A hot spring that shoots boiling water and steam high into the air","Movement of a melted interior under the Earth''s thin solid crust"],"notes":"Outdated science: we now know the Earth''s mantle is mostly solid rock that flows very slowly, the crust is broken into moving plates, and most earthquakes happen where plates grind past each other. Volcanoes are not ''safety valves'' that prevent earthquakes."}]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-u11","title":"Unit 11: Shells, Pearls, and the Sea (Chapters LXX–LXXV)","lessons":[{"id":"story-book-of-science-11-1","title":"Chapter LXX: Shells","steps":["Read Chapter LXX aloud: Uncle Paul opens his drawer of shells from around the world","Narration: your child explains the difference between a snail and a slug","Hold a large shell or a cupped hand to your ear and listen to the ''sea''; then talk about Uncle Paul''s real explanation","Nature walk: look for snail shells in a garden or park (or shells at a beach) and sort them into sea, freshwater, and land shells"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch70"}],"questions":["What kind of animal makes a shell?","Does a slug ever grow a shell?","Is the roar in a seashell really the sound of the sea?"],"answers":["A mollusk, such as a snail","No; a slug is always a slug","No; it is the sound of air moving in the curled hollow of the shell"],"notes":"Today we''d say the roar is surrounding sounds echoing inside the shell. The book''s description of where some shells came from uses an outdated word for Africa; you may rephrase it."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-11-2","title":"Chapter LXXI: The Spiral Snail","steps":["Read Chapter LXXI aloud: Uncle Paul explains that a snail''s ''horns'' are really tentacles","Narration: your child lists all the jobs the snail''s tentacles do","Observation: find a garden snail after rain, set it on a clear plate or a wet leaf, and watch its tentacles stretch and pull in when you bring a leaf near (handle gently and return it where you found it)","Nature journal: draw the snail and label the long tentacles (eyes and nose) and the short ones (feelers)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch71"}],"questions":["What are a snail''s ''horns'' really called?","Where are the snail''s eyes?","What does a snail do when light or a smell bothers it?"],"answers":["Tentacles","At the tips of the long upper tentacles","It pulls its tentacles back inside, like the finger of a glove"],"notes":"Emile admits to putting a snail on hot coals, and Uncle Paul tells him to stop; a good moment to talk about kindness to small animals. Wash hands after handling snails."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-11-3","title":"Chapter LXXII: Mother-of-Pearl and Pearls","steps":["Read Chapter LXXII aloud: Uncle Paul explains mother-of-pearl and how pearls are made","Narration: your child tells how an oyster makes a pearl","Observation: look at the inside of a mussel or oyster shell (from a beach or a seafood meal) and tilt it in the light to see the rainbow colors","Nature journal: draw how a pearl grows in layers around a tiny grain"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch72"}],"questions":["Where does mother-of-pearl come from?","What makes the pearl oyster start to make a pearl?","Why were pearls so costly, according to Uncle Paul?"],"answers":["The shiny inner layer of certain shells","Something tiny gets inside and irritates it, and it coats it with mother-of-pearl","Divers risked their lives holding their breath deep underwater, sometimes meeting sharks"],"notes":"Today most pearls are cultured: farmers place a tiny bead in the oyster to start the pearl."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-11-4","title":"Chapter LXXIII: The Sea","steps":["Read Chapter LXXIII aloud: Uncle Paul describes the size, depth, and color of the sea","Narration: your child describes what you see from a ship in the middle of the ocean","Demonstration: color a world map or globe; notice that about three-quarters of the Earth is ocean","Demonstration: fill a clear glass and a deep white bucket with water; does the deep water look more blue-green?"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch73"}],"questions":["How much of the Earth''s surface is covered by sea?","How do sailors measure how deep the sea is (in Fabre''s time)?","Is the sea bottom flat?"],"answers":["About three-quarters","They lower a weight (plummet) on a long line until it touches bottom","No; it has deep chasms, mountains, and plains like the land"],"notes":"Modern figures differ: the average ocean depth is about 3,700 meters, and the deepest spot, the Mariana Trench in the Pacific, is about 11,000 meters. Today depth is measured by sonar."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-11-5","title":"Chapter LXXIV: Waves, Salt, Seaweeds","steps":["Read Chapter LXXIV aloud: Uncle Paul explains waves, sea salt, and seaweeds","Narration: your child explains how all the creatures of the sea are fed by sea plants","Demonstration: stir salt into warm water, pour a little into a dark plate, and set it in the sun for a few days; the water evaporates and salt crystals remain, like a tiny salt marsh","Blow across a pan of water to make waves and notice that the water underneath stays calm"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch74"}],"questions":["What makes waves?","How is salt collected from the sea?","How are seaweeds different from land plants?"],"answers":["The wind blowing on the surface of the sea","Sea water is let into shallow basins, the sun evaporates the water, and the salt is raked up","They have no flowers, leaves, or roots, and they hold onto rocks instead of feeding from soil"],"notes":"Never drink sea water, even when very thirsty; it makes thirst worse."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-11-6","title":"Chapter LXXV: Running Waters","steps":["Read Chapter LXXV aloud: Uncle Paul explains why the sea never overflows","Narration: your child traces one drop of water from the sea, to a cloud, to rain, to a brook, and back to the sea","Map work: trace a river near you on a map all the way to the sea","Nature journal: draw the full water cycle with arrows, adding the salt that stays behind in the sea"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch75"}],"questions":["Why doesn''t the sea overflow, even though all rivers pour into it?","If rivers come from the sea, why isn''t brook water salty?","Where does the water in a dewdrop come from and go to?"],"answers":["It loses as much water to evaporation as the rivers bring in","When sea water evaporates the salt stays behind, so rain and snow are fresh","From the sea, and back to the sea"]}]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-u12","title":"Unit 12: The Honeybees (Chapters LXXVI–LXXX)","lessons":[{"id":"story-book-of-science-12-1","title":"Chapter LXXVI: The Swarm","steps":["Read Chapter LXXVI aloud: a swarm of bees leaves the old hive and settles on the currant bush","Narration: your child tells how the bees hung together in a bunch and how Jacques gave them a new home","Look at photos or a video of a bee swarm hanging from a branch","Nature journal: draw the swarm on the currant bush and the new hive Jacques built"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch76"}],"questions":["Why did the swarm leave the old hive?","Who leads the swarm?","Did banging on pots really make the bees land?"],"answers":["The hive was too crowded, so some bees left to start a new colony","The queen","Uncle Paul doesn''t think so; bees land where they choose, not because of the noise"],"notes":"If you ever see a swarm, keep your distance and call a local beekeeper, who will often collect it for free. Never disturb bees."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-12-2","title":"Chapter LXXVII: Wax","steps":["Read Chapter LXXVII aloud: Uncle Paul describes the queen, the drones, and the workers, and how bees make wax","Narration: your child explains the jobs of the three kinds of bees","Demonstration: look closely at a beeswax candle or a piece of beeswax; smell it and warm a small piece in your hands to feel it soften","Nature journal: draw a queen, a drone, and a worker side by side and label how they differ"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch77"}],"questions":["How many queens live in one hive?","Where does a bee get its wax?","What does the word ''insect'' mean?"],"answers":["One","It makes the wax itself; it oozes from folds on the underside of its abdomen","Cut into pieces (its body is made of segments)"],"notes":"Fabre has the order of jobs backwards: young worker bees stay inside as nurses and wax-builders, and older bees become foragers that fly out to the flowers."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-12-3","title":"Chapter LXXVIII: The Cells","steps":["Read Chapter LXXVIII aloud: Uncle Paul explains why honeycomb cells have six sides","Narration: your child explains why round cells and square cells would not be as good","Demonstration: draw circles touching each other and notice the gaps; then try fitting paper triangles, squares, and hexagons together with no gaps","Look at a piece of honeycomb (from a store or farmers'' market) and count the sides of a cell"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch78"}],"questions":["What shape are honeycomb cells?","Why don''t bees make round cells?","Which shapes fit together with no space left over?"],"answers":["Six-sided (hexagons)","Circles leave empty gaps between them, wasting space and wax","Triangles, squares, and hexagons"]},{"id":"story-book-of-science-12-4","title":"Chapter LXXIX: Honey","steps":["Read Chapter LXXIX aloud: Uncle Paul explains how bees gather nectar and pollen and make honey","Narration: your child follows one bee from the flowers back to the hive and tells what it does with its load","Observation: on a sunny day, watch bees on flowers from a safe distance and look for yellow pollen balls on their hind legs","Taste test: compare two kinds of honey (such as clover and wildflower) and talk about how flowers change the flavor"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch79"}],"questions":["What are the yellow balls on a bee''s hind legs?","Do bees make honey for people?","How long does a young bee stay a larva before it seals itself in?"],"answers":["Pollen loads packed in the ''baskets'' on its legs","No; they make it for themselves and their young; we take some of it","About six days"],"notes":"Never give honey to a baby under one year old."},{"id":"story-book-of-science-12-5","title":"Chapter LXXX: The Queen Bee","steps":["Read Chapter LXXX aloud: Uncle Paul tells how bees raise a new queen and what happens when there are rivals","Narration: your child explains how a plain egg can become a queen","Nature journal: draw a queen cell shaped like a thimble beside ordinary six-sided cells","Celebrate the end of the book: let each child choose a favorite of Uncle Paul''s stories and retell it to the family"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/56795/pg56795-images.html#ch80"}],"questions":["What decides whether an egg becomes a queen or a worker?","What do the bees do when the old queen dies?","Why does Uncle Paul stop telling stories?"],"answers":["The food the larva is given; a queen larva gets the special ''royal'' food","They choose a young larva, enlarge its cell, and feed it royal food to make a new queen","The grain is ripe and he must work on the harvest"],"notes":"Today we call the special food royal jelly. Fabre describes the hive as a kingdom with ''civil war''; real bees are not making choices like people, which you may mention as you read."}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"“The Story-Book of Science” by Jean-Henri Fabre (1917), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/56795. Lesson plan by Freerange."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (listing_id, version) do update set content = excluded.content;
insert into public.listings (id, seller_name, seller_kind, seller_verified, title, subjects, grade_min, grade_max, philosophies, format, price, description, includes, lessons, lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement, family_style, lesson_steps, cover, color, esa_eligible, created_at, package_id, package_version, license, attribution, outline) values ('l-lib-story-of-the-greeks', 'H. A. Guerber', 'open', false, 'The Story of the Greeks', array['history']::text[], 3, 6, array['classical', 'charlotte_mason']::text[], 'online', 0, 'H. A. Guerber''s classic 1896 read-aloud tells the story of ancient Greece in short, lively chapters, from the old myths and the Trojan War through Marathon, Pericles, Socrates, and Alexander to the Roman conquest. It suits the whole family in grades 3–6: each day you read one chapter aloud, your child narrates it back, finds the place on a map, and adds it to a timeline. The full text is free online.', array['115 lessons in 15 units, one chapter per lesson', 'Link to the free full text for every chapter', 'Narration prompt, map work, and timeline entry for each lesson', 'Comprehension questions with answers', 'Parent notes flagging myth told as history, dated viewpoints, and mature content']::text[], 115, 4, 20, 'high', true, '{}'::text[], '🏛️', '#E3A33B', false, '2026-11-11T12:00:00.000Z', 'pk-lib-story-of-the-greeks', 1, '{"kind":"public_domain"}'::jsonb, '“The Story of the Greeks” by H. A. Guerber (1896), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/23495. Lesson plan by Freerange.', '[{"title":"Unit 1: Myths and Heroes (Chapters 1–12)","lessons":["Chapter 1: Early Inhabitants of Greece","Chapter 2: The Deluge of Ogyges","Chapter 3: The Founding of Many Important Cities","Chapter 4: Story of Deucalion","Chapter 5: Story of Dædalus and Icarus","Chapter 6: The Adventures of Jason","Chapter 7: Theseus Visits the Labyrinth","Chapter 8: The Terrible Prophecy","Chapter 9: The Sphinx''s Riddle","Chapter 10: Blindness and Death of Œdipus","Chapter 11: The Brothers'' Quarrel","Chapter 12: The Taking of Thebes"]},{"title":"Unit 2: Troy, Homer, and the End of the Heroic Age (Chapters 13–20)","lessons":["Chapter 13: The Childhood of Paris","Chapter 14: The Muster of the Troops","Chapter 15: The Sacrifice of Iphigenia","Chapter 16: The Wrath of Achilles","Chapter 17: Death of Hector and Achilles","Chapter 18: The Burning of Troy","Chapter 19: Heroic Death of Codrus","Chapter 20: The Blind Poet"]},{"title":"Unit 3: Sparta and the Laws of Lycurgus (Chapters 21–28)","lessons":["Chapter 21: The Rise of Sparta","Chapter 22: The Spartan Training","Chapter 23: The Brave Spartan Boy","Chapter 24: Public Tables in Sparta","Chapter 25: Laws of Lycurgus","Chapter 26: The Messenian War","Chapter 27: The Music of Tyrtæus","Chapter 28: Aristomenes'' Escape"]},{"title":"Unit 4: The Olympic Games and the Lawgivers of Athens (Chapters 29–35)","lessons":["Chapter 29: The Olympic Games","Chapter 30: Milo of Croton","Chapter 31: The Jealous Athlete","Chapter 32: The Girls'' Games","Chapter 33: The Bloody Laws of Draco","Chapter 34: The Laws of Solon","Chapter 35: The First Plays"]},{"title":"Unit 5: Tyrants of Athens and the Great King (Chapters 36–41)","lessons":["Chapter 36: The Tyrant Pisistratus","Chapter 37: The Tyrant''s Insult","Chapter 38: Death of the Conspirators","Chapter 39: Hippias Driven Out of Athens","Chapter 40: The Great King","Chapter 41: Hippias Visits Darius"]},{"title":"Unit 6: The Persian Wars (Chapters 42–53)","lessons":["Chapter 42: Destruction of the Persian Host","Chapter 43: The Advance of the Second Host","Chapter 44: The Battle of Marathon","Chapter 45: Miltiades'' Disgrace","Chapter 46: Aristides the Just","Chapter 47: Two Noble Spartan Youths","Chapter 48: The Great Army","Chapter 49: Preparations for Defense","Chapter 50: Leonidas at Thermopylæ","Chapter 51: Death of Leonidas","Chapter 52: The Burning of Athens","Chapter 53: The Battles of Salamis and Platæa"]},{"title":"Unit 7: The Golden Age of Athens (Chapters 54–59)","lessons":["Chapter 54: The Rebuilding of Athens","Chapter 55: Death of Pausanias","Chapter 56: Cimon Improves Athens","Chapter 57: The Earthquake","Chapter 58: The Age of Pericles","Chapter 59: The Teachings of Anaxagoras"]},{"title":"Unit 8: The Peloponnesian War (Chapters 60–68)","lessons":["Chapter 60: Beginning of the Peloponnesian War","Chapter 61: Death of Pericles","Chapter 62: The Philosopher Socrates","Chapter 63: Socrates'' Favorite Pupil","Chapter 64: Youth of Alcibiades","Chapter 65: Greek Colonies in Italy","Chapter 66: Alcibiades in Disgrace","Chapter 67: Death of Alcibiades","Chapter 68: The Overthrow of the Thirty Tyrants"]},{"title":"Unit 9: Socrates, Xenophon, and Agesilaus (Chapters 69–75)","lessons":["Chapter 69: Accusation of Socrates","Chapter 70: Death of Socrates","Chapter 71: The Defeat of Cyrus","Chapter 72: The Retreat of the Ten Thousand","Chapter 73: Agesilaus in Asia","Chapter 74: A Strange Interview","Chapter 75: The Peace of Antalcidas"]},{"title":"Unit 10: The Rise of Thebes (Chapters 76–80)","lessons":["Chapter 76: The Theban Friends","Chapter 77: Thebes Free Once More","Chapter 78: The Battle of Leuctra","Chapter 79: Death of Pelopidas","Chapter 80: The Battle of Mantinea"]},{"title":"Unit 11: The Tyrants of Syracuse (Chapters 81–86)","lessons":["Chapter 81: The Tyrant of Syracuse","Chapter 82: Story of Damon and Pythias","Chapter 83: The Sword of Damocles","Chapter 84: Dion and Dionysius","Chapter 85: Civil War in Syracuse","Chapter 86: Death of Dion"]},{"title":"Unit 12: Philip of Macedon and Young Alexander (Chapters 87–94)","lessons":["Chapter 87: Philip of Macedon","Chapter 88: Philip Begins His Conquests","Chapter 89: The Orator Demosthenes","Chapter 90: Philip Masters Greece","Chapter 91: Birth of Alexander","Chapter 92: The Steed Bucephalus","Chapter 93: Alexander as King","Chapter 94: Alexander and Diogenes"]},{"title":"Unit 13: Alexander the Great (Chapters 95–103)","lessons":["Chapter 95: Alexander''s Brilliant Beginning","Chapter 96: The Gordian Knot","Chapter 97: Alexander''s Royal Captives","Chapter 98: Alexander at Jerusalem","Chapter 99: The African Desert","Chapter 100: Death of Darius","Chapter 101: Defeat of Porus","Chapter 102: The Return to Babylon","Chapter 103: Death of Alexander the Great"]},{"title":"Unit 14: After Alexander (Chapters 104–109)","lessons":["Chapter 104: The Division of the Realm","Chapter 105: Death of Demosthenes","Chapter 106: The Last of the Athenians","Chapter 107: The Colossus of Rhodes","Chapter 108: The Battle of Ipsus","Chapter 109: Demetrius and the Athenians"]},{"title":"Unit 15: The Last Days of Free Greece (Chapters 110–115)","lessons":["Chapter 110: The Achæan League","Chapter 111: Division in Sparta","Chapter 112: Death of Agis","Chapter 113: The War of the Two Leagues","Chapter 114: The Last of the Greeks","Chapter 115: Greece a Roman Province"]}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set seller_name = excluded.seller_name, seller_kind = excluded.seller_kind, seller_verified = excluded.seller_verified, title = excluded.title, subjects = excluded.subjects, grade_min = excluded.grade_min, grade_max = excluded.grade_max, philosophies = excluded.philosophies, format = excluded.format, price = excluded.price, description = excluded.description, includes = excluded.includes, lessons = excluded.lessons, lessons_per_week = excluded.lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson = excluded.minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement = excluded.parent_involvement, family_style = excluded.family_style, lesson_steps = excluded.lesson_steps, cover = excluded.cover, color = excluded.color, esa_eligible = excluded.esa_eligible, package_id = excluded.package_id, package_version = excluded.package_version, license = excluded.license, attribution = excluded.attribution, outline = excluded.outline;
insert into public.package_releases (listing_id, version, content) values ('l-lib-story-of-the-greeks', 1, '{"units":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u1","title":"Unit 1: Myths and Heroes (Chapters 1–12)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-1","title":"Chapter 1: Early Inhabitants of Greece","steps":["Read Chapter I, \"Early Inhabitants of Greece,\" aloud together","Narration: tell who lived in Greece long ago and who came by sea to teach them","Map work: Find Greece, the Mediterranean Sea, Egypt, and Phoenicia on a map; notice the islands like stepping-stones","Timeline: Add \"Pelasgians live in Greece\" at c. 2000 BC (the book''s estimate)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter I","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#I_EARLY_INHABITANTS_OF_GREECE"}],"questions":["How did the earliest Greeks, the Pelasgians, live?","Why did Egyptian and Phoenician sailors hop from island to island instead of crossing open sea?"],"answers":["In forests and caves, hunting with clubs and stone-tipped weapons and wearing animal skins","They had no compass, so they stayed in sight of land"],"notes":"The book dates events by Bible stories (\"when Isaac went to Egypt\"). Modern historians describe early Greece differently; treat the Egyptian-teacher story as an old tradition."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-2","title":"Chapter 2: The Deluge of Ogyges","steps":["Read Chapter II, \"The Deluge of Ogyges,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Inachus helped the Pelasgians and what happened in the great flood","Map work: Find Argos on a map of Greece","Timeline: Add \"Deluge of Ogyges\" to the myths strip of your timeline (legend, no real date)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter II","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#II_THE_DELUGE_OF_OGYGES"}],"questions":["Why did later Greeks call the huge old stone walls \"Cyclopean\"?","Which city did Inachus found?"],"answers":["They said one-eyed giants called Cyclopes must have built them","Argos"],"notes":"Inachus and Ogyges are legendary figures, though the giant \"Cyclopean\" walls are real and can still be seen."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-3","title":"Chapter 3: The Founding of Many Important Cities","steps":["Read Chapter III, \"The Founding of Many Important Cities,\" aloud together","Narration: name the founders in this chapter and what each one gave the Greeks","Map work: Find Athens, Thebes, Argos, Mycenae, and the Isthmus of Corinth on a map of Greece","Timeline: Add \"Mycenae''s great walls and gold masks\" at c. 1600–1200 BC; put Cecrops and Cadmus on the myths strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter III","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#III_THE_FOUNDING_OF_MANY_IMPORTANT_CITIES"}],"questions":["What very important gift did Cadmus bring to Greece?","Whose name was given to the city of Athens?","What surprising things were found in the old tombs at Mycenae?"],"answers":["The alphabet (writing)","The goddess Athene","Thin masks of pure gold, plus weapons, armor, and pottery"],"notes":"Cecrops, Cadmus, and Danaus are founders from legend. The Mycenae finds are real (dug up in 1876)."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-4","title":"Chapter 4: Story of Deucalion","steps":["Read Chapter IV, \"Story of Deucalion,\" aloud together","Narration: tell the story of Deucalion and Pyrrha after the flood","Map work: Find Thessaly, Delphi, and the pass of Thermopylae on a map of Greece","Timeline: Add \"Deucalion''s flood\" to the myths strip; add \"Amphictyonic Council meets at Delphi\" (a real league, its beginning is legendary)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter IV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#IV_STORY_OF_DEUCALION"}],"questions":["What did Deucalion decide \"your mother''s bones\" meant?","What does the name Thermopylae mean?"],"answers":["The stones of the Earth","The Hot Gateway (hot springs in a narrow pass)"],"notes":"The Deucalion story is a myth; the book says so."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-5","title":"Chapter 5: Story of Dædalus and Icarus","steps":["Read Chapter V, \"Story of Dædalus and Icarus,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Daedalus and Icarus escaped from Crete","Map work: Find Crete and the Icarian Sea on a map","Timeline: Add \"Daedalus and Icarus\" and \"King Minos of Crete\" to the myths strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter V","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#V_STORY_OF_DAEDALUS_AND_ICARUS"}],"questions":["Which useful inventions does the book credit to Daedalus?","Why did Icarus fall into the sea?","Who were the Hellenes named after?"],"answers":["The saw, the potter''s wheel, and sails for ships","He flew too near the sun and the wax holding his wings melted","Hellen, son of Deucalion"],"notes":"The book suggests the wing story grew out of Daedalus inventing sails. That is the author''s guess; the Minoan civilization on Crete was real."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-6","title":"Chapter 6: The Adventures of Jason","steps":["Read Chapter VI, \"The Adventures of Jason,\" aloud together","Narration: tell who the heroes were and what Jason went looking for","Map work: Find the Peloponnesus, then trace the Argo''s route from Greece across the Black Sea to Colchis","Timeline: Add \"Voyage of the Argo\" to the myths strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter VI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#VI_THE_ADVENTURES_OF_JASON"}],"questions":["How many heroes sailed on the Argo?","What did the Golden Fleece really stand for, according to the book?","Where does the name Peloponnesus come from?"],"answers":["Fifty","The riches the Greeks brought home from trading in the East","Pelops, who ruled the peninsula"],"notes":"Jason''s voyage is legend; the book explains it as an early trading journey."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-7","title":"Chapter 7: Theseus Visits the Labyrinth","steps":["Read Chapter VII, \"Theseus Visits the Labyrinth,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Theseus beat the monster in the Labyrinth, and what happened to his father","Map work: Find Athens, Crete, the Aegean Sea, and the island of Scyros","Timeline: Add \"Theseus and the Labyrinth\" to the myths strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter VII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#VII_THESEUS_VISITS_THE_LABYRINTH"}],"questions":["How did Theseus find his way out of the Labyrinth?","Why did King Aegeus think his son was dead?","What festival did Theseus start for Athene?"],"answers":["By following a long thread the king''s daughter gave him","The ship came back with black sails instead of white","The Panathenaea"],"notes":"Theseus is a legendary hero. The Acropolis named here is real and still stands in Athens."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-8","title":"Chapter 8: The Terrible Prophecy","steps":["Read Chapter VIII, \"The Terrible Prophecy,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why baby Oedipus was sent away and how he ended up in Corinth","Map work: Find Thebes, Delphi, and Corinth on a map of Greece","Timeline: Add \"Oedipus\" to the myths strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter VIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#VIII_THE_TERRIBLE_PROPHECY"}],"questions":["Why did King Laius want to get rid of his baby son?","What does the name Oedipus mean?"],"answers":["An oracle said the son would kill his father and bring ruin to the city","Swollen-footed"],"notes":"The Oedipus myth spans three chapters and has dark themes (a man unknowingly marries his mother). Preview it and shorten or skip parts if needed for younger children."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-9","title":"Chapter 9: The Sphinx''s Riddle","steps":["Read Chapter IX, \"The Sphinx''s Riddle,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what happened at the crossroads and how Oedipus beat the Sphinx","Map work: Find Thebes in Boeotia and trace a road toward Delphi","Timeline: Add \"The Sphinx''s riddle\" to the myths strip next to Oedipus"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter IX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#IX_THE_SPHINXS_RIDDLE"}],"questions":["What was the Sphinx''s riddle, and what was the answer?","What did the Sphinx look like?"],"answers":["What walks on four feet in the morning, two at noon, and three at night? Man","A woman''s head, a lion''s body, and an eagle''s wings"],"notes":"Myth. The violence at the crossroads is told plainly; you may want to summarize it."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-10","title":"Chapter 10: Blindness and Death of Œdipus","steps":["Read Chapter X, \"Blindness and Death of Œdipus,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the truth about Oedipus came out and what Antigone did","Map work: Find Thebes again and talk about how far a blind man could wander on foot"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter X","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#X_BLINDNESS_AND_DEATH_OF_OEDIPUS"}],"questions":["What did the oracle say would stop the plague in Thebes?","Which daughter stayed to guide her blind father?"],"answers":["Finding and punishing the man who killed King Laius","Antigone"],"notes":"Contains Jocasta''s suicide and Oedipus blinding himself. Read ahead and soften or summarize for younger listeners."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-11","title":"Chapter 11: The Brothers'' Quarrel","steps":["Read Chapter XI, \"The Brothers'' Quarrel,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why Eteocles and Polynices fought and how their quarrel ended","Map work: Find Argos, Nemea, and Thebes and trace the march of the Seven","Timeline: Add \"Seven against Thebes\" to the myths strip; note the real Nemean Games"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XI_THE_BROTHERS_QUARREL"}],"questions":["What was the brothers'' agreement, and who broke it?","Which games did the seven chiefs start, and in whose honor?","How long did the first siege of Thebes last?"],"answers":["To rule a year each in turn; Eteocles broke it","The Nemean Games, honoring Hercules'' victory over the Nemean lion","Seven years"],"notes":"The book itself says this family story is a myth."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-1-12","title":"Chapter 12: The Taking of Thebes","steps":["Read Chapter XII, \"The Taking of Thebes,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the sons of the Seven finally took Thebes","Map work: Find Thebes and Argos on a map of Greece","Timeline: Add \"War of the Epigoni\" to the myths strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XII_THE_TAKING_OF_THEBES"}],"questions":["What does \"Epigoni\" mean?","Who was put on the throne of Thebes after it was taken?"],"answers":["Descendants (the sons of the first warriors)","Thersander, son of Polynices"]}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u2","title":"Unit 2: Troy, Homer, and the End of the Heroic Age (Chapters 13–20)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-2-1","title":"Chapter 13: The Childhood of Paris","steps":["Read Chapter XIII, \"The Childhood of Paris,\" aloud together","Narration: tell the story of Paris from his mother''s dream to his journey home with Helen","Map work: Find Troy in Asia Minor (modern Turkey), Sparta, and the port of Aulis","Timeline: Add \"Paris and Helen\" to the myths strip, beginning the Trojan War story"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XIII_THE_CHILDHOOD_OF_PARIS"}],"questions":["What did Queen Hecuba dream about baby Paris?","How was Paris recognized when he came to Troy?","Where did Menelaus tell his allies to gather?"],"answers":["A flaming brand in his cradle that set the palace on fire","He won all the prizes at the games, looked like the royal family, and Cassandra named him","Aulis"],"notes":"The Trojan War comes from Homer''s poems. Troy was a real city, but the story with gods and heroes is legend, not proven history."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-2-2","title":"Chapter 14: The Muster of the Troops","steps":["Read Chapter XIV, \"The Muster of the Troops,\" aloud together","Narration: tell who the Greek chiefs were and how Ulysses found Achilles","Map work: Find Aulis and the island of Scyros","Timeline: Add \"Greek army gathers at Aulis\" to the myths strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XIV_THE_MUSTER_OF_THE_TROOPS"}],"questions":["Why was Achilles'' heel the only place he could be hurt?","How did Ulysses trick Achilles into showing who he was?","Who was chosen leader of the whole Greek army?"],"answers":["His mother held him by the heel when she dipped him in the river Styx","He put a fine sword among his trinkets, and only Achilles grabbed it","Agamemnon, King of Mycenae"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-2-3","title":"Chapter 15: The Sacrifice of Iphigenia","steps":["Read Chapter XV, \"The Sacrifice of Iphigenia,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why the Greek fleet could not sail and what happened once they reached Troy","Map work: Trace the route from Aulis across the Aegean Sea to Troy","Timeline: Add \"Siege of Troy begins\" to the myths strip (ancient Greeks placed it near 1200 BC)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XV_THE_SACRIFICE_OF_IPHIGENIA"}],"questions":["Why was there no wind to carry the Greek ships?","How long had the war gone on before anyone was close to winning?"],"answers":["Agamemnon had offended the goddess Diana, and the oracle said she must be appeased","Nine years"],"notes":"The story of a father asked to sacrifice his daughter is upsetting; the book has the goddess rescue her. A good chance to talk about myths versus history."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-2-4","title":"Chapter 16: The Wrath of Achilles","steps":["Read Chapter XVI, \"The Wrath of Achilles,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why Achilles stopped fighting and what made him return","Map work: Find the plain between Troy and the sea where the armies camped","Timeline: Add \"The Wrath of Achilles (the Iliad)\" to the myths strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XVI_THE_WRATH_OF_ACHILLES"}],"questions":["Why did Achilles refuse to fight for Agamemnon?","Whose armor did Patroclus wear into battle, and who killed him?"],"answers":["Agamemnon unfairly took away the captive girl given to Achilles","Achilles'' armor; Hector killed him"],"notes":"The book mentions captives being made slaves. Gods sending a plague is part of the myth."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-2-5","title":"Chapter 17: Death of Hector and Achilles","steps":["Read Chapter XVII, \"Death of Hector and Achilles,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the fight between Hector and Achilles and King Priam''s visit","Map work: Find Troy and imagine the walls the old king watched from","Timeline: Add \"Death of Hector and Achilles\" to the myths strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XVII_DEATH_OF_HECTOR_AND_ACHILLES"}],"questions":["Why did old King Priam secretly visit Achilles'' tent?","How did Achilles die?"],"answers":["To beg for the body of his son Hector so he could bury it","A poisoned arrow shot by Paris hit his heel"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-2-6","title":"Chapter 18: The Burning of Troy","steps":["Read Chapter XVIII, \"The Burning of Troy,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the wooden horse tricked the Trojans","Map work: Find Troy, then Sicily and southern Italy (\"Great Greece\"), and the Tiber River where Aeneas landed","Timeline: Add \"Fall of Troy\" to the myths strip (Greek tradition said c. 1184 BC); add the real \"Troy excavated by Schliemann, 1870s\" to the modern end"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XVIII_THE_BURNING_OF_TROY"}],"questions":["Whose idea was the wooden horse?","Why did the Trojans tear down part of their own wall?","Which Trojan prince escaped to start a new kingdom in Italy?"],"answers":["Ulysses","The horse was too big to fit through the gates","Aeneas"],"notes":"Archaeologists did find a burned city at Troy, but the wooden horse and the heroes are legend."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-2-7","title":"Chapter 19: Heroic Death of Codrus","steps":["Read Chapter XIX, \"Heroic Death of Codrus,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how King Codrus saved Athens","Map work: Find the Peloponnesus (the \"mulberry leaf\"), Sparta, Athens, and the Ionian cities of Ephesus and Miletus in Asia Minor","Timeline: Add \"Dorians move into the Peloponnesus\" at c. 1100 BC and \"Death of Codrus\" at c. 1070 BC (traditional date)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XIX_HEROIC_DEATH_OF_CODRUS"}],"questions":["What had the oracle promised about the coming battle?","Why did Codrus dress as an ordinary soldier?","What title did the Athenians give their rulers after Codrus instead of king?"],"answers":["The army whose king was killed would win","So the enemy would not recognize him and spare him","Archon"],"notes":"The book says real history begins here. Codrus is still partly legendary; written records came later."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-2-8","title":"Chapter 20: The Blind Poet","steps":["Read Chapter XX, \"The Blind Poet,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what we know about Homer and how his poems spread","Map work: Find the island of Chios and the coast of Asia Minor","Timeline: Add \"Homer''s Iliad and Odyssey\" at c. 750 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XX_THE_BLIND_POET"}],"questions":["What are Homer''s two great poems about?","How were Homer''s poems kept alive before they were written down?","Which island was most often named as Homer''s birthplace?"],"answers":["The Iliad tells of the Trojan War; the Odyssey of Ulysses'' ten-year voyage home","People memorized and recited them from place to place","Chios"],"notes":"The pirate-blinding story is a legend; historians are not sure Homer was one real person."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u3","title":"Unit 3: Sparta and the Laws of Lycurgus (Chapters 21–28)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-3-1","title":"Chapter 21: The Rise of Sparta","steps":["Read Chapter XXI, \"The Rise of Sparta,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Lycurgus protected the baby king","Map work: Find Sparta and Laconia on a map of Greece","Timeline: Add \"Lycurgus gives Sparta its laws\" at c. 800 BC (traditional date)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXI_THE_RISE_OF_SPARTA"}],"questions":["Who were the Helots?","Why did Sparta have two kings at once?","How did Lycurgus keep the baby king Charilaus safe?"],"answers":["Prisoners of war who became Spartan slaves","Aristodemus''s twin sons both became king, and each line continued","He pretended to agree to kill him, then took him to the council hall and had him named king"],"notes":"Many historians think Lycurgus may be partly or wholly legendary."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-3-2","title":"Chapter 22: The Spartan Training","steps":["Read Chapter XXII, \"The Spartan Training,\" aloud together","Narration: describe a Spartan boy''s life after age seven","Map work: Find the Eurotas River near Sparta","Timeline: Add \"Spartan training system\" alongside Lycurgus (c. 800–600 BC)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXII_THE_SPARTAN_TRAINING"}],"questions":["At what age were Spartan children given over to the state?","What did Spartan boys sleep on?"],"answers":["Seven","A pile of rushes from the Eurotas River"],"notes":"The chapter describes leaving weak babies to die. Read ahead and decide how to handle it."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-3-3","title":"Chapter 23: The Brave Spartan Boy","steps":["Read Chapter XXIII, \"The Brave Spartan Boy,\" aloud together","Narration: tell the story of the boy and the fox, and what \"laconic\" means","Map work: Find Laconia, the land around Sparta"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXIII_THE_BRAVE_SPARTAN_BOY"}],"questions":["Why were Spartan boys trained to steal food?","What does \"laconic\" speech mean, and where does the word come from?"],"answers":["Spartan armies had no supply wagons and had to find their own food in war","Saying a lot in few words; from Laconia, the land of Sparta"],"notes":"The book praises harsh customs (flogging, the fox story). Ask your child whether these customs were good or wise."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-3-4","title":"Chapter 24: Public Tables in Sparta","steps":["Read Chapter XXIV, \"Public Tables in Sparta,\" aloud together","Narration: describe the Spartan common tables and what mothers told sons going to war","Map work: Find Sparta and Messenia next to it"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXIV_PUBLIC_TABLES_IN_SPARTA"}],"questions":["What was the Spartan men''s favorite food?","What did a Spartan mother say when handing her son his shield, and what did she mean?"],"answers":["Black broth, a thick dark stew","\"Come back with it or on it\": come home with honor or die bravely"],"notes":"The book describes deliberately getting a slave drunk as a lesson, a cruel custom worth discussing."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-3-5","title":"Chapter 25: Laws of Lycurgus","steps":["Read Chapter XXV, \"Laws of Lycurgus,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what Lycurgus did about money and how he made the laws last","Map work: Find Sparta and talk about how hard it would be to cart heavy iron money there","Timeline: Add \"Sparta uses iron money\" next to Lycurgus"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXV_LAWS_OF_LYCURGUS"}],"questions":["Why did Lycurgus make the Spartans use iron money?","How did Lycurgus make sure the laws would be kept after he left?"],"answers":["It was so heavy that no one could hide it, steal it, or grow greedy for wealth","He made the citizens swear to obey them until he came back, then never returned"],"notes":"Story of the oath is a legend about a figure who may not have existed."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-3-6","title":"Chapter 26: The Messenian War","steps":["Read Chapter XXVI, \"The Messenian War,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why Sparta attacked Messenia and what happened at Ithome","Map work: Find Messenia and Mount Ithome west of Sparta","Timeline: Add \"First Messenian War\" at c. 743–724 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXVI_THE_MESSENIAN_WAR"}],"questions":["Why did the Spartans want Messenia''s land?","Why couldn''t the Spartans climb up to Ithome?"],"answers":["Its fields were more fertile than their own","It stood on a very high, steep rock and the defenders rolled stones down"],"notes":"Includes a king sacrificing his daughter and then taking his own life. Preview before reading aloud."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-3-7","title":"Chapter 27: The Music of Tyrtæus","steps":["Read Chapter XXVII, \"The Music of Tyrtæus,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how a lame schoolmaster helped the Spartans win","Map work: Find Messenia, Sparta, and Athens","Timeline: Add \"Second Messenian War\" at c. 685–668 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXVII_THE_MUSIC_OF_TYRTAEUS"}],"questions":["What bold thing did Aristomenes do inside Sparta?","Whom did the Athenians send to lead the Spartans, and how did he help?"],"answers":["He hung a trophy of captured arms in the main temple and wrote his name under it","Tyrtaeus, a lame schoolmaster, who stirred them with war songs"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-3-8","title":"Chapter 28: Aristomenes'' Escape","steps":["Read Chapter XXVIII, \"Aristomenes'' Escape,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Aristomenes got out of the pit","Map work: Find Messenia, then Sicily, the city of Messina, and the Strait of Messina","Timeline: Add \"Messenians found colonies in Italy and Sicily\" at c. 650 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXVIII_ARISTOMENES_ESCAPE"}],"questions":["What animal led Aristomenes out of the pit?","Which city in Sicily was named after the Messenians'' homeland?"],"answers":["A fox","Messina"],"notes":"The escape story is a legend told about a real war."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u4","title":"Unit 4: The Olympic Games and the Lawgivers of Athens (Chapters 29–35)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-4-1","title":"Chapter 29: The Olympic Games","steps":["Read Chapter XXIX, \"The Olympic Games,\" aloud together","Narration: describe the Olympic games: where, how often, and what the winners received","Map work: Find Elis and Olympia, northwest of Sparta","Timeline: Add \"First recorded Olympic games\" at 776 BC (the book says 774) and \"Modern Olympics revived in Athens\" at AD 1896"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXIX_THE_OLYMPIC_GAMES"}],"questions":["What prize did Olympic winners receive?","How did Greeks use the games to count time?","Where were the first modern Olympics held, and when?"],"answers":["A crown of wild olive leaves","They counted by Olympiads, the four-year spans between games","Athens, in 1896"],"notes":"Hercules founding the games is legend. The book was written in 1896, the year the modern games began, so it calls them brand new."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-4-2","title":"Chapter 30: Milo of Croton","steps":["Read Chapter XXX, \"Milo of Croton,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Milo became so strong and how he died","Map work: Find Croton in southern Italy (a Greek colony)","Timeline: Add \"Milo of Croton, Olympic wrestler\" at c. 540–510 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXX_MILO_OF_CROTON"}],"questions":["How did Milo train to grow stronger each day?","What trapped Milo in the forest?"],"answers":["He carried a calf every day as it grew into an ox","His hands got caught when a split tree trunk snapped shut"],"notes":"Milo was a real champion wrestler, but stories like eating a whole ox are exaggerated legends."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-4-3","title":"Chapter 31: The Jealous Athlete","steps":["Read Chapter XXXI, \"The Jealous Athlete,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what happened to the jealous man and to the statue","Map work: Find Olympia again on a map of Greece","Timeline: Add \"Theagenes, champion athlete\" at c. 480 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXXI_THE_JEALOUS_ATHLETE"}],"questions":["How did the jealous man die?","What punishment did the judges give the statue?"],"answers":["He shook the statue until it fell and crushed him","It was thrown into the sea"],"notes":"Good chance to discuss what the book calls superstition."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-4-4","title":"Chapter 32: The Girls'' Games","steps":["Read Chapter XXXII, \"The Girls'' Games,\" aloud together","Narration: describe the girls'' torch race and the chariot races","Map work: Find Olympia and sketch a stadium and a hippodrome"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXXII_THE_GIRLS_GAMES"}],"questions":["How did a girl win the torch race?","Where were the horse and chariot races held?"],"answers":["By reaching the goal first with her torch still lit, or keeping it lit longest","In the hippodrome"],"notes":"Girls'' races at Olympia were held at a separate festival honoring the goddess Hera."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-4-5","title":"Chapter 33: The Bloody Laws of Draco","steps":["Read Chapter XXXIII, \"The Bloody Laws of Draco,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Draco''s laws and what happened to Cylon''s followers","Map work: Find Athens, the Acropolis, and the island of Aegina","Timeline: Add \"Cylon tries to seize Athens\" at c. 632 BC and \"Draco''s laws\" at c. 621 BC (the book says 602)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXXIII_THE_BLOODY_LAWS_OF_DRACO"}],"questions":["Why do we still call very harsh rules \"draconian\"?","What punishment did Draco set for stealing a cabbage?","Why did Cylon''s men hold a cord tied to Athene''s statue?"],"answers":["Draco''s laws punished small wrongs as severely as great crimes","Death","They believed it kept them under the goddess''s protection"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-4-6","title":"Chapter 34: The Laws of Solon","steps":["Read Chapter XXXIV, \"The Laws of Solon,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what Solon changed in Athens","Map work: Find Athens and the Areopagus hill beside the Acropolis","Timeline: Add \"Solon''s laws\" at 594 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXXIV_THE_LAWS_OF_SOLON"}],"questions":["Why were trials at the Areopagus held in the dark?","How long did Epimenides sleep in the cave?","How long did Solon ask the Athenians to keep his laws while he traveled?"],"answers":["So the jury would judge by the facts, not by how the accused looked","Fifty-eight years","Ten years"],"notes":"Epimenides'' long sleep is a legend (like Rip Van Winkle)."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-4-7","title":"Chapter 35: The First Plays","steps":["Read Chapter XXXV, \"The First Plays,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Greek plays began and what the Theater of Dionysus was like","Map work: Find Athens and the Theater of Dionysus below the Acropolis","Timeline: Add \"Thespis, first actor\" at c. 534 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXXV_THE_FIRST_PLAYS"}],"questions":["What does \"tragedy\" mean in Greek?","Why did Solon dislike the plays?","Name one Greek writer of tragedies and one of comedies."],"answers":["Goat song","He said they taught people to love a lie","Tragedies: Aeschylus, Sophocles, or Euripides; comedies: Aristophanes"],"notes":"Actors are still called \"thespians\" after Thespis."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u5","title":"Unit 5: Tyrants of Athens and the Great King (Chapters 36–41)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-5-1","title":"Chapter 36: The Tyrant Pisistratus","steps":["Read Chapter XXXVI, \"The Tyrant Pisistratus,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Pisistratus tricked his way to power and what good he did","Map work: Find Athens and its Acropolis","Timeline: Add \"Pisistratus becomes tyrant\" at 561 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXXVI_THE_TYRANT_PISISTRATUS"}],"questions":["What trick did Pisistratus use to get a bodyguard?","Which two English words come from the Greek \"aristoi\" and \"demos\"?","What did Pisistratus do with Homer''s poems?"],"answers":["He wounded himself and said nobles had attacked him","Aristocrat and democrat","Had them written down and put in a public library"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-5-2","title":"Chapter 37: The Tyrant''s Insult","steps":["Read Chapter XXXVII, \"The Tyrant''s Insult,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why Harmodius and Aristogiton turned against the tyrants","Map work: Find Athens; Solon had been traveling in Asia Minor across the Aegean","Timeline: Add \"Death of Pisistratus; his sons rule\" at 527 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXXVII_THE_TYRANTS_INSULT"}],"questions":["How did Solon feel when he came home and found Pisistratus ruling?","How did Hipparchus insult Harmodius?"],"answers":["Sorry at first, but he forgave him because he ruled wisely","He banned Harmodius''s sister from carrying flowers in the procession"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-5-3","title":"Chapter 38: Death of the Conspirators","steps":["Read Chapter XXXVIII, \"Death of the Conspirators,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what happened at the festival and how Leaena kept the secret","Map work: Find Athens on a map of Greece","Timeline: Add \"Hipparchus killed\" at 514 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXXVIII_DEATH_OF_THE_CONSPIRATORS"}],"questions":["Which tyrant did Harmodius kill?","What image was put on Leaena''s tomb, and why?"],"answers":["Hipparchus","A lioness without a tongue, because she kept silent under torture"],"notes":"Contains torture; you may want to summarize Leaena''s story."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-5-4","title":"Chapter 39: Hippias Driven Out of Athens","steps":["Read Chapter XXXIX, \"Hippias Driven Out of Athens,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Hippias was driven out and how ostracism worked","Map work: Find Delphi, Athens, and Sparta","Timeline: Add \"Hippias driven out\" at 510 BC and \"Clisthenes'' reforms\" at 508 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XXXIX_HIPPIAS_DRIVEN_OUT_OF_ATHENS"}],"questions":["How did the Alcmaeonidae win the favor of the priests at Delphi?","What was written on the shells in an ostracism vote?","How many votes were needed to ostracize a man?"],"answers":["They rebuilt the temple in white marble, paying the extra cost themselves","The name of the man to be banished","Six thousand"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-5-5","title":"Chapter 40: The Great King","steps":["Read Chapter XL, \"The Great King,\" aloud together","Narration: describe the Great King and how he ruled his huge empire","Map work: Find Persia, Ecbatana, and Ionia on the coast of Asia Minor","Timeline: Add \"Darius I becomes king of Persia\" at 522 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XL","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XL_THE_GREAT_KING"}],"questions":["What were satraps?","What was special about the walls of Ecbatana?"],"answers":["Governors who ruled the provinces (satrapies) for the king","There were seven walls, each painted a different bright color"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-5-6","title":"Chapter 41: Hippias Visits Darius","steps":["Read Chapter XLI, \"Hippias Visits Darius,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how a Greek doctor helped make Darius want to conquer Greece","Map work: Trace a route from Persia across Asia Minor to Greece","Timeline: Add \"Democedes, Greek doctor at the Persian court\" at c. 520 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XLI_HIPPIAS_VISITS_DARIUS"}],"questions":["How did Democedes become the king''s doctor?","Why did Darius decide to conquer Greece?"],"answers":["He healed the king''s hurt foot after the Persian doctors failed","His officers'' reports on Greece made him curious, and Hippias urged him on"],"notes":"The book tells this mostly from the historian Herodotus; scholars think the real reasons for the war were more political."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u6","title":"Unit 6: The Persian Wars (Chapters 42–53)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-1","title":"Chapter 42: Destruction of the Persian Host","steps":["Read Chapter XLII, \"Destruction of the Persian Host,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why Darius was angry with the Athenians and what happened to his first army and fleet","Map work: Find Sardis, Ionia, the Hellespont, and Mount Athos","Timeline: Add \"Ionian Revolt; Sardis burned\" at 499–498 BC and \"Persian fleet wrecked off Mount Athos\" at 492 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XLII_DESTRUCTION_OF_THE_PERSIAN_HOST"}],"questions":["What did Darius''s slave say to him every day at dinner?","What destroyed the Persian fleet near Mount Athos?"],"answers":["\"Master, remember the Athenians!\"","A terrible storm"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-2","title":"Chapter 43: The Advance of the Second Host","steps":["Read Chapter XLIII, \"The Advance of the Second Host,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Athens and Sparta answered Darius and how Miltiades came to command","Map work: Find Marathon, Athens, Sparta, and Plataea","Timeline: Add \"Persians demand earth and water\" at 491 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XLIII_THE_ADVANCE_OF_THE_SECOND_HOST"}],"questions":["What did \"earth and water\" stand for?","Why did the Spartans say they could not come at once?","How did Miltiades become sole commander?"],"answers":["Accepting the Persian king as master of their land and sea","They believed they had to wait until the moon was full","Aristides and the other generals gave him their days of command"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-3","title":"Chapter 44: The Battle of Marathon","steps":["Read Chapter XLIV, \"The Battle of Marathon,\" aloud together","Narration: tell the story of the Battle of Marathon and the runner who carried the news","Map work: Find Marathon on a map of Greece and measure about how far it is to Athens","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Marathon\" at 490 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XLIV_THE_BATTLE_OF_MARATHON"}],"questions":["Who attacked first at Marathon?","What did the runner say when he reached Athens?","Why did Miltiades march his tired army straight back to Athens?"],"answers":["The Greeks","\"Rejoice, we have conquered!\"","To defend the city in case the Persian fleet sailed there"],"notes":"The famous Marathon runner story is a later legend, the origin of today''s marathon race. Some of the gory details can be skipped."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-4","title":"Chapter 45: Miltiades'' Disgrace","steps":["Read Chapter XLV, \"Miltiades'' Disgrace,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Athens honored Miltiades and how he fell from favor","Map work: Find the Acropolis in Athens and the island of Paros","Timeline: Add \"Miltiades dies in disgrace\" at 489 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XLV_MILTIADES_DISGRACE"}],"questions":["What was made from the captured Persian shields and arms?","Why was Miltiades put on trial?","Who was Miltiades'' son?"],"answers":["A huge statue of Athene on the Acropolis","He used the fleet to attack Paros over a personal quarrel and failed","Cimon"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-5","title":"Chapter 46: Aristides the Just","steps":["Read Chapter XLVI, \"Aristides the Just,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what happened when the farmer asked Aristides to write on his shell","Map work: Find Athens and the market place (agora)","Timeline: Add \"Aristides ostracized\" at 482 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XLVI_ARISTIDES_THE_JUST"}],"questions":["What did Themistocles and Aristides disagree about?","Why did the farmer want Aristides banished?","What did Aristides write on the shell?"],"answers":["Themistocles wanted more ships; Aristides wanted a bigger land army","He was tired of hearing him called \"the Just\"","His own name"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-6","title":"Chapter 47: Two Noble Spartan Youths","steps":["Read Chapter XLVII, \"Two Noble Spartan Youths,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what Bulis and Sperthias offered Xerxes and how he answered","Map work: Find Sparta and Persia","Timeline: Add \"Darius dies; Xerxes becomes king\" at 486 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XLVII_TWO_NOBLE_SPARTAN_YOUTHS"}],"questions":["Why did Bulis and Sperthias go to Persia?","Why wouldn''t they bow before Xerxes?"],"answers":["To offer their lives to make up for the Persian messengers Sparta had killed","They said such honor belonged only to their gods"],"notes":"The army sizes here (two million) come from ancient writers; modern historians think it was far smaller."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-7","title":"Chapter 48: The Great Army","steps":["Read Chapter XLVIII, \"The Great Army,\" aloud together","Narration: describe Xerxes'' army crossing the Hellespont","Map work: Find the Hellespont and the canal at Mount Athos","Timeline: Add \"Xerxes crosses the Hellespont\" at 480 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XLVIII_THE_GREAT_ARMY"}],"questions":["What did Xerxes do after a storm wrecked his bridges?","Why did Xerxes weep when he looked at his army?","Who were the Immortals?"],"answers":["Had the waves whipped and chains thrown into the strait","In a hundred years every one of those men would be dead","The king''s bodyguard of picked soldiers"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-8","title":"Chapter 49: Preparations for Defense","steps":["Read Chapter XLIX, \"Preparations for Defense,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the Greeks prepared to meet Xerxes","Map work: Find Corinth, Thermopylae, and Artemisium","Timeline: Add \"Greek council at Corinth\" at 481 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XLIX_PREPARATIONS_FOR_DEFENSE"}],"questions":["Why did so few soldiers go with Leonidas?","How many Spartans went to guard Thermopylae?"],"answers":["Most Greeks were at the Olympic games and believed it was wrong to fight during them","Three hundred"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-9","title":"Chapter 50: Leonidas at Thermopylæ","steps":["Read Chapter L, \"Leonidas at Thermopylæ,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Leonidas and his men held the pass","Map work: Find Thermopylae on a map of Greece and look at the narrow strip between mountains and sea","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Thermopylae\" at 480 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter L","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#L_LEONIDAS_AT_THERMOPYLAE"}],"questions":["What did Leonidas answer when told to give up his weapons?","What did he say about the arrows darkening the sun?"],"answers":["\"Come and take them!\"","\"Then we can fight in the shade.\""],"notes":"The \"fight in the shade\" line is usually credited to the Spartan Dieneces in Herodotus."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-10","title":"Chapter 51: Death of Leonidas","steps":["Read Chapter LI, \"Death of Leonidas,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the Persians got around the Greeks at Thermopylae and what happened next","Map work: Find Thermopylae, Delphi, Artemisium, and the Isthmus of Corinth","Timeline: Add \"Death of Leonidas\" at 480 BC (same year as Thermopylae)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LI_DEATH_OF_LEONIDAS"}],"questions":["Who showed the Persians the secret path?","Which allies stayed to die with the Spartans?","What did the Greeks start building across the Isthmus of Corinth?"],"answers":["Ephialtes, a Greek shepherd","Seven hundred Thespians","A huge wall"],"notes":"The storm at Delphi is told as if the gods protected their temple; this comes from Herodotus."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-11","title":"Chapter 52: The Burning of Athens","steps":["Read Chapter LII, \"The Burning of Athens,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what \"wooden walls\" meant and what happened to Athens","Map work: Find Athens and the Bay of Salamis","Timeline: Add \"Persians burn Athens\" at 480 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LII_THE_BURNING_OF_ATHENS"}],"questions":["What did Themistocles say the \"wooden walls\" were?","Where did the Athenian families go?","What did Themistocles say when Eurybiades raised his staff?"],"answers":["Their ships","To the Peloponnesus","\"Strike if you will, but hear me!\""]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-6-12","title":"Chapter 53: The Battles of Salamis and Platæa","steps":["Read Chapter LIII, \"The Battles of Salamis and Platæa,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the victories at Salamis and Plataea, and Pausanias''s two suppers","Map work: Find Salamis, Plataea, and Mycale (on the coast of Asia Minor)","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Salamis\" at 480 BC and \"Battles of Plataea and Mycale\" at 479 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LIII_THE_BATTLES_OF_SALAMIS_AND_PLATAEA"}],"questions":["Who rowed out to warn the Greek fleet of Xerxes'' plan?","Where did Xerxes watch the sea battle from?","What point did Pausanias make with the two suppers?"],"answers":["Aristides","A throne on a mountain","That the plain Spartans had beaten the luxurious Persians"],"notes":"The Greek commander at Mycale was actually the Spartan king Leotychides, not Eurybiades as the book says."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u7","title":"Unit 7: The Golden Age of Athens (Chapters 54–59)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-7-1","title":"Chapter 54: The Rebuilding of Athens","steps":["Read Chapter LIV, \"The Rebuilding of Athens,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the Athenians rebuilt their city and how Themistocles fooled the Spartans","Map work: Find Athens, Sparta, and Miletus","Timeline: Add \"Walls of Athens rebuilt\" at 478 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LIV_THE_REBUILDING_OF_ATHENS"}],"questions":["What \"good omen\" encouraged the Athenians?","How did Themistocles give Athens time to finish the walls?"],"answers":["A new shoot grew from the burned sacred olive tree","He drew out the talks in Sparta until the walls were high enough"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-7-2","title":"Chapter 55: Death of Pausanias","steps":["Read Chapter LV, \"Death of Pausanias,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what happened to Pausanias and to Themistocles","Map work: Find Sparta, then Persia, where Themistocles ended his life","Timeline: Add \"Pausanias dies\" at c. 470 BC and \"Themistocles ostracized\" at c. 471 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LV_DEATH_OF_PAUSANIAS"}],"questions":["What wrong did Pausanias do?","What did Themistocles refuse to do for the Persian king?"],"answers":["He secretly offered to help the Persians if they made him king of all Greece","Lead a Persian army against the Greeks"],"notes":"Mentions starvation and suicide; summarize if needed."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-7-3","title":"Chapter 56: Cimon Improves Athens","steps":["Read Chapter LVI, \"Cimon Improves Athens,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Aristides and Cimon each served Athens","Map work: Find Athens, the island of Scyros, and the Theseum (Temple of Hephaestus) in Athens","Timeline: Add \"Theseus''s bones brought to Athens\" at c. 475 BC and \"Death of Aristides\" at c. 468 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LVI_CIMON_IMPROVES_ATHENS"}],"questions":["How rich was Aristides when he died?","What did the Athenians wrongly do with the allies'' money?","Where did Athenians go to hear philosophers?"],"answers":["So poor there was not enough to pay for his funeral","Spent it to beautify their own city instead of on the army and navy","The gardens of the Academy (and the Lyceum)"],"notes":"The bones were believed to be Theseus''s; the building called the Theseum is actually a temple of Hephaestus."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-7-4","title":"Chapter 57: The Earthquake","steps":["Read Chapter LVII, \"The Earthquake,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Cimon changed and what happened after the earthquake at Sparta","Map work: Find Sparta and Messenia, and Athens to the northeast","Timeline: Add \"Great earthquake at Sparta; Helots revolt\" at 464 BC and \"Cimon ostracized\" at 461 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LVII_THE_EARTHQUAKE"}],"questions":["Who scolded the young Cimon for being lazy?","How did Cimon help poor people who were too proud to ask?","Why did the Athenians ostracize Cimon?"],"answers":["Aristides the Just","His servants found out their needs and helped them secretly","They were angry that the Spartans had insulted the army Cimon urged them to send"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-7-5","title":"Chapter 58: The Age of Pericles","steps":["Read Chapter LVIII, \"The Age of Pericles,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what Pericles built and how Cimon served Athens one last time","Map work: Find the Acropolis, the Parthenon, and the port of Piraeus with the Long Walls","Timeline: Add \"Parthenon built\" at 447–432 BC and \"Thirty Years'' Peace\" at 446 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LVIII_THE_AGE_OF_PERICLES"}],"questions":["What did the Long Walls connect?","Who made the carvings and great statue for the Parthenon?","Why was Cimon''s death kept secret for a month?"],"answers":["Athens and its seaport, Piraeus","Phidias","So the army could get used to a new leader without losing courage"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-7-6","title":"Chapter 59: The Teachings of Anaxagoras","steps":["Read Chapter LIX, \"The Teachings of Anaxagoras,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what Anaxagoras taught about the sun and moon and why he was exiled","Map work: Find Athens and the Peloponnesus (Anaxagoras guessed the sun was about as big as the Peloponnesus)","Timeline: Add \"Anaxagoras teaches in Athens\" at c. 460–430 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LIX_THE_TEACHINGS_OF_ANAXAGORAS"}],"questions":["What did Anaxagoras say the sun really was?","What did he think the moon was like?"],"answers":["A great glowing rock, not a god in a chariot","Much like the earth, with mountains, plains, and seas"],"notes":"The author writes from a 19th-century Christian viewpoint (Anaxagoras \"had never heard of the true God\"). Adjust the framing to suit your family."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u8","title":"Unit 8: The Peloponnesian War (Chapters 60–68)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-8-1","title":"Chapter 60: Beginning of the Peloponnesian War","steps":["Read Chapter LX, \"Beginning of the Peloponnesian War,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Pericles explained the eclipse to his pilot","Map work: Find Attica, Athens, Piraeus, and the Peloponnesus","Timeline: Add \"Peloponnesian War begins\" at 431 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LX_BEGINNING_OF_THE_PELOPONNESIAN_WAR"}],"questions":["What was Pericles'' plan for fighting Sparta?","How did Pericles show his pilot what an eclipse is?"],"answers":["Bring everyone inside the city walls and fight with the navy at sea","He threw his cloak over the man''s head, blocking the sun the way the moon does"],"notes":"Try the cloak demonstration with a lamp and a ball."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-8-2","title":"Chapter 61: Death of Pericles","steps":["Read Chapter LXI, \"Death of Pericles,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the plague in Athens and Pericles'' last words","Map work: Find Athens and the island of Cos, home of Hippocrates","Timeline: Add \"Plague in Athens\" at 430 BC and \"Death of Pericles\" at 429 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXI_DEATH_OF_PERICLES"}],"questions":["Why did Hippocrates refuse the Persian king''s offer?","What was Pericles proudest of as he died?"],"answers":["He said his duty was to save his own countrymen first","That he never caused any fellow citizen to put on mourning"],"notes":"Hippocrates was a real doctor, but the story of his coming to cure Athens is a later legend. Some plague descriptions are grim."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-8-3","title":"Chapter 62: The Philosopher Socrates","steps":["Read Chapter LXII, \"The Philosopher Socrates,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what kind of man and teacher Socrates was","Map work: Find Athens on a map of Greece","Timeline: Add \"Socrates teaching in Athens\" at c. 430–399 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXII_THE_PHILOSOPHER_SOCRATES"}],"questions":["What was Socrates'' trade?","What did Socrates say after being drenched with water?"],"answers":["A stonecutter","\"After the thunder comes the rain.\""],"notes":"The book''s view of Xanthippe as a scold comes from old anecdotes and is unkind; little is really known about her. The author also reads Christian ideas into Socrates."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-8-4","title":"Chapter 63: Socrates'' Favorite Pupil","steps":["Read Chapter LXIII, \"Socrates'' Favorite Pupil,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what Alcibiades was like as a boy and how Socrates saved him","Map work: Find the Academy outside Athens where Socrates taught","Timeline: Add \"Socrates saves Alcibiades in battle\" at c. 432 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXIII_SOCRATES_FAVORITE_PUPIL"}],"questions":["Name Socrates'' three famous pupils from this chapter.","What did little Alcibiades do when a wagon would not stop?","Why was Alcibiades in danger in his first battle?"],"answers":["Plato, Xenophon, and Alcibiades","He lay down in the road and dared the driver to come on","His showy gold armor was weak and his flatterers left him"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-8-5","title":"Chapter 64: Youth of Alcibiades","steps":["Read Chapter LXIV, \"Youth of Alcibiades,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the tricks Alcibiades played to get people talking","Map work: Find Olympia, where Alcibiades raced his chariots","Timeline: Add \"Alcibiades wins at Olympia\" at 416 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXIV_YOUTH_OF_ALCIBIADES"}],"questions":["Why did Alcibiades cut off his dog''s tail?","How was Alcibiades different from Nicias?"],"answers":["To give the Athenians something to talk about","Alcibiades was bold and ambitious; Nicias was careful and slow"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-8-6","title":"Chapter 65: Greek Colonies in Italy","steps":["Read Chapter LXV, \"Greek Colonies in Italy,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the Sybarites and why Athens sent a fleet to Sicily","Map work: Find Sybaris, Croton, and Tarentum in southern Italy, and Syracuse and Messina in Sicily","Timeline: Add \"Athenian expedition to Sicily sails\" at 415 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXV_GREEK_COLONIES_IN_ITALY"}],"questions":["What does it mean to call someone a Sybarite?","What happened to the statues of Hermes just before the fleet sailed?"],"answers":["A person who loves luxury and ease too much","They were broken, and Alcibiades was blamed"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-8-7","title":"Chapter 66: Alcibiades in Disgrace","steps":["Read Chapter LXVI, \"Alcibiades in Disgrace,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Alcibiades turned against Athens and then tried to help it again","Map work: Find Syracuse, Sparta, and Decelea near Athens","Timeline: Add \"Athenian defeat at Syracuse\" at 413 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXVI_ALCIBIADES_IN_DISGRACE"}],"questions":["Where did Alcibiades go instead of returning to Athens for trial?","What did the Spartans fortify only twelve miles from Athens?"],"answers":["Sparta","Decelea"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-8-8","title":"Chapter 67: Death of Alcibiades","steps":["Read Chapter LXVII, \"Death of Alcibiades,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the war ended and how Alcibiades died","Map work: Find the Chersonese (Gallipoli peninsula) by the Hellespont, then Athens","Timeline: Add \"Athenian fleet destroyed at Aegospotami\" at 405 BC, \"Athens surrenders\" at 404 BC, and \"Death of Alcibiades\" at 404 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXVII_DEATH_OF_ALCIBIADES"}],"questions":["What did the Spartans tear down to the sound of music?","Who were the Thirty Tyrants?"],"answers":["The Long Walls of Athens","Thirty cruel men the Spartans put in charge of Athens"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-8-9","title":"Chapter 68: The Overthrow of the Thirty Tyrants","steps":["Read Chapter LXVIII, \"The Overthrow of the Thirty Tyrants,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Thrasybulus freed Athens","Map work: Find Athens and Sparta","Timeline: Add \"Thrasybulus overthrows the Thirty Tyrants\" at 403 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXVIII_THE_OVERTHROW_OF_THE_THIRTY_TYRANTS"}],"questions":["Who drove out the Thirty Tyrants?","Why did Sparta have to pay for Lysander''s funeral?"],"answers":["Thrasybulus","He kept none of the booty and died poor"]}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u9","title":"Unit 9: Socrates, Xenophon, and Agesilaus (Chapters 69–75)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-9-1","title":"Chapter 69: Accusation of Socrates","steps":["Read Chapter LXIX, \"Accusation of Socrates,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how a comedy helped turn people against Socrates","Map work: Find Delphi, whose oracle called Socrates the wisest man","Timeline: Add \"Aristophanes'' play The Clouds\" at 423 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXIX_ACCUSATION_OF_SOCRATES"}],"questions":["What did the oracle at Delphi say about Socrates?","Why did Socrates go to see the play that mocked him?","What two charges were made against Socrates?"],"answers":["That he was the most learned (wisest) man in Greece","To find out if any of the faults it showed were ones he could correct","Giving bad advice to young men and speaking ill of the gods"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-9-2","title":"Chapter 70: Death of Socrates","steps":["Read Chapter LXX, \"Death of Socrates,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why Socrates would not escape from prison","Map work: Find Athens and the island of Delos","Timeline: Add \"Death of Socrates\" at 399 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXX_DEATH_OF_SOCRATES"}],"questions":["Why was Socrates'' death delayed a month?","Why did Socrates refuse to escape?","Which pupil wrote down his last conversation?"],"answers":["No one could be executed while the sacred ship was away at Delos","It would be breaking the law, which he had never done","Plato"],"notes":"This chapter describes his death by poison calmly but in detail. The book again presents Socrates in Christian terms."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-9-3","title":"Chapter 71: The Defeat of Cyrus","steps":["Read Chapter LXXI, \"The Defeat of Cyrus,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why Cyrus fought his brother and what trouble the Greek soldiers were left in","Map work: Find Persia and the Euphrates River near Babylon, where Cunaxa was","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Cunaxa\" at 401 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXI_THE_DEFEAT_OF_CYRUS"}],"questions":["Why did Cyrus think he should be king instead of Artaxerxes?","What happened to the Greek officers after the battle?","Who came up with a plan to save the Greek army?"],"answers":["He was the first son born after their father became king","They were invited to a council and killed","Xenophon"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-9-4","title":"Chapter 72: The Retreat of the Ten Thousand","steps":["Read Chapter LXXII, \"The Retreat of the Ten Thousand,\" aloud together","Narration: describe the long march of the Ten Thousand","Map work: Trace the route from Babylon north through the mountains to the Black Sea and on to Pergamus","Timeline: Add \"Retreat of the Ten Thousand\" at 401–400 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXII_THE_RETREAT_OF_THE_TEN_THOUSAND"}],"questions":["What did Xenophon ask of the soldiers before agreeing to lead?","About how far did the Greeks march to reach the sea?","What is the name of Xenophon''s book about the march?"],"answers":["That each would promise to obey him and not quarrel","About a thousand miles","The Anabasis"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-9-5","title":"Chapter 73: Agesilaus in Asia","steps":["Read Chapter LXXIII, \"Agesilaus in Asia,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what kind of man and general Agesilaus was","Map work: Find Sparta, then Ionia on the coast of Asia Minor","Timeline: Add \"Agesilaus fights the Persians in Asia Minor\" at 396–394 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXIII_AGESILAUS_IN_ASIA"}],"questions":["How did Agesilaus look different from most Spartans?","Why did the Persians think Agesilaus was strange for a king?"],"answers":["He was small, weak, and lame","He dressed like his soldiers and led them himself, without pomp"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-9-6","title":"Chapter 74: A Strange Interview","steps":["Read Chapter LXXIV, \"A Strange Interview,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the meeting under the tree","Map work: Find Asia Minor (Phrygia) where Pharnabazus governed"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXIV_A_STRANGE_INTERVIEW"}],"questions":["What was Agesilaus eating when Pharnabazus arrived?","What did the Persian king try when his soldiers could not beat Agesilaus?"],"answers":["Bread and onions","Bribery: he paid Athens and its allies to rise against Sparta"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-9-7","title":"Chapter 75: The Peace of Antalcidas","steps":["Read Chapter LXXV, \"The Peace of Antalcidas,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why Agesilaus had to come home and what the Peace of Antalcidas gave away","Map work: Find Coronea in Boeotia and the island of Cyprus","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Coronea\" at 394 BC and \"Peace of Antalcidas (King''s Peace)\" at 387 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXV_THE_PEACE_OF_ANTALCIDAS"}],"questions":["What did Agesilaus mean by \"ten thousand Persian archers\"?","What did the treaty hand over to the Persian king?"],"answers":["Ten thousand Persian coins stamped with an archer, used as a bribe","The Greek cities of Asia Minor and the island of Cyprus"]}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u10","title":"Unit 10: The Rise of Thebes (Chapters 76–80)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-10-1","title":"Chapter 76: The Theban Friends","steps":["Read Chapter LXXVI, \"The Theban Friends,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Sparta took Thebes and how Pelopidas and Epaminondas became friends","Map work: Find Thebes in Boeotia","Timeline: Add \"Sparta seizes Thebes\" at 382 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXVI_THE_THEBAN_FRIENDS"}],"questions":["How did the Spartans take Thebes without a fight?","How did Pelopidas and Epaminondas become friends?"],"answers":["They came during a festival when the Thebans were unarmed in the temple","Epaminondas saved the wounded Pelopidas in battle"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-10-2","title":"Chapter 77: Thebes Free Once More","steps":["Read Chapter LXXVII, \"Thebes Free Once More,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how twelve exiles freed Thebes","Map work: Trace the road from Athens to Thebes","Timeline: Add \"Thebes freed from Sparta\" at 379 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXVII_THEBES_FREE_ONCE_MORE"}],"questions":["How did the exiles get into the Spartan banquet?","What did Charon leave with Pelopidas to prove he was loyal?"],"answers":["Dressed as dancing girls","His only son, a baby"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-10-3","title":"Chapter 78: The Battle of Leuctra","steps":["Read Chapter LXXVIII, \"The Battle of Leuctra,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the Sacred Battalion and the Battle of Leuctra","Map work: Find Leuctra in Boeotia, then Laconia","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Leuctra: Thebes defeats Sparta\" at 371 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXVIII_THE_BATTLE_OF_LEUCTRA"}],"questions":["What oath did the Sacred Battalion take?","What did Epaminondas say about the bad omens?","What terms did Epaminondas give Sparta?"],"answers":["Never to turn their backs on the enemy or surrender","That no omen forbids fighting to defend your country","Never attack Thebes again and set the Messenians free"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-10-4","title":"Chapter 79: Death of Pelopidas","steps":["Read Chapter LXXIX, \"Death of Pelopidas,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Pelopidas was captured, freed, and finally killed","Map work: Find Thessaly in northern Greece","Timeline: Add \"Death of Pelopidas\" at 364 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXIX_DEATH_OF_PELOPIDAS"}],"questions":["Why was Pelopidas sent to Thessaly?","How did Epaminondas end up commanding when he was only a soldier?"],"answers":["To warn the cruel tyrant Alexander of Thessaly","The soldiers begged him to lead after their generals failed"],"notes":"The ending describes harsh treatment of the tyrant''s body; skip if needed."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-10-5","title":"Chapter 80: The Battle of Mantinea","steps":["Read Chapter LXXX, \"The Battle of Mantinea,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Epaminondas''s last battle and his last words","Map work: Find Mantinea in the central Peloponnesus","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Mantinea; death of Epaminondas\" at 362 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXX_THE_BATTLE_OF_MANTINEA"}],"questions":["What two things did the wounded Epaminondas ask about first?","What did he call Leuctra and Mantinea?"],"answers":["How the battle was going and whether his shield was safe","His \"daughters,\" who would keep his name alive"]}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u11","title":"Unit 11: The Tyrants of Syracuse (Chapters 81–86)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-11-1","title":"Chapter 81: The Tyrant of Syracuse","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXI, \"The Tyrant of Syracuse,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Philoxenus answered the tyrant''s poems","Map work: Find Syracuse on the island of Sicily","Timeline: Add \"Dionysius the Elder rules Syracuse\" at 405–367 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXXI_THE_TYRANT_OF_SYRACUSE"}],"questions":["Why did Dionysius send Philoxenus to the Quarries?","What did Philoxenus say the second time he was asked about the poems?"],"answers":["He honestly said the tyrant''s poems were trash","\"Take me back to the Quarries!\""]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-11-2","title":"Chapter 82: Story of Damon and Pythias","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXII, \"Story of Damon and Pythias,\" aloud together","Narration: tell the story of Damon and Pythias","Map work: Find Syracuse on a map of Sicily","Timeline: Add \"Damon and Pythias\" near Dionysius (c. 4th century BC; a famous story)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXXII_STORY_OF_DAMON_AND_PYTHIAS"}],"questions":["What did Damon offer to do for Pythias?","Name two things that delayed Pythias on his way back.","What did Dionysius do when Pythias returned?"],"answers":["Stay in prison and die in his place if he did not return","Robbers tied him to a tree; a flooded stream; a hot, dry plain","Set both of them free"],"notes":"A famous friendship tale, probably more legend than history."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-11-3","title":"Chapter 83: The Sword of Damocles","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXIII, \"The Sword of Damocles,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what Damocles learned at the banquet","Map work: Find Syracuse in Sicily"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXXIII_THE_SWORD_OF_DAMOCLES"}],"questions":["What was hanging over Damocles'' head?","What lesson did Dionysius teach him?"],"answers":["A sword hanging by a single hair","A person always living in fear cannot be truly happy"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-11-4","title":"Chapter 84: Dion and Dionysius","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXIV, \"Dion and Dionysius,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Plato came to Syracuse and what happened to him and Dion","Map work: Find Athens, Syracuse, and Olympia","Timeline: Add \"Plato visits Syracuse\" at c. 367 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXXIV_DION_AND_DIONYSIUS"}],"questions":["Who was Dion''s teacher in Athens?","How did the crowd at Olympia honor Plato?"],"answers":["Plato","They gave him an olive crown like a champion''s"],"notes":"Ancient sources place Plato being sold into slavery on his earlier visit to Dionysius the Elder; the book merges the stories."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-11-5","title":"Chapter 85: Civil War in Syracuse","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXV, \"Civil War in Syracuse,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Dion returned and trapped Dionysius in the citadel","Map work: Find Syracuse and its harbor","Timeline: Add \"Dion returns to Syracuse\" at 357 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXXV_CIVIL_WAR_IN_SYRACUSE"}],"questions":["Why did Dion come back to Syracuse with an army?","Why did the people start to tire of Dion?"],"answers":["To punish Dionysius, who had forced Dion''s wife to marry another man","He was so strict and harsh in demanding virtue and obedience"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-11-6","title":"Chapter 86: Death of Dion","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXVI, \"Death of Dion,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Dionysius''s letter turned the people against Dion, and how the tyrant ended up","Map work: Find Syracuse and Corinth","Timeline: Add \"Dion murdered\" at 354 BC and \"Dionysius the Younger exiled to Corinth\" at 344 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXXVI_DEATH_OF_DION"}],"questions":["How did Dionysius trick the people into turning against Dion?","What job did Dionysius have at the end of his life in Corinth?"],"answers":["He wrote a letter that made it sound as if Dion wanted to be tyrant","Schoolteacher"]}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u12","title":"Unit 12: Philip of Macedon and Young Alexander (Chapters 87–94)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-12-1","title":"Chapter 87: Philip of Macedon","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXVII, \"Philip of Macedon,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Philip became king and how he built his army","Map work: Find Macedon north of Greece, and Thebes where Philip grew up","Timeline: Add \"Philip II becomes king of Macedon\" at 359 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXXVII_PHILIP_OF_MACEDON"}],"questions":["Where did young Philip live as a hostage, and who looked after him?","What was the Macedonian phalanx?","What did the widow mean by appealing \"from Philip drunk to Philip sober\"?"],"answers":["In Thebes, in the house of Epaminondas","Soldiers packed in a solid block, shields locked, with long spears pointing out in rows","She wanted the king to judge her case again when his head was clear"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-12-2","title":"Chapter 88: Philip Begins His Conquests","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXVIII, \"Philip Begins His Conquests,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Aster''s arrow and Demosthenes'' speeches","Map work: Find Olynthus and Thrace in the north","Timeline: Add \"Demosthenes'' first Philippic\" at 351 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXXVIII_PHILIP_BEGINS_HIS_CONQUESTS"}],"questions":["How did Philip treat Nicanor, who spoke badly of him?","What was written on Aster''s arrow?","What is a \"Philippic\"?"],"answers":["Kindly, with a seat at his table and rich gifts","\"To Philip''s left eye\"","A fierce speech against someone, named after Demosthenes'' speeches against Philip"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-12-3","title":"Chapter 89: The Orator Demosthenes","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXIX, \"The Orator Demosthenes,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Demosthenes taught himself to be a great speaker","Map work: Find Olynthus, which Philip destroyed, and Athens","Timeline: Add \"Philip destroys Olynthus\" at 348 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#LXXXIX_THE_ORATOR_DEMOSTHENES"}],"questions":["Why did Demosthenes practice with pebbles in his mouth by the sea?","Why did he shave half his head?"],"answers":["To speak clearly and loudly enough to be heard over the waves","So he would be too embarrassed to go out and would stay home to study"],"notes":"Good chance to talk about practice and perseverance."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-12-4","title":"Chapter 90: Philip Masters Greece","steps":["Read Chapter XC, \"Philip Masters Greece,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Philip became master of Greece","Map work: Find Phocis, Delphi, and Chaeronea in Boeotia","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Chaeronea\" at 338 BC and \"Philip is murdered\" at 336 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XC","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XC_PHILIP_MASTERS_GREECE"}],"questions":["Why was the Sacred War fought?","How old was Alexander at Chaeronea, and what did he do there?","What did Demosthenes do during the battle?"],"answers":["The Phocians took land set aside for Apollo and robbed the temple at Delphi","Eighteen; he crushed the Theban Sacred Battalion","He ran away and begged for mercy when a bush caught his cloak"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-12-5","title":"Chapter 91: Birth of Alexander","steps":["Read Chapter XCI, \"Birth of Alexander,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the day Alexander was born and how he was raised","Map work: Find Pella in Macedon and Ephesus in Asia Minor","Timeline: Add \"Alexander born; Temple of Diana at Ephesus burned\" at 356 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XCI_BIRTH_OF_ALEXANDER"}],"questions":["What three events were said to happen on the day Alexander was born?","Which books did Alexander keep under his pillow?"],"answers":["Parmenio won a victory, Philip''s horses won at Olympia, and the Temple of Diana at Ephesus burned","The Iliad and the Odyssey"],"notes":"The three events falling on one day is an ancient tradition, not certain fact."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-12-6","title":"Chapter 92: The Steed Bucephalus","steps":["Read Chapter XCII, \"The Steed Bucephalus,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Alexander tamed Bucephalus","Map work: Find Pella and Macedon; Aristotle taught Alexander nearby at Mieza","Timeline: Add \"Aristotle becomes Alexander''s tutor\" at 343 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XCII_THE_STEED_BUCEPHALUS"}],"questions":["What does \"Bucephalus\" mean?","What was frightening the horse?","What did Alexander send Aristotle from his travels?"],"answers":["Ox-head","His own shadow","Collections of animals and plants"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-12-7","title":"Chapter 93: Alexander as King","steps":["Read Chapter XCIII, \"Alexander as King,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what Alexander did when the Greeks rebelled after Philip died","Map work: Find Thrace, Thebes, and Corinth","Timeline: Add \"Alexander becomes king\" at 336 BC and \"Thebes destroyed\" at 335 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XCIII_ALEXANDER_AS_KING"}],"questions":["Why were Demosthenes and the Athenians happy when Philip died?","Which single house did Alexander spare in Thebes?"],"answers":["They thought a young boy could not hold onto power and they would be free","The house of the poet Pindar"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-12-8","title":"Chapter 94: Alexander and Diogenes","steps":["Read Chapter XCIV, \"Alexander and Diogenes,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what happened when Alexander visited Diogenes","Map work: Find Corinth on a map of Greece","Timeline: Add \"Alexander meets Diogenes\" at c. 336 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XCIV_ALEXANDER_AND_DIOGENES"}],"questions":["Why did Diogenes throw away his wooden bowl?","What was Diogenes looking for with his lantern?","What did Diogenes ask Alexander to do for him?"],"answers":["He saw a child drink from its hand and decided he didn''t need it","An honest man","Stand out of his sunshine"],"notes":"The story that they died on the same night is an old tradition."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u13","title":"Unit 13: Alexander the Great (Chapters 95–103)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-13-1","title":"Chapter 95: Alexander''s Brilliant Beginning","steps":["Read Chapter XCV, \"Alexander''s Brilliant Beginning,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Alexander began his war on Persia","Map work: Find the Hellespont, Troy, the Granicus River, Sardis, Ephesus, and Caria","Timeline: Add \"Battle of the Granicus\" at 334 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XCV_ALEXANDERS_BRILLIANT_BEGINNING"}],"questions":["What did Alexander say he kept for himself after giving gifts away?","Who saved Alexander''s life at the Granicus?","What was Aristotle''s \"recipe\" for a good appetite?"],"answers":["His hopes","Clytus, son of his old nurse","A march before daybreak for dinner, and a light dinner for supper"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-13-2","title":"Chapter 96: The Gordian Knot","steps":["Read Chapter XCVI, \"The Gordian Knot,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the Gordian knot and Alexander''s trust in his doctor","Map work: Find Gordium in Phrygia, Tarsus, and the Cydnus River","Timeline: Add \"Alexander cuts the Gordian knot\" at 333 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XCVI_THE_GORDIAN_KNOT"}],"questions":["What was promised to whoever untied the Gordian knot?","What does \"cutting the Gordian knot\" mean today?","What did Alexander do with the warning letter about his doctor?"],"answers":["Rule over all Asia","Solving a hard problem by a bold, direct action","Handed it to the doctor while he drank the medicine"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-13-3","title":"Chapter 97: Alexander''s Royal Captives","steps":["Read Chapter XCVII, \"Alexander''s Royal Captives,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Alexander treated the Persian royal family","Map work: Find Issus, at the corner of Asia Minor and Syria","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Issus\" at 333 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XCVII_ALEXANDERS_ROYAL_CAPTIVES"}],"questions":["Why did the queen mother kneel to the wrong man?","What did Alexander say about Hephaestion?","Why did Sisygambis cry when Alexander offered to teach spinning?"],"answers":["Hephaestion was taller, and they wore plain armor","\"He is my other self\"","Persian royal ladies thought work was for slaves"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-13-4","title":"Chapter 98: Alexander at Jerusalem","steps":["Read Chapter XCVIII, \"Alexander at Jerusalem,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the siege of Tyre and Alexander''s visit to Jerusalem","Map work: Find Damascus, Sidon, Tyre, and Jerusalem","Timeline: Add \"Siege of Tyre\" at 332 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XCVIII_ALEXANDER_AT_JERUSALEM"}],"questions":["How did Alexander reach the island city of Tyre without a fleet?","Who came out to meet Alexander at Jerusalem?"],"answers":["He built a causeway out to the island","The high priest Jaddua with the Levites"],"notes":"The Jerusalem visit comes from the Jewish historian Josephus; many historians doubt it happened. The book tells it as fact, from a religious viewpoint. Tyre''s punishment is grim."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-13-5","title":"Chapter 99: The African Desert","steps":["Read Chapter XCIX, \"The African Desert,\" aloud together","Narration: describe Alexander''s march across the desert and the battle at Arbela","Map work: Find Alexandria in Egypt, the Siwa oasis in the Libyan desert, the Euphrates and Tigris rivers, and Arbela","Timeline: Add \"Alexandria founded\" at 331 BC and \"Battle of Arbela (Gaugamela)\" at 331 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#XCIX_THE_AFRICAN_DESERT"}],"questions":["Which city did Alexander found at the mouth of the Nile?","What is a mirage?","How did the soldiers cross the Tigris?"],"answers":["Alexandria","Seeing water and trees in the desert that are not really there","They swam, holding their shields over their heads"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-13-6","title":"Chapter 100: Death of Darius","steps":["Read Chapter C, \"Death of Darius,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Alexander changed after his victories and how Darius died","Map work: Find Babylon, Susa, and Persepolis","Timeline: Add \"Persepolis burned; death of Darius III\" at 330 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter C","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#C_DEATH_OF_DARIUS"}],"questions":["What did Alexander find at Susa and send back to Athens?","Why did Alexander refuse to share Persia with Darius?","Who killed Darius?"],"answers":["A bronze statue that Xerxes had carried off","He said the world could not have two masters any more than two suns","Bessus, one of his own followers"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-13-7","title":"Chapter 101: Defeat of Porus","steps":["Read Chapter CI, \"Defeat of Porus,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Alexander treated Porus","Map work: Find Persia, then northwest India and the Indus River region","Timeline: Add \"Death of Clytus\" at 328 BC and \"Battle with Porus\" at 326 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CI_DEFEAT_OF_PORUS"}],"questions":["Why did Alexander kill his friend Clytus?","What did Porus answer when asked how he wished to be treated?","What city did Alexander name after his horse?"],"answers":["Clytus scolded him for his drinking, and Alexander struck him in a drunken rage","\"Like a king!\"","Bucephala"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-13-8","title":"Chapter 102: The Return to Babylon","steps":["Read Chapter CII, \"The Return to Babylon,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why Alexander turned back and how his army and fleet returned","Map work: Find the Hyphasis and Indus rivers, the Indian Ocean coast, and the Persian Gulf","Timeline: Add \"Army refuses to go on at the Hyphasis\" at 326 BC and \"Nearchus''s voyage\" at 325 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CII_THE_RETURN_TO_BABYLON"}],"questions":["Why did Alexander have to stop his conquests?","What did Alexander do with the little water his men brought him?","Why was Nearchus''s voyage important?"],"answers":["His soldiers refused to go any farther","Poured it on the sand, since his men could not drink too","It opened a sea route for trade with the East"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-13-9","title":"Chapter 103: Death of Alexander the Great","steps":["Read Chapter CIII, \"Death of Alexander the Great,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Alexander''s last days and what he said about who should rule","Map work: Find Babylon, where Alexander died, and Alexandria, where he was buried","Timeline: Add \"Death of Alexander the Great\" at 323 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CIII_DEATH_OF_ALEXANDER_THE_GREAT"}],"questions":["How old was Alexander when he died?","Whom did Alexander say should have the throne?","Why did Ptolemy want Alexander''s body in Egypt?"],"answers":["Thirty-three","The strongest","An oracle said whoever buried Alexander would be master of his kingdom"],"notes":"The mass weddings actually took place at Susa, and Hephaestion died at Ecbatana; the book moves both to Babylon."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u14","title":"Unit 14: After Alexander (Chapters 104–109)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-14-1","title":"Chapter 104: The Division of the Realm","steps":["Read Chapter CIV, \"The Division of the Realm,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the generals tried to share Alexander''s empire","Map work: Find Macedon, Babylon, and Egypt, and look at how huge the empire was","Timeline: Add \"Generals divide Alexander''s empire\" at 323 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CIV_THE_DIVISION_OF_THE_REALM"}],"questions":["Who were named to follow Alexander as king?","Into how many provinces was the kingdom divided?"],"answers":["His half-brother Arridaeus and his baby son","Thirty-three"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-14-2","title":"Chapter 105: Death of Demosthenes","steps":["Read Chapter CV, \"Death of Demosthenes,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the Greeks revolted and how Demosthenes died","Map work: Find Lamia near Thermopylae and the island of Calauria (Poros), where Demosthenes died","Timeline: Add \"Lamian War\" at 323–322 BC and \"Death of Demosthenes\" at 322 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CV_DEATH_OF_DEMOSTHENES"}],"questions":["What did Phocion say when people rushed to revolt?","How did Demosthenes escape being taken prisoner?"],"answers":["If Alexander is dead today he will still be dead tomorrow, so there is time to think","He took poison hidden in his writing reed"],"notes":"Describes a suicide; summarize if needed."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-14-3","title":"Chapter 106: The Last of the Athenians","steps":["Read Chapter CVI, \"The Last of the Athenians,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what happened to Perdiccas, Phocion, and Alexander''s family","Map work: Find the Nile in Egypt, Athens, and Macedon","Timeline: Add \"Death of Phocion\" at 318 BC and \"Alexander''s son killed\" at c. 310 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CVI_THE_LAST_OF_THE_ATHENIANS"}],"questions":["How did Perdiccas die?","Why is Phocion called the \"Last of the Athenians\"?"],"answers":["His soldiers turned on him after many were eaten by crocodiles crossing the Nile","He was the last great Athenian statesman; no one after him tried to win back the city''s freedom"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-14-4","title":"Chapter 107: The Colossus of Rhodes","steps":["Read Chapter CVII, \"The Colossus of Rhodes,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the Colossus of Rhodes came to be built","Map work: Find the island of Rhodes, then mark the Seven Wonders on a map","Timeline: Add \"Siege of Rhodes\" at 305–304 BC and \"Colossus of Rhodes\" at c. 280–226 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CVII_THE_COLOSSUS_OF_RHODES"}],"questions":["What nickname did Demetrius earn at Rhodes, and what does it mean?","What paid for the Colossus?","How was the Colossus destroyed?"],"answers":["Poliorcetes, \"the city taker\"","Selling the war machines Demetrius left behind","An earthquake knocked it down"],"notes":"The idea that ships sailed between its legs is a later legend. The book''s list of wonders differs a little from the usual seven."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-14-5","title":"Chapter 108: The Battle of Ipsus","steps":["Read Chapter CVIII, \"The Battle of Ipsus,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the Battle of Ipsus decided who got which part of the empire","Map work: Find Ipsus in Asia Minor, Egypt, Syria and Antioch, Thrace, and Macedon","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Ipsus\" at 301 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CVIII_THE_BATTLE_OF_IPSUS"}],"questions":["Who ruled Egypt after Ipsus, and who was the last of his family?","How did the Athenians treat Demetrius after his defeat?"],"answers":["Ptolemy; Cleopatra","They turned against him and made it a crime to speak well of him"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-14-6","title":"Chapter 109: Demetrius and the Athenians","steps":["Read Chapter CIX, \"Demetrius and the Athenians,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Demetrius surprised the Athenians, and how the Gauls were driven off","Map work: Find Athens, Delphi, and Galatia in Asia Minor","Timeline: Add \"Demetrius takes Athens\" at 295 BC and \"Gauls attack Delphi\" at 279 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CIX_DEMETRIUS_AND_THE_ATHENIANS"}],"questions":["What did Demetrius do instead of punishing the Athenians?","Where did the defeated Gauls go?"],"answers":["He forgave them and sent grain to every house","To a part of Asia Minor later called Galatia"]}]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-u15","title":"Unit 15: The Last Days of Free Greece (Chapters 110–115)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-15-1","title":"Chapter 110: The Achæan League","steps":["Read Chapter CX, \"The Achæan League,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Aratus freed Sicyon and captured the fortress of Corinth","Map work: Find Sicyon, Corinth, and Acrocorinth on the Isthmus","Timeline: Add \"Aratus frees Sicyon\" at 251 BC and \"Acrocorinth captured\" at 243 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CX_THE_ACHAEAN_LEAGUE"}],"questions":["Where did young Aratus hide from the tyrant?","How many years in a row was Aratus chosen leader?","Why did Aratus pawn his wife''s jewels?"],"answers":["In the house of the tyrant''s own sister","Thirty-five","To pay the man who showed the secret path up to Acrocorinth"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-15-2","title":"Chapter 111: Division in Sparta","steps":["Read Chapter CXI, \"Division in Sparta,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what Agis tried to change in Sparta and how Chilonis stood by her family","Map work: Find Sparta in Laconia","Timeline: Add \"Agis IV tries to reform Sparta\" at c. 244 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CXI_DIVISION_IN_SPARTA"}],"questions":["What did Agis want to do with the land and money?","How did Chilonis show loyalty?"],"answers":["Divide it equally among the Spartans","She stayed with whichever of her father or husband was in trouble and followed him into exile"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-15-3","title":"Chapter 112: Death of Agis","steps":["Read Chapter CXII, \"Death of Agis,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Agis was betrayed and how Cleomenes carried on his plan","Map work: Find Sparta and Macedon","Timeline: Add \"Death of Agis\" at 241 BC and \"Cleomenes'' reforms\" at 227 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CXII_DEATH_OF_AGIS"}],"questions":["How was Agis caught?","Who taught Cleomenes about Agis''s ideas?","What does the nickname \"Doson\" mean?"],"answers":["Two false friends told the ephors he left the temple each night to bathe","Agiatis, Agis''s widow, who became Cleomenes'' wife","\"Who will give,\" because he promised gifts he never gave"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-15-4","title":"Chapter 113: The War of the Two Leagues","steps":["Read Chapter CXIII, \"The War of the Two Leagues,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Aratus''s mistake let Macedon and then Rome into Greece","Map work: Find Sellasia in Laconia, Aetolia, and Rome in Italy","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Sellasia\" at 222 BC and \"Death of Aratus\" at 213 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CXIII_THE_WAR_OF_THE_TWO_LEAGUES"}],"questions":["Whom did Aratus ask for help against Sparta, and why was it a mistake?","To whom did the Spartans and Aetolians turn for help?"],"answers":["The Macedonian king, who really wanted to rule Greece himself","Rome"],"notes":"Includes the cruel \"statue\" of Nabis and a murder-suicide; preview. The order of events in this chapter is compressed."},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-15-5","title":"Chapter 114: The Last of the Greeks","steps":["Read Chapter CXIV, \"The Last of the Greeks,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Philopoemen, the \"Last of the Greeks\"","Map work: Find Megalopolis, Messenia, and Sparta","Timeline: Add \"Death of Philopoemen\" at 183 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CXIV_THE_LAST_OF_THE_GREEKS"}],"questions":["What happened when a hostess mistook Philopoemen for a servant?","Why is he called the \"Last of the Greeks\"?","What were his last words about?"],"answers":["He calmly split wood as she asked","He was the last to try to keep Greece independent","He was glad the Achaean army had escaped safely"]},{"id":"story-of-the-greeks-15-6","title":"Chapter 115: Greece a Roman Province","steps":["Read Chapter CXV, \"Greece a Roman Province,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Greece went from being \"freed\" by Rome to being ruled by it","Map work: Find Corinth and the Isthmus, Macedon, and Rome","Timeline: Add \"Flamininus proclaims Greek freedom\" at 196 BC and \"Corinth destroyed; Greece under Rome\" at 146 BC. Look back over your whole timeline"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23495/pg23495-images.html#CXV_GREECE_A_ROMAN_PROVINCE"}],"questions":["What did the Roman consul announce at the Isthmian games?","What happened to Corinth?","What name did the Romans give the province of Greece?"],"answers":["That Rome had beaten the king of Macedon and the Greek states were free","It was burned and its art carried off to Rome","Achaia"],"notes":"Corinth was destroyed by the Roman general Mummius, not Aemilius Paulus, who had defeated Macedon in 168 BC."}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"“The Story of the Greeks” by H. A. Guerber (1896), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/23495. Lesson plan by Freerange."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (listing_id, version) do update set content = excluded.content;
insert into public.listings (id, seller_name, seller_kind, seller_verified, title, subjects, grade_min, grade_max, philosophies, format, price, description, includes, lessons, lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement, family_style, lesson_steps, cover, color, esa_eligible, created_at, package_id, package_version, license, attribution, outline) values ('l-lib-story-of-the-romans', 'H. A. Guerber', 'open', false, 'The Story of the Romans', array['history']::text[], 3, 6, array['classical', 'charlotte_mason']::text[], 'online', 0, 'H. A. Guerber''s classic 1896 read-aloud tells the story of Rome in short, lively chapters, from Aeneas and Romulus through Hannibal, Caesar, and the emperors to the fall of the West in 476. It suits the whole family in grades 3–6: each day you read one chapter aloud, your child narrates it back, finds the place on a map, and adds it to a timeline. The full scanned book is free online.', array['102 lessons in 12 units, one chapter per lesson', 'Link to the exact page of the free scanned book for every chapter', 'Narration prompt, map work, and timeline entry for each lesson', 'Comprehension questions with answers', 'Parent notes flagging legend told as history, dated viewpoints, factual errors, and mature content']::text[], 102, 4, 20, 'high', true, '{}'::text[], '🦅', '#E07A5F', false, '2026-11-11T12:00:00.000Z', 'pk-lib-story-of-the-romans', 1, '{"kind":"public_domain"}'::jsonb, '“The Story of the Romans (Eclectic School Readings)” by H. A. Guerber (1896), in the public domain. Free full text: https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog. Lesson plan by Freerange.', '[{"title":"Unit 1: Legends of Aeneas and Romulus (Chapters 1–9)","lessons":["Chapter 1: The First Settlers","Chapter 2: The Escape from the Burning City","Chapter 3: The Clever Trick","Chapter 4: The Boards are Eaten","Chapter 5: The Wolf and the Twins","Chapter 6: Romulus builds Rome","Chapter 7: The Maidens Carried off","Chapter 8: Union of Sabines and Romans","Chapter 9: Death of Romulus"]},{"title":"Unit 2: The Kings of Rome (Chapters 10–21)","lessons":["Chapter 10: The Strange Signs of the Romans","Chapter 11: The Quarrel with Alba","Chapter 12: The Fight between the Horatii and the Curiatii","Chapter 13: Tarquin and the Eagle","Chapter 14: The Roman Youths","Chapter 15: The King Outwitted","Chapter 16: The Murder of Tarquin","Chapter 17: The Ungrateful Children","Chapter 18: The Mysterious Books","Chapter 19: Tarquin''s Poppies","Chapter 20: The Oracle of Delphi","Chapter 21: The Death of Lucretia"]},{"title":"Unit 3: The Young Republic (Chapters 22–31)","lessons":["Chapter 22: The Stern Father","Chapter 23: A Roman Triumph","Chapter 24: A Roman Triumph (Continued)","Chapter 25: The Defense of the Bridge","Chapter 26: The Burnt Hand","Chapter 27: The Twin Gods","Chapter 28: The Wrongs of the Poor","Chapter 29: The Fable of the Stomach","Chapter 30: The Story of Coriolanus","Chapter 31: The Farmer Hero"]},{"title":"Unit 4: Written Laws, the Gauls, and the Samnites (Chapters 32–39)","lessons":["Chapter 32: The New Laws","Chapter 33: The Death of Virginia","Chapter 34: The Plans of a Traitor","Chapter 35: The School-Teacher Punished","Chapter 36: The Invasion of the Gauls","Chapter 37: The Sacred Geese","Chapter 38: Two Heroes of Rome","Chapter 39: The Disaster at the Caudine Forks"]},{"title":"Unit 5: Pyrrhus and the Punic Wars (Chapters 40–48)","lessons":["Chapter 40: Pyrrhus and his Elephants","Chapter 41: The Elephants Routed","Chapter 42: Ancient Ships","Chapter 43: Regulus and the Snake","Chapter 44: Hannibal crosses the Alps","Chapter 45: The Romans Defeated","Chapter 46: The Inventor Archimedes","Chapter 47: The Roman Conquests","Chapter 48: Destruction of Carthage"]},{"title":"Unit 6: Rich and Poor: The Gracchi, Marius, and Sulla (Chapters 49–57)","lessons":["Chapter 49: Roman Amusements","Chapter 50: The Jewels of Cornelia","Chapter 51: The Death of Tiberius Gracchus","Chapter 52: Caius Gracchus","Chapter 53: Jugurtha, King of Numidia","Chapter 54: The Barbarians","Chapter 55: The Social War","Chapter 56: The Flight of Marius","Chapter 57: The Proscription Lists"]},{"title":"Unit 7: Pompey and Caesar (Chapters 58–65)","lessons":["Chapter 58: Sertorius and his Doe","Chapter 59: The Revolt of the Slaves","Chapter 60: Pompey''s Conquests","Chapter 61: The Conspiracy of Catiline","Chapter 62: Caesar''s Conquests","Chapter 63: The Crossing of the Rubicon","Chapter 64: The Battle of Pharsalia","Chapter 65: The Death of Caesar"]},{"title":"Unit 8: From Republic to Empire (Chapters 66–72)","lessons":["Chapter 66: The Second Triumvirate","Chapter 67: The Vision of Brutus","Chapter 68: Antony and Cleopatra","Chapter 69: The Poisonous Snake","Chapter 70: The Augustan Age","Chapter 71: Death of Augustus","Chapter 72: Varus Avenged"]},{"title":"Unit 9: Tiberius to Nero (Chapters 73–80)","lessons":["Chapter 73: Death of Germanicus","Chapter 74: Tiberius Smothered","Chapter 75: The Wild Caligula","Chapter 76: The Wicked Wives of Claudius","Chapter 77: Nero''s First Crimes","Chapter 78: The Christians Persecuted","Chapter 79: Nero''s Cruelty","Chapter 80: Two Short Reigns"]},{"title":"Unit 10: Vespasian to Antoninus Pius (Chapters 81–89)","lessons":["Chapter 81: The Siege of Jerusalem","Chapter 82: The Buried Cities","Chapter 83: The Terrible Banquet","Chapter 84: The Emperor''s Tablets","Chapter 85: The Good Trajan","Chapter 86: Trajan''s Column","Chapter 87: The Great Wall","Chapter 88: Hadrian''s Death","Chapter 89: Antoninus Pius"]},{"title":"Unit 11: Marcus Aurelius to Zenobia (Chapters 90–96)","lessons":["Chapter 90: The Model Pagan","Chapter 91: Another Cruel Emperor","Chapter 92: An Unnatural Son","Chapter 93: The Senate of Women","Chapter 94: The Gigantic Emperor","Chapter 95: Invasion of the Goths","Chapter 96: Zenobia, Queen of Palmyra"]},{"title":"Unit 12: Christian Emperors and the Fall of the West (Chapters 97–102)","lessons":["Chapter 97: A Prophecy Fulfilled","Chapter 98: The First Christian Emperor","Chapter 99: The Roman Empire Divided","Chapter 100: An Emperor''s Penance","Chapter 101: Sieges of Rome","Chapter 102: End of the Empire of the West"]}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set seller_name = excluded.seller_name, seller_kind = excluded.seller_kind, seller_verified = excluded.seller_verified, title = excluded.title, subjects = excluded.subjects, grade_min = excluded.grade_min, grade_max = excluded.grade_max, philosophies = excluded.philosophies, format = excluded.format, price = excluded.price, description = excluded.description, includes = excluded.includes, lessons = excluded.lessons, lessons_per_week = excluded.lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson = excluded.minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement = excluded.parent_involvement, family_style = excluded.family_style, lesson_steps = excluded.lesson_steps, cover = excluded.cover, color = excluded.color, esa_eligible = excluded.esa_eligible, package_id = excluded.package_id, package_version = excluded.package_version, license = excluded.license, attribution = excluded.attribution, outline = excluded.outline;
insert into public.package_releases (listing_id, version, content) values ('l-lib-story-of-the-romans', 1, '{"units":[{"id":"story-of-the-romans-u1","title":"Unit 1: Legends of Aeneas and Romulus (Chapters 1–9)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-romans-1-1","title":"Chapter 1: The First Settlers","steps":["Read Chapter I, \"The First Settlers,\" aloud together (pp. 11–13)","Narration: tell where Italy is, what its land is like, and how its first people came there","Map work: Find Italy (the boot), the Mediterranean and Adriatic seas, the Alps, and the Apennines","Timeline: Begin a Rome timeline: tribes settle in Italy (long ago, no exact date)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter I (p. 11)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n17/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 11–13","questions":["What shape does Italy look like on a map?","Why did people want to live in Italy?","Where did the oldest stories about Rome come from?"],"answers":["A boot","It had a mild climate, rich soil, and beautiful country","Tales told by parents to children for many generations, written down centuries later"],"notes":"Guerber says plainly that the early stories are partly made up. Keep a ''legend'' strip on your timeline for the next chapters."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-1-2","title":"Chapter 2: The Escape from the Burning City","steps":["Read Chapter II, \"The Escape from the Burning City,\" aloud together (pp. 14–16)","Narration: tell how Aeneas escaped from burning Troy and whom he carried with him","Map work: Find Troy in Asia Minor (modern Turkey) and trace a path west across the Aegean toward Italy; find Etruria and Latium","Timeline: Add \"Fall of Troy\" at c. 1200 BC (the traditional date) on the legend strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter II (p. 14)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n20/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 14–16","questions":["How did Aeneas carry his old father out of Troy?","What strange thing were the Trojans told they must do before they could settle?"],"answers":["On his back","Eat the boards their food was served on"],"notes":"Aeneas''s story comes from Virgil''s Aeneid, a poem; Romans treated it as their family history, but it is legend."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-1-3","title":"Chapter 3: The Clever Trick","steps":["Read Chapter III, \"The Clever Trick,\" aloud together (pp. 17–20)","Narration: tell how Dido got land for her city with an oxhide","Map work: Find Sicily and the coast of Tunisia in North Africa where Carthage stood","Timeline: Add \"Dido founds Carthage\" on the legend strip (Carthage was really founded c. 814 BC)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter III (p. 17)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n23/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 17–20","questions":["Who was the woman Aeneas met near Carthage?","How did Dido trick the people into giving her a large piece of land?"],"answers":["His mother, the goddess Venus, in disguise","She cut an oxhide into thin strips and stretched them around a large area"],"notes":"Carthage was a real Phoenician city, but Dido and Aeneas could not have met: Troy fell centuries before Carthage was built."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-1-4","title":"Chapter 4: The Boards are Eaten","steps":["Read Chapter IV, \"The Boards are Eaten,\" aloud together (pp. 20–22)","Narration: tell how Aeneas left Carthage and how the prophecy of the boards came true","Map work: Find the Tiber River and Latium on a map of Italy","Timeline: Add \"Aeneas lands in Latium\" on the legend strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter IV (p. 20)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n26/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 20–22","questions":["What did Iulus eat that made Aeneas shout for joy?","Which king welcomed Aeneas in Latium?"],"answers":["The flat cake he had used as a plate","King Latinus"],"notes":"Dido takes her own life in this chapter. The book says such a deed is wrong; preview with sensitive children."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-1-5","title":"Chapter 5: The Wolf and the Twins","steps":["Read Chapter V, \"The Wolf and the Twins,\" aloud together (pp. 22–25)","Narration: tell the story of Romulus and Remus from their birth to the shepherd''s hut","Map work: Find Alba Longa (south-east of Rome) and the Tiber River","Timeline: Add \"Romulus and Remus\" on the legend strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter V (p. 22)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n28/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 22–25","questions":["Who saved the twin babies after their cradle drifted ashore?","What was the duty of the Vestal Virgins?"],"answers":["A she-wolf (and a woodpecker brought berries), then a shepherd","To keep the sacred fire of Vesta burning"],"notes":"The wolf and twins are legend. The chapter mentions punishment by burial alive, which may upset young listeners."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-1-6","title":"Chapter 6: Romulus builds Rome","steps":["Read Chapter VI, \"Romulus builds Rome,\" aloud together (pp. 25–27)","Narration: tell how the twins chose a name for their city and what happened to Remus","Map work: Find the Palatine Hill on a map of the seven hills of Rome","Timeline: Add \"Rome founded\" at 753 BC (the date the Romans themselves used)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter VI (p. 25)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n31/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 25–27","questions":["How many vultures did Romulus see?","On which hill did Romulus plow the first wall?","What kind of people first came to live in Rome?"],"answers":["Twelve","The Palatine","Hot-headed and lawless men from neighboring lands"],"notes":"753 BC is the Romans'' traditional date. Archaeology shows huts on the Palatine around then, but Romulus himself is legend."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-1-7","title":"Chapter 7: The Maidens Carried off","steps":["Read Chapter VII, \"The Maidens Carried off,\" aloud together (pp. 28–31)","Narration: tell how the Romans got wives and what happened to Tarpeia","Map work: Find the land of the Sabines, north-east of Rome, and the Capitoline Hill","Timeline: Add \"Sabine women taken\" on the legend strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter VII (p. 28)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n34/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 28–31","questions":["What did Tarpeia ask the Sabines to give her?","What did the Tarpeian Rock later become used for?"],"answers":["What they wore on their left arms (she meant gold armlets)","Throwing criminals off to their deaths"],"notes":"The ''carrying off'' of the Sabine women was a kidnapping; you may want to talk about why it was wrong, even in a legend."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-1-8","title":"Chapter 8: Union of Sabines and Romans","steps":["Read Chapter VIII, \"Union of Sabines and Romans,\" aloud together (pp. 31–32)","Narration: tell how the women stopped the battle and how Rome''s government began","Map work: Find the Palatine and Capitoline hills and the low ground between them (the later Forum)","Timeline: Add \"Romans and Sabines unite\" on the legend strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter VIII (p. 31)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n37/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 31–32","questions":["Who stopped the battle between the Romans and Sabines?","What did Romulus call his council of old men?","What were the two main classes of Roman people called?"],"answers":["The Sabine women, who ran between the armies","Senators (the senate)","Patricians (nobles) and plebeians (ordinary people)"]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-1-9","title":"Chapter 9: Death of Romulus","steps":["Read Chapter IX, \"Death of Romulus,\" aloud together (pp. 32–33)","Narration: tell the two stories of how Romulus disappeared","Map work: Find the Quirinal Hill","Timeline: Add \"Romulus disappears\" (legend says 716 BC)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter IX (p. 32)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n38/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 32–33","questions":["How long did Romulus reign, according to the story?","What name did the Romans give Romulus when they worshiped him?"],"answers":["Thirty-seven years","Quirinus"]}]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-u2","title":"Unit 2: The Kings of Rome (Chapters 10–21)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-romans-2-1","title":"Chapter 10: The Strange Signs of the Romans","steps":["Read Chapter X, \"The Strange Signs of the Romans,\" aloud together (pp. 34–36)","Narration: tell what kind of king Numa was and what priests he set up","Map work: Find the Forum, where the round Temple of Vesta and the Temple of Janus stood","Timeline: Add \"Numa Pompilius, second king\" at c. 715 BC (traditional)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter X (p. 34)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n40/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 34–36","questions":["Why were the doors of the Temple of Janus closed in Numa''s time?","Which month is named after Janus?","What did the augurs watch to tell the future?"],"answers":["Because Rome was at peace","January","The weather, the flight of birds, and the sacred geese"],"notes":"Guerber calls Roman religion ''nonsense'' and ''humbug'' and scolds superstitious readers; this reflects her 1890s schoolbook tone."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-2-2","title":"Chapter 11: The Quarrel with Alba","steps":["Read Chapter XI, \"The Quarrel with Alba,\" aloud together (pp. 37–39)","Narration: tell what happened to Numa''s books and why Rome and Alba chose champions","Map work: Find Alba Longa again and measure how close it was to Rome","Timeline: Add \"Tullus Hostilius, third king\" at c. 673 BC (traditional)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XI (p. 37)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n43/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 37–39","questions":["What happened to the books found in Numa''s tomb?","Why was Camilla so unhappy about the fight?"],"answers":["A farmer burned them","Her brothers would fight the man she was going to marry"]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-2-3","title":"Chapter 12: The Fight between the Horatii and the Curiatii","steps":["Read Chapter XII, \"The Fight between the Horatii and the Curiatii,\" aloud together (pp. 39–42)","Narration: tell how the last Horatius won the fight","Map work: Find the Caelian Hill, where the Albans were settled","Timeline: Add \"Horatii and Curiatii; Alba destroyed\" at c. 650 BC (legend)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XII (p. 39)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n45/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 39–42","questions":["What trick did the last Horatius use against the three Curiatii?","Why did Camilla weep when her brother came home?","What disease killed many Romans, including King Tullus?"],"answers":["He pretended to run away so they would spread out, then fought them one by one","He wore the cloak she had made for her dead betrothed","A plague"],"notes":"Horatius kills his own sister in this chapter. The story is legend; it was told to praise duty to Rome."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-2-4","title":"Chapter 13: Tarquin and the Eagle","steps":["Read Chapter XIII, \"Tarquin and the Eagle,\" aloud together (pp. 42–45)","Narration: tell about King Ancus Martius and the strange sign that came to Tarquin","Map work: Find the Etruscan town of Tarquinii, north of Rome","Timeline: Add \"Ancus Martius, fourth king\" at c. 640 BC (traditional)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XIII (p. 42)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n48/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 42–45","questions":["What did King Ancus build because Rome had so many wrongdoers?","What did the eagle do to Tarquin''s cap?","What did Tanaquil say the eagle meant?"],"answers":["A strong stone prison","It snatched it off his head and then put it back","That Tarquin would one day be king of Rome"],"notes":"The book calls Tarquin a Greek; Roman tradition says his father was a Greek from Corinth who settled in Etruria, so he is usually called Etruscan."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-2-5","title":"Chapter 14: The Roman Youths","steps":["Read Chapter XIV, \"The Roman Youths,\" aloud together (pp. 45–46)","Narration: describe how Roman boys trained and how Romans built their roads","Map work: Find Ostia at the mouth of the Tiber, then trace the Appian Way from Rome to Brundisium (Brindisi)","Timeline: Add \"Appian Way begun\" at 312 BC (the book mentions it early, but it was built later)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XIV (p. 45)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n51/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 45–46","questions":["What was the training ground of the Roman youths called?","How did the Romans make their roads last so long?","Finish the saying: \"All roads lead to ___.\""],"answers":["The Field of Mars","They dug a deep trench, packed it with stones, and topped it with big paving blocks","Rome"],"notes":"Activity idea: layer gravel, pebbles, and flat stones in a box to build a model Roman road."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-2-6","title":"Chapter 15: The King Outwitted","steps":["Read Chapter XV, \"The King Outwitted,\" aloud together (pp. 47–49)","Narration: tell what Tarquin the Elder built in Rome and how the augur tested him","Map work: Find the Forum, the Capitoline Hill, and all seven hills of Rome","Timeline: Add \"Tarquin the Elder, fifth king\" at c. 616 BC; add \"Cloaca Maxima (great drain)\""],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XV (p. 47)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n53/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 47–49","questions":["What was the Cloaca Maxima?","Name three of the seven hills of Rome.","What did the lictors carry?"],"answers":["A great drain or sewer across Rome","Any three of: Palatine, Capitoline, Quirinal, Caelian, Aventine, Viminal, Esquiline","Bundles of rods with an ax in the middle"],"notes":"The razor-and-pebble tale is legend. The Cloaca Maxima is real and still carries water today."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-2-7","title":"Chapter 16: The Murder of Tarquin","steps":["Read Chapter XVI, \"The Murder of Tarquin,\" aloud together (pp. 50–51)","Narration: tell how Servius Tullius was chosen and how Tanaquil kept the throne for him","Map work: Point to the royal palace area near the Forum on a map of ancient Rome","Timeline: Add \"Servius Tullius, sixth king\" at c. 578 BC (traditional)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XVI (p. 50)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n56/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 50–51","questions":["What strange sign did Tanaquil see over the baby Servius?","How did Tanaquil hide Tarquin''s death?"],"answers":["A flame hovering over his head without hurting him","She said he was only badly hurt and wanted Servius to rule until he was better"]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-2-8","title":"Chapter 17: The Ungrateful Children","steps":["Read Chapter XVII, \"The Ungrateful Children,\" aloud together (pp. 51–54)","Narration: tell the good things Servius did and how he lost his throne","Map work: Find the Field of Mars, where the census was taken","Timeline: Add \"First census\" at c. 570 BC (traditional)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XVII (p. 51)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n57/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 51–54","questions":["What was a freedman?","How often did Servius count the people?","What was this counting called?"],"answers":["A slave who had been set free","Every five years","Taking a census"],"notes":"This chapter has two murders and the violent death of the king; you may want to summarize those parts for young children."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-2-9","title":"Chapter 18: The Mysterious Books","steps":["Read Chapter XVIII, \"The Mysterious Books,\" aloud together (pp. 54–58)","Narration: tell how the Sibyl sold her books to Tarquin the Proud","Map work: Find Cumae on the coast near Naples, where the Sibyl lived","Timeline: Add \"Tarquin the Proud, seventh king\" at c. 534 BC; add \"Temple of Jupiter on the Capitol\""],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XVIII (p. 54)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n60/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 54–58","questions":["How many books did the Sibyl first bring, and how many did Tarquin finally buy?","Did the price go up or down as the books were burned?","Where were the books kept?"],"answers":["Nine; three","It stayed the same for fewer books","In a stone chest in the Capitol"],"notes":"Tullia driving over her father''s body is a grim scene; skim it if needed. The Sibylline Books were real and kept by priests."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-2-10","title":"Chapter 19: Tarquin''s Poppies","steps":["Read Chapter XIX, \"Tarquin''s Poppies,\" aloud together (pp. 58–60)","Narration: tell how Sextus took the city of Gabii and what the poppies meant","Map work: Find Gabii, east of Rome","Timeline: Add \"Gabii taken by trickery\" (legend)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XIX (p. 58)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n64/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 58–60","questions":["What lie did Sextus tell the people of Gabii?","What did Tarquin do instead of sending a spoken answer?","What did that action mean?"],"answers":["That his father had treated him badly and he needed protection","He knocked the heads off the tallest poppies","Get rid of the leading men of Gabii"],"notes":"This story is almost the same as one Herodotus told about a Greek tyrant, a clue that it is borrowed legend."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-2-11","title":"Chapter 20: The Oracle of Delphi","steps":["Read Chapter XX, \"The Oracle of Delphi,\" aloud together (pp. 60–62)","Narration: tell how Brutus understood the oracle better than the princes","Map work: Find Delphi in Greece and draw the journey from Rome","Timeline: Add \"Brutus at Delphi\" on the legend strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XX (p. 60)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n66/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 60–62","questions":["Why did people think Brutus was stupid?","What was hidden in Brutus''s staff?","Whom did Brutus kiss as \"mother\"?"],"answers":["He pretended to be, so the king would not kill him","Gold","The earth"]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-2-12","title":"Chapter 21: The Death of Lucretia","steps":["Read Chapter XXI, \"The Death of Lucretia,\" aloud together (pp. 62–66)","Narration: tell how the Romans came to drive out their kings","Map work: Find Ardea, south of Rome, and Etruria, where Tarquin fled","Timeline: Add \"Kings driven out of Rome\" at 509 BC (traditional)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXI (p. 62)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n68/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 62–66","questions":["What was Lucretia doing when the princes visited?","What did Brutus swear over Lucretia''s body?","How many kings did Rome have?"],"answers":["Spinning and teaching her maids","That he would drive the Tarquins out of Rome","Seven"],"notes":"Sextus ''insulted'' Lucretia (the old story says he assaulted her), and she takes her own life. Preview and summarize as needed."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-u3","title":"Unit 3: The Young Republic (Chapters 22–31)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-romans-3-1","title":"Chapter 22: The Stern Father","steps":["Read Chapter XXII, \"The Stern Father,\" aloud together (pp. 66–69)","Narration: tell how the Republic began and how Brutus judged his own sons","Map work: Find Veii, the Etruscan city north of Rome","Timeline: Add \"Roman Republic begins; first consuls\" at 509 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXII (p. 66)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n72/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 66–69","questions":["What were the two yearly rulers of the Republic called?","Who told the consuls about the plot to bring back Tarquin?","How did Brutus die?"],"answers":["Consuls","A slave who overheard them while sharpening knives","Fighting one of Tarquin''s sons; they killed each other"],"notes":"Brutus watching his sons'' execution was praised as duty; children may find it harsh. Worth a talk about justice and mercy."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-3-2","title":"Chapter 23: A Roman Triumph","steps":["Read Chapter XXIII, \"A Roman Triumph,\" aloud together (pp. 69–71)","Narration: describe a Roman triumph from start to finish","Map work: Trace the triumph''s route through the Forum and up to the Capitol","Timeline: Add \"Valerius''s triumph\" at c. 509 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXIII (p. 69)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n75/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 69–71","questions":["What pulled the victorious general''s chariot?","What crown did the general wear?","Where did the procession end?"],"answers":["Four white horses","A crown of laurel","At the Capitol (Temple of Jupiter), with a sacrifice"],"notes":"Activity idea: draw or act out a triumph procession."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-3-3","title":"Chapter 24: A Roman Triumph (Continued)","steps":["Read Chapter XXIV, \"A Roman Triumph (Continued),\" aloud together (pp. 71–72)","Narration: tell how the Romans kept a winner humble and how they rewarded soldiers","Map work: No new place today; review the seven hills and the Capitol","Timeline: No new date; note that triumphs went on for about 1,000 years"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXIV (p. 71)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n77/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 71–72","questions":["What did the slave whisper to the general?","What was a civic crown made of, and who earned it?","How was an ovation different from a triumph?"],"answers":["Remember you are nothing but a man","Oak leaves; a man who saved a fellow citizen''s life","The general walked, wore myrtle, and a sheep was sacrificed instead of a bull"]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-3-4","title":"Chapter 25: The Defense of the Bridge","steps":["Read Chapter XXV, \"The Defense of the Bridge,\" aloud together (pp. 73–75)","Narration: tell how Horatius held the bridge","Map work: Find Clusium in Etruria and the Tiber crossing into Rome","Timeline: Add \"Horatius at the bridge; Porsena attacks Rome\" at c. 508 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXV (p. 73)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n79/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 73–75","questions":["Why did the Romans cut down the bridge?","What did Horatius do once the bridge fell?","Why were the people worried about Valerius''s new house?"],"answers":["So Porsena''s army could not cross the Tiber into Rome","He jumped into the Tiber and swam back to Rome","They feared he wanted to make himself king"],"notes":"Horatius may have been a real hero, but the details are legend. Macaulay''s poem \"Horatius\" (in Lays of Ancient Rome) makes a good companion read."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-3-5","title":"Chapter 26: The Burnt Hand","steps":["Read Chapter XXVI, \"The Burnt Hand,\" aloud together (pp. 76–78)","Narration: tell the stories of Mucius Scaevola and of Cloelia","Map work: Trace the Tiber where Cloelia swam across","Timeline: Add \"Mucius Scaevola; Porsena makes peace\" at c. 508 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXVI (p. 76)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n82/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 76–78","questions":["What did Mucius do to show he was not afraid of Porsena?","What does the nickname Scaevola mean?","Why did the Romans send Cloelia back to Porsena?"],"answers":["He held his right hand in the fire until it was burned","Left-handed","Because they had promised to keep the hostages there, and breaking a promise would be dishonorable"],"notes":"Mucius tries to assassinate Porsena and kills the wrong man; the book praises his courage, which is worth discussing."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-3-6","title":"Chapter 27: The Twin Gods","steps":["Read Chapter XXVII, \"The Twin Gods,\" aloud together (pp. 78–81)","Narration: tell about the first dictator and the battle at Lake Regillus","Map work: Find Lake Regillus, south-east of Rome, and the Temple of Castor and Pollux in the Forum","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Lake Regillus\" at c. 496 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXVII (p. 78)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n84/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 78–81","questions":["What was a dictator, and how long did he rule?","Who did the Romans say helped them win at Lake Regillus?","Why did the state have to pay for Valerius''s funeral?"],"answers":["One man given full power while the city was in danger","The twin gods Castor and Pollux on white horses","He had spent all his money on Rome and died poor"],"notes":"The battle was real; the twin gods are legend. The temple''s three columns still stand in the Forum."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-3-7","title":"Chapter 28: The Wrongs of the Poor","steps":["Read Chapter XXVIII, \"The Wrongs of the Poor,\" aloud together (pp. 81–83)","Narration: tell why the plebeians were unhappy and what they did about it","Map work: Find the Sacred Mountain (Mons Sacer), just north-east of Rome","Timeline: Add \"Plebeians leave Rome (first secession)\" at 494 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXVIII (p. 81)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n87/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 81–83","questions":["Why could plebeians end up as slaves?","Who were not allowed to marry each other?","What happened in Rome when the plebeians left?"],"answers":["If they could not pay their debts, the lender could sell them","Patricians and plebeians","There was no one to farm, sell food, or trade"]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-3-8","title":"Chapter 29: The Fable of the Stomach","steps":["Read Chapter XXIX, \"The Fable of the Stomach,\" aloud together (pp. 84–86)","Narration: retell Menenius''s fable of the stomach in your own words","Map work: No new place today; point to the Sacred Mountain again","Timeline: Add \"First tribunes of the people\" at 494 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXIX (p. 84)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n90/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 84–86","questions":["In the fable, who were the stomach and who were the limbs?","What Latin word could the tribunes say to stop a law?","What did the aediles look after?"],"answers":["The patricians were the stomach; the plebeians were the limbs","Veto (\"I forbid\")","Public buildings, clean houses, good food, and the welfare of the poor"],"notes":"Connect this to the U.S. President''s veto, as the book does."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-3-9","title":"Chapter 30: The Story of Coriolanus","steps":["Read Chapter XXX, \"The Story of Coriolanus,\" aloud together (pp. 87–91)","Narration: tell how Coriolanus turned against Rome and why he spared it","Map work: Find the land of the Volscians, south-east of Rome","Timeline: Add \"Coriolanus marches on Rome\" at c. 491 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXX (p. 87)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n93/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 87–91","questions":["Why did the plebeians hate Coriolanus?","Who finally persuaded Coriolanus to spare Rome?","What temple did the Romans build where the women knelt?"],"answers":["He wanted to give them wheat only if they gave up their tribunes","His mother, Veturia","A temple to the Fortune of Women"],"notes":"Coriolanus is half history, half legend. Shakespeare wrote a play about him."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-3-10","title":"Chapter 31: The Farmer Hero","steps":["Read Chapter XXXI, \"The Farmer Hero,\" aloud together (pp. 91–94)","Narration: tell about the 306 Fabii and about Cincinnatus the farmer","Map work: Find the Aequians'' hill country east of Rome; on a U.S. map find Cincinnati, Ohio","Timeline: Add \"Fabii fall\" at 477 BC and \"Cincinnatus dictator\" at 458 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXI (p. 91)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n97/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 91–94","questions":["What was Cincinnatus doing when the senators found him?","What was ''passing under the yoke''?","How long did Cincinnatus keep the power of dictator?"],"answers":["Plowing his field","Making the beaten enemy walk under three spears while the Romans jeered","Only a few days (sixteen in the old story)"],"notes":"George Washington was often called the American Cincinnatus because he gave up power and went home to his farm."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-u4","title":"Unit 4: Written Laws, the Gauls, and the Samnites (Chapters 32–39)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-romans-4-1","title":"Chapter 32: The New Laws","steps":["Read Chapter XXXII, \"The New Laws,\" aloud together (pp. 94–97)","Narration: tell how the Romans got written laws and how Appius Claudius misused his power","Map work: Find Athens in Greece, where the Romans went to study laws","Timeline: Add \"Twelve Tables (written laws)\" at 451–450 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXII (p. 94)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n100/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 94–97","questions":["Why did the Romans send three men to Athens?","Where were the new laws set up for all to read?","What did Appius Claudius plan to do to Virginia?"],"answers":["To study the government and bring back Solon''s laws","On bronze tablets in the Forum","Have his client claim her as a slave so he could take her"],"notes":"Modern historians doubt the trip to Athens, but the Twelve Tables were real Roman laws."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-4-2","title":"Chapter 33: The Death of Virginia","steps":["Read Chapter XXXIII, \"The Death of Virginia,\" aloud together (pp. 97–98)","Narration: tell how the rule of the decemvirs ended","Map work: No new place today; find the Forum where the trial took place","Timeline: Add \"Decemvirs overthrown\" at 449 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXIII (p. 97)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n103/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 97–98","questions":["What false thing did the client say about Virginia?","What happened to the laws on the bronze tablets after the decemvirs fell?"],"answers":["That her mother had been his slave","They stayed in force because they were mostly good and fair"],"notes":"Virginius kills his daughter to keep her from slavery. This is very upsetting; consider summarizing it for young children."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-4-3","title":"Chapter 34: The Plans of a Traitor","steps":["Read Chapter XXXIV, \"The Plans of a Traitor,\" aloud together (pp. 98–101)","Narration: tell what new rights the plebeians won and how Veii was finally taken","Map work: Find Veii and measure its distance from Rome (about 10 miles)","Timeline: Add \"Spurius Maelius killed\" at 439 BC and \"Veii captured by Camillus\" at 396 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXIV (p. 98)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n104/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 98–101","questions":["What did censors do?","Why was Spurius Maelius giving away free grain?","How did Camillus get inside Veii?"],"answers":["Counted the people, shared out public land, chose senators, and punished wrongdoing","He wanted the people to help him become king","His men dug a tunnel into the citadel"]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-4-4","title":"Chapter 35: The School-Teacher Punished","steps":["Read Chapter XXXV, \"The School-Teacher Punished,\" aloud together (pp. 101–104)","Narration: tell what Camillus did with the dishonest schoolteacher","Map work: Find Falerii north of Rome; then find the land of the Gauls north of the Alps and Clusium","Timeline: Add \"Camillus goes into exile\" at c. 391 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXV (p. 101)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n107/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 101–104","questions":["What did the schoolteacher offer to give Camillus?","How did Camillus punish him?","Why was Brennus angry at the Roman ambassadors?"],"answers":["His pupils, the sons of the city''s leading men, as prisoners","He had the boys whip the teacher back into the city","They were supposed to stay neutral but joined the fight against the Gauls"]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-4-5","title":"Chapter 36: The Invasion of the Gauls","steps":["Read Chapter XXXVI, \"The Invasion of the Gauls,\" aloud together (pp. 104–106)","Narration: tell what happened when the Gauls entered Rome","Map work: Find the Allia River just north of Rome","Timeline: Add \"Gauls sack Rome\" at 390 BC (modern date often 387 BC)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXVI (p. 104)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n110/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 104–106","questions":["Where were the old senators and priests sitting when the Gauls came?","What did one Gaul do that started the killing?","Why do we know so little for certain about Rome''s early history?"],"answers":["In their ivory chairs in the Forum","He stroked a priest''s beard","Many records burned when the Gauls set fire to the city"],"notes":"Guerber says here that the earlier stories are mostly not true. Good moment to review the legend strip of your timeline."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-4-6","title":"Chapter 37: The Sacred Geese","steps":["Read Chapter XXXVII, \"The Sacred Geese,\" aloud together (pp. 106–109)","Narration: tell how the geese saved the Capitol and how the Gauls left","Map work: Find the Capitoline Hill and the steep cliff side the Gauls climbed","Timeline: Add \"Geese save the Capitol\" at 390 BC; add \"Manlius executed\" at 384 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXVII (p. 106)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n112/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 106–109","questions":["What woke the Romans when the Gauls climbed the cliff?","What did Brennus throw into the scales?","What did Camillus say about paying with gold?"],"answers":["The sacred geese cackling","His sword","Rome pays its ransom with the sword, not with gold"],"notes":"Roman writers added Camillus''s rescue to soften the shame; most historians think the Gauls left with the gold."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-4-7","title":"Chapter 38: Two Heroes of Rome","steps":["Read Chapter XXXVIII, \"Two Heroes of Rome,\" aloud together (pp. 109–112)","Narration: tell about Curtius and the chasm, and how Valerius got the name Corvus","Map work: Find the spot of the Curtian Lake (Lacus Curtius) in the middle of the Forum","Timeline: Add \"Death of Camillus\" at c. 365 BC; add \"Valerius Corvus fights the Gaul\" at c. 349 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXVIII (p. 109)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n115/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 109–112","questions":["What did Curtius say was Rome''s most precious thing?","What does the Latin word corvus mean?","Why was Camillus called the second founder of Rome?"],"answers":["Its brave men","Raven","He encouraged the people to rebuild Rome after the Gauls burned it"],"notes":"Guerber herself says the Curtius story is probably not true. The raven story is legend too."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-4-8","title":"Chapter 39: The Disaster at the Caudine Forks","steps":["Read Chapter XXXIX, \"The Disaster at the Caudine Forks,\" aloud together (pp. 112–115)","Narration: tell about Torquatus, the Caudine Forks, and Decius","Map work: Find Samnium in the mountains east and south-east of Latium, and the Caudine Forks pass","Timeline: Add \"Torquatus executes his son\" at 340 BC and \"Caudine Forks\" at 321 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXIX (p. 112)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n118/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 112–115","questions":["How did Torquatus get his name?","What happened to the Roman army at the Caudine Forks?","Who were the Samnites?"],"answers":["He took a gold neck ring (torque) from a Gaul he killed","It was trapped in a mountain pass and had to surrender and pass under the yoke","A brave mountain people east and south-east of Latium who fought Rome three times"],"notes":"Another father executes his son for disobeying; the Romans admired this, but it is worth talking about."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-u5","title":"Unit 5: Pyrrhus and the Punic Wars (Chapters 40–48)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-romans-5-1","title":"Chapter 40: Pyrrhus and his Elephants","steps":["Read Chapter XL, \"Pyrrhus and his Elephants,\" aloud together (pp. 115–118)","Narration: tell why Pyrrhus came to Italy and what happened at Heraclea","Map work: Find Tarentum (Taranto) in the heel of Italy and Epirus across the Adriatic in Greece","Timeline: Add \"Pyrrhus wins at Heraclea\" at 280 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XL (p. 115)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n121/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 115–118","questions":["Why were the Romans so frightened at Heraclea?","What did Pyrrhus say about his costly victory?","What did Pyrrhus notice about the dead Roman soldiers?"],"answers":["They had never seen fighting elephants before","One more victory like that and he would go home without an army","They all had their wounds in front, so none had run away"],"notes":"A \"Pyrrhic victory\" means a win that costs so much it is almost a loss."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-5-2","title":"Chapter 41: The Elephants Routed","steps":["Read Chapter XLI, \"The Elephants Routed,\" aloud together (pp. 119–121)","Narration: tell how Fabricius showed his honesty and how the Romans beat the elephants","Map work: Find Asculum in southern Italy and Sicily across the strait","Timeline: Add \"Pyrrhus leaves Italy\" at 275 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLI (p. 119)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n125/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 119–121","questions":["What did Pyrrhus try to scare Fabricius with?","What did Fabricius do with the doctor''s letter?","How did the Romans turn the elephants back?"],"answers":["An elephant hidden behind a curtain that suddenly trumpeted","He sent it to Pyrrhus to warn him","They threw burning balls of rosin and flax at them"]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-5-3","title":"Chapter 42: Ancient Ships","steps":["Read Chapter XLII, \"Ancient Ships,\" aloud together (pp. 121–124)","Narration: describe ancient warships and tell how Rome built its first navy","Map work: Find Mylae on the north coast of Sicily, and Syracuse","Timeline: Add \"First Punic War begins\" at 264 BC and \"Victory at Mylae\" at 260 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLII (p. 121)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n127/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 121–124","questions":["What was a trireme?","What did the Romans use as a model for their ships?","What were the ''rostra''?"],"answers":["A galley with three rows of rowers on each side","A wrecked Carthaginian quinquereme","The bronze beaks of captured ships, put up in the Forum"],"notes":"The speakers'' platform in the Forum was called the Rostra after these beaks; that is where our word ''rostrum'' comes from."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-5-4","title":"Chapter 43: Regulus and the Snake","steps":["Read Chapter XLIII, \"Regulus and the Snake,\" aloud together (pp. 124–128)","Narration: tell about Regulus in Africa, the giant snake, and his promise","Map work: Find Carthage in North Africa and the short sea crossing from Sicily","Timeline: Add \"Regulus captured\" at 255 BC; add \"First Punic War ends\" at 241 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLIII (p. 124)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n130/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 124–128","questions":["What did ''Punic faith'' come to mean?","How did the Romans kill the great snake?","Why did Regulus go back to Carthage?"],"answers":["Treachery, broken promises","They threw stones at it with a catapult (ballista)","He had promised to return if peace was not made, and he kept his word"],"notes":"Guerber jokes that the 120-foot snake is a ''snake story.'' The story of Regulus''s return may also be a later legend."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-5-5","title":"Chapter 44: Hannibal crosses the Alps","steps":["Read Chapter XLIV, \"Hannibal crosses the Alps,\" aloud together (pp. 128–130)","Narration: tell how the Second Punic War began and how Hannibal crossed the Alps","Map work: Trace Hannibal''s route from Spain over the Pyrenees, across Gaul (France), and over the Alps into Italy","Timeline: Add \"Hannibal crosses the Alps\" at 218 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLIV (p. 128)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n134/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 128–130","questions":["What promise had Hannibal made as a boy?","Which Spanish city did Hannibal attack?","How long did it take to cross the Alps?"],"answers":["To fight Rome as long as he lived","Saguntum","About two weeks"],"notes":"The book does not mention it, but Hannibal brought war elephants over the Alps; children love to know that."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-5-6","title":"Chapter 45: The Romans Defeated","steps":["Read Chapter XLV, \"The Romans Defeated,\" aloud together (pp. 130–132)","Narration: tell about Hannibal''s victories and how Fabius fought him","Map work: Find the Trebia River, Lake Trasimene, Cannae, and Capua","Timeline: Add \"Lake Trasimene\" at 217 BC and \"Battle of Cannae\" at 216 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLV (p. 130)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n136/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 130–132","questions":["What was the ''Fabian policy''?","How did Hannibal escape from the mountain pass?","Why did Hannibal''s army get weak at Capua?"],"answers":["Avoiding big battles and wearing the enemy down by cutting off supplies","He tied torches to oxen''s horns and stampeded them through the Romans","Easy living made the soldiers fat and lazy"]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-5-7","title":"Chapter 46: The Inventor Archimedes","steps":["Read Chapter XLVI, \"The Inventor Archimedes,\" aloud together (pp. 133–137)","Narration: tell about Archimedes and how the Second Punic War ended","Map work: Find Syracuse in Sicily, Numidia west of Carthage, and Zama","Timeline: Add \"Syracuse falls; Archimedes killed\" at 212 BC and \"Battle of Zama\" at 202 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLVI (p. 133)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n139/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 133–137","questions":["Name one invention of Archimedes.","How did Archimedes die?","What name was Scipio given after his victory?"],"answers":["Any one: war catapults, giant grappling hooks, burning mirrors, the water screw","A Roman soldier killed him while he was working on a problem","Africanus"],"notes":"The burning mirrors are probably legend. Hasdrubal''s head being thrown into the camp is a gruesome detail."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-5-8","title":"Chapter 47: The Roman Conquests","steps":["Read Chapter XLVII, \"The Roman Conquests,\" aloud together (pp. 137–139)","Narration: tell how Rome defeated Macedon and Syria, and what Hannibal said about great generals","Map work: Find Macedonia, Greece, Syria, Asia Minor, and Pydna","Timeline: Add \"Greeks declared free\" at 196 BC and \"Battle of Pydna\" at 168 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLVII (p. 137)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n143/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 137–139","questions":["Whom did Hannibal name as the greatest general?","How did Hannibal die?","What happened to Macedonia in the end?"],"answers":["Alexander the Great","He took poison he kept in a ring","It became a Roman province"],"notes":"The conversation between Hannibal and Scipio is a famous story but may never have happened. Hannibal''s suicide is mentioned."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-5-9","title":"Chapter 48: Destruction of Carthage","steps":["Read Chapter XLVIII, \"Destruction of Carthage,\" aloud together (pp. 139–142)","Narration: tell how Carthage defended itself and how it was destroyed","Map work: Find Carthage, Corinth in Greece, and Numantia in Spain","Timeline: Add \"Carthage and Corinth destroyed\" at 146 BC and \"Numantia falls\" at 133 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLVIII (p. 139)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n145/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 139–142","questions":["What did Cato say at the end of every speech?","How did the women of Carthage help defend the city?","Which two other cities were also destroyed?"],"answers":["Carthage must be destroyed!","They gave their jewelry and cut off their hair for ropes and bowstrings","Corinth and Numantia"],"notes":"The tale that the Romans sowed salt on Carthage is a modern invention; it is not in this book."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-u6","title":"Unit 6: Rich and Poor: The Gracchi, Marius, and Sulla (Chapters 49–57)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-romans-6-1","title":"Chapter 49: Roman Amusements","steps":["Read Chapter XLIX, \"Roman Amusements,\" aloud together (pp. 142–145)","Narration: tell how Roman life changed and describe the gladiator shows","Map work: Mark on a map all the Roman provinces named: Spain, Africa, Asia Minor, Greece, Macedonia, northern Italy","Timeline: Add \"Rome rules the Mediterranean\" at c. 146 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLIX (p. 142)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n148/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 142–145","questions":["Who taught the Roman children to read Greek?","Where did the word ''gladiator'' come from?","Why did the lion not attack Androclus?"],"answers":["Greek slaves","From gladius, the Latin word for a short sword","Androclus had once pulled a thorn from its paw"],"notes":"The Pantheon was built much later (27 BC, rebuilt about AD 125), and the Androclus tale is set in the time of the emperors. Gladiator fights are described plainly."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-6-2","title":"Chapter 50: The Jewels of Cornelia","steps":["Read Chapter L, \"The Jewels of Cornelia,\" aloud together (pp. 146–148)","Narration: tell how the gap between rich and poor grew, and what Cornelia called her jewels","Map work: No new place today; review Rome and the provinces","Timeline: Add \"Tiberius Gracchus elected tribune\" at 133 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter L (p. 146)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n152/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 146–148","questions":["Why did so many poor people crowd into Rome?","What did the Licinian Law say?","Whom did Cornelia call her jewels?"],"answers":["Rich men owned the land and worked it with slaves, so the poor had no land or work","No citizen could own more than a certain amount of land","Her sons, Tiberius and Caius Gracchus"]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-6-3","title":"Chapter 51: The Death of Tiberius Gracchus","steps":["Read Chapter LI, \"The Death of Tiberius Gracchus,\" aloud together (pp. 148–150)","Narration: tell what Tiberius Gracchus tried to do for the poor and what happened to him","Map work: Find Pergamum in Asia Minor, whose king left his wealth to Rome","Timeline: Add \"Tiberius Gracchus killed\" at 133 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LI (p. 148)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n154/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 148–150","questions":["What did Tiberius want to do with the extra public land?","What signal did Tiberius give, and how did the senators twist its meaning?"],"answers":["Divide it among the poor citizens","He raised his hands to his head to warn his friends; they said he was asking for a crown"],"notes":"This chapter marks the start of a century of political violence in Rome."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-6-4","title":"Chapter 52: Caius Gracchus","steps":["Read Chapter LII, \"Caius Gracchus,\" aloud together (pp. 151–152)","Narration: tell about Caius Gracchus and his mother Cornelia","Map work: Find the Aventine Hill, where Caius''s followers gathered","Timeline: Add \"Caius Gracchus killed\" at 121 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LII (p. 151)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n157/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 151–152","questions":["What did Caius do to help hungry people?","What was written on Cornelia''s statue?"],"answers":["Got the price of grain lowered","Cornelia, the mother of the Gracchi"],"notes":"There are very gory details about Caius''s head; you may wish to skip that paragraph when reading aloud."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-6-5","title":"Chapter 53: Jugurtha, King of Numidia","steps":["Read Chapter LIII, \"Jugurtha, King of Numidia,\" aloud together (pp. 153–155)","Narration: tell how Jugurtha bribed the Romans and how he was finally caught","Map work: Find Numidia (modern Algeria) in North Africa","Timeline: Add \"War with Jugurtha\" at 112–105 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LIII (p. 153)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n159/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 153–155","questions":["How did Jugurtha keep the Roman senators from punishing him?","What did Jugurtha call Rome as he left?","How did the Roman soldier climb the steep fortress rock?"],"answers":["He bribed them with rich presents","A city for sale to anyone rich enough to buy it","He stuck his bare toes in snail holes and cracks"]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-6-6","title":"Chapter 54: The Barbarians","steps":["Read Chapter LIV, \"The Barbarians,\" aloud together (pp. 155–158)","Narration: tell how Marius stopped the Cimbri and Teutons","Map work: Find the Alps, southern Gaul (France), and Lombardy in northern Italy","Timeline: Add \"Marius defeats the Teutons and Cimbri\" at 102–101 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LIV (p. 155)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n161/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 155–158","questions":["Who were Marius and Sulla?","Why did the Cimbri tie themselves together?","How many times had Marius been consul after this victory?"],"answers":["Two ambitious Roman generals and rivals; Marius was poor-born, Sulla a patrician","To hold each other up in battle","Six times"],"notes":"The book calls these peoples ''barbarians'' and describes the women killing their children; consider skipping that sentence."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-6-7","title":"Chapter 55: The Social War","steps":["Read Chapter LV, \"The Social War,\" aloud together (pp. 158–160)","Narration: tell why the Italian allies went to war with Rome","Map work: Shade the parts of Italy held by Rome''s Italian allies","Timeline: Add \"Social War\" at 91–88 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LV (p. 158)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n164/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 158–160","questions":["What did the Italian allies want?","Why is it called the Social War?","How did the war end?"],"answers":["The rights of Roman citizens","The allies were called socii in Latin","The Italians were given most of the rights they asked for"]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-6-8","title":"Chapter 56: The Flight of Marius","steps":["Read Chapter LVI, \"The Flight of Marius,\" aloud together (pp. 160–162)","Narration: tell how Marius had to flee and how he escaped death","Map work: Find Minturnae on the coast south of Rome, then the ruins of Carthage","Timeline: Add \"Sulla marches on Rome\" at 88 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LVI (p. 160)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n166/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 160–162","questions":["What did Sulla do instead of giving up his army?","Where did old Marius hide?","Why did the slave run away from Marius''s cell?"],"answers":["He marched his army into Rome","In a marsh, up to his chin in mud","Marius''s fierce look and words frightened him"],"notes":"Sulla''s march on Rome was the first time a Roman army took the city; Caesar would later follow his example."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-6-9","title":"Chapter 57: The Proscription Lists","steps":["Read Chapter LVII, \"The Proscription Lists,\" aloud together (pp. 162–164)","Narration: tell what Marius and Sulla did when each came back to power","Map work: Find Pontus in Asia Minor, where Sulla fought Mithridates","Timeline: Add \"Death of Marius\" at 86 BC and \"Sulla dictator; proscriptions\" at 82 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LVII (p. 162)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n168/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 162–164","questions":["What were the proscription lists?","What title did Sulla force the Romans to give him?","What did Sulla call himself on his tomb?"],"answers":["Posted lists of people Sulla wanted killed, with rewards for killing them","Dictator for life","Sulla the Fortunate"],"notes":"This chapter describes mass killings; summarize as needed."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-u7","title":"Unit 7: Pompey and Caesar (Chapters 58–65)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-romans-7-1","title":"Chapter 58: Sertorius and his Doe","steps":["Read Chapter LVIII, \"Sertorius and his Doe,\" aloud together (pp. 165–167)","Narration: tell about Sertorius and his white doe, and how Pompey ended the war in Spain","Map work: Find Spain''s mountains and Sardinia","Timeline: Add \"Sertorius killed; war in Spain ends\" at 72 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LVIII (p. 165)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n171/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 165–167","questions":["Why did the Spaniards think the gods favored Sertorius?","What did Pompey do with Sertorius''s secret papers?"],"answers":["A white doe, sacred to Diana, followed him everywhere","He burned them without reading them"],"notes":"The book calls the Spaniards ''credulous''; Sertorius likely used the doe on purpose to win their trust."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-7-2","title":"Chapter 59: The Revolt of the Slaves","steps":["Read Chapter LIX, \"The Revolt of the Slaves,\" aloud together (pp. 167–170)","Narration: tell the story of Spartacus and the slave revolt","Map work: Find Capua and Mount Vesuvius, then the road from Capua to Rome","Timeline: Add \"Revolt of Spartacus\" at 73–71 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LIX (p. 167)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n173/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 167–170","questions":["Who was Spartacus?","Why did Spartacus kill his horse before the battle?","Who won the battle, and who claimed the credit?"],"answers":["A Thracian gladiator who led the slave revolt","If he won he would have better horses; if he lost he would need none","Crassus won; Pompey claimed the credit"],"notes":"The crucifixion of 6,000 rebels is mentioned in the last line."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-7-3","title":"Chapter 60: Pompey''s Conquests","steps":["Read Chapter LX, \"Pompey''s Conquests,\" aloud together (pp. 170–172)","Narration: tell how Pompey cleared the sea of pirates and conquered the East","Map work: Find Pontus, Syria, Phoenicia, Judea, and Jerusalem","Timeline: Add \"Pompey clears the pirates\" at 67 BC and \"Pompey takes Jerusalem\" at 63 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LX (p. 170)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n176/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 170–172","questions":["How long did it take Pompey to clear the pirates?","What did Pompey advise doing with the captured pirates?","How did moonlight help Pompey win?"],"answers":["About four months","Send them out to start new colonies","His soldiers'' long shadows made the enemy think giants were coming"],"notes":"The book''s comment that Pompey''s power fell as a punishment for entering the Holy of Holies is a view, not history."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-7-4","title":"Chapter 61: The Conspiracy of Catiline","steps":["Read Chapter LXI, \"The Conspiracy of Catiline,\" aloud together (pp. 172–175)","Narration: tell about Catiline''s plot and how the First Triumvirate began","Map work: Find Gaul, the province Caesar asked to govern","Timeline: Add \"Catiline''s conspiracy\" at 63 BC and \"First Triumvirate\" at 60 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXI (p. 172)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n178/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 172–175","questions":["Who discovered Catiline''s plot?","Who were the three men of the Triumvirate?","What did Caesar say he would rather be?"],"answers":["Cicero, the consul","Julius Caesar, Pompey, and Crassus","First in a village than second in Rome"]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-7-5","title":"Chapter 62: Caesar''s Conquests","steps":["Read Chapter LXII, \"Caesar''s Conquests,\" aloud together (pp. 176–178)","Narration: tell about Caesar''s wars in Gaul and Britain and what happened to Crassus","Map work: Find Gaul (France), Belgium, Switzerland, Britain, and the Channel crossing from Calais to Deal","Timeline: Add \"Caesar lands in Britain\" at 55 BC and \"Crassus killed by the Parthians\" at 53 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXII (p. 176)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n182/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 176–178","questions":["What did Caesar write about his wars?","How did the Britons look when Caesar landed?","Why did Caesar''s soldiers love him?"],"answers":["An account of the Gallic Wars, still read by Latin students","Dressed in animal skins with their skin painted blue","He spoke kindly, knew them by name, and led by example"]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-7-6","title":"Chapter 63: The Crossing of the Rubicon","steps":["Read Chapter LXIII, \"The Crossing of the Rubicon,\" aloud together (pp. 179–181)","Narration: tell why Caesar crossed the Rubicon and what it meant","Map work: Find the Rubicon River in northern Italy and Brundisium in the heel","Timeline: Add \"Caesar crosses the Rubicon\" at 49 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXIII (p. 179)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n185/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 179–181","questions":["Why was it against the law for Caesar to cross the Rubicon with his army?","What did Caesar cry as he crossed?","What does ''crossing the Rubicon'' mean today?"],"answers":["A governor had to leave his army behind before entering Roman territory","The die is cast!","Making a bold decision that cannot be undone"]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-7-7","title":"Chapter 64: The Battle of Pharsalia","steps":["Read Chapter LXIV, \"The Battle of Pharsalia,\" aloud together (pp. 181–184)","Narration: tell how Caesar beat Pompey at Pharsalia and what happened to Pompey","Map work: Find Dyrrachium (on the Adriatic coast of modern Albania), Thessaly, Pharsalus, and Egypt","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Pharsalus\" and \"Pompey killed in Egypt\" at 48 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXIV (p. 181)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n187/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 181–184","questions":["What did Caesar tell the frightened fishermen in the storm?","What did Caesar do with Pompey''s papers?","Where was Pompey killed?"],"answers":["Go on boldly, for you carry Caesar and his fortunes","He burned them without reading them","On the shore of Egypt, as he landed"],"notes":"The king who had Pompey killed is usually numbered Ptolemy XIII today (the book says XII)."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-7-8","title":"Chapter 65: The Death of Caesar","steps":["Read Chapter LXV, \"The Death of Caesar,\" aloud together (pp. 184–187)","Narration: tell what Caesar did after Pharsalia and how he died","Map work: Find Alexandria in Egypt and Pontus in Asia Minor","Timeline: Add \"Caesar killed (the Ides of March)\" at 44 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXV (p. 184)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n190/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 184–187","questions":["What three Latin words did Caesar send after his quick victory?","Which month is named after Julius Caesar?","Who were the two leaders of the plot against Caesar?"],"answers":["Veni, vidi, vici (I came, I saw, I conquered)","July","Brutus and Cassius"],"notes":"Historians still debate how much of the Library of Alexandria burned in Caesar''s war; it was not all lost at once. \"And you, too, Brutus\" is the famous line from Shakespeare''s play."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-u8","title":"Unit 8: From Republic to Empire (Chapters 66–72)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-romans-8-1","title":"Chapter 66: The Second Triumvirate","steps":["Read Chapter LXVI, \"The Second Triumvirate,\" aloud together (pp. 188–189)","Narration: tell what happened in Rome right after Caesar''s death","Map work: Find Macedonia and Syria, where Brutus and Cassius went","Timeline: Add \"Second Triumvirate\" at 43 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXVI (p. 188)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n194/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 188–189","questions":["Why did the senators slip away while Brutus was speaking?","What did Caesar''s will give to the people?","Who were the three men of the Second Triumvirate?"],"answers":["They were not sure how the people would take Caesar''s death","His gardens for public use and a sum of money to every poor man","Mark Antony, Octavius, and Lepidus"],"notes":"Shakespeare''s Julius Caesar (Antony''s speech, ''Friends, Romans, countrymen'') pairs well with this chapter for older children."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-8-2","title":"Chapter 67: The Vision of Brutus","steps":["Read Chapter LXVII, \"The Vision of Brutus,\" aloud together (pp. 189–192)","Narration: tell about the new proscriptions and the battle of Philippi","Map work: Find Philippi in northern Greece and Athens","Timeline: Add \"Cicero killed\" at 43 BC and \"Battle of Philippi\" at 42 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXVII (p. 189)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n195/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 189–192","questions":["Which famous orator was killed in the proscriptions?","What did the specter tell Brutus?","How did Lucilius help Brutus?"],"answers":["Cicero","That it was his evil spirit and Brutus would see it again at Philippi","He pretended to be Brutus so the real Brutus could escape"],"notes":"Cicero''s death is described in grisly detail, and Brutus and Cassius take their own lives. The ghost is a legend from Plutarch."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-8-3","title":"Chapter 68: Antony and Cleopatra","steps":["Read Chapter LXVIII, \"Antony and Cleopatra,\" aloud together (pp. 192–195)","Narration: tell how Antony came under Cleopatra''s spell and what happened at Actium","Map work: Find Egypt, Sicily, and Actium on the west coast of Greece","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Actium\" at 31 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXVIII (p. 192)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n198/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 192–195","questions":["What happened to Lepidus?","What did Cleopatra do with a priceless pearl?","What did Antony do when Cleopatra''s ship fled?"],"answers":["His power was taken away, and he was made chief priest","Dissolved it in vinegar and drank it","He left his fleet and followed her"],"notes":"The book''s picture of Cleopatra comes from her enemies'' writings; she was also a clever ruler who spoke many languages."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-8-4","title":"Chapter 69: The Poisonous Snake","steps":["Read Chapter LXIX, \"The Poisonous Snake,\" aloud together (pp. 195–197)","Narration: tell how Antony and Cleopatra died and what this meant for Rome","Map work: Find Alexandria in Egypt again","Timeline: Add \"Deaths of Antony and Cleopatra; Egypt becomes Roman\" at 30 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXIX (p. 195)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n201/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 195–197","questions":["Why did Cleopatra not want to go to Rome?","What kind of snake bit Cleopatra?","What ended and what began with Antony''s death?"],"answers":["She would have to walk in Octavius''s triumph","An asp","The Roman Republic ended and the Roman Empire began"],"notes":"This whole chapter is about suicide. Preview it, or summarize for younger children."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-8-5","title":"Chapter 70: The Augustan Age","steps":["Read Chapter LXX, \"The Augustan Age,\" aloud together (pp. 197–200)","Narration: tell how Octavius became Augustus and what the Augustan Age was famous for","Map work: Find Bethlehem in Judea on a map of the Roman Empire","Timeline: Add \"Augustus, first emperor\" at 27 BC; add \"Birth of Jesus\" at c. 4 BC"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXX (p. 197)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n203/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 197–200","questions":["Name one great writer of the Augustan Age.","What did people say Augustus did to the city of Rome?","What did Augustus do when Cinna plotted to kill him?"],"answers":["Any of: Virgil, Ovid, Horace, Livy, Cornelius Nepos","He found it brick and left it marble","He forgave him"],"notes":"Scholars date Jesus''s birth to about 6–4 BC, a little before the year 1 used in our calendar. The book is written from a Christian viewpoint."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-8-6","title":"Chapter 71: Death of Augustus","steps":["Read Chapter LXXI, \"Death of Augustus,\" aloud together (pp. 201–203)","Narration: tell about the lost legions of Varus and the last days of Augustus","Map work: Find the Rhine River and the Teutoburg Forest in Germany, and the island of Rhodes","Timeline: Add \"Varus''s legions lost\" at AD 9 and \"Death of Augustus; Tiberius emperor\" at AD 14"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXI (p. 201)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n207/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 201–203","questions":["Which month is named after Augustus?","What did Augustus cry out at night?","How did the astrologer Thrasyllus save his life?"],"answers":["August","Varus, Varus, give me back my legions!","He told Tiberius he would be emperor and said he himself was in danger"]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-8-7","title":"Chapter 72: Varus Avenged","steps":["Read Chapter LXXII, \"Varus Avenged,\" aloud together (pp. 203–206)","Narration: tell about Germanicus and how he avenged Varus","Map work: Find the Rhine frontier and Fidenae, just north of Rome","Timeline: Add \"Germanicus defeats Arminius\" at AD 16"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXII (p. 203)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n209/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 203–206","questions":["How did Caligula get his name?","What did Germanicus do at the place where Varus''s army died?","What did the coin made for Germanicus''s triumph say?"],"answers":["From the little soldier''s boots he wore (caligae)","He gathered and buried the bones of the dead soldiers","The return of good luck"]}]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-u9","title":"Unit 9: Tiberius to Nero (Chapters 73–80)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-romans-9-1","title":"Chapter 73: Death of Germanicus","steps":["Read Chapter LXXIII, \"Death of Germanicus,\" aloud together (pp. 206–208)","Narration: tell how Germanicus died and how Sejanus gained power","Map work: Find Antioch in Syria and the island of Capri","Timeline: Add \"Death of Germanicus\" at AD 19"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXIII (p. 206)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n212/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 206–208","questions":["How did Germanicus die?","What was the Praetorian Guard?","Where did Tiberius go to live?"],"answers":["He drank poison given by Piso","The emperor''s bodyguard","The island of Capri"],"notes":"Ancient writers blamed Tiberius for Germanicus''s death, but it was never proved; the book states it as rumor."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-9-2","title":"Chapter 74: Tiberius Smothered","steps":["Read Chapter LXXIV, \"Tiberius Smothered,\" aloud together (pp. 208–211)","Narration: tell how Sejanus fell and how Tiberius died","Map work: Find Jerusalem in Judea","Timeline: Add \"Fall of Sejanus\" at AD 31; add \"Crucifixion of Jesus\" at c. AD 30–33; add \"Death of Tiberius\" at AD 37"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXIV (p. 208)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n214/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 208–211","questions":["How did Tiberius trick Sejanus?","Who was the Roman governor in Jerusalem when Jesus was crucified?","How did Tiberius die?"],"answers":["He sent Sejanus a friendly letter but sent the senate an order to arrest him","Pontius Pilate","He was smothered under mattresses and pillows"],"notes":"The story of Pilate''s report and Tiberius asking to make Jesus a Roman god comes from early Christian writers, not from Roman records."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-9-3","title":"Chapter 75: The Wild Caligula","steps":["Read Chapter LXXV, \"The Wild Caligula,\" aloud together (pp. 211–215)","Narration: tell some of the crazy and cruel things Caligula did","Map work: Find the Rhine and the coast facing Britain where Caligula collected shells","Timeline: Add \"Caligula emperor\" at AD 37–41"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXV (p. 211)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n217/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 211–215","questions":["What was the name of Caligula''s favorite horse?","What did Caligula bring back as ''spoils of the ocean''?","How long did Caligula rule?"],"answers":["Incitatus","Seashells","About three years (nearly four)"],"notes":"Many of these stories come from writers who hated Caligula and may be exaggerated."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-9-4","title":"Chapter 76: The Wicked Wives of Claudius","steps":["Read Chapter LXXVI, \"The Wicked Wives of Claudius,\" aloud together (pp. 215–218)","Narration: tell how Claudius became emperor and what his wives did","Map work: Find Britain and Dalmatia (on the east coast of the Adriatic)","Timeline: Add \"Claudius emperor\" at AD 41 and \"Romans conquer southern Britain\" at AD 43"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXVI (p. 215)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n221/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 215–218","questions":["Where did the soldiers find Claudius?","Which land partly became a Roman province under Claudius?","How did Agrippina get rid of Claudius?"],"answers":["Hiding behind a curtain in the palace","Britain","She had him eat poisoned mushrooms"],"notes":"The book calls Claudius half-witted; modern historians see him as a capable scholar and ruler with a disability. Arria''s suicide is described."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-9-5","title":"Chapter 77: Nero''s First Crimes","steps":["Read Chapter LXXVII, \"Nero''s First Crimes,\" aloud together (pp. 218–221)","Narration: tell how Nero began his reign and how he treated his own family","Map work: Find the Bay of Naples, where Agrippina''s ship fell apart","Timeline: Add \"Nero emperor\" at AD 54; add \"Agrippina killed\" at AD 59"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXVII (p. 218)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n224/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 218–221","questions":["Who were Nero''s two wise advisers?","What did Nero say when he first had to sign a death warrant?","How did Nero first try to get rid of his mother?"],"answers":["Seneca and Burrhus","I wish I did not know how to write!","He put her on a ship built to fall apart at sea"],"notes":"Poisoning and murder within the family; preview if you have sensitive listeners."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-9-6","title":"Chapter 78: The Christians Persecuted","steps":["Read Chapter LXXVIII, \"The Christians Persecuted,\" aloud together (pp. 221–225)","Narration: tell about Nero''s follies, the great fire, and the persecution of the Christians","Map work: Find Rome and trace Peter''s and Paul''s journeys there from Judea","Timeline: Add \"Great Fire of Rome\" at AD 64; add \"Peter and Paul martyred\" at c. AD 64–67"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXVIII (p. 221)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n227/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 221–225","questions":["What did Nero like to do on the stage?","Whom did Nero blame for the fire?","What does the word ''martyr'' mean here?"],"answers":["Sing and act, though his voice was poor","The Christians","Someone who dies for their faith"],"notes":"Nero singing while Rome burned is a legend; the historian Tacitus says Nero was away at Antium and came back to help. The tortures of Christians are described plainly."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-9-7","title":"Chapter 79: Nero''s Cruelty","steps":["Read Chapter LXXIX, \"Nero''s Cruelty,\" aloud together (pp. 225–227)","Narration: tell about Nero''s Golden Palace and how his reign ended","Map work: Find Spain, where the legions chose Galba","Timeline: Add \"Death of Nero\" at AD 68"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXIX (p. 225)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n231/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 225–227","questions":["Why was Nero''s palace called the Golden Palace?","How did Epicharis keep the secret of the plot?","What did Nero say just before he died?"],"answers":["It glittered with gold inside and out","She refused to speak under torture and then took her own life","What a pity that such a fine musician should perish!"],"notes":"Several forced suicides are described, including Seneca''s."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-9-8","title":"Chapter 80: Two Short Reigns","steps":["Read Chapter LXXX, \"Two Short Reigns,\" aloud together (pp. 227–228)","Narration: tell about the short reigns of Galba and Otho","Map work: Find Bedriacum in northern Italy and the Rhine frontier","Timeline: Add \"Year of the Four Emperors\" at AD 69"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXX (p. 227)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n233/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 227–228","questions":["Why could old Galba not do what he wished?","Where did Otho get the money to bribe the soldiers?","Which army chose Vitellius?"],"answers":["He was not firm, and his favorites pushed him to be too harsh or too soft","He stole it from Galba''s treasury","The legions on the Rhine"]}]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-u10","title":"Unit 10: Vespasian to Antoninus Pius (Chapters 81–89)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-romans-10-1","title":"Chapter 81: The Siege of Jerusalem","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXI, \"The Siege of Jerusalem,\" aloud together (pp. 228–231)","Narration: tell about Vitellius, Vespasian, and the siege of Jerusalem","Map work: Find Jerusalem; then find the Colosseum and the Arch of Titus on a map of Rome","Timeline: Add \"Vespasian emperor\" at AD 69; add \"Jerusalem and the Temple destroyed\" at AD 70"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXI (p. 228)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n234/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 228–231","questions":["What was Vitellius''s great fault?","What huge building did Vespasian begin?","What treasure from the Temple is carved on the Arch of Titus?"],"answers":["Gluttony (greedy eating)","The Colosseum","The seven-branched golden candlestick (menorah)"],"notes":"The book connects the fall of Jerusalem with Christ''s prophecy, a faith viewpoint. Casualty numbers come from Josephus and are probably too high."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-10-2","title":"Chapter 82: The Buried Cities","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXII, \"The Buried Cities,\" aloud together (pp. 231–234)","Narration: tell about good Emperor Titus and what happened to Pompeii","Map work: Find Mount Vesuvius, Pompeii, and Herculaneum on the Bay of Naples","Timeline: Add \"Vesuvius buries Pompeii\" at AD 79"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXII (p. 231)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n237/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 231–234","questions":["What did Titus say when a day passed without helping anyone?","Who died trying to see the eruption up close?","How were the buried cities found again?"],"answers":["Alas, I have lost a day!","Pliny the Elder, the naturalist","A man digging a well struck an old house"],"notes":"Pompeii was buried mostly by ash and hot volcanic flows, not lava. A picture book or photos of Pompeii make a great follow-up."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-10-3","title":"Chapter 83: The Terrible Banquet","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXIII, \"The Terrible Banquet,\" aloud together (pp. 235–237)","Narration: tell about Domitian''s cruelty and his strange black banquet","Map work: Find Dacia (modern Romania), north of the Danube River","Timeline: Add \"Domitian emperor\" at AD 81"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXIII (p. 235)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n241/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 235–237","questions":["How did Domitian show off his archery?","Why did Domitian keep Agricola at home?","What were the little black columns really made of?"],"answers":["He shot arrows between the spread fingers of a slave''s hand","He was jealous of his success","Pure gold"]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-10-4","title":"Chapter 84: The Emperor''s Tablets","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXIV, \"The Emperor''s Tablets,\" aloud together (pp. 237–238)","Narration: tell how Domitian''s own list led to his death","Map work: Find the island of Patmos in the Aegean Sea and Ephesus","Timeline: Add \"Death of Domitian\" at AD 96; add \"John writes Revelation on Patmos\" at c. AD 95"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXIV (p. 237)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n243/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 237–238","questions":["How did Domitia learn she was going to be killed?","Where was John sent, and what did he write there?"],"answers":["She found her name on the emperor''s tablets of people to be killed","The island of Patmos; the Book of Revelation"],"notes":"The boiling-oil story about John is a church tradition; the book itself says some stories have him living to old age."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-10-5","title":"Chapter 85: The Good Trajan","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXV, \"The Good Trajan,\" aloud together (pp. 239–240)","Narration: tell about the good emperors Nerva and Trajan","Map work: Find Germany''s Rhine frontier, where Trajan was when he became emperor","Timeline: Add \"Nerva\" at AD 96 and \"Trajan emperor\" at AD 98"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXV (p. 239)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n245/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 239–240","questions":["Why did Trajan not hurry back to Rome?","What did Trajan say when he handed a sword to the guard captain?","How did Trajan help the poor get bread?"],"answers":["He finished his duty on the frontier first","Use this for me if I do my duty, against me if I do not","He built granaries and sold grain cheaply in honest measures"]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-10-6","title":"Chapter 86: Trajan''s Column","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXVI, \"Trajan''s Column,\" aloud together (pp. 240–242)","Narration: tell about Trajan''s conquests and his column","Map work: Find Dacia, Mesopotamia, and Babylon","Timeline: Add \"Dacia conquered\" at AD 106; add \"Empire at its largest\" at AD 117"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXVI (p. 240)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n246/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 240–242","questions":["What surname did Trajan earn?","What still stands in Rome to honor his victories?","What did later senators say to every new emperor?"],"answers":["The Dacian","Trajan''s Column","Reign as fortunately as Augustus, as virtuously as Trajan!"],"notes":"Plutarch''s Lives, mentioned here, is the source for many stories in this book."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-10-7","title":"Chapter 87: The Great Wall","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXVII, \"The Great Wall,\" aloud together (pp. 243–244)","Narration: tell about Hadrian''s travels and his wall","Map work: Find Hadrian''s Wall across northern England, then trace Hadrian''s journeys to Gaul, Spain, Africa, and Athens","Timeline: Add \"Hadrian emperor\" at AD 117 and \"Hadrian''s Wall begun\" at AD 122"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXVII (p. 243)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n249/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 243–244","questions":["What did Hadrian say to an old enemy?","How did Hadrian share his soldiers'' hardships?","How long was Hadrian''s Wall, and whom did it keep out?"],"answers":["My good friend, you have escaped, for I am made emperor","He marched with them and ate their plain food","About 73 miles; tribes from Scotland"],"notes":"The idea that Hadrian wanted to make Christ a Roman god is a late legend."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-10-8","title":"Chapter 88: Hadrian''s Death","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXVIII, \"Hadrian''s Death,\" aloud together (pp. 244–246)","Narration: tell what Hadrian built and how his life ended","Map work: Find Tivoli (Tibur) near Rome and the Castle of Sant''Angelo by the Tiber","Timeline: Add \"Jewish revolt crushed\" at AD 132–135; add \"Death of Hadrian\" at AD 138"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXVIII (p. 244)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n250/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 244–246","questions":["What is Hadrian''s tomb called today?","Why did Hadrian become cruel near the end?","Whom did Hadrian choose to rule after him?"],"answers":["The Castle of Sant''Angelo (St. Angelo)","He became ill, which made him cross and suspicious","Antoninus"]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-10-9","title":"Chapter 89: Antoninus Pius","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXIX, \"Antoninus Pius,\" aloud together (pp. 246–248)","Narration: tell why Antoninus was called Pius and what kind of ruler he was","Map work: Find Nîmes and the Pont du Gard in southern France","Timeline: Add \"Antoninus Pius emperor\" at AD 138–161"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXIX (p. 246)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n252/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 246–248","questions":["What did Antoninus do when hungry people threw stones at him?","Whose writings changed his mind about the Christians?","What was his favorite saying?"],"answers":["He forgave them and gave them the food from his palace","Justin, a Christian philosopher","I would rather save one citizen than kill a thousand enemies"],"notes":"The Pont du Gard was actually built about a century earlier. The catacombs were mainly Christian burial places, not hiding places."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-u11","title":"Unit 11: Marcus Aurelius to Zenobia (Chapters 90–96)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-romans-11-1","title":"Chapter 90: The Model Pagan","steps":["Read Chapter XC, \"The Model Pagan,\" aloud together (pp. 248–251)","Narration: tell what kind of man Marcus Aurelius was and what troubles came in his reign","Map work: Find the Danube frontier and Vienna (Vindobona), where Marcus Aurelius died","Timeline: Add \"Marcus Aurelius emperor\" at AD 161–180"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XC (p. 248)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n254/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 248–251","questions":["Who were the Stoics?","What is the name of the book Marcus Aurelius wrote?","Who was the lame slave whose teaching he admired?"],"answers":["Philosophers who taught people to bear troubles nobly and seek to be good rather than happy","The Meditations","Epictetus"],"notes":"The ''rain miracle'' was claimed by both pagans and Christians. The plague brought home from the East was real (the Antonine Plague)."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-11-2","title":"Chapter 91: Another Cruel Emperor","steps":["Read Chapter XCI, \"Another Cruel Emperor,\" aloud together (pp. 251–254)","Narration: tell how Commodus was different from his father","Map work: Point to the Colosseum, where Commodus fought as a gladiator","Timeline: Add \"Commodus emperor\" at AD 180–192"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCI (p. 251)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n257/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 251–254","questions":["What happened when Commodus ordered a slave thrown into the fire?","Why were Commodus''s fights in the arena not fair?","What did Commodus like to call himself?"],"answers":["The servants burned a sheepskin instead and saved the slave","His opponents had lead swords that could not hurt him","The Roman Hercules"]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-11-3","title":"Chapter 92: An Unnatural Son","steps":["Read Chapter XCII, \"An Unnatural Son,\" aloud together (pp. 254–256)","Narration: tell how Septimius Severus became emperor and what his son Caracalla did","Map work: Find Byzantium on the Bosporus, Lyons in Gaul, and York in Britain","Timeline: Add \"Septimius Severus emperor\" at AD 193; add \"Severus dies at York\" at AD 211"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCII (p. 254)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n260/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 254–256","questions":["What was an apotheosis?","What did Severus offer Caracalla when Caracalla tried to kill him?","What did Severus say to his funeral urn?"],"answers":["A ceremony to make a dead emperor a god","A sword, daring him to kill him openly","Little urn, you will soon hold one for whom the world seemed too small"],"notes":"Septimius Severus was born in Leptis Magna in North Africa, a sign of how wide the empire had grown."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-11-4","title":"Chapter 93: The Senate of Women","steps":["Read Chapter XCIII, \"The Senate of Women,\" aloud together (pp. 256–259)","Narration: tell about Caracalla, Heliogabalus, and Alexander Severus","Map work: Find Alexandria in Egypt and the Rhine frontier","Timeline: Add \"Baths of Caracalla\" at c. AD 216; add \"Alexander Severus\" at AD 222–235"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCIII (p. 256)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n262/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 256–259","questions":["What famous buildings did Caracalla build in Rome?","What was unusual about the senate Heliogabalus made?","What was Alexander good at, and what was he not good at?"],"answers":["Public baths","It was a senate of women, led by his mother","Painting, sculpture, poetry, math, and music; he had no skill as a soldier"],"notes":"Writers who hated Heliogabalus made up or exaggerated many stories about him; treat them as gossip."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-11-5","title":"Chapter 94: The Gigantic Emperor","steps":["Read Chapter XCIV, \"The Gigantic Emperor,\" aloud together (pp. 259–262)","Narration: tell about giant Maximinus and Rome''s thousandth birthday","Map work: Find Thrace (where Maximinus came from) and the Danube","Timeline: Add \"Rome''s 1,000th birthday games\" at AD 248"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCIV (p. 259)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n265/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 259–262","questions":["How did Maximinus become a soldier and then emperor?","What did Philip celebrate with great games?","What happened to the army that Decius was sent to calm?"],"answers":["He was a herdsman who joined the army and impressed everyone with his strength","Rome''s one thousandth anniversary","They made him emperor and forced him to fight Philip"],"notes":"Maximinus''s eight-foot height and huge meals come from an unreliable ancient book and are surely exaggerated."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-11-6","title":"Chapter 95: Invasion of the Goths","steps":["Read Chapter XCV, \"Invasion of the Goths,\" aloud together (pp. 262–264)","Narration: tell about the coming of the Goths and what happened to Emperor Valerian","Map work: Find the Danube, Thrace, and Persia; find Antioch in Syria","Timeline: Add \"Goths kill Emperor Decius\" at AD 251; add \"Valerian captured by Persians\" at AD 260"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCV (p. 262)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n268/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 262–264","questions":["What were the three groups of Goths?","Which god did the Goths worship?","What did the Persian king Sapor use Valerian for?"],"answers":["Ostrogoths (East Goths), Visigoths (West Goths), and Gepidae","Odin","A mounting block to climb onto his horse"],"notes":"The story of Valerian''s skin is told by Christian writers and may be exaggerated."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-11-7","title":"Chapter 96: Zenobia, Queen of Palmyra","steps":["Read Chapter XCVI, \"Zenobia, Queen of Palmyra,\" aloud together (pp. 264–266)","Narration: tell the story of Queen Zenobia of Palmyra","Map work: Find Palmyra in the Syrian desert, Egypt, and Tivoli near Rome","Timeline: Add \"Aurelian defeats Zenobia\" at AD 272"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCVI (p. 264)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n270/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 264–266","questions":["What title did Zenobia take?","Which land did Zenobia conquer?","How did Zenobia spend the rest of her life?"],"answers":["Empress of the East","Egypt","Quietly in a palace near Tibur (Tivoli), raising her children"],"notes":"The book says France is named after the Franks, which is true. Palmyra''s ruins still stand, though some were damaged in 2015."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-u12","title":"Unit 12: Christian Emperors and the Fall of the West (Chapters 97–102)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-romans-12-1","title":"Chapter 97: A Prophecy Fulfilled","steps":["Read Chapter XCVII, \"A Prophecy Fulfilled,\" aloud together (pp. 266–269)","Narration: tell how Diocletian divided the rule and how Constantine won at the Milvian Bridge","Map work: Find Nicomedia, Milan, Sirmium, and Trier (Treves); find the Arch of Constantine in Rome","Timeline: Add \"Diocletian emperor\" at AD 284; add \"Battle of the Milvian Bridge\" at AD 312"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCVII (p. 266)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n272/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 266–269","questions":["What did ''Aper'' mean, and why did it matter?","How many rulers did Diocletian share the empire among?","What words did Constantine see with the cross?"],"answers":["Boar; Diocletian killed Aper, fulfilling the prophecy","Four","By this sign conquer"],"notes":"Diocletian''s persecution of Christians was the worst of all; the book mentions it briefly."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-12-2","title":"Chapter 98: The First Christian Emperor","steps":["Read Chapter XCVIII, \"The First Christian Emperor,\" aloud together (pp. 269–271)","Narration: tell what Constantine did as the first Christian emperor and who Julian was","Map work: Find Nicaea and Byzantium/Constantinople (modern Istanbul); find Paris (Lutetia)","Timeline: Add \"Christians free to worship\" at AD 313; \"Council of Nicaea\" at AD 325; \"Constantinople founded\" at AD 330"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCVIII (p. 269)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n275/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 269–271","questions":["Who was Constantine''s devout Christian mother?","What new name did Byzantium get?","Why was Julian called the Apostate?"],"answers":["Helena","Constantinople, the city of Constantine","He gave up Christianity and went back to the old gods"],"notes":"Constantine was baptized only on his deathbed, and gladiator games went on for some years after him."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-12-3","title":"Chapter 99: The Roman Empire Divided","steps":["Read Chapter XCIX, \"The Roman Empire Divided,\" aloud together (pp. 271–273)","Narration: tell how the empire was split between East and West","Map work: Find Tarsus, Thrace, and Adrianople; color the Eastern and Western halves of the empire","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Adrianople\" at AD 378; add \"Empire divided for good\" at AD 395"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XCIX (p. 271)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n277/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 271–273","questions":["What did Julian try to rebuild, and what stopped it?","Who translated the Bible into the Gothic language?","Which emperor was the last to rule the whole empire?"],"answers":["The Temple at Jerusalem; an earthquake and a war with Persia","Ulfilas","Theodosius the Great"],"notes":"The book calls one group the ''true Christians''; this is the author''s viewpoint on the Arian debate."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-12-4","title":"Chapter 100: An Emperor''s Penance","steps":["Read Chapter C, \"An Emperor''s Penance,\" aloud together (pp. 273–275)","Narration: tell about Theodosius''s penance and how the Goths came into Italy","Map work: Find Thessalonica in Greece, Milan, and Ravenna","Timeline: Add \"Theodosius''s penance\" at AD 390; add \"Honorius moves to Ravenna\" at AD 402"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter C (p. 273)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n279/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 273–275","questions":["What did Theodosius do that he later repented of?","Who made him do public penance?","Why did Honorius move to Ravenna?"],"answers":["He ordered a massacre of the people of Thessalonica","St. Ambrose","He was frightened and thought it would be easier to defend"]},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-12-5","title":"Chapter 101: Sieges of Rome","steps":["Read Chapter CI, \"Sieges of Rome,\" aloud together (pp. 275–277)","Narration: tell how Alaric took Rome and how Attila was turned back","Map work: Trace the Goths from Italy to Spain; find Châlons in France and the route of the Huns into Italy","Timeline: Add \"Alaric sacks Rome\" at AD 410; add \"Battle of Châlons\" at AD 451"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CI (p. 275)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n281/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 275–277","questions":["How long had it been since an enemy army had entered Rome?","What was Attila called?","Who persuaded Attila to spare Rome?"],"answers":["About 800 years, since the Gauls","The Scourge of God","Pope Leo"],"notes":"The book''s marriage condition for Attila is a simplified version of the story of Honoria, Valentinian''s sister."},{"id":"story-of-the-romans-12-6","title":"Chapter 102: End of the Empire of the West","steps":["Read Chapter CII, \"End of the Empire of the West,\" aloud together (pp. 277–278)","Narration: tell how the Western Empire came to an end","Map work: Find Carthage again (the Vandals sailed from there) and Constantinople","Timeline: Add \"Vandals sack Rome\" at AD 455; add \"End of the Western Empire\" at AD 476; add \"Constantinople falls to the Turks\" at 1453"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter CII (p. 277)","url":"https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog/page/n283/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 277–278","questions":["What did people decide Romulus''s twelve vultures meant?","Who was the last emperor of the West?","Where did the Eastern Empire go on, and until when?"],"answers":["Twelve centuries of Roman rule","Romulus Augustulus","At Constantinople, until 1453"],"notes":"A good closing activity: review your whole timeline from the legend of Aeneas to AD 476."}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"“The Story of the Romans (Eclectic School Readings)” by H. A. Guerber (1896), in the public domain. Free full text: https://archive.org/details/storyromans02guergoog. Lesson plan by Freerange."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (listing_id, version) do update set content = excluded.content;
insert into public.listings (id, seller_name, seller_kind, seller_verified, title, subjects, grade_min, grade_max, philosophies, format, price, description, includes, lessons, lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement, family_style, lesson_steps, cover, color, esa_eligible, created_at, package_id, package_version, license, attribution, outline) values ('l-lib-story-of-the-thirteen-colonies', 'H. A. Guerber', 'open', false, 'The Story of the Thirteen Colonies', array['history']::text[], 3, 6, array['classical', 'charlotte_mason']::text[], 'online', 0, 'H. A. Guerber''s classic 1898 read-aloud tells America''s early story in short, lively chapters, from the first peoples and the Vikings through Columbus, Jamestown, the Pilgrims, and the French and Indian Wars to the Revolution and Washington''s farewell. It suits the whole family in grades 3–6: each day you read one chapter aloud, your child narrates it back, finds the places on a map, and adds the event to a timeline. The full text is free online.', array['84 lessons in 13 units, one chapter per lesson', 'Link to the free full text for every chapter', 'Narration prompt, map work, and timeline entry for each lesson', 'Comprehension questions with answers', 'Parent notes flagging legends told as history, dated views of Native Americans and enslaved Africans, and factual errors']::text[], 84, 4, 20, 'high', true, '{}'::text[], '🔔', '#4C7FD9', false, '2026-11-11T12:00:00.000Z', 'pk-lib-story-of-the-thirteen-colonies', 1, '{"kind":"public_domain"}'::jsonb, '“The Story of the Thirteen Colonies” by H. A. Guerber (1898), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/48051. Lesson plan by Freerange.', '[{"title":"Unit 1: The First Americans and the Northmen (Chapters 1–6)","lessons":["Chapter 1: Our Country Long Ago","Chapter 2: The Barbarous Indians","Chapter 3: The Mounds","Chapter 4: Where the Northmen Went","Chapter 5: The Northmen in America","Chapter 6: Queer Ideas"]},{"title":"Unit 2: Prince Henry and Columbus (Chapters 7–14)","lessons":["Chapter 7: Prince Henry the Navigator","Chapter 8: Youth of Columbus","Chapter 9: Columbus and the Queen","Chapter 10: \"Land! Land!\"","Chapter 11: Columbus and the Savages","Chapter 12: Home Again","Chapter 13: Columbus Illtreated","Chapter 14: Death of Columbus"]},{"title":"Unit 3: Explorers of the New World (Chapters 15–22)","lessons":["Chapter 15: How America Got Its Name","Chapter 16: The Fountain of Youth","Chapter 17: \"The Father of Waters\"","Chapter 18: The French in Canada","Chapter 19: French and Spanish Quarrels","Chapter 20: The Sky City","Chapter 21: Around the World","Chapter 22: Nothing but Smoke"]},{"title":"Unit 4: Jamestown and New Netherland (Chapters 23–27)","lessons":["Chapter 23: Smith''s Adventures","Chapter 24: The Jamestown Men","Chapter 25: Smith Wounded","Chapter 26: The Visit of Pocahontas to England","Chapter 27: Hudson and the Indians"]},{"title":"Unit 5: Pilgrims and Puritans (Chapters 28–35)","lessons":["Chapter 28: The Mayflower","Chapter 29: Plymouth Rock","Chapter 30: The First American Thanksgiving","Chapter 31: The Snake Skin and the Bullets","Chapter 32: The Beginning of Boston","Chapter 33: Stories of Two Ministers","Chapter 34: Williams and the Indians","Chapter 35: The Quakers"]},{"title":"Unit 6: King Philip, New York, Pennsylvania, and Maryland (Chapters 36–40)","lessons":["Chapter 36: The King-Killers","Chapter 37: King Philip''s War","Chapter 38: The Beginning of New York","Chapter 39: Penn and the Indians","Chapter 40: The Catholics in Maryland"]},{"title":"Unit 7: Southern Colonies and Colonial Troubles (Chapters 41–46)","lessons":["Chapter 41: The Old Dominion","Chapter 42: Bacon''s Rebellion","Chapter 43: A Journey Inland","Chapter 44: The Carolina Pirates","Chapter 45: Charter Oak","Chapter 46: Salem Witches"]},{"title":"Unit 8: New France and the French and Indian Wars (Chapters 47–56)","lessons":["Chapter 47: Down the Mississippi","Chapter 48: La Salle''s Adventures","Chapter 49: Indians on the Warpath","Chapter 50: Two Wars With the French","Chapter 51: Washington''s Boyhood","Chapter 52: Washington''s Journey","Chapter 53: Washington''s First Battle","Chapter 54: Stories of Franklin","Chapter 55: Braddock''s Defeat","Chapter 56: Wolfe at Quebec"]},{"title":"Unit 9: The Road to Revolution (Chapters 57–62)","lessons":["Chapter 57: How England Treated Her Colonies","Chapter 58: The Stamp Tax","Chapter 59: The Anger of the Colonies","Chapter 60: The Boston Tea Party","Chapter 61: The Minutemen","Chapter 62: The Battle of Lexington"]},{"title":"Unit 10: The War Begins (Chapters 63–68)","lessons":["Chapter 63: Bunker Hill","Chapter 64: The Boston Boys","Chapter 65: The British Leave Boston","Chapter 66: Declaration of Independence","Chapter 67: A Lady''s Way of Helping","Chapter 68: Christmas Eve"]},{"title":"Unit 11: Saratoga to Stony Point (Chapters 69–74)","lessons":["Chapter 69: The Fight at Bennington","Chapter 70: Burgoyne''s Surrender","Chapter 71: The Winter at Valley Forge","Chapter 72: The Quaker Woman","Chapter 73: Putnam''s Adventures","Chapter 74: Indian Cruelty"]},{"title":"Unit 12: The Frontier, the Sea, and the South (Chapters 75–80)","lessons":["Chapter 75: Boone in Kentucky","Chapter 76: Famous Sea Fights","Chapter 77: The \"Swamp Fox\"","Chapter 78: The Poor Soldiers","Chapter 79: The Spy","Chapter 80: A Traitor''s Death"]},{"title":"Unit 13: Victory and Farewell (Chapters 81–84)","lessons":["Chapter 81: Two Unselfish Women","Chapter 82: The Surrender of Cornwallis","Chapter 83: The British Flag Hauled Down","Chapter 84: Washington''s Farewell"]}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set seller_name = excluded.seller_name, seller_kind = excluded.seller_kind, seller_verified = excluded.seller_verified, title = excluded.title, subjects = excluded.subjects, grade_min = excluded.grade_min, grade_max = excluded.grade_max, philosophies = excluded.philosophies, format = excluded.format, price = excluded.price, description = excluded.description, includes = excluded.includes, lessons = excluded.lessons, lessons_per_week = excluded.lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson = excluded.minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement = excluded.parent_involvement, family_style = excluded.family_style, lesson_steps = excluded.lesson_steps, cover = excluded.cover, color = excluded.color, esa_eligible = excluded.esa_eligible, package_id = excluded.package_id, package_version = excluded.package_version, license = excluded.license, attribution = excluded.attribution, outline = excluded.outline;
insert into public.package_releases (listing_id, version, content) values ('l-lib-story-of-the-thirteen-colonies', 1, '{"units":[{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-u1","title":"Unit 1: The First Americans and the Northmen (Chapters 1–6)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-1-1","title":"Chapter 1: Our Country Long Ago","steps":["Read Chapter I, \"Our Country Long Ago,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the first peoples of America lived, built wigwams, and made canoes","Map work: Find the Rocky Mountains, Greenland, Alaska, and Cape Horn; trace the Americas from north to south","Timeline: Add \"First peoples arrive in the Americas\" at before 13,000 BC (modern estimate; the book gives no date)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter I","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_13"}],"pages":"pp. 13–17","questions":["How did people make a dugout canoe with only stone axes?","How did families boil water without metal pots?"],"answers":["They burned down a tree, then burned and scraped out the log to hollow it","They dropped stones heated in the fire into clay-lined baskets of water"],"notes":"The book sorts Native peoples into \"savage,\" \"barbarous,\" and \"half-civilized\" and describes them by skin color. These are 1890s labels that historians no longer use; talk about how Native nations had their own rich ways of life."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-1-2","title":"Chapter 2: The Barbarous Indians","steps":["Read Chapter II, \"The Barbarous Indians,\" aloud together","Narration: describe how the eastern woodland peoples farmed, built long houses, and organized clans","Map work: Find New York, the St. Lawrence River, and the Missouri River","Timeline: Add \"Iroquois long-house villages\" at c. AD 1300–1600"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter II","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_17"}],"pages":"pp. 17–20","questions":["Name three crops these peoples grew.","How many families shared one fireplace in a long house?","What was a clan''s sign, and where was it shown?"],"answers":["Any three of corn, beans, pumpkins, squash, tomatoes, tobacco, sunflowers","Four","An animal such as a bear, turtle, or beaver, painted on bodies and carved on belongings"],"notes":"The chapter calls Native religion \"nonsense\" and says men left all work to women. Share that these are outsiders'' judgments from the 1890s; women in Iroquois nations, for example, owned the fields and helped choose leaders."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-1-3","title":"Chapter 3: The Mounds","steps":["Read Chapter III, \"The Mounds,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the pueblos, cliff dwellings, and the mound builders","Map work: Find New Mexico, Arizona, and Ohio; locate the Serpent Mound in southern Ohio","Timeline: Add \"Serpent Mound built in Ohio\" at c. AD 1000 (one modern estimate); add \"Cliff Palace at Mesa Verde\" at c. AD 1200"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter III","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_20"}],"pages":"pp. 20–24","questions":["How did cliff dwellers keep enemies out of their homes?","What shape is the famous Ohio mound?","What were wampum beads used for?"],"answers":["They pulled up the ladders and threw stones and arrows from above","A snake (a serpent swallowing an egg shape)","As money and for belts and necklaces"],"notes":"The book treats the mound builders as a possible \"older race.\" Historians now know the mounds were built by the ancestors of Native Americans."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-1-4","title":"Chapter 4: Where the Northmen Went","steps":["Read Chapter IV, \"Where the Northmen Went,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the Northmen sailed from Norway to Iceland and Greenland","Map work: Find Norway, Iceland, Greenland, the Strait of Gibraltar, and Constantinople","Timeline: Add \"Norse settle Iceland\" at c. AD 874; add \"Eric the Red settles Greenland\" at c. 985"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter IV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_24"}],"pages":"pp. 24–27","questions":["What does the word \"vik\" mean?","Why did Eric the Red leave Iceland?","Why did he choose the name Greenland?"],"answers":["A bay","He was banished for murder","To attract settlers"],"notes":"The book calls the Eskimos (Inuit) \"a race of Indians\"; they are a distinct Arctic people, and the Norse in Greenland met them later than the book suggests."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-1-5","title":"Chapter 5: The Northmen in America","steps":["Read Chapter V, \"The Northmen in America,\" aloud together","Narration: tell the story of Biarni, Leif the Lucky, and Vinland","Map work: Trace a route from Greenland to Labrador and Newfoundland; find Cape Cod","Timeline: Add \"Leif Ericson reaches Vinland\" at c. AD 1000"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter V","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_27"}],"pages":"pp. 27–31","questions":["What did Leif the Lucky find so much of that he named the land Vinland?","Who was the first European child born in America?","How do we know about the Norse voyages today?"],"answers":["Wild grapes","Snorri","From old Norse stories called sagas (and, today, the ruins at L''Anse aux Meadows)"],"notes":"The book places Leif in Rhode Island. The only proven Norse site is L''Anse aux Meadows in Newfoundland, found in 1960. The Madoc story is a legend, as the book itself hints."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-1-6","title":"Chapter 6: Queer Ideas","steps":["Read Chapter VI, \"Queer Ideas,\" aloud together","Narration: explain what sailors feared about the \"Sea of Darkness\" and how printing spread new ideas","Map work: Find the Atlantic Ocean, Greece, and Constantinople (now Istanbul)","Timeline: Add \"Turks take Constantinople\" at 1453; add \"Gutenberg''s printing press\" at c. 1450"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter VI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_31"}],"pages":"pp. 31–33","questions":["What did sailors believe the Loadstone Mountain would do?","When did Greek thinkers begin to say the earth was round?"],"answers":["Pull the nails out of their ships so they fell apart","About 300 years before Christ (even earlier, in fact)"],"notes":"The idea that ordinary medieval people thought the earth was flat is a 19th-century myth; educated Europeans had known it was round for many centuries."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-u2","title":"Unit 2: Prince Henry and Columbus (Chapters 7–14)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-2-1","title":"Chapter 7: Prince Henry the Navigator","steps":["Read Chapter VII, \"Prince Henry the Navigator,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why Europeans wanted a sea road to the East and what Prince Henry did about it","Map work: Find Portugal, Venice, Genoa, the Canary Islands, Madeira, the Azores, and Cape Verde","Timeline: Add \"Prince Henry the Navigator sends ships down Africa\" at c. 1420–1460"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter VII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_33"}],"pages":"pp. 33–36","questions":["What goods did traders bring from the East?","Why did the trade overland become hard?","What frightened Henry''s sailors at Tenerife?"],"answers":["Silk, sugar, and spices","The Turks treated the traders badly and the trade nearly stopped","Smoke from the volcano"],"notes":"Sir John Mandeville''s travels are now known to be mostly invented. Henry''s voyages also began the Portuguese slave trade from Africa, which the book leaves out; you may mention it gently."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-2-2","title":"Chapter 8: Youth of Columbus","steps":["Read Chapter VIII, \"Youth of Columbus,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Columbus''s boyhood, his escape at sea, and how he came to his big idea","Map work: Find Genoa, Lisbon, Cape St. Vincent, and Iceland","Timeline: Add \"Christopher Columbus born in Genoa\" at 1451 (the book says about 1436)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter VIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_36"}],"pages":"pp. 36–39","questions":["How did Columbus reach shore when his ship burned?","What did Toscanelli send Columbus?","Why did Columbus think Asia was not far west?"],"answers":["He held on to a floating oar and swam","A map showing Japan where Cuba really is","He believed the earth was smaller and Asia stretched farther east than it does"],"notes":"Most historians now date Columbus''s birth to 1451, and doubt he studied at the university of Pavia."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-2-3","title":"Chapter 9: Columbus and the Queen","steps":["Read Chapter IX, \"Columbus and the Queen,\" aloud together","Narration: tell all the people Columbus asked for help, and who finally said yes","Map work: Find Spain, Córdova, Salamanca, Palos, and the Cape of Good Hope","Timeline: Add \"Dias rounds the Cape of Good Hope\" at 1488 (the book says 1487); add \"Isabella agrees to back Columbus\" at 1492"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter IX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_39"}],"pages":"pp. 39–44","questions":["How did King John of Portugal try to steal Columbus''s idea?","Who helped Columbus at the monastery of La Rábida?","What did Columbus ask for in return for his voyage?"],"answers":["He secretly sent his own ship west, but it turned back in a storm","Prior Perez (and the Pinzon brothers)","The title of admiral, the right to govern the new lands, and a tenth of the riches"],"notes":"Most historians say the Salamanca scholars doubted Columbus because they (rightly) thought the ocean was far too wide to cross, not because they believed the earth was flat."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-2-4","title":"Chapter 10: \"Land! Land!\"","steps":["Read Chapter X, \"Land! Land!\" aloud together","Narration: tell the story of the first crossing, from Palos to the landing on San Salvador","Map work: Trace the route from Palos to the Canary Islands, across the Sargasso Sea, to the Bahamas","Timeline: Add \"Columbus lands in the Bahamas\" at October 12, 1492"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter X","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_44"}],"pages":"pp. 44–48","questions":["What were the names of Columbus''s three ships?","What signs told the sailors land was near?","Why did Columbus call the people he met \"Indians\"?"],"answers":["The Santa Maria, the Pinta, and the Niña","A branch with berries, a carved stick, a board, and birds flying south","He thought he had reached the Indies near Asia"],"notes":"The island''s people were the Taíno, a farming people with villages and chiefs. The book calls them \"savages\"; you might ask your child what the Taíno may have thought of the visitors."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-2-5","title":"Chapter 11: Columbus and the Savages","steps":["Read Chapter XI, \"Columbus and the Savages,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what Columbus found on the islands and why he kept searching","Map work: Find Cuba and Haiti (Hispaniola)","Timeline: Add \"La Navidad, first Spanish fort in the Americas\" at December 1492"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_48"}],"pages":"pp. 48–51","questions":["What made Columbus think he was near rich Asian kingdoms?","What new plant did the Spaniards learn about in Cuba?","Why were forty men left behind on Haiti?"],"answers":["The people wore gold ornaments and pointed south when asked","Tobacco","The Santa Maria was wrecked, so they stayed in a fort"],"notes":"Columbus took several Taíno people with him by force; the book says they were \"happy.\" Ask how they might have felt."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-2-6","title":"Chapter 12: Home Again","steps":["Read Chapter XII, \"Home Again,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the stormy trip home and Columbus''s welcome in Spain","Map work: Find the Azores, Lisbon, Palos, and Barcelona","Timeline: Add \"Columbus returns to Spain\" at March 1493; add \"Second voyage, 17 ships\" at September 1493"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_51"}],"pages":"pp. 51–54","questions":["How did Columbus try to make sure news of his discovery survived the storm?","What was the lesson of the egg story?","How big was the second fleet?"],"answers":["He sealed written accounts in wax inside casks to float on the sea","Things are easy once someone shows you how","Seventeen ships and about 1,500 men"],"notes":"The egg story is a legend told about others before Columbus. Accounts of Carib cannibalism came from their enemies and Spaniards seeking an excuse to enslave them; historians treat them with care."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-2-7","title":"Chapter 13: Columbus Illtreated","steps":["Read Chapter XIII, \"Columbus Illtreated,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what went wrong in the Haiti colony and why Columbus was sent home in chains","Map work: Find Jamaica, Trinidad, and the mouth of the Orinoco River","Timeline: Add \"Third voyage reaches South America\" at 1498; add \"Columbus arrested and sent to Spain\" at 1500"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_54"}],"pages":"pp. 54–57","questions":["What happened to the men left at the first fort?","What did Columbus send to Spain besides gold?","What did Columbus think the Orinoco River might be?"],"answers":["Some died of sickness; the rest were killed by the islanders after treating them cruelly","Native people to be sold as slaves","One of the four rivers of the Garden of Eden"],"notes":"This chapter deals with slavery and cruelty to the Taíno, including taking women. Preview it and decide how much to read aloud to younger children."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-2-8","title":"Chapter 14: Death of Columbus","steps":["Read Chapter XIV, \"Death of Columbus,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Columbus''s last voyage and his death","Map work: Find Central America, Valladolid, and Seville","Timeline: Add \"Fourth voyage\" at 1502; add \"Columbus dies\" at May 1506"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_57"}],"pages":"pp. 57–60","questions":["Why did Columbus keep his chains?","How did Columbus show he understood the weather better than the governor?","What qualities does the author praise in Columbus?"],"answers":["To remind people how unjustly he had been treated","He warned of a storm; the governor ignored him and the gold ships were wrecked","His faith, courage, and perseverance"],"notes":"The book ends with high praise. Today many people also remember the suffering his rule brought to the Taíno; older children can weigh both sides."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-u3","title":"Unit 3: Explorers of the New World (Chapters 15–22)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-3-1","title":"Chapter 15: How America Got Its Name","steps":["Read Chapter XV, \"How America Got Its Name,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how John Cabot, Vasco da Gama, and Americus Vespucius each added to the map","Map work: Find Newfoundland, Labrador, Calicut in India, and Brazil","Timeline: Add \"John Cabot reaches North America for England\" at 1497; add \"America named on a map\" at 1507"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_60"}],"pages":"pp. 60–62","questions":["Why did England later claim North America?","Who first reached India by sailing around Africa?","Why is our continent called America?"],"answers":["Because John Cabot had explored and claimed it","Vasco da Gama","A German mapmaker named it after Americus Vespucius, who wrote about it"],"notes":"The 1497 Vespucius voyage mentioned at the end is doubted by most historians."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-3-2","title":"Chapter 16: The Fountain of Youth","steps":["Read Chapter XVI, \"The Fountain of Youth,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Balboa, Ponce de Leon, and Magellan","Map work: Find the Isthmus of Panama, Puerto Rico, Florida, the Strait of Magellan, and the Philippines","Timeline: Add \"Balboa sees the Pacific\" at 1513; add \"Ponce de Leon names Florida\" at 1513; add \"Magellan''s voyage around the world\" at 1519–1522"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_62"}],"pages":"pp. 62–66","questions":["How did Balboa sneak onto the ship?","How did Florida get its name?","Why did Magellan call the ocean \"Pacific\"?"],"answers":["He hid in a cask","Ponce de Leon landed at Easter time (Pascua florida)","It seemed so peaceful and calm"],"notes":"The Fountain of Youth search is a legend attached to Ponce de Leon after his death; he really went seeking land and gold."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-3-3","title":"Chapter 17: \"The Father of Waters\"","steps":["Read Chapter XVII, \"The Father of Waters,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the Spanish explorers Narvaez, Cabeza de Vaca, Coronado, and De Soto","Map work: Find Mexico City, Tampa Bay, the Mississippi River, Acoma in New Mexico, and the Grand Canyon","Timeline: Add \"Cortez conquers Mexico\" at 1519–1521; add \"Coronado''s expedition\" at 1540–1542; add \"De Soto reaches the Mississippi\" at 1541"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_66"}],"pages":"pp. 66–70","questions":["How did Cabeza de Vaca and his friends survive eight years among strangers?","What were the \"Seven Cities of Cibola\" really?","Why did De Soto''s men bury him in the river at night?"],"answers":["They acted as healers (\"magicians\") and wandered across the continent","Zuñi pueblo villages","They feared the people they had fought would find and harm his body"],"notes":"The \"negro servant\" who guided Marcos was Estevanico, an enslaved Moroccan man and one of the first Africans known to explore North America. Narvaez''s party was lost near Florida and Texas, not at the Mississippi''s mouth."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-3-4","title":"Chapter 18: The French in Canada","steps":["Read Chapter XVIII, \"The French in Canada,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why French fishermen and explorers came to North America and what Cartier found","Map work: Find Brittany, the Banks of Newfoundland, Cape Breton, the St. Lawrence River, Quebec, Montreal, and Niagara Falls","Timeline: Add \"Verrazano explores the coast\" at 1524; add \"Cartier claims Canada for France\" at 1534"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_70"}],"pages":"pp. 70–73","questions":["Why was fish in such demand in Europe?","How did the St. Lawrence River get its name?","What does \"Mont Real\" (Montreal) mean?"],"answers":["Catholics ate fish instead of meat on the many fast days","Cartier arrived on Saint Lawrence''s day","Royal mountain"],"notes":"Cartier kidnapped several Native people, which explains the later trouble with their relatives. The meaning \"Thunder of Waters\" for Niagara is a popular tradition, not certain."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-3-5","title":"Chapter 19: French and Spanish Quarrels","steps":["Read Chapter XIX, \"French and Spanish Quarrels,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what happened between the French Huguenots and the Spaniards in Florida","Map work: Find the St. Johns River, St. Augustine, and Santa Fe; color Spanish Florida and New Mexico","Timeline: Add \"St. Augustine founded\" at 1565; add \"Oñate founds a colony in New Mexico\" at 1598"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_73"}],"pages":"pp. 73–77","questions":["Who were the Huguenots?","What is special about St. Augustine?","What trap did the people of Acoma set for Oñate?"],"answers":["French Protestants","It is the oldest city founded by Europeans in the mainland United States","They invited him to step through a trapdoor into a dark room where armed men waited"],"notes":"This chapter includes a massacre, revenge killings, and starving sailors resorting to cannibalism. Santa Fe was actually founded about 1610; San Diego''s mission dates from 1769, not soon after St. Augustine."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-3-6","title":"Chapter 20: The Sky City","steps":["Read Chapter XX, \"The Sky City,\" aloud together","Narration: tell the story of the battle for Acoma and of Fray Ramirez","Map work: Find Acoma Pueblo west of Albuquerque, New Mexico; draw the mesa from the side","Timeline: Add \"Spaniards attack Acoma\" at 1599; add \"Fray Ramirez comes to Acoma\" at 1629"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_77"}],"pages":"pp. 77–80","questions":["How did the Spaniards get a cannon close to the town?","What made the people of Acoma stop throwing stones at Fray Ramirez?","What is the stone stairway called?"],"answers":["They dragged it up a neighboring rock pinnacle","He rescued a girl who had fallen onto a ledge, and they believed it a miracle","The Path of the Father"],"notes":"The book tells the battle as Spanish heroism. After it, Oñate punished the people of Acoma very harshly, including cutting off men''s feet; the Acoma people remember it as a tragedy. Older children may want to hear both sides."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-3-7","title":"Chapter 21: Around the World","steps":["Read Chapter XXI, \"Around the World,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Hawkins, Frobisher, Davis, and Drake","Map work: Find Frobisher Bay, Davis Strait, Drake''s Bay in California, and the Strait of Magellan","Timeline: Add \"Frobisher searches for the Northwest Passage\" at 1576; add \"Drake sails around the world\" at 1577–1580"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_80"}],"pages":"pp. 80–83","questions":["What did Frobisher bring home, thinking it was gold?","What was the \"Northwest Passage\" explorers hoped to find?","What did Drake mean by \"singeing the King of Spain''s beard\"?"],"answers":["Fool''s gold (pyrite)","A sea route through or around northern America to Asia","Raiding Spanish ports and burning their ships"],"notes":"Hawkins began England''s part in the African slave trade; Africans had already been enslaved by the Spanish and Portuguese for decades. The book repeats the excuse that slavery among Christians was \"better\"; talk about why that was wrong."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-3-8","title":"Chapter 22: Nothing but Smoke","steps":["Read Chapter XXII, \"Nothing but Smoke,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Sir Walter Raleigh, Roanoke, and the lost colony","Map work: Find Roanoke Island, North Carolina, Cape Cod, and Cuttyhunk Island","Timeline: Add \"Virginia Dare born at Roanoke\" at 1587; add \"Roanoke found empty\" at 1590"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_83"}],"pages":"pp. 83–87","questions":["How did Virginia get its name?","What was the only clue left at Roanoke?","How did Raleigh measure the weight of smoke?"],"answers":["In honor of Queen Elizabeth, the \"virgin queen\"","The word \"Croatoan\" carved on a tree (the book says Croatan)","He weighed the tobacco before and the ashes after; the difference was the smoke"],"notes":"The cloak, the water-on-the-smoker, and the smoke-weighing stories are legends. Potatoes came to Europe from South America through the Spanish, not through Raleigh."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-u4","title":"Unit 4: Jamestown and New Netherland (Chapters 23–27)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-4-1","title":"Chapter 23: Smith''s Adventures","steps":["Read Chapter XXIII, \"Smith''s Adventures,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the founding of Jamestown and John Smith''s early adventures","Map work: Find Chesapeake Bay, Capes Charles and Henry, the James River, and Jamestown","Timeline: Add \"Jamestown founded\" at 1607"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_87"}],"pages":"pp. 87–91","questions":["Why were the first Jamestown settlers so short of food?","What new food did the colonists taste at an abandoned campfire?","What does the author say about the story of Pocahontas saving Smith?"],"answers":["They hunted for gold instead of building and planting","Roasted oysters","It is told of other explorers too, so we do not know if it is true"],"notes":"Smith''s tales of his own adventures in Hungary and Turkey come only from his own books and may be exaggerated. Pocahontas was about 11 at this time; there was no romance."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-4-2","title":"Chapter 24: The Jamestown Men","steps":["Read Chapter XXIV, \"The Jamestown Men,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Smith was freed, how he ran the colony, and how Powhatan was crowned","Map work: Trace the Chesapeake Bay shoreline, as Smith did for his map","Timeline: Add \"John Smith leads Jamestown\" at 1608"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_91"}],"pages":"pp. 91–94","questions":["Why were the Indians amazed by Smith''s letter?","What was Smith''s rule about work?","How did Smith stop the swearing?"],"answers":["It seemed to \"speak\" and tell the colonists exactly what to give","If you will not work, you shall not eat","He had cold water poured down the offender''s sleeve"],"notes":"The book makes Powhatan comic. He was the powerful leader of about 30 tribes, and his return gift may have been his way of showing he did not accept being King James''s subject."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-4-3","title":"Chapter 25: Smith Wounded","steps":["Read Chapter XXV, \"Smith Wounded,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Pocahontas warned Smith, how Smith was hurt, and what happened in the Starving Time","Map work: Trace the James River from Jamestown down to Chesapeake Bay; find New England on a map","Timeline: Add \"Starving Time at Jamestown\" at winter 1609–1610; add \"Smith names New England\" at 1614"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_94"}],"pages":"pp. 94–97","questions":["What kind of settlers did Smith beg the company to send?","How was Smith hurt?","What saved the Jamestown settlers just as they were leaving?"],"answers":["Farmers, carpenters, blacksmiths, masons, and woodcutters","Gunpowder in his boat exploded and burned him","Three ships arrived from England with food and new colonists"],"notes":"About 500 people were at Jamestown before the Starving Time and around 60 survived. Archaeologists have confirmed that some desperate settlers did resort to cannibalism; you may skip that line with young children."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-4-4","title":"Chapter 26: The Visit of Pocahontas to England","steps":["Read Chapter XXVI, \"The Visit of Pocahontas to England,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Jamestown began to thrive, and what happened to Pocahontas","Map work: Find Virginia and London; draw a tobacco plantation beside a river","Timeline: Add \"Pocahontas marries John Rolfe\" at 1614; add \"House of Burgesses meets\" and \"First Africans sold in Virginia\" at 1619"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_97"}],"pages":"pp. 97–102","questions":["How did Governor Dale get the lazy colonists to work?","What crop made Virginia rich?","What important thing happened at Jamestown in 1619 for self-government?"],"answers":["Each man had to work for his own food instead of sharing a common store","Tobacco","The House of Burgesses, the first elected assembly, met"],"notes":"Pocahontas was kidnapped and held as a hostage, which the book tells lightly. The Africans of 1619 were probably first treated as servants for a term; laws making slavery lifelong and inherited came in the following decades. Talk honestly about that 1619 contrast of freedom and slavery."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-4-5","title":"Chapter 27: Hudson and the Indians","steps":["Read Chapter XXVII, \"Hudson and the Indians,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Henry Hudson''s voyage and the beginning of New Netherland","Map work: Find the Hudson River, Albany, Manhattan Island, Delaware Bay, and Hudson Bay; find Port Royal in Acadia (Nova Scotia)","Timeline: Add \"Port Royal founded in Acadia\" at 1605 (the book says 1604); add \"Hudson sails up his river\" at 1609"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_102"}],"pages":"pp. 102–105","questions":["Why did the Indians call Hudson''s ship a \"great white bird\"?","Why did Hudson at first think the river might lead to Asia?","What happened to Hudson on his last voyage?"],"answers":["Because of its spreading white sails","The water was salty far upstream","His crew set him adrift in a small boat and he was never seen again"],"notes":"The \"fire water\" story reflects a real harm that alcohol trade did to Native communities. New Amsterdam was not formally founded until about 1624–1625."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-u5","title":"Unit 5: Pilgrims and Puritans (Chapters 28–35)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-5-1","title":"Chapter 28: The Mayflower","steps":["Read Chapter XXVIII, \"The Mayflower,\" aloud together","Narration: explain who the Pilgrims were and why they sailed on the Mayflower","Map work: Find England, Leyden in Holland, Delfshaven, Southampton, Plymouth (England), and Cape Cod","Timeline: Add \"Pilgrims move to Holland\" at 1608 (the book says 1607); add \"Mayflower sails\" at September 1620"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_105"}],"pages":"pp. 105–109","questions":["What happened to Squanto before the Pilgrims came?","Why did the Separatists want to leave Holland?","Why did the Mayflower sail alone?"],"answers":["He was kidnapped and taken to Spain and England, and when he came home his tribe had died of a plague","Their children were becoming Dutch, losing English ways","The Speedwell leaked and had to be left behind"],"notes":"The Mayflower carried both Separatists and others hired or seeking land; the Pilgrims called the others \"strangers.\""},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-5-2","title":"Chapter 29: Plymouth Rock","steps":["Read Chapter XXIX, \"Plymouth Rock,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the Pilgrims chose a place to settle and survived the first winter","Map work: Draw Cape Cod''s hook and mark Provincetown harbor and Plymouth","Timeline: Add \"Mayflower Compact signed\" at November 11, 1620; add \"Pilgrims land at Plymouth\" at December 1620"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_109"}],"pages":"pp. 109–113","questions":["What did the men promise in the paper they signed on the ship?","Why did the Pilgrims not mark the graves of those who died?","About how many Pilgrims died that first winter?"],"answers":["To obey laws made for the good of the colony (the Mayflower Compact)","So the Indians would not learn how few were left","Nearly half"],"notes":"The corn the Pilgrims took had been stored by the Wampanoag; they paid for it months later. The book does not name the Mayflower Compact but describes it. The rock landing story was first told over 100 years later."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-5-3","title":"Chapter 30: The First American Thanksgiving","steps":["Read Chapter XXX, \"The First American Thanksgiving,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Samoset, Squanto, the treaty with Massasoit, and the first Thanksgiving","Map work: Find Plymouth and the Wampanoag homeland in southeastern Massachusetts and Rhode Island","Timeline: Add \"Treaty with Massasoit\" at March 1621; add \"Harvest feast (the first Thanksgiving)\" at autumn 1621"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_113"}],"pages":"pp. 113–118","questions":["What did Samoset say when he walked into the village?","How did Squanto teach the Pilgrims to plant corn?","Who came to the harvest feast?"],"answers":["\"Welcome, Englishmen\"","Plant when oak leaves were as big as a mouse''s ear, with a fish in each hill","Massasoit and about ninety of his men"],"notes":"The pumpkin pies and naming of \"Thanksgiving Day\" are later legend; the 1621 feast was a harvest celebration, and the national holiday dates from 1863. Thanksgiving is now the fourth Thursday in November."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-5-4","title":"Chapter 31: The Snake Skin and the Bullets","steps":["Read Chapter XXXI, \"The Snake Skin and the Bullets,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Winslow nursing Massasoit, the snake skin, and Sunday meeting in Plymouth","Map work: Find Narragansett Bay and Salem, Massachusetts","Timeline: Add \"Massachusetts Bay settlers reach Salem\" at 1628"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_118"}],"pages":"pp. 118–122","questions":["What did Bradford send back inside the snake skin?","Why did the boys sit on the pulpit steps?","Why did the Plymouth colony grow slowly?"],"answers":["Powder and bullets","So everyone could see them and they would behave","Only church members could vote, and they were very strict"],"notes":"Squanto''s explanation of the snake skin was his own; he sometimes used his position for his own power. The Massachusetts seal''s \"Come over and help us\" shows how the English saw themselves, not how Native people did."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-5-5","title":"Chapter 32: The Beginning of Boston","steps":["Read Chapter XXXII, \"The Beginning of Boston,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Boston began and what kind of governor John Winthrop was","Map work: Find Boston, Charlestown, Weymouth, and Cambridge","Timeline: Add \"Boston founded\" at 1630; add \"Harvard College founded\" at 1636"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_122"}],"pages":"pp. 122–126","questions":["Why did Blackstone leave Plymouth?","How did Winthrop cure a man of stealing his firewood?","Who gave his books to the new college?"],"answers":["He would not let the Pilgrims force him to believe as they did","He sent him all the wood he needed until spring","John Harvard"],"notes":"The story that Boston''s crooked streets follow Blackstone''s cow paths is a legend."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-5-6","title":"Chapter 33: Stories of Two Ministers","steps":["Read Chapter XXXIII, \"Stories of Two Ministers,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about John Eliot''s Indian Bible and Roger Williams''s idea of religious freedom","Map work: Find Roxbury, Salem, and Providence; color Rhode Island","Timeline: Add \"Roger Williams founds Providence\" at 1636; add \"Eliot''s Indian Bible printed\" at 1663"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_126"}],"pages":"pp. 126–129","questions":["Why did the treasurer tie Eliot''s pay in knots?","How did the Massachusetts colonists earn money from the sea?","What two big ideas got Roger Williams in trouble?"],"answers":["So he would not give it all away before he got home","They caught fish and sent it to England (and traded lumber in the West Indies)","That people should be free to worship as they choose, and that the land belonged to the Indians"],"notes":"The book says no Massachusett speakers are left; today the Wampanoag are reviving their language, partly using Eliot''s Bible. The rum trade the chapter mentions was later tied to the slave trade."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-5-7","title":"Chapter 34: Williams and the Indians","steps":["Read Chapter XXXIV, \"Williams and the Indians,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Anne Hutchinson, the founding of Connecticut, and the Pequot War","Map work: Find Newport, Portsmouth and Dover in New Hampshire, the Connecticut River, Hartford, Saybrook, and New Haven","Timeline: Add \"Thomas Hooker founds Hartford\" at 1636; add \"Pequot War\" at 1636–1638; add \"New England Confederation\" at 1643"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_129"}],"pages":"pp. 129–134","questions":["Why was Anne Hutchinson banished?","How did Roger Williams help the colonists who had driven him out?","What new idea about voting did Hooker''s settlers bring to Connecticut?"],"answers":["She kept preaching and holding meetings when the Puritan leaders told her to stop","He paddled through a storm and persuaded the Narragansetts not to join the Pequots","A man did not have to be a church member to vote"],"notes":"The attack on the Pequot fort at Mystic burned hundreds of people, mostly women, children, and elders; the book calls it a massacre. Consider summarizing that part for younger children."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-5-8","title":"Chapter 35: The Quakers","steps":["Read Chapter XXXV, \"The Quakers,\" aloud together","Narration: tell who the Quakers were and how they were treated in Massachusetts","Map work: Find England and Massachusetts; mark Rhode Island as the refuge","Timeline: Add \"English Civil War and Cromwell''s Commonwealth\" at 1642–1660; add \"Quaker martyrs hanged in Boston\" at 1659–1661"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_134"}],"pages":"pp. 134–136","questions":["Why were Friends called Quakers?","Why did Quakers number the days instead of naming them?","How did the Navigation Act hurt the colonists?"],"answers":["They said people should quake at the thought of God''s wrath","The names came from old heathen gods","Goods had to be carried in English ships, which raised their prices"],"notes":"The first Navigation Act came in 1651, but the ban on colonial shipbuilding the book mentions is not accurate; colonial ships counted as English. The war began in 1642, not 1643."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-u6","title":"Unit 6: King Philip, New York, Pennsylvania, and Maryland (Chapters 36–40)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-6-1","title":"Chapter 36: The King-Killers","steps":["Read Chapter XXXVI, \"The King-Killers,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the hidden regicides and why Massasoit''s sons grew angry","Map work: Find New Haven, Hadley, and Mount Hope (Bristol, Rhode Island)","Timeline: Add \"Charles II restored to the throne\" at 1660; add \"Connecticut charter unites New Haven\" at 1662"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_136"}],"pages":"pp. 136–138","questions":["Who were the \"king-killers\"?","What did the chief show the colonist with the log?","Why did the Wampanoag feel cramped?"],"answers":["Judges who had condemned King Charles I to death","That his people had moved as far as they could and had no more room","Colonists had bought nearly all their land and would not let them hunt or fish on it"],"notes":"The log story is a traditional tale. Native people often understood a land sale as sharing use, not giving up all rights; that misunderstanding is real history."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-6-2","title":"Chapter 37: King Philip''s War","steps":["Read Chapter XXXVII, \"King Philip''s War,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how King Philip''s War began and ended","Map work: Find Swansea, Boston''s Beacon Hill, Deerfield, Hadley, and Mount Hope","Timeline: Add \"King Philip''s War\" at 1675–1676 (the book says to 1678)"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_138"}],"pages":"pp. 138–142","questions":["How did the warning signal reach Boston?","What happened at Bloody Brook?","What happened to Philip''s wife and son?"],"answers":["Signal fires were lit on Beacon Hill","Soldiers put their guns in a cart to eat grapes and were ambushed","They were captured, and his son was sold as a slave in the West Indies"],"notes":"This chapter is violent, describing killings of children and captives. Philip''s Wampanoag name was Metacom. The story of Goffe saving Hadley is a legend."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-6-3","title":"Chapter 38: The Beginning of New York","steps":["Read Chapter XXXVIII, \"The Beginning of New York,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how New Amsterdam began and how it became New York","Map work: Find Manhattan, Wall Street, the Bowery, Long Island, Wilmington, and New Jersey","Timeline: Add \"Minuit buys Manhattan\" at 1626; add \"English take New Amsterdam; renamed New York\" at 1664"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_142"}],"pages":"pp. 142–147","questions":["How did Wall Street get its name?","What happened to New Sweden?","Did Stuyvesant want to surrender? What happened?"],"answers":["A wooden wall (palisade) once stood there","Stuyvesant and the Dutch conquered it","No, he wanted to fight, but the people refused, so the city surrendered"],"notes":"The \"$24\" price is a 19th-century estimate. Mrs. Hutchinson''s surviving daughter Susanna was later returned. The Dutch retook New York in 1673 and gave it back for good in 1674, after the renaming."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-6-4","title":"Chapter 39: Penn and the Indians","steps":["Read Chapter XXXIX, \"Penn and the Indians,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how William Penn founded Pennsylvania and treated the Lenape","Map work: Find Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, Germantown, and Delaware","Timeline: Add \"Penn arrives; treaty under the elm\" at 1682"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_147"}],"pages":"pp. 147–152","questions":["What does \"Pennsylvania\" mean?","How were quarrels between a settler and an Indian to be judged?","What does \"Philadelphia\" mean?"],"answers":["Penn''s woods (Penn''s woodland)","By a jury of six Indians and six colonists","City of Brotherly Love"],"notes":"The great treaty is a cherished tradition with little written record. Later, Penn''s sons cheated the Lenape in the 1737 \"Walking Purchase.\" The snake story is a folk tale."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-6-5","title":"Chapter 40: The Catholics in Maryland","steps":["Read Chapter XL, \"The Catholics in Maryland,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Maryland began and what troubles Virginia had","Map work: Find the Potomac River, St. Marys, Annapolis, Baltimore; draw the Mason and Dixon line","Timeline: Add \"Maryland founded\" at 1634; add \"Virginia becomes a royal colony\" at 1624; add \"Mason-Dixon line surveyed\" at 1763–1767"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XL","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_152"}],"pages":"pp. 152–155","questions":["Why did Lord Baltimore want a colony?","Whom was Maryland named for?","What did the surveyors put at every mile of the Mason and Dixon line?"],"answers":["As a safe home for English Catholics","Queen Henrietta Maria","A stone post marked with the initial of each colony"],"notes":"The 1622 Powhatan attack killed about a quarter of Virginia''s colonists. The Mason-Dixon line later became the symbolic border between free and slave states."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-u7","title":"Unit 7: Southern Colonies and Colonial Troubles (Chapters 41–46)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-7-1","title":"Chapter 41: The Old Dominion","steps":["Read Chapter XLI, \"The Old Dominion,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why Virginia was called the Old Dominion and why the planters disliked the Navigation Act","Map work: Find Virginia and Jamestown; trace a tobacco ship''s route from a plantation dock to England and back","Timeline: Add \"Charles I beheaded\" at 1649; add \"Monarchy restored under Charles II\" at 1660"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_155"}],"pages":"pp. 155–157","questions":["What were the king''s supporters called, and what were the Puritans called?","Why did the Navigation Act make the planters poorer?","How did rich Virginia children get their schooling?"],"answers":["Cavaliers and Roundheads","Goods had to go on English ships, which charged high prices both ways","From private tutors, and some sons went to English universities"],"notes":"The governor who hoped for no schools or printing was William Berkeley (1671). Older children can discuss why a ruler might not want people educated."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-7-2","title":"Chapter 42: Bacon''s Rebellion","steps":["Read Chapter XLII, \"Bacon''s Rebellion,\" aloud together","Narration: tell the story of Bacon''s Rebellion","Map work: Find Jamestown and the frontier along the fall line of Virginia''s rivers","Timeline: Add \"Bacon''s Rebellion; Jamestown burned\" at 1676"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_157"}],"pages":"pp. 157–160","questions":["Why did the colonists choose Bacon as their leader?","What was the \"white-apron brigade\"?","What did King Charles say about Berkeley''s punishments?"],"answers":["Governor Berkeley would not defend them from Indian attacks","The wives of Berkeley''s men, whom Bacon placed in front of his troops","That he had killed more people than the king had for his own father''s murder"],"notes":"Bacon''s men attacked friendly as well as hostile tribes. Archaeologists have since found much of Jamestown''s original fort, which the book says had vanished."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-7-3","title":"Chapter 43: A Journey Inland","steps":["Read Chapter XLIII, \"A Journey Inland,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the founding of William and Mary College and Spotswood''s ride over the mountains","Map work: Find Williamsburg, the Blue Ridge Mountains, and the Shenandoah Valley","Timeline: Add \"College of William and Mary founded\" at 1693; add \"Spotswood''s Knights of the Golden Horseshoe\" at 1716"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_160"}],"pages":"pp. 160–162","questions":["What payment did the king ask for the college charter?","What happened when a deer saw itself in Spotswood''s mirror?","What did Spotswood''s coat of arms show?"],"answers":["Two copies of Latin verse each year","It smashed the mirror with its horns","A golden horseshoe"],"notes":"The claim that no white people were ever sold as servants in America after this is wrong; indentured and convict servants kept coming until the Revolution. Spotswood''s party crossed the Blue Ridge but not the Alleghenies."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-7-4","title":"Chapter 44: The Carolina Pirates","steps":["Read Chapter XLIV, \"The Carolina Pirates,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the Carolinas and Georgia were founded and what they grew","Map work: Find North and South Carolina, Charleston, Savannah, St. Augustine, and Frederica on St. Simons Island","Timeline: Add \"Carolina granted to the lords proprietors\" at 1663; add \"Oglethorpe founds Georgia\" at 1733"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_162"}],"pages":"pp. 162–166","questions":["What crops made Carolina rich?","Why did Oglethorpe start Georgia?","What did the Indians say the eagle feathers and buffalo skin meant?"],"answers":["Rice, indigo, and tobacco (plus tar and turpentine)","To give poor debtors a fresh start","Love and protection"],"notes":"The planter''s daughter was Eliza Lucas Pinckney. Rice growing depended on the skill and labor of enslaved West Africans, who knew rice farming from home; the book mentions only that planters \"bought many negroes.\""},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-7-5","title":"Chapter 45: Charter Oak","steps":["Read Chapter XLV, \"Charter Oak,\" aloud together","Narration: tell the story of the Charter Oak and what happened to Governor Andros","Map work: Find Hartford, Boston, and New Haven","Timeline: Add \"Charter hidden in the oak\" at 1687; add \"Glorious Revolution; William and Mary\" at 1688–1689"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_166"}],"pages":"pp. 166–170","questions":["How did Captain Wadsworth save the Connecticut charter?","Why did King Charles let Massachusetts keep its pine-tree shillings?","How was Andros caught trying to escape from prison?"],"answers":["He threw his cloak over the candles and hid the charter in a hollow oak in the dark","He was told the tree was the Royal Oak that had hidden him","His big feet gave him away under his lady''s dress"],"notes":"The Charter Oak and Andros-in-a-dress stories are traditions without firm proof. Leisler was hanged in 1691; his name was cleared in 1695."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-7-6","title":"Chapter 46: Salem Witches","steps":["Read Chapter XLVI, \"Salem Witches,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what happened during the Salem witch trials and how they ended","Map work: Find Salem and Boston","Timeline: Add \"Salem witch trials\" at 1692; add \"Boylston inoculates against smallpox\" at 1721"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_170"}],"pages":"pp. 170–174","questions":["How many people were put to death in the Salem trials?","Who was the famous minister who later admitted he was wrong?","What did Dr. Boylston try, even though people threatened him?"],"answers":["Nineteen (by hanging; one more man was pressed to death)","Cotton Mather","Inoculation against smallpox"],"notes":"The accused were not mostly senile old women; many were ordinary adults. Boylston''s method was inoculation with smallpox itself; true vaccination came later. Cotton Mather learned of it partly from Onesimus, an enslaved African man."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-u8","title":"Unit 8: New France and the French and Indian Wars (Chapters 47–56)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-8-1","title":"Chapter 47: Down the Mississippi","steps":["Read Chapter XLVII, \"Down the Mississippi,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Marquette and Joliet traveled down the Mississippi","Map work: Trace their route: Green Bay, Fox River, Wisconsin River, down the Mississippi past the Missouri and Ohio to the Arkansas, then back by the Illinois and Chicago rivers","Timeline: Add \"Champlain founds Quebec\" at 1608; add \"Marquette and Joliet on the Mississippi\" at 1673"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_174"}],"pages":"pp. 174–180","questions":["Why did the Iroquois hate the French?","What were the French woodsmen called?","How did Marquette and Joliet know the river flowed to the Gulf of Mexico?"],"answers":["Champlain helped their Algonquin enemies defeat them","Voyageurs or coureurs de bois","It kept flowing south, and they met Indians with European goods from the south"],"notes":"Port Royal was founded in 1605. The dog-feast detail is from Marquette''s own journal."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-8-2","title":"Chapter 48: La Salle''s Adventures","steps":["Read Chapter XLVIII, \"La Salle''s Adventures,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about La Salle''s journeys and how New Orleans began","Map work: Find Peoria, the Falls of St. Anthony (Minneapolis), the mouth of the Mississippi, Matagorda Bay in Texas, Biloxi, and New Orleans","Timeline: Add \"La Salle claims Louisiana\" at 1682; add \"New Orleans founded\" at 1718"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_180"}],"pages":"pp. 180–183","questions":["What is a portage?","Whom was Louisiana named for?","Why did the Natchez attack come two days too soon?"],"answers":["A place where canoes are carried overland between waters","King Louis XIV of France","A boy burned two of his father''s counting sticks"],"notes":"The Natchez attacked in 1729 after a French officer tried to seize their sacred village land; the French then destroyed the Natchez nation."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-8-3","title":"Chapter 49: Indians on the Warpath","steps":["Read Chapter XLIX, \"Indians on the Warpath,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what happened in King William''s War, including the raid on Schenectady","Map work: Find Quebec, Montreal, Schenectady, Albany, Dover, Haverhill, and Port Royal","Timeline: Add \"King William''s War\" at 1689–1697"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter XLIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_183"}],"pages":"pp. 183–187","questions":["Why did the French want the Hudson valley?","Why was Schenectady caught by surprise?","How did the colonists try to strike back?"],"answers":["To control the river and push the English out; they claimed Verrazano saw it first","Its gates were left open on a cold night, guarded only by snowmen","They took Port Royal and attacked Quebec and Montreal (which failed)"],"notes":"This chapter describes torture, the killing of a baby, and Hannah Dustin scalping her captors for a bounty. Preview it and summarize for younger children; older ones can discuss why her statue is now debated."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-8-4","title":"Chapter 50: Two Wars With the French","steps":["Read Chapter L, \"Two Wars With the French,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Queen Anne''s War, the capture of Louisburg, and why Washington was sent west","Map work: Find Deerfield, Nova Scotia, Cape Breton Island, Louisburg, and the Ohio River","Timeline: Add \"Raid on Deerfield\" at 1704; add \"Treaty of Utrecht gives Acadia to Britain\" at 1713; add \"New Englanders take Louisburg\" at 1745"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter L","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_187"}],"pages":"pp. 187–191","questions":["What was Acadia renamed?","Why were New Englanders so upset by the peace of 1748?","Why did the colonists want to cross the mountains?"],"answers":["Nova Scotia","Louisburg, which they had captured, was given back to France","Hunters described rich lands along the Ohio, and the population was growing"],"notes":"The story of the Deerfield bell is a legend; historians find no proof it happened."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-8-5","title":"Chapter 51: Washington''s Boyhood","steps":["Read Chapter LI, \"Washington''s Boyhood,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about George Washington''s boyhood and his work as a young surveyor","Map work: Find the Potomac and Rappahannock rivers, Mount Vernon, and Natural Bridge in Virginia","Timeline: Add \"George Washington born\" at February 22, 1732; add \"Washington surveys for Lord Fairfax\" at 1748"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_191"}],"pages":"pp. 191–195","questions":["What did Washington copy into his school notebook?","What did his mother say when he admitted killing her colt?","What was Washington''s first real job?"],"answers":["Rules of good behavior (civility)","She regretted the colt but was glad her son told the truth","Surveying land for Lord Fairfax"],"notes":"The cherry-tree story was invented by Parson Weems; the colt and Natural Bridge tales are also legends. The book mentions enslaved children only in passing; Washington''s family held many enslaved people."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-8-6","title":"Chapter 52: Washington''s Journey","steps":["Read Chapter LII, \"Washington''s Journey,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Washington''s winter journey to the French fort","Map work: Trace a route from Williamsburg to the forks of the Ohio (Pittsburgh), up the Allegheny to Venango and Fort Le Boeuf","Timeline: Add \"Washington carries the warning to the French\" at 1753–1754"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_195"}],"pages":"pp. 195–198","questions":["Whose estate did Washington inherit?","What happened when Washington crossed the Allegheny on a raft?","What did the French do to the fort the Virginians began?"],"answers":["His brother Lawrence''s estate, Mount Vernon","He was jerked into the icy river and his clothes froze","Drove the Virginians off, finished it, and named it Fort Duquesne"],"notes":"The \"Indian queen\" was Queen Aliquippa, a Seneca leader."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-8-7","title":"Chapter 53: Washington''s First Battle","steps":["Read Chapter LIII, \"Washington''s First Battle,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Fort Necessity and the question the Indian chief asked","Map work: Find Great Meadows (Fort Necessity) in western Pennsylvania and Albany, New York","Timeline: Add \"Washington surrenders Fort Necessity\" at July 4, 1754"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_198"}],"pages":"pp. 198–199","questions":["What were Washington''s men allowed to take when they surrendered?","What did older Washington say about loving the sound of bullets?","What did the chief ask about the land?"],"answers":["Their flag and their weapons","\"If I said so, it was when I was young\"","If the French own the north and the English the south, where is the land of the Indians?"],"notes":"The first skirmish, at Jumonville Glen, helped start a world war; the French said it killed an envoy. This short chapter pairs well with an extra map activity."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-8-8","title":"Chapter 54: Stories of Franklin","steps":["Read Chapter LIV, \"Stories of Franklin,\" aloud together","Narration: tell some stories of Benjamin Franklin, from the whistle to the kite","Map work: Find Boston, New York, and Philadelphia; trace Franklin''s trip between them","Timeline: Add \"Benjamin Franklin born\" at 1706; add \"Franklin''s kite experiment\" at 1752"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_199"}],"pages":"pp. 199–206","questions":["What does \"paying too much for the whistle\" mean?","How did Franklin get his first writings into his brother''s paper?","What useful invention came from the kite experiment?"],"answers":["Paying more than something is worth","He disguised his handwriting and slipped them under the shop door","The lightning rod"],"notes":"Franklin''s own Autobiography tells many of these stories and makes a good follow-up for older children."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-8-9","title":"Chapter 55: Braddock''s Defeat","steps":["Read Chapter LV, \"Braddock''s Defeat,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why Braddock''s army was defeated and what happened to the Acadians","Map work: Find Fort Duquesne, the Monongahela River, Lake George, Oswego, and Nova Scotia; trace the Acadians to Louisiana","Timeline: Add \"Albany Congress, ''Join or Die''\" at 1754; add \"Braddock''s defeat\" and \"Expulsion of the Acadians\" at 1755"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_206"}],"pages":"pp. 206–210","questions":["What did Franklin''s \"Join or die\" snake stand for?","Why were Braddock''s soldiers easy targets?","How did Washington show care for the tired men on the retreat?"],"answers":["The colonies had to unite or be destroyed","They wore red and marched in close ranks while the enemy hid behind trees","He walked and let them load their muskets and baggage on his horse"],"notes":"The Acadian expulsion broke up families; their descendants in Louisiana are the Cajuns."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-8-10","title":"Chapter 56: Wolfe at Quebec","steps":["Read Chapter LVI, \"Wolfe at Quebec,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Wolfe took Quebec and what the Treaty of Paris changed","Map work: Find Pittsburgh, Louisburg, Quebec and the Plains of Abraham, Detroit, and Michilimackinac; shade what France lost in 1763","Timeline: Add \"Wolfe takes Quebec\" at 1759; add \"Treaty of Paris\" and \"Pontiac''s War\" at 1763"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_210"}],"pages":"pp. 210–214","questions":["How did Wolfe get his army to the Plains of Abraham?","How was Pittsburgh named?","How did the Indians get into the fort at Michilimackinac?"],"answers":["He drifted downriver at night, answered the sentries in French, and climbed a steep path","For William Pitt","They chased a ball game close to the gates and dashed in"],"notes":"Pontiac was killed in 1769, six years later. Ponce de Leon reached Florida in 1513."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-u9","title":"Unit 9: The Road to Revolution (Chapters 57–62)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-9-1","title":"Chapter 57: How England Treated Her Colonies","steps":["Read Chapter LVII, \"How England Treated Her Colonies,\" aloud together","Narration: explain the trade laws England made and why the colonists hated search warrants","Map work: Find Boston, Narragansett Bay, and Providence; mark Gaspee Point","Timeline: Add \"James Otis speaks against the writs of assistance\" at 1761; add \"Burning of the Gaspee\" at 1772"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_214"}],"pages":"pp. 214–218","questions":["Could a colonial farmer sell a hoe he made to a neighbor in another colony?","Why were the big straight trees saved?","What was James Otis''s main argument?"],"answers":["No","For masts for England''s ships","The colonists had no seats in Parliament, so laws they had no say in did not bind them"],"notes":"Many of these laws were loosely enforced for decades, which is why smuggling flourished. The book presents only the colonists'' side; Britain saw the laws as fair payment for protection."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-9-2","title":"Chapter 58: The Stamp Tax","steps":["Read Chapter LVIII, \"The Stamp Tax,\" aloud together","Narration: explain why Parliament passed the Stamp Act and how Franklin tried to stop it","Map work: Find London and Philadelphia; trace the month-long Atlantic crossing","Timeline: Add \"Stamp Act passed\" at 1765"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_218"}],"pages":"pp. 218–220","questions":["Why did Britain think the colonists should pay new taxes?","What had to be printed on stamped paper?","What was the point of Franklin''s red-hot poker story?"],"answers":["To pay war debts and the cost of an army to defend the new lands","Newspapers and legal papers such as deeds","Britain wanted the colonists to pay even for the trouble of harming them"],"notes":"The Sugar Act (1764) and Stamp Act (1765) were separate laws."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-9-3","title":"Chapter 59: The Anger of the Colonies","steps":["Read Chapter LIX, \"The Anger of the Colonies,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the colonists resisted the Stamp Act until it was repealed","Map work: Find Williamsburg, New York City, and Bowling Green in lower Manhattan","Timeline: Add \"Patrick Henry''s speech; Stamp Act Congress\" at 1765; add \"Stamp Act repealed\" at 1766"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_220"}],"pages":"pp. 220–224","questions":["What did the Sons of Liberty do?","What does \"Taxation without representation is tyranny\" mean?","Why did British merchants want the Stamp Act repealed?"],"answers":["Warned the stamp sellers not to sell the stamped paper","It is unjust to be taxed by a government in which you have no vote","The colonists stopped buying British goods and their business suffered"],"notes":"The book blends two speeches: \"If this be treason\" (1765) and \"Give me liberty, or give me death\" (1775). Both come to us from later memories."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-9-4","title":"Chapter 60: The Boston Tea Party","steps":["Read Chapter LX, \"The Boston Tea Party,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the Townshend taxes, the Boston Massacre, and the Boston Tea Party","Map work: Find Boston Harbor, Griffin''s Wharf, Faneuil Hall, and the Old South Meeting House","Timeline: Add \"Boston Massacre\" at March 5, 1770; add \"Boston Tea Party\" at December 16, 1773"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_224"}],"pages":"pp. 224–228","questions":["Why did the colonists not want the king to pay their governors and judges?","What did Samuel Adams whisper to his friends?","Why did the colonists refuse cheap tea?"],"answers":["Governors paid by the king would only try to please him","\"Both regiments or none\"","They objected to any tax at all, not to the price"],"notes":"Crispus Attucks, a man of African and Native descent, was among those killed in the Boston Massacre. John Adams defended the soldiers in court. The Tea Party men disguised as Mohawks."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-9-5","title":"Chapter 61: The Minutemen","steps":["Read Chapter LXI, \"The Minutemen,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the colonies came together after the Intolerable Acts","Map work: Find Philadelphia and Carpenter''s Hall, Marblehead, Salem, and Cambridge","Timeline: Add \"Intolerable Acts\" at 1774; add \"First Continental Congress\" at September 1774"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_228"}],"pages":"pp. 228–233","questions":["Why were the minutemen called that?","How did women help the cause?","Which colony did not send delegates to the First Congress?"],"answers":["They were ready to march at a minute''s notice","They gave up British goods and spun and wove their own cloth","Georgia"],"notes":"The \"Olive Branch Petition\" came from the Second Congress in 1775."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-9-6","title":"Chapter 62: The Battle of Lexington","steps":["Read Chapter LXII, \"The Battle of Lexington,\" aloud together","Narration: tell what happened on April 19, 1775","Map work: Trace the road from Boston through Lexington to Concord and back to Charlestown","Timeline: Add \"Battles of Lexington and Concord\" at April 19, 1775"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_233"}],"pages":"pp. 233–237","questions":["What did the two lanterns in the Old North Church mean?","Why were Samuel Adams and John Hancock hiding in Lexington?","How did the minutemen fight the British on the march back?"],"answers":["The British were moving by water","Gage wanted to arrest them and send them to England for trial","From behind trees, walls, and barns along the road"],"notes":"Revere did not shout \"The British are coming!\" (colonists still thought of themselves as British) and was captured before Concord; others carried the news on."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-u10","title":"Unit 10: The War Begins (Chapters 63–68)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-10-1","title":"Chapter 63: Bunker Hill","steps":["Read Chapter LXIII, \"Bunker Hill,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Ticonderoga, Washington''s choice as general, and the battle of Bunker Hill","Map work: Find Fort Ticonderoga, Crown Point, Breed''s Hill, Bunker Hill, and Charlestown","Timeline: Add \"Ticonderoga taken\" at May 10, 1775; add \"Washington named commander\" and \"Bunker Hill\" at June 1775"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_237"}],"pages":"pp. 237–242","questions":["Why was capturing Ticonderoga so useful?","What pay did Washington accept?","Why did the Americans finally have to retreat?"],"answers":["It gave the patriots cannon and powder","None, only his expenses","They ran out of gunpowder"],"notes":"Ethan Allen''s famous words may have been polished later. The battle was mostly fought on Breed''s Hill."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-10-2","title":"Chapter 64: The Boston Boys","steps":["Read Chapter LXIV, \"The Boston Boys,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Washington trained the army at Cambridge and what the Boston boys told General Gage","Map work: Find Cambridge, Boston Common, and Marblehead","Timeline: Add \"Washington takes command at Cambridge\" at July 3, 1775"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_242"}],"pages":"pp. 242–245","questions":["How did Washington fool spies about his gunpowder?","How did Washington teach the proud corporal a lesson?","What did Gage say about the boys?"],"answers":["He filled barrels with sand and put a little powder on top","He got off his horse and helped lift the log himself","Children breathe in a love of liberty with the air"],"notes":"The corporal and Boston boys stories are popular traditions."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-10-3","title":"Chapter 65: The British Leave Boston","steps":["Read Chapter LXV, \"The British Leave Boston,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the British were forced out of Boston and how Fort Moultrie held out","Map work: Find Montreal, Quebec, Dorchester Heights, Halifax, and Charleston with Fort Moultrie on Sullivan''s Island","Timeline: Add \"Attack on Quebec fails\" at December 31, 1775; add \"British leave Boston\" at March 17, 1776; add \"Fort Moultrie holds\" at June 28, 1776"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_245"}],"pages":"pp. 245–248","questions":["What did the British see when they woke up and looked at Dorchester Heights?","Why did British cannon balls do little harm to Fort Moultrie?","Why did Sergeant Jasper turn down a promotion?"],"answers":["American cannon aimed down at them","They sank into the soft palmetto logs and sand","He could not read or write and felt unfit to be an officer"],"notes":"Washington''s letter about prisoners went to the British General Gage, not to the American General Gates as the book says. The cannon came from Ticonderoga thanks to Henry Knox."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-10-4","title":"Chapter 66: Declaration of Independence","steps":["Read Chapter LXVI, \"Declaration of Independence,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the Declaration of Independence was written and signed","Map work: Find Philadelphia and Independence Hall, Staten Island, and Valcour Island on Lake Champlain","Timeline: Add \"Common Sense published\" at January 1776; add \"Declaration of Independence adopted\" at July 4, 1776"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_248"}],"pages":"pp. 248–253","questions":["Who wrote most of the Declaration?","What did Franklin mean by \"hang together, or hang separately\"?","Why did Washington refuse letters addressed to \"George Washington, Esq.\"?"],"answers":["Thomas Jefferson","They must stay united, or the king would hang them one by one as traitors","Accepting them would treat him as a private man, not the general of a nation"],"notes":"The boy shouting \"Ring, grandpa!\" is a legend from an 1840s story; most delegates signed on August 2. Notice the bell''s words about liberty for \"all the inhabitants\" while slavery continued; older children can discuss it."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-10-5","title":"Chapter 67: A Lady''s Way of Helping","steps":["Read Chapter LXVII, \"A Lady''s Way of Helping,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the retreat from New York, Mrs. Murray''s dinner, and Nathan Hale","Map work: Find Long Island, Brooklyn Heights, the East River, Kips Bay, White Plains, Forts Washington and Lee, and Hackensack","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Long Island\" at August 27, 1776; add \"Nathan Hale hanged\" at September 22, 1776"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_253"}],"pages":"pp. 253–258","questions":["How did Washington get his army off Brooklyn Heights?","How did Mrs. Murray help Putnam''s men escape?","What did Nathan Hale say before he died?"],"answers":["He moved them across the river at night under cover of fog","She kept General Howe at her house for hours with food and talk","\"I regret only that I have but one life to lose for my country\""],"notes":"Historians doubt that Mrs. Murray''s lunch changed much. The prison-ship deaths were real and terrible."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-10-6","title":"Chapter 68: Christmas Eve","steps":["Read Chapter LXVIII, \"Christmas Eve,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Washington crossed the Delaware and won at Trenton and Princeton","Map work: Trace the route across the Delaware River to Trenton, then to Princeton and Morristown","Timeline: Add \"Crossing the Delaware; Battle of Trenton\" at December 25–26, 1776; add \"Battle of Princeton\" at January 3, 1777"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_258"}],"pages":"pp. 258–262","questions":["How did Washington keep the British from following him across the Delaware?","Who rowed the boats across the icy river?","How did Washington slip away from Cornwallis at Trenton?"],"answers":["He took every boat for a hundred miles","Marblehead fishermen","He left campfires burning and marched off by back roads in the night"],"notes":"The Hessians were tired, not mostly drunk; that story is doubtful. Prescott was captured in July 1777."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-u11","title":"Unit 11: Saratoga to Stony Point (Chapters 69–74)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-11-1","title":"Chapter 69: The Fight at Bennington","steps":["Read Chapter LXIX, \"The Fight at Bennington,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Lafayette and other foreigners came to help, and about the fight at Bennington","Map work: Find Paris, Brandywine Creek (Chadds Ford), Germantown, Ticonderoga, Fort Edward, and Bennington, Vermont","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Bennington\" at August 16, 1777; add \"Battle of Brandywine; British take Philadelphia\" at September 1777"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_262"}],"pages":"pp. 262–267","questions":["Why did Lafayette sail secretly?","What did Franklin mean when he said Philadelphia had taken Howe?","What did Stark tell his men before the battle?"],"answers":["The French king forbade him to leave","Howe and his officers grew lazy enjoying parties in the city","\"We beat them today or Molly Stark''s a widow\""],"notes":"The book praises a father glad his son died bravely; you might talk about how families felt such losses. Bennington was actually fought just over the line in New York."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-11-2","title":"Chapter 70: Burgoyne''s Surrender","steps":["Read Chapter LXX, \"Burgoyne''s Surrender,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Burgoyne was trapped and surrendered at Saratoga","Map work: Find Fort Stanwix (Rome, New York), Oriskany, the Mohawk River, Bemis Heights, and Saratoga","Timeline: Add \"Stars and Stripes adopted\" at June 14, 1777; add \"Burgoyne surrenders at Saratoga\" at October 17, 1777"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_267"}],"pages":"pp. 267–271","questions":["How did Arnold frighten St. Leger''s army away?","What were the first stars and stripes at Fort Stanwix made from?","How was Schuyler kind to Burgoyne?"],"answers":["He sent a lad who pointed at the leaves to show how many Americans were coming","An old shirt, a blue jacket, and a red petticoat","He forgave him for burning his house"],"notes":"The Betsy Ross story comes from her grandson''s 1870 account and is not proven. The battles are now called Freeman''s Farm and Bemis Heights. Joseph Brant (Thayendanegea) was a Mohawk leader allied with Britain."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-11-3","title":"Chapter 71: The Winter at Valley Forge","steps":["Read Chapter LXXI, \"The Winter at Valley Forge,\" aloud together","Narration: tell why Saratoga mattered and what the army suffered at Valley Forge","Map work: Find Valley Forge, northwest of Philadelphia, and Baltimore","Timeline: Add \"France allies with the United States\" at February 1778; add \"Winter at Valley Forge\" at 1777–1778"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_271"}],"pages":"pp. 271–275","questions":["How did the victory at Saratoga help the Americans in France?","How did Martha Washington help the soldiers?","What was the Conway cabal?"],"answers":["It persuaded France to openly help them","She knitted stockings and visited the soldiers with clothing","A plot to replace Washington with Gates"],"notes":"The story of Washington praying in the snow comes from a single later account and is doubted by many historians."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-11-4","title":"Chapter 72: The Quaker Woman","steps":["Read Chapter LXXII, \"The Quaker Woman,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Lydia Darrah, Baron Steuben, and the battle of Monmouth","Map work: Find Philadelphia, Valley Forge, and Monmouth, New Jersey","Timeline: Add \"Steuben drills the army\" at spring 1778; add \"Battle of Monmouth\" at June 28, 1778"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_275"}],"pages":"pp. 275–279","questions":["How did Lydia Darrah get past the British to warn the Americans?","What did Steuben learn about American soldiers?","What did Molly Pitcher do at Monmouth?"],"answers":["She said she needed flour and got a pass to go buy some","They wanted to be told the reason before they would obey","Took her fallen husband''s place loading and firing the cannon"],"notes":"\"Molly Pitcher\" is probably a blend of several women; Mary Ludwig Hays is usually named. Lee was court-martialed for disobedience, but not proven a traitor."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-11-5","title":"Chapter 73: Putnam''s Adventures","steps":["Read Chapter LXXIII, \"Putnam''s Adventures,\" aloud together","Narration: tell some of Israel Putnam''s adventures, from the wolf den to the French and Indian War","Map work: Find West Point, Newport, Fort Edward, Ticonderoga, and Lake Ontario","Timeline: Add \"Putnam captured and nearly burned\" at 1758; add \"French fleet fails at Newport\" at August 1778"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_279"}],"pages":"pp. 279–283","questions":["How did Putnam''s friends know when to pull him out of the wolf den?","How did Putnam discover what was killing the sentries?","How did Putnam wreck the French ship?"],"answers":["He tied a rope to his foot and they pulled when it twitched or they heard a shot","He shot a \"bear\" that turned out to be an enemy dressed in a bearskin","He drove wedges behind its rudder so it could not steer"],"notes":"The wolf-den story is a famous legend from Putnam''s early biography. The capture scene includes torture; you may summarize it."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-11-6","title":"Chapter 74: Indian Cruelty","steps":["Read Chapter LXXIV, \"Indian Cruelty,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Stony Point, the frontier raids, and George Rogers Clark''s march","Map work: Find Horseneck (Greenwich, Connecticut), Stony Point, the Wyoming Valley in Pennsylvania, Cherry Valley in New York, and Vincennes in Indiana","Timeline: Add \"Wyoming and Cherry Valley raids\" at 1778; add \"Clark takes Vincennes\" at February 1779; add \"Wayne storms Stony Point\" at July 16, 1779"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_283"}],"pages":"pp. 283–288","questions":["How did the Americans get close to Stony Point without being seen?","What was Burke''s point about the lions and bears?","How did the little drummer boy cross the deep water?"],"answers":["A man who sold strawberries in the camp gave the password and distracted the sentry","You cannot let fierce warriors loose and then expect them to be gentle","He floated on his drum"],"notes":"The strawberry seller was Pompey Lamb, an enslaved man. Sullivan''s expedition destroyed Iroquois towns and crops, causing great hunger among Native families; the book tells only one side. Some details of the Wyoming massacre are exaggerated."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-u12","title":"Unit 12: The Frontier, the Sea, and the South (Chapters 75–80)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-12-1","title":"Chapter 75: Boone in Kentucky","steps":["Read Chapter LXXV, \"Boone in Kentucky,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about Daniel Boone, the rescue of the girls, and his escape from captivity","Map work: Find the Cumberland Gap, Boonesborough in Kentucky, the Ohio River, Detroit, and Missouri","Timeline: Add \"Boonesborough founded\" at 1775; add \"Boone captured and escapes\" at 1778"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_288"}],"pages":"pp. 288–293","questions":["How did Boone''s daughter leave a trail for her rescuers?","How did Boone save extra ammunition while a captive?","Why did Boone keep moving farther west?"],"answers":["She left footprints, broke twigs, and tied bits of her dress on briers","He cut bullets in half and used less powder","He felt crowded when too many settlers came"],"notes":"Elizabeth Zane''s powder run really happened at Fort Henry (Wheeling, West Virginia) in 1782, not Boonesborough. Kentucky''s name probably does not mean \"dark and bloody ground\"; that is a legend."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-12-2","title":"Chapter 76: Famous Sea Fights","steps":["Read Chapter LXXVI, \"Famous Sea Fights,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about John Paul Jones and the fight of the Bonhomme Richard","Map work: Find Whitehaven in England, Kirkcaldy in Scotland, and Flamborough Head","Timeline: Add \"Bonhomme Richard defeats Serapis\" at September 23, 1779"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXVI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_293"}],"pages":"pp. 293–297","questions":["Where did the name Bonhomme Richard come from?","What did Jones answer when asked if he had surrendered?","What happened to the Bonhomme Richard after the battle?"],"answers":["Franklin''s \"Poor Richard\"","\"I have not yet begun to fight\"","It sank, and Jones moved his crew onto the Serapis"],"notes":"The famous reply was first written down years later. The Kirkcaldy minister''s prayer is a folk tale."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-12-3","title":"Chapter 77: The \"Swamp Fox\"","steps":["Read Chapter LXXVII, \"The Swamp Fox,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the war moved south, and the story of Marion''s sweet-potato dinner","Map work: Find Savannah, Augusta, Charleston, Camden, and the swamps along the Pee Dee River in South Carolina","Timeline: Add \"British take Savannah\" at 1778; add \"Charleston surrenders\" at May 1780; add \"Battle of Camden\" at August 1780"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXVII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_297"}],"pages":"pp. 297–302","questions":["What did \"Tarleton''s quarter\" come to mean?","What happened to Gates at Camden?","What did Marion say his sweetheart''s name was?"],"answers":["Being killed instead of spared","He was badly beaten and fled about eighty miles","Liberty"],"notes":"The potato dinner is a traditional story. Many enslaved people escaped to the British during the southern campaign after being promised freedom."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-12-4","title":"Chapter 78: The Poor Soldiers","steps":["Read Chapter LXXVIII, \"The Poor Soldiers,\" aloud together","Narration: explain why the soldiers suffered from lack of money and how Robert Morris helped","Map work: Find Morristown, New Jersey, and Philadelphia","Timeline: Add \"Hard winter at Morristown\" at 1779–1780"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXVIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_302"}],"pages":"pp. 302–304","questions":["What does \"not worth a continental\" mean?","Why did paper money lose its value?","What did the Philadelphia ladies send the soldiers?"],"answers":["Worthless","Congress had no gold or silver to back it, too much was printed, and the British printed fakes","Twenty-two thousand shirts"],"notes":"A short chapter; a good day to look at a picture of continental currency online."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-12-5","title":"Chapter 79: The Spy","steps":["Read Chapter LXXIX, \"The Spy,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Benedict Arnold turned traitor and how André was caught","Map work: Find West Point, the Hudson River, Tarrytown, White Plains, and Tappan","Timeline: Add \"Arnold''s treason discovered; André captured\" at September 1780"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXIX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_304"}],"pages":"pp. 304–309","questions":["What did Arnold plan to give the British?","Where did André''s captors find the secret papers?","What was the plan to capture Arnold after he fled?"],"answers":["The fort at West Point","In his boots","A soldier pretended to desert, joined Arnold''s regiment, and planned to seize him in his garden"],"notes":"The pretend deserter was Sergeant John Champe (the book spells it Campe)."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-12-6","title":"Chapter 80: A Traitor''s Death","steps":["Read Chapter LXXX, \"A Traitor''s Death,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Arnold lived out his life, and about the victories at Kings Mountain and Cowpens","Map work: Find New London, Connecticut; Kings Mountain on the North and South Carolina line; and Cowpens, South Carolina","Timeline: Add \"Battle of Kings Mountain\" at October 7, 1780; add \"Battle of Cowpens\" at January 17, 1781"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXX","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_309"}],"pages":"pp. 309–312","questions":["Why is one niche on the Saratoga monument empty?","How did the patriots win at Kings Mountain?","What did the lady mean when she said Colonel Washington could \"make his mark\"?"],"answers":["It was meant for Arnold, who became a traitor","They climbed up by three paths and fired from behind rocks and trees","He had wounded Tarleton, leaving a mark on him"],"notes":"Arnold''s deathbed words about his old uniform are a legend. The Saratoga monument''s empty niche is real; a separate \"Boot Monument\" honors Arnold''s wounded leg without naming him."}]},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-u13","title":"Unit 13: Victory and Farewell (Chapters 81–84)","lessons":[{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-13-1","title":"Chapter 81: Two Unselfish Women","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXI, \"Two Unselfish Women,\" aloud together","Narration: tell about the race to the Dan River and how Mrs. Steele and Mrs. Motte helped","Map work: Trace the race from the Catawba River to the Dan River; find Guilford Courthouse (Greensboro) and Eutaw Springs","Timeline: Add \"Race to the Dan\" at February 1781; add \"Battle of Guilford Courthouse\" at March 15, 1781"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXI","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_312"}],"pages":"pp. 312–315","questions":["What did Mrs. Steele give General Greene?","How did Mrs. Motte help drive the British out of her house?","How did Greene describe his way of fighting?"],"answers":["Warm clothes, food, and all her savings","She let Lee set it on fire and gave him a bow and arrows to shoot flaming wood onto the roof","\"We fight, get beat, rise and fight again\""],"notes":"Fort Motte was taken in May 1781. The book''s claim that the British were cruel \"all through\" is one-sided; both patriots and loyalists committed cruelties in the southern backcountry war."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-13-2","title":"Chapter 82: The Surrender of Cornwallis","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXII, \"The Surrender of Cornwallis,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Washington and the French trapped Cornwallis at Yorktown","Map work: Find New York, Mount Vernon, Chesapeake Bay, and Yorktown; trace Washington''s march south","Timeline: Add \"New London burned\" at September 1781; add \"Cornwallis surrenders at Yorktown\" at October 19, 1781"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_315"}],"pages":"pp. 315–319","questions":["How did Washington fool Clinton about his plans?","Whose fleet blocked Cornwallis by sea?","Why did Washington have the sword handed to General Lincoln?"],"answers":["He let letters saying he would attack New York fall into British hands","The French fleet under De Grasse","Cornwallis sent a deputy, and the British had shamed Lincoln at Charleston"],"notes":"The tune \"The World Turned Upside Down\" is a tradition with little proof. Enslaved people who had fled to the British were often returned to slavery after Yorktown."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-13-3","title":"Chapter 83: The British Flag Hauled Down","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXIII, \"The British Flag Hauled Down,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how the news of Yorktown was received and how Washington kept the army loyal at Newburgh","Map work: Find Newburgh, New York, and Paris; color the new United States from the Atlantic to the Mississippi as set in 1783","Timeline: Add \"Newburgh Address\" at March 1783; add \"Treaty of Paris recognizes independence\" at September 3, 1783"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXIII","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_319"}],"pages":"pp. 319–323","questions":["What did the Philadelphia watchman add to his cry of \"All''s well\"?","What did Washington say as he put on his glasses?","How did the boy get the British flag down from the greased pole?"],"answers":["\"And Cornwallis is taken!\"","His eyes had grown dim in the service of his country","He nailed cleats up the pole and climbed them one by one"],"notes":"The treaty did not include any Native nations, who had lived on and fought for the western lands it handed over."},{"id":"story-of-the-thirteen-colonies-13-4","title":"Chapter 84: Washington''s Farewell","steps":["Read Chapter LXXXIV, \"Washington''s Farewell,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Washington said goodbye to his officers and gave up his command","Map work: Find New York City (Fraunces Tavern), Annapolis, Mount Vernon, and Cincinnati, Ohio","Timeline: Add \"Washington resigns his command\" at December 23, 1783"],"links":[{"label":"Read Chapter LXXXIV","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/48051/pg48051-images.html#Page_323"}],"pages":"pp. 323–327","questions":["Why did the officers name their society after Cincinnatus?","What did Washington give Congress instead of asking for pay?","How were many soldiers paid for their service?"],"answers":["Like that Roman, Washington left his farm to save his country and went back to it afterward","An exact account of his expenses","With grants of land in the Northwest Territory"],"notes":"Finish by reviewing the whole timeline. Washington''s farm work at Mount Vernon was done largely by enslaved people; he arranged in his will for them to be freed after Martha''s death."}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"“The Story of the Thirteen Colonies” by H. A. Guerber (1898), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/48051. Lesson plan by Freerange."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (listing_id, version) do update set content = excluded.content;
insert into public.listings (id, seller_name, seller_kind, seller_verified, title, subjects, grade_min, grade_max, philosophies, format, price, description, includes, lessons, lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement, family_style, lesson_steps, cover, color, esa_eligible, created_at, package_id, package_version, license, attribution, outline) values ('l-lib-tales-from-shakespeare', 'Charles and Mary Lamb', 'open', false, 'Tales from Shakespeare', array['reading', 'writing']::text[], 3, 7, array['charlotte_mason', 'classical', 'traditional']::text[], 'online', 0, 'Charles and Mary Lamb''s beloved 1807 retellings of twenty Shakespeare plays, written so children could meet the stories before the plays themselves. Each long tale is split into two or three sittings: read a portion aloud, let your child tell it back, then draw a scene or copy a famous line. A classic first step into Shakespeare for ages 8 to 12, and the full text is free online.', array['45 lessons in 6 units covering all 20 tales', 'Every tale split into 2–3 read-aloud sittings with clear start and stop points', 'Narration plus a drawing, copywork, or map step in each lesson', 'Questions for every lesson, with answers', 'Parent notes flagging the tragedies and grown-up themes', 'Link to the free full text for every tale']::text[], 45, 3, 25, 'high', true, '{}'::text[], '🎭', '#9B6BD6', false, '2026-11-11T12:00:00.000Z', 'pk-lib-tales-from-shakespeare', 1, '{"kind":"public_domain"}'::jsonb, '“Tales from Shakespeare” by Charles Lamb and Mary Lamb (1807), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/573. Lesson plan by Freerange.', '[{"title":"Unit 1: The Tempest to Much Ado about Nothing","lessons":["Lesson 1: The Tempest, Part 1 of 2","Lesson 2: The Tempest, Part 2 of 2","Lesson 3: A Midsummer Night''s Dream, Part 1 of 2","Lesson 4: A Midsummer Night''s Dream, Part 2 of 2","Lesson 5: The Winter''s Tale, Part 1 of 2","Lesson 6: The Winter''s Tale, Part 2 of 2","Lesson 7: Much Ado about Nothing, Part 1 of 2","Lesson 8: Much Ado about Nothing, Part 2 of 2"]},{"title":"Unit 2: As You Like It to The Merchant of Venice","lessons":["Lesson 9: As You Like It, Part 1 of 3","Lesson 10: As You Like It, Part 2 of 3","Lesson 11: As You Like It, Part 3 of 3","Lesson 12: The Two Gentlemen of Verona, Part 1 of 2","Lesson 13: The Two Gentlemen of Verona, Part 2 of 2","Lesson 14: The Merchant of Venice, Part 1 of 2","Lesson 15: The Merchant of Venice, Part 2 of 2"]},{"title":"Unit 3: Cymbeline, King Lear, and Macbeth","lessons":["Lesson 16: Cymbeline, Part 1 of 2","Lesson 17: Cymbeline, Part 2 of 2","Lesson 18: King Lear, Part 1 of 3","Lesson 19: King Lear, Part 2 of 3","Lesson 20: King Lear, Part 3 of 3","Lesson 21: Macbeth, Part 1 of 2","Lesson 22: Macbeth, Part 2 of 2"]},{"title":"Unit 4: All''s Well that Ends Well to Measure for Measure","lessons":["Lesson 23: All''s Well that Ends Well, Part 1 of 2","Lesson 24: All''s Well that Ends Well, Part 2 of 2","Lesson 25: The Taming of the Shrew, Part 1 of 2","Lesson 26: The Taming of the Shrew, Part 2 of 2","Lesson 27: The Comedy of Errors, Part 1 of 2","Lesson 28: The Comedy of Errors, Part 2 of 2","Lesson 29: Measure for Measure, Part 1 of 2","Lesson 30: Measure for Measure, Part 2 of 2"]},{"title":"Unit 5: Twelfth Night, Timon of Athens, and Romeo and Juliet","lessons":["Lesson 31: Twelfth Night, Part 1 of 2","Lesson 32: Twelfth Night, Part 2 of 2","Lesson 33: Timon of Athens, Part 1 of 2","Lesson 34: Timon of Athens, Part 2 of 2","Lesson 35: Romeo and Juliet, Part 1 of 3","Lesson 36: Romeo and Juliet, Part 2 of 3","Lesson 37: Romeo and Juliet, Part 3 of 3"]},{"title":"Unit 6: Hamlet, Othello, and Pericles","lessons":["Lesson 38: Hamlet, Prince of Denmark, Part 1 of 3","Lesson 39: Hamlet, Prince of Denmark, Part 2 of 3","Lesson 40: Hamlet, Prince of Denmark, Part 3 of 3","Lesson 41: Othello, Part 1 of 2","Lesson 42: Othello, Part 2 of 2","Lesson 43: Pericles, Prince of Tyre, Part 1 of 3","Lesson 44: Pericles, Prince of Tyre, Part 2 of 3","Lesson 45: Pericles, Prince of Tyre, Part 3 of 3"]}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set seller_name = excluded.seller_name, seller_kind = excluded.seller_kind, seller_verified = excluded.seller_verified, title = excluded.title, subjects = excluded.subjects, grade_min = excluded.grade_min, grade_max = excluded.grade_max, philosophies = excluded.philosophies, format = excluded.format, price = excluded.price, description = excluded.description, includes = excluded.includes, lessons = excluded.lessons, lessons_per_week = excluded.lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson = excluded.minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement = excluded.parent_involvement, family_style = excluded.family_style, lesson_steps = excluded.lesson_steps, cover = excluded.cover, color = excluded.color, esa_eligible = excluded.esa_eligible, package_id = excluded.package_id, package_version = excluded.package_version, license = excluded.license, attribution = excluded.attribution, outline = excluded.outline;
insert into public.package_releases (listing_id, version, content) values ('l-lib-tales-from-shakespeare', 1, '{"units":[{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-u1","title":"Unit 1: The Tempest to Much Ado about Nothing","lessons":[{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-1-1","title":"Lesson 1: The Tempest, Part 1 of 2","steps":["Read “The Tempest” aloud from the beginning until Prospero leads the spell-bound Ferdinand into his cave","Have your child tell back how Prospero and Miranda came to the island","Draw Ariel playing a trick on Caliban, or the ship in the storm"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Tempest”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#tempest"}],"questions":["Who put Prospero and baby Miranda into a small boat to die at sea?","Who secretly packed food and books into their boat?","Why did Prospero pretend to be harsh to Ferdinand?"],"answers":["His brother Antonio, with help from the King of Naples","Gonzalo, a kind lord of the court","To test whether Ferdinand''s love for Miranda was true"],"notes":"Before you start, tell your child that every story in this book is a play by Shakespeare, retold as a story for young readers."},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-1-2","title":"Lesson 2: The Tempest, Part 2 of 2","steps":["Read on from where Prospero sets Ferdinand to work piling logs, to the end of the tale","Narrate the ending: how Prospero treats his enemies, and what becomes of Ariel","Copy Miranda''s words: “O wonder! … It must surely be a brave world that has such people in it.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Tempest” (Part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#tempest"}],"questions":["What hard job did Prospero give Ferdinand?","What frightening shape did Ariel take to scare the king and Antonio?","What did Prospero do with his magic books and wand at the end?"],"answers":["Piling up heavy logs of wood","A harpy, a winged monster that made the feast vanish","He buried them deep in the earth and gave up magic"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-1-3","title":"Lesson 3: A Midsummer Night''s Dream, Part 1 of 2","steps":["Read “A Midsummer Night''s Dream” aloud from the start until Lysander runs after Helena and leaves Hermia asleep","Narrate who loves whom; sketch the four lovers'' names with arrows to keep them straight","Draw Puck playing one of his village pranks"],"links":[{"label":"Read “A Midsummer Night''s Dream”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#dream"}],"questions":["Why did Hermia and Lysander run away into the wood?","What does the juice of the little purple flower do?","What mistake did Puck make?"],"answers":["Hermia''s father wanted her to marry Demetrius, and the law said she could be put to death if she refused","Put on a sleeper''s eyelids, it makes them love the first thing they see when they wake","He put the juice on Lysander''s eyes instead of Demetrius''s"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-1-4","title":"Lesson 4: A Midsummer Night''s Dream, Part 2 of 2","steps":["Read on from where Hermia wakes alone in the wood, to the end","Narrate how Oberon and Puck set everything right","Draw Titania with the ass-headed clown and her fairies Pease-blossom, Cobweb, Moth, and Mustard-seed"],"links":[{"label":"Read “A Midsummer Night''s Dream” (Part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#dream"}],"questions":["What did Oberon put on the sleeping clown?","What did Titania give up to Oberon?","How does the storyteller say we should think of the whole adventure?"],"answers":["An ass''s (donkey''s) head","The little changeling boy","As if it were all a dream"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-1-5","title":"Lesson 5: The Winter''s Tale, Part 1 of 2","steps":["Read “The Winter''s Tale” aloud from the start through the oracle and Antigonus leaving the baby in Bohemia","Narrate what jealousy made King Leontes do","Draw brave Paulina laying the baby at the king''s feet"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Winter''s Tale”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#tale"}],"questions":["What did Leontes order Camillo to do to Polixenes, and what did Camillo do instead?","What did the oracle of Apollo say about Hermione?","What name was written on the paper pinned to the baby?"],"answers":["Poison him; Camillo warned Polixenes and escaped with him to Bohemia","That she was innocent (and Leontes was a jealous tyrant)","Perdita"],"notes":"This half is sad: young Mamillius dies of grief and Antigonus is killed by a bear. Reassure your child that the second half ends in joy."},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-1-6","title":"Lesson 6: The Winter''s Tale, Part 2 of 2","steps":["Read on from where a shepherd finds the baby Perdita, to the end","Narrate how Perdita is found to be the king''s daughter","Draw the statue scene, when Paulina draws back the curtain"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Winter''s Tale” (Part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#tale"}],"questions":["Where did Prince Florizel meet Perdita?","How did the old shepherd prove who Perdita really was?","What was the “statue” really?"],"answers":["At the old shepherd''s home (he saw her while hunting nearby)","He showed her baby mantle, a jewel, and the paper Antigonus had written","Queen Hermione herself, alive"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-1-7","title":"Lesson 7: Much Ado about Nothing, Part 1 of 2","steps":["Read “Much Ado about Nothing” aloud from the start until Beatrice overhears Hero in the arbour and decides to love Benedick","Narrate the prince''s merry trick on Benedick and Beatrice","Act out the scene where Benedick hides in the arbour and listens"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Much Ado about Nothing”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#nothing"}],"questions":["What rude name did Beatrice call Benedick that he hated most?","How did the friends make Benedick believe Beatrice loved him?","Who was getting married to Hero?"],"answers":["“The prince''s jester”","They talked about it near the arbour where he was sitting, so he would overhear","Claudio"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-1-8","title":"Lesson 8: Much Ado about Nothing, Part 2 of 2","steps":["Read on from Don John''s wicked plot, to the end","Narrate how Hero was wronged and how the truth came out","Draw Hero lifting her mask at the second wedding"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Much Ado about Nothing” (Part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#nothing"}],"questions":["Who dressed in Hero''s clothes and talked from her window?","Whose plan was it to say Hero had died?","How was Don John''s plot found out?"],"answers":["Margaret, Hero''s attendant","The wise old friar''s","Borachio was overheard boasting about it and confessed"]}]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-u2","title":"Unit 2: As You Like It to The Merchant of Venice","lessons":[{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-2-1","title":"Lesson 9: As You Like It, Part 1 of 3","steps":["Read “As You Like It” aloud from the start until Rosalind and Celia set out in disguise as Ganymede and Aliena","Narrate the wrestling match and why Rosalind was banished","Copy the good duke''s saying that he could find “tongues in trees, books in the running brooks, sermons in stones, and good in everything”"],"links":[{"label":"Read “As You Like It”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#like"}],"questions":["Who had taken the good duke''s dukedom from him?","What gift did Rosalind give Orlando after the wrestling?","Why did Celia leave her father''s palace?"],"answers":["His younger brother, Duke Frederick","A chain from her neck","She would not be parted from Rosalind, so she went with her into banishment"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-2-2","title":"Lesson 10: As You Like It, Part 2 of 3","steps":["Read on from where the cousins reach the forest of Arden, until Ganymede begins the pretend courtship with Orlando","Narrate how faithful old Adam helped Orlando","Draw Orlando carrying old Adam to the duke''s dinner under the trees"],"links":[{"label":"Read “As You Like It” (Part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#like"}],"questions":["What did old Adam give Orlando to help him escape?","Why did Orlando draw his sword on the duke and his friends?","What did Orlando carve on the trees of the forest?"],"answers":["His savings of five hundred crowns","He and Adam were starving, and he meant to take their food by force","Rosalind''s name"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-2-3","title":"Lesson 11: As You Like It, Part 3 of 3","steps":["Read on from the pretend courtship to the end of the tale","Narrate how Orlando saved his brother Oliver, and how everyone''s troubles were mended","Draw Orlando fighting the lioness while the green snake slides away"],"links":[{"label":"Read “As You Like It” (Part 3)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#like"}],"questions":["Which two dangers were threatening the sleeping Oliver?","Why did Ganymede faint?","What happened to the wicked Duke Frederick at the end?"],"answers":["A green snake around his neck and a hungry lioness","He heard Orlando had been wounded by the lioness","A hermit turned his heart; he gave the dukedom back and went to live in a religious house"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-2-4","title":"Lesson 12: The Two Gentlemen of Verona, Part 1 of 2","steps":["Read “The Two Gentlemen of Verona” aloud from the start until Proteus decides to betray Valentine''s secret to the duke","Narrate how two best friends came to be rivals","Draw Julia piecing together the torn-up letter"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Two Gentlemen of Verona”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#verona"}],"questions":["What did Julia do with Proteus''s first letter, and then what?","Why did Proteus''s father send him to Milan?","What was Valentine going to use to help Silvia escape?"],"answers":["She tore it up, then pieced the bits together and wrote him a kind letter","A friend said young men should travel, and Proteus pretended Valentine had invited him","A ladder of ropes"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-2-5","title":"Lesson 13: The Two Gentlemen of Verona, Part 2 of 2","steps":["Read on from where Proteus tells the duke Valentine''s plan, to the end","Narrate how Julia, dressed as the page Sebastian, won Proteus back","Discuss: was Proteus a good friend? What made him change?"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Two Gentlemen of Verona” (Part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#verona"}],"questions":["How did the duke trick Valentine into showing the rope-ladder?","What did Valentine become in the forest?","How did Proteus discover that his page was really Julia?"],"answers":["He asked for advice on wooing, then borrowed Valentine''s cloak and found the ladder under it","Captain of a band of robbers (outlaws)","She showed him the ring he had given her at Verona"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-2-6","title":"Lesson 14: The Merchant of Venice, Part 1 of 2","steps":["Read “The Merchant of Venice” aloud from the start until Bassanio hurries back to Venice and finds Antonio in prison","Narrate the strange bargain Antonio made with Shylock","Draw Antonio''s ships at sea, or Portia giving Bassanio her ring"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Merchant of Venice”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#venice"}],"questions":["Why did Bassanio need three thousand ducats?","What was the penalty in the bond if Antonio could not repay on time?","What bad news came in Antonio''s letter?"],"answers":["To go courting the rich lady Portia at Belmont in fitting style","A pound of his flesh","His ships were all lost, so the bond was forfeited"],"notes":"Shylock is drawn with the prejudice of Shakespeare''s day, and the book often calls him simply “the Jew.” Talk with your child about how unfair it is to judge a whole people by one character."},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-2-7","title":"Lesson 15: The Merchant of Venice, Part 2 of 2","steps":["Read on from the day of the trial to the end of the tale","Narrate how Portia saved Antonio in the court","Copy Portia''s words: “How far that little candle throws its beams, so shines a good deed in a naughty world.”"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Merchant of Venice” (Part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#venice"}],"questions":["How did Portia get into the court?","What clever point about the bond saved Antonio?","What did Portia ask Bassanio for as her reward?"],"answers":["Dressed as a young lawyer (Balthasar), with a letter from her cousin Bellario","The bond gave Shylock flesh but not one drop of blood","The ring she had given him"]}]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-u3","title":"Unit 3: Cymbeline, King Lear, and Macbeth","lessons":[{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-3-1","title":"Lesson 16: Cymbeline, Part 1 of 2","steps":["Read “Cymbeline” aloud from the start until Pisanio leaves Imogen with the queen''s bottle of “cordial”","Narrate the wicked wager Iachimo made","Draw Iachimo creeping out of the trunk to take the bracelet"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Cymbeline”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#cymbeline"}],"questions":["What tokens did Imogen and Posthumus give each other?","How did Iachimo trick Posthumus?","What was really in the bottle the queen gave Pisanio?"],"answers":["She gave him a diamond ring; he gave her a bracelet","He hid in a trunk in her room, wrote down what he saw, took the bracelet, and lied that Imogen gave it to him","A drug that makes a person sleep as if dead for a few hours (not real poison)"],"notes":"Posthumus orders Imogen killed in a jealous rage. The servant Pisanio refuses, and all ends well."},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-3-2","title":"Lesson 17: Cymbeline, Part 2 of 2","steps":["Read on from where Imogen finds the cave in the forest, to the end","Narrate how all the lost people were found again before the king","Draw the brothers covering “Fidele” with leaves and flowers"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Cymbeline” (Part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#cymbeline"}],"questions":["Who were the young hunters Polydore and Cadwal, really?","What boon (favor) did Imogen ask from the king?","How did Posthumus help Britain in the battle?"],"answers":["Cymbeline''s two stolen sons, Guiderius and Arviragus","That Iachimo confess where he got the ring on his finger","He fought bravely and helped save the king''s life and win the battle"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-3-3","title":"Lesson 18: King Lear, Part 1 of 3","steps":["Read “King Lear” aloud from the start until Cordelia leaves for France with a heavy heart","Narrate what each of the three daughters said to her father, and what Lear did","Copy one sentence from the book that shows Cordelia''s honest answer, then talk about flattery versus true love"],"links":[{"label":"Read “King Lear”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#lear"}],"questions":["Why did Lear give Cordelia''s share of the kingdom to her sisters?","What happened to the Earl of Kent when he spoke up for Cordelia?","Why did the King of France still want to marry Cordelia?"],"answers":["She would not flatter him; she said she loved him according to her duty, no more and no less","Lear banished him from Britain","He saw that her only “fault” was refusing to flatter, and said her goodness was worth more than a kingdom"],"notes":"King Lear is a tragedy. The last part tells of several deaths, including Cordelia''s. Preview it first if your child is sensitive."},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-3-4","title":"Lesson 19: King Lear, Part 2 of 3","steps":["Read on from where Goneril begins to treat her father coldly, until the sisters shut their doors on Lear in the storm","Narrate how Goneril and Regan broke their promises","Draw Kent (as Caius) in the stocks when Lear arrives at Regan''s castle"],"links":[{"label":"Read “King Lear” (Part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#lear"}],"questions":["What name did Kent use when he came back in disguise to serve Lear?","How many knights did Goneril and Regan say Lear could keep, in the end?","Who stayed with Lear and tried to cheer him with jokes and songs?"],"answers":["Caius","They argued him down from a hundred to fifty, twenty-five, ten, five, and finally none","His fool, or jester"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-3-5","title":"Lesson 20: King Lear, Part 3 of 3","steps":["Read on from Lear on the stormy heath to the end of the tale","Narrate the meeting of Lear and Cordelia at Dover","Talk together: who in this story stayed loyal, and how did they show it?"],"links":[{"label":"Read “King Lear” (Part 3)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#lear"}],"questions":["Where did Kent have Lear carried after the night of the storm?","What was Lear wearing on his head when Cordelia''s people found him?","Who ruled Britain after Lear died?"],"answers":["To the castle of Dover","A crown he had made of straw, nettles, and weeds","The Duke of Albany, Goneril''s good husband"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-3-6","title":"Lesson 21: Macbeth, Part 1 of 2","steps":["Read “Macbeth” aloud from the start until Macbeth is crowned king","Narrate the witches'' three greetings and how Macbeth let them change him","Draw the three weird sisters on the heath"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Macbeth”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#macbeth"}],"questions":["What three titles did the witches give Macbeth?","What did they promise Banquo?","How did Lady Macbeth try to put the blame for the murder on others?"],"answers":["Thane of Glamis, thane of Cawdor, and king hereafter","That his sons would be kings, though he would not","She smeared the sleeping grooms'' faces with blood and left the daggers with them"],"notes":"Macbeth is the darkest tale in the book: murders, a ghost, and a witches'' cauldron. For younger children, read it yourself first and soften or skip as you see fit."},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-3-7","title":"Lesson 22: Macbeth, Part 2 of 2","steps":["Read on from where Macbeth plots against Banquo, to the end","Narrate how each of the spirits'' promises came true in an unexpected way","Draw Malcolm''s soldiers carrying branches from Birnam wood"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Macbeth” (Part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#macbeth"}],"questions":["Who escaped when Banquo was attacked?","What did the third spirit say had to happen before Macbeth could be beaten?","How did Birnam wood “come to Dunsinane”?"],"answers":["Banquo''s son Fleance","Birnam wood would have to come to Dunsinane Hill","Malcolm''s soldiers each cut a bough and carried it to hide how many they were"]}]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-u4","title":"Unit 4: All''s Well that Ends Well to Measure for Measure","lessons":[{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-4-1","title":"Lesson 23: All''s Well that Ends Well, Part 1 of 2","steps":["Read “All''s Well that Ends Well” aloud from the start until Bertram leaves Helena without even saying goodbye","Narrate how a poor physician''s daughter cured the King of France","Draw Helena offering her father''s medicine to the sick king"],"links":[{"label":"Read “All''s Well that Ends Well”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#allwell"}],"questions":["What did Helena''s father leave her when he died?","What reward did the king promise Helena if she cured him?","How did Bertram treat Helena after they were married?"],"answers":["Some rare and tested medicines (prescriptions)","Her choice of any man in France (except the princes) for a husband","He refused to stay with her and sent her home to his mother"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-4-2","title":"Lesson 24: All''s Well that Ends Well, Part 2 of 2","steps":["Read on from where Helena returns to the countess, to the end","Narrate how Helena got the ring from Bertram''s finger","Talk together: was Bertram worthy of Helena? What made him change?"],"links":[{"label":"Read “All''s Well that Ends Well” (Part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#allwell"}],"questions":["What did Bertram''s letter say Helena must do before he would call her his wife?","Where did Helena go, dressed as a pilgrim?","Whose ring did the king notice on Bertram''s finger?"],"answers":["Get the ring from his finger, which he said would never come off","To Florence (on her way to the shrine of St. Jaques le Grand)","The ring the king had given Helena"],"notes":"Helena wins the ring by secretly taking Diana''s place at a night meeting with Bertram. You may want to read this part yourself first and summarize it for younger children."},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-4-3","title":"Lesson 25: The Taming of the Shrew, Part 1 of 2","steps":["Read “The Taming of the Shrew” aloud from the start until Petruchio carries Katharine away from her own wedding feast","Narrate Petruchio''s strange courtship and wild wedding","Draw Petruchio arriving at the wedding in his ragged clothes"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Taming of the Shrew”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#shrew"}],"questions":["Why couldn''t Bianca marry until Katharine did?","What did Katharine do to her music-master?","How did Petruchio plan to answer Katharine if she railed at him?"],"answers":["Their father would not let anyone court Bianca until her older sister was married","She broke his head with her lute","By telling her she sang as sweetly as a nightingale"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-4-4","title":"Lesson 26: The Taming of the Shrew, Part 2 of 2","steps":["Read on from the journey to Petruchio''s house, to the end","Narrate the argument about the sun and the moon","Talk together: Petruchio wins the wager, but do you think he treated Katharine fairly?"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Taming of the Shrew” (Part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#shrew"}],"questions":["Why did Petruchio throw the supper on the floor?","What did Katharine finally agree to call the sun?","Which wife came when her husband sent for her?"],"answers":["He pretended it was not good enough for her, so she would go without","Whatever Petruchio wanted, even the moon","Katharine"],"notes":"The ending praises a wife''s obedience in the way of Shakespeare''s time. It is a good chance to talk about how people saw marriage then and now."},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-4-5","title":"Lesson 27: The Comedy of Errors, Part 1 of 2","steps":["Read “The Comedy of Errors” aloud from the start until Antipholus of Syracuse goes home to dine with Adriana, who thinks he is her husband","Narrate old Ægeon''s story of the shipwreck and the two sets of twins","Draw the family tied to the masts in the storm"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Comedy of Errors”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#comedy"}],"questions":["Why was Ægeon going to be put to death in Ephesus?","What were the two masters both named, and what were the two servants both named?","How long did the duke give Ægeon to find the money?"],"answers":["Merchants from Syracuse were not allowed in Ephesus unless they paid a thousand marks","The masters were both Antipholus; the servants were both Dromio","One day, until sunset"],"notes":"Keep a little chart of the twins: Antipholus and Dromio “of Ephesus” and “of Syracuse.” It makes the mix-ups much funnier."},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-4-6","title":"Lesson 28: The Comedy of Errors, Part 2 of 2","steps":["Read on from where the real husband is locked out of his own house, to the end","Narrate the mix-up of the gold chain","Act out one mix-up with two people wearing the same hat"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Comedy of Errors” (Part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#comedy"}],"questions":["Why wouldn''t the servants let Antipholus of Ephesus into his own house?","Who was the lady abbess of the convent, really?","Who paid Ægeon''s ransom in the end?"],"answers":["They thought their master was already inside eating dinner (it was his twin)","Ægeon''s long-lost wife, the twins'' mother","No one; the duke pardoned him for free"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-4-7","title":"Lesson 29: Measure for Measure, Part 1 of 2","steps":["Read “Measure for Measure” aloud from the start until Angelo tells Isabel to answer him by tomorrow","Narrate why the duke left Angelo in charge and what Angelo did","Copy one of Isabel''s sentences about mercy from her plea to Angelo"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Measure for Measure”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#measure"}],"questions":["Why did the duke pretend to leave Vienna?","What disguise did the duke wear when he secretly returned?","Who was Isabel, and where was she about to live?"],"answers":["So someone else could enforce the forgotten laws without his people thinking him a tyrant","A friar","Claudio''s sister; she was about to enter the convent of Saint Clare as a nun"],"notes":"This is the most grown-up tale in the book: it turns on a couple living together unmarried, and a ruler who offers to spare Isabel''s brother if she will give herself to him. Read it yourself first; it may suit your older children better, or you may choose to skip it."},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-4-8","title":"Lesson 30: Measure for Measure, Part 2 of 2","steps":["Read on from where Isabel goes to the prison, to the end","Narrate how the duke, disguised as a friar, set things right","Talk together: why did Isabel kneel to ask mercy for Angelo?"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Measure for Measure” (Part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#measure"}],"questions":["Who was Mariana?","How did the provost save Claudio from execution?","What was the duke''s punishment for Angelo in the end?"],"answers":["Angelo''s wife, whom he had abandoned when her dowry was lost at sea","He sent Angelo the head of a prisoner who had died that morning instead","He pardoned him, because Mariana and Isabel begged for him, and told him to love his wife"]}]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-u5","title":"Unit 5: Twelfth Night, Timon of Athens, and Romeo and Juliet","lessons":[{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-5-1","title":"Lesson 31: Twelfth Night, Part 1 of 2","steps":["Read “Twelfth Night” aloud from the start until Viola realizes Olivia has fallen in love with her","Narrate how Viola became the page Cesario","Draw Viola, dressed as Cesario, asking to see Olivia''s face under the veil"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Twelfth Night”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#twelfth"}],"questions":["Where were Viola and Sebastian shipwrecked?","Why did Viola dress like her brother?","What did Olivia send after Cesario as a hint of her love?"],"answers":["On the coast of Illyria","To serve Duke Orsino safely as a page, alone in a strange land","A diamond ring"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-5-2","title":"Lesson 32: Twelfth Night, Part 2 of 2","steps":["Read on from Viola''s return to Orsino''s palace, to the end","Narrate how the twins were mistaken for each other and finally met","Copy Viola''s line about the sister who “sat like Patience on a monument, smiling at Grief” (from Part 1)"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Twelfth Night” (Part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#twelfth"}],"questions":["Who rescued Viola from the duel, and who did he think she was?","Who did Olivia marry, thinking he was Cesario?","Whom did Orsino marry at the end?"],"answers":["Antonio the sea-captain; he thought she was Sebastian","Sebastian","Viola"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-5-3","title":"Lesson 33: Timon of Athens, Part 1 of 2","steps":["Read “Timon of Athens” aloud from the start until Timon''s house is besieged by people he owes money to","Narrate how Timon lost his fortune and found out who his true friends were","Draw Timon''s crowded feast, with the honest steward Flavius weeping alone"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Timon of Athens”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#timon"}],"questions":["How did the false friends get rich gifts from Timon?","Who tried again and again to warn Timon about his money?","What did Lucullus offer Timon''s servant to say?"],"answers":["They gave him small presents or praised his things, knowing he would give back something far richer","His honest steward, Flavius","A bribe to tell Timon he had not found Lucullus at home"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-5-4","title":"Lesson 34: Timon of Athens, Part 2 of 2","steps":["Read on from Timon''s last feast to the end of the tale","Narrate the trick Timon played at his final banquet","Talk together: Flavius was faithful, yet Timon turned against everyone. What could Timon have done instead?"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Timon of Athens” (Part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#timon"}],"questions":["What was under the covers of the dishes at Timon''s last feast?","What did Timon find while digging for roots in the woods?","Why did the senators of Athens come to beg Timon to return?"],"answers":["Only smoke and lukewarm water","A great heap of buried gold","Alcibiades was attacking Athens, and they needed Timon to lead its defence"],"notes":"This is a bitter tale with a grim ending: Timon hates all people and dies alone. Its lesson is about flattery and true friendship."},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-5-5","title":"Lesson 35: Romeo and Juliet, Part 1 of 3","steps":["Read “Romeo and Juliet” aloud from the start until the two lovers say goodnight at Juliet''s window","Narrate the Capulet feast and the meeting in the orchard","Copy Juliet''s question: “O Romeo, Romeo! wherefore art thou Romeo?” and talk about what “wherefore” means (why)"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Romeo and Juliet”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#romeo"}],"questions":["What were the names of the two quarrelling families?","Who recognized Romeo at the feast and wanted to fight him?","Why was it dangerous for Romeo to be in Juliet''s orchard?"],"answers":["The Capulets and the Montagues","Tybalt, Lord Capulet''s nephew","If any of her family found him there, a Montague, they would kill him"],"notes":"Romeo and Juliet ends in tragedy with the deaths of both young people. Preview the last part before reading it aloud to younger children."},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-5-6","title":"Lesson 36: Romeo and Juliet, Part 2 of 3","steps":["Read on from where Romeo goes to Friar Lawrence at daybreak, until Romeo leaves for Mantua","Narrate the fight in the street and why Romeo was banished","Draw Friar Lawrence''s cell, or the street fight in Verona"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Romeo and Juliet” (Part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#romeo"}],"questions":["Why did Friar Lawrence agree to marry Romeo and Juliet?","How did Mercutio die?","What punishment did the prince give Romeo?"],"answers":["He hoped their marriage would end the quarrel between their families","Tybalt killed him in a sword fight","He banished him from Verona"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-5-7","title":"Lesson 37: Romeo and Juliet, Part 3 of 3","steps":["Read on from Lord Capulet''s plan for Juliet to marry Paris, to the end","Narrate the friar''s plan and how it went wrong","Talk together: how did the families'' hatred lead to this sad ending?"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Romeo and Juliet” (Part 3)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#romeo"}],"questions":["What would Friar Lawrence''s potion do to Juliet?","Why did Romeo never learn that Juliet was only asleep?","What did the two fathers do at the end?"],"answers":["Make her seem dead for forty-two hours","The friar''s letters to Mantua were delayed and never reached him","They made peace and promised to raise golden statues of Romeo and Juliet"]}]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-u6","title":"Unit 6: Hamlet, Othello, and Pericles","lessons":[{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-6-1","title":"Lesson 38: Hamlet, Prince of Denmark, Part 1 of 3","steps":["Read “Hamlet” aloud from the start until Hamlet decides to pretend to be mad","Narrate what the ghost told Hamlet","Draw the soldiers'' platform at midnight as the ghost appears in armour"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Hamlet, Prince of Denmark”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#hamlet"}],"questions":["Why was young Hamlet so sad at the start of the story?","How did the ghost say King Hamlet had really died?","Why did Hamlet decide to act as if he were mad?"],"answers":["His father had died, and his mother had married his uncle Claudius less than two months later","Claudius poured poison (henbane) into his ears while he slept in the garden","So his uncle would not suspect he knew the truth or was planning anything"],"notes":"Hamlet is a tragedy with a ghost and many deaths. Preview Parts 2 and 3 if your child is sensitive."},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-6-2","title":"Lesson 39: Hamlet, Prince of Denmark, Part 2 of 3","steps":["Read on from where Hamlet begins to act strangely, until he weeps over Polonius","Narrate Hamlet''s plan with the players and how the king gave himself away","Draw the players acting out the poisoning in the garden while the king watches"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Hamlet, Prince of Denmark” (Part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#hamlet"}],"questions":["Whose daughter was Ophelia?","What happened in the play Hamlet had the actors perform?","Who was hiding behind the hangings in the queen''s room?"],"answers":["Polonius, the king''s chief counsellor","A man poisoned a duke in his garden to take his place, just as Claudius had done","Polonius"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-6-3","title":"Lesson 40: Hamlet, Prince of Denmark, Part 3 of 3","steps":["Read on from where the king sends Hamlet to England, to the end","Narrate how Hamlet escaped the king''s plot at sea","Talk together: what did Hamlet ask his friend Horatio to do at the end?"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Hamlet, Prince of Denmark” (Part 3)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#hamlet"}],"questions":["What did Hamlet change in the king''s secret letters?","How did Hamlet get back to Denmark?","What two tricks had the king prepared for the fencing match?"],"answers":["He put the two courtiers'' names in place of his own, so they would be put to death instead","Pirates captured him and set him ashore in Denmark","A sharp, poisoned sword for Laertes and a cup of poisoned drink"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-6-4","title":"Lesson 41: Othello, Part 1 of 2","steps":["Read “Othello” aloud from the start until Othello takes away Cassio''s place as lieutenant","Narrate how Othello won Desdemona and why her father was angry","Find Venice and Cyprus on a map of the Mediterranean, and trace Othello''s voyage"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Othello”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#othello"}],"questions":["How did Othello win Desdemona''s love?","Why was Othello sent to Cyprus?","Why did Iago hate Cassio?"],"answers":["By telling her the story of his adventures and dangers","To defend it against a Turkish fleet","Othello made Cassio his lieutenant instead of Iago"],"notes":"The book describes Othello''s color with the prejudice of its day. Talk about how the story shows Othello as noble and brave, and Iago''s lies as the real evil. The tale ends with Desdemona''s murder and Othello''s death."},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-6-5","title":"Lesson 42: Othello, Part 2 of 2","steps":["Read on from where Iago advises Cassio to ask Desdemona''s help, to the end","Narrate how Iago used small things, like a handkerchief, to poison Othello''s mind","Talk together: how could Othello have found out the truth before it was too late?"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Othello” (Part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#othello"}],"questions":["Why did Desdemona keep asking Othello to forgive Cassio?","How did Iago get Desdemona''s handkerchief?","How was Iago''s wickedness finally discovered?"],"answers":["Cassio had asked for her help, as Iago told him to, and she was kind","He made his wife Emilia steal it","Letters proving it were found on the man Iago had sent to kill Cassio"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-6-6","title":"Lesson 43: Pericles, Prince of Tyre, Part 1 of 3","steps":["Read “Pericles” aloud from the start until Pericles sets the chest with Thaisa into the sea","Narrate how the sea took things from Pericles and gave them back","Draw the fishermen pulling Pericles''s armour up in their net"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Pericles, Prince of Tyre”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#pericles"}],"questions":["How did Pericles help the city of Tarsus?","What did the fishermen catch in their net?","Why did the sailors want the queen put overboard?"],"answers":["He brought food when the city was starving","His father''s suit of armour, lost in the shipwreck","They believed the storm would not stop while a dead body was on the ship"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-6-7","title":"Lesson 44: Pericles, Prince of Tyre, Part 2 of 3","steps":["Read on from where Cerimon opens the chest on the shore, until Marina is taken to Mitylene","Narrate what happened to Thaisa and what happened to baby Marina","Find Tyre, Tarsus, and Ephesus on a map of the eastern Mediterranean"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Pericles, Prince of Tyre” (Part 2)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#pericles"}],"questions":["How did Cerimon know Thaisa was not really dead?","Why was Marina given that name?","Why did Dionysia want Marina killed?"],"answers":["Her face looked fresh and not like death, so he warmed her and she revived","Because she was born at sea","She was jealous that Marina outshone her own daughter"]},{"id":"tales-from-shakespeare-6-8","title":"Lesson 45: Pericles, Prince of Tyre, Part 3 of 3","steps":["Read on from Dionysia''s false funeral for Marina, to the end of the book","Narrate how the family found each other again","Draw the reunion at Diana''s temple at Ephesus"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Pericles, Prince of Tyre” (Part 3)","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/573/pg573-images.html#pericles"}],"questions":["How did Marina get her father to speak again?","What did Pericles dream?","How did Thaisa know Pericles at the temple?"],"answers":["She told him the sad story of her own life, and he saw she looked like his wife","That the goddess Diana told him to go to her temple at Ephesus and tell his story","She knew his face and voice, and saw the ring her father had given him"],"notes":"Marina is sold as a slave, and the book closes with a grim punishment for Cleon and Dionysia. Celebrate finishing the whole book together!"}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"“Tales from Shakespeare” by Charles Lamb and Mary Lamb (1807), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/573. Lesson plan by Freerange."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (listing_id, version) do update set content = excluded.content;
insert into public.listings (id, seller_name, seller_kind, seller_verified, title, subjects, grade_min, grade_max, philosophies, format, price, description, includes, lessons, lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement, family_style, lesson_steps, cover, color, esa_eligible, created_at, package_id, package_version, license, attribution, outline) values ('l-lib-tanglewood-tales', 'Nathaniel Hawthorne', 'open', false, 'Tanglewood Tales', array['reading', 'history', 'geography']::text[], 3, 6, array['charlotte_mason', 'classical', 'traditional']::text[], 'online', 0, 'Nathaniel Hawthorne''s classic retelling of six Greek myths for children: Theseus and the Minotaur, Hercules and the Pygmies, Cadmus, Circe, Proserpina, and Jason and the Golden Fleece. Each long tale is split into sittings: you read a section aloud, your child narrates, and you finish with map work, a drawing, or copywork. A rich first taste of mythology for ages 8 to 12, and the full text is free online.', array['26 lessons in 6 units covering the introduction and all six tales', 'Clear start and stop points so each long tale fits 3 to 5 sittings', 'Narration every lesson, plus map work, drawing, or copywork', '2 to 3 questions per lesson, with answers', 'Parent notes on Hawthorne''s changes to the old myths and on sad scenes', 'Link to the free full text for every lesson']::text[], 26, 3, 25, 'high', true, '{}'::text[], '🐏', '#9B6BD6', false, '2026-11-11T12:00:00.000Z', 'pk-lib-tanglewood-tales', 1, '{"kind":"public_domain"}'::jsonb, '“Tanglewood Tales for Girls and Boys; Being a Second Wonder-Book” by Nathaniel Hawthorne (1853), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/976. Lesson plan by Freerange.', '[{"title":"Unit 1: The Wayside and The Minotaur","lessons":["Lesson 1: The Wayside","Lesson 2: The Minotaur, part 1: The Stone and the Sword","Lesson 3: The Minotaur, part 2: Medea''s Cup","Lesson 4: The Minotaur, part 3: Black Sails to Crete","Lesson 5: The Minotaur, part 4: The Labyrinth"]},{"title":"Unit 2: The Pygmies","lessons":["Lesson 6: The Pygmies, part 1: The Giant and the Little People","Lesson 7: The Pygmies, part 2: Hercules and Antaeus","Lesson 8: The Pygmies, part 3: The Pygmies'' War"]},{"title":"Unit 3: The Dragon''s Teeth","lessons":["Lesson 9: The Dragon''s Teeth, part 1: Europa and the White Bull","Lesson 10: The Dragon''s Teeth, part 2: The Long Search","Lesson 11: The Dragon''s Teeth, part 3: Follow the Cow","Lesson 12: The Dragon''s Teeth, part 4: The Armed Men"]},{"title":"Unit 4: Circe''s Palace","lessons":["Lesson 13: Circe''s Palace, part 1: The Green Island","Lesson 14: Circe''s Palace, part 2: The Marble Palace","Lesson 15: Circe''s Palace, part 3: The Feast","Lesson 16: Circe''s Palace, part 4: The Snow-White Flower"]},{"title":"Unit 5: The Pomegranate Seeds","lessons":["Lesson 17: The Pomegranate Seeds, part 1: The Wonderful Shrub","Lesson 18: The Pomegranate Seeds, part 2: Pluto''s Kingdom","Lesson 19: The Pomegranate Seeds, part 3: The Torch That Never Went Out","Lesson 20: The Pomegranate Seeds, part 4: The Baby Prince and the Barren Earth","Lesson 21: The Pomegranate Seeds, part 5: Six Seeds"]},{"title":"Unit 6: The Golden Fleece","lessons":["Lesson 22: The Golden Fleece, part 1: The Old Woman at the River","Lesson 23: The Golden Fleece, part 2: The Man with One Sandal","Lesson 24: The Golden Fleece, part 3: The Voyage of the Argo","Lesson 25: The Golden Fleece, part 4: The Brazen Bulls","Lesson 26: The Golden Fleece, part 5: The Grove of Mars"]}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set seller_name = excluded.seller_name, seller_kind = excluded.seller_kind, seller_verified = excluded.seller_verified, title = excluded.title, subjects = excluded.subjects, grade_min = excluded.grade_min, grade_max = excluded.grade_max, philosophies = excluded.philosophies, format = excluded.format, price = excluded.price, description = excluded.description, includes = excluded.includes, lessons = excluded.lessons, lessons_per_week = excluded.lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson = excluded.minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement = excluded.parent_involvement, family_style = excluded.family_style, lesson_steps = excluded.lesson_steps, cover = excluded.cover, color = excluded.color, esa_eligible = excluded.esa_eligible, package_id = excluded.package_id, package_version = excluded.package_version, license = excluded.license, attribution = excluded.attribution, outline = excluded.outline;
insert into public.package_releases (listing_id, version, content) values ('l-lib-tanglewood-tales', 1, '{"units":[{"id":"tanglewood-tales-u1","title":"Unit 1: The Wayside and The Minotaur","lessons":[{"id":"tanglewood-tales-1-1","title":"Lesson 1: The Wayside","steps":["Read “The Wayside” aloud: the narrator shows Eustace Bright his snowy hillside and reads the new stories","Narrate: who is Eustace Bright, and who are the children he tells his stories to?","Look ahead: read the six story titles together and guess what each one might be about"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Wayside”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_0001"}],"questions":["Where did the narrator take Eustace to sit in the snow?","What earlier book of stories had Eustace already written?","Name two of the Tanglewood children."],"answers":["To a ruined little summer house of branches halfway up his hillside","The “Wonder-Book”","Any two, e.g. Primrose, Periwinkle, Sweet Fern, Clover, Dandelion, Buttercup"],"notes":"This short frame story is a sequel to Hawthorne''s “A Wonder-Book.” It is wordy for young listeners; you may summarize it instead of reading every line."},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-1-2","title":"Lesson 2: The Minotaur, part 1: The Stone and the Sword","steps":["Read from the start of “The Minotaur” to the point where Theseus enters Athens and hears people praising him","Narrate how Theseus finally got his father''s sword and sandals","Map work: find Troezen and Athens on a map of ancient Greece and trace his road by land around the Saronic Gulf, through the Isthmus of Corinth"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Minotaur”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_0002"}],"questions":["What did Theseus find under the great rock?","Why did Theseus choose to go to Athens by land instead of by sea?","What did the robber Procrustes do to travelers?"],"answers":["A gold-hilted sword and a pair of sandals that belonged to his father","He wanted to meet the robbers and monsters on the road and fight them","Made them fit his bed by stretching them or cutting them shorter"]},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-1-3","title":"Lesson 3: The Minotaur, part 2: Medea''s Cup","steps":["Continue from where Theseus''s jealous cousins meet him at the palace door; stop after Theseus catches the mad bull and the trouble with the Minotaur is about to begin","Narrate the scene in the throne room, step by step","Draw Medea flying away in her chariot pulled by winged serpents, with jewels falling on the crowd"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Minotaur”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_0002"}],"questions":["What did Medea put in the goblet of wine?","What made King Aegeus stop and pull back the cup?","What happened to the jewels Medea stole?"],"answers":["Poison","He saw the gold-hilted sword at Theseus''s side and knew it was his own","She dropped them over the city, and the king let the people keep them"]},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-1-4","title":"Lesson 4: The Minotaur, part 3: Black Sails to Crete","steps":["Continue from the morning Theseus wakes to the sound of the whole city weeping; stop when the captives are locked in the dungeon for the night","Narrate why Athens had to send young people to Crete, and what Theseus offered to do","Map work: trace the voyage from Athens across the Aegean Sea to the island of Crete"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Minotaur”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_0002"}],"questions":["How many young men and maidens did Athens send to the Minotaur each year?","What did Aegeus ask Theseus to do with the sails if he won?","Who was Talus?"],"answers":["Seven young men and seven maidens","Take down the black sails and raise bright, sunny ones","A giant man of brass who walked around Crete three times a day guarding it"]},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-1-5","title":"Lesson 5: The Minotaur, part 4: The Labyrinth","steps":["Read from Ariadne coming to the dungeon with her torch to the end of the story","Narrate the fight in the labyrinth and how Theseus found his way out","Draw a maze with the Minotaur at the center, then trace Theseus''s silken string through it"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Minotaur”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_0002"}],"questions":["How did Theseus find his way back out of the labyrinth?","Why did Ariadne refuse to sail away with Theseus?","Why did King Aegeus throw himself into the sea?"],"answers":["He followed the silk string that Ariadne held at the entrance","Her father was old and had no one else to love him","He saw black sails and thought Theseus had been eaten"],"notes":"Hawthorne changes the ending: in the older myth Ariadne sails away with Theseus and he leaves her on an island. The sea where Aegeus fell is still called the Aegean. The king''s death is sad, so preview it for sensitive listeners."}]},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-u2","title":"Unit 2: The Pygmies","lessons":[{"id":"tanglewood-tales-2-1","title":"Lesson 6: The Pygmies, part 1: The Giant and the Little People","steps":["Read from the start of “The Pygmies” to the promise of a far more astonishing battle than any with the cranes","Narrate how the Pygmies and the Giant Antaeus lived together as brothers","Draw a Pygmy city of straw, feathers, and eggshells with Antaeus towering over it"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Pygmies”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_pyg"}],"questions":["What were the Pygmies'' houses made of?","Who were the Pygmies always at war with?","How did Antaeus help the Pygmies when the sun was too hot?"],"answers":["Straw, feathers, eggshells, and bits of stuff stuck together with clay, like birds'' nests","The cranes","He sat down so his shadow covered their whole kingdom"],"notes":"These Pygmies are tiny made-up people from Greek legend, not the real peoples of central Africa sometimes called by that name. Hawthorne also gives Antaeus one eye, which is his own invention."},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-2-2","title":"Lesson 7: The Pygmies, part 2: Hercules and Antaeus","steps":["Continue from the Pygmy who climbs the sleeping Giant''s shoulder and spots a moving mountain; stop when Hercules lies down on his lion''s skin and falls asleep","Narrate the fight and explain the trick Hercules used to win","Act it out: one child is Antaeus, who gets stronger every time he touches the floor"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Pygmies”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_pyg"}],"questions":["What happened to Antaeus every time he touched the ground?","How did Hercules finally beat him?","Where was Hercules going?"],"answers":["He grew much stronger, because the Earth was his mother","He lifted Antaeus up into the air until his strength and breath were gone","To the garden of the Hesperides to get three golden apples"]},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-2-3","title":"Lesson 8: The Pygmies, part 3: The Pygmies'' War","steps":["Read from the Pygmy orator climbing onto a toadstool to the end of the story","Narrate the Pygmies'' plan to defeat Hercules and what happened","Copywork: copy Hercules''s question about how big the Pygmy''s soul is, and the Pygmy''s answer; talk about what it means"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Pygmies”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_pyg"}],"questions":["What did the Pygmies pile around Hercules''s head?","Which Pygmy did Hercules happen to pick up and hold on his palm?","Why did Hercules leave the kingdom in peace?"],"answers":["Sticks, straw, and dry weeds, which they set on fire","The orator who had offered to fight Hercules alone","He admired the Pygmies'' courage and did not want to hurt such brave people"]}]},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-u3","title":"Unit 3: The Dragon''s Teeth","lessons":[{"id":"tanglewood-tales-3-1","title":"Lesson 9: The Dragon''s Teeth, part 1: Europa and the White Bull","steps":["Read from the start of “The Dragon''s Teeth” to the place where we must leave King Agenor on his throne and follow the queen","Narrate how Europa was carried away","Map work: find Phoenicia on the eastern shore of the Mediterranean (today''s Lebanon)"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Dragon''s Teeth”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_dragon"}],"questions":["What were Europa''s brothers chasing when the bull came?","Where did the white bull carry Europa?","What did King Agenor tell his sons?"],"answers":["A butterfly","Into the sea and far away across the water","Never to come home unless they brought Europa back with them"]},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-3-2","title":"Lesson 10: The Dragon''s Teeth, part 2: The Long Search","steps":["Continue from the searchers crossing mountains, rivers, and seas; stop after Cadmus plants flowers on his mother''s grave","Narrate what happened to each searcher: Phoenix, Cilix, Thasus, and Queen Telephassa","Make a chart with four columns, one for each searcher who stopped, and tell why each stopped"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Dragon''s Teeth”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_dragon"}],"questions":["What happened at each place where a searcher stopped and built a hut?","Why did the people make Thasus king, even though he had no royal blood?","Where did Telephassa tell Cadmus to go next?"],"answers":["Other people came, a city grew up, and he was made its king","Because he was upright, true-hearted, and brave","To the oracle at Delphi"],"notes":"Queen Telephassa dies peacefully in this part. It is told gently, but preview it if your child is sensitive."},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-3-3","title":"Lesson 11: The Dragon''s Teeth, part 3: Follow the Cow","steps":["Continue from Cadmus setting off alone for Delphi; stop when he says the plain where the cow lay down will be their home","Narrate what the oracle said and how Cadmus found his new home","Map work: find Mount Parnassus and Delphi, then find Thebes, the city tradition says Cadmus built"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Dragon''s Teeth”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_dragon"}],"questions":["What did the voice at Delphi tell Cadmus?","What kind of cow did Cadmus follow?","Why did other people start following the cow too?"],"answers":["Seek her no more; follow the cow; where the cow lies down is your home","A brindled cow","She was enchanted, and anyone who followed her a few steps could not help going on"]},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-3-4","title":"Lesson 12: The Dragon''s Teeth, part 4: The Armed Men","steps":["Read from Cadmus''s friends going to fetch water at the tuft of trees to the end of the story","Narrate what grew from the dragon''s teeth and how Cadmus made peace","Draw helmets and spears sprouting from the field like a crop"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Dragon''s Teeth”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_dragon"}],"questions":["How did Cadmus defeat the dragon?","What made the armed men fight one another?","Who was waiting in Cadmus''s new palace?"],"answers":["He jumped into its mouth, past its teeth, and fought it from inside","Cadmus threw a stone into the middle of them","Harmonia, a daughter of the sky, not his sister Europa"],"notes":"Hawthorne ends by saying Cadmus invented the ABC. The old Greeks believed Cadmus brought the alphabet from Phoenicia, and our alphabet really does descend from the Phoenician one."}]},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-u4","title":"Unit 4: Circe''s Palace","lessons":[{"id":"tanglewood-tales-4-1","title":"Lesson 13: Circe''s Palace, part 1: The Green Island","steps":["Read from the start of “Circe''s Palace” to Eurylochus and his twenty-two men setting off for the palace","Narrate the troubles Ulysses had already met, and how he decided who would go to the palace","Map work: find Troy and Ithaca, and talk about how far Ulysses had to sail to get home"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Circe''s Palace”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_0003"}],"questions":["What was inside the leather bags the sailors opened?","What did the little purple bird try to do?","How did Ulysses decide which half of the crew would go?"],"answers":["Winds and storms that King Aeolus had tied up","Keep Ulysses from going toward the palace","By drawing lots: two shells marked “Go” and “Stay” in his helmet"],"notes":"Ulysses is the Roman name for the Greek hero Odysseus. This tale retells part of Homer''s Odyssey."},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-4-2","title":"Lesson 14: Circe''s Palace, part 2: The Marble Palace","steps":["Continue from the men climbing the cliff and smelling the kitchen smoke; stop when Eurylochus, hidden behind a pillar, sees the fountain take the shape of a hog","Narrate everything strange the men met on the way to the palace","Draw the magic fountain changing shapes: a man, a lion, a wolf, a hog"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Circe''s Palace”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_0003"}],"questions":["How did the lions, tigers, and wolves behave toward the sailors?","Why did Eurylochus stay behind a pillar?","What did the fountain look like at the end of this part?"],"answers":["They fawned on them like friendly house dogs","He was wary and thought something bad would happen","A hog wallowing in the basin"]},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-4-3","title":"Lesson 15: Circe''s Palace, part 3: The Feast","steps":["Continue from the beautiful woman showing the sailors her tapestry; stop after Quicksilver explains what became of the sailors","Narrate the feast and what Circe did when it was over","Discuss: why do you think Circe turned these men into pigs and not some other animal?"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Circe''s Palace”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_0003"}],"questions":["What pictures were woven into Circe''s tapestry?","What happened when Circe waved her wand at the end of the feast?","Who was the purple bird, according to Quicksilver?"],"answers":["The sailors'' own adventures, like the Cyclops''s cave and the bags of wind","The sailors turned into hogs","A king named Picus who had been too proud of his fine clothes and crown"]},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-4-4","title":"Lesson 16: Circe''s Palace, part 4: The Snow-White Flower","steps":["Read from Ulysses asking whether he can still help his men to the end of the story","Narrate how Ulysses beat Circe''s magic","Copywork: copy the sentence where Ulysses tells his men their future behavior will decide whether they end up back in the sty"],"links":[{"label":"Read “Circe''s Palace”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_0003"}],"questions":["What did Quicksilver give Ulysses?","Why did Ulysses leave the lions, tigers, and wolves as beasts?","How did King Picus change after he was a man again?"],"answers":["A rare snow-white flower to smell so the magic could not change him","So they would warn people of their cruel natures instead of pretending to be kind men","He stopped being proud and saw himself as a servant of his people"],"notes":"Quicksilver is Hawthorne''s playful name for Mercury (the Greek Hermes). In Homer the magic plant is called moly."}]},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-u5","title":"Unit 5: The Pomegranate Seeds","lessons":[{"id":"tanglewood-tales-5-1","title":"Lesson 17: The Pomegranate Seeds, part 1: The Wonderful Shrub","steps":["Read from the start of “The Pomegranate Seeds” until Proserpina''s last scream as she passes her mother in the grain field","Narrate how Proserpina was carried off","Map work: find Sicily, Mount Etna (Aetna), and the town of Enna in the middle of the island"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Pomegranate Seeds”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_0004"}],"questions":["What did the sea nymphs make for Proserpina?","What happened when Proserpina pulled up the shrub?","What did Pluto offer Proserpina?"],"answers":["A necklace of shells","The hole grew deeper and deeper, and Pluto''s chariot burst out of it","His palace, his crown, and all the gems and gold under the earth"],"notes":"Hawthorne uses the Roman names: Ceres, Proserpina, and Pluto are the Greek Demeter, Persephone, and Hades."},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-5-2","title":"Lesson 18: The Pomegranate Seeds, part 2: Pluto''s Kingdom","steps":["Continue from the road growing gloomy between rocks and precipices; stop when Ceres puts the strange flower in her bosom and goes on searching","Narrate Proserpina''s arrival in Pluto''s kingdom, then what Ceres did when she found her daughter gone","Draw Cerberus with his three heads and his dragon tail"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Pomegranate Seeds”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_0004"}],"questions":["What does the water of the River Lethe do?","Why would Proserpina not drink it?","What is the rule about eating food in the land of magic?"],"answers":["It makes people forget all their cares and sorrows","She would rather be sad and remember her mother than forget her","If you taste any food there, you can never go back home"]},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-5-3","title":"Lesson 19: The Pomegranate Seeds, part 3: The Torch That Never Went Out","steps":["Continue from Ceres knocking at cottage doors all night; stop when Ceres tells Phoebus he has a harp instead of a heart","Narrate whom Ceres asked for help and what each one said","Discuss: how were Hecate and Phoebus different from each other, and was either one a good friend?"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Pomegranate Seeds”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_0004"}],"questions":["What was strange about Ceres''s torch?","What had Hecate heard nine days earlier?","Who finally told Ceres where Proserpina was?"],"answers":["It never went out, in rain or wind, day or night","A girl screaming, and wheels rumbling toward the east","Phoebus"]},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-5-4","title":"Lesson 20: The Pomegranate Seeds, part 4: The Baby Prince and the Barren Earth","steps":["Continue from Hecate going back to her cave and Ceres wandering on alone; stop when Quicksilver goes up the stairs to see King Pluto","Narrate what happened at the palace in Eleusis, and why nothing would grow","Map work: find Eleusis, near Athens"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Pomegranate Seeds”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_0004"}],"questions":["Why did Ceres lay baby Demophoon in the hot embers?","What did Queen Metanira''s interruption cost her son?","What did Ceres forbid until Proserpina came back?"],"answers":["To make him immortal","He would grow old and die like other people instead of living forever","Every crop, fruit, blade of grass, and flower from growing"]},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-5-5","title":"Lesson 21: The Pomegranate Seeds, part 5: Six Seeds","steps":["Read from Proserpina''s six months in Pluto''s palace to the end of the story","Narrate how Proserpina came home and why she must go back each year","Science link: connect the six months above and six below to the seasons, and draw the year as a circle half green, half bare"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Pomegranate Seeds”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_0004"}],"questions":["Why was there only one dried-up pomegranate to bring her?","What told Ceres something had changed before she saw Proserpina?","How many months a year must Proserpina spend with Pluto, and why?"],"answers":["Ceres had stopped all fruit from growing on the earth","Her torch went out and the fields turned green","Six, one for each pomegranate seed left in her mouth"],"notes":"The old Greeks used this story to explain winter: the earth is bare while Persephone is away."}]},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-u6","title":"Unit 6: The Golden Fleece","lessons":[{"id":"tanglewood-tales-6-1","title":"Lesson 22: The Golden Fleece, part 1: The Old Woman at the River","steps":["Read from the start of “The Golden Fleece” until the old woman hobbles away with her peacock","Narrate what Jason learned from Chiron, and what happened at the river","Map work: find Mount Olympus and Iolcus (Iolchos) in Thessaly"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Golden Fleece”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_0005"}],"questions":["What kind of creature was Jason''s teacher Chiron?","What did Jason lose crossing the river?","What reason did the old woman give for why Jason should help her?"],"answers":["A centaur: half man, half horse","One of his father''s golden-stringed sandals","Kings are meant to help the weak and needy"]},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-6-2","title":"Lesson 23: The Golden Fleece, part 2: The Man with One Sandal","steps":["Continue from Jason arriving at a crowd gathered by the sea; stop when the oaken figurehead speaks to Jason for the first time","Narrate how Jason ended up promising to fetch the Golden Fleece","Draw the Argo with its fifty oars and the carved woman on its prow"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Golden Fleece”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_0005"}],"questions":["Why was King Pelias afraid of a man with one sandal?","What did the Talking Oak tell Jason to do first?","What was special about the figurehead?"],"answers":["The Speaking Oak had said such a man would take his throne","Go to Argus and have him build a ship with fifty oars","It was carved from a branch of the Talking Oak and could speak and give advice"]},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-6-3","title":"Lesson 24: The Golden Fleece, part 3: The Voyage of the Argo","steps":["Continue from Jason asking the figurehead where to find fifty bold rowers; stop after the steel-feathered birds fly off and Orpheus is asked to stop singing","Narrate the voyage, one adventure at a time","Make a list of the Argonauts named in the story and one special gift each one had"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Golden Fleece”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_0005"}],"questions":["How did the Argonauts launch the heavy ship?","How did the Argo''s crew stop the Harpies from stealing King Phineus''s food?","How did they drive off the birds shooting steel feathers?"],"answers":["Orpheus played his harp while they sat with oars raised, and the ship slid into the sea","They set out a feast as bait, and the winged sons of the North Wind chased the Harpies off","By banging their swords on their shields"],"notes":"Hawthorne pokes fun at Atalanta for talking about \"the rights of women\", a dated 1853 joke worth a quick conversation."},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-6-4","title":"Lesson 25: The Golden Fleece, part 4: The Brazen Bulls","steps":["Continue from two young princes sailing up to the island; stop when Jason has plowed the field and sown the dragon''s teeth","Narrate King Aetes''s tasks and how Medea helped Jason","Map work: trace the Argo''s route through the Black Sea to Colchis (today''s country of Georgia)"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Golden Fleece”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_0005"}],"questions":["What two things did King Aetes say Jason must do before facing the dragon?","What protected Jason from the bulls'' fiery breath?","What saying comes from the way Jason tamed the bulls?"],"answers":["Tame and yoke the fire-breathing brass bulls, then plow a field and sow dragon''s teeth","Medea''s magic ointment","“Taking the bull by the horns”"],"notes":"This Medea is the same enchantress who tried to poison Theseus in “The Minotaur.” Hawthorne also calls Circe her aunt."},{"id":"tanglewood-tales-6-5","title":"Lesson 26: The Golden Fleece, part 5: The Grove of Mars","steps":["Read from Jason asking Medea how long he must wait for harvest to the end of the book","Narrate how Jason won the Golden Fleece and escaped","Draw the Golden Fleece glowing on its tree in the dark grove, with the dragon coiled around the trunk"],"links":[{"label":"Read “The Golden Fleece”","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/976/pg976-images.html#link2H_4_0005"}],"questions":["How did Jason defeat the army that grew from the dragon''s teeth?","How did Jason feel after the battle, and what did he say?","How did Medea get past the dragon?"],"answers":["Medea told him to throw a stone among them, and they fought each other","Sad; he said the Fleece no longer seemed so worth winning","She threw a sleeping potion down its throat"],"notes":"Medea hints that the old woman with the peacock was a goddess: in the old myths she is Juno (Hera). Hawthorne stops the story as the Argo sails home; the older myth goes on to darker events not suited to young children."}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"“Tanglewood Tales for Girls and Boys; Being a Second Wonder-Book” by Nathaniel Hawthorne (1853), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/976. Lesson plan by Freerange."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (listing_id, version) do update set content = excluded.content;
insert into public.listings (id, seller_name, seller_kind, seller_verified, title, subjects, grade_min, grade_max, philosophies, format, price, description, includes, lessons, lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement, family_style, lesson_steps, cover, color, esa_eligible, created_at, package_id, package_version, license, attribution, outline) values ('l-lib-this-country-of-ours', 'H. E. Marshall', 'open', false, 'This Country of Ours', array['history', 'civics', 'geography']::text[], 3, 7, array['charlotte_mason', 'classical']::text[], 'online', 0, 'H. E. Marshall''s classic story of America, told as one lively tale after another, from the Vikings and Columbus through the colonies, the Revolution, the Civil War, and up to 1917. Each day you read one chapter aloud, your children narrate it back, find the places on a map, and add a date to the family timeline. Written for children in a warm storytelling voice, and the full text is free online.', array['99 lessons in 12 units, one chapter each', 'Link to the free full text for every chapter', 'Map and timeline work in every lesson', 'Questions with answers for each chapter', 'Parent notes on 53 chapters where the 1917 telling is dated or one-sided']::text[], 99, 3, 30, 'high', true, '{}'::text[], '🗺️', '#2F6B5E', false, '2026-11-11T12:00:00.000Z', 'pk-lib-this-country-of-ours', 1, '{"kind":"public_domain"}'::jsonb, '“This Country of Ours: The Story of the United States” by H. E. Marshall (1917), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3761. Lesson plan by Freerange.', '[{"title":"Part I: Stories of Explorers and Pioneers","lessons":["Chapter 1: How the Vikings of Old Sought and Found New Lands","Chapter 2: The Sea of Darkness and the Great Faith of Columbus","Chapter 3: How Columbus Fared Forth Upon The Sea of Darkness and Came to Pleasant Lands Beyond","Chapter 4: How Columbus Returned Home in Triumph","Chapter 5: How America Was Named","Chapter 6: How The Flag of England Was Planted on the Shores of the New World","Chapter 7: How The Flag of France Was Planted in Florida","Chapter 8: How The French Founded a Colony in Florida","Chapter 9: How the Spaniards Drove the French Out of Florida","Chapter 10: How a Frenchman Avenged the Death of His Countrymen","Chapter 11: The Adventures of Sir Humphrey Gilbert","Chapter 12: About Sir Walter Raleigh''s Adventures in the Golden West"]},{"title":"Part II: Stories of Virginia","lessons":["Chapter 13: The Adventures of Captain John Smith","Chapter 14: More Adventures of Captain John Smith","Chapter 15: How the Colony was Saved","Chapter 16: How Pocahontas Took a Journey Over the Seas","Chapter 17: How the Redmen Fought Against Their White Brothers","Chapter 18: How Englishmen Fought a Duel With Tyranny","Chapter 19: The Coming of the Cavaliers","Chapter 20: Bacon''s Rebellion","Chapter 21: The Story of the Knights of the Golden Horseshoe"]},{"title":"Part III: Stories of New England (Pilgrims to Harvard)","lessons":["Chapter 22: The Story of the Pilgrim Fathers","Chapter 23: The Founding of Massachusetts","Chapter 24: The Story of Harry Vane","Chapter 25: The Story of Anne Hutchinson and the Founding of Rhode Island","Chapter 26: The Founding of Harvard","Chapter 27: How Quakers First Came to New England","Chapter 28: How Maine and New Hampshire Were Founded"]},{"title":"Part III: Stories of New England (Connecticut to Salem)","lessons":["Chapter 29: The Founding of Connecticut and War with the Indians","Chapter 30: The Founding of New Haven","Chapter 31: The Hunt for the Regicides","Chapter 32: King Philip''s War","Chapter 33: How The Charter of Connecticut Was Saved","Chapter 34: The Witches of Salem"]},{"title":"Part IV: Stories of the Middle and Southern Colonies","lessons":["Chapter 35: The Founding of Maryland","Chapter 36: How New Amsterdam Became New York","Chapter 37: How a German Ruled New York","Chapter 38: Pirates!","Chapter 39: The Founding of New Jersey","Chapter 40: The Founding of Pennsylvania","Chapter 41: How Benjamin Franklin Came to Philadelphia","Chapter 42: The Founding of North and South Carolina","Chapter 43: War With the Indians in North and South Carolina","Chapter 44: The Founding of Georgia"]},{"title":"Part V: Stories of the French in America","lessons":["Chapter 45: How the Mississippi was Discovered","Chapter 46: King William''s War and Queen Anne''s War","Chapter 47: The Mississippi Bubble","Chapter 48: How a Terrible Disaster Befell the British Army","Chapter 49: The End of the French Rule in America","Chapter 50: The Rebellion of Pontiac"]},{"title":"Part VI: Stories of the Struggle for Liberty (Tea Party to Saratoga)","lessons":["Chapter 51: The Boston Tea party","Chapter 52: Paul Revere''s Ride - The Unsheathing of the Sword","Chapter 53: The First Thrust-The Battle of Bunker Hill","Chapter 54: The War In Canada","Chapter 55: The Birth of A Great Nation","Chapter 56: The Darkest Hour - Trenton and Princeton","Chapter 57: Burgoyne''s Campaign - Bennington and Oriskany","Chapter 58: Burgoyne''s Campaign - Bemis Heights and Saratoga"]},{"title":"Part VI: Stories of the Struggle for Liberty (Valley Forge to Yorktown)","lessons":["Chapter 59: Brandywine - Germantown - Valley Forge","Chapter 60: War on the Sea","Chapter 61: The Battle of Monmouth - The Story of Captain Molly","Chapter 62: The Story of a Great Crime","Chapter 63: A Turning Point in the World''s History"]},{"title":"Part VII: The Young Nation (Washington to Jackson)","lessons":["Chapter 64: Washington First In War, First In Peace","Chapter 65: Adams - How He Kept Peace with France","Chapter 66: Jefferson - How the Territory of the United States was Doubled","Chapter 67: Jefferson - How the Door Into the Far West was Opened","Chapter 68: Jefferson - About An American Who Wanted to be a King","Chapter 69: Madison - The Shooting Star and the Prophet","Chapter 70: Madison - War with Great Britain","Chapter 71: Monroe-The First Whispers of a Storm-Monroe''s Famous Doctrine","Chapter 72: Adams - The Tariff of Abominations","Chapter 73: Jackson - \"Liberty and Union, Now and Forever\" - Van Buren - Hard Times"]},{"title":"Part VII: Growth and Division (Harrison to Buchanan)","lessons":["Chapter 74: Harrison - The Hero of Tippecanoe","Chapter 75: Tyler - Florida Becomes a State","Chapter 76: Polk - How Much Land Was Added to the United States","Chapter 77: Polk - The Finding of Gold","Chapter 78: Taylor - Union or Disunion","Chapter 79: Fillmore - The Underground Railroad","Chapter 80: Pierce - The Story of \"Bleeding Kansas\"","Chapter 81: Buchanan - The Story of the Mormons","Chapter 82: Buchanan - The First Shots"]},{"title":"Part VII: The Civil War (Lincoln)","lessons":["Chapter 83: Lincoln - From Bull Run to Fort Donelson","Chapter 84: Lincoln - The Story of the First Battle Between Ironclads","Chapter 85: Lincoln - The Battle of Shiloh and the Taking of New Orleans","Chapter 86: Lincoln - The Slaves are Made Free","Chapter 87: Lincoln - Chancellorsville - The Death of Stonewall Jackson","Chapter 88: Lincoln - The Battle of Gettysburg","Chapter 89: Lincoln-Grant''s Campaign-Sheridan''s Ride","Chapter 90: Lincoln - Sherman''s March to the Sea - Lincoln Re-Elected President","Chapter 91: Lincoln - The End of the War - The President''s Death"]},{"title":"Part VII: Reunion and a New Century (Johnson to Wilson)","lessons":["Chapter 92: Johnson - How The President Was Impeached","Chapter 93: Grant - A Peaceful Victory","Chapter 94: Hayes - Garfield - Arthur","Chapter 95: Cleveland - Harrison - Cleveland","Chapter 96: McKinley - War and Sudden Death","Chapter 97: Roosevelt - Taft","Chapter 98: Wilson - Troubles With Mexico","Chapter 99: Wilson -The Great War"]}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set seller_name = excluded.seller_name, seller_kind = excluded.seller_kind, seller_verified = excluded.seller_verified, title = excluded.title, subjects = excluded.subjects, grade_min = excluded.grade_min, grade_max = excluded.grade_max, philosophies = excluded.philosophies, format = excluded.format, price = excluded.price, description = excluded.description, includes = excluded.includes, lessons = excluded.lessons, lessons_per_week = excluded.lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson = excluded.minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement = excluded.parent_involvement, family_style = excluded.family_style, lesson_steps = excluded.lesson_steps, cover = excluded.cover, color = excluded.color, esa_eligible = excluded.esa_eligible, package_id = excluded.package_id, package_version = excluded.package_version, license = excluded.license, attribution = excluded.attribution, outline = excluded.outline;
insert into public.package_releases (listing_id, version, content) values ('l-lib-this-country-of-ours', 1, '{"units":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u1","title":"Part I: Stories of Explorers and Pioneers","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-1","title":"Chapter 1: How the Vikings of Old Sought and Found New Lands","steps":["Read chapter 1 aloud together; listen for the three names Leif gives the new lands","Oral narration: tell the story of Leif''s voyage, then what happened to Thorvald and Thorfinn","Find Greenland, Labrador, Newfoundland and Nova Scotia on a map","Timeline: Leif Ericson reaches Vineland (about 1000)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 1","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00019"}],"questions":["Who first saw the new land but never went ashore?","What did Tyrker find that gave Vineland its name?","What was the name of the first child of European parents born in Vineland?"],"answers":["Bjarni","Grapes (wild vines)","Snorri"],"notes":"The book calls the Native people ''savages'' and describes them as ugly; the Norse sagas only tell the newcomers'' side. Talk about how the people already living there might have seen armed strangers arriving."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-2","title":"Chapter 2: The Sea of Darkness and the Great Faith of Columbus","steps":["Read chapter 2 aloud together; listen for the dangers people believed lived in the Sea of Darkness","Oral narration: tell how Columbus finally won the help of Spain","Find Portugal, Spain, Granada and Palos on a map","Timeline: Columbus sails from Palos (August 3, 1492)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 2","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00091"}],"questions":["Why did Columbus think he could reach India by sailing west?","Which king first turned Columbus down and then secretly sent out his own ships?","What did Queen Isabella say she would do to pay for the voyage?"],"answers":["Because the world is round","King John of Portugal","Pawn her jewels"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-3","title":"Chapter 3: How Columbus Fared Forth Upon The Sea of Darkness and Came to Pleasant Lands Beyond","steps":["Read chapter 3 aloud together; listen for how Columbus kept his crew from losing heart","Oral narration: tell about the first sight of land and the landing","Find the Bahamas (San Salvador), Cuba and the West Indies on a map","Timeline: Columbus lands on San Salvador (October 12, 1492)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 3","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00122"}],"questions":["Why did Columbus keep two different records of how far the ships had sailed?","Why are these islands called the West Indies?","What two new things did the Spaniards see but not bother to bring home?"],"answers":["So the frightened sailors would not know how far from home they were","Columbus thought he had reached islands near India","Tobacco and potatoes"],"notes":"The Taíno people are called ''naked savages'' here and are seen only as sources of gold. A good moment to talk about who already lived on these islands and what the coming of the Spaniards meant for them."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-4","title":"Chapter 4: How Columbus Returned Home in Triumph","steps":["Read chapter 4 aloud together; listen for the story of the egg","Oral narration: tell about Columbus''s welcome in Spain and what he found when he went back to his fort","Find Haiti (Española), Barcelona and the mouth of the Orinoco River on a map","Timeline: Columbus returns to Palos (March 15, 1493)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 4","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00158"}],"questions":["How was the Santa Maria wrecked?","What point did Columbus make with the egg?","What had happened to the fort of La Navidad when Columbus returned?"],"answers":["A boy was left at the tiller and ran her onto a sandbank","Once a thing has been done, anyone can see how easy it is","It had been burned and all the men were dead"],"notes":"The chapter mentions ''dark-skinned savages'' Columbus brought to Spain; they were Taíno people taken across the ocean. Worth a short talk about how they came to be there."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-5","title":"Chapter 5: How America Was Named","steps":["Read chapter 5 aloud together; listen for who first wrote down the word ''America''","Oral narration: explain how the New World got its name","Find Florence, Venezuela, South America and the District of Columbia on a map","Timeline: The name ''America'' first appears in print (1507)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 5","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00185"}],"questions":["What work did Amerigo Vespucci do in Florence before he went to sea?","Was the name America first given to North or South America?","Name one place that was named to honour Columbus."],"answers":["He was a clerk for the Medici merchant house","South America","The District of Columbia (also British Columbia or Colombia)"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-6","title":"Chapter 6: How The Flag of England Was Planted on the Shores of the New World","steps":["Read chapter 6 aloud together; listen for the name of Cabot''s ship","Oral narration: tell the story of John Cabot''s voyage from Bristol","Find Bristol, Ireland, Cape Breton and Labrador on a map","Timeline: John Cabot reaches North America (June 24, 1497)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 6","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00209"}],"questions":["Which English king gave Cabot permission to sail?","How did Cabot''s men catch fish in the new land?","How much did the king give Cabot as a reward?"],"answers":["Henry VII","They lowered a basket weighted with a stone","Ten pounds"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-7","title":"Chapter 7: How The Flag of France Was Planted in Florida","steps":["Read chapter 7 aloud together; listen for why the Huguenots wanted to leave France","Oral narration: tell what happened to the men left at Charlesfort","Find Florida and Beaufort, South Carolina, on a map","Timeline: Ribaut''s Huguenots land in Florida (1562)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 7","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00235"}],"questions":["Who were the Huguenots?","Who kept the Charlesfort men from starving?","What were the sails of their homemade ship made from?"],"answers":["French Protestants","The local Indian chiefs who shared their food","Shirts and bedclothes"],"notes":"Uses ''Redmen'' and ''Pale-faces'', but also shows how generous the local people were. The voyage home ends with the starving men killing one of their own for food; preview before reading to young children."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-8","title":"Chapter 8: How The French Founded a Colony in Florida","steps":["Read chapter 8 aloud together; listen for how many times ships arrive at Fort Caroline","Oral narration: tell the troubles of Fort Caroline from its building to Ribaut''s arrival","Find the St. Johns River in Florida and the West Indies on a map","Timeline: Fort Caroline is built (1564)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 8","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00282"}],"questions":["What did the French name their fort, and why?","Why did the colonists starve when there was rich land all around them?","Which English admiral helped the starving French?"],"answers":["Fort Caroline, in honour of King Charles","They never ploughed or planted; they waited for food from France","John Hawkins"],"notes":"Chief Satouriona and his people are called ''savages'' with ''fiendish'' yells. Notice that the French broke their promise to him; talk about the Timucua side of the story."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-9","title":"Chapter 9: How the Spaniards Drove the French Out of Florida","steps":["Read chapter 9 aloud together; listen for the question Menendez asks the French every time he meets them","Oral narration: tell how Menendez took Fort Caroline and what happened to Ribaut''s men","Find St. Augustine and the St. Johns River in Florida on a map","Timeline: Menendez founds St. Augustine and destroys Fort Caroline (1565)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 9","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00354"}],"questions":["What town did Menendez found, and why is it famous today?","Why were there almost no guards on the walls when the Spaniards attacked Fort Caroline?","Why did Menendez say he was killing the Frenchmen?"],"answers":["St. Augustine, the oldest town in the United States","It was raining so hard the captain sent the guards to bed","Because they were Protestants (''Lutherans''), not because they were French"],"notes":"A grim chapter of massacres driven by religious hatred between Catholics and Protestants; preview it for younger children. A chance to talk about religious freedom."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-10","title":"Chapter 10: How a Frenchman Avenged the Death of His Countrymen","steps":["Read chapter 10 aloud together; listen for who joins Gourges in the attack","Oral narration: tell how Dominique de Gourges took revenge","Find Fort Caroline (Jacksonville, Florida), Cuba and France on a map","Timeline: Gourges destroys the Spanish forts on the St. Johns River (1568)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 10","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00432"}],"questions":["How did Gourges pay for his expedition?","Which Indian chief helped the French against the Spaniards?","What did the King of Spain do when he heard what Gourges had done?"],"answers":["He sold everything he had and borrowed money","Satouriona","He offered a large reward for his head"],"notes":"Revenge is shown as heroic here, and the Native warriors are described as ''mad with the desire of blood.'' Ask your child whether revenge solved anything, and why Satouriona''s people hated the Spaniards."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-11","title":"Chapter 11: The Adventures of Sir Humphrey Gilbert","steps":["Read chapter 11 aloud together; listen for the names of Sir Humphrey''s ships","Oral narration: tell about Sir Humphrey''s last night at sea","Find St. John''s, Newfoundland, and Plymouth, England, on a map","Timeline: Sir Humphrey Gilbert claims Newfoundland for England (August 5, 1583)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 11","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00470"}],"questions":["What did Sir Humphrey hold in his hands to show he was taking the land?","Why were so many ships from different countries at St. John''s?","What did Sir Humphrey call out from the little Squirrel during the storm?"],"answers":["A hazel twig and a piece of sod","They came every summer to fish","That we are as near to heaven by sea as by land"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-1-12","title":"Chapter 12: About Sir Walter Raleigh''s Adventures in the Golden West","steps":["Read chapter 12 aloud together; listen for the word carved on the post","Oral narration: tell the story of the Lost Colony of Roanoke","Find Roanoke Island, Chesapeake Bay and North Carolina on a map","Timeline: Virginia Dare is born on Roanoke Island (August 18, 1587)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 12","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00498"}],"questions":["Who was the land of Virginia named for?","What two things did Lane''s colonists bring back to England?","What word did John White find carved on a post?"],"answers":["Queen Elizabeth, the ''Virgin Queen''","Tobacco and potatoes","Croatoan"],"notes":"The book calls the Native people ''cruel and revengeful'' while also showing the English burning their cornfields over a stolen cup. Talk about how the Roanoke and Croatoan people experienced these visits."}]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u2","title":"Part II: Stories of Virginia","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-2-1","title":"Chapter 13: The Adventures of Captain John Smith","steps":["Read chapter 13 aloud together; listen for how Smith used his compass","Oral narration: tell how the Jamestown settlers lived their first summer and how Smith was captured","Find Chesapeake Bay, Cape Henry, the James River and the Chickahominy River on a map","Timeline: Jamestown is founded (1607)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 13","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00554"}],"questions":["What were the names of the three ships?","Why was Jamestown short of food so soon?","Who saved John Smith''s life, according to his story?"],"answers":["Susan Constant, Godspeed and Discovery","Most men were gentlemen who would not work, and they could not hunt or farm well","Pocahontas"],"notes":"The book calls the Powhatan people ''savages'' and ''wily.'' Historians question whether the Pocahontas rescue happened the way Smith told it; he wrote it years later, and it may have been a ceremony he did not understand."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-2-2","title":"Chapter 14: More Adventures of Captain John Smith","steps":["Read chapter 14 aloud together; listen for what kind of workers Smith asked England to send","Oral narration: tell about the crowning of Powhatan and how Pocahontas warned Smith","Find Jamestown, Werowocomoco and the Bermudas on a map","Timeline: The Powhatan is ''crowned'' by order of King James (1608)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 14","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00605"}],"questions":["Why would the Powhatan not kneel to be crowned?","What rule did Smith make for the lazy settlers?","Why did Smith have to leave Virginia?"],"answers":["He had never seen a crown and did not see why a king should kneel","He who will not work shall not eat","He was badly hurt when a bag of gunpowder exploded"],"notes":"The Powhatan is called ''wily'' and ''savage,'' and his wish to see the English leave his land is treated as treachery. Ask: whose land was it, and why might he want the newcomers gone?"},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-2-3","title":"Chapter 15: How the Colony was Saved","steps":["Read chapter 15 aloud together; listen for the names of the two ships built on Bermuda","Oral narration: tell how the colony was almost abandoned and how it was saved","Find the Bermudas, the James River and the Appomattox River on a map","Timeline: Lord Delaware arrives and saves Jamestown (1610)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 15","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00662"}],"questions":["What was the terrible winter at Jamestown called?","How did Sir Thomas Dale get the settlers to work harder?","What did the chief receive for helping Argall capture Pocahontas?"],"answers":["The Starving Time","He gave each man three acres of his own land to farm","A copper kettle"],"notes":"The book treats the kidnapping of Pocahontas as a clever plan and calls the Powhatan people ''wily Redmen.'' Mention that she was taken as a hostage against her will. The Starving Time includes a brief mention of cannibalism."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-2-4","title":"Chapter 16: How Pocahontas Took a Journey Over the Seas","steps":["Read chapter 16 aloud together; listen for how the Indian counselor tried to count the English","Oral narration: tell about Pocahontas''s visit to England","Find London, Plymouth and Gravesend in England on a map","Timeline: Pocahontas dies at Gravesend (1617)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 16","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00732"}],"questions":["What crop made the Virginia farmers money?","What did King James think of smoking?","Where did Pocahontas die?"],"answers":["Tobacco","He hated it and wrote a book against it","Gravesend, England"],"notes":"Pocahontas (Matoaka) is called a ''half naked savage'' and ''heathen'' here. Point out that she was a real young woman far from home, and that she died very young."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-2-5","title":"Chapter 17: How the Redmen Fought Against Their White Brothers","steps":["Read chapter 17 aloud together; listen for the three important things that happened in 1619","Oral narration: tell about the first House of Burgesses","Find Jamestown and Richmond, Virginia, on a map","Timeline: First House of Burgesses meets; first enslaved Africans arrive in Virginia (1619)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 17","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00767"}],"questions":["What was Virginia''s first parliament called?","Where did it meet?","Who warned his master and saved many settlers in 1622?"],"answers":["The House of Burgesses","In the little wooden church at Jamestown","Chanco"],"notes":"The 1622 attack is told only from the settlers'' side, with the Powhatan as ''treacherous''; the brutal English revenge is described too. The book rightly calls the arrival of enslaved Africans in 1619 the sad beginning of slavery; a good place to stop and talk."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-2-6","title":"Chapter 18: How Englishmen Fought a Duel With Tyranny","steps":["Read chapter 18 aloud together; listen for what the company kept shouting at the meeting","Oral narration: tell how the company members stood up to King James","Find London and Virginia on a map","Timeline: Virginia becomes a royal (crown) colony (1624)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 18","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00786"}],"questions":["Why was Sir Walter Raleigh put to death?","Whom did King James not want the company to choose as treasurer?","Whom did the company elect instead?"],"answers":["The King of Spain demanded it after Raleigh failed to find a gold mine","Sir Edwin Sandys","The Earl of Southampton"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-2-7","title":"Chapter 19: The Coming of the Cavaliers","steps":["Read chapter 19 aloud together; listen for why Virginia was called the Old Dominion","Oral narration: tell how Virginia took the King''s side and what happened when Cromwell''s ships came","Find Jamestown, Virginia, and England on a map","Timeline: Cavaliers flee to Virginia after King Charles I is beheaded (1649)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 19","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00806"}],"questions":["What did Governor Berkeley think of free schools and printing?","Who were the Cavaliers?","What did the Navigation Law say?"],"answers":["He was glad Virginia had none and hoped it never would","Supporters of the King who fled England after Charles I was beheaded","Colonies could only ship goods in British ships and trade mainly with England"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-2-8","title":"Chapter 20: Bacon''s Rebellion","steps":["Read chapter 20 aloud together; listen for what Queen Pamunky kept crying out","Oral narration: tell the story of Bacon''s Rebellion from beginning to end","Find Jamestown and the Eastern Shore of Virginia (Accomac) on a map","Timeline: Bacon''s Rebellion; Jamestown is burned (1676)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 20","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00831"}],"questions":["Why were the settlers angry at Governor Berkeley at first?","What happened to Jamestown during the rebellion?","How did Bacon''s Rebellion come to an end?"],"answers":["He would not protect them or let them fight the Indians","Bacon burned it to the ground","Bacon died, and the rebellion fell apart"],"notes":"The book calls Native people ''Indian wolves'' (quoting Bacon) and celebrates the ''Battle of Bloody Run.'' It does show Native anger at losing their land, and Queen Cockacoeske of the Pamunkey grieving her husband; talk about her side."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-2-9","title":"Chapter 21: The Story of the Knights of the Golden Horseshoe","steps":["Read chapter 21 aloud together; listen for the Latin words on the golden horseshoes","Oral narration: tell about Spotswood''s journey over the mountains","Find the Blue Ridge Mountains, the Shenandoah Valley and Williamsburg on a map","Timeline: Spotswood''s Knights of the Golden Horseshoe cross the Blue Ridge (1716)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 21","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00903"}],"questions":["What did Spotswood want to name the Shenandoah River?","Why did he give his friends golden horseshoes?","Which town became the capital of Virginia after Jamestown?"],"answers":["The Euphrates","Their horses had been shod with iron for the trip, which was unusual in Virginia","Williamsburg"]}]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u3","title":"Part III: Stories of New England (Pilgrims to Harvard)","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-3-1","title":"Chapter 22: The Story of the Pilgrim Fathers","steps":["Read chapter 22 aloud together; listen for why the Pilgrims left Holland","Oral narration: tell the Pilgrims'' journey from Scrooby to Plymouth","Find Scrooby, Leyden (Leiden), Plymouth in England and Cape Cod on a map","Timeline: The Mayflower lands at Plymouth (1620)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 22","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00923"}],"questions":["Why did the Speedwell not cross the ocean?","Who taught the Pilgrims to plant corn?","What did the Governor send back to Canonicus in the snakeskin?"],"answers":["Her captain said she leaked too badly, so she was sent back","Squanto","Bullets and gunpowder"],"notes":"Native people are ''wild Redmen'' and ''savages,'' and the land is called empty. Squanto''s own village had just been wiped out by disease brought by Europeans; worth explaining why the coast seemed so empty."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-3-2","title":"Chapter 23: The Founding of Massachusetts","steps":["Read chapter 23 aloud together; listen for what made the Massachusetts charter different","Oral narration: tell how Boston was founded and why Roger Williams was sent away","Find Salem, Boston, the Charles River and Providence on a map","Timeline: John Winthrop''s Puritans found Boston (1630)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 23","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00977"}],"questions":["Who was the first governor of the Massachusetts Bay colony?","Who could vote in early Massachusetts?","What did Roger Williams say about the land in America?"],"answers":["John Winthrop","Only church members","It belonged to the Native people, so settlers should buy it from them"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-3-3","title":"Chapter 24: The Story of Harry Vane","steps":["Read chapter 24 aloud together; listen for how old Harry Vane was when he became governor","Oral narration: tell the story of young Governor Vane","Find Boston and London on a map","Timeline: Harry Vane is elected Governor of Massachusetts (1636)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 24","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id00997"}],"questions":["Why did Harry Vane leave England for New England?","How did Vane show his grandness as Governor?","What did Winthrop, his rival, say about him later?"],"answers":["For conscience'' sake; he was a Puritan","He had soldiers march before him and made a royal tour","That he was a true friend to New England with a noble, generous mind"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-3-4","title":"Chapter 25: The Story of Anne Hutchinson and the Founding of Rhode Island","steps":["Read chapter 25 aloud together; listen for what Anne Hutchinson''s husband said about her","Oral narration: tell why Anne Hutchinson was sent away and what she founded","Find Portsmouth, Newport and Providence in Rhode Island on a map","Timeline: Anne Hutchinson is banished and settles in Rhode Island (1638)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 25","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01011"}],"questions":["Why did Anne Hutchinson start her meetings?","Which two towns did she and her friends found?","What made Rhode Island''s charter special?"],"answers":["Women were not allowed at the men''s meetings","Portsmouth and Newport","No one could be punished for their religious opinions"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-3-5","title":"Chapter 26: The Founding of Harvard","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for how the college got its name","Ask your child to narrate the story of John Harvard and his gift","Find Cambridge and Charlestown, Massachusetts, near Boston on a map","Timeline: Massachusetts votes money to found a college (1636)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 26","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01022"}],"questions":["What did John Harvard leave to the new college when he died?","What was the town of Newtown renamed, and why?","What happened to John Harvard''s books about a hundred years later?"],"answers":["Half his money and all his books","Cambridge, after the English university many settlers came from","They burned in a library fire; only one book survived"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-3-6","title":"Chapter 27: How Quakers First Came to New England","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for what made the Quakers different","Ask your child to narrate how Boston treated the Quakers and how it finally changed","Find Boston and England on a map; trace the voyage the Quakers were sent back on","Timeline: First Quakers arrive in Boston (1656)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 27","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01033"}],"questions":["Name two customs that set the Quakers apart from other people.","Who was the old man who paid for food for the first two Quaker women?","Which king wrote to tell Massachusetts to stop persecuting the Quakers?"],"answers":["Any two: refused to fight, swear oaths, use titles, or take off their hats; said thee and thou","Nicholas Upshal","King Charles (Charles II)"],"notes":"This chapter includes harsh punishments and hangings; a good chance to talk about religious freedom and how the Puritans, who fled persecution, persecuted others."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-3-7","title":"Chapter 28: How Maine and New Hampshire Were Founded","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for why these colonies were founded","Ask your child to narrate how Beacon Hill got its name","Find Maine, New Hampshire, and Boston on a map","Timeline: Mason and Gorges divide their land; New Hampshire is named (1629)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 28","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01058"}],"questions":["Who were the two men chiefly behind the founding of Maine and New Hampshire?","Why did Mason call his land New Hampshire?","Until what year was Maine part of Massachusetts?"],"answers":["Sir Ferdinando Gorges and Captain John Mason","After Hampshire, the English county he was fond of","1820"]}]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u4","title":"Part III: Stories of New England (Connecticut to Salem)","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-4-1","title":"Chapter 29: The Founding of Connecticut and War with the Indians","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for why Thomas Hooker left Massachusetts","Ask your child to narrate Hooker''s journey through the wilderness","Find Boston, Hartford, and the Connecticut River on a map","Timeline: Hooker''s congregation settles Hartford (1636); Pequot War (1637)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 29","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01076"}],"questions":["Why did Thomas Hooker and his people want to leave Massachusetts?","What town did they build on the Connecticut River?","How did the Fundamental Orders differ from the rules of Massachusetts?"],"answers":["He disliked rule by a few, and the land around Boston was crowded and poor","Hartford","A man no longer had to be a church member to vote (and the King was not mentioned)"],"notes":"The Pequots are called \"savages\" and \"wily,\" and the burning of the Pequot fort at Mystic, where hundreds of people including women and children died, is told as just vengeance. Talk about how the Pequots might tell this story."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-4-2","title":"Chapter 30: The Founding of New Haven","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for how New Haven was governed","Ask your child to narrate how the New Haven settlers got their land","Find New Haven, Connecticut, on a map along Long Island Sound","Timeline: New Haven founded (1638); United Colonies of New England formed (1643)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 30","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01112"}],"questions":["Who founded New Haven?","Why were no juries allowed in New Haven?","Which colony was not invited to join the United Colonies of New England?"],"answers":["John Davenport","Juries are not mentioned in the Bible","Rhode Island"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-4-3","title":"Chapter 31: The Hunt for the Regicides","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for the hiding places Goffe and Whalley used","Ask your child to narrate how the people of New Haven and Guilford kept the regicides safe","Find New Haven and Guilford, Connecticut, and Hadley, Massachusetts, on a map","Timeline: Charles II restored; regicides Whalley and Goffe flee to New England (1660)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 31","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01123"}],"questions":["Why were Whalley and Goffe hunted by the King''s messengers?","Why couldn''t the Royalist messengers ride on to New Haven on Sunday?","Where did the two men hide during their last years?"],"answers":["They had helped condemn King Charles I to death","Puritan law forbade riding on Sunday except to church","In a secret room in minister John Russell''s house in Hadley"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-4-4","title":"Chapter 32: King Philip''s War","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for why Philip turned to war","Ask your child to narrate the mysterious stranger who saved Hadley","Find Plymouth, Swansea, and Hadley, Massachusetts, and Rhode Island on a map","Timeline: King Philip''s War begins (1675)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 32","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01145"}],"questions":["What was King Philip''s real name, and who was his father?","Who was the strange leader who appeared at Hadley?","How did King Philip die?"],"answers":["Metacomet; his father was Massasoit","Colonel Goffe, the hidden regicide","He was killed by one of his own followers"],"notes":"The Wampanoag and their allies are described as \"savages\" and compared to wild animals, and the war is told only from the colonists'' side. Discuss Philip''s real grievance: settlers taking more and more of his people''s land."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-4-5","title":"Chapter 33: How The Charter of Connecticut Was Saved","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for what happened when the candles went out","Ask your child to narrate the story of the Charter Oak","Find Hartford, Connecticut, and Boston on a map","Timeline: Andros takes over Connecticut''s government; Charter Oak legend (1687)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 33","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01171"}],"questions":["Who was the royal Governor sent to rule all of New England?","Where was the Connecticut charter said to be hidden?","How did the sentry recognize Andros when he tried to escape in woman''s clothes?"],"answers":["Sir Edmund Andros","In a hollow oak tree, the Charter Oak","By his boots"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-4-6","title":"Chapter 34: The Witches of Salem","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for how the accusations started","Ask your child to narrate how the people of Salem came back to their senses and what the judge and jury did afterward","Find Salem, Massachusetts, on a map","Timeline: Salem witch trials (1692)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 34","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01201"}],"questions":["Whose daughter and niece first began acting strangely in Salem village?","How many people were put to death by hanging?","What did Judge Sewall do to show he was sorry?"],"answers":["The minister Samuel Parris","Twenty","He confessed his guilt before the congregation and kept a yearly day of fasting and prayer"],"notes":"The servant first accused (Tituba) was an enslaved woman, likely of Indigenous Caribbean or South American origin, which the book does not mention. The chapter also links old age and ugliness with suspicion; worth a gentle discussion."}]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u5","title":"Part IV: Stories of the Middle and Southern Colonies","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-5-1","title":"Chapter 35: The Founding of Maryland","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for why Lord Baltimore wanted a colony","Ask your child to narrate the landing of the Ark and the Dove","Find Chesapeake Bay, the Potomac River, and the Isle of Kent on a map","Timeline: Maryland Toleration Act (1649)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 35","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01222"}],"questions":["What two ships carried the first Maryland colonists?","What did Lord Baltimore have to give the King each year for Maryland?","What did the Toleration Act promise?"],"answers":["The Ark and the Dove","Two Indian arrows (plus a fifth of any gold or silver found)","That Christians would not be troubled for their religion"],"notes":"The book says King James granted Maryland in 1632; it was actually King Charles I, whose wife Henrietta Maria gave the colony its name."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-5-2","title":"Chapter 36: How New Amsterdam Became New York","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for what kind of man Peter Stuyvesant was","Ask your child to narrate how New Amsterdam became New York without a battle","Find the Hudson River, Manhattan Island, Albany, and the Delaware River on a map","Timeline: The English take New Amsterdam and rename it New York (1664)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 36","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01245"}],"questions":["Who sailed up the river that now bears his name, and for which country?","What was special about Peter Stuyvesant''s wooden leg?","Why did Stuyvesant finally surrender the town?"],"answers":["Henry Hudson, sailing for the Dutch","It was bound with silver","Everyone, even his own son, begged him to; the fort had little powder, food, or water"],"notes":"The book repeats the story that Manhattan was bought for trinkets; the Lenape likely understood the deal as sharing use of the land, not giving it up forever."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-5-3","title":"Chapter 37: How a German Ruled New York","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for why people feared Nicholson","Ask your child to narrate Leisler''s rise and fall","Find New York City and Germany on a map","Timeline: Leisler takes control of New York (1689)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 37","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01296"}],"questions":["Why did Jacob Leisler refuse to pay a tax?","What did Leisler demand before he would let Ingoldsby''s soldiers into the fort?","How did people feel about Leisler after he was hanged?"],"answers":["The tax collector was a Catholic (\"Papist\")","Written orders from the King or the Governor","Many were sorry and saw him as a martyr who had meant well"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-5-4","title":"Chapter 38: Pirates!","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for the difference between a privateer and a pirate","Ask your child to narrate how Captain Kidd went from pirate hunter to pirate","Find New York, Boston, Long Island Sound, the West Indies, and Madagascar on a map","Timeline: Captain Kidd hanged as a pirate (1701)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 38","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01318"}],"questions":["What was a letter of marque?","What was the name of Captain Kidd''s first ship?","Why did people search for treasure along Long Island Sound for years afterward?"],"answers":["A paper allowing a private ship to attack enemy ships in wartime","The Adventure","Little treasure was found on Kidd''s sloop, so people thought he had buried it"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-5-5","title":"Chapter 39: The Founding of New Jersey","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for how New Jersey got its name","Ask your child to narrate how Philip Carteret arrived and why the settlers rebelled","Find the Isle of Jersey in the English Channel and Elizabeth, New Jersey, on a map","Timeline: Philip Carteret lands and founds Elizabeth (1665)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 39","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01340"}],"questions":["Why was the colony named New Jersey?","What did Philip Carteret carry on his shoulder when he landed, and why?","Into which two parts was New Jersey divided?"],"answers":["To honor Sir George Carteret, who had defended the Isle of Jersey for the King","A hoe, to show he would live and work among the settlers","East Jersey and West Jersey"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-5-6","title":"Chapter 40: The Founding of Pennsylvania","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for how Pennsylvania got its name","Ask your child to narrate Penn''s meeting with the Lenape chiefs under the great elm","Find Philadelphia, the Delaware River, Delaware Bay, and the Mason-Dixon Line on a map","Timeline: William Penn receives his charter for Pennsylvania (1681)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 40","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01349"}],"questions":["How did William Penn come to own Pennsylvania?","What does the name Philadelphia mean?","What name did the Indians give Penn?"],"answers":["King Charles II gave him the land to pay a debt owed to Penn''s father","City of brotherly love","Onas"],"notes":"The treaty scene is told in a romantic way and calls Native people \"Redmen\" with \"wild dances.\" Penn''s fair dealing was real, but later Pennsylvania leaders broke its spirit (for example, the Walking Purchase of 1737)."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-5-7","title":"Chapter 41: How Benjamin Franklin Came to Philadelphia","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for how young Ben got his writing printed","Ask your child to narrate Ben Franklin''s arrival in Philadelphia","Trace Franklin''s trip from Boston to New York to Philadelphia on a map","Timeline: Benjamin Franklin arrives in Philadelphia (1723)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 41","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01385"}],"questions":["How did Ben get his first article into his brother''s newspaper?","What did he buy at the bakery when he arrived in Philadelphia?","Name two things Franklin did for the city of Philadelphia."],"answers":["He disguised his handwriting and slipped it under the door at night","Three big rolls","Any two: a library, fire brigade, police force, the post office, a hospital, the University of Pennsylvania"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-5-8","title":"Chapter 42: The Founding of North and South Carolina","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for the kinds of people who came to Carolina","Ask your child to narrate why the settlers rejected the Grand Model","Find North Carolina, South Carolina, and the Carolina coast on a map; compare latitudes 30 and 36 degrees","Timeline: Charles II grants Carolina to eight Lords Proprietors (1663)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 42","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01399"}],"questions":["Whom was Carolina named for by the French, and by the English?","Why did the Huguenots leave France?","Why did the Virginians call Carolina \"Rogues'' Harbour\"?"],"answers":["Charles IX of France; Charles II of England","Louis XIV revoked the Edict of Nantes and forbade their worship","Many people came to escape debts they owed elsewhere"],"notes":"The book says white men could not endure rice-field work and that \"no one saw anything wrong\" with slavery; neither is accurate. Enslaved Africans were forced into the work, many brought rice-growing skill from West Africa, and some people did object at the time."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-5-9","title":"Chapter 43: War With the Indians in North and South Carolina","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for why the Tuscaroras grew angry","Ask your child to narrate how Sanute warned his friend Fraser","Find Albemarle Sound, New Bern, Charleston, and St. Augustine on a map","Timeline: Tuscarora War begins (1711); Yamasee War (1715)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 43","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01415"}],"questions":["Why did the Tuscaroras come to hate the settlers?","Whom did the Tuscaroras join after their defeat, and what did the Five Nations become?","Who warned the Fraser family to flee?"],"answers":["They were being pushed farther and farther from their lands","The Five Nations in New York, which became the Six Nations","The chief Sanute"],"notes":"Native people are called \"silent and crafty\" and \"raging demons,\" and only the settlers'' losses are described; many captured Tuscaroras were sold into slavery. The book also says King William made South Carolina royal in 1719; the king then was George I."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-5-10","title":"Chapter 44: The Founding of Georgia","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for why Oglethorpe wanted a new colony","Ask your child to narrate how Oglethorpe tricked the Spaniards with a letter","Find the Savannah and Altamaha rivers, Savannah, St. Simons Island, and St. Augustine on a map","Timeline: Oglethorpe''s colonists found Savannah, Georgia (1733)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 44","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01442"}],"questions":["What kind of people did Oglethorpe first want to help with his colony?","Which two things were forbidden in early Georgia?","Who was the old Creek chief who welcomed the settlers?"],"answers":["Poor debtors who had been in prison (and people persecuted for religion)","Slavery and rum","Tomo-chi-chi"],"notes":"The chapter uses the word \"squaw,\" now considered offensive, and quotes a Creek speaker saying the Great Spirit gave white people more wisdom; that speech comes from English records. Georgia''s ban on slavery ended in 1749."}]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u6","title":"Part V: Stories of the French in America","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-6-1","title":"Chapter 45: How the Mississippi was Discovered","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for the troubles that kept stopping La Salle","Ask your child to narrate La Salle''s thousand-mile walk and how he finally reached the sea","Trace the Mississippi River from the Great Lakes to the Gulf of Mexico; find the Missouri, Arkansas, and Illinois rivers","Timeline: La Salle reaches the mouth of the Mississippi and claims it for France (1682)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 45","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01486"}],"questions":["Who were the first Frenchmen to travel down the Mississippi?","Why did the Indians call Tonty \"Iron Hand\"?","What happened to La Salle''s ship, the Griffin?"],"answers":["Marquette and Joliet (1673)","He had an iron hand, hidden under a glove, from a war wound","It sailed away with furs and was never heard of again"],"notes":"Native people are called \"almost as wild\" as animals and \"wondering savages.\" Also, only Marquette was a priest; Joliet was a fur trader and mapmaker."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-6-2","title":"Chapter 46: King William''s War and Queen Anne''s War","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for how Frontenac treated the English messenger","Ask your child to narrate Frontenac''s answer to Phips","Find Quebec, the St. Lawrence River, Schenectady, and Nova Scotia on a map","Timeline: Phips''s attack on Quebec fails (1690); Treaty of Utrecht (1713)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 46","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01519"}],"questions":["Why did King Louis want Frontenac to capture New York?","How did Frontenac reply to the demand to surrender Quebec?","What lands did Britain gain at the Treaty of Utrecht?"],"answers":["To reach the Hudson and get a port that did not freeze in winter","He would answer only with the mouths of his cannon","Nova Scotia, Newfoundland, and the Hudson Bay Territory"],"notes":"Indian allies of both sides are mentioned mainly in raids and massacres; discuss that Native nations had their own reasons for choosing sides in these European wars."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-6-3","title":"Chapter 47: The Mississippi Bubble","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for the tall tales told about Louisiana","Ask your child to narrate how the Mississippi Bubble grew and then burst","Find New Orleans, the mouth of the Mississippi, and Cape Breton Island (Louisbourg) on a map","Timeline: Bienville founds New Orleans (1718); New Englanders capture Louisbourg (1745)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 47","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01557"}],"questions":["How did Bienville get the English ship to turn back on the Mississippi?","What did John Law tell the French people about Louisiana?","Why were the New Englanders angry after the Peace of Aix-la-Chapelle?"],"answers":["He told the captain the river belonged to France and was not the Mississippi","That it had endless gold and silver and was an earthly paradise","Louisbourg, which they had captured, was given back to France"],"notes":"The book says John Law fled France in 1790; the correct year is 1720."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-6-4","title":"Chapter 48: How a Terrible Disaster Befell the British Army","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for why Braddock would not take advice","Ask your child to narrate the ambush near the Monongahela","Find the forks of the Ohio (Pittsburgh), the Monongahela River, Williamsburg, and Albany on a map","Timeline: Braddock''s defeat near Fort Duquesne (1755)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 48","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01584"}],"questions":["Why was young George Washington sent to the French fort?","Why did Braddock''s redcoats lose so badly?","How did Washington''s clothes show how close he came to danger?"],"answers":["To tell the French they were on British land and must leave","They stood in tight lines in bright coats while hidden enemies fired from the trees","They were riddled with bullet holes, and two horses were shot under him"],"notes":"Native people are called \"savages\" throughout, and the Indian-hunter Captain Jack is admired. A good chance to notice that Braddock''s own contempt for Native allies helped cause his defeat."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-6-5","title":"Chapter 49: The End of the French Rule in America","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for how Wolfe''s men got past the sentries","Ask your child to narrate the climb up the cliffs and the battle on the Plains of Abraham","Find Quebec, the St. Lawrence River, Montreal, and Pittsburgh on a map","Timeline: Battle of the Plains of Abraham; Quebec falls (1759)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 49","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01634"}],"questions":["What new name was Fort Duquesne given, and in whose honor?","How did the Highland officer trick the French sentries?","What did the Treaty of Paris of 1763 do?"],"answers":["Pittsburgh, for William Pitt","He answered in French that the boats were French provision boats","Gave Britain nearly all of France''s lands in North America (Spain got Louisiana)"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-6-6","title":"Chapter 50: The Rebellion of Pontiac","steps":["Read the chapter aloud together; listen for why the Indians preferred the French","Ask your child to narrate how Major Gladwin learned of Pontiac''s plan","Find Detroit, Michilimackinac (Mackinac), Fort Pitt, and Fort Niagara on a map","Timeline: Pontiac''s War begins (1763)"],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 50","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01689"}],"questions":["Why did the Indians become unhappy after the British took Canada?","Who was Pontiac?","How did the warriors get into the fort at Michilimackinac?"],"answers":["The British gave no gifts, looked down on them, and cleared their hunting lands","Chief of the Ottawas and leader of the uprising","They chased a ball game toward the open gate and grabbed hidden weapons"],"notes":"Pontiac''s side is called \"treachery\" and the chiefs \"hideously painted,\" and \"squaw\" is now considered offensive. The chapter does admit British contempt and land-clearing caused the war; discuss Pontiac as a leader defending his people''s lands."}]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u7","title":"Part VI: Stories of the Struggle for Liberty (Tea Party to Saratoga)","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-7-1","title":"Chapter 51: The Boston Tea party","steps":["Read chapter 51 aloud together. Listen for the two taxes Britain tried to put on the colonies.","Oral narration: Narrate how Boston answered the tea ships.","On a map, find Boston and its harbor; Salem.","Add to your timeline: Boston Tea Party (1773)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 51","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01735"}],"questions":["What was the colonists'' cry against the Stamp Act?","Which tax did King George keep after removing the others?","How did the King punish Boston after the Tea Party?"],"answers":["No taxation without representation","The tax on tea","He closed its port and moved the capital to Salem"],"notes":"The tea raiders'' disguise as \"Red Indians\" is described in stereotyped terms; a good moment to talk about why costumes of real peoples can be hurtful."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-7-2","title":"Chapter 52: Paul Revere''s Ride - The Unsheathing of the Sword","steps":["Read chapter 52 aloud together. Listen for the signal Paul Revere waited for.","Oral narration: Narrate Paul Revere''s ride from start to finish.","On a map, find Boston, Lexington, and Concord, Massachusetts.","Add to your timeline: Battles of Lexington and Concord (1775)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 52","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01763"}],"questions":["Where did the signal lights for Paul Revere appear?","Which two patriot leaders were the British sent to arrest?","What were ''minute men''?"],"answers":["On the tower of the Old North Church","Samuel Adams and John Hancock","Men ready to fight at a minute''s notice"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-7-3","title":"Chapter 53: The First Thrust-The Battle of Bunker Hill","steps":["Read chapter 53 aloud together. Listen for why the battle has the ''wrong'' name.","Oral narration: Narrate Ethan Allen''s capture of Fort Ticonderoga.","On a map, find Fort Ticonderoga and Lake Champlain; Breed''s Hill near Boston.","Add to your timeline: Battle of Bunker Hill (1775)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 53","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01796"}],"questions":["On which hill was the Battle of Bunker Hill really fought?","Why did the Americans finally leave the hill?","Who did Congress choose as commander-in-chief?"],"answers":["Breed''s Hill","They ran out of ammunition","George Washington"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-7-4","title":"Chapter 54: The War In Canada","steps":["Read chapter 54 aloud together. Listen for the hardships of Arnold''s march through Maine.","Oral narration: Narrate the attack on Quebec in the snowstorm.","On a map, find Maine, the St. Lawrence River, Quebec, and Lake Champlain.","Add to your timeline: Battle of Valcour Island on Lake Champlain (1776)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 54","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01837"}],"questions":["Which American general was killed in the attack on Quebec?","How did Captain Douglas get his ship to Quebec through the ice?","How did Arnold''s fleet escape the British the first night?"],"answers":["General Richard Montgomery","He rammed through the ice at full speed","They slipped through the British line in the dark and fog"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-7-5","title":"Chapter 55: The Birth of A Great Nation","steps":["Read chapter 55 aloud together. Listen for how Washington got cannon to Boston.","Oral narration: Narrate how the British were forced out of Boston.","On a map, find Dorchester Heights, Boston; Philadelphia.","Add to your timeline: Declaration of Independence (1776)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 55","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01855"}],"questions":["Who dragged the captured cannon to Boston?","Who wrote the Declaration of Independence?","Who signed the Declaration first?"],"answers":["Colonel Henry Knox","Thomas Jefferson","John Hancock"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-7-6","title":"Chapter 56: The Darkest Hour - Trenton and Princeton","steps":["Read chapter 56 aloud together. Listen for how the weather helped the Americans more than once.","Oral narration: Narrate the crossing of the Delaware and the surprise at Trenton.","On a map, find Long Island, Manhattan, the Delaware River, Trenton and Princeton.","Add to your timeline: Battle of Trenton (1776)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 56","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01871"}],"questions":["What hid the Americans'' retreat from Long Island?","What was Washington''s password the night he crossed the Delaware?","Who were the hired German soldiers at Trenton called?"],"answers":["A thick fog","Victory or death","Hessians"],"notes":"The book misprints the year of the Battle of Trenton. It was December 26, 1776."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-7-7","title":"Chapter 57: Burgoyne''s Campaign - Bennington and Oriskany","steps":["Read chapter 57 aloud together. Listen for how the first American flag was made at Fort Stanwix.","Oral narration: Narrate General Herkimer''s stand at Oriskany.","On a map, find Ticonderoga, Bennington (Vermont), and Fort Stanwix in New York.","Add to your timeline: Battles of Bennington and Oriskany (1777)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 57","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01907"}],"questions":["What was the British plan for the Hudson-St. Lawrence waterway?","How did Schuyler slow Burgoyne''s army?","What did Herkimer do after his leg was shattered?"],"answers":["To take it and cut the colonies in two","He blocked the roads with felled trees and burned bridges","He sat against a tree on his saddle and kept giving orders"],"notes":"Oriskany is framed as ''savage'' warfare by Brant''s men; note that the Haudenosaunee (Iroquois) were divided, with Oneida warriors fighting on the American side. Ticonderoga fell to Burgoyne in July 1777, not August as the book says."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-7-8","title":"Chapter 58: Burgoyne''s Campaign - Bemis Heights and Saratoga","steps":["Read chapter 58 aloud together. Listen for Arnold''s trick to scatter the enemy at Fort Stanwix.","Oral narration: Narrate Arnold''s charge at the second battle.","On a map, find Saratoga and Bemis Heights on the Hudson River.","Add to your timeline: Burgoyne surrenders at Saratoga (1777)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 58","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01930"}],"questions":["Who commanded the Americans at Saratoga instead of Schuyler?","Which leg was Arnold wounded in at Saratoga?","About how many men surrendered with Burgoyne?"],"answers":["General Horatio Gates","The same leg wounded at Quebec","Nearly six thousand"],"notes":"The Fort Stanwix story uses dated words (''redskin'', ''fire water'') and mocks both a mentally disabled man and Native allies; worth pausing to discuss."}]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u8","title":"Part VI: Stories of the Struggle for Liberty (Valley Forge to Yorktown)","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-8-1","title":"Chapter 59: Brandywine - Germantown - Valley Forge","steps":["Read chapter 59 aloud together. Listen for why Washington nearly resigned.","Oral narration: Narrate what the winter at Valley Forge was like.","On a map, find Brandywine Creek, Germantown, Philadelphia, and Valley Forge in Pennsylvania.","Add to your timeline: Winter at Valley Forge (1777-1778)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 59","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01966"}],"questions":["Which city did Howe capture?","Who drilled the army at Valley Forge?","What made the Battle of Germantown confusing?"],"answers":["Philadelphia","Baron von Steuben","A thick fog, so Americans fired on each other"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-8-2","title":"Chapter 60: War on the Sea","steps":["Read chapter 60 aloud together. Listen for John Paul Jones''s famous answer.","Oral narration: Narrate the fight between the Bonhomme Richard and the Serapis.","On a map, find Narragansett Bay (Rhode Island); the North Sea off England.","Add to your timeline: Bonhomme Richard defeats the Serapis (1779)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 60","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id01986"}],"questions":["Where was John Paul Jones born?","Who was the ship Bonhomme Richard named to honor?","What did Jones answer when asked if he had struck his flag?"],"answers":["Scotland","Benjamin Franklin (and France)","\"I haven''t begun to fight yet\""]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-8-3","title":"Chapter 61: The Battle of Monmouth - The Story of Captain Molly","steps":["Read chapter 61 aloud together. Listen for why Washington was so angry with General Lee.","Oral narration: Narrate Captain Molly''s deeds at Monmouth.","On a map, find Philadelphia, Monmouth (New Jersey), and New York.","Add to your timeline: Battle of Monmouth (1778)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 61","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02037"}],"questions":["Why did Clinton leave Philadelphia?","What did Captain Molly do when her husband fell?","What reward did Washington give her?"],"answers":["He thought holding it was worthless and moved his army to New York","She fired his cannon herself all day","He made her a sergeant"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-8-4","title":"Chapter 62: The Story of a Great Crime","steps":["Read chapter 62 aloud together. Listen for where Major Andre hid the papers.","Oral narration: Narrate how Andre was caught.","On a map, find West Point on the Hudson River.","Add to your timeline: Benedict Arnold''s treason discovered (1780)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 62","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02066"}],"questions":["Which fort did Arnold plan to hand to the British?","Where did the three Americans find the secret papers?","How did Arnold escape?"],"answers":["West Point","In Andre''s boots","He rode off and fled to the British ship Vulture"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-8-5","title":"Chapter 63: A Turning Point in the World''s History","steps":["Read chapter 63 aloud together. Listen for the tune the British band played at the surrender.","Oral narration: Narrate how Cornwallis was trapped at Yorktown.","On a map, find Yorktown and Chesapeake Bay, Virginia.","Add to your timeline: Cornwallis surrenders at Yorktown (1781)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 63","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02098"}],"questions":["Which country''s fleet and soldiers helped at Yorktown?","Why couldn''t Cornwallis escape by sea?","What became the western boundary of the United States in the peace?"],"answers":["France","The French fleet had beaten the British fleet at Chesapeake Bay","The Mississippi River"]}]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u9","title":"Part VII: The Young Nation (Washington to Jackson)","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-9-1","title":"Chapter 64: Washington First In War, First In Peace","steps":["Read chapter 64 aloud together. Listen for how many states had to accept the Constitution.","Oral narration: Narrate Washington''s first inauguration.","On a map, find Philadelphia, New York City, Vermont, Kentucky, and the Potomac River.","Add to your timeline: Washington becomes first President (1789)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 64","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02114"}],"questions":["Which state was first to accept the Constitution?","Who straightened out the nation''s money matters?","Which treaty with Britain made people angry at Washington?"],"answers":["Delaware","Alexander Hamilton","The Jay Treaty"],"notes":"Kentucky settlement is told only from the settlers'' side, and enslaved people carried off in the war appear only as property owed; worth a short discussion."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-9-2","title":"Chapter 65: Adams - How He Kept Peace with France","steps":["Read chapter 65 aloud together. Listen for the nicknames given to the new capital.","Oral narration: Narrate how Adams kept peace with France.","On a map, find France; Tennessee; Washington, D.C..","Add to your timeline: Government moves to Washington, D.C. (1800)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 65","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02183"}],"questions":["What song was written during the trouble with France?","What did Adams want written on his tomb?","What was Tennessee called when it was a separate republic?"],"answers":["Hail Columbia","That he took responsibility for peace with France","Franklin"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-9-3","title":"Chapter 66: Jefferson - How the Territory of the United States was Doubled","steps":["Read chapter 66 aloud together. Listen for why the Mississippi mattered so much to western farmers.","Oral narration: Narrate how the Louisiana Purchase happened.","On a map, find The Ohio River, Marietta, Cleveland, New Orleans, and the Louisiana Territory.","Add to your timeline: Louisiana Purchase (1803)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 66","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02200"}],"questions":["How did Jefferson arrive at his inauguration?","Why did Napoleon sell Louisiana?","What price was agreed?"],"answers":["He rode alone and tied up his horse himself","He needed money for his war with Britain","Fifteen million dollars"],"notes":"The Ohio wars are told only from the settlers'' side, with dated terms like ''Redmen''; the nations there were defending their homelands. (The book''s ''1893'' for Ohio is a misprint for 1803.)"},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-9-4","title":"Chapter 67: Jefferson - How the Door Into the Far West was Opened","steps":["Read chapter 67 aloud together. Listen for Lewis''s narrow escape from the grizzly bear.","Oral narration: Narrate one adventure of the journey west.","On a map, find The Missouri River, Fort Mandan (North Dakota), the Rocky Mountains, and the Columbia River.","Add to your timeline: Lewis and Clark Expedition (1804-1806)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 67","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02226"}],"questions":["Which river did the explorers follow from St. Louis?","Where did they spend their first winter?","Which river carried them to the Pacific?"],"answers":["The Missouri","Fort Mandan","The Columbia"],"notes":"Native peoples are called ''Redskins'' and said to understand nothing; the chapter leaves out Sacagawea and the many nations who guided and fed the party."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-9-5","title":"Chapter 68: Jefferson - About An American Who Wanted to be a King","steps":["Read chapter 68 aloud together. Listen for what title Burr hoped to take.","Oral narration: Narrate how Perkins caught and brought Burr in.","On a map, find New Orleans, the Mississippi River, and Richmond, Virginia.","Add to your timeline: Burr-Hamilton duel (1804)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 68","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02261"}],"questions":["Whom did Aaron Burr kill in a duel?","What did Burr want to make of the West?","Was Burr found guilty of treason?"],"answers":["Alexander Hamilton","An empire with himself as emperor","No, he was set free"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-9-6","title":"Chapter 69: Madison - The Shooting Star and the Prophet","steps":["Read chapter 69 aloud together. Listen for what the Prophet taught his people.","Oral narration: Narrate the Battle of Tippecanoe.","On a map, find Indiana and Tippecanoe.","Add to your timeline: Battle of Tippecanoe (1811)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 69","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02298"}],"questions":["What was Tecumseh''s great goal?","Who was the Prophet?","Why was Tecumseh angry with the Prophet after the battle?"],"answers":["To unite all the Indian nations and stop white settlement","Tecumseh''s brother, Tenskwatawa","He fought too soon and lost, ruining Tecumseh''s plans"],"notes":"The chapter shows Tecumseh''s cause with some sympathy but also speaks of Indian ''treachery''; talk about Harrison''s 1809 land treaty from the Shawnee point of view."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-9-7","title":"Chapter 70: Madison - War with Great Britain","steps":["Read chapter 70 aloud together. Listen for how the White House may have got its name.","Oral narration: Narrate how ''The Star-Spangled Banner'' was written.","On a map, find Lake Erie, the Thames River (Ontario), Washington, D.C., and Baltimore.","Add to your timeline: Star-Spangled Banner written at Fort McHenry (1814)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 70","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02321"}],"questions":["What nickname did the ship Constitution earn?","What was Perry''s message after Lake Erie?","Which treaty ended the War of 1812?"],"answers":["Old Ironsides","\"We have met the enemy, and they are ours\"","The Treaty of Ghent"],"notes":"Native warriors are called ''savage'' and ''fiery Redmen''; Tecumseh is shown protecting prisoners, which is a good counterpoint to discuss."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-9-8","title":"Chapter 71: Monroe-The First Whispers of a Storm-Monroe''s Famous Doctrine","steps":["Read chapter 71 aloud together. Listen for what the Missouri Compromise decided.","Oral narration: Narrate the Monroe Doctrine in your own words.","On a map, find Florida, Missouri, Maine, Mississippi, Illinois, and Alabama.","Add to your timeline: Missouri Compromise (1820)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 71","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02365"}],"questions":["Which country sold Florida to the United States?","Which state came in as a free state to balance Missouri?","What did the Monroe Doctrine tell Europe?"],"answers":["Spain","Maine","Not to start new colonies or reconquer free nations in the Americas"],"notes":"The Seminole war is told only from the American side, and the claim that white men could not work in southern heat is an old excuse for slavery, not a fact."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-9-9","title":"Chapter 72: Adams - The Tariff of Abominations","steps":["Read chapter 72 aloud together. Listen for why the North and South disagreed about tariffs.","Oral narration: Narrate what a tariff is and why the South disliked it.","On a map, find The industrial North and the cotton South (point to regions).","Add to your timeline: Tariff of Abominations (1828)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 72","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02394"}],"questions":["Whose son was John Quincy Adams?","What new inventions did Adams want built?","Why did the North want high tariffs?"],"answers":["John Adams, the second President","Canals, railroads, and bridges","To protect its factories from foreign goods"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-9-10","title":"Chapter 73: Jackson - \"Liberty and Union, Now and Forever\" - Van Buren - Hard Times","steps":["Read chapter 73 aloud together. Listen for Jackson''s famous toast.","Oral narration: Narrate Webster''s ''Liberty and Union'' speech or the nullification crisis.","On a map, find South Carolina, Arkansas, and Michigan.","Add to your timeline: South Carolina''s nullification crisis (1832)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 73","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02406"}],"questions":["Why was Jackson called Old Hickory?","What was the ''spoils system''?","What hard times began under Van Buren?"],"answers":["For his tough, unbending will","Firing government workers to give their jobs to the President''s friends","Banks and businesses failed and food grew dear"],"notes":"The chapter never mentions Indian Removal, a major act of Jackson''s presidency (the Trail of Tears followed in 1838); consider adding it."}]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u10","title":"Part VII: Growth and Division (Harrison to Buchanan)","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-10-1","title":"Chapter 74: Harrison - The Hero of Tippecanoe","steps":["Read chapter 74 aloud together. Listen for the campaign slogans.","Oral narration: Narrate the Log Cabin and Hard Cider campaign.","On a map, find Indiana (Tippecanoe) and Washington, D.C..","Add to your timeline: President Harrison dies after one month (1841)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 74","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02452"}],"questions":["What slogan did Harrison''s supporters use?","What did Harrison refuse to wear at his inauguration?","How long was Harrison President?"],"answers":["Log Cabin and Hard Cider (also ''Tippecanoe and Tyler too'')","An overcoat","About one month"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-10-2","title":"Chapter 75: Tyler - Florida Becomes a State","steps":["Read chapter 75 aloud together. Listen for who led the Seminoles.","Oral narration: Narrate how the Maine boundary quarrel was settled.","On a map, find Maine, Florida, and the lands west of the Mississippi.","Add to your timeline: Florida becomes a state (1845)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 75","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02469"}],"questions":["Who became President when Harrison died?","Who settled the Maine boundary for the United States?","How long did the war with the Seminoles last?"],"answers":["John Tyler","Daniel Webster","Seven years"],"notes":"Seminole removal is presented approvingly; the Seminoles resisted being forced from their homes, and many escaped slaves lived among them."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-10-3","title":"Chapter 76: Polk - How Much Land Was Added to the United States","steps":["Read chapter 76 aloud together. Listen for three ways the country grew: Texas, Oregon, and the lands won from Mexico.","Narrate how Santa Anna was captured after San Jacinto (oral narration).","On a map, find Texas, the Oregon Country, the 49th parallel, the Rio Grande, and Mexico City.","Add to your timeline: Mexican War ends; U.S. gains the Southwest (1848)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 76","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02479"}],"questions":["Why was Texas called the Lone Star State?","What did the cry \"Fifty-four Forty or Fight\" mean?","Where was the Oregon boundary finally set?"],"answers":["Its flag as a republic had a single star","People wanted all of Oregon up to that line, or war with Britain","At the 49th parallel"],"notes":"Marshall tells the Texas story and Mexican War mostly from the American side (though it admits Polk wanted an excuse for war); a good chance to talk about how Mexicans and Tejanos saw these events."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-10-4","title":"Chapter 77: Polk - The Finding of Gold","steps":["Read chapter 77 aloud together. Listen for how the news of gold changed everyday life.","Narrate how Marshall tested the shining grains to see if they were gold (oral narration).","On a map, find the Sacramento Valley and the routes to California by land and sea.","Add to your timeline: Gold found at Sutter''s Mill (1848)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 77","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02527"}],"questions":["Who found the first gold, and where?","What were the big covered wagons called?","Did Sutter and Marshall get rich from the gold?"],"answers":["James Marshall, in the stream at Sutter''s sawmill","Prairie schooners","No, both died poor"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-10-5","title":"Chapter 78: Taylor - Union or Disunion","steps":["Read chapter 78 aloud together. Listen for the three great speeches in the Senate.","Narrate why the South did not want California to come in as a free state (oral narration).","On a map, find California and the Missouri Compromise line.","Add to your timeline: Laying the Washington Monument cornerstone; President Taylor dies (1850)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 78","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02560"}],"questions":["What nickname did Zachary Taylor''s soldiers give him?","Which old senator spoke for the South even though he was too ill to read his own speech?","How did President Taylor become ill?"],"answers":["Old Rough and Ready","John C. Calhoun","After sitting in the hot sun at a July 4th ceremony and eating cherries and iced milk"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-10-6","title":"Chapter 79: Fillmore - The Underground Railroad","steps":["Read chapter 79 aloud together. Listen for how the Underground Railroad worked.","Narrate the story of Harriet Tubman''s escapes (oral narration).","On a map, find the route north from the slave states to Canada, and San Francisco.","Add to your timeline: California becomes a free state; Fugitive Slave Law (1850)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 79","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02588"}],"questions":["Was the Underground Railroad really a railroad?","Why did people call Harriet Tubman \"Moses\"?","What famous book made many people hate slavery?"],"answers":["No, it was a chain of safe houses helping people escape north","She led so many enslaved people to freedom","Uncle Tom''s Cabin"],"notes":"The chapter claims many enslaved people with kind masters lived happily; that was a common view in 1917 but historians reject it. Worth discussing alongside first-person accounts such as Frederick Douglass."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-10-7","title":"Chapter 80: Pierce - The Story of \"Bleeding Kansas\"","steps":["Read chapter 80 aloud together. Listen for why Kansas became a battleground.","Narrate what happened on election day in Kansas in 1855 (oral narration).","On a map, find Kansas, Nebraska, Missouri, and the Gadsden Purchase in Arizona and New Mexico.","Add to your timeline: Kansas-Nebraska Act (1854)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 80","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02612"}],"questions":["What did the Kansas-Nebraska Bill let the people of a territory decide?","Why were there more votes than voters in Kansas?","Was Kansas finally admitted free or slave?"],"answers":["Whether to be a free state or a slave state","Thousands of Missourians crossed the border and voted illegally","Free, in 1861"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-10-8","title":"Chapter 81: Buchanan - The Story of the Mormons","steps":["Read chapter 81 aloud together. Listen for where the Mormons traveled and why.","Narrate the Mormon journey to the Great Salt Lake (oral narration).","On a map, find Nauvoo, Illinois, and the Great Salt Lake in Utah.","Add to your timeline: Building of Salt Lake City begins (1847)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 81","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02646"}],"questions":["Who became the Mormon leader after Joseph Smith''s death?","What does the name Utah mean, according to the book?","Was a battle fought in the \"Mormon War\"?"],"answers":["Brigham Young","Mountain Home","No"],"notes":"The author''s portrait of Joseph Smith and the Latter-day Saints is openly scornful in a way modern histories avoid; you may want to note that it reflects one outsider''s opinion."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-10-9","title":"Chapter 82: Buchanan - The First Shots","steps":["Read chapter 82 aloud together. Listen for Lincoln''s early life and what turned him against slavery.","Narrate how Lincoln grew from a poor boy into a leader (oral narration).","On a map, find Kentucky, Illinois, South Carolina, and Charleston harbor with Fort Sumter.","Add to your timeline: Lincoln elected; South Carolina secedes (1860)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 82","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02672"}],"questions":["Why was Lincoln called Honest Abe?","Which state was the first to leave the Union?","What did Lincoln say about a house divided against itself?"],"answers":["He was so upright in his dealings","South Carolina","It cannot stand"]}]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u11","title":"Part VII: The Civil War (Lincoln)","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-11-1","title":"Chapter 83: Lincoln - From Bull Run to Fort Donelson","steps":["Read chapter 83 aloud together. Listen for how Stonewall Jackson and Grant each got their nicknames.","Narrate the fight for Fort Donelson (oral narration).","On a map, find Fort Sumter, Bull Run, West Virginia, and Fort Donelson on the Cumberland River.","Add to your timeline: Fort Sumter falls; Battle of Bull Run (1861)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 83","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02691"}],"questions":["How did Thomas Jackson get the name Stonewall?","What did U. S. Grant''s initials come to stand for?","Which new state was formed during the war from part of Virginia?"],"answers":["His men stood firm \"like a stone wall\" at Bull Run","Unconditional Surrender","West Virginia"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-11-2","title":"Chapter 84: Lincoln - The Story of the First Battle Between Ironclads","steps":["Read chapter 84 aloud together. Listen for what made the two strange ships different from wooden ships.","Narrate the battle between the Monitor and the Merrimac (oral narration).","On a map, find Hampton Roads and Norfolk, Virginia, and the Southern coastline under blockade.","Add to your timeline: Monitor fights the Merrimac (1862)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 84","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02731"}],"questions":["Why was the blockade so harmful to the South?","What did people say the Monitor looked like?","Who invented the Monitor?"],"answers":["It cut off the cotton trade and supplies from abroad","A cheesebox on a raft","John Ericsson"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-11-3","title":"Chapter 85: Lincoln - The Battle of Shiloh and the Taking of New Orleans","steps":["Read chapter 85 aloud together. Listen for why the Mississippi River mattered so much.","Narrate the first morning of the battle of Shiloh (oral narration).","On a map, find Pittsburg Landing on the Tennessee River, New Orleans, and Vicksburg.","Add to your timeline: Battle of Shiloh; New Orleans taken (1862)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 85","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02751"}],"questions":["How did the battle of Shiloh get its name?","Which naval commander took New Orleans?","Which river city still held out for the South?"],"answers":["From a little log meeting house near the fighting","David Farragut","Vicksburg"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-11-4","title":"Chapter 86: Lincoln - The Slaves are Made Free","steps":["Read chapter 86 aloud together. Listen for Lincoln''s reasons for waiting to free the slaves.","Narrate what happened at Fredericksburg (oral narration).","On a map, find Antietam in Maryland and Fredericksburg in Virginia.","Add to your timeline: Emancipation Proclamation signed (1863)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 86","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02784"}],"questions":["After which battle did Lincoln announce the Emancipation Proclamation?","When did the proclamation take effect?","What did many freed men do after emancipation?"],"answers":["Antietam","January 1, 1863","Joined the Union army"],"notes":"Marshall writes that the proclamation ended slavery; it actually freed people only in states in rebellion, and slavery ended everywhere with the 13th Amendment in 1865."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-11-5","title":"Chapter 87: Lincoln - Chancellorsville - The Death of Stonewall Jackson","steps":["Read chapter 87 aloud together. Listen for Jackson''s daring march through the Wilderness.","Narrate how Stonewall Jackson was wounded (oral narration).","On a map, find Chancellorsville and the Wilderness in Virginia.","Add to your timeline: Battle of Chancellorsville (1863)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 87","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02814"}],"questions":["What first warned the Federals that Jackson''s men were coming?","Who wounded Stonewall Jackson?","What nickname did Hooker''s men give him?"],"answers":["Frightened birds and animals rushing out of the woods","His own men, by mistake in the dark","Fighting Joe"],"notes":"The book praises Confederate leaders warmly, as was common in 1917; you might talk about honoring courage while remembering what the Confederacy fought to keep."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-11-6","title":"Chapter 88: Lincoln - The Battle of Gettysburg","steps":["Read chapter 88 aloud together. Listen for what happened on each of the three days.","Narrate Pickett''s Charge (oral narration).","On a map, find Gettysburg, Pennsylvania, with Seminary Ridge and Cemetery Hill.","Add to your timeline: Battle of Gettysburg and the Gettysburg Address (1863)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 88","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02854"}],"questions":["Who commanded the Union army at Gettysburg?","Which Confederate general led the great charge on the third day?","Why did Lincoln give a speech at Gettysburg?"],"answers":["George Meade","George Pickett","To dedicate a cemetery for the soldiers who died there"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-11-7","title":"Chapter 89: Lincoln-Grant''s Campaign-Sheridan''s Ride","steps":["Read chapter 89 aloud together. Listen for how the people of Vicksburg lived during the siege.","Narrate Sheridan''s ride to Cedar Creek (oral narration).","On a map, find Vicksburg, the Wilderness, Cold Harbor, Petersburg, and the Shenandoah Valley.","Add to your timeline: Vicksburg surrenders (1863)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 89","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02878"}],"questions":["On what day did the Union army enter Vicksburg?","Where did families in Vicksburg hide from the shells?","What did Lincoln say when people wanted Grant removed?"],"answers":["July 4th","In cellars and caves dug in the hillsides","\"I cannot spare that man; he fights.\""]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-11-8","title":"Chapter 90: Lincoln - Sherman''s March to the Sea - Lincoln Re-Elected President","steps":["Read chapter 90 aloud together. Listen for what Sherman''s army destroyed and what it spared.","Narrate Sherman''s march from Atlanta to Savannah (oral narration).","On a map, find Atlanta, Savannah, and the Carolinas.","Add to your timeline: Sherman''s March to the Sea (1864)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 90","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02925"}],"questions":["What Christmas gift did Sherman send Lincoln?","What does Nevada mean?","Why did Lincoln say people should keep him as President?"],"answers":["The city of Savannah","Snowy","It was not wise to swap horses while crossing a stream"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-11-9","title":"Chapter 91: Lincoln - The End of the War - The President''s Death","steps":["Read chapter 91 aloud together. Listen for how Grant treated Lee''s army at the surrender.","Narrate the meeting at Appomattox (oral narration).","On a map, find Petersburg, Richmond, Appomattox Court House, and Washington, D.C..","Add to your timeline: Lee surrenders; Lincoln is assassinated (1865)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 91","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02946"}],"questions":["Where did Lee surrender to Grant?","Why did Grant let the soldiers keep their horses?","Who shot President Lincoln?"],"answers":["In a house at Appomattox Court House","They would need them for spring plowing","John Wilkes Booth"],"notes":"The closing lines quote formerly enslaved people in a mocking dialect; you may want to pause there and talk about how freedpeople themselves described Lincoln''s death."}]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-u12","title":"Part VII: Reunion and a New Century (Johnson to Wilson)","lessons":[{"id":"this-country-of-ours-12-1","title":"Chapter 92: Johnson - How The President Was Impeached","steps":["Read chapter 92 aloud together. Listen for why Congress turned against President Johnson.","Narrate the quarrel over the Secretary of War''s office (oral narration).","On a map, find the former Confederate states, Nebraska, and Alaska with Sitka.","Add to your timeline: Alaska bought from Russia (1867); Johnson impeached (1868)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 92","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id02974"}],"questions":["By how many votes was Johnson saved from removal?","Which country sold Alaska to the United States?","What did Nebraska''s Native name mean, according to the book?"],"answers":["One vote","Russia","Shallow water"],"notes":"The Reconstruction section reflects the old view that Black voters were ignorant and Northern \"carpet-baggers\" ruined the South; modern historians see Reconstruction as a real, if short-lived, effort at equal rights that was ended largely by violence. The book says Johnson was found guilty. In fact the Senate acquitted him in 1868, by a single vote."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-12-2","title":"Chapter 93: Grant - A Peaceful Victory","steps":["Read chapter 93 aloud together. Listen for how a quarrel with Britain was settled without war.","Narrate what the Alabama Claims were about (oral narration).","On a map, find Geneva in Switzerland, the Canadian border, and Colorado.","Add to your timeline: Geneva arbitration settles the Alabama Claims (1872)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 93","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id03002"}],"questions":["What was the Alabama?","How was the quarrel settled?","Which state was admitted in 1876?"],"answers":["A Confederate warship built in Britain","By arbitration; Britain paid the U.S. money","Colorado"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-12-3","title":"Chapter 94: Hayes - Garfield - Arthur","steps":["Read chapter 94 aloud together. Listen for what the spoils system was.","Narrate how President Garfield was shot (oral narration).","On a map, find Washington, D.C., and the Ohio Canal where Garfield worked as a boy.","Add to your timeline: Civil service law passed (1883)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 94","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id03013"}],"questions":["What jobs did Garfield do as a young man?","Why did Guiteau shoot Garfield?","What did the 1883 law require for many government jobs?"],"answers":["Barge boy, farmer, carpenter, teacher, lawyer, and soldier","He was angry at being refused a government job","Competitive examinations"],"notes":"The book presents the end of Reconstruction as a happy return to order; it also meant Black Southerners lost much of their voting power for decades."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-12-4","title":"Chapter 95: Cleveland - Harrison - Cleveland","steps":["Read chapter 95 aloud together. Listen for the six new states and what their names mean.","Narrate why Cleveland was called the Veto Mayor (oral narration).","On a map, find North and South Dakota, Montana, Washington, Idaho, Wyoming, Utah, and Chicago.","Add to your timeline: Columbian World''s Fair in Chicago (1893)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 95","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id03035"}],"questions":["Which state first gave women the vote?","What made Cleveland''s presidency unusual?","What anniversary did the Chicago World''s Fair celebrate?"],"answers":["Wyoming","He served two terms with another President in between","400 years since Columbus reached America"]},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-12-5","title":"Chapter 96: McKinley - War and Sudden Death","steps":["Read chapter 96 aloud together. Listen for the islands that came under American rule.","Narrate how President McKinley was shot and how he acted afterward (oral narration).","On a map, find Cuba, Havana, Puerto Rico, the Philippines with Manila, Hawaii, and Buffalo.","Add to your timeline: Spanish-American War; Hawaii annexed (1898)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 96","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id03059"}],"questions":["What happened to the battleship Maine?","Who was the last queen of Hawaii?","Which islands in the Pacific did Spain give up?"],"answers":["It was blown up in Havana harbor","Queen Liliuokalani","The Philippines"],"notes":"The book calls the Filipino fight for independence a \"rebellion\" and says Filipinos grew content with American rule; the Philippine-American War was long and costly, and Hawaii''s queen was overthrown largely by American businessmen."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-12-6","title":"Chapter 97: Roosevelt - Taft","steps":["Read chapter 97 aloud together. Listen for how the Panama Canal came to be built.","Narrate the Oklahoma land rush (oral narration).","On a map, find Panama, Colombia, Oklahoma, New Mexico, and Arizona.","Add to your timeline: Oklahoma becomes a state (1907)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 97","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id03103"}],"questions":["Which country first tried to build a canal at Panama and gave up?","How old was Roosevelt when he became President?","What happened at noon on April 22, 1889, in Oklahoma?"],"answers":["France","Forty-two","Settlers rushed over the border to claim land"],"notes":"The land rush opened Indian Territory that had been promised to Native nations; worth noting whose land was being \"opened.\""},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-12-7","title":"Chapter 98: Wilson - Troubles With Mexico","steps":["Read chapter 98 aloud together. Listen for the changes Wilson made at home.","Narrate the trouble at Tampico and Vera Cruz (oral narration).","On a map, find Mexico, Tampico, Vera Cruz, and Niagara Falls.","Add to your timeline: Income tax begins under Wilson (1913)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 98","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id03127"}],"questions":["What new tax did Wilson''s government begin?","What did the Child Labour Act try to do?","Who were the A.B.C. Powers?"],"answers":["An income tax","Keep young children from being made to work too hard","Argentina, Brazil, and Chile"],"notes":"Mexico is described as a \"troublous neighbor\" in a revolution the author only sees from the outside; the Mexican Revolution had real causes worth learning about."},{"id":"this-country-of-ours-12-8","title":"Chapter 99: Wilson -The Great War","steps":["Read chapter 99 aloud together. Listen for why America first stayed neutral.","Narrate why President Wilson finally asked Congress to declare war (oral narration).","On a map, find Europe, Belgium, Germany, Britain, and France across the Atlantic.","Add to your timeline: United States enters the Great War (1917)."],"links":[{"label":"Read chapter 99","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3761/pg3761-images.html#id03152"}],"questions":["When did Wilson proclaim U.S. neutrality?","What German weapon at sea angered Americans most?","On what date did Wilson ask Congress for war?"],"answers":["August 1914","Submarines attacking ships","April 2, 1917"],"notes":"This chapter was written in the middle of World War I and reads like wartime persuasion against Germany; the book ends there, so you may want to tell how the war ended in 1918."}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"“This Country of Ours: The Story of the United States” by H. E. Marshall (1917), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3761. Lesson plan by Freerange."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (listing_id, version) do update set content = excluded.content;
insert into public.listings (id, seller_name, seller_kind, seller_verified, title, subjects, grade_min, grade_max, philosophies, format, price, description, includes, lessons, lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement, family_style, lesson_steps, cover, color, esa_eligible, created_at, package_id, package_version, license, attribution, outline) values ('l-lib-viking-tales', 'Jennie Hall', 'open', false, 'Viking Tales', array['history', 'reading']::text[], 2, 4, array['charlotte_mason', 'classical']::text[], 'online', 0, 'Jennie Hall''s classic 1902 read-aloud follows the Norse sagas from the boyhood of Harald Fairhair in Norway to the settling of Iceland and Greenland and Leif Ericsson''s voyage to Wineland in North America. It is gentle enough for grades 2–4 and great for siblings together: read a short chapter or part, have your child tell it back, find the place on a map, and add it to a Viking timeline. The full text is free online.', array['25 lessons in 4 units, every chapter in order (long chapters split into parts)', 'Link to the exact page of the free full text for every lesson', 'Narration prompt, map work or drawing, and timeline date for each lesson', 'Comprehension questions with answers', 'Parent notes on legend versus history, dated 1902 views, and violent or mature moments']::text[], 25, 4, 20, 'high', true, '{}'::text[], '⛵', '#4C7FD9', false, '2026-11-11T12:00:00.000Z', 'pk-lib-viking-tales', 1, '{"kind":"public_domain"}'::jsonb, '“Viking Tales” by Jennie Hall (1902), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/24811. Lesson plan by Freerange.', '[{"title":"Unit 1: The Sagas and Harald''s Boyhood","lessons":["What the Sagas Were","The Baby","The Tooth Thrall","Olaf''s Farm","Olaf''s Fight With Havard","Foes''-fear"]},{"title":"Unit 2: Harald, King of All Norway","lessons":["Harald is King","Harald''s Battle","Gyda''s Saucy Message","The Sea Fight","King Harald''s Wedding","King Harald Goes West-Over-Seas"]},{"title":"Unit 3: Homes in Iceland","lessons":["Homes in Iceland, Part 1: The Vow","Homes in Iceland, Part 2: Landfall","Homes in Iceland, Part 3: The High-Seat Pillars","Homes in Iceland, Part 4: Reykjavik","Homes in Iceland, Part 5: The Father of Iceland"]},{"title":"Unit 4: Greenland and Wineland","lessons":["Eric the Red, Part 1: Outlawed","Eric the Red, Part 2: Greenland","Leif and His New Land, Part 1: King Olaf and a Strange Shore","Leif and His New Land, Part 2: Wineland","Wineland the Good, Part 1: Thorfinn and Gudrid","Wineland the Good, Part 2: The First Winelander","Wineland the Good, Part 3: Strangers in Canoes","Descriptive Notes: Norse Homes, Names, and Gods"]}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set seller_name = excluded.seller_name, seller_kind = excluded.seller_kind, seller_verified = excluded.seller_verified, title = excluded.title, subjects = excluded.subjects, grade_min = excluded.grade_min, grade_max = excluded.grade_max, philosophies = excluded.philosophies, format = excluded.format, price = excluded.price, description = excluded.description, includes = excluded.includes, lessons = excluded.lessons, lessons_per_week = excluded.lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson = excluded.minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement = excluded.parent_involvement, family_style = excluded.family_style, lesson_steps = excluded.lesson_steps, cover = excluded.cover, color = excluded.color, esa_eligible = excluded.esa_eligible, package_id = excluded.package_id, package_version = excluded.package_version, license = excluded.license, attribution = excluded.attribution, outline = excluded.outline;
insert into public.package_releases (listing_id, version, content) values ('l-lib-viking-tales', 1, '{"units":[{"id":"viking-tales-u1","title":"Unit 1: The Sagas and Harald''s Boyhood","lessons":[{"id":"viking-tales-1-1","title":"What the Sagas Were","steps":["Read the opening section, \"What the Sagas Were,\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Iceland families spent long winter evenings and where the old stories came from","Map work: Find Iceland and Norway on a map; trace the sea between them with your finger","Timeline: Start a Viking timeline strip from 800 to 1050 AD; add \"Sagas written down in Iceland\" at c. 1200 AD"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"What the Sagas Were\"","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_9"}],"pages":"pp. 9–12","questions":["What was a skald?","What did the people of Iceland write the sagas on?","Why did fathers tell these stories to their children?"],"answers":["A man who sang or told the old stories, often with a harp","Sheepskin (vellum)","To make them brave and wise and teach them to love their country"],"notes":"The sagas were written down in Iceland in the 1200s, two or three hundred years after the events in this book, so they mix real history with legend. The old vellum books are kept today in Reykjavik and Copenhagen as well as Norway."},{"id":"viking-tales-1-2","title":"The Baby","steps":["Read \"The Baby\" aloud together","Narration: tell the queen''s dream and how King Halfdan named his son","Map work: Find Norway and point to its long, jagged west coast full of fiords","Timeline: Add \"Harald born\" at c. 850 AD"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"The Baby\"","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_15"}],"pages":"pp. 15–18","questions":["What did the queen see in her dream?","What name did the king give the baby?","What was the king''s naming gift?"],"answers":["A thorn that grew into a huge tree spreading over all Norway","Harald","Ten pounds of gold"],"notes":"Harald Halfdansson is a real king, but stories of his birth, like the queen''s dream, come from sagas written centuries later. Treat the dream as legend."},{"id":"viking-tales-1-3","title":"The Tooth Thrall","steps":["Read \"The Tooth Thrall\" aloud together","Narration: tell what Harald and Olaf saw from the hill and the story of Aegir''s Rock","Map work: On a map of Norway, find a fiord; draw a quick picture of a fiord with steep rock walls","Timeline: Add \"Norse people worship Odin and Thor\" across 800–1000 AD"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"The Tooth Thrall\"","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_19"}],"pages":"pp. 19–26","questions":["What is a thrall?","What did Olaf say made thunder and lightning?","What was the name of Odin''s great feast hall?"],"answers":["A servant who is not free (a slave)","Thor hurling his hammer","Valhalla"],"notes":"Thralls were enslaved people; Olaf wears an iron collar. The book treats this lightly, so talk about what it means. Aegir''s leap is told as a brave deed in the Norse way of thinking; you may want to discuss it with sensitive children."},{"id":"viking-tales-1-4","title":"Olaf''s Farm","steps":["Read \"Olaf''s Farm\" aloud together (it is a longer chapter; take a break halfway)","Narration: tell how Olaf built his dragon ship and how he spent a winter at the farmer''s house","Map work: Find Denmark, Olaf''s home, and trace a route across the water to Norway","Timeline: Add \"Viking raids along the coasts\" across 793–1066 AD"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"Olaf''s Farm\"","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_27"}],"pages":"pp. 27–39","questions":["What did Olaf name his ship?","How old was Olaf when he built his boat?","How did Olaf come to be Harald''s thrall?"],"answers":["Waverunner","Fifteen","He was captured, and the queen asked the king to spare him and give him to the baby"],"notes":"Olaf tells raiding and taking over a family''s farm as a merry joke. Ask your child how the farmer might have felt. This is a good chance to talk about how Viking raids looked to the people being raided."},{"id":"viking-tales-1-5","title":"Olaf''s Fight With Havard","steps":["Read \"Olaf''s Fight With Havard\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Olaf''s men escaped from the farmhouse","Draw: a Viking ship with its dragon head, sail, and oars","Timeline: Add \"Viking ships sail the North Sea\" at c. 800–900 AD"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"Olaf''s Fight With Havard\"","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_40"}],"pages":"pp. 40–46","questions":["How did Olaf''s men find a way out of the house?","Why couldn''t King Havard follow them by sea?","What did Olaf do to tease Havard as he sailed off?"],"answers":["Thorkel fell against a panel and found a secret passage","Olaf''s men had broken the oars of his ships and thrown the sails in the water","He sang a mocking song"],"notes":"This chapter has a fight in which a farmer is killed and men die. It is short and not graphic, but preview it for very young listeners."},{"id":"viking-tales-1-6","title":"Foes''-fear","steps":["Read \"Foes''-fear\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Harald made his spear and what happened on the way home","Map work: Find France, Greece, Rome, and Arabia, places the things in Harald''s home came from","Timeline: Add \"Norse traders reach Constantinople and Baghdad\" at c. 850–950 AD"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"Foes''-fear\"","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_47"}],"pages":"pp. 47–52","questions":["What did the runes on Harald''s spear-point say?","What are skees?","What attacked Harald after he cut the spear handle?"],"answers":["Foes''-fear","Skis","A wolf"],"notes":"The Vikings really did trade far away; Arab silver coins and goods from Constantinople have been dug up in Scandinavia."}]},{"id":"viking-tales-u2","title":"Unit 2: Harald, King of All Norway","lessons":[{"id":"viking-tales-2-1","title":"Harald is King","steps":["Read \"Harald is King\" aloud together","Narration: describe the funeral feast in the hall: the shields, the food, the mead-horns, the skald","Draw: a Norse feast hall with the fire down the middle and the high seats","Timeline: Add \"Harald becomes king at age 10\" at c. 860 AD"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"Harald is King\"","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_53"}],"pages":"pp. 53–61","questions":["How old was Harald when his father died?","Where did the men hang their shields at the feast?","Why did the skald get a bracelet?"],"answers":["Ten","On the wall behind them","As thanks for his song about King Halfdan"],"notes":"The sagas say Harald became king at ten; the exact year is uncertain (c. 858–860)."},{"id":"viking-tales-2-2","title":"Harald''s Battle","steps":["Read \"Harald''s Battle\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Harald called his men to war and how the battle field was marked","Map work: Find the inland valleys of southeastern Norway, where Harald''s first kingdom lay","Timeline: Add \"Harald''s first battles for his kingdom\" at c. 860s AD"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"Harald''s Battle\"","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_62"}],"pages":"pp. 62–70","questions":["How did Harald call his men to war?","What did Thorstein raise on his spear to show he came in peace?","What did Harald do with the things taken from the enemy?"],"answers":["He sent arrows from farm to farm","A white shield","He divided them into piles for Thor, the king, the chiefs, and the men"],"notes":"There is battle and burial of the dead here, told simply. The sacrifice to Thor shows Norse religion of the time."},{"id":"viking-tales-2-3","title":"Gyda''s Saucy Message","steps":["Read \"Gyda''s Saucy Message\" aloud together","Narration: tell Gyda''s message and the vow Harald made because of it","Map work: Compare Norway with Denmark and Sweden on a map; which were already one kingdom in the story?","Timeline: Add \"Harald''s vow to rule all Norway\" at c. 865 AD (legend)"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"Gyda''s Saucy Message\"","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_71"}],"pages":"pp. 71–80","questions":["Why wouldn''t Gyda marry Harald at first?","What did Harald vow not to do until he was king of all Norway?","What new name did his uncle give him?"],"answers":["He was king of too small a land; she wanted him to rule all of Norway","Cut or comb his hair","Harald Shockhead"],"notes":"The Gyda story comes from Snorri Sturluson''s saga and is probably legend. The book says Harald''s army burned houses as it went; a good place to ask how the common people felt."},{"id":"viking-tales-2-4","title":"The Sea Fight","steps":["Read \"The Sea Fight\" aloud together","Narration: describe the ships and how the sea battle was fought","Draw: two lines of ships tied together prow to prow","Timeline: Add \"Harald''s great sea battles\" at c. 870 AD"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"The Sea Fight\"","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_81"}],"pages":"pp. 81–88","questions":["Why did Arnvid''s men tie their ships together?","What did the shield-guard on the high decks throw?","Who won the sea fight?"],"answers":["So they could fight like one big floating battlefield, side by side","Darts, stones, spears, and arrows","King Harald"],"notes":"Some warriors leap into the sea to die rather than surrender; the book presents this as brave. You may want to talk about it. The famous battle that finished Harald''s conquest is called Hafrsfjord, traditionally dated 872."},{"id":"viking-tales-2-5","title":"King Harald''s Wedding","steps":["Read \"King Harald''s Wedding\" aloud together","Narration: tell how Harald got his new name and what happened at the wedding","Map work: Trace Harald''s kingdom: the whole coast of Norway from south to far north","Timeline: Add \"Harald Fairhair king of all Norway\" at c. 872 AD"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"King Harald''s Wedding\"","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_89"}],"pages":"pp. 89–94","questions":["How many years did Harald fight before he cut his hair?","What new name did the earl give him?","What did Harald tie to Gyda''s belt, and what did it mean?"],"answers":["Ten years","Harald Hairfair (Fairhair)","A bunch of keys; she was mistress of his house"],"notes":"Historians today think Harald ruled mostly the western coast; \"king of all Norway\" is how the sagas remembered him."},{"id":"viking-tales-2-6","title":"King Harald Goes West-Over-Seas","steps":["Read \"King Harald Goes West-Over-Seas\" aloud together","Narration: tell where the unhappy chiefs went and why Harald sailed west","Map work: Find Normandy in France, Ireland, Scotland, and the Orkney and Shetland Islands","Timeline: Add \"Rollo (Rolf) gets Normandy\" at 911 AD"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"King Harald Goes West-Over-Seas\"","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_95"}],"pages":"pp. 95–100","questions":["Why did many men leave Norway?","What did Rolf name his land in France?","How did people explain the queen''s old dream at the end?"],"answers":["They would not kneel to Harald or pay him taxes","Normandy","Harald was the great tree; his kingdom and sons spread far like its branches"],"notes":"Rolf (Rollo) got Normandy in 911. His descendant William the Conqueror became king of England in 1066, five generations later, so \"his sons'' sons\" means great-great-great-grandsons."}]},{"id":"viking-tales-u3","title":"Unit 3: Homes in Iceland","lessons":[{"id":"viking-tales-3-1","title":"Homes in Iceland, Part 1: The Vow","steps":["Read pp. 103–109 of \"Homes in Iceland\" aloud, stopping after the night of the storm","Narration: tell why Ingolf and Leif decided to go to Iceland and what they packed","Map work: Trace a route from Norway to the Shetland Islands","Timeline: Add \"Ingolf''s first voyage to Iceland\" at c. 870 AD"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"Homes in Iceland\" from p. 103","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_103"}],"pages":"pp. 103–109","questions":["Why did Ingolf and Leif need to leave Norway?","How many men did Ingolf take, and why only that many?","What happened to one man in the storm?"],"answers":["They had killed two men and feared their kinsmen and King Harald","Thirty, one for each oar, because food and room were short","He was washed overboard and drowned"],"notes":"This chapter is long, so it is split over five lessons. The sagas call Leif \"Hjorleif\" (Leif the Sword); he is a different man from Leif Ericsson later in the book."},{"id":"viking-tales-3-2","title":"Homes in Iceland, Part 2: Landfall","steps":["Read pp. 110–117 aloud, from the rocky islands to the campfire talk under the northern lights","Narration: tell what strange things Ingolf found in Iceland","Map work: Find the Faroe Islands and Iceland; notice how the islands are like stepping-stones","Timeline: Add \"Norse explorers reach Iceland\" at c. 870 AD"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"Homes in Iceland\" from p. 110","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_110"}],"pages":"pp. 110–117","questions":["How long had they been at sea when they reached Iceland?","What did Ingolf think made the hot springs and rumbling ground?","What did Ingolf say the northern lights were?"],"answers":["Four months","Giants of fire and frost","The flash of the Valkyrias'' armor"],"notes":"Iceland really does have volcanoes, geysers, hot springs, and glaciers. A short look at photos of geysers will help."},{"id":"viking-tales-3-3","title":"Homes in Iceland, Part 3: The High-Seat Pillars","steps":["Read pp. 118–126 aloud, from the return to Norway through the long, gloomy winter","Narration: tell what Ingolf did with the pillars of his high seat and why","Hands-on: carve or draw a tall pillar decorated with dragons and Thor''s hammer","Timeline: Add \"Ingolf settles in Iceland\" at 874 AD (traditional date)"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"Homes in Iceland\" from p. 118","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_118"}],"pages":"pp. 118–126","questions":["What did Ingolf bring from under the old altar?","Why did Ingolf throw the pillars overboard?","Why was Ingolf''s wife sad at Yule-time?"],"answers":["Earth (soil of Norway) in a little bronze chest","So Thor would guide them to the place where he should build his home","She missed the merry feasts and friends in Norway"],"notes":"Ingolf sacrifices horses to Odin; this is how Norse religion worked, and the book describes it plainly. 874 is the date Icelanders traditionally give for the first settlement."},{"id":"viking-tales-3-4","title":"Homes in Iceland, Part 4: Reykjavik","steps":["Read pp. 127–135 aloud, from the bad news about Leif to the games at the new home","Narration: tell what happened to Leif and how Ingolf finally found his pillars","Map work: Find Reykjavik and the Westman Islands off Iceland''s south coast","Timeline: Add \"Reykjavik founded\" at c. 874 AD"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"Homes in Iceland\" from p. 127","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_127"}],"pages":"pp. 127–135","questions":["How long did Ingolf wait before his pillars were found?","What does Reykjavik mean, and why did Ingolf choose that name?","How did Ingolf claim the land?"],"answers":["Three years","\"Smoky sea\" (smoky bay), because of the steaming hot springs","He built fires at the mouth of the rivers"],"notes":"Leif is killed by the thralls he brought from Ireland, and Ingolf kills them in return. Preview this for sensitive listeners. The islands where the thralls hid are still called the Westman Islands (\"Islands of the Westmen,\" meaning Irishmen)."},{"id":"viking-tales-3-5","title":"Homes in Iceland, Part 5: The Father of Iceland","steps":["Read pp. 136–142 aloud, to the end of the chapter","Narration: tell how Iceland changed by the time Ingolf was old and how he was buried","Draw: Ingolf''s ship grave under its mound","Timeline: Add \"Iceland''s Althing (assembly) founded\" at 930 AD"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"Homes in Iceland\" from p. 136","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_136"}],"pages":"pp. 136–142","questions":["Why were Ingolf and his men so happy to see a sail?","What did ships take from Iceland to Norway to sell?","What did people call Ingolf after he died?"],"answers":["They had lived alone for years and were lonely for new faces and news","Cloth, wool, fish, oil, and bird feathers","The father of Iceland"],"notes":"The burial includes killing horses and dogs to go with Ingolf to Valhalla. Ship burials like this are real; famous ones have been dug up in Norway (Oseberg, Gokstad). The book''s note calling Reykjavik \"the only city in Iceland\" was true in 1902."}]},{"id":"viking-tales-u4","title":"Unit 4: Greenland and Wineland","lessons":[{"id":"viking-tales-4-1","title":"Eric the Red, Part 1: Outlawed","steps":["Read pp. 143–150 of \"Eric the Red\" aloud, until they set off","Narration: tell why Eric had to leave Iceland and what land he wanted to find","Map work: Find western Iceland and look west across the sea toward Greenland","Timeline: Add \"Eric the Red outlawed from Iceland\" at c. 982 AD"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"Eric the Red\"","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_143"}],"pages":"pp. 143–150","questions":["Why was Eric outlawed?","Who first told of the shining white land in the west?","Where did Eric and his men hide from Thorgest?"],"answers":["He had killed men in his quarrels","Gunnbiorn","In the woods and a secret cave at Eyjolf''s farm"],"notes":"Being \"outlawed\" meant Eric had to leave Iceland for three years, and anyone could harm him if he stayed."},{"id":"viking-tales-4-2","title":"Eric the Red, Part 2: Greenland","steps":["Read pp. 151–160 aloud, to the end of the chapter","Narration: tell what Eric found on his voyage and why he chose the name Greenland","Map work: Find Greenland; notice the ice sheet in the middle and the green fiords on the southwest coast","Timeline: Add \"Eric settles Greenland\" at c. 985 AD"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"Eric the Red\" from p. 151","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_151"}],"pages":"pp. 151–160","questions":["What did Eric first think was one of Gunnbiorn''s ships?","What did the men burn for fuel when there was no wood?","Why did Eric call the land Greenland?"],"answers":["An iceberg","Animal bones and fat","So people would like the sound of it and want to come"],"notes":"The sagas say 25 ships set out and 14 arrived; this book says 35 and 15. The \"strong river\" in the sea is a real ocean current along Greenland''s east coast. Eric''s settlements lasted about 450 years."},{"id":"viking-tales-4-3","title":"Leif and His New Land, Part 1: King Olaf and a Strange Shore","steps":["Read pp. 161–167 of \"Leif and His New Land\" aloud, until Leif goes to look for Tyrker","Narration: tell about Leif''s visit to King Olaf and the storm that blew him to a new land","Map work: Trace Leif''s path from Greenland to Norway, then follow the storm southwest toward North America","Timeline: Add \"Leif Ericsson reaches North America\" at c. 1000 AD"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"Leif and His New Land\"","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_161"}],"pages":"pp. 161–167","questions":["Who was King Olaf related to?","What did King Olaf give Leif when he left?","What did the men like about the new land?"],"answers":["He was the great-grandson of Harald Hairfair","Two thralls","The tall trees, soft grass, and rich black soil"],"notes":"The sagas say King Olaf Tryggvason made Leif a Christian and sent him to bring Christianity to Greenland; this book leaves that out."},{"id":"viking-tales-4-4","title":"Leif and His New Land, Part 2: Wineland","steps":["Read pp. 168–173 aloud, to the end of the chapter","Narration: tell what Tyrker found and what Leif brought home","Map work: Find Newfoundland in Canada, where a real Norse camp has been found","Timeline: Add \"Leif names Wineland\" at c. 1000 AD"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"Leif and His New Land\" from p. 168","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_168"}],"pages":"pp. 168–173","questions":["What had Tyrker found in the woods?","Why did Tyrker know what they were?","Why were the Greenlanders so pleased with the lumber?"],"answers":["Wild grapes","He grew up in Germany, where grapes grow everywhere","No trees grow in Greenland"],"notes":"Many experts think the \"grapes\" may have been other wild berries, or that the Norse reached farther south where wild grapes grow, beyond their camp in Newfoundland."},{"id":"viking-tales-4-5","title":"Wineland the Good, Part 1: Thorfinn and Gudrid","steps":["Read pp. 174–179 of \"Wineland the Good\" aloud, until they decide to go","Narration: tell how Thorfinn helped Eric at Yule and why he wanted to sail to Wineland","Map work: Find Greenland again and trace a route south along the coast of Labrador","Timeline: Add \"Thorfinn and Gudrid sail to Wineland\" at c. 1010 AD"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"Wineland the Good\"","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_174"}],"pages":"pp. 174–179","questions":["Why was Eric gloomy before Yule?","How did Thorfinn solve Eric''s problem?","Whom did Thorfinn marry?"],"answers":["His storehouses were empty and he could not give his guests a good Yule feast","He gave Eric the flour, grain, and mead from his ship","Gudrid"],"notes":"Gudrid (Gudrid Thorbjarnardottir) is one of the most traveled women of the Middle Ages; she later went on pilgrimage to Rome."},{"id":"viking-tales-4-6","title":"Wineland the Good, Part 2: The First Winelander","steps":["Read pp. 180–184 aloud, until Snorri is born","Narration: tell how the settlers lived in Wineland and who was born there","Draw: the settlers'' little huts by the lake with cloaks hung in the doorways","Timeline: Add \"Snorri, first European child born in America\" at c. 1010 AD"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"Wineland the Good\" from p. 180","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_180"}],"pages":"pp. 180–184","questions":["How many men sailed to Wineland in three ships?","What did they use for doors on their huts?","What was the baby''s name, and what did the men call him?"],"answers":["One hundred and sixty","Their cloaks","Snorri; the \"Wineland king\""],"notes":"Biarni says the land is empty, but people already lived there, as the next lesson shows."},{"id":"viking-tales-4-7","title":"Wineland the Good, Part 3: Strangers in Canoes","steps":["Read pp. 185–193 aloud, to the end of the story","Narration: tell about the first meeting, the trading, and why the Norsemen went home","Map work: Find L''Anse aux Meadows on the northern tip of Newfoundland, Canada","Timeline: Add \"Norse camp at L''Anse aux Meadows\" at c. 1000–1020 AD; add \"Columbus reaches America\" at 1492 for comparison"],"links":[{"label":"Read \"Wineland the Good\" from p. 185","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_185"}],"pages":"pp. 185–193","questions":["What did the strangers want to trade for?","What frightened the strangers away the first time?","Why did Thorfinn and his people leave Wineland?"],"answers":["Red cloth (for their furs)","Thorfinn''s bull came bellowing out of the woods","They were attacked and feared more people would come back"],"notes":"The book calls the Native people \"savages,\" \"ugly,\" and \"not men.\" These are prejudiced 1902 words; talk about how the people who already lived there would have seen the strangers. They were probably ancestors of First Nations such as the Beothuk or Mi''kmaq. The book says Wineland was on the eastern shore of the United States, but the only Norse site ever found (in 1960) is at L''Anse aux Meadows in Newfoundland, Canada."},{"id":"viking-tales-4-8","title":"Descriptive Notes: Norse Homes, Names, and Gods","steps":["Read the Descriptive Notes at the back of the book (pp. 194–199) together","Narration: explain how names like \"Ericsson\" began and what a foster-brother was","Hands-on: make your own Norse name from your parent''s first name (Annasdottir, Peterson) and a nickname","Timeline: Add \"Viking Age ends\" at 1066 AD and look back over your whole strip"],"links":[{"label":"Read the Descriptive Notes","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24811/pg24811-images.html#Page_194"}],"pages":"pp. 194–199","questions":["What does the name \"Thorstein\" mean?","What were peace-bands for?","What were the narrow slits high in a feast hall called?"],"answers":["Thor''s stone","To tie a sword into its scabbard to show you came in peace","Wind''s-eyes"],"notes":"The note on Eskimos repeats a 1902 guess that has since been disproved. The Inuit moved east from Alaska into Greenland around 1200, after Eric''s settlers arrived; Eric found only ruins of earlier peoples. The \"Suggestions to Teachers\" and reading list that follow are for you, not the child."}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"“Viking Tales” by Jennie Hall (1902), in the public domain. Free full text: https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/24811. Lesson plan by Freerange."}'::jsonb)
  on conflict (listing_id, version) do update set content = excluded.content;
insert into public.listings (id, seller_name, seller_kind, seller_verified, title, subjects, grade_min, grade_max, philosophies, format, price, description, includes, lessons, lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement, family_style, lesson_steps, cover, color, esa_eligible, created_at, package_id, package_version, license, attribution, outline) values ('l-lib-webster-speller', 'Noah Webster', 'open', false, 'Webster''s Blue-Backed Speller', array['phonics', 'writing']::text[], 1, 4, array['classical', 'traditional']::text[], 'online', 0, 'Noah Webster''s famous "Blue-Backed Speller," the book that taught generations of American children to read and spell. It climbs from two-letter syllables like ba and ab up to six-syllable words, with short reading sentences and seven fables along the way. Each day you review a sound, read a table aloud, spell words from dictation, and copy a sentence. The full scanned book is free online.', array['136 lessons in 17 units, covering all 152 numbered tables', 'The sound key, alphabet, seven fables, and the reference tables on sound-alike words, plurals, numbers, and punctuation', 'Daily read-aloud, dictation, and copywork', 'Spelling and phonics questions with answers', 'A link to the exact page of the free scanned book for every lesson']::text[], 136, 4, 20, 'high', false, '{}'::text[], '📘', '#4C7FD9', false, '2026-11-11T12:00:00.000Z', 'pk-lib-webster-speller', 1, '{"kind":"public_domain"}'::jsonb, '“The Elementary Spelling Book: Being an Improvement on the American Spelling Book (revised edition)” by Noah Webster (1829), in the public domain. Free full text: https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web. Lesson plan by Freerange.', '[{"title":"Unit 1: Sounds, Marks, and Letters","lessons":["Analysis of Sounds: Vowels, Consonants, and Diphthongs","Analysis of Sounds: The Consonant Letters","Accent and the Key to Pronunciation","The Alphabet: Roman, Italic, Old English, and Script"]},{"title":"Unit 2: First Syllables (Nos. 1–11)","lessons":["Nos. 1–2: ba, be, bi, bo, bu","Nos. 3–4: pa, ra, sa and ab, eb, ib","Nos. 5–7: aj, ak, al and ok, ul, op","Nos. 8–9: bra, cra, dra and pra, tra, wra","Nos. 10–11: pha, qua, spa and spla, spra"]},{"title":"Unit 3: Words of Three and Four Letters (Nos. 12–25)","lessons":["No. 12: cab, lad, bid, rod","No. 13: log, dog, bag, pig","Nos. 14–15: hem, fan, pen; fun, map, top","No. 16: bat, met, fox, fix","Nos. 17–18: babe, cage, lake; bake, mile, yoke","Nos. 19–20: rule, ripe, hope; nine, bone, pure","Nos. 21–22: camp, lamp, scalp; romp, sharp, act","Nos. 23–24: shaft, left, colt; went, wept, last","No. 25: fast, best, mist, first"]},{"title":"Unit 4: Two Syllables and Blends (Nos. 26–35)","lessons":["No. 26: baker, lady, pony (Two Syllables, Accent on the First)","Nos. 27–28: scab, crib, grub; clam, trim, swim","No. 29: snap, flip, chop, shop","Nos. 30–31: bold, gold, hand; bird, herd, latch","No. 32: abase, reclaim, away (Accent on the Second)","Nos. 33–34: deed, seed, bee; steer, steel, keen","No. 35: coo, loop, boot, troop"]},{"title":"Unit 5: Endings and Plurals (Nos. 36–49)","lessons":["Nos. 36–37: back, quick, brick; rock, luck, tank","Nos. 38–39: shrink, mark, ask; snarl, barn, corn","Nos. 40–42: staff, call, well; push, brass, cross","No. 43: egg, eggs; cliff, cliffs (Singular and Plural)","No. 44, Part 1: banquet, pitcher, candy","No. 44, Part 2: The Reading Sentences","Nos. 45–46: judge, hinge, fringe; bench, catch","Nos. 47–48: rise, nose, guide; spoil, point, loud","No. 49: poach, owl, brown, flour"]},{"title":"Unit 6: Vowel Pairs and Longer Words (Nos. 50–59)","lessons":["Nos. 50–51: sea, bead, maid; loaf, chief, teach","Nos. 52–53: break, oak, nail; dream, grain, moan","No. 54: botany, colony, harmony (Three Syllables)","No. 55: globe, bribe, place, slice","No. 56: banter, letter, doctor, dinner","Nos. 57–58: heap, year, spear; near, air, wait","No. 59: float, saint, dew, crew"]},{"title":"Unit 7: Broad a and Three Syllables (Nos. 60–68)","lessons":["Nos. 60–61: fraud, awl, dawn; brine, shave, drive","Nos. 62–63: dignity, vanity; amazement, excitement","Nos. 64–65: selfish, varnish; horseback, padlock","No. 66: senate, private, pirate (Words in -ate)","No. 67: luminary, delicacy (Four Syllables)","No. 68: immortal, maternal, December"]},{"title":"Unit 8: Plain and Tricky Vowels (Nos. 69–79)","lessons":["Nos. 69–72: play, boy, claw, salt, shirt","No. 73: ladder, udder, gander","No. 74: bracelet, poet, labor, major","No. 75: arrival, approval, renewal","No. 76: overlook, disapprove, intervene","No. 77: atlas, copy, hurry, poppy","No. 78: banishment, tendency, interest","No. 79: aerial, memorial, material"]},{"title":"Unit 9: More Two- and Three-Syllable Words (Nos. 80–89)","lessons":["No. 80: muslin, linen, chicken, kitchen","No. 81: inventive, forgetful, successful","Nos. 82–83: missionary; relative, marvelous","No. 84: bonfire, empire, umpire, brimstone","No. 85: heroism, porcupine, origin","No. 86: compel, forget, regret, elect","No. 87: formal, charcoal, quarrel, squirrel","Nos. 88–89: beaver, weaver, mayor; address, distress"]},{"title":"Unit 10: Longer Words and the th Sound (Nos. 90–99)","lessons":["No. 90: perennial, terrestrial, evaporate","No. 91: acquire, admire, adore, before","No. 92: between, ordain, complain, explain","Nos. 93–94: entertainment, desperado; cinnamon, skeleton","No. 95: numerous, humorous, treacherous","No. 96: theme, three, north, truth","No. 97: Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, filbert","No. 98: borrow, elbow, shadow, narrow","No. 99: treatise, oatmeal, floral, plural"]},{"title":"Unit 11: Five and Six Syllables (Nos. 100–110)","lessons":["No. 100: anonymous, amphibious, miraculous","No. 101: majority, dexterity, humility","Nos. 102–103: imaginary; compatibility","No. 104: benefit, alphabet, elephant","No. 105: opportunity, punctuality","No. 106, Part 1: plantation, vacation, quotation","No. 106, Part 2: temptation, location, and more","No. 107: publication, navigation, legislation","No. 108: definite, opposite, appetite, anecdote","No. 109: condense, resolve, embark","No. 110: agreement, engagement, opponent"]},{"title":"Unit 12: Endings and Seven Syllables (Nos. 111–121)","lessons":["No. 111: estimate, celebrate, separate","No. 112: villain, curtain, dolphin, mittens","No. 113: sentinel, furniture, handkerchief","Nos. 114–115: author, saucy, tawny; captive, stocking","No. 116: motion, nation, lotion, mansion","No. 117: democracy, geography, biography","No. 118: diagonal, millennium, republican","Nos. 119–120: congratulate; librarian, victorious","No. 121: individuality, incompressibility"]},{"title":"Unit 13: Special Sounds (Nos. 122–133)","lessons":["Nos. 122–123: thirteen, theory; mother, weather","No. 124: accomplish, establish, astonish","No. 125: ceremonial, testimonial, equatorial","No. 126: twelve, shelf, fresh; this, path, paths","No. 127: whale, wheat, which; who, whole","No. 128: exact, exist, example (x like gz)","Nos. 129–130: question, Christian; alien, savior; glazier, illusion","No. 131: Christ, scheme, ache, chorus, school","Nos. 132–133: gift, tiger, dagger; tragic, agile, legible"]},{"title":"Unit 14: Silent Letters and Word Endings (Nos. 134–146)","lessons":["Nos. 134–135: conscious, partial; precious, ambition","Nos. 136–137, Part 1: associate, appreciate; critic, logic","No. 137, Part 2: Words in -ical and -istic","Nos. 138–139: civilize, organize; characterize","No. 140: song, string; finger, anger","No. 141: capture, culture, feature","Nos. 142–143: gnat, knack, knead; machine, magazine","Nos. 144–146: bread, health; sign, assign; bacon, bitten"]},{"title":"Unit 15: Reading Lessons and Fables (No. 147)","lessons":["The Dog, The Stag, and The Squirrel","Fable I: Of the Boy That Stole Apples","Fable II: The Country Maid and Her Milk Pail","Fable III: The Two Dogs","Fable IV: The Partial Judge","Fable V: The Cat and the Rat","Fable VI: The Fox and the Bramble","Fable VII: The Bear and the Two Friends","Days, Weeks, and Hours"]},{"title":"Unit 16: Words Alike and Not Alike (No. 148)","lessons":["Words Nearly Alike: accept and except","Words Spelled Alike but Said Differently; Sound-Alikes (ail to climb)","Sound-Alike Words: council to wreak","Sound-Alike Words: rest to the end of the list","Sound-Alike Sentences, Part 1","Sound-Alike Sentences, Part 2"]},{"title":"Unit 17: Spelling Rules and Reference Tables (Nos. 149–152)","lessons":["No. 149: Words of Irregular Spelling","No. 149: Silent Letters","No. 150: Rules for Verbs (-ed and -ing)","Plurals of Nouns","Making New Words: -y, -ly, -ness, -less, -ful","Of Numbers: Figures, Roman Numerals, and Number Words","Words and Phrases from Foreign Languages","Abbreviations Explained","Punctuation and Capital Letters","No. 151: qu Words, Silent t, and ei / ie","No. 152, Part 1: Words Difficult to Spell (abeyance to journal)","No. 152, Part 2: Words Difficult to Spell (to the end)"]}]'::jsonb)
  on conflict (id) do update set seller_name = excluded.seller_name, seller_kind = excluded.seller_kind, seller_verified = excluded.seller_verified, title = excluded.title, subjects = excluded.subjects, grade_min = excluded.grade_min, grade_max = excluded.grade_max, philosophies = excluded.philosophies, format = excluded.format, price = excluded.price, description = excluded.description, includes = excluded.includes, lessons = excluded.lessons, lessons_per_week = excluded.lessons_per_week, minutes_per_lesson = excluded.minutes_per_lesson, parent_involvement = excluded.parent_involvement, family_style = excluded.family_style, lesson_steps = excluded.lesson_steps, cover = excluded.cover, color = excluded.color, esa_eligible = excluded.esa_eligible, package_id = excluded.package_id, package_version = excluded.package_version, license = excluded.license, attribution = excluded.attribution, outline = excluded.outline;
insert into public.package_releases (listing_id, version, content) values ('l-lib-webster-speller', 1, '{"units":[{"id":"webster-speller-u1","title":"Unit 1: Sounds, Marks, and Letters","lessons":[{"id":"webster-speller-1-1","title":"Analysis of Sounds: Vowels, Consonants, and Diphthongs","steps":["Parent: read pp. 8–10 first; then explain that vowels are open sounds and consonants are closed or stopped sounds","Say the long and short sound of each vowel: a (fame, fat), e (mete, met), i (fine, fin), o (note, not), u (mute, but)","Find the straight line (long) and curve (short) marks over vowels on p. 8"],"links":[{"label":"Analysis of Sounds (p. 8)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n7/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 8–10","questions":["Is the sound of b a vowel or a consonant?","What do we call two vowel sounds joined together, like ou in \"out\"?","Which mark shows a long vowel: a straight line or a curve?"],"answers":["A consonant","A diphthong","A straight line"],"notes":"These pages are mostly for the parent. Teach only the long/short marks now; the rest will come up table by table."},{"id":"webster-speller-1-2","title":"Analysis of Sounds: The Consonant Letters","steps":["Read together what the book says about one consonant a day, starting with B, C, and G","Hunt for silent letters: say dumb, gnaw, knave, wring, and listen for the letter you cannot hear","Sort these by the sound of c: cat, cell, cup, city"],"links":[{"label":"Consonants (p. 10)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n9/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 10–13","questions":["In \"knave,\" which letter is silent?","Does c sound like k or s in \"cell\"?","How is \"when\" said: with the h sound first or the w sound first?"],"answers":["k","s","The h sound comes first (hwen)"]},{"id":"webster-speller-1-3","title":"Accent and the Key to Pronunciation","steps":["Say a two-syllable word like ba-ker and clap on the syllable you say loudest; that is the accent","Read the Key to the Pronunciation on pp. 13–14 and find one example word for each vowel mark","Make a small card of the key to keep in the book for the year"],"links":[{"label":"Accent and Key (p. 13)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n12/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 13–14","questions":["Which syllable is accented in \"baker\"?","What mark shows the accented syllable in this book?","Does the o in \"son\" sound like short o or short u?"],"answers":["The first (ba'')","The accent mark ( '' )","Short u"]},{"id":"webster-speller-1-4","title":"The Alphabet: Roman, Italic, Old English, and Script","steps":["Say the names of the letters from the table on p. 15, pointing to each capital and small letter","Compare the Roman and italic forms of a, g, and k","Copy the small script letters a through g on p. 16"],"links":[{"label":"The Alphabet (p. 15)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n14/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 15–16","questions":["How many letters are in the English alphabet?","What does the character & stand for?","What is the name of the letter h?"],"answers":["Twenty-six","and","aitch"]}]},{"id":"webster-speller-u2","title":"Unit 2: First Syllables (Nos. 1–11)","lessons":[{"id":"webster-speller-2-1","title":"Nos. 1–2: ba, be, bi, bo, bu","steps":["Read each row of two-letter syllables aloud: ba be bi bo bu, ca ce ci co cu","Read the tiny sentences in No. 1, like \"go on\" and \"is he?\"","Write five syllables from the table on a slate or paper"],"links":[{"label":"No. 1 (p. 16)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n15/mode/1up"},{"label":"No. 2 (p. 17)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n16/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 16–17","questions":["Read and spell: g-o","Spell the syllable that sounds like the word \"be.\"","Does ce in No. 1 sound like \"see\" or \"key\"?"],"answers":["go","be","\"see\" (soft c)"]},{"id":"webster-speller-2-2","title":"Nos. 3–4: pa, ra, sa and ab, eb, ib","steps":["Read pa pe pi po pu py and ra re ri ro ru ry across the rows","Now read the reversed syllables in No. 4: ab eb ib ob ub, ad ed id od ud","Read the sentence \"I am to go in\" and copy it"],"links":[{"label":"Nos. 3–4 (p. 17)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n16/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 17–18","questions":["Spell the syllable that starts with r and ends with o.","Which is the reversed form of \"ba\"?","How many letters are in \"ud\"?"],"answers":["ro","ab","Two"]},{"id":"webster-speller-2-3","title":"Nos. 5–7: aj, ak, al and ok, ul, op","steps":["Read Nos. 5–7 down the columns: ak ek ik, al el il, am em im","Read the short sentences in No. 6, such as \"he is to go up by it\"","Copy one sentence from No. 6 with a capital letter at the start"],"links":[{"label":"Nos. 5–7 (p. 18)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n17/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 18","questions":["Spell the word made of u and p.","Spell the little word made of o and n.","Read and spell the word in \"he is to go up by it\" that starts with b."],"answers":["up","on","by"]},{"id":"webster-speller-2-4","title":"Nos. 8–9: bra, cra, dra and pra, tra, wra","steps":["Blend the two consonants first, then add the vowel: b-r-a, bra","Read the first little sentences in No. 9, like \"She fed the old hen\"","Spell 6–8 words from the columns from dictation, then check them against the book"],"links":[{"label":"No. 8 (p. 18)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n17/mode/1up"},{"label":"No. 9 (p. 19)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n18/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 18–19","questions":["Spell the syllable made of t, r, and y.","In \"wra,\" which letter is silent?","Who did she feed in the sentence from No. 9?"],"answers":["try","w","The old hen"]},{"id":"webster-speller-2-5","title":"Nos. 10–11: pha, qua, spa and spla, spra","steps":["Review: ph sounds like f, and qu sounds like kw","Read the three-letter blends spla, sple, spli, then spra, spre, spri","Write pha, qua, spa, and spra and read them back"],"links":[{"label":"Nos. 10–11 (p. 19)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n18/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 19–20","questions":["What sound does ph make in \"pha\"?","What letter always follows q?","How many consonants come before the vowel in \"spla\"?"],"answers":["f","u","Three (s, p, l)"]}]},{"id":"webster-speller-u3","title":"Unit 3: Words of Three and Four Letters (Nos. 12–25)","lessons":[{"id":"webster-speller-3-1","title":"No. 12: cab, lad, bid, rod","steps":["Read each column of short words aloud, then read \"A new cap\" and the little sentences","Spell 6–8 words from the columns from dictation, then check them against the book","Copy \"He has got a new tub.\""],"links":[{"label":"No. 12 (p. 20)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n19/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 20","questions":["Spell the word for a boy that rhymes with \"bad.\"","What did he get, in the first sentence?","Spell the opposite of \"good\" from the table."],"answers":["lad","A new tub","bad"]},{"id":"webster-speller-3-2","title":"No. 13: log, dog, bag, pig","steps":["Read the columns: log dog bog, bag rag wag, pig fig wig, jug mug hug","Read the sentences and point to each word from the table","Spell 6–8 words from the columns from dictation, then check them against the book"],"links":[{"label":"No. 13 (p. 20)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n19/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 20–21","questions":["Spell the word that rhymes with \"pig\" and is a fruit.","Which animal has been fed in the sentences?","Spell the farm animal in the table that rhymes with \"wig.\""],"answers":["fig","The pig","pig"]},{"id":"webster-speller-3-3","title":"Nos. 14–15: hem, fan, pen; fun, map, top","steps":["Read Nos. 14 and 15 aloud, listening for the short vowel in the middle of each word","Sort five words by their middle vowel: a, e, i, o, u","Spell 6–8 words from the columns from dictation, then check them against the book"],"links":[{"label":"Nos. 14–15 (p. 21)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n20/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 21","questions":["Spell the word that rhymes with \"top\" and is used to clean floors.","What vowel is in the middle of \"pen\"?","Spell the word that rhymes with \"sun\" and means a good time."],"answers":["mop","e","fun"]},{"id":"webster-speller-3-4","title":"No. 16: bat, met, fox, fix","steps":["Read the words ending in t and x","Read the sentences, such as \"I met six men to-day\"","Copy two sentences and underline the words from the table"],"links":[{"label":"No. 16 (p. 21)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n20/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 21–22","questions":["How many men did I meet?","Spell the animal in No. 16 that ends with x.","What does she have that is new in the first sentence?"],"answers":["Six","fox","A fan"]},{"id":"webster-speller-3-5","title":"Nos. 17–18: babe, cage, lake; bake, mile, yoke","steps":["Teach the rule: a final e makes the vowel before it say its name (lak → lake)","Read the long-vowel words in Nos. 17 and 18","Spell 6–8 words from the columns from dictation, then check them against the book"],"links":[{"label":"Nos. 17–18 (p. 22)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n21/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 22","questions":["Is the a in \"lake\" long or short?","Spell the word: a body of water, rhymes with \"cake.\"","Which letter in \"bake\" makes no sound?"],"answers":["Long","lake","The final e"]},{"id":"webster-speller-3-6","title":"Nos. 19–20: rule, ripe, hope; nine, bone, pure","steps":["Review the silent-e rule with u and o: rule, mope, hope, bone","Read every column in Nos. 19 and 20 aloud","Write pairs that change with a final e: rip/ripe, hop/hope, not/note"],"links":[{"label":"Nos. 19–20 (p. 22)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n21/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 22–23","questions":["Add e to \"hop.\" What word do you get?","Spell the number after eight.","Is the i in \"ripe\" long or short?"],"answers":["hope","nine","Long"]},{"id":"webster-speller-3-7","title":"Nos. 21–22: camp, lamp, scalp; romp, sharp, act","steps":["Read the words that end with two consonants: mp, lp, sp, ct","Clap each word: they are all one syllable","Spell 6–8 words from the columns from dictation, then check them against the book"],"links":[{"label":"Nos. 21–22 (p. 23)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n22/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 23","questions":["Spell the word for a light that rhymes with \"camp.\"","Which two letters end \"crisp\"?","How many syllables in \"sharp\"?"],"answers":["lamp","sp","One"]},{"id":"webster-speller-3-8","title":"Nos. 23–24: shaft, left, colt; went, wept, last","steps":["Read the words ending in ft, lt, nt, pt, and st","Make a list of three words from No. 24 that end in st","Spell 6 words from dictation"],"links":[{"label":"Nos. 23–24 (p. 23)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n22/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 23–24","questions":["Spell the word for a young horse.","Spell the past of \"weep\" from No. 24.","Which word means the opposite of \"first\"?"],"answers":["colt","wept","last"]},{"id":"webster-speller-3-9","title":"No. 25: fast, best, mist, first","steps":["Read the st words: fast, best, rest, west, first, thirst","Read the sentences on p. 25 aloud, such as \"Coal and wood will make a fire\"","Copy two sentences carefully, with capitals and periods"],"links":[{"label":"No. 25 (p. 24)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n23/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 24–25","questions":["What will fire burn, according to the first sentence?","Spell the direction where the sun sets.","How long does it take the world to turn round?"],"answers":["Wood and coal","west","A day"]}]},{"id":"webster-speller-u4","title":"Unit 4: Two Syllables and Blends (Nos. 26–35)","lessons":[{"id":"webster-speller-4-1","title":"No. 26: baker, lady, pony (Two Syllables, Accent on the First)","steps":["Read each word in parts, then whole: ba-ker, la-dy, po-ny","Clap the syllables and stress the first one","Read the sentences, such as \"A pony is a very little horse,\" and copy one"],"links":[{"label":"No. 26 (p. 25)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n24/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 25–26","questions":["Who bakes bread and cakes?","What is a pony, says the book?","Which syllable is accented in \"lady\"?"],"answers":["Bakers","A very little horse","The first"]},{"id":"webster-speller-4-2","title":"Nos. 27–28: scab, crib, grub; clam, trim, swim","steps":["Read words that begin with two consonants: sc, cr, gr, cl, tr, sw","Make new words by changing the first blend: trim → grim, slim","Spell 6–8 words from the columns from dictation, then check them against the book"],"links":[{"label":"Nos. 27–28 (p. 26)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n25/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 26","questions":["Spell the bed for a baby.","Spell the word that rhymes with \"trim\" and means to move in water.","What blend begins \"clam\"?"],"answers":["crib","swim","cl"]},{"id":"webster-speller-4-3","title":"No. 29: snap, flip, chop, shop","steps":["Review the sounds of ch and sh","Read the columns of No. 29 and the sentences below them","Spell 6 words from dictation and copy one sentence"],"links":[{"label":"No. 29 (p. 26)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n25/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 26–27","questions":["Spell the place where things are sold, rhymes with \"chop.\"","What two letters make the sound at the start of \"chop\"?","How many letters are in \"flip\"?"],"answers":["shop","ch","Four"]},{"id":"webster-speller-4-4","title":"Nos. 30–31: bold, gold, hand; bird, herd, latch","steps":["Read the words ending in ld, nd, rd, and tch","Read \"A bird sits on a branch to sing\" and copy it","Spell 6–8 words from the columns from dictation, then check them against the book"],"links":[{"label":"Nos. 30–31 (p. 27)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n26/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 27–28","questions":["Where does the bird sit to sing?","Spell the metal that rhymes with \"bold.\"","What does \"to filch\" mean, from No. 31?"],"answers":["On a branch","gold","To steal (something small)"]},{"id":"webster-speller-4-5","title":"No. 32: abase, reclaim, away (Accent on the Second)","steps":["Read each word with the stress on the second part: a-way'', re-claim''","Compare with No. 26, where the first part was stressed","Read three sentences from p. 29 and copy one"],"links":[{"label":"No. 32 (p. 28)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n27/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 28–29","questions":["Which syllable is accented in \"away\"?","What do men fill balloons with, says the book?","What do teachers like to see their pupils be?"],"answers":["The second","Gas","Polite"]},{"id":"webster-speller-4-6","title":"Nos. 33–34: deed, seed, bee; steer, steel, keen","steps":["Read all the ee words; ee says long e","Find three ee words that rhyme with \"feed\"","Spell 6–8 words from the columns from dictation, then check them against the book"],"links":[{"label":"Nos. 33–34 (p. 29)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n28/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 29–30","questions":["Spell the word that rhymes with \"see\" and makes honey.","What two letters say long e in these tables?","Spell the strong metal word in No. 34 that rhymes with \"feel.\""],"answers":["bee","ee","steel"]},{"id":"webster-speller-4-7","title":"No. 35: coo, loop, boot, troop","steps":["Read the oo words: coo, loop, boot, troop, stoop","Read the sentences, such as \"Eels swim in the brook\"","Copy two sentences and circle every oo"],"links":[{"label":"No. 35 (p. 30)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n29/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 30–31","questions":["What do plants grow from?","What are sharp tools made of?","What does a man cut trees down with?"],"answers":["Seeds","Steel","His ax"]}]},{"id":"webster-speller-u5","title":"Unit 5: Endings and Plurals (Nos. 36–49)","lessons":[{"id":"webster-speller-5-1","title":"Nos. 36–37: back, quick, brick; rock, luck, tank","steps":["Read the ck words in No. 36 and the nk and lk words in No. 37","Explain that ck comes right after a short vowel","Spell 6–8 words from the columns from dictation, then check them against the book"],"links":[{"label":"Nos. 36–37 (p. 31)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n30/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 31","questions":["Spell the word for a baby hen.","Which two letters end \"brick\"?","Spell the word that rhymes with \"duck\" and means good fortune."],"answers":["chick","ck","luck"]},{"id":"webster-speller-5-2","title":"Nos. 38–39: shrink, mark, ask; snarl, barn, corn","steps":["Read the nk, rk, and sk words, then the rl, rn, and rm words","Read \"I can play when my task is done\" and copy it","Spell 6 words from dictation"],"links":[{"label":"No. 38 (p. 31)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n30/mode/1up"},{"label":"No. 39 (p. 32)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n31/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 31–32","questions":["When can I play?","Spell where a farmer keeps his hay and animals.","Spell the grain that rhymes with \"born.\""],"answers":["When my task is done","barn","corn"]},{"id":"webster-speller-5-3","title":"Nos. 40–42: staff, call, well; push, brass, cross","steps":["Read the words ending in double letters: ff, ll, ss","Tell the rule: after a short vowel at the end of a short word, f, l, and s are often doubled","Spell 6–8 words from the columns from dictation, then check them against the book"],"links":[{"label":"Nos. 40–42 (p. 32)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n31/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 32–33","questions":["Spell \"kiss.\"","What double letter ends \"well\"?","Spell the word that rhymes with \"moss\" and means angry."],"answers":["kiss","ll","cross"]},{"id":"webster-speller-5-4","title":"No. 43: egg, eggs; cliff, cliffs (Singular and Plural)","steps":["Read the pairs across: one egg, two eggs; one bell, two bells","Read the sentences, such as \"A cliff is a high steep rock\"","Write the plural of five words from the table"],"links":[{"label":"No. 43 (p. 33)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n32/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 33–34","questions":["What is the plural of \"bell\"?","What is a skiff?","What is a tarn?"],"answers":["bells","A small rowboat","A small lake among the mountains"]},{"id":"webster-speller-5-5","title":"No. 44, Part 1: banquet, pitcher, candy","steps":["Read the first columns in syllables: ban-quet, pitch-er, can-dy","Stress the first syllable of each word","Spell 6–8 words from the columns from dictation, then check them against the book"],"links":[{"label":"No. 44 (p. 34)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n33/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 34–35","questions":["How many syllables in \"candy\"?","Spell the word: pitch + er.","Which syllable is accented in \"banquet\"?"],"answers":["Two","pitcher","The first"]},{"id":"webster-speller-5-6","title":"No. 44, Part 2: The Reading Sentences","steps":["Finish the columns of No. 44","Read the sentences, such as \"Silver spoons are not apt to rust\"","Copy two sentences from memory, then check"],"links":[{"label":"No. 44 (p. 35)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n34/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 35–36","questions":["What is cotton velvet like to the touch?","What are not apt to rust?","What should visitors not make too long?"],"answers":["Very soft","Silver spoons","Their visits"]},{"id":"webster-speller-5-7","title":"Nos. 45–46: judge, hinge, fringe; bench, catch","steps":["Read the dge and nge words, then the ch and tch words","Read the sentences, such as \"Doors are hung on hinges\"","Spell 6–8 words from the columns from dictation, then check them against the book"],"links":[{"label":"No. 45 (p. 36)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n35/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 36–37","questions":["What are doors hung on?","What does a farmer split rails with?","How long is the first joint of a man''s thumb?"],"answers":["Hinges","A wedge","One inch"]},{"id":"webster-speller-5-8","title":"Nos. 47–48: rise, nose, guide; spoil, point, loud","steps":["Notice that s in rise, wise, and nose sounds like z","Read the oi and ou words in No. 48: spoil, point, coin, loud, round","Read and copy \"A sphere is a round body or globe\""],"links":[{"label":"Nos. 47–48 (p. 37)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n36/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 37–38","questions":["Does the s in \"nose\" sound like s or z?","What is a sphere?","Spell the word that rhymes with \"join\" and is money."],"answers":["z","A round body or globe","coin"]},{"id":"webster-speller-5-9","title":"No. 49: poach, owl, brown, flour","steps":["Read the ow and ou words: owl, cowl, brown, gown, flour, sour","Read the sentences, such as \"The owl has large eyes and can see in the night\"","Copy two sentences and spell 6 words from dictation"],"links":[{"label":"No. 49 (p. 38)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n37/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 38–39","questions":["When can the owl see?","Where do the cat and mouse live?","Spell the color word that rhymes with \"town.\""],"answers":["In the night","In the house","brown"]}]},{"id":"webster-speller-u6","title":"Unit 6: Vowel Pairs and Longer Words (Nos. 50–59)","lessons":[{"id":"webster-speller-6-1","title":"Nos. 50–51: sea, bead, maid; loaf, chief, teach","steps":["Read ea, ai, oa, and ie words; most say the long sound of the first vowel","Read \"Few men can afford to keep a coach\"","Spell 6–8 words from the columns from dictation, then check them