ils/elementaryspelli00web/page/n127/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 128–130","questions":["Add -al to \"comic.\"","How many syllables in \"appreciate\"?","Spell \"logic.\""],"answers":["comical","Four","logic"]},{"id":"webster-speller-14-3","title":"No. 137, Part 2: Words in -ical and -istic","steps":["Finish the columns of No. 137","Read the sentences, such as the one about thermometrical observations","Spell 6 words"],"links":[{"label":"No. 137 (p. 130)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n129/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 130–132","questions":["What do thermometrical observations show?","What ending turns \"critic\" into a word that describes?","Spell \"classic.\""],"answers":["The temperature of the air","-al (critical)","classic"]},{"id":"webster-speller-14-4","title":"Nos. 138–139: civilize, organize; characterize","steps":["Read the -ize words: au-thor-ize, civ-il-ize, or-gan-ize","Find the root word in each: author, civil, organ","Read the sentence about tyrannizing over the weak and copy it"],"links":[{"label":"No. 138 (p. 132)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n131/mode/1up"},{"label":"No. 139 (p. 133)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n132/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 132–133","questions":["What is the root word in \"realize\"?","Over whom should we never tyrannize?","What ending do these words share?"],"answers":["real","Those weaker than ourselves","-ize"]},{"id":"webster-speller-14-5","title":"No. 140: song, string; finger, anger","steps":["Read the ng words with the plain sound: sing, song, bring","Then read the words where ng is followed by a hard g: fin-ger, an-ger, hun-ger","Spell 6–8 words from the columns from dictation, then check them against the book"],"links":[{"label":"No. 140 (p. 133)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n132/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 133–135","questions":["In \"finger,\" do you hear a hard g after the ng?","In \"singer,\" do you hear a hard g?","Spell \"tongue.\""],"answers":["Yes","No","tongue"]},{"id":"webster-speller-14-6","title":"No. 141: capture, culture, feature","steps":["Read the -ture words: cap-ture, cul-ture, fea-ture","Read the sentences, such as the one about the lungs","Spell 6 words"],"links":[{"label":"No. 141 (p. 135)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n134/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 135–136","questions":["What are the lungs the organs of?","What is a pang?","Spell \"culture.\""],"answers":["Respiration (breathing)","A severe pain","culture"]},{"id":"webster-speller-14-7","title":"Nos. 142–143: gnat, knack, knead; machine, magazine","steps":["Read the words where g and k before n are silent","Then read the words where ch says sh (machine, chaise)","Spell 6–8 words from the columns from dictation, then check them against the book"],"links":[{"label":"No. 142 (p. 136)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n135/mode/1up"},{"label":"No. 143 (p. 137)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n136/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 136–137","questions":["In \"gnat,\" which letter is silent?","What sound does ch make in \"machine\"?","What should you do to the dough to have good bread?"],"answers":["g","sh","Knead it thoroughly"]},{"id":"webster-speller-14-8","title":"Nos. 144–146: bread, health; sign, assign; bacon, bitten","steps":["No. 144: ea says short e (bread, dead, health)","No. 145: g is silent in sign and assign, but heard in signify","No. 146: the vowel before n is silent in bacon and bitten"],"links":[{"label":"No. 144 (p. 137)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n136/mode/1up"},{"label":"Nos. 145–146 (p. 138)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n137/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 137–139","questions":["Which letter is silent in \"sign\"?","Is the g heard in \"signify\"?","What vowel sound does ea make in \"bread\"?"],"answers":["g","Yes","Short e"]}]},{"id":"webster-speller-u15","title":"Unit 15: Reading Lessons and Fables (No. 147)","lessons":[{"id":"webster-speller-15-1","title":"The Dog, The Stag, and The Squirrel","steps":["Read the three short animal pieces aloud, taking turns","Tell back one of them in your own words","Copy two hard words and their meaning: mastiff, pricket"],"links":[{"label":"No. 147 (p. 139)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n138/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 139–140","questions":["What kind of dog is the mastiff used as?","What is the female of the red deer called?","Where do gray squirrels make their nests?"],"answers":["A watchdog","A hind","On the high branches of trees"]},{"id":"webster-speller-15-2","title":"Fable I: Of the Boy That Stole Apples","steps":["Read the fable together","Tell it back: the boy, the old man, the grass, the stones","Copy the moral in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Fable I (p. 140)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n139/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 140–141","questions":["What did the old man first throw at the boy?","What finally made the boy come down?","What is the lesson of the fable?"],"answers":["Turf, or grass","Stones","If kind words fail, the wicked must be dealt with more severely"]},{"id":"webster-speller-15-3","title":"Fable II: The Country Maid and Her Milk Pail","steps":["Read the fable aloud","List the maid''s dreams in order: eggs, chickens, a new gown","Tell what went wrong and why"],"links":[{"label":"Fable II (p. 141)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n140/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 141–142","questions":["What was the maid carrying on her head?","What color gown did she want?","What happened to the milk?"],"answers":["A pail of milk","Green","It fell and spilled when she tossed her head"]},{"id":"webster-speller-15-4","title":"Fable III: The Two Dogs","steps":["Read the fable aloud","Tell it back, naming Tray and Tiger","Talk about choosing friends wisely"],"links":[{"label":"Fable III (p. 142)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n141/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 142","questions":["Which dog was the good-natured spaniel?","What did Tiger do in the village?","Why was Tray beaten?"],"answers":["Tray","Attacked every dog he met","Because he was found in bad company"]},{"id":"webster-speller-15-5","title":"Fable IV: The Partial Judge","steps":["Read the fable aloud","Act out the farmer and the lawyer","Discuss what \"partial\" means here"],"links":[{"label":"Fable IV (p. 143)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n142/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 143","questions":["Whose bull gored the ox at first, in the farmer''s story?","How did the lawyer change when he learned it was his own bull?","What is the lesson?"],"answers":["The farmer''s","He wanted to look into the case instead of paying","Be as ready to do justice to others as to demand it from them"]},{"id":"webster-speller-15-6","title":"Fable V: The Cat and the Rat","steps":["Read the fable aloud","Tell the cat''s two tricks in order","Copy the old rat''s saying about caution in your own words"],"links":[{"label":"Fable V (p. 143)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n142/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 143–144","questions":["What was the cat''s first trick?","Where did she hide the second time?","What did the old rat say caution is the parent of?"],"answers":["Hanging by her feet from a hook, pretending to be dead","In the bottom of a meal tub, covered in flour","Safety"]},{"id":"webster-speller-15-7","title":"Fable VI: The Fox and the Bramble","steps":["Read the fable aloud","Tell why the fox stopped complaining about the thorns","Talk about a time something hard also kept you safe"],"links":[{"label":"Fable VI (p. 144)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n143/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 144–145","questions":["What was chasing the fox?","Where did he hide?","What good did the brambles do?"],"answers":["A pack of dogs","Under a bramble","They kept off the dogs and saved his life"]},{"id":"webster-speller-15-8","title":"Fable VII: The Bear and the Two Friends","steps":["Read the fable aloud","Tell what each friend did when the bear came","Discuss what a true friend does in danger"],"links":[{"label":"Fable VII (p. 145)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n144/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 145","questions":["What did the first friend do?","What did the other friend do?","What \"advice\" did the bear whisper?"],"answers":["Climbed a tree","Lay flat and pretended to be dead","Never to go with a friend who deserts you in danger"]},{"id":"webster-speller-15-9","title":"Days, Weeks, and Hours","steps":["Read the questions and answers about time aloud, one person asking and one answering","Say the days of the week in order and spell two of them","Tell what makes day and night"],"links":[{"label":"Time (p. 146)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n145/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 146","questions":["How many days are in a year?","How many hours in a day and night?","What makes the night?"],"answers":["365","Twenty-four","The shade (shadow) of the earth"]}]},{"id":"webster-speller-u16","title":"Unit 16: Words Alike and Not Alike (No. 148)","lessons":[{"id":"webster-speller-16-1","title":"Words Nearly Alike: accept and except","steps":["Read each pair and its meaning: accept / except, affect / effect","Say both words of each pair carefully so they sound different","Write three pairs with their meanings"],"links":[{"label":"No. 148 (p. 146)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n145/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 146–147","questions":["Which means \"to take\": accept or except?","Which means \"with a great voice\": aloud or allowed?","What is an errand?"],"answers":["accept","aloud","A message"]},{"id":"webster-speller-16-2","title":"Words Spelled Alike but Said Differently; Sound-Alikes (ail to climb)","steps":["Read the words spelled the same but said differently","Begin the sound-alike words: aunt / ant, ark / arc, climb / clime","Spell 6–8 words from the columns from dictation, then check them against the book"],"links":[{"label":"Words Spelled Alike (p. 147)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n146/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 147–148","questions":["Which is a vessel: ark or arc?","Which means \"to ascend\": climb or clime?","Who is an aunt?"],"answers":["ark","climb","A sister to a parent"]},{"id":"webster-speller-16-3","title":"Sound-Alike Words: council to wreak","steps":["Read the pairs and their meanings: hale / hail, lesson / lessen","Choose the right word in spoken sentences","Write three pairs with meanings"],"links":[{"label":"Sound-Alikes (p. 149)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n148/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 149–150","questions":["Which means \"healthy\": hale or hail?","Which means \"to diminish\": lesson or lessen?","Which means \"to revenge\": reek or wreak?"],"answers":["hale","lessen","wreak"]},{"id":"webster-speller-16-4","title":"Sound-Alike Words: rest to the end of the list","steps":["Finish the list of pairs: vain / vein, vice / vise, weigh / way","Read the first sentences that use the pairs, such as \"An oak bears acorns\"","Spell 6–8 words from the columns from dictation, then check them against the book"],"links":[{"label":"Sound-Alikes (p. 151)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n150/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 151–152","questions":["Which carries blood: vain or vein?","Which means \"to find the weight\": weigh or way?","What does an oak bear?"],"answers":["vein","weigh","Acorns"]},{"id":"webster-speller-16-5","title":"Sound-Alike Sentences, Part 1","steps":["Read the sentences aloud and find the sound-alike word in each","Take dictation of three sentences; the child must choose the right spelling","Check against the book"],"links":[{"label":"Sentences (p. 152)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n151/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 152–153","questions":["What does the Niagara River pour down?","Which is a large gun: cannon or canon?","Spell the word in \"Bears live in the woods\" that sounds like \"bares.\""],"answers":["A precipice","cannon","Bears"]},{"id":"webster-speller-16-6","title":"Sound-Alike Sentences, Part 2","steps":["Read the rest of the sentences","Make up your own sentence for two pairs, such as sleigh / slay","Spell 6 words"],"links":[{"label":"Sentences (p. 154)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n153/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 154–155","questions":["What do we sweat through?","What does a sleigh run on?","Who should children never slight?"],"answers":["The pores of the skin","Snow and ice","Their parents"]}]},{"id":"webster-speller-u17","title":"Unit 17: Spelling Rules and Reference Tables (Nos. 149–152)","lessons":[{"id":"webster-speller-17-1","title":"No. 149: Words of Irregular Spelling","steps":["Read the words that are not said the way they are spelled, with the book''s way of saying them","Pick five and practice: one, once, done, gone, half","Spell 6–8 words from the columns from dictation, then check them against the book"],"links":[{"label":"No. 149 (p. 156)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n155/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 156–157","questions":["Which letter is silent in \"half\"?","How is \"one\" said?","Spell \"once.\""],"answers":["l","wun","once"]},{"id":"webster-speller-17-2","title":"No. 149: Silent Letters","steps":["Read the lists of silent letters: l in calf and walk, h after r, g before n","Find the silent letter in each word you read","Spell 6 words"],"links":[{"label":"No. 149 (p. 157)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n156/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 157–159","questions":["Which letter is silent in \"calf\"?","Which letter is silent in \"rhyme\"?","Which letter is silent in \"gnaw\"?"],"answers":["l","h","g"]},{"id":"webster-speller-17-3","title":"No. 150: Rules for Verbs (-ed and -ing)","steps":["Read rules 1–5: add ed and ing; double the last consonant after a single short vowel","Read rules 6–12 about the agent (-er) and verbs ending in e, y, and ie","Write the -ed and -ing forms of hop, play, and carry"],"links":[{"label":"No. 150 (p. 159)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n158/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 159–161","questions":["Write the past tense of \"hop.\"","Write the -ing form of \"die.\"","Write the past tense of \"carry.\""],"answers":["hopped","dying","carried"]},{"id":"webster-speller-17-4","title":"Plurals of Nouns","steps":["Read rules 13–18 for making plurals","Make plurals: box, lady, day, leaf, church","Check each one by its rule"],"links":[{"label":"Plurals (p. 161)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n160/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 161–162","questions":["What is the plural of \"box\"?","What is the plural of \"lady\"?","What is the plural of \"leaf\"?"],"answers":["boxes","ladies","leaves"]},{"id":"webster-speller-17-5","title":"Making New Words: -y, -ly, -ness, -less, -ful","steps":["Read the tables of words made by adding endings","Build words: hand + y, kind + ness, care + less, joy + ful","Write five new words and use one in a sentence"],"links":[{"label":"Derivatives (p. 162)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n161/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 162–164","questions":["Add -ness to \"kind.\"","Add -ful to \"joy.\"","Add -less to \"care.\""],"answers":["kindness","joyful","careless"]},{"id":"webster-speller-17-6","title":"Of Numbers: Figures, Roman Numerals, and Number Words","steps":["Read the table of figures, letters (Roman numerals), and names","Write 1–20 in Roman numerals","Spell the ordinal words first through tenth"],"links":[{"label":"Of Numbers (p. 165)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n164/mode/1up"}],"pages":"p. 165","questions":["What Roman numeral is 10?","What number is XIII?","Spell the number word for 12."],"answers":["X","13","twelve"]},{"id":"webster-speller-17-7","title":"Words and Phrases from Foreign Languages","steps":["Read a handful of the phrases and their English meanings","Pick three you have heard before and say what they mean","Copy one phrase and its meaning"],"links":[{"label":"Foreign Phrases (p. 166)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n165/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 166–167","questions":["What does \"Veni, vidi, vici\" mean?","What does \"vice versa\" mean?"],"answers":["I came, I saw, I conquered","The terms being turned around (the other way round)"],"notes":"Choose phrases suited to your child''s age; this table is mostly for older students."},{"id":"webster-speller-17-8","title":"Abbreviations Explained","steps":["Read the list of abbreviations with their meanings","Find abbreviations you see in daily life: Mr., Mrs., No., P.O.","Write five abbreviations and what they stand for"],"links":[{"label":"Abbreviations (p. 167)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n166/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 167–168","questions":["What does \"No.\" stand for?","What does \"Mrs.\" stand for?","What does \"P.S.\" stand for?"],"answers":["Number","Mistress","Postscript"]},{"id":"webster-speller-17-9","title":"Punctuation and Capital Letters","steps":["Read about each mark: comma, semicolon, colon, period, question, exclamation, dash, apostrophe","Read the rules for capital letters","Copy a sentence from the fables and name every mark in it"],"links":[{"label":"Punctuation (p. 168)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n167/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 168–169","questions":["What does an apostrophe show?","What does a dash show?","Does a person''s name begin with a capital letter?"],"answers":["That a letter or letters are left out","A sudden stop or change of subject","Yes"]},{"id":"webster-speller-17-10","title":"No. 151: qu Words, Silent t, and ei / ie","steps":["Read the qu words (liquid, equity) and the words with silent t (glisten, chasten)","Learn the two ei and ie tables; memorize a few of each","Spell 6–8 words from the columns from dictation, then check them against the book"],"links":[{"label":"No. 151 (p. 169)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n168/mode/1up"},{"label":"ei and ie (p. 170)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n169/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 169–171","questions":["Spell \"receive.\"","Spell \"achieve.\"","Which letter is silent in \"glisten\"?"],"answers":["receive","achieve","t"]},{"id":"webster-speller-17-11","title":"No. 152, Part 1: Words Difficult to Spell (abeyance to journal)","steps":["Read the first groups of hard words with their sayings in parentheses","Choose 6 words suited to your child and practice them","Spell them from dictation"],"links":[{"label":"No. 152 (p. 171)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n170/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 171–172","questions":["Spell \"answer.\"","Which letter is silent in \"answer\"?","Spell \"ghost.\""],"answers":["answer","w","ghost"]},{"id":"webster-speller-17-12","title":"No. 152, Part 2: Words Difficult to Spell (to the end)","steps":["Read the rest of the hard words, including the French words at the end","Choose 6 words and practice them","Spell them from dictation, then review any from Part 1 you missed"],"links":[{"label":"No. 152 (p. 173)","url":"https://archive.org/details/elementaryspelli00web/page/n172/mode/1up"}],"pages":"pp. 173–174","questions":["Spell \"people.\"","How is \"yacht\" said?","Spell \"orphan.\""],"answers":["people","yot","orphan"]}]}],"version":1,"license":{"kind":"public_domain"},"attribution":"“The Elementary Spelling Book: Being an Improvement on the American Spelling Book (revised edition)” by Noah Webster (1829), in the public domain. Free full text: https://archive.org/details/eleme